1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:01,188
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:09,668
This course prepares you for the AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate Exam, exam code B, EAC01.

3
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:13,280
Before any of the technical content, spend 9 minutes here.

4
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:17,828
This module covers what the exam actually measures and how it is scored.

5
00:00:18,318 --> 00:00:21,412
It also covers what changed in the syllabus recently.

6
00:00:23,022 --> 00:00:28,580
Then it covers how to study in a way that moves your score rather than just feeling productive.

7
00:00:29,422 --> 00:00:33,028
That last part matters more than most people expect.

8
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:40,612
A word on where this content comes from because it makes a real difference to what you get out of it.

9
00:00:41,198 --> 00:00:45,732
Most preparation material in this category is recycled from leaked question banks.

10
00:00:46,254 --> 00:00:49,700
Courses like that teach you to recognize specific questions.

11
00:00:50,414 --> 00:00:52,484
They do not teach you to answer new ones.

12
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,108
This course was built differently.

13
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:02,404
A bank of 341 questions was analyzed, but only to work out which decisions the exam tests and how often.

14
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:07,460
Not one question option or explanation from it appears anywhere in this course.

15
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:17,348
Every scenario you will see is original and every explanation traces back to AWS documentation or the published exam guide.

16
00:01:19,022 --> 00:01:27,440
AWS describes the target candidate as someone with 2 to 3 years of data engineering experience and 1 to 2 years of hands-on

17
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:28,484
AWS.

18
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:35,972
That is the level the questions are pitched at. Notice the fourth item carries as much weight as you might expect the first two.

19
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:43,600
Security and governance is not a footnote on this exam. And notice what is explicitly out of scope.

20
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:49,360
You are not asked to train machine learning models. You are not asked for language-specific syntax.

21
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,964
And you are never asked to draw a business conclusion from data.

22
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:59,140
If an option seems to require one of those, it is almost certainly a distractor.

23
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:03,460
Some of these numbers change how you should behave in the room.

24
00:02:03,982 --> 00:02:08,880
15 of the 65 questions are unscored trial questions and they are not marked as such.

25
00:02:09,102 --> 00:02:13,604
So a question that feels bizarre or unreasonably obscure may simply not count.

26
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,840
Do not let it rattle you.

27
00:02:16,398 --> 00:02:24,772
The scoring is compensatory, which means a weak domain can be carried by strong ones. You need 720 overall, not a pass in each section.

28
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:29,156
An unanswered question score as incorrect with no penalty for a wrong guess.

29
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:31,588
There is never a reason to leave one blank.

30
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:37,348
These weights come straight from the exam guide, and this course mirrors them.

31
00:02:40,782 --> 00:02:44,480
That sounds obvious, but it is where a lot of self-study goes wrong.

32
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:53,120
Question banks are heavily skewed towards Glue, S3, and Redshift because those services appear in the wording of almost every scenario.

33
00:02:53,774 --> 00:03:02,020
In the bank, analyzed for this course, security terms accounted for under 5% of mentions, while security is 18% of the exam.

34
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:10,640
Study from that emphasis and you walk in around 9 scored questions short in the one domain where the gap is entirely avoidable.

35
00:03:11,982 --> 00:03:18,308
Now the most important thing in this module and the reason to watch an introduction rather than skip to module 1.

36
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:26,660
AWS published version 1.1 of the exam guide in December 2025.

37
00:03:27,054 --> 00:03:29,252
It added 8 new skills.

38
00:03:29,742 --> 00:03:34,212
Almost every question bank and video course currently available predates it.

39
00:03:36,046 --> 00:03:41,956
If you have already started studying from other material, there is a good chance you have a blind spot.

40
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,052
It is a blind spot you cannot see from inside that material.

41
00:03:46,574 --> 00:03:49,680
The next two slides tell you exactly what it is.

42
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:55,748
These are genuinely new areas, not renamed old ones.

43
00:03:58,222 --> 00:04:00,036
Iceberg in particular has moved on.

44
00:04:00,590 --> 00:04:03,140
It was a file format you might mention.

45
00:04:03,854 --> 00:04:10,596
It is now a managed capability with its own maintenance model and it has a dedicated module in this course.

46
00:04:11,694 --> 00:04:14,340
Three services were also removed from scope.

47
00:04:15,182 --> 00:04:21,092
AWS Cloud9, AWS Code Commit, and the AWS Schema Conversion Tool.

48
00:04:22,318 --> 00:04:24,548
There is one oddity worth knowing.

49
00:04:26,446 --> 00:04:30,692
A task statement still names the schema conversion tool as an example.

50
00:04:32,174 --> 00:04:36,992
Yet the tool was removed from the inScope service list in the same division.

51
00:04:37,518 --> 00:04:44,132
When the guide contradicts itself like that, learn the concept and do not sync time into the product.

52
00:04:45,614 --> 00:04:51,140
Certification content always lags the product catalog, and this exam is no exception.

53
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:53,924
The first item is the sharpest example.

54
00:04:55,950 --> 00:05:00,324
Kinesis Data Analytics for SQL was a correct answer in older material.

55
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:05,700
And as of January 2026, the applications have been deleted outright.

56
00:05:06,670 --> 00:05:07,844
You cannot buy it.

57
00:05:08,238 --> 00:05:10,308
It may still appear in question wording.

58
00:05:12,654 --> 00:05:14,596
The two renames matter differently.

59
00:05:15,246 --> 00:05:22,672
Both old and new names circulate in practice questions, so you need to recognize either and know they are the same product.

60
00:05:23,246 --> 00:05:28,484
Wherever this comes up in the course, the affected slide says plainly what changed,

61
00:05:29,198 --> 00:05:31,140
when and what replaced it.

62
00:05:32,782 --> 00:05:35,236
27 modules in 5 blocks.

63
00:05:35,712 --> 00:05:37,348
The order is deliberate.

64
00:05:37,632 --> 00:05:40,612
The domain modules come first and build vocabulary.

65
00:05:41,966 --> 00:05:46,212
By the time you reach the confusion pairs module, you are not learning services.

66
00:05:46,672 --> 00:05:49,732
You are learning to tell them apart under time pressure.

67
00:05:50,702 --> 00:05:57,796
That module contains almost no new content and it is often the most valuable 20 minutes in a course like this.

68
00:05:58,990 --> 00:06:02,820
The exam mechanics module contains no product content at all.

69
00:06:03,472 --> 00:06:13,540
It is about how AWS writes questions and it reliably gains marks because a real share of wrong answers come from misreading rather than not knowing.

70
00:06:14,958 --> 00:06:20,676
There are around 90 work scenarios across these modules plus 60 practice exam questions.

71
00:06:21,134 --> 00:06:22,724
Each one is two slides.

72
00:06:23,022 --> 00:06:27,236
First, the situation and the options, then a pause, then the walkthrough.

73
00:06:27,472 --> 00:06:29,700
When the pause comes, actually choose.

74
00:06:30,222 --> 00:06:32,644
Say the letter out loud if you are alone.

75
00:06:33,646 --> 00:06:40,740
Watching a walkthrough without committing first feels like learning and is very close to worthless because you never find out what you would have got wrong.

76
00:06:42,510 --> 00:06:46,244
Committing is uncomfortable, which is exactly why it works.

77
00:06:46,926 --> 00:06:50,948
Let me show you the shape once so the format is familiar when it matters.

78
00:06:52,718 --> 00:06:56,932
A retail company loads clickstream files into Amazon S3 every night.

79
00:06:57,454 --> 00:07:04,932
An analyst runs the same three aggregate queries each morning against the raw JSON files, and those queries have become slow and expensive.

80
00:07:05,422 --> 00:07:07,556
The company wants to reduce query cost.

81
00:07:08,462 --> 00:07:14,660
Nobody is waiting on the results before 9 in the morning, and the engineering team has no capacity to operate a new pipeline.

82
00:07:15,662 --> 00:07:19,524
Which change will reduce query cost with the least operational overhead?

83
00:07:20,718 --> 00:07:26,084
a Load the data into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster and query it there.

84
00:07:28,878 --> 00:07:34,916
Convert the files to Apache Parquet and partition by date using an AWS Blue job.

85
00:07:35,726 --> 00:07:36,032
C.

86
00:07:36,654 --> 00:07:40,164
Increase the Amazon Athena work group data scan limit.

87
00:07:40,718 --> 00:07:40,992
D.

88
00:07:41,582 --> 00:07:46,352
Move the files to S3 Glacier, deep archive between queries.

89
00:07:47,534 --> 00:07:48,912
Pause and decide.

90
00:07:55,662 --> 00:07:59,620
The answer is B, but the answer is not the lesson here.

91
00:08:00,430 --> 00:08:02,564
Look at how the question was decided.

92
00:08:02,816 --> 00:08:04,612
Two sentences did the work.

93
00:08:04,942 --> 00:08:12,484
Nobody is waiting before 9 in the morning does you latency is not a constraint, so an expensive low latency store is not needed.

94
00:08:13,230 --> 00:08:19,428
No capacity to operate a new pipeline eliminates option A before you compare a single technical merit.

95
00:08:20,014 --> 00:08:22,628
Option C is the classic trap on this exam.

96
00:08:23,150 --> 00:08:27,236
A setting whose name sounds relevant while doing the opposite of what was asked.

97
00:08:28,078 --> 00:08:35,812
Learning to read those two sentences as instructions rather than as background color is most of what separates a pass from a near miss.

98
00:08:38,606 --> 00:08:40,256
Here is the study route.

99
00:08:40,430 --> 00:08:45,988
Watch the modules in order once. Then sit practice exam 1 under timed conditions.

100
00:08:46,256 --> 00:08:52,548
Then, and this is the part people skip, look at where the wrong answers cluster rather than at the score.

101
00:08:53,102 --> 00:08:54,948
Re-watch only that domain.

102
00:08:55,790 --> 00:09:03,204
Take exam 2 a few days later, not the same evening, because spacing them is what makes them diagnostic rather than a memory test.

103
00:09:04,878 --> 00:09:10,500
The left column is the trap and it is seductive precisely because it is comfortable.

104
00:09:11,342 --> 00:09:15,716
Revising what you already know keeps your scores high and teaches you nothing.

105
00:09:17,486 --> 00:09:19,044
Five things before we begin.

106
00:09:19,662 --> 00:09:25,540
Keep the official exam guide open in a tab because it is the only authoritative list of what is in scope.

107
00:09:26,830 --> 00:09:30,736
If you have an AWS account, build one small thing per domain.

108
00:09:30,976 --> 00:09:34,660
A single blue job you actually ran will outlast an hour of watching.

109
00:09:36,718 --> 00:09:38,500
Expect the qualifier words.

110
00:09:39,374 --> 00:09:44,576
In the bank analyzed for this course, least operational overhead appeared 71 times.

111
00:09:44,878 --> 00:09:47,556
That is across 341 questions.

112
00:09:49,838 --> 00:09:52,416
Most cost effective appeared 42 times.

113
00:09:52,576 --> 00:09:57,572
Roughly one question in three is decided by the qualifier rather than by product knowledge.

114
00:09:58,478 --> 00:10:02,884
Module 1 is next, and it is the mental model that everything else hangs on.

115
00:10:04,494 --> 00:10:09,696
This module has no new services in it. It is the frame that every later module hangs on.

116
00:10:09,966 --> 00:10:13,456
By the end, you will have a 5-stage picture of any data pipeline.

117
00:10:13,616 --> 00:10:21,956
You will have a way of reading which constraint a question is really about, and you will have the vocabulary AWS uses to signal each one.

118
00:10:22,638 --> 00:10:25,956
Spend the time here rather than skipping to the services.

119
00:10:26,446 --> 00:10:31,136
Candidates who fail this exam rarely fail because they had never heard of a service.

120
00:10:31,470 --> 00:10:36,708
They fail because four options all sounded plausible with 40 seconds left on the clock.

121
00:10:37,422 --> 00:10:40,708
Everything in this module exists to make that moment shorter.

122
00:10:41,998 --> 00:10:47,456
Here is the single most useful thing to understand before you study any service.

123
00:10:48,046 --> 00:10:49,696
This is not a recall exam.

124
00:10:49,806 --> 00:10:53,188
You are rarely offered an option that is technically impossible.

125
00:10:53,934 --> 00:11:01,060
You are offered three or four architectures that would all move the data successfully and asked which one fits a stated constraint.

126
00:11:01,616 --> 00:11:04,900
That is why knowing what a service does is not enough.

127
00:11:05,616 --> 00:11:11,972
You need to know what each one costs you in money, in latency, and in the work of running it.

128
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:19,108
Every pipeline on this exam decomposes into these five stages, whatever services it uses.

129
00:11:19,726 --> 00:11:24,804
When a question describes a situation, place it on this line before you read the options.

130
00:11:25,646 --> 00:11:30,692
A question about a slow morning report is a serve problem and possibly a store problem.

131
00:11:31,470 --> 00:11:37,220
It is almost never an ingest problem, however much the question talks about how the data arrives.

132
00:11:37,774 --> 00:11:44,868
That sounds trivial and it saves you from a whole class of wrong answer where an option optimizes a stage that was never the bottleneck.

133
00:11:45,358 --> 00:11:46,564
Notice the call out.

134
00:11:47,374 --> 00:11:52,484
Governance and monitoring are not a sixth stage you bolt on at the end. They apply to all five.

135
00:11:53,230 --> 00:11:58,244
That is exactly why security is 18% of an exam that never has a security-only pipeline.

136
00:11:59,726 --> 00:12:03,620
The mapping is not perfectly clean and the exception is worth remembering.

137
00:12:04,718 --> 00:12:12,656
Orchestration appears in domain 1 as building workflows and again in domain 3 as automating and troubleshooting them.

138
00:12:13,614 --> 00:12:21,284
It is one of the few topics that earns marks in two domains, which makes it better value than its apparent size suggests.

139
00:12:23,374 --> 00:12:31,748
In the bank analyzed for this course, event bridge and step functions together appeared more often than Amazon Athena.

140
00:12:32,590 --> 00:12:36,420
Most study plans give orchestration a fraction of the time.

141
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:38,240
They give Athena.

142
00:12:39,854 --> 00:12:43,172
Three constraints decide almost every question on this paper.

143
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:48,964
How much it costs, how quickly the answer has to arrive, and how much human work it takes to keep the thing running.

144
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:54,180
The third is the one candidates underweight, and it is the one AWS asks about most often.

145
00:12:54,958 --> 00:13:01,252
Operational overhead means the ongoing labor of patching, sizing, scaling, upgrading, and babysitting.

146
00:13:01,838 --> 00:13:03,332
It is not the same as difficulty.

147
00:13:05,006 --> 00:13:08,996
A cluster can be easy to set up on a Friday and a burden every week afterwards.

148
00:13:09,742 --> 00:13:13,636
AWS is a managed services company and its exams reflect that view.

149
00:13:14,478 --> 00:13:23,044
When a question mentions operational overhead, the answer almost always moves toward the more managed option, even when a cheaper unmanaged one exists.

150
00:13:24,654 --> 00:13:28,160
Learn these phases as instructions rather than as decoration.

151
00:13:28,494 --> 00:13:30,800
Two of them get confused constantly.

152
00:13:31,022 --> 00:13:32,196
So separate them now.

153
00:13:33,454 --> 00:13:36,900
Least operational overhead is about running the thing over time.

154
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:41,572
Least development effort is about how much code you write once at the start.

155
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:52,772
Those pull in different directions more often than you would expect. Amazon EMR on EC2 is low development effort if your team already has Spark code that runs.

156
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:57,540
It is high operational overhead forever because somebody owns that cluster.

157
00:13:58,318 --> 00:14:01,604
And most cost effective never simply means cheapest.

158
00:14:02,574 --> 00:14:07,920
It means the cheapest option that still satisfies every other requirement stated in the question.

159
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:14,564
An option that is cheaper and misses the latency target is not the cost-effective answer, it is the wrong answer.

160
00:14:16,110 --> 00:14:19,908
This axis explains more wrong answers than any other single idea.

161
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:27,556
The instinct is that serverless is always the modern correct choice and the exam does lean that way, but not unconditionally.

162
00:14:29,326 --> 00:14:31,108
Think about what you are actually paying for.

163
00:14:32,110 --> 00:14:36,260
Provision means you rent capacity whether you use it or not.

164
00:14:36,974 --> 00:14:41,028
Serverless means you pay per request or per second of execution.

165
00:14:41,582 --> 00:14:46,372
So a cluster running flat out 24 hours a day is usually cheaper provisioned.

166
00:14:46,926 --> 00:14:51,280
Every unit of capacity you rent is capacity you genuinely consume.

167
00:14:51,918 --> 00:14:58,852
Serverless wins when the load is spiky, occasional or unpredictable because you stop paying entirely between runs.

168
00:14:59,598 --> 00:15:03,268
Read the workload description before you assume which side of this you are on.

169
00:15:04,078 --> 00:15:07,396
The words steady, constant, and continuous point one way.

170
00:15:08,398 --> 00:15:12,292
Occasional, ad hoc, and unpredictable point the other.

171
00:15:13,902 --> 00:15:22,276
Almost every service in this exam now exists in more than one form, and the exam expects you to know which one an option means.

172
00:15:23,982 --> 00:15:30,480
Amazon EMR exists on EC2 as EMR serverless and on EKS.

173
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:34,404
Redshift exists provisioned and serverless.

174
00:15:36,174 --> 00:15:39,684
Amazon MSK exists provisioned and serverless.

175
00:15:40,590 --> 00:15:44,324
Aurora exists provisioned and serverless version 2.

176
00:15:44,750 --> 00:15:50,244
This matters because the same service name can be the right answer or the wrong one depending on the form.

177
00:15:50,894 --> 00:15:56,400
When an option names a service without saying which form, read the rest of the option for the queue.

178
00:15:56,974 --> 00:16:03,972
The word cluster almost always means the provisioned form. The word workgroup means redshift serverless.

179
00:16:05,358 --> 00:16:10,564
And if an option mentions node types or instance sizing, it is provisioned by definition.

180
00:16:12,206 --> 00:16:18,240
Cost questions become much easier once you stop thinking about price and start thinking about the billing unit.

181
00:16:18,542 --> 00:16:21,156
Look at the pattern rather than the individual rows.

182
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:24,836
Some services charge you continuously for capacity you hold.

183
00:16:25,518 --> 00:16:31,140
Redshift provision nodes and kinesis shards keep billing whether anyone uses them or not.

184
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:34,628
Others charge only when work actually happens.

185
00:16:37,998 --> 00:16:39,396
Glue builds the seconds.

186
00:16:39,822 --> 00:16:40,804
A job runs.

187
00:16:41,326 --> 00:16:43,556
Lambda builds invocations and duration.

188
00:16:44,526 --> 00:16:51,716
That gives you a rule that works without memorizing a single price. Anything built continuously is expensive when idle.

189
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,484
Anything built per use becomes expensive when demand is constant and heavy.

190
00:16:57,294 --> 00:17:03,300
So a question describing an expensive service that sits mostly unused is pointing at the first group.

191
00:17:03,726 --> 00:17:07,876
The fix is almost always to move to the second.

192
00:17:09,326 --> 00:17:13,796
This is the first fork in most architecture questions, and there is a reliable test for it.

193
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:16,164
Ask who is waiting for the result.

194
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:22,564
If a fraud check must block a transaction before it completes, someone is waiting and it is streaming.

195
00:17:23,598 --> 00:17:29,348
If an analyst opens a dashboard at 9 in the morning, nobody was waiting overnight and it is batch.

196
00:17:30,190 --> 00:17:33,060
Now the trap, because it catches a lot of people.

197
00:17:33,614 --> 00:17:39,044
Watch for questions that describe data arriving continuously but being consumed once a day.

198
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:43,012
Continuous arrival does not make something a streaming problem.

199
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:45,220
Continuous consumption does.

200
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:51,492
Sensors that emit every second into an IT report are a batch workload with a chatty source.

201
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,908
The first verb scenario in this module is exactly that shape.

202
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:57,796
So hold that thought.

203
00:17:59,406 --> 00:18:07,140
Anchor these numbers because questions state a latency requirement and expect you to land on the right rung without hesitating.

204
00:18:07,822 --> 00:18:08,996
Work down from the top.

205
00:18:10,190 --> 00:18:15,012
Subsecond and per user means an in-memory or key value store.

206
00:18:15,854 --> 00:18:18,148
Seconds means a genuine stream.

207
00:18:18,734 --> 00:18:20,804
Minutes is comfortable batch territory.

208
00:18:21,582 --> 00:18:24,560
Next day means the cheapest thing that works.

209
00:18:24,942 --> 00:18:29,348
One detail is worth knowing precisely because older material gets it wrong.

210
00:18:29,870 --> 00:18:36,036
Amazon data firehose is usually described as near real time because it buffers before delivering.

211
00:18:36,718 --> 00:18:40,324
Since December 2023, it supports zero buffering.

212
00:18:41,294 --> 00:18:46,308
With the buffer interval set to zero, most records are delivered in about 5 seconds.

213
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:54,660
So a question demanding delivery within a few seconds does not automatically rule firehose out. A question demanding sub-second processing does.

214
00:18:56,270 --> 00:19:01,028
A logistics company receives GPS pings from 10,000 vehicles.

215
00:19:01,902 --> 00:19:05,796
They arrive continuously at about 2000 records per second.

216
00:19:06,702 --> 00:19:14,436
The pings are currently written to Amazon S3 by a custom application running on Amazon EC2 that the team maintains.

217
00:19:16,142 --> 00:19:17,956
The data is used for one thing.

218
00:19:18,926 --> 00:19:22,436
A report produced every morning showing yesterday's route efficiency.

219
00:19:23,438 --> 00:19:27,620
The operations team has asked for lower cost and less to maintain.

220
00:19:28,206 --> 00:19:36,560
A proposal on the table is to rebuild ingestion using Amazon Kinesis data streams with a managed service for Apache Flink

221
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:37,220
application.

222
00:19:38,190 --> 00:19:40,164
What should the data engineer recommend?

223
00:19:40,974 --> 00:19:43,108
a Adopt the proposal.

224
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:47,588
Kinesis Data Streams with the Flink application handles the throughput.

225
00:19:48,160 --> 00:19:55,748
B Replace the EC2 application with Amazon data, fire hose delivering directly to Amazon S3.

226
00:19:56,430 --> 00:19:56,640
C.

227
00:19:57,806 --> 00:20:04,772
Keep the EC2 application but move it to a larger instance type with auto scaling.

228
00:20:05,440 --> 00:20:05,680
D.

229
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:12,420
Adopt the proposal but use Amazon MSK instead of Kinesis Data Streams.

230
00:20:12,942 --> 00:20:14,148
Pause and decide.

231
00:20:20,878 --> 00:20:26,340
The answer is B, and the interesting part is that A and D are not wrong technically.

232
00:20:26,798 --> 00:20:28,164
They would both work.

233
00:20:28,416 --> 00:20:31,716
They are wrong because they solve a problem nobody has.

234
00:20:32,430 --> 00:20:35,684
Nothing in that situation consumes the data in real time.

235
00:20:36,398 --> 00:20:38,660
One report once a morning.

236
00:20:39,630 --> 00:20:48,932
Building a stream processing application to feed a daily report adds cost and adds an application to operate, which is the opposite of both stated requirements.

237
00:20:49,376 --> 00:20:54,884
When the impressive tool is offered and the requirements do not need it, that is usually the distractor.

238
00:20:55,296 --> 00:20:57,028
Also notice option C.

239
00:20:57,614 --> 00:21:01,572
It fixes throughput, and throughput was never described as a problem.

240
00:21:03,214 --> 00:21:08,804
Now the second big fork, and it is where a lot of domain 2 marks are won or lost.

241
00:21:09,296 --> 00:21:16,196
Candidates tend to reach for a store based on volume, thinking that large means red shift or S3.

242
00:21:17,358 --> 00:21:19,300
That is not how these questions work.

243
00:21:20,206 --> 00:21:25,696
Volume is almost never the deciding factor because every store in scope handles large volumes.

244
00:21:26,158 --> 00:21:32,804
What decides it is how the data is read, one row at a time by a key or millions of rows aggregated into a total.

245
00:21:33,454 --> 00:21:36,004
Fixed known queries or free text search.

246
00:21:38,478 --> 00:21:41,028
A schema you control or one you discover.

247
00:21:41,998 --> 00:21:46,788
Get the access pattern right and the store falls out of it almost mechanically.

248
00:21:47,566 --> 00:21:53,252
Get it wrong and every option looks defensible, which is the feeling of being about to lose a mark.

249
00:21:54,958 --> 00:21:56,164
Memorize this table.

250
00:21:56,718 --> 00:21:59,204
It resolves a large share of domain 2 on its own.

251
00:21:59,822 --> 00:22:05,936
The one row that needs unpacking is the Athena and Redshift pair because both run SQL over large datasets.

252
00:22:06,510 --> 00:22:08,060
Both appear as options constantly.

253
00:22:09,166 --> 00:22:14,244
The separator is not how complex the query is, it is how often the query runs.

254
00:22:14,958 --> 00:22:21,668
Athena charges per query by bytes scanned with no standing cost when nobody is querying.

255
00:22:22,256 --> 00:22:30,916
That is ideal for occasional or exploratory work and genuinely terrible for a dashboard refreshing every minute for 400 people.

256
00:22:31,534 --> 00:22:35,524
Redshift charges for capacity you keep, which is the reverse shape.

257
00:22:36,430 --> 00:22:39,616
Occasional and unpredictable favors Athena.

258
00:22:40,176 --> 00:22:42,916
Constant and repeated favors redshift.

259
00:22:44,782 --> 00:22:51,076
If a question tells you how many analysts and how often that sentence is there to decide this row.

260
00:22:52,656 --> 00:22:57,380
This is usually presented as an opposition and in practice it rarely is one.

261
00:22:57,936 --> 00:23:00,644
The common correct architecture uses both.

262
00:23:01,936 --> 00:23:06,340
Raw data lands in S3 because storage there is cheap and accepts anything.

263
00:23:07,246 --> 00:23:13,456
It is cleaned and curated, and the curated subset is loaded into Redshift because that is what gets queried repeatedly.

264
00:23:13,616 --> 00:23:18,596
Redshift spectrum then reaches back into S3 for the colder data without loading it at all.

265
00:23:19,150 --> 00:23:26,148
So when both appear as options, read carefully for whether the question is asking where data lands or where it is served from.

266
00:23:27,502 --> 00:23:30,404
Those have different answers within the same architecture.

267
00:23:31,278 --> 00:23:34,148
Confusing the two is a reliable way to lose a mark.

268
00:23:35,022 --> 00:23:41,988
You choose an option that is genuinely part of the right design and still not the answer to the question asked.

269
00:23:43,630 --> 00:23:49,860
Lakehouse is a word that means very little on its own, so anchor it to specific components rather than to the marketing.

270
00:23:50,286 --> 00:23:52,804
The idea is that data sits once in S3.

271
00:23:53,262 --> 00:23:56,612
It is described once in the AWS Blue Data Catalog.

272
00:23:56,976 --> 00:24:01,028
So every engine knows the table exists and what columns it has.

273
00:24:01,936 --> 00:24:08,816
It is permissioned once in AWS Lake Formation, so you are not maintaining four different sets of access rules.

274
00:24:08,976 --> 00:24:15,236
And it is then readable by Athena, Redshift, EMR, and Blue without copying it anywhere.

275
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:22,276
Apache ICB is what makes that safe for data that changes.

276
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:29,092
It brings transactions, time travel, and schema evolution to what is otherwise just a pile of files.

277
00:24:29,360 --> 00:24:31,600
Module 9 covers this properly.

278
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:35,236
For now, hold the shape and the four component names.

279
00:24:36,782 --> 00:24:41,188
This matters far more than it first appears, and the reason is pricing.

280
00:24:41,902 --> 00:24:45,444
Athena and Redshift Spectrum charge you for bytes scanned.

281
00:24:46,094 --> 00:24:48,676
Think about what a columnar format does to that number.

282
00:24:49,806 --> 00:24:54,960
A query touching three columns out of 40 reads roughly three-fortieths of the data.

283
00:24:55,502 --> 00:24:59,268
The columns are stored separately rather than interleaved row by row.

284
00:24:59,920 --> 00:25:07,908
Compression then shrinks it again and compresses better than a row format would because a single column holds similar values sitting next to each other.

285
00:25:08,814 --> 00:25:13,988
Partitioning attacks the same bin from the other direction by skipping entire folders.

286
00:25:14,286 --> 00:25:15,556
The query does not need.

287
00:25:17,678 --> 00:25:23,076
Convert to parquet and partition sensibly is the single most reusable answer on this exam.

288
00:25:23,822 --> 00:25:28,932
When a question mentions query cost and mentions raw text files, that is the answer forming.

289
00:25:30,702 --> 00:25:35,524
Apply these in order when a question describes a slow or expensive data lake.

290
00:25:36,398 --> 00:25:41,028
Partitioning first because skipping data entirely beats reading it efficiently.

291
00:25:41,742 --> 00:25:43,972
Then format, then compression.

292
00:25:44,846 --> 00:25:49,092
The last two are the ones people forget and both appear as exam answers.

293
00:25:49,998 --> 00:25:57,220
The small file problem happens when a streaming ingest writes an object every few seconds, leaving millions of tiny files behind.

294
00:25:58,702 --> 00:26:02,800
Every engine then spends more time listing and opening files than reading data.

295
00:26:03,214 --> 00:26:06,404
The symptom is a query that is slow for no obvious reason.

296
00:26:07,278 --> 00:26:10,020
Overall partitioning is the mirror image failure.

297
00:26:11,278 --> 00:26:18,020
Partitioning by the hour when you always query by month creates thousands of nearly empty folders.

298
00:26:19,086 --> 00:26:22,596
The metadata lookup becomes the bottleneck instead of the data.

299
00:26:23,054 --> 00:26:27,140
Both problems look like bad performance and have opposite fixes.

300
00:26:28,878 --> 00:26:36,324
The exam guide names, replayability, and the stateful vs. stateless distinction explicitly. So these are not background terms.

301
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:38,880
Here are two anchors you can rely on.

302
00:26:39,342 --> 00:26:46,948
Amazon Data Firehose uses at least one semantics, meaning a delivery retry after a timeout can produce a duplicate record.

303
00:26:47,822 --> 00:26:54,180
That applies to every destination except Amazon S3, Apache Icybug tables, and Snowflake.

304
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:59,236
Knowing that exception is the kind of detail that separates two otherwise identical options.

305
00:26:59,694 --> 00:27:02,756
Second, replay depends entirely on retention.

306
00:27:03,758 --> 00:27:12,292
Kinesis DataStreams keeps records for a configurable window, so you can go back and reprocess from a point in time after fixing a bug.

307
00:27:13,038 --> 00:27:21,892
A queue that deletes a message once it is consumed cannot do that, no matter how reliable it is. Durability and replayability are different properties.

308
00:27:23,566 --> 00:27:28,356
A media company stores viewing events as compressed JSON files in Amazon.

309
00:27:30,030 --> 00:27:32,452
S3 Partition by ingestion date.

310
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:35,332
Analysts query them with Amazon Athena.

311
00:27:36,142 --> 00:27:42,244
A new customer facing feature needs to show a single viewer their own watch history when they open the app.

312
00:27:43,118 --> 00:27:46,052
The feature must respond in under 100 milliseconds.

313
00:27:46,240 --> 00:27:49,220
It will be called several thousand times per second at peak.

314
00:27:49,646 --> 00:27:52,836
The team's first attempt used Athena and the page timed out.

315
00:27:53,966 --> 00:27:56,932
Why did the first attempt fail and what should replace it?

316
00:27:57,358 --> 00:27:59,812
a Athena was not partitioned correctly.

317
00:28:00,622 --> 00:28:04,484
Repartition by viewer ID and keep using Athena.

318
00:28:05,134 --> 00:28:11,012
b. Convert the JSON files to Apache Parquet, which will bring the query under the latency target.

319
00:28:11,726 --> 00:28:16,000
C. Serve the feature from Amazon DynamoDB keyed on viewer.

320
00:28:17,006 --> 00:28:19,524
ID populated from the same events.

321
00:28:20,302 --> 00:28:20,560
D

322
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:27,108
load the events into Amazon Redshift and query it from the application.

323
00:28:27,726 --> 00:28:29,280
Pause and decide.

324
00:28:35,918 --> 00:28:37,344
The answer is C.

325
00:28:38,064 --> 00:28:43,584
Two words in that situation decided it a single viewer and 100 milliseconds.

326
00:28:43,918 --> 00:28:50,404
Single item lookup by key at low latency is the Dynamo DB access pattern almost by definition.

327
00:28:51,182 --> 00:28:59,332
Option B is the interesting distractor because converting to Parquet is genuinely good advice and is the correct answer to a different question.

328
00:28:59,744 --> 00:29:01,604
It reduces bytes scanned.

329
00:29:01,824 --> 00:29:05,476
It does nothing about the seconds Athena takes to start a query at all.

330
00:29:05,904 --> 00:29:08,708
Notice this pairs with scenario 1 deliberately.

331
00:29:09,390 --> 00:29:11,904
There the sophisticated tool was too much.

332
00:29:12,206 --> 00:29:15,428
Here the analytical tool is the wrong shape entirely.

333
00:29:17,166 --> 00:29:21,732
The last thought before we finish, and questions usually hand you the answer in the wording.

334
00:29:22,734 --> 00:29:26,756
As soon as the data arrives or immediately after upload is an event trigger.

335
00:29:27,534 --> 00:29:31,396
Every night or hourly or on the first of the month is a schedule.

336
00:29:31,982 --> 00:29:33,540
One trap is worth naming.

337
00:29:34,510 --> 00:29:38,064
A scheduled job that runs and finds nothing still costs money.

338
00:29:38,254 --> 00:29:41,124
It still counts as a run and still shows up in the bill.

339
00:29:41,744 --> 00:29:49,220
So, a question complaining about wasted runs or about paying for jobs that did nothing is pointing you towards event driven.

340
00:29:49,998 --> 00:29:51,748
It may never use that phrase.

341
00:29:52,366 --> 00:29:54,224
The reverse trap also exists.

342
00:29:54,544 --> 00:29:59,684
Event-driven ingestion of thousands of tiny files can trigger thousands of tiny jobs.

343
00:30:01,166 --> 00:30:04,548
That is how you create the small file problem from two slides ago.

344
00:30:06,062 --> 00:30:13,060
Run these four questions in order on every scenario and the field narrows before you have compared any technical merits at all.

345
00:30:13,486 --> 00:30:17,092
The fourth one does the most work and gets the least attention.

346
00:30:17,486 --> 00:30:22,948
Sentences like the team has no capacity to operate a new pipeline are not seen setting.

347
00:30:23,694 --> 00:30:29,732
Sentences like the company wants minimal changes to the existing architecture are not background.

348
00:30:29,984 --> 00:30:37,316
They are eliminations placed there deliberately, and they usually kill the two options that would otherwise be hardest to separate.

349
00:30:39,470 --> 00:30:43,424
Practice spotting them and you buy yourself time on every question.

350
00:30:43,984 --> 00:30:51,984
From here we go into domain 1, the largest at 34%, starting with batch ingestion and the AWS Glue

351
00:30:52,144 --> 00:30:53,060
ETL engine.

352
00:30:53,344 --> 00:30:58,436
Glue was the single most mentioned service in the entire question bank and it earns its module.

353
00:30:59,758 --> 00:31:05,156
AWS Glue appeared 485 times in the question bank analyzed for this course.

354
00:31:05,870 --> 00:31:09,956
That is more than Amazon S3 and more than Amazon Redshift.

355
00:31:10,702 --> 00:31:16,132
No other service comes close, and nothing else in the syllabus rewards study time as reliably.

356
00:31:16,864 --> 00:31:28,944
This is the longest module in the course and it earns the time. We will cover how batch data gets into AWS, then the Glue ETL engine in real detail, then connectivity, then cost,

357
00:31:29,184 --> 00:31:30,544
then failure modes.

358
00:31:30,784 --> 00:31:32,804
Six vote scenarios along the way.

359
00:31:33,424 --> 00:31:40,516
If you only have time to master one service before sitting this exam, it is this one and it is not close.

360
00:31:42,254 --> 00:31:49,184
Before anything else, unpangle the name because questions exploit the ambiguity deliberately.

361
00:31:49,504 --> 00:31:54,944
When an option says use AWS glue, it could mean at least six different products.

362
00:31:55,104 --> 00:32:00,004
They have different pricing models, different use cases, and different correct answers.

363
00:32:00,814 --> 00:32:08,704
Candidates who think of Glue as one thing find these questions genuinely confusing because two options both say glue

364
00:32:08,864 --> 00:32:10,436
and only one of them is right.

365
00:32:10,784 --> 00:32:15,424
There is no way to separate them without knowing which component each option means.

366
00:32:16,110 --> 00:32:20,612
Learn the six separately and a whole category of confusion simply disappears.

367
00:32:21,390 --> 00:32:25,732
This is also why a study plan that says revise GU is too coarse to be useful.

368
00:32:27,342 --> 00:32:33,220
Six components and the exam tests them as separate topics with separate answers.

369
00:32:33,742 --> 00:32:37,988
Blue ETL jobs run Spark and Bill per D.

370
00:32:38,830 --> 00:32:40,224
PU hour.

371
00:32:40,910 --> 00:32:46,020
The data catalog stores metadata and bills per object stored and per million requests.

372
00:32:46,798 --> 00:32:52,708
Crawlers build per DPUR with a 10 minute minimum, which is a different minimum to the one jobs use.

373
00:32:53,582 --> 00:32:58,544
Data Blue bills per interactive session and per node are on top of everything else.

374
00:32:59,054 --> 00:33:01,444
Data quality is metered separately again.

375
00:33:02,190 --> 00:33:04,900
This module covers ETL jobs in depth.

376
00:33:05,744 --> 00:33:10,916
The catalog and crawlers get module 12 and data quality gets module 15.

377
00:33:11,184 --> 00:33:14,500
So if those feel thin here, that is deliberate.

378
00:33:15,664 --> 00:33:22,980
Batch ingestion questions almost always name the source, and the source narrows the tool immediately, often to a single option.

379
00:33:23,264 --> 00:33:28,704
A relational database being migrated or kept in sync points at AWSDMS.

380
00:33:31,214 --> 00:33:35,268
A sales application like Salesforce points at Amazon App Flow.

381
00:33:36,206 --> 00:33:42,276
Petabytes with poor connectivity points at Snowball. Files sitting on a network share point at Datasync.

382
00:33:42,768 --> 00:33:45,408
Module 5 covers every one of these properly.

383
00:33:45,568 --> 00:33:47,780
So do not worry about the detail yet.

384
00:33:48,128 --> 00:33:51,168
What matters here is the shape they all share.

385
00:33:51,758 --> 00:33:58,116
Almost every one of them lands raw data in Amazon S3 before anything transforms it.

386
00:33:58,798 --> 00:34:02,564
S3 is cheap, effectively unlimited and durable.

387
00:34:02,926 --> 00:34:06,692
It also accepts any format without being told what it is.

388
00:34:08,334 --> 00:34:14,916
The row worth dwelling on is the first pair because both options appear together constantly and both are defensible.

389
00:34:15,408 --> 00:34:16,996
DMS and a glue.

390
00:34:17,518 --> 00:34:21,220
JDB, C job can both read from a database.

391
00:34:22,158 --> 00:34:26,372
DMS is built for continuous replication and can do change data capture.

392
00:34:27,278 --> 00:34:31,876
It performs an initial full load, then streams ongoing changes indefinitely.

393
00:34:32,494 --> 00:34:38,692
A glue job connecting over JDBC reads whatever is there at the moment it runs and then stops.

394
00:34:39,088 --> 00:34:45,636
So the signal words are ongoing, continuously or keep in sync, which all point at DMS.

395
00:34:46,368 --> 00:34:54,148
If the question says nightly extract or daily snapshot, a glue job is simpler, cheaper, and the intended answer.

396
00:34:55,406 --> 00:35:02,404
Every batch pipeline makes this choice, and the exam usually asks about it through cost rather than asking directly.

397
00:35:02,798 --> 00:35:08,484
A full load is simple and always correct, and it rereads yesterday's data every single night, forever.

398
00:35:09,454 --> 00:35:15,940
Incremental processing reads only what is new, which requires something that remembers where the last run got to.

399
00:35:16,334 --> 00:35:20,100
On AWS, that something is usually a glue job bookmark.

400
00:35:20,494 --> 00:35:21,860
Here is the exam pattern.

401
00:35:22,702 --> 00:35:28,448
Watch for a job getting steadily slower and more expensive while the daily volume of new data has not changed.

402
00:35:28,686 --> 00:35:31,248
That is a full load that should be incremental.

403
00:35:31,726 --> 00:35:35,140
Bookmarks are almost always the answer, and we come to them shortly.

404
00:35:36,398 --> 00:35:41,444
Here is the mental model for the rest of this module, and it is worth stating plainly.

405
00:35:41,728 --> 00:35:49,732
A glue ETL job is Spark, not something Spark-like, not a wrapper around Spark, not a simplified version.

406
00:35:49,968 --> 00:35:55,428
It is Apache Spark running on infrastructure you never see and never log into.

407
00:35:56,288 --> 00:36:00,932
Everything that makes Spark fast or slow applies unchanged.

408
00:36:01,328 --> 00:36:13,092
Partitioning, shuffles, memory pressure on executors, data skew across keys, and everything AWS adds sits on top of that engine rather than replacing it.

409
00:36:13,728 --> 00:36:15,908
This matters for a practical reason.

410
00:36:16,462 --> 00:36:19,012
Blue troubleshooting questions are usually

411
00:36:19,790 --> 00:36:26,768
Spark questions wearing a blue costume, and the correct answer is a spark fix expressed in blue vocabulary.

412
00:36:28,110 --> 00:36:32,164
You will not be asked to recite version numbers, but two facts here do come up.

413
00:36:32,686 --> 00:36:37,668
First, auto-scaling and the flex execution class both require Blue 3.0 or later.

414
00:36:38,382 --> 00:36:41,636
An option proposing Flex on an older job is quietly wrong.

415
00:36:41,888 --> 00:36:44,644
Second, the Python shell job type is not Spark.

416
00:36:45,582 --> 00:36:52,100
It is a single process running plain Python available at 116th of a DPU or one full DPU.

417
00:36:52,608 --> 00:36:59,428
For a small script that calls an API or runs one SQL statement, a Spark cluster is absurd overkill.

418
00:36:59,888 --> 00:37:01,540
The Python shell is the answer.

419
00:37:03,246 --> 00:37:05,284
Anchor one number and the rest follow.

420
00:37:05,870 --> 00:37:10,980
A DPU is 4 virtual CPUs and 16GB of memory.

421
00:37:11,822 --> 00:37:16,068
G1X is 1 DPU, G2X is 2, and so on doubling.

422
00:37:16,686 --> 00:37:19,684
The R types are the ones people miss because they are newer.

423
00:37:20,494 --> 00:37:23,588
They give you double the memory for the same virtual CPU count.

424
00:37:24,462 --> 00:37:27,364
That is what you want for memory intensive.

425
00:37:27,760 --> 00:37:32,676
Spark work like caching, shuffling, and skewed aggregations.

426
00:37:33,646 --> 00:37:39,172
So a question describing out of memory errors during a large join has two plausible answers.

427
00:37:39,758 --> 00:37:43,716
Move up the G scale or move sideways to an R type.

428
00:37:45,200 --> 00:37:51,396
This slide is worth memorizing because blue cost questions are common and mechanical once you know the rules.

429
00:37:51,680 --> 00:37:59,360
The default allocation is 10 dP, as whether the job needs them or not, and the minimum for a spark job is 2.

430
00:37:59,918 --> 00:38:07,076
A large share of real-world blue overspend is nothing more sophisticated than that default being left untouched for a year.

431
00:38:07,630 --> 00:38:12,676
Notice there are two different minimum billing durations, and questions do exploit this.

432
00:38:13,134 --> 00:38:16,228
Jobs and interactive sessions bill from 1 minute.

433
00:38:17,294 --> 00:38:20,452
Crawlers and development endpoints bill from 10.

434
00:38:20,910 --> 00:38:27,716
So a question about a lightweight crawler scheduled every 5 minutes is pointing straight at that 10 minute floor.

435
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:32,676
You pay for 10 minutes each time it runs for 30 seconds.

436
00:38:34,414 --> 00:38:38,820
This distinction is blue specific and it appears in questions regularly.

437
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:44,420
A spark data frame requires every record to fit one schema decided up front.

438
00:38:45,422 --> 00:38:48,240
Real ingested data often does not oblige.

439
00:38:49,166 --> 00:38:55,396
A field that arrives as a number in most records and a string in a few will break a data frame read outright.

440
00:38:56,398 --> 00:39:04,004
A dynamic frame tolerates that, keeping both possibilities alive until you resolve them deliberately using resolve choice.

441
00:39:04,878 --> 00:39:11,172
The cost is that dynamic frames are slower and exist only inside glue, so the code is not portable.

442
00:39:11,470 --> 00:39:12,228
Spark.

443
00:39:13,134 --> 00:39:18,180
The common real-world pattern is to read as a dynamic frame and resolve the mess early.

444
00:39:18,480 --> 00:39:22,852
Then convert to a data frame with 2DF and do the heavy work there.

445
00:39:23,662 --> 00:39:26,308
That is the shape the exam treats as correct.

446
00:39:27,918 --> 00:39:30,788
Two of these come up considerably more than the others.

447
00:39:31,246 --> 00:39:39,524
Relationalize is the standard answer when a question describes deeply nested JSON that needs to land in redshift or another relational target.

448
00:39:40,462 --> 00:39:48,000
It unnests the structure into a set of flag tables joined by generated keys, which is exactly the shape a warehouse wants.

449
00:39:48,270 --> 00:39:51,972
Doing that by hand is painful, which is why the exam likes it.

450
00:39:52,494 --> 00:39:58,692
Find matches is the answer when a question describes deduplicating records that are similar but not identical.

451
00:39:59,534 --> 00:40:04,484
Customer records with typos, abbreviated street names, transpose digits.

452
00:40:05,326 --> 00:40:12,560
It is a machine learning transform you train on labeled examples, and no amount of exact matching or SQL will do that job.

453
00:40:14,318 --> 00:40:19,140
Bookmarks answer a whole family of questions, and there is one trap worth knowing about.

454
00:40:20,046 --> 00:40:23,908
They depend on the job script calling job.init at the start

455
00:40:24,622 --> 00:40:26,404
and job.comic at the end.

456
00:40:27,280 --> 00:40:36,772
A job missing those calls will run happily with bookmarks enabled, reprocess everything every single time, and report no error at all.

457
00:40:37,040 --> 00:40:38,340
Nothing warns you.

458
00:40:39,214 --> 00:40:47,040
For JDBC sources, the bookmark needs a column that only ever increases, such as an auto-incrementing key or a last

459
00:40:47,200 --> 00:40:48,580
modified timestamp.

460
00:40:49,518 --> 00:40:53,220
If the source table has neither, bookmarks cannot work on it.

461
00:40:53,870 --> 00:40:59,684
The correct answer becomes a different incremental approach, usually change data capture to DMS.

462
00:41:01,358 --> 00:41:04,036
Three of these produce exam questions on their own.

463
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:06,480
The timeout defaults to 48 hours.

464
00:41:06,702 --> 00:41:13,280
A job that hangs rather than fails keeps billing DPU hours for two days before anyone notices.

465
00:41:14,606 --> 00:41:18,000
Setting a realistic timeout is a standard cost answer.

466
00:41:18,574 --> 00:41:20,720
Max concurrent runs defaults to 1.

467
00:41:20,814 --> 00:41:22,084
And this is the subtle one.

468
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:28,772
If a job is triggered per file and two files land together, the second trigger is rejected outright rather than queued.

469
00:41:30,766 --> 00:41:35,364
A question describing files being silently skipped under load is pointing at that default.

470
00:41:36,174 --> 00:41:42,372
And retrees are only safe when rerunning produces the same result, which is what idem potent means.

471
00:41:43,086 --> 00:41:44,676
We come back to that shortly.

472
00:41:46,414 --> 00:41:55,076
Flex is one of the highest value things to know for cost questions. It is a single setting with a large discount and an obvious trigger phrase.

473
00:41:57,774 --> 00:42:03,520
Jobs run on spare capacity, so they may start late and may have resources reclaimed mid run.

474
00:42:04,558 --> 00:42:11,652
That makes it wrong for anything with a deadline and right for nightly batch backfills and test workloads.

475
00:42:12,366 --> 00:42:17,124
Watch for an overnight job with no SLA where the question asks how to cut cost.

476
00:42:18,094 --> 00:42:20,708
Flex is usually one of the two things being tested.

477
00:42:21,358 --> 00:42:23,524
The other is right sizing the DPS.

478
00:42:25,198 --> 00:42:31,396
Auto scaling changes the question you have to answer from how many workers do I need to what is the most I would ever want.

479
00:42:33,102 --> 00:42:40,480
View then adds and removes executors as the job progresses, and you are billed only for what actually ran rather than for the ceiling you set.

480
00:42:41,390 --> 00:42:50,052
That is genuinely useful when one night's file is 10 times larger than another's, which describes a lot of real pipelines.

481
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:57,124
The last point matters for troubleshooting questions and it is counterintuitive.

482
00:42:57,934 --> 00:43:05,360
If a job is slow because all of the work landed on one executor, adding more executors changes nothing at all.

483
00:43:05,600 --> 00:43:08,772
They will sit idle while the same single task grinds on.

484
00:43:08,960 --> 00:43:11,748
That is SKU and it needs a SKU fix.

485
00:43:13,166 --> 00:43:21,956
A company runs a nightly AWS Glue job that transforms the previous day's order files in Amazon S3 and writes parquet output.

486
00:43:22,480 --> 00:43:26,640
The job was created 18 months ago and has never been reconfigured.

487
00:43:26,862 --> 00:43:28,644
It uses the default settings.

488
00:43:29,518 --> 00:43:34,052
The job now takes 4 hours and the monthly glue bill has grown steadily.

489
00:43:34,734 --> 00:43:36,836
Finance has asked for a reduction.

490
00:43:37,200 --> 00:43:43,396
The job must finish before the business day begins at 8 in the morning and it currently starts at midnight.

491
00:43:43,918 --> 00:43:46,884
Data volume grows by about 2% a month.

492
00:43:47,840 --> 00:43:52,324
Which combination will reduce cost with the least risk to the deadline?

493
00:43:52,974 --> 00:43:53,280
A.

494
00:43:53,806 --> 00:43:58,180
Switch the job to the flex execution class and enable job bookmarks.

495
00:43:59,150 --> 00:44:04,900
d. Increase the worker type from G1X to G8X so the job finishes sooner.

496
00:44:05,520 --> 00:44:05,760
C

497
00:44:07,118 --> 00:44:12,164
Reduce the DPU allocation from the default of 10 to 2 and accept a longer runtime.

498
00:44:13,006 --> 00:44:13,280
D.

499
00:44:14,190 --> 00:44:19,120
Migrate the job to Amazon EMR on EC2 with spot instances.

500
00:44:20,398 --> 00:44:21,508
Pause and decide.

501
00:44:28,174 --> 00:44:29,440
The answer is A.

502
00:44:30,894 --> 00:44:34,080
Two details in that scenario, were doing the work.

503
00:44:34,574 --> 00:44:38,116
Never been reconfigured and uses the default settings.

504
00:44:39,214 --> 00:44:42,756
That is the exam telling you the 10 dPU default is still in place.

505
00:44:43,630 --> 00:44:50,660
And the 8 hour window against a 4 hour runtime is enormous slack, which is exactly the condition under which Flex is safe.

506
00:44:51,086 --> 00:44:53,200
Option C is the interesting trap.

507
00:44:53,422 --> 00:44:54,692
Reducing DP

508
00:44:55,662 --> 00:45:01,412
us genuinely is a valid blue cost lever. And this course told you so two slides ago.

509
00:45:02,318 --> 00:45:09,476
Reducing to the absolute floor with no measurement on a job with a hard deadline is not the same as right sizing it.

510
00:45:10,894 --> 00:45:13,956
This choice has consequences well beyond convenience.

511
00:45:14,318 --> 00:45:24,644
Reading from the catalog means Blue already knows which partitions exist. That is the precondition for pushdown predicates doing anything useful because you cannot prune a list you have not got.

512
00:45:25,710 --> 00:45:35,840
It is also the only path where AWS Lake Formation permissions apply, Since lake formation grants permissions on catalog tables rather than on S3

513
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:36,708
prefixes.

514
00:45:38,158 --> 00:45:42,160
Reading straight from a path bypasses both mechanisms.

515
00:45:43,118 --> 00:45:48,804
So when a question requires column or row level access control on a blue job, watch the read path.

516
00:45:49,358 --> 00:45:55,748
An option, reading directly from an S3 location, is quietly incompatible with that requirement.

517
00:45:57,360 --> 00:46:00,772
The second point is worth real marks and gets overlooked.

518
00:46:01,262 --> 00:46:08,836
The traditional pattern is that a job writes new data, then a crawler runs afterwards to discover the new partitions and register them.

519
00:46:09,200 --> 00:46:15,364
That is two moving parts, plus a delay between them during which the new data is not queryable.

520
00:46:16,240 --> 00:46:21,796
With Enable Update Catalog, the job registers its own partitions as it writes them.

521
00:46:22,480 --> 00:46:29,028
Watch for a question asking how to make new data queryable immediately with the least operational overhead.

522
00:46:29,360 --> 00:46:33,444
If one option removes the crawler entirely, that is usually the answer.

523
00:46:35,726 --> 00:46:38,180
The last point restates module 1.

524
00:46:39,086 --> 00:46:45,380
Partition on what you filter by, which is rarely how the source happens to organize itself.

525
00:46:47,086 --> 00:46:54,756
This is one of the newly examinable areas, so material written before December 2025 will not prepare you for it.

526
00:46:56,046 --> 00:47:03,076
The problem these formats solve is that a plain pile of Parquet files in S3 has no concept of a transaction.

527
00:47:03,694 --> 00:47:05,668
You cannot update a single row.

528
00:47:06,478 --> 00:47:12,324
You cannot delete one customer's records for a privacy request without rewriting whole files.

529
00:47:12,942 --> 00:47:15,716
And a reader can see a half-written state.

530
00:47:16,846 --> 00:47:21,604
Iceberg adds a metadata layer that makes those operations atomic.

531
00:47:22,446 --> 00:47:30,820
For a blue job, the practical effect is that you can write merge statements against a data lake, which changes what is architecturally possible.

532
00:47:31,310 --> 00:47:33,444
Module 9 covers this in full.

533
00:47:34,702 --> 00:47:40,432
Now, a topic that produces more exam questions than its apparent size suggests.

534
00:47:41,038 --> 00:47:48,740
Dew runs in an AWS managed environment, not inside your account's network, until you attach a connection to the job.

535
00:47:49,518 --> 00:47:55,748
The moment a job must reach something private, networking gets involved and the surface for failure grows sharply.

536
00:47:56,558 --> 00:48:00,388
That means a database in a VPC or a bucket behind a private route.

537
00:48:01,006 --> 00:48:04,260
Because Blue is serverless, these failures look mysterious.

538
00:48:04,992 --> 00:48:09,796
There is no instance to log into and no obvious place to run a connectivity test from.

539
00:48:10,192 --> 00:48:12,932
The good news is that the causes are few and repeat.

540
00:48:13,454 --> 00:48:18,596
Learn the three or four standard ones and this becomes a source of quick marks rather than a source of dread.

541
00:48:20,302 --> 00:48:23,044
The third point catches almost everyone the first time.

542
00:48:23,232 --> 00:48:30,692
When Glue puts network interfaces into your subnet, the workers talk to each other and that traffic is governed by the security group.

543
00:48:31,214 --> 00:48:37,572
Because the source and destination are both inside that group, the group must allow inbound traffic from itself.

544
00:48:37,872 --> 00:48:43,108
Without that self-referencing rule, the job hangs and then fails with something unhelpful.

545
00:48:43,982 --> 00:48:45,860
And the fourth point is the classic.

546
00:48:46,352 --> 00:48:49,892
Glue reads its own script and its libraries from S3.

547
00:48:50,830 --> 00:48:56,420
Put a job in a private subnet with no root to S3 and it dies before your code starts.

548
00:48:57,934 --> 00:49:03,204
The one that confuses people is the network connection type because it does not connect to anything.

549
00:49:03,566 --> 00:49:11,204
Its only purpose is to tell Blue which VPC, subnet, and security group to place the job's network interfaces in.

550
00:49:12,942 --> 00:49:18,084
You use it when the job must reach something private that is not a JDBC database.

551
00:49:19,022 --> 00:49:25,412
The common case is an S3 bucket reached through a VPC endpoint or an API inside the VPC.

552
00:49:26,126 --> 00:49:33,348
So a question describing a blue job that must run inside a VPC with no database involved is describing a network connection.

553
00:49:33,902 --> 00:49:37,924
The options will usually offer JDBC as the plausible wrong answer.

554
00:49:39,182 --> 00:49:43,812
This distinction is worth real marks and it is easy to get wrong under pressure.

555
00:49:45,358 --> 00:49:49,988
A gateway endpoint is not a device, it is an entry added to a root table.

556
00:49:50,414 --> 00:49:57,188
That is why a question about a gateway endpoint that looks configured but does not work is almost always a root table question.

557
00:49:58,062 --> 00:50:05,828
An interface endpoint is a genuine network interface with a private address inside your subnet using AWS private link.

558
00:50:06,766 --> 00:50:13,552
Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB. They are free and they cannot be reached from outside the VPC.

559
00:50:13,902 --> 00:50:16,260
Everything else needs an interface endpoint.

560
00:50:17,902 --> 00:50:23,140
A data engineer has configured an AWS Glue job to read from an Amazon S3 bucket.

561
00:50:23,312 --> 00:50:32,612
A glue connection has been created with the correct VPC, private subnet, and security group. The IAM role attached to the job grants full access to the bucket.

562
00:50:33,486 --> 00:50:40,112
The job fails on every run with an error referring to the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint.

563
00:50:40,430 --> 00:50:44,836
The bucket policy is permissive and the IAM role has been verified as correct.

564
00:50:45,806 --> 00:50:47,396
What is the most likely cause?

565
00:50:48,046 --> 00:50:48,272
A.

566
00:50:49,294 --> 00:50:54,020
The Blue Security Group does not allow inbound traffic from the S3 gateway endpoint.

567
00:50:54,478 --> 00:50:54,752
b.

568
00:50:54,990 --> 00:50:58,628
The bucket policy does not explicitly name the Blue service principle.

569
00:50:59,470 --> 00:51:03,232
C. The Blue connection is missing a fully qualified domain

570
00:51:03,232 --> 00:51:04,432
name. D.

571
00:51:05,614 --> 00:51:09,924
The Subnet's root table has no root to the S3 gateway endpoint.

572
00:51:10,574 --> 00:51:11,844
Pause and decide.

573
00:51:18,606 --> 00:51:22,468
The answer is D, and the reasoning is the previous slide applied directly.

574
00:51:22,926 --> 00:51:25,156
A gateway endpoint is a root table entry.

575
00:51:25,550 --> 00:51:32,644
If the root is not there, traffic to S3 leaves by the default path, which in a private subnet goes nowhere.

576
00:51:33,102 --> 00:51:39,428
Option A is designed to catch anyone who half remembers that security groups matter for blue.

577
00:51:39,822 --> 00:51:40,272
They do.

578
00:51:40,590 --> 00:51:46,628
For the self-referencing rule between workers, but a gateway endpoint is not a thing that sends you traffic.

579
00:51:47,982 --> 00:51:50,244
And notice how B was disposed of.

580
00:51:51,150 --> 00:51:55,460
The question explicitly verified IAM and the bucket policy.

581
00:51:56,046 --> 00:52:02,084
When a question hands you a verified fact, it is eliminating an option, not decorating the scenario.

582
00:52:03,790 --> 00:52:08,132
These two get confused constantly, and the separator is simple once you have it.

583
00:52:08,718 --> 00:52:14,596
Amazon Macy looks at data already sitting in S3 and tells you what sensitive information is in there.

584
00:52:15,438 --> 00:52:21,380
It is a discovery and monitoring tool that runs on a schedule across buckets and it reports findings.

585
00:52:22,318 --> 00:52:28,100
Blue Sensitive data detection runs inside the job as records pass through.

586
00:52:28,526 --> 00:52:33,476
It can act on what it finds, masking or redacting before the data is ever written down.

587
00:52:34,062 --> 00:52:39,140
So a question about discovering what personal data already exists across an estate is messy.

588
00:52:39,792 --> 00:52:46,404
A question about preventing personal data from reaching the curated layer in the first place is blue.

589
00:52:47,632 --> 00:52:55,940
Serverless means there is no machine to log into, so the exam expects you to know which observability switch answers which complaint.

590
00:52:56,432 --> 00:52:59,204
The last point is the one that generates questions.

591
00:52:59,632 --> 00:53:02,756
By default, logs are written when the job completes.

592
00:53:03,534 --> 00:53:07,556
For a long job, that means waiting hours to discover why it failed.

593
00:53:07,982 --> 00:53:12,420
For a job that hangs and is eventually killed, it can mean no useful logs at all.

594
00:53:13,038 --> 00:53:15,460
Continuous logging streams them to CloudWatch.

595
00:53:16,206 --> 00:53:17,604
Logs as the job runs.

596
00:53:18,446 --> 00:53:20,804
The Spark UI answers a different question.

597
00:53:21,552 --> 00:53:28,708
It shows which stage consumed the time, which is what you need when a job succeeded but took far longer than expected.

598
00:53:30,352 --> 00:53:40,004
The exam guide names Databrew explicitly, so it is examinable and it also names Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio alongside it as of version 1.1.

599
00:53:40,672 --> 00:53:42,756
Here is the practical relationship.

600
00:53:43,232 --> 00:53:50,148
Data Brew is the older standalone tool for analysts with over 250 pre-built transformations.

601
00:53:50,894 --> 00:53:56,068
Blue Studio now has its own no-code data preparation experience.

602
00:53:56,494 --> 00:54:02,596
It does much the same thing, runs on Blue Spark, and can import existing Data Brew recipes.

603
00:54:03,918 --> 00:54:11,552
When a question describes a business analyst who cannot write code needing to clean data, either answer can be correct.

604
00:54:12,046 --> 00:54:14,084
Read for which one the question names.

605
00:54:15,342 --> 00:54:18,980
Python shell jobs are underused and they show up in cost questions.

606
00:54:19,232 --> 00:54:24,420
Consider a job that just calls an API, moves a file, or issues one SQL statement.

607
00:54:24,974 --> 00:54:27,364
Spinning up a Spark cluster for that is waste.

608
00:54:27,758 --> 00:54:32,868
A Python shell job at 116th of a DPU is the right shape and a fraction of the price.

609
00:54:37,552 --> 00:54:42,192
It closed to new customers at the end of April 2026.

610
00:54:43,566 --> 00:54:49,860
Existing customers carry on and AWS points new ray workloads at Amazon EKS.

611
00:54:50,606 --> 00:54:54,980
It remains in older question banks, so recognize it and know its status.

612
00:54:55,886 --> 00:55:01,188
Existing customers carry on and AWS points new Ray workloads at Amazon EKS.

613
00:55:01,646 --> 00:55:05,764
It remains in older question banks, so recognize it and know its status.

614
00:55:07,054 --> 00:55:12,900
Glue has its own small orchestration system and knowing where its boundary sits is the exam point.

615
00:55:13,720 --> 00:55:20,292
Glue workflows can chain crawlers, jobs, and triggers together with conditional logic on success or failure.

616
00:55:21,230 --> 00:55:29,316
For a pipeline made entirely of blue components, that is genuinely enough and it needs no additional service, which makes it the lowest overhead answer.

617
00:55:30,126 --> 00:55:38,272
The moment the pipeline includes something that is not blue, a lambda function, a redshift statement, a human approval step,

618
00:55:38,542 --> 00:55:40,452
blue workflows run out of road.

619
00:55:41,102 --> 00:55:45,712
The answer then becomes AWS Step Functions or Amazon MWAA.

620
00:55:47,150 --> 00:55:49,732
Module 6 covers that comparison properly.

621
00:55:51,438 --> 00:55:56,452
This connects the reliability vocabulary from module 1 to something concrete.

622
00:55:56,974 --> 00:56:04,420
Max retrees looks like free resilience, and it is not because a job that failed halfway may already have written half of its output.

623
00:56:05,006 --> 00:56:07,876
Retree it and that half gets written a second time.

624
00:56:08,878 --> 00:56:11,236
Three mechanisms make reruns safe.

625
00:56:12,142 --> 00:56:13,860
Overwriting a whole partition.

626
00:56:14,222 --> 00:56:18,788
So the second run replaces the first run's work rather than adding to it.

627
00:56:19,502 --> 00:56:20,324
Bookmarks.

628
00:56:20,910 --> 00:56:24,580
So the second run does not reread the same source records.

629
00:56:25,326 --> 00:56:32,932
Or an iceberg merge keyed on an identifier, so a repeated record updates in place rather than duplicating.

630
00:56:33,902 --> 00:56:41,732
Without one of those, turning on retrees converts a visible failure into an invisible data quality problem, which is much worse.

631
00:56:43,374 --> 00:56:50,212
Troubleshooting questions are a reliable part of both domain 1 and domain 3, and they follow a recognizable pattern.

632
00:56:50,672 --> 00:56:59,524
A job is slow or failing, 4 plausible fixes are offered, and the right one depends entirely on which underlying problem the symptoms describe.

633
00:57:00,334 --> 00:57:09,348
There are essentially four causes not enough parallelism, too much data being read, data skew, across keys, and the small file problem.

634
00:57:09,632 --> 00:57:12,260
Adding workers only helps the first of those.

635
00:57:12,974 --> 00:57:20,004
Getting into the habit of asking which of the four this is before you look at the options will earn you marks across two domains.

636
00:57:20,398 --> 00:57:26,148
It also stops you picking the option that sounds most thorough, which is how these questions catch people.

637
00:57:27,854 --> 00:57:29,444
Learn this table as a lookup.

638
00:57:30,126 --> 00:57:35,140
The two rows to separate carefully are the first two because both describe a slow job.

639
00:57:35,918 --> 00:57:42,432
If every executor is working and the job is simply slow, you are short of capacity and more workers help.

640
00:57:42,926 --> 00:57:49,412
If one task runs for an hour while the others finished in minutes, that is skewed, and more workers will sit idle watching.

641
00:57:49,806 --> 00:57:52,912
The fix for skew is to change how the data is distributed.

642
00:57:53,232 --> 00:58:00,036
Repartition on a better key, add a random salt to spread a hotkey, or give each worker more memory with an R type.

643
00:58:01,294 --> 00:58:08,356
This is the glue expression of the partitioning lesson from module 1, and the exam tests it as a cost question rather than a performance one.

644
00:58:08,752 --> 00:58:17,312
Without a pushdown predicate, a job asked for one day of data, reads the entire table into executor memory and then discards almost all of it.

645
00:58:17,870 --> 00:58:22,308
You paid for every byte, every second of every worker, and the result is identical.

646
00:58:24,430 --> 00:58:32,032
With a pushdown predicate, Blue evaluates the filter against partition metadata first and simply never opens the folders it does not need.

647
00:58:32,192 --> 00:58:35,460
The catalog partition predicate variant goes one better.

648
00:58:35,886 --> 00:58:39,460
It evaluates the filter inside the data catalog itself.

649
00:58:39,758 --> 00:58:42,628
So Blue never even lists the partitions.

650
00:58:44,174 --> 00:58:49,668
Small files are the most common real cause of an inexplicably slow lake, and the exam knows it.

651
00:58:51,054 --> 00:58:53,028
There are three levels of fix and questions.

652
00:58:53,358 --> 00:58:54,512
Test different ones.

653
00:58:54,752 --> 00:59:00,868
On write, reduce the number of output partitions so blue emits fewer larger files.

654
00:59:01,710 --> 00:59:05,872
On read, blue can group small files together so one task handles

655
00:59:05,872 --> 00:59:13,316
many. Structurally, run a compaction job or let Apache IZBOG do it for you, which module 9 covers.

656
00:59:15,214 --> 00:59:17,552
The last bullet is the mirror image.

657
00:59:18,158 --> 00:59:24,772
One enormous Gizip file cannot be split, so exactly one executor reads it, however many workers you have.

658
00:59:26,414 --> 00:59:36,228
A view job reads a partitioned Parquet dataset in Amazon S3 containing 5 years of transaction history partitioned by year and month.

659
00:59:36,622 --> 00:59:40,324
The job produces a report covering the most recent month only.

660
00:59:40,782 --> 00:59:45,284
The job runs on 20 G2X workers and takes 90 minutes.

661
00:59:46,126 --> 00:59:51,972
Monitoring shows all executors are busy throughout and the job reads roughly 4TB on every run.

662
00:59:52,366 --> 00:59:56,804
The team has been asked to reduce both runtime and cost without changing the report.

663
00:59:57,358 --> 00:59:59,396
What should the data engineer do first?

664
01:00:01,454 --> 01:00:06,180
a Increase the number of workers from 20 to 60 to shorten the runtime.

665
01:00:06,702 --> 01:00:06,912
b.

666
01:00:07,118 --> 01:00:11,428
Add a pushdown predicate so the job reads only the current month's partitions.

667
01:00:11,950 --> 01:00:16,752
C. Switch the workers from G2X to R2X to provide more memory per worker.

668
01:00:17,102 --> 01:00:17,312
D.

669
01:00:17,552 --> 01:00:20,548
Enable autoscaling with a maximum of 20 workers.

670
01:00:22,912 --> 01:00:24,964
Pause and decide.

671
01:00:31,374 --> 01:00:32,612
The answer is B.

672
01:00:33,070 --> 01:00:38,820
The decisive number is 4TB, read to produce 1 month of a 5-year dataset.

673
01:00:40,526 --> 01:00:44,260
That is roughly 60 times more data than the report needs.

674
01:00:44,910 --> 01:00:49,924
Option A is the one worth dwelling on because it is not wrong about the mechanism.

675
01:00:50,862 --> 01:00:54,756
All executors busy really does mean more workers would finish faster.

676
01:00:55,054 --> 01:01:00,324
It fails on the second half of the requirement, cost, and this is the standard shape of a glue trap.

677
01:01:02,414 --> 01:01:07,460
An option that satisfies one stated goal while making the other worse is a distractor.

678
01:01:07,680 --> 01:01:09,572
However, correct its reasoning.

679
01:01:10,894 --> 01:01:16,480
An AWS Glue job loads a nightly extract into a curated Amazon S3 dataset.

680
01:01:16,814 --> 01:01:21,476
Max retrease is set to 3 because the source database occasionally times out.

681
01:01:21,680 --> 01:01:24,836
The job appends its output to the current days partition.

682
01:01:25,390 --> 01:01:29,956
Analysts report that on some days the revenue totals are roughly double what finance expects.

683
01:01:30,734 --> 01:01:34,640
The affected days correlate with nights when the source database was slow.

684
01:01:34,800 --> 01:01:37,572
The job reports success on every one of those nights.

685
01:01:38,254 --> 01:01:40,452
What is happening and what is the correct fix?

686
01:01:41,198 --> 01:01:41,440
A.

687
01:01:42,318 --> 01:01:44,836
The source database is sending duplicate rows.

688
01:01:45,040 --> 01:01:47,076
Add a distinct to the extract query.

689
01:01:47,600 --> 01:01:51,524
b Retriees rerun a job that had already written output.

690
01:01:52,014 --> 01:01:55,588
Write with overwrite on the partition instead of appending.

691
01:01:56,174 --> 01:01:56,400
C.

692
01:01:57,040 --> 01:02:01,988
Max retrieve should be set to zero so that failures are surfaced to the operations team.

693
01:02:02,798 --> 01:02:03,040
D.

694
01:02:04,238 --> 01:02:09,220
The job needs more DPS so that it completes before the database times out.

695
01:02:09,840 --> 01:02:11,076
Pause and decide.

696
01:02:17,454 --> 01:02:18,468
The answer is B.

697
01:02:18,958 --> 01:02:20,868
Two details. Give it away together.

698
01:02:21,200 --> 01:02:24,612
The job reports success, so this is not a failure question at all.

699
01:02:24,880 --> 01:02:29,280
And the bad days correlate with slow nights, which is when retrees fire.

700
01:02:29,840 --> 01:02:37,188
A job that failed after lighting half its output, then retried from the beginning and appended again, produces exactly this.

701
01:02:37,710 --> 01:02:41,252
Option C is the tempting one because it does stop the duplication.

702
01:02:41,998 --> 01:02:49,348
It also throws away the resilience that made the pipeline survive a flaky database, converting a silent data problem into a nightly operational one.

703
01:02:50,094 --> 01:02:52,000
The exam rewards fixing the write.

704
01:02:52,302 --> 01:02:56,612
So retrees become safe rather than removing retrees so writes stay unsafe.

705
01:02:58,286 --> 01:03:01,988
This pair appears constantly and there is a clean rule for it.

706
01:03:02,254 --> 01:03:05,412
Lambda has a hard 15-minute execution limit.

707
01:03:05,870 --> 01:03:12,836
If the work described could ever exceed that, lambda is eliminated regardless of how convenient it otherwise looks.

708
01:03:13,358 --> 01:03:15,440
That is the first thing to check.

709
01:03:15,950 --> 01:03:21,092
Below that ceiling, lambda is cheaper and starts in milliseconds rather than minutes.

710
01:03:21,614 --> 01:03:26,948
For reacting to a single uploaded file with a light transform, it usually wins outright.

711
01:03:27,470 --> 01:03:30,980
The second decider is whether the work is genuinely distributed.

712
01:03:31,822 --> 01:03:36,160
Joining two large datasets needs Spark and therefore Blue.

713
01:03:36,654 --> 01:03:40,880
Renaming 3 columns in an 8 MB file does not.

714
01:03:42,254 --> 01:03:48,356
Module 5 goes deeper into engine choice, including where Amazon EMR fits alongside both.

715
01:03:49,902 --> 01:03:56,868
Blue and EMR overlap heavily and questions rely on you finding the separator rather than weighing them generally.

716
01:03:57,582 --> 01:04:00,164
Two things reliably point at EMR.

717
01:04:00,718 --> 01:04:03,524
First, an engine Glue does not offer.

718
01:04:04,000 --> 01:04:10,320
If the question mentions high weighted HBase, Presto or Trino by name, Blue is out immediately.

719
01:04:10,862 --> 01:04:19,760
Second, an existing Hadoop estate being migrated where rewriting everything for Blue is the expensive path and lift and shift is the cheap one.

720
01:04:20,206 --> 01:04:21,636
Two things point at Blue

721
01:04:22,638 --> 01:04:29,124
Least operational overhead phasing and a straightforward scheduled ETL job with no exotic requirements.

722
01:04:29,934 --> 01:04:39,428
And remember that EMR serverless exists, which blunts the operational overhead argument considerably and is often the option people forget to consider.

723
01:04:40,878 --> 01:04:46,212
This is a genuinely modern option and it appears in newer questions, so it is worth knowing well.

724
01:04:46,926 --> 01:04:55,364
Zero ETL integrations replicate from a supported source into Amazon Redshift continuously with AWS operating the entire mechanism.

725
01:04:56,302 --> 01:05:00,548
There is no job to write, nothing to schedule, and no failure to handle.

726
01:05:01,454 --> 01:05:04,740
The limits are what make it answerable in an exam.

727
01:05:05,838 --> 01:05:11,204
It works only between supported pairs and it moves data rather than transforming it.

728
01:05:12,142 --> 01:05:21,316
So watch for a question wanting operational data available for analytics with the least possible overhead where no transformation is mentioned.

729
01:05:22,286 --> 01:05:25,424
Zero ETL beats any pipeline you could build.

730
01:05:25,806 --> 01:05:29,664
When the data must be reshaped on the way, it does not apply at all.

731
01:05:31,086 --> 01:05:35,044
Knowing when a service is wrong is worth as much as knowing when it is right.

732
01:05:35,470 --> 01:05:41,540
The exam builds its distractors out of services that are nearly appropriate rather than obviously absurd.

733
01:05:42,064 --> 01:05:45,636
The fourth point deserves naming as a pattern in its own right.

734
01:05:46,510 --> 01:05:54,756
If data already sits in Redshift and needs transforming into another Redshift table, the answer is almost always SQL executed inside Redshift.

735
01:05:55,104 --> 01:05:59,876
It is not a blue job that reads the data out, transforms it, and writes it back.

736
01:06:00,782 --> 01:06:08,580
Moving data out of a warehouse to transform it and then moving it back in is a shape the exam consistently marks wrong.

737
01:06:09,326 --> 01:06:13,540
It adds cost, latency, and a pipeline for no benefit.

738
01:06:14,798 --> 01:06:23,264
A financial services company receives one CSV file from each of 40 partner banks every night between 2 and 8 MB

739
01:06:23,664 --> 01:06:23,972
each.

740
01:06:24,398 --> 01:06:33,124
Each file needs the same light transformation, 3 columns we named, 1 date reformatted, and the result written as parquet to Amazon S3.

741
01:06:33,838 --> 01:06:36,836
Files arrive at unpredictable times through the night.

742
01:06:37,358 --> 01:06:43,396
The current design runs a single blue spark job at 6 in the morning that processes whatever has arrived.

743
01:06:43,824 --> 01:06:48,932
The team wants results available as soon as each file lands and wants to reduce cost.

744
01:06:51,054 --> 01:06:53,476
Which design best meets both requirements?

745
01:06:54,544 --> 01:06:54,864
A

746
01:06:56,174 --> 01:07:00,196
Keep the GRU job but schedule it hourly instead of once at 6.

747
01:07:00,624 --> 01:07:08,356
b Trigger an AWS Lambda function from S3 event notifications to transform each file on arrival.

748
01:07:08,944 --> 01:07:09,264
C.

749
01:07:09,664 --> 01:07:14,980
Trigger a GNU Spar job from S3 event notifications for each file that arrives.

750
01:07:15,984 --> 01:07:16,224
D.

751
01:07:17,582 --> 01:07:23,364
Replace the design with an Amazon EMR cluster running continuously through the night.

752
01:07:24,366 --> 01:07:25,764
Pause and decide.

753
01:07:32,462 --> 01:07:39,236
The answer is B and option C is the distractor the whole module has been building towards.

754
01:07:39,630 --> 01:07:41,572
C gets the architecture right.

755
01:07:41,904 --> 01:07:46,340
Event driven on arrival is exactly the correct pattern for unpredictable timing.

756
01:07:46,944 --> 01:07:48,676
It then picks the wrong engine.

757
01:07:49,614 --> 01:07:59,076
40 separate spark jobs, each paying a 1 minute minimum billing floor and a startup delay measured in minutes to transform 8 megabytes.

758
01:07:59,584 --> 01:08:07,664
This is why the module spent time on the billing minimums. Notice this pairs with scenario 1 deliberately.

759
01:08:07,904 --> 01:08:10,852
There Glue was right and only misconfigured.

760
01:08:11,598 --> 01:08:15,204
Here glue is the wrong tool no matter how you configure it.

761
01:08:16,590 --> 01:08:22,784
A healthcare analytics company runs a Glue job that reads patient records from Amazon S3.

762
01:08:23,104 --> 01:08:28,356
It joins them with reference data and writes a curated dataset used by three analytics teams.

763
01:08:29,230 --> 01:08:36,036
Compliance requires that one team can see all columns and the other two must not see the patient identifier column at all.

764
01:08:36,384 --> 01:08:42,340
The job currently reads directly from an S3 path using Create Dynamic Frame from Options.

765
01:08:43,278 --> 01:08:49,444
The security team has asked for column level access control without maintaining three separate copies of the data.

766
01:08:49,904 --> 01:08:52,068
What should the data engineer change?

767
01:08:52,558 --> 01:08:59,652
a Write three output datasets to different S3 prefixes and control access with bucket policies.

768
01:09:00,462 --> 01:09:00,704
b

769
01:09:01,966 --> 01:09:09,904
Register the dataset in the view data catalog, read from the catalog, and grant column level permissions in AWS Lake

770
01:09:10,144 --> 01:09:10,468
Formation.

771
01:09:12,142 --> 01:09:20,228
C Encrypt the patient identifier column with AWS KMS and grant key access only to the permitted team.

772
01:09:21,344 --> 01:09:21,504
D

773
01:09:22,926 --> 01:09:27,620
Enable blue sensitive data detection on the job to redact the patient identifier.

774
01:09:28,464 --> 01:09:29,796
Pause and decide.

775
01:09:36,302 --> 01:09:41,060
The answer is B, and it depends on the slide about catalog reads earlier in this module.

776
01:09:41,280 --> 01:09:44,644
Lake Formation grants permissions on data catalog tables.

777
01:09:45,422 --> 01:09:51,812
A job reading straight from an S3 path never concerns the catalog, so no lake formation grant can apply to it.

778
01:09:52,526 --> 01:09:57,444
Changing the read path is therefore not incidental to the answer, it is the answer.

779
01:09:57,920 --> 01:10:05,920
Option D is the well-designed destructor because sensitive data detection is genuinely the right tool for masking personal data in a GRU

780
01:10:06,080 --> 01:10:06,308
job.

781
01:10:07,214 --> 01:10:09,572
It fails on one word in the requirement.

782
01:10:10,478 --> 01:10:15,204
One team must still see the column, and redaction is not selective by consumer.

783
01:10:16,878 --> 01:10:22,980
Five questions to run on any GRU scenario, and between them they resolve most of what this exam asks about the service.

784
01:10:23,440 --> 01:10:25,892
The last two are where the marks hide.

785
01:10:26,350 --> 01:10:33,156
Both describe a job that is working rather than failing, and both have a specific name fix rather than a general one.

786
01:10:34,080 --> 01:10:40,004
A job whose cost grows while volume is static is reprocessing, and bookmarks stop that.

787
01:10:40,240 --> 01:10:44,196
A job reading far more than it reports on needs a push-down predicate.

788
01:10:44,640 --> 01:10:49,920
Neither is solved by adding capacity, which is exactly what the tempting distractor will offer.

789
01:10:50,080 --> 01:10:52,420
Because adding capacity sounds decisive.

790
01:10:53,230 --> 01:11:00,836
Next module is streaming ingestion, the other half of domain one, where the deciding constraint becomes latency instead of cost.

791
01:11:02,350 --> 01:11:04,240
Module 2 was about cost.

792
01:11:04,750 --> 01:11:06,724
This module is about latency.

793
01:11:07,182 --> 01:11:15,588
Streaming accounted for roughly 7% of term hits in the bank analyzed for this course, and that number understates its importance.

794
01:11:16,526 --> 01:11:27,108
Streaming questions are disproportionately the ones people get wrong because four services here overlap heavily and the exam builds its distractors precisely out of that overlap.

795
01:11:28,718 --> 01:11:31,588
Two of them can land data in Amazon S3.

796
01:11:32,494 --> 01:11:34,404
Two of them can be read by Lambda.

797
01:11:35,374 --> 01:11:37,476
Three of them can carry the same records.

798
01:11:37,680 --> 01:11:46,180
By the end of this module, you will be able to separate all four in a sentence each, and you will know the specific numbers that decide the throughput questions.

799
01:11:46,990 --> 01:11:55,748
Four work scenarios along the way and one availability warning that invalidates a correct answer in most existing question banks.

800
01:11:57,262 --> 01:12:02,720
Carry this over from module 1 because it is the most common error in this whole area.

801
01:12:03,040 --> 01:12:07,268
Data arriving continuously does not make something a streaming problem.

802
01:12:07,680 --> 01:12:11,492
Sensors emitting every second into a report read once.

803
01:12:11,680 --> 01:12:15,300
A morning is a batch workload with a talkative source.

804
01:12:16,142 --> 01:12:22,500
What makes it streaming is that a consumer is waiting and the value of the answer decays with time.

805
01:12:23,566 --> 01:12:25,444
A fraud check blocking a payment.

806
01:12:26,510 --> 01:12:28,740
An alert that a pipeline pressure is rising.

807
01:12:29,788 --> 01:12:31,168
A dashboard traders are watching.

808
01:12:32,398 --> 01:12:39,588
If nobody suffers from a 12 hour delay, the correct answer is almost certainly not a stream, however continuous the source is.

809
01:12:39,982 --> 01:12:41,636
This matters commercially too.

810
01:12:42,862 --> 01:12:48,480
Streaming infrastructure costs money every hour of every day, whether anyone is reading or not.

811
01:12:48,880 --> 01:12:54,628
That is why the exam treats an unnecessary stream as a wrong answer rather than a harmless one.

812
01:12:55,918 --> 01:13:02,756
Every streaming architecture on this exam is some version of this line, and the third box is the one that matters most.

813
01:13:04,750 --> 01:13:08,580
A consumer reads at its own pace and remembers where it got to.

814
01:13:10,542 --> 01:13:17,600
Multiple consumers can read the same records independently, each sitting at a different position in the log.

815
01:13:18,542 --> 01:13:23,716
One might be seconds behind, another an hour behind, and neither affects the other.

816
01:13:24,240 --> 01:13:28,804
That is possible only because the stream retains the data after it has been read,

817
01:13:29,454 --> 01:13:31,652
rather than deleting it on consumption.

818
01:13:32,558 --> 01:13:39,140
Hold on to that property because the next slide turns it into an exam decision that comes up constantly.

819
01:13:39,680 --> 01:13:46,244
It is also the property that makes replay possible, with the exam guide names as a skill in its own right.

820
01:13:47,822 --> 01:13:54,308
This pair appears more often than people expect, and there are three signals that force a stream rather than a queue.

821
01:13:55,054 --> 01:13:59,172
First, more than one system needs the same records for different purposes.

822
01:13:59,440 --> 01:14:03,716
A queue cannot do that because the first consumer to take a message removes it.

823
01:14:04,782 --> 01:14:05,956
Second, replay.

824
01:14:06,894 --> 01:14:13,156
If a bug is discovered on Thursday and Monday's data must be reprocessed, a queue deleted it days ago.

825
01:14:15,662 --> 01:14:17,092
Third, strict ordering.

826
01:14:18,062 --> 01:14:25,216
Kinesis guarantees order within a shard, and standard Amazon SQS makes no ordering guarantee at all.

827
01:14:25,742 --> 01:14:34,532
If none of those three signals appear in the question, SQS is simpler, cheaper, and almost certainly the intended answer.

828
01:14:35,758 --> 01:14:39,684
Reaching for a stream when a queue would do is a wrong answer.

829
01:14:40,096 --> 01:14:42,180
The exam sets deliberately.

830
01:14:43,936 --> 01:14:48,484
Learn these four sentences, and a large share of the module is already done.

831
01:14:48,896 --> 01:14:53,092
The distinction that earns marks is between transporting and delivering.

832
01:14:53,456 --> 01:15:00,996
Kinesis, DataStreams, and Amazon MSK hold data and let you write a consumer that does whatever you want with it.

833
01:15:01,776 --> 01:15:05,252
That means flexibility and it also means code you own.

834
01:15:05,536 --> 01:15:08,356
Amazon Data Firehose has no consumers at all.

835
01:15:08,576 --> 01:15:17,380
You point it at a destination and it puts the data there, managing everything in between. Managed service for Apache Flink does neither of those jobs.

836
01:15:17,966 --> 01:15:22,416
It sits in the middle and computes over the stream, which is a different verb again.

837
01:15:22,656 --> 01:15:31,076
When you see four options naming four services, ask which verb the question actually needs, and usually three of them drop away immediately.

838
01:15:32,462 --> 01:15:40,932
Kinesis DataStreams is the service most likely to appear in a throughput calculation, and those calculations all come down to shards.

839
01:15:41,518 --> 01:15:43,300
A shard is two things at once.

840
01:15:45,518 --> 01:15:49,124
It is a unit of capacity and it is a unit of ordering.

841
01:15:50,094 --> 01:15:56,196
Records carrying the same partition key always land on the same chart, and within that shard they stay in the order they arrived.

842
01:15:56,846 --> 01:15:58,692
Add shards and you add throughput.

843
01:16:00,398 --> 01:16:06,724
Add shards and you also spread your partition keys further apart, which is where a whole family of exam problems begins.

844
01:16:07,598 --> 01:16:11,524
Because spreading is only useful if the keys were well distributed to start with.

845
01:16:13,710 --> 01:16:18,180
The next slide has the numbers and they are worth memorizing rather than reasoning about.

846
01:16:19,214 --> 01:16:22,788
Because questions do the arithmetic in front of you and expect you to follow.

847
01:16:24,416 --> 01:16:33,216
The right limit is really two limits and the callout is the exam point, 1 MB per second or 1000 records per second,

848
01:16:33,390 --> 01:16:34,916
whichever you reach first.

849
01:16:35,758 --> 01:16:45,284
A producer sending tiny records, say 200 bytes of telemetry each, will hit the 1000 record ceiling at roughly 200 kilobytes per second.

850
01:16:45,934 --> 01:16:51,300
It looks like it is using a fifth of the shard's bandwidth and it is being throttled anyway.

851
01:16:52,014 --> 01:17:00,420
That is why the standard advice is to aggregate small records before sending, which is exactly what the kinesis producer library does for you.

852
01:17:01,102 --> 01:17:09,412
On the read side, note that the 2 MB per second is shared across every standard consumer on that shard, not granted to each.

853
01:17:10,158 --> 01:17:17,060
5 consumers get 400 kilobytes per second each, which is the problem enhanced. Fanode exists to solve.

854
01:17:18,734 --> 01:17:23,716
This mirrors the serverless argument from Module 1 applied to streams.

855
01:17:26,126 --> 01:17:35,940
The words that point at on demand are unpredictable, variable, spiky, bursty, or a new workload nobody has measured yet.

856
01:17:36,590 --> 01:17:40,292
The words that point at provisioned are steady, consistent.

857
01:17:41,038 --> 01:17:47,236
And a specific throughput figure the question actually hands you because a stated number means somebody measured it.

858
01:17:47,536 --> 01:17:50,052
One useful detail that appears in questions.

859
01:17:50,416 --> 01:17:54,052
You can switch an existing stream between the two modes without downtime.

860
01:17:54,976 --> 01:17:59,268
No application code changes, no republishing, no gap in the data.

861
01:17:59,616 --> 01:18:05,636
That makes on demand a reasonable answer even for an existing provisioned stream that keeps getting throttled.

862
01:18:06,478 --> 01:18:09,924
And it removes the objection that migrating would be disruptive.

863
01:18:11,214 --> 01:18:15,780
These numbers moved recently and a lot of preparation material has not caught up.

864
01:18:16,176 --> 01:18:21,892
On demand used to cap at 200 MB per second, then 1GB, then 2.

865
01:18:22,606 --> 01:18:28,132
Since November 2024, it reaches 10 GB per second right in three regions.

866
01:18:28,896 --> 01:18:35,652
The provision shard quota went from 500 to 20,000 per account in April 2025.

867
01:18:36,176 --> 01:18:46,052
The practical exam consequence is that on demand is now a credible answer for genuinely large workloads where older material would have forced you to provisioned.

868
01:18:46,736 --> 01:18:52,964
The last bullet still matters though, on demand scales, but it does not scale instantaneously.

869
01:18:54,574 --> 01:19:00,324
This is the single most reliable streaming troubleshooting question, and it has a memorable shape.

870
01:19:00,928 --> 01:19:03,108
The symptom is uneven throttling.

871
01:19:04,174 --> 01:19:11,396
Some shards sit at their limit while others are barely used, and the total throughput across the stream is nowhere near capacity.

872
01:19:12,334 --> 01:19:19,300
The instinct is to add shards, and the exam knows that is your instinct, which is why it is always offered.

873
01:19:22,638 --> 01:19:23,808
It does not work.

874
01:19:23,968 --> 01:19:32,288
The hotkey hashes to exactly one shard, however many shards exist. If 90% of your traffic carries device, ID1,

875
01:19:33,198 --> 01:19:37,732
splitting the stream in two gives you one shard at 90% and one at 10.

876
01:19:38,350 --> 01:19:41,572
The fix is always to change the key so traffic spreads.

877
01:19:42,126 --> 01:19:46,084
Higher cardinality, a composite key or a random suffix.

878
01:19:47,118 --> 01:19:49,968
If ordering per original key is not required,

879
01:19:51,246 --> 01:19:53,828
retention is the quietly important setting here.

880
01:19:54,542 --> 01:20:01,092
The default is 24 hours, which is fine until the day a consumer has a bug and you want to reprocess a week.

881
01:20:01,678 --> 01:20:06,368
Extended retention takes you to 7 days and long-term retention reaches a full

882
01:20:06,368 --> 01:20:16,996
year. So when a question describes needing to replay several days of data after a downstream failure, the answer usually involves extending retention.

883
01:20:17,568 --> 01:20:23,556
And an option that proposes rebuilding from the original source is doing far more work than necessary.

884
01:20:24,462 --> 01:20:28,836
Retention is also built, which is why nobody sets it to a year by default.

885
01:20:30,286 --> 01:20:33,828
The exam tests this through symptoms rather than by name.

886
01:20:34,510 --> 01:20:40,100
A question describes five applications reading the same stream and the newest one has made the other slow.

887
01:20:40,608 --> 01:20:44,292
That is five consumers dividing two MB per second between them.

888
01:20:45,198 --> 01:20:50,596
An enhanced fan out is the fix because each consumer then gets its own two megabytes.

889
01:20:51,118 --> 01:20:52,900
The second trigger is latency.

890
01:20:54,766 --> 01:20:58,980
Standard consumers poll, which puts them around 200 milliseconds behind.

891
01:20:59,726 --> 01:21:03,748
Enhanced fan out pushes, which brings that down to roughly 70.

892
01:21:04,718 --> 01:21:11,300
If a question states a latency budget tighter than about 100 milliseconds, standard polling will not meet it.

893
01:21:12,718 --> 01:21:16,804
The kinesis client library is the one worth understanding conceptually.

894
01:21:17,168 --> 01:21:26,884
Reading a stream reliably means tracking which records you have processed, handling a shard splitting underneath you, and coordinating when several workers share the load.

895
01:21:27,822 --> 01:21:34,756
The KCL does all of that, storing its checkpoints in a DynamoDB table it creates for you.

896
01:21:36,046 --> 01:21:45,188
That DynamoDB table is a nice exam detail because a question about mysterious Dynamo DB costs alongside a kinesis application is pointing at it.

897
01:21:45,408 --> 01:21:48,164
Note the end of support dates on the last bullet.

898
01:21:48,910 --> 01:21:57,156
Version 1 of the client library and version 0 of the producer library are both finished as of January 2026.

899
01:21:58,766 --> 01:22:02,308
This is a favorite exam scenario because the symptom is so misleading.

900
01:22:02,528 --> 01:22:08,768
A Lambda function reading a kinesi stream hits a record, it cannot process, perhaps malform JSON.

901
01:22:09,088 --> 01:22:10,848
Lambda retrieves the whole batch.

902
01:22:11,086 --> 01:22:11,972
It fails again.

903
01:22:12,848 --> 01:22:22,628
Because ordering must be preserved within a shard, nothing behind that batch can proceed. So the entire shard stalls while iterator age climbs steadily.

904
01:22:23,088 --> 01:22:27,844
It looks exactly like a capacity problem, and adding concurrency does nothing.

905
01:22:30,894 --> 01:22:32,132
Two settings solve it.

906
01:22:33,070 --> 01:22:38,532
Bisec batch on error splits the failing batch repeatedly to isolate the single bat record.

907
01:22:39,534 --> 01:22:44,964
And an on-failure destination sends that record to a queue or topic so processing continues.

908
01:22:45,966 --> 01:22:54,464
The parallelization factor is the other setting worth knowing because it is the one legitimate way to get more than

909
01:22:54,784 --> 01:22:57,700
one concurrent invocation per shard.

910
01:22:59,438 --> 01:23:04,996
A Ride hailing company streams vehicle location updates into Amazon Kinesis data.

911
01:23:06,606 --> 01:23:09,636
Data streams in provision mode with 12 shards.

912
01:23:10,478 --> 01:23:15,908
Records use the city name as the partition key and the company operates in 11 cities.

913
01:23:16,654 --> 01:23:19,812
The operations team reports intermittent ride throttling.

914
01:23:20,814 --> 01:23:27,172
CloudWatch shows two shards consistently at their light limit, while the other 10 average under 15% utilization.

915
01:23:28,224 --> 01:23:32,068
Total ingest across the stream is well under 12 MB per second.

916
01:23:32,384 --> 01:23:34,084
What should the data engineer do?

917
01:23:35,086 --> 01:23:38,084
a increase the shard count from 12 to 24.

918
01:23:38,830 --> 01:23:43,076
b switch the stream to on-demand capacity mode.

919
01:23:43,504 --> 01:23:43,824
C.

920
01:23:44,398 --> 01:23:48,740
Change the partition key to include the vehicle identifier as well as the city.

921
01:23:49,104 --> 01:23:49,424
D.

922
01:23:49,838 --> 01:23:53,060
Enable enhanced fan out for the consumer applications.

923
01:23:53,582 --> 01:23:54,788
Pause and decide.

924
01:24:01,518 --> 01:24:02,944
The answer is C.

925
01:24:03,264 --> 01:24:10,948
The decisive detail is that total ingest is well under 12 MB per second while two shards are saturated.

926
01:24:12,142 --> 01:24:17,156
Crunch shards can carry 12 MB per second, so there is no capacity shortage at all.

927
01:24:17,646 --> 01:24:20,260
The capacity is simply in the wrong place.

928
01:24:21,230 --> 01:24:26,980
11 partition keys cannot spread across 12 shards evenly and the two largest cities dominate.

929
01:24:27,470 --> 01:24:34,180
Option B is a thoughtful distractor here because on-demand does eventually split hot shards, so it is not useless.

930
01:24:35,054 --> 01:24:39,104
It converts a design problem into a bill, which is the kind of answer.

931
01:24:39,264 --> 01:24:42,308
The exam consistently ranks below fixing the cause.

932
01:24:43,758 --> 01:24:50,532
Firehose is the most misunderstood service in this module, and understanding it properly is worth several marks.

933
01:24:51,022 --> 01:24:52,644
It is not a stream you read from.

934
01:24:52,974 --> 01:24:59,396
There is no shard count, no consumer library, no checkpointing, and no position to track.

935
01:25:00,270 --> 01:25:08,516
You configure a source, optionally a transformation, and a destination, and AWS runs the delivery end-to-end.

936
01:25:09,326 --> 01:25:17,092
That gives it by far the lowest operational overhead of anything in this module, which is why it wins so many least overhead questions.

937
01:25:17,870 --> 01:25:20,516
It also means it can only do what it does.

938
01:25:21,422 --> 01:25:26,948
If a question needs custom consumer logic or two independent readers of the same records,

939
01:25:27,664 --> 01:25:33,380
or the ability to rewind and reprocess, Firehose is out no matter how convenient it sounds.

940
01:25:34,190 --> 01:25:38,116
Knowing what it cannot do is as valuable as knowing what it can.

941
01:25:39,886 --> 01:25:44,644
The third and fifth points together are the reason Firehose beats a pipeline in a lot of questions.

942
01:25:45,024 --> 01:25:50,144
Module 1 established that parquet plus partitioning is the standard fix for query cost.

943
01:25:50,624 --> 01:25:55,012
Firehose does both of those on the way in with configuration rather than code.

944
01:25:55,854 --> 01:26:02,564
So consider a question describing raw JSON landing in S3 with analysts complaining about Athena costs.

945
01:26:03,086 --> 01:26:05,604
The answer involves no glue job whatsoever.

946
01:26:05,998 --> 01:26:09,412
Just turn on format conversion and dynamic partitioning.

947
01:26:10,224 --> 01:26:20,932
When an option offers a scheduled job to convert files that Firehose could have converted on arrival, that option is doing unnecessary work.

948
01:26:22,574 --> 01:26:24,388
Two rows earn their place here.

949
01:26:25,390 --> 01:26:29,828
The redshift row explains a behavior that otherwise looks odd in questions.

950
01:26:30,478 --> 01:26:34,372
Firehose does not stream rows into Redshift the way you might imagine.

951
01:26:35,630 --> 01:26:40,004
It writes objects to an S3 bucket and then issues a copy command against them.

952
01:26:40,910 --> 01:26:45,892
That is why a Redshift destination still requires you to configure an S3 bucket.

953
01:26:46,382 --> 01:26:52,388
Why only GZIP compression works on that path and why the latency is higher than the buffer interval alone suggests.

954
01:26:53,326 --> 01:26:56,164
The ICBerg row is newly examinable and worth flagging.

955
01:26:57,056 --> 01:27:05,412
Firehose delivering into Apache ICBerg tables can express updates and deletes, not only inserts,

956
01:27:05,616 --> 01:27:10,052
which is a genuinely different capability from appending files into a prefix.

957
01:27:10,576 --> 01:27:14,852
Material written before late 2024 does not cover it at all.

958
01:27:16,496 --> 01:27:20,228
This is the correction from module 1, stated in full.

959
01:27:20,910 --> 01:27:26,308
Firehose is conventionally described as near real time because it buffers before delivering.

960
01:27:26,830 --> 01:27:29,764
And for years the flow really was 60 seconds.

961
01:27:30,574 --> 01:27:36,708
Since December 2023, you can set the buffer interval to zero and get delivery in about 5 seconds.

962
01:27:37,198 --> 01:27:40,420
That materially changes which questions Firehose can answer.

963
01:27:41,136 --> 01:27:48,740
A requirement for data available within 10 seconds no longer rules it out, and a lot of study material still assumes it does.

964
01:27:49,614 --> 01:27:53,060
Two caveats matter and both appear in questions.

965
01:27:53,998 --> 01:28:01,156
Zero buffering is not available when dynamic partitioning is enabled because firehose buffers separately per partition.

966
01:28:01,936 --> 01:28:10,096
And very small buffers produce a flood of tiny objects, which is precisely the small file problem module too warned

967
01:28:10,256 --> 01:28:12,196
about created deliberately.

968
01:28:13,710 --> 01:28:19,428
This is the most common pairing in streaming questions, and the trap is treating it as either or.

969
01:28:19,936 --> 01:28:22,596
A very standard architecture uses both.

970
01:28:24,494 --> 01:28:27,364
Kinesis data streams receives the records.

971
01:28:27,536 --> 01:28:31,216
A Flink application reads them for real-time alerting.

972
01:28:31,694 --> 01:28:37,376
A Firehose stream reads the same records and lands them in S3 for later analysis.

973
01:28:37,616 --> 01:28:40,644
Each consumer gets what it needs from one source.

974
01:28:41,166 --> 01:28:46,468
So watch for a question describing both a real-time reaction and an archival requirement.

975
01:28:46,896 --> 01:28:49,444
An option combining the two is often correct.

976
01:28:50,254 --> 01:28:53,668
An option forcing you to pick one is the distractor.

977
01:28:55,374 --> 01:28:57,700
The first bullet is the one that appears in questions.

978
01:28:58,016 --> 01:29:04,656
Firehose will keep retrying a failing destination for up to 24 hours and then it discards those records permanently.

979
01:29:04,816 --> 01:29:09,056
There is no dead letter queue by default and no way to recover them afterwards.

980
01:29:09,550 --> 01:29:13,316
That is why configuring S3 backup matters so much.

981
01:29:13,616 --> 01:29:19,908
And it is why a question about an open search cluster down over a long weekend has a data loss answer.

982
01:29:22,606 --> 01:29:25,412
That is true unless backup was enabled.

983
01:29:26,606 --> 01:29:30,820
The delivery semantics on the last two bullets are worth memorizing as a pair.

984
01:29:31,758 --> 01:29:37,892
At least once means a retry after a timeout can produce a duplicate, which your consumer must tolerate.

985
01:29:38,446 --> 01:29:43,748
The exception list Amazon S3, Apache IZBerg tables, and Snowflake

986
01:29:44,622 --> 01:29:46,308
is short enough to learn outright.

987
01:29:47,936 --> 01:29:56,452
This is the detail that separates two plausible options in a warehouse question. There are two ways to get a stream into Redshift and they behave differently.

988
01:29:56,896 --> 01:30:03,332
Firehose with Redshift as the destination writes objects to S3 and then issues a copy command.

989
01:30:04,142 --> 01:30:07,876
So there is always a staging hop and always S3 latency.

990
01:30:08,256 --> 01:30:18,084
Redshift streaming ingestion connects the warehouse directly to a Kinesis stream or an MSK topic. Materializing records into a view without touching S3 at all.

991
01:30:18,542 --> 01:30:20,548
That gets you seconds instead of minutes.

992
01:30:21,056 --> 01:30:28,356
So a question asking for near real time dashboards in Redshift with the fewest moving parts is pointing at streaming ingestion.

993
01:30:28,590 --> 01:30:30,916
And Firehose is the well designed distractor.

994
01:30:32,462 --> 01:30:37,956
A media company streams clickstream events into Amazon S3 for analysis with Amazon Athena.

995
01:30:38,606 --> 01:30:44,836
Events arrive as raw JSON, one object every 30 seconds, into a single prefix with no partitioning.

996
01:30:45,710 --> 01:30:47,716
The pipeline is a kinesis.

997
01:30:48,910 --> 01:30:54,436
Data stream stream led by an AWS Lambda function that writes objects to S3.

998
01:30:55,758 --> 01:31:02,084
Analysts report that Athena queries filtering on a single day scan the entire dataset and are becoming expensive.

999
01:31:02,368 --> 01:31:06,532
The team also maintains the Lambda function and wants less to operate.

1000
01:31:07,374 --> 01:31:11,012
Which change addresses both the query cost and the operational burden?

1001
01:31:11,694 --> 01:31:18,532
a add a scheduled AWS Glue job to convert the JSON to Parquet and rewrite it partition by date.

1002
01:31:19,054 --> 01:31:26,052
b. Replace the Lambda consumer with an Amazon data Firehose stream using format conversion and dynamic partitioning.

1003
01:31:26,688 --> 01:31:27,008
C.

1004
01:31:27,822 --> 01:31:32,708
Increase the Lambda function's memory so it can write larger, better organized objects.

1005
01:31:33,488 --> 01:31:33,808
D.

1006
01:31:34,288 --> 01:31:38,756
Move the data into Amazon Redshift and have analyst query it there instead.

1007
01:31:39,534 --> 01:31:40,644
Pause and decide.

1008
01:31:47,342 --> 01:31:57,408
The answer is B, and option A is the distractor worth studying. Converting to parquet and partitioning by date is exactly the correct advice, and this course gave you that advice twice.

1009
01:31:57,648 --> 01:32:01,956
It is wrong here because the question asks for two things, and A only delivers one.

1010
01:32:02,862 --> 01:32:08,132
It fixes query cost while adding a Blue job to a team that explicitly asks for less to operate.

1011
01:32:10,094 --> 01:32:11,812
This is a recurring exam shape.

1012
01:32:13,166 --> 01:32:17,700
The technically correct optimization offered in a form that ignores a stated constraint.

1013
01:32:18,606 --> 01:32:26,020
Firehose does the same transformation as configuration rather than as code and deletes a component while doing it.

1014
01:32:27,648 --> 01:32:33,728
Amazon MSK is managed apache Kafka, and the important thing is that it really is Kafka.

1015
01:32:34,128 --> 01:32:42,756
The same APAs, the same client libraries, the same ecosystem of connectors and monitoring tools. That is the entire point of the service.

1016
01:32:43,342 --> 01:32:49,508
Questions therefore rarely test Kafka internals because AWS is not examining you on open source software.

1017
01:32:50,446 --> 01:32:51,588
They test two things.

1018
01:32:52,270 --> 01:33:00,292
Whether MSK or Kinesis is the right choice for a described situation and which of the MSK deployment options fits the workload.

1019
01:33:01,102 --> 01:33:07,620
Both are answerable from a handful of signals in the question text, which is what the next few slides give you.

1020
01:33:08,334 --> 01:33:14,724
Treat any deep Kafka configuration detail in an option as noise rather than as something you are expected to know.

1021
01:33:16,334 --> 01:33:23,172
The mapping is close enough that if you understand kinesis shards, you already understand Kafka partitions.

1022
01:33:23,488 --> 01:33:29,796
Ordering is guaranteed within a partition and not across partitions, exactly as with shards.

1023
01:33:30,766 --> 01:33:37,220
A consumer group in Kafka does roughly what the Kinesis client library does for you, coordinating which consumer handles which partition.

1024
01:33:37,582 --> 01:33:44,644
The one genuine difference is the broker, which has no Kinesis equivalent, because in kinesis you never see or size a server at all.

1025
01:33:45,230 --> 01:33:48,932
That single difference is the entire operational story of MSK.

1026
01:33:49,262 --> 01:33:56,068
And it is why MSK questions so often turn on capacity and patching while Kinesis questions turn on shards and keys.

1027
01:33:56,366 --> 01:33:57,476
Note the call out too.

1028
01:33:58,286 --> 01:34:06,468
Kafka has moved from Zookeeper to Kraft for cluster metadata, and AWS includes those metadata nodes at no extra charge.

1029
01:34:08,174 --> 01:34:13,540
The call out matters because the naming genuinely is confusing and the exam can exploit it.

1030
01:34:14,478 --> 01:34:15,844
MSK Provision

1031
01:34:16,526 --> 01:34:19,556
and MSK Serverless are the two cluster types.

1032
01:34:20,110 --> 01:34:26,756
Express is a broker type you choose inside provision, sitting alongside standard rather than being a third cluster type.

1033
01:34:27,598 --> 01:34:36,804
Express brokers arrived in 2024 and removed the need to provision storage at all while delivering roughly 3 times the throughput per broker,

1034
01:34:37,614 --> 01:34:42,468
scaling around 20 times faster and recovering from broker failure far more quickly.

1035
01:34:43,438 --> 01:34:49,936
So an option describing high throughput Kafka with less broker and storage management is pointing at Express.

1036
01:34:51,214 --> 01:34:56,996
An option describing no capacity planning whatsoever with billing by usage is pointing at serverless.

1037
01:34:57,998 --> 01:35:04,132
And an option emphasizing fine-grained configuration control is pointing at standard brokers.

1038
01:35:05,518 --> 01:35:11,940
This is one of the cleanest tiebreakers in the whole exam and questions signal it plainly rather than hiding it.

1039
01:35:12,782 --> 01:35:20,736
If the scenario mentions an existing Kafka deployment, Kafka client libraries, Kafka Connect, or a team with Kafka expertise,

1040
01:35:20,896 --> 01:35:23,268
the answer is Amazon MSK.

1041
01:35:24,046 --> 01:35:29,616
Rewriting working Kafka producers and consumers to use Kinesis A B.

1042
01:35:31,438 --> 01:35:34,468
Is this exactly the kind of unnecessary migration

1043
01:35:36,206 --> 01:35:36,496
cost?

1044
01:35:39,214 --> 01:35:41,636
The exam consistently marks wrong.

1045
01:35:43,118 --> 01:35:49,604
If the scenario is a new pipeline with no Kafka history anywhere in it, Kinesis is simpler,

1046
01:35:50,286 --> 01:35:55,332
has fewer moving parts and integrates with more AWS services out of the box.

1047
01:35:56,206 --> 01:36:04,576
Absent IDA signal lean towards kinesis on operational overhead grounds because there are no brokers, no partitions to plan

1048
01:36:04,750 --> 01:36:06,180
and no cluster to size.

1049
01:36:07,918 --> 01:36:08,932
Three of these come up.

1050
01:36:09,376 --> 01:36:10,084
MSK.

1051
01:36:12,334 --> 01:36:22,436
Connect is the managed way to run Kafka Connect connectors. That is how Kafka moves data to and from databases and object stores without you writing consumers.

1052
01:36:23,246 --> 01:36:26,436
MSK Replicator handles cross-region replication.

1053
01:36:27,056 --> 01:36:31,236
It is the low overhead answer to Kafka disaster recovery questions.

1054
01:36:31,886 --> 01:36:35,936
The distractor there is running MirrorMaker yourself on EC2.

1055
01:36:38,030 --> 01:36:43,296
And the Blue Schema Registry is worth knowing because it also works with Kinesis and Flink.

1056
01:36:43,918 --> 01:36:52,804
It validates that producers are sending what consumers expect, which is a data quality control applied at the stream rather than after landing.

1057
01:36:54,094 --> 01:36:58,148
The callout is the architectural difference worth carrying into the exam.

1058
01:36:58,542 --> 01:37:02,692
An Amazon MSK cluster is deployed into your own VPC

1059
01:37:04,334 --> 01:37:07,536
with brokers holding private addresses in your subnets.

1060
01:37:07,696 --> 01:37:12,356
So network isolation is the default state rather than something you configure.

1061
01:37:13,262 --> 01:37:19,108
Kinesis Data Streams is a regional service reached through a public service endpoint.

1062
01:37:19,936 --> 01:37:20,256
So,

1063
01:37:21,294 --> 01:37:29,296
traffic originating in a private subnet needs an interface, VBC endpoint, using AWS private link,

1064
01:37:29,456 --> 01:37:33,252
exactly as module 2 described for AWS Blue.

1065
01:37:34,094 --> 01:37:40,644
That means a question about keeping streaming traffic off the public internet has genuinely different answers depending on which service is involved.

1066
01:37:40,816 --> 01:37:44,164
And the options will offer you the wrong services answered deliberately.

1067
01:37:44,736 --> 01:37:49,572
On authentication, MSK is the flexible one, supporting IAM.

1068
01:37:50,510 --> 01:37:54,628
SASN with S C R A M and Mutual T L S.

1069
01:37:55,984 --> 01:38:02,212
A retailer runs a self-managed Apache Kafka cluster on Amazon EC2 in one region.

1070
01:38:02,624 --> 01:38:12,836
Around 40 producer and consumer applications use the standard Kafka client libraries, and several use Kafka Connect connectors to move data into Amazon S3.

1071
01:38:13,742 --> 01:38:20,464
The platform team spends significant time patching brokers, rebalancing partitions, and recovering from broker failures.

1072
01:38:20,704 --> 01:38:27,524
They want to reduce that work, keep all existing applications running unchanged, and add cross-region disaster recovery.

1073
01:38:28,686 --> 01:38:30,052
What should the company do?

1074
01:38:31,790 --> 01:38:38,084
Migrate to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams in on-demand mode and rewrite the client applications.

1075
01:38:38,958 --> 01:38:48,772
b Migrate to Amazon MSK provisioned with Express Brokers, use MSK Connect and add MSK Replicator.

1076
01:38:49,264 --> 01:38:49,504
C.

1077
01:38:50,254 --> 01:38:56,580
Keep Kafka on EC2 but add an auto-scaling group and a second cluster with Mirror Maker.

1078
01:38:58,766 --> 01:39:04,100
Migrate to Amazon Data Firehose with Amazon S3 as the destination.

1079
01:39:05,070 --> 01:39:06,500
Pause and decide.

1080
01:39:13,102 --> 01:39:18,308
The answer is B, and it should have been quick, because OnePlus did all the work.

1081
01:39:18,926 --> 01:39:21,668
Keep all existing applications running unchanged.

1082
01:39:22,062 --> 01:39:29,476
In a scenario built entirely around Kafka clients and Kafka Connect is a requirement only Kafka compatible options can satisfy.

1083
01:39:30,414 --> 01:39:37,956
That eliminates A and D before you weigh a single technical merit because both require abandoning the Kafka APEs.

1084
01:39:38,894 --> 01:39:47,524
Then, C fails on the stated goal of reducing operational work since it adds a second cluster to patch rather than removing the first.

1085
01:39:48,366 --> 01:39:54,692
What remains is B and each of its three components maps to one of the three stated requirements.

1086
01:39:55,854 --> 01:39:59,652
Express Brokers, Reduce Broker, and Storage Management.

1087
01:40:01,518 --> 01:40:02,384
MSK

1088
01:40:03,278 --> 01:40:05,156
Connect takes over the connectors.

1089
01:40:06,478 --> 01:40:09,860
MSK Replicator provides the cross-region recovery.

1090
01:40:10,784 --> 01:40:16,420
This is the four questions from module 1 running to completion in about 15 seconds.

1091
01:40:17,742 --> 01:40:20,932
Everything so far moves records from one place to another.

1092
01:40:21,424 --> 01:40:26,116
Amazon Managed Service for Apache, Flink does something categorically different.

1093
01:40:26,638 --> 01:40:31,300
It computes over a stream continuously, holding state as it goes,

1094
01:40:31,982 --> 01:40:33,636
counting events per minute.

1095
01:40:36,142 --> 01:40:38,788
Joining two streams on a shared key.

1096
01:40:39,344 --> 01:40:44,292
Detecting that a value has exceeded a threshold three times within five minutes.

1097
01:40:44,544 --> 01:40:51,428
None of that is possible with delivery alone because all of it requires remembering what happened before the current record arrived.

1098
01:40:52,174 --> 01:40:58,660
Recognizing when a question needs state is the skill this section teaches and it is a genuinely transferable one.

1099
01:40:59,278 --> 01:41:09,380
Once you can spot it, a whole class of question resolves in seconds because only one of the four options can hold state and the other three are eliminated without further thought.

1100
01:41:10,670 --> 01:41:16,516
The callout is the practical test and it is worth applying deliberately on every streaming question.

1101
01:41:17,390 --> 01:41:22,084
Ask whether handling this record requires knowing anything about any other record.

1102
01:41:22,702 --> 01:41:25,156
Converting a timestamp format does not.

1103
01:41:25,614 --> 01:41:27,588
So a lambda function is enough.

1104
01:41:28,046 --> 01:41:30,340
And flink would be expensive overkill.

1105
01:41:31,184 --> 01:41:36,612
Counting how many events this user has produced in the last 5 minutes absolutely does.

1106
01:41:36,784 --> 01:41:43,684
So does detecting that a temperature has risen for three consecutive readings or joining an order stream to a payment stream.

1107
01:41:44,494 --> 01:41:52,100
Without a stream processor, you would need an external state store, plus logic for expiry, out of order arrival, and concurrent access.

1108
01:41:53,550 --> 01:42:03,588
That distinction between stateless and stateful is named explicitly in the exam guide, which tells you AWS considers it something you should identify on site.

1109
01:42:04,910 --> 01:42:11,300
Window types are testable vocabulary and the distinction is easy once you hear it in the right words.

1110
01:42:12,816 --> 01:42:17,284
Tumbling windows are like a report that runs on the R covering that R.

1111
01:42:17,966 --> 01:42:22,736
Each record belongs to exactly one window and windows never overlap.

1112
01:42:23,662 --> 01:42:32,132
Sliding windows do overlap, so a single record can be counted in several of them, which is what you want for a continuously updating rolling average.

1113
01:42:33,518 --> 01:42:35,748
Session windows have no fixed length at all.

1114
01:42:36,142 --> 01:42:39,856
They group activity and close after a defined period of silence.

1115
01:42:40,430 --> 01:42:46,500
That is exactly how you measure a visit to a website without deciding in advance how long a visit lasts.

1116
01:42:47,406 --> 01:42:56,324
The phrase to listen for is rolling, which almost always means sliding, and the phrase every n minutes, which almost always means tumbling.

1117
01:42:58,016 --> 01:43:04,740
The checkpoint and save point distinction is the kind of detail that separates two otherwise identical options.

1118
01:43:05,296 --> 01:43:13,860
Checkpoints happen automatically on a schedule and they exist so that an application which crashes resumes from where it was rather than from nothing.

1119
01:43:15,278 --> 01:43:16,516
You do not manage them.

1120
01:43:17,518 --> 01:43:23,716
Save points you take deliberately because you are about to deploy new code or change the parallelism of the application,

1121
01:43:23,936 --> 01:43:29,316
and you want the new version to pick up the old version's accumulated state rather than starting empty.

1122
01:43:30,254 --> 01:43:37,252
So a question about upgrading a flink application without losing its running aggregations is a save point question.

1123
01:43:38,062 --> 01:43:41,764
A question about surviving a node failure is a checkpoint question.

1124
01:43:42,176 --> 01:43:45,092
The last bullet is a real production concern.

1125
01:43:45,806 --> 01:43:53,636
State keyed on something unbounded, like a session identifier that never repeats, grows until the application falls over.

1126
01:43:55,150 --> 01:44:00,068
This is the availability warning for this module, and it is the sharpest one in the whole course.

1127
01:44:00,590 --> 01:44:03,940
There were two distinct products sharing the kinesis data.

1128
01:44:04,814 --> 01:44:08,004
Analytics name, which is the root of the confusion.

1129
01:44:08,494 --> 01:44:17,156
The Flink 1 was renamed to Manage Service for Apache Flink in 2023 and is alive, current, and heavily examinable.

1130
01:44:18,094 --> 01:44:25,220
The SQL 1, which let you write SQL directly against a stream, has been discontinued entirely.

1131
01:44:26,126 --> 01:44:35,172
AWS stopped allowing new applications from October 2025 and deleted remaining applications from the 27th of January 2026.

1132
01:44:35,616 --> 01:44:41,924
It appears 9 times in the bank analyzed for this course, sometimes as the marked correct answer.

1133
01:44:42,702 --> 01:44:48,164
Recognize the name, know that it is gone, and answer with Managed Service for Apache.

1134
01:44:49,870 --> 01:44:51,172
Flink instead.

1135
01:44:52,942 --> 01:45:00,576
Lambda reading from a stream is a genuinely good pattern and often the right answer, so do not assume that streaming implies FLINK.

1136
01:45:01,056 --> 01:45:05,668
Lambda receives a batch of records, does something to each of them, and exits.

1137
01:45:06,958 --> 01:45:09,252
It remembers nothing between invocations.

1138
01:45:09,870 --> 01:45:12,496
That covers a surprising amount of real work.

1139
01:45:13,358 --> 01:45:21,376
Filter out irrelevant events, reformat fees, root records to different destinations, call an external API for each one.

1140
01:45:21,536 --> 01:45:26,980
It is cheap, there is nothing running when no data flows and there is no application to operate.

1141
01:45:27,696 --> 01:45:34,736
The moment the requirement involves a time window, a running total or matching records arriving on two different streams.

1142
01:45:35,150 --> 01:45:39,258
Lambda would need an external state store and careful concurrency handling.

1143
01:45:40,430 --> 01:45:44,516
At that point, you are building FLINK by hand and the answer becomes FLINK.

1144
01:45:46,254 --> 01:45:51,652
An online payments company streams transaction events into Amazon Kinesis data streams.

1145
01:45:52,302 --> 01:46:02,656
A new requirement is to flag any account that makes more than 5 transactions in any rolling 1 minute period and to raise an alert within 2 seconds of the fifth transaction.

1146
01:46:03,296 --> 01:46:10,816
An existing AWS Lambda function already reads the stream and writes each transaction to Amazon S3.

1147
01:46:11,296 --> 01:46:18,532
A proposal is to extend that function with the new logic storing counts in an Amazon DynamoDB table.

1148
01:46:20,430 --> 01:46:22,532
What is the most appropriate design?

1149
01:46:25,102 --> 01:46:30,308
Extend the existing Lambda function to maintain per account counts in Dynamo DB.

1150
01:46:30,702 --> 01:46:36,832
B Add a managed service for Apache Flink application with a sliding window over one minute. C.

1151
01:46:37,072 --> 01:46:42,992
Deliver the stream to Amazon S3 with Data Firehose and run an Athena query every minute.

1152
01:46:43,312 --> 01:46:43,552
D

1153
01:46:44,526 --> 01:46:51,876
Load the transactions into Amazon Redshift and use a materialized view refreshed each minute.

1154
01:46:52,686 --> 01:46:53,956
Pause and decide.

1155
01:47:00,814 --> 01:47:02,112
The answer is B.

1156
01:47:02,992 --> 01:47:04,740
Two phrases decided it.

1157
01:47:05,230 --> 01:47:12,548
Rolling 1 minute period is the textbook description of a sliding window, and 2 seconds eliminates any design that buffers before computing.

1158
01:47:14,446 --> 01:47:19,332
Option A deserves attention because it is not absurd and teams really do build it.

1159
01:47:20,046 --> 01:47:22,916
The difficulty is everything the description leaves out.

1160
01:47:23,438 --> 01:47:32,356
Expiring counts as the window moves, handling records that arrive out of order, and coping when the same account is processed by two concurrent invocations.

1161
01:47:32,910 --> 01:47:35,300
Flink solves all three as a matter of course.

1162
01:47:36,174 --> 01:47:42,180
An option that re-implements a managed capability by hand is a recurring wrong answer on this exam.

1163
01:47:43,790 --> 01:47:48,768
This pairing shows up regularly, and the separator is retention and fanout.

1164
01:47:50,388 --> 01:47:58,852
DynamoDB Streams is built-in, needs no extra service, and keeps 24 hours of item level changes that a Lambda function can react to.

1165
01:47:59,662 --> 01:48:07,876
For the common case of updating something, when a record changes, that is entirely sufficient and it is the lower overhead answer.

1166
01:48:09,358 --> 01:48:18,468
When you need more than 24 hours of replay or several independent consumers leading the same change, freed for different purposes.

1167
01:48:19,312 --> 01:48:21,604
Routing changes into Kinesis data.

1168
01:48:22,032 --> 01:48:26,180
Streams gives you both at the cost of another service in the design.

1169
01:48:27,152 --> 01:48:32,324
The 24 hour limit is not extendable, which is usually what decides these questions.

1170
01:48:32,878 --> 01:48:35,556
The last bullet is newer and often overlooked.

1171
01:48:36,302 --> 01:48:46,884
For keeping a search index synchronized, the zero ETL integration between Dynamo DB and Amazon OpenSear removes the change pipeline from the design entirely.

1172
01:48:48,590 --> 01:48:53,316
Pipes is easy to overlook and it answers a specific shape of question.

1173
01:48:54,158 --> 01:48:59,876
A large number of real pipelines contain a lambda function whose entire job is to read a stream.

1174
01:49:00,622 --> 01:49:06,244
Throw away the records that do not matter, reshape the rest slightly and hand them to something else.

1175
01:49:07,182 --> 01:49:11,588
That is code to write, deploy, monitor, and pay for.

1176
01:49:12,782 --> 01:49:13,636
Event bridge.

1177
01:49:14,414 --> 01:49:22,340
Pipes does it as configuration with filtering applied before the target is invoked so you are not beyond events you discard.

1178
01:49:23,022 --> 01:49:25,092
The last bullet keeps it in its lane.

1179
01:49:26,032 --> 01:49:28,836
It moves and filters records one at a time.

1180
01:49:29,072 --> 01:49:32,672
So anything needing memory of other records is still flink.

1181
01:49:34,414 --> 01:49:39,652
The exam guide names managing fan in and fan out as a skill, so expect it.

1182
01:49:40,270 --> 01:49:41,956
The last bullet is the trap.

1183
01:49:42,638 --> 01:49:48,516
When several teams want the same data, the intuitive answer is to give each team its own stream.

1184
01:49:49,006 --> 01:49:53,028
And it is wrong on cost, on consistency, and on operational overhead.

1185
01:49:53,952 --> 01:50:01,252
One stream with multiple consumers is the design using enhanced fan out if they contend for read capacity.

1186
01:50:02,222 --> 01:50:05,156
The SNS variant is worth separating.

1187
01:50:06,062 --> 01:50:12,432
If what is being distributed is a notification that something happened rather than a continuous data feed.

1188
01:50:13,868 --> 01:50:18,588
SNS with multiple subscribers is simpler than any stream.

1189
01:50:21,070 --> 01:50:24,260
This table answers a question shape that appears reliably.

1190
01:50:24,624 --> 01:50:30,084
A downstream system was broken for several days, the bug is fixed, and the data must be reprocessed.

1191
01:50:30,304 --> 01:50:34,304
What you can do depends entirely on where the data was held while it passed through.

1192
01:50:34,544 --> 01:50:37,092
Kinesis and MSK keep it and let you rewind.

1193
01:50:37,838 --> 01:50:42,660
Firehose does not because it is a delivery mechanism rather than a store.

1194
01:50:42,784 --> 01:50:47,268
So recovering there means going back to whatever fed the firehose stream.

1195
01:50:48,078 --> 01:50:55,044
That is a good reason to have kinesis data streams in front of firehose rather than writing to firehose directly.

1196
01:50:56,784 --> 01:51:01,348
Apply the billing unit rule from module 1 and the cost answers fallout.

1197
01:51:01,664 --> 01:51:04,580
Anything charged continuously punishes idle time.

1198
01:51:05,264 --> 01:51:11,364
Provision shards and MSK brokers both bill around the clock whether records flow or not.

1199
01:51:12,142 --> 01:51:17,892
An MSK provision carries a three-broker floor for a multi-availability zone production cluster.

1200
01:51:18,414 --> 01:51:23,108
That makes it expensive for a low volume workload in a way that supplies people.

1201
01:51:23,904 --> 01:51:32,932
Firehose and on demand, kinesis charge for data, so they cost almost nothing when idle and become expensive at sustained high volume.

1202
01:51:34,784 --> 01:51:41,028
A question about a low throughput stream on an MSK cluster is pointing straight at that broker floor.

1203
01:51:42,734 --> 01:51:48,740
Iterator age deserves its own sentence because it is the metric CloudWatch questions are built around.

1204
01:51:49,184 --> 01:51:56,544
It reports how old the record currently being processed is. In other words, how far behind real time your consumer has fallen.

1205
01:51:57,166 --> 01:52:01,764
A steadily rising iterator age means consumption is lower than production.

1206
01:52:02,542 --> 01:52:08,996
And it will keep rising until either the consumer speeds up or the retention window is exceeded and data is lost.

1207
01:52:09,902 --> 01:52:22,116
A flat, low iterator age means you are keeping up. If a question hands you a graph description of a climbing iterator age, it is asking about consumer capacity, not producer capacity.

1208
01:52:23,566 --> 01:52:29,252
5 questions and between them they resolve nearly every streaming scenario on this exam.

1209
01:52:29,744 --> 01:52:35,524
Run them in that order because each one eliminates options rather than merely ranking them.

1210
01:52:36,784 --> 01:52:40,004
The second is the fastest win in the whole module.

1211
01:52:42,542 --> 01:52:45,444
Existing Kafka is effectively a one-word answer,

1212
01:52:46,382 --> 01:52:52,900
and candidates still spend the minute carefully weighing kinesis against MSK when the question already told them.

1213
01:52:53,424 --> 01:52:55,588
The fifth is the one people forget.

1214
01:52:55,904 --> 01:53:03,076
Because Firehose is easy to overlook when four services are offered and three of them sound more powerful and more interesting.

1215
01:53:03,790 --> 01:53:11,748
Managed delivery to a destination with no custom logic is a common requirement and firehose is usually the intended answer for it.

1216
01:53:13,358 --> 01:53:20,516
Next module is migration and connectivity, covering how everything that is not a stream gets into AWS.

1217
01:53:22,158 --> 01:53:27,024
Modules 2 and 3 covered what happens to data once it is in AWS.

1218
01:53:27,278 --> 01:53:29,344
This one covers how it arrives.

1219
01:53:29,584 --> 01:53:37,264
AWS DMS appeared 72 times in the bank analyzed for this course, which puts it ahead of Amazon Athena.

1220
01:53:38,126 --> 01:53:44,196
The other services here appear less often individually, but collectively they carry a lot of questions.

1221
01:53:44,384 --> 01:53:48,932
They are unusually easy marks because each one has a narrow, well defined job.

1222
01:53:50,894 --> 01:53:56,144
Get the mapping from source type to service right, and these questions take 15 seconds.

1223
01:53:56,654 --> 01:54:00,836
This module also carries the largest availability warning in the whole course.

1224
01:54:01,358 --> 01:54:07,588
That is because a service that has been a standard, correct answer for a decade is being switched off.

1225
01:54:09,230 --> 01:54:15,012
Almost every question in this module names a source and asks how to get it into AWS.

1226
01:54:15,344 --> 01:54:20,932
The destination is nearly always Amazon S3 or a database and it rarely decides anything.

1227
01:54:21,424 --> 01:54:23,812
What decides it is what the source is:

1228
01:54:24,462 --> 01:54:32,676
a relational database, a file share, a SaaS application, a partner uploading over the internet or a dataset somebody else owns.

1229
01:54:33,262 --> 01:54:39,236
Each of those has one obvious service and three plausible wrong ones. So the reading technique is simple.

1230
01:54:40,174 --> 01:54:49,028
Find the sentence describing the source, classify it, and most of the options fall away before you have thought about anything technical.

1231
01:54:49,934 --> 01:54:55,620
The next slide is that classification in one table and it is worth memorizing outright.

1232
01:54:56,878 --> 01:54:59,556
This table resolves most of the module.

1233
01:54:59,982 --> 01:55:03,428
The call-out is the distinction people get wrong most often.

1234
01:55:03,694 --> 01:55:04,676
So start there.

1235
01:55:05,870 --> 01:55:12,324
AWS DataSync is something you run to go and fetch data from a location you control on a schedule you set.

1236
01:55:13,104 --> 01:55:19,460
AWS Transfer Family is an endpoint you stand up so that other people can send data to you.

1237
01:55:19,984 --> 01:55:24,388
They do it on their schedule using protocols their systems already speak.

1238
01:55:25,024 --> 01:55:29,220
Same broad job of moving files, opposite direction of initiation.

1239
01:55:30,190 --> 01:55:36,996
So a question mentioning partners, suppliers, or external customers uploading is a transfer family question.

1240
01:55:38,318 --> 01:55:42,180
A question about your own file servers is a data sync question.

1241
01:55:43,790 --> 01:55:46,500
The call out is the ranking to carry into the exam.

1242
01:55:48,974 --> 01:55:54,052
Manage connector first because it is pure configuration and somebody else maintains it.

1243
01:55:54,926 --> 01:55:57,796
Then a blue marketplace or custom connector.

1244
01:55:58,606 --> 01:56:06,436
The connector itself is still somebody else's problem and the job orchestration, retrees, and logging all come from Blue rather than from you.

1245
01:56:06,784 --> 01:56:14,436
Then bespoke code as a genuine last resort. Everything about it becomes yours to maintain forever, including the vendor's next breaking.

1246
01:56:15,024 --> 01:56:16,324
API change.

1247
01:56:17,166 --> 01:56:23,460
Questions in this area almost always include one option from each tier, which is what makes them answerable.

1248
01:56:24,144 --> 01:56:27,108
The qualifier tells you which tier is wanted.

1249
01:56:27,664 --> 01:56:31,172
Least development effort pushes you up the list towards App Flow.

1250
01:56:31,824 --> 01:56:34,660
A source that nobody supports pushes you down it.

1251
01:56:36,270 --> 01:56:40,804
Understanding this sequence explains most of what DMS does and why.

1252
01:56:41,184 --> 01:56:49,956
A full load alone would require freezing the source database for however long the copy takes, which for a large production system means an unacceptable outage.

1253
01:56:50,304 --> 01:56:52,196
Change data capture solves that.

1254
01:56:52,959 --> 01:57:01,039
The full load runs against a live database, and while it runs, every change happening on the source is captured and applied to the target afterwards.

1255
01:57:01,039 --> 01:57:07,339
The target converges on the source and the cut-over window shrinks from hours to minutes.

1256
01:57:08,599 --> 01:57:14,699
When a question mentions minimal downtime or the database must remain available during migration,

1257
01:57:15,539 --> 01:57:18,039
it is describing full load plus CD.

1258
01:57:18,990 --> 01:57:21,476
That phrase is effectively the answer.

1259
01:57:23,182 --> 01:57:26,692
The name misleads people and the exam exploits it.

1260
01:57:27,184 --> 01:57:35,716
Database migration service sounds like something you use once and switch off, and a large share of its exam appearances are nothing of the kind.

1261
01:57:37,344 --> 01:57:44,068
DMS is very commonly the answer for keeping an analytic store in sync with an operational database.

1262
01:57:44,944 --> 01:57:48,624
It runs indefinitely with no migration in sight.

1263
01:57:48,784 --> 01:57:56,036
It can write to Amazon S3, to Kinesis DataStreams, to Amazon MSK, and to Amazon Redshift.

1264
01:57:56,974 --> 01:58:00,580
None of those is a database you would migrate to in the traditional sense.

1265
01:58:01,486 --> 01:58:12,548
So when a question describes wanting operational data available for analytics without touching the source application, think DMS even though nobody is migrating anything.

1266
01:58:13,774 --> 01:58:20,132
Four components and the exam mostly tests the relationships between them rather than the details of each.

1267
01:58:20,304 --> 01:58:23,844
The call out describes a pattern that comes up regularly.

1268
01:58:24,224 --> 01:58:31,588
Consolidating several source databases into one target warehouse uses several tasks pointing at the same target endpoint.

1269
01:58:32,366 --> 01:58:40,260
It does not need several separate DMS deployments, and the table mappings deserve a mention because they are how you migrate a subset.

1270
01:58:41,038 --> 01:58:45,348
Selecting only some schemas, filtering rows, or renaming a table on the way.

1271
01:58:45,966 --> 01:58:51,556
Watch for a question asking how to migrate only certain tables or how to rename objects on the way.

1272
01:58:51,904 --> 01:58:56,612
The answer is table mappings, not a separate transformation step afterwards.

1273
01:58:58,158 --> 01:59:00,100
The callout is where the marks are.

1274
01:59:00,512 --> 01:59:02,532
Change data capture is not something.

1275
01:59:03,054 --> 01:59:05,828
DMS can simply switch on from outside.

1276
01:59:06,990 --> 01:59:13,060
It reads the source database's own transaction log, and that log has to be producing the right information.

1277
01:59:14,032 --> 01:59:18,116
On Oracle, that means archive log mode and supplemental logging.

1278
01:59:19,086 --> 01:59:22,272
On MySQL, it means binary logging in row format.

1279
01:59:22,638 --> 01:59:33,860
On SQL Server, it means change data capture or application enabled. So watch for a DMS task that completes the full load and then captures no ongoing changes at all.

1280
01:59:34,734 --> 01:59:37,860
That is almost always a source configuration problem.

1281
01:59:38,912 --> 01:59:43,236
The fix is on the database rather than in DMS.

1282
01:59:44,814 --> 01:59:48,996
This is the same serverless argument as everywhere else in the course.

1283
01:59:50,062 --> 01:59:56,676
DMS serverless has been generally available since 2023, so it is a fair exam answer rather than a novelty.

1284
01:59:57,454 --> 02:00:01,828
The reason it appears often is that sizing a replication instance is genuinely hard to do when.

1285
02:00:30,319 --> 02:00:33,316
the workload is long running and its load is genuinely known.

1286
02:00:35,159 --> 02:00:40,619
The third bullet is the one worth remembering because it enables an architecture people do not expect.

1287
02:00:41,899 --> 02:00:48,619
Pointing DMS at Kinesis data streams turns any supported relational database into a change event stream.

1288
02:00:49,518 --> 02:00:52,679
There are no application changes and no triggers on the source.

1289
02:00:53,838 --> 02:00:58,919
Every insert, update, and delete becomes a record on a stream that anything can consume.

1290
02:00:59,839 --> 02:01:06,179
That is a genuinely powerful answer to questions about reacting to database changes when you cannot modify the application.

1291
02:01:07,579 --> 02:01:15,199
The distractors will usually offer database triggers or polling, both of which put load on the source and are marked wrong for that reason.

1292
02:01:16,750 --> 02:01:22,199
This is the detail that separates a working design from one that surprises people later.

1293
02:01:24,399 --> 02:01:32,679
When DMS writes change data capture output to Amazon S3, it does not update rows in place because S3 objects are immutable.

1294
02:01:33,919 --> 02:01:40,299
It appends records describing what changed, each tagged with whether it was an insert, an update or a delete.

1295
02:01:40,974 --> 02:01:46,532
Query that raw output and you get every historical version of every row rather than the current state.

1296
02:01:47,086 --> 02:01:52,676
Something has to apply those changes, and this is exactly the problem OpenTable Format solve.

1297
02:01:53,152 --> 02:02:01,956
Landing DMS output into Apache Iseberg or Hoodie lets a merge operation collapse. The change log into a current table.

1298
02:02:03,552 --> 02:02:08,516
The two-step nature of a heterogeneous migration is the examinable point.

1299
02:02:09,006 --> 02:02:16,068
Data types, stored procedures, triggers and functions do not translate between engines automatically.

1300
02:02:16,654 --> 02:02:20,804
So something has to convert them before DMS moves a single row.

1301
02:02:21,550 --> 02:02:26,352
That converter used to be a separate download called the AWS Schema Conversion Tool.

1302
02:02:26,512 --> 02:02:31,268
It is now built into the DMS console as DMS Schema Conversion.

1303
02:02:32,334 --> 02:02:34,628
Note something odd in the current exam guide.

1304
02:02:35,310 --> 02:02:40,356
It removed AWSS CT from the InScope service list in December 2025.

1305
02:02:41,294 --> 02:02:44,324
Meanwhile, a task statement still names it as an example.

1306
02:02:44,814 --> 02:02:49,700
When the guide contradicts itself, learn the concept and expect either name.

1307
02:02:51,342 --> 02:02:56,992
These three appear together constantly, and separating them is worth real marks.

1308
02:02:58,702 --> 02:03:03,332
Start with 0 EDL because when it applies, it wins on overhead outright.

1309
02:03:03,950 --> 02:03:13,092
It requires a supported source and target pair, currently Aurora, Amazon, RDS, and DynamoDB into Redshift

1310
02:03:13,390 --> 02:03:15,812
and it moves data without transforming it.

1311
02:03:16,750 --> 02:03:21,604
Suppose the pair is not supported or the target is a data lake rather than Redshift.

1312
02:03:22,478 --> 02:03:26,148
Then DMS with change data capture is the continuous option.

1313
02:03:27,150 --> 02:03:35,620
And for a scheduled extract with real transformation applied on the way, a blue job over a JDBC connection is simpler than either.

1314
02:03:35,904 --> 02:03:41,316
The mistake to avoid is reaching for DMS automatically because the source is a database.

1315
02:03:42,958 --> 02:03:46,596
The second bullet catches people in real projects and in exams.

1316
02:03:47,118 --> 02:03:53,508
DMS handles large objects, things like documents or images stored in a column in one of three modes.

1317
02:03:54,446 --> 02:04:02,948
Limited LOB mode is fast because it allocates a fixed amount of memory per value, and anything larger than that limit is silently truncated.

1318
02:04:03,264 --> 02:04:07,300
Full LOB mode moves everything correctly and is considerably slower.

1319
02:04:08,142 --> 02:04:13,796
Inline LOB mode is the compromise, handling small values inline and large ones separately.

1320
02:04:14,064 --> 02:04:19,172
So watch for a migration that completed successfully where some column values are cut short.

1321
02:04:19,664 --> 02:04:23,664
That is limited LOB mode with the limit set too low.

1322
02:04:25,006 --> 02:04:31,332
The masking capability is worth knowing because it appears as a distractor against other masking options.

1323
02:04:32,718 --> 02:04:38,980
DMS can transform column contents on the way through so sensitive values never land in the target at all.

1324
02:04:39,822 --> 02:04:47,396
That is different from masking in the warehouse afterwards and different again from view sensitive data detection, which module 2 covered.

1325
02:04:47,662 --> 02:04:50,276
And note the availability warning on this slide.

1326
02:05:05,904 --> 02:05:06,224
It

1327
02:05:06,254 --> 02:05:10,500
appears in older material as the answer to database discovery questions.

1328
02:05:12,206 --> 02:05:17,984
Cut over is where migration questions become operations questions and one metric does most of the work.

1329
02:05:18,304 --> 02:05:22,464
CD C latency measures how far behind the target is running.

1330
02:05:22,734 --> 02:05:26,852
While the full load is happening and for some time afterwards it will be high.

1331
02:05:27,310 --> 02:05:33,124
As the target catches up, it falls towards zero. And a latency near zero means the target is effectively current.

1332
02:05:33,504 --> 02:05:36,004
That is the moment to stop writes at the source.

1333
02:05:36,398 --> 02:05:40,484
Let the last few changes drain through and repoint the applications.

1334
02:05:41,422 --> 02:05:47,396
A question asking how to know when a migration is ready to cut over is asking about that metric.

1335
02:05:47,664 --> 02:05:52,388
The distractors will offer row counts which can match while data is still stale.

1336
02:05:53,710 --> 02:05:59,812
A retailer runs a self-managed Oracle database on Amazon EC2, supporting its order system.

1337
02:06:00,302 --> 02:06:07,300
The analytics team wants order data available in Amazon Redshift for reporting refreshed within a few minutes.

1338
02:06:07,822 --> 02:06:12,900
The application team has refused any changes to the Oracle database or its application code.

1339
02:06:13,806 --> 02:06:21,380
A data engineer configures an AWS DMS task with the migration type set to full load plus CDC.

1340
02:06:21,664 --> 02:06:25,984
The full load completes successfully and all existing rows appear in Redshift.

1341
02:06:26,304 --> 02:06:31,780
No subsequent changes ever arrive and the task remains in a running state without reporting an error.

1342
02:06:32,622 --> 02:06:34,244
What is the most likely cause?

1343
02:06:34,830 --> 02:06:39,876
a The replication instance is undersized and cannot keep up with the change volume.

1344
02:06:40,384 --> 02:06:45,316
b supplemental logging is not enabled on the source Oracle database.

1345
02:06:45,934 --> 02:06:46,144
C.

1346
02:06:46,624 --> 02:06:51,652
The target redshift cluster requires a manifest file for incremental loads.

1347
02:06:53,264 --> 02:06:59,264
The task should have used CDC only rather than full load plus cdc.

1348
02:07:01,024 --> 02:07:02,116
Pause and decide.

1349
02:07:09,134 --> 02:07:10,384
The answer is B.

1350
02:07:10,624 --> 02:07:16,964
Three details point at it together. The full load worked. So connectivity, permissions, and the target are all fine.

1351
02:07:17,184 --> 02:07:23,972
No error is reported, which rules out most failure modes because DMS is quite noisy when something breaks.

1352
02:07:24,718 --> 02:07:31,620
And nothing arrives at all rather than arriving slowly, which distinguishes it from a capacity problem.

1353
02:07:32,590 --> 02:07:36,740
Notice the constraint about no changes to the database being refused.

1354
02:07:37,424 --> 02:07:43,584
That is there to make you hesitate because enabling supplemental logging is a database change.

1355
02:07:44,142 --> 02:07:53,956
It is a configuration change rather than a schema or application change, and it is the only thing that makes the requirement achievable.

1356
02:07:55,214 --> 02:08:00,868
File movement questions split on two axes, and both are usually stated plainly in the question text.

1357
02:08:01,742 --> 02:08:05,860
The first is the direction of initiation, which the earlier table introduced.

1358
02:08:06,350 --> 02:08:09,860
Do you go and fetch the data or do other people send it to you?

1359
02:08:10,944 --> 02:08:14,500
The second is whether the network is adequate for the volume.

1360
02:08:14,704 --> 02:08:18,628
A question may hand you a data volume and a connection speed together.

1361
02:08:19,024 --> 02:08:22,820
It wants you to notice the arithmetic does not work in the time available.

1362
02:08:23,694 --> 02:08:27,268
Historically, that pointed at physically shipping a device.

1363
02:08:28,064 --> 02:08:34,624
The next few slides cover why that answer is now disappearing from reality while remaining in the exam.

1364
02:08:34,864 --> 02:08:39,844
Between them, these two axes resolve almost every file transfer question you will meet.

1365
02:08:41,358 --> 02:08:47,236
DataSync is a purpose-built transfer service and the exam usually contrasts it with writing a script.

1366
02:08:47,504 --> 02:08:56,164
The fourth bullet is the differentiator that questions turn on. Copying files with the CLI moves the bytes and loses the file system metadata.

1367
02:08:57,104 --> 02:09:02,820
That means the permissions, ownership, and timestamps a Windows or Unix file share carries.

1368
02:09:03,264 --> 02:09:05,028
DataSync preserves them.

1369
02:09:05,904 --> 02:09:13,348
So a question about migrating a file share where permissions must be retained rules out a scripted copy immediately.

1370
02:09:13,984 --> 02:09:17,188
The incremental behavior in the callout matters too.

1371
02:09:18,094 --> 02:09:24,452
After the first run, a scheduled data sync task compares source and destination and moves only differences.

1372
02:09:25,294 --> 02:09:29,636
That makes it viable as an ongoing sync rather than only a one-off migration.

1373
02:09:31,278 --> 02:09:35,012
Transfer family exists for a specific and very common situation.

1374
02:09:35,264 --> 02:09:42,544
You have partners, suppliers, or customers who send you files. They have been doing it over SFTP for 15 years.

1375
02:09:42,784 --> 02:09:46,532
Nobody is going to rewrite their systems to call an AWS API.

1376
02:09:47,214 --> 02:09:54,084
Historically, that meant running an SFTP server on an instance, patching it, monitoring it, and backing it up.

1377
02:09:54,766 --> 02:09:58,468
Transfer Family removes the server while keeping the protocol.

1378
02:09:59,374 --> 02:10:06,944
Files arrive over SFTP and land directly in an S3 bucket where an event notification can start a pipeline immediately.

1379
02:10:07,264 --> 02:10:12,676
When a question mentions partners uploading and an SFTP server to maintain, this is the answer.

1380
02:10:14,224 --> 02:10:21,028
This is one of the fastest tiebreakers in the module because the wording gives it away rather than hiding it.

1381
02:10:21,584 --> 02:10:30,020
Words like partner, supplier, vendor, customer, or third party all mean somebody outside your control is initiating the transfer.

1382
02:10:30,734 --> 02:10:32,676
That is transfer family.

1383
02:10:33,582 --> 02:10:42,436
Words like a file servers or on premises storage or a data center mean you are initiating. That is data sync.

1384
02:10:44,942 --> 02:10:51,812
The only genuine ambiguity is a partner who would accept either arrangement, and even then the protocol usually settles it.

1385
02:10:52,624 --> 02:11:01,124
If the question mentions SFPP anywhere, the answer is transfer family because datasync does not speak SFTP at all.

1386
02:11:01,824 --> 02:11:10,372
And if the question mentions preserving file permissions, that is data sync, because transfer family is delivering into object storage.

1387
02:11:12,078 --> 02:11:19,876
This is the largest availability warning in the course and it needs care because the exam and reality now disagree.

1388
02:11:20,622 --> 02:11:26,084
For a decade, petabytes of data plus a slow network has been a snowball question.

1389
02:11:26,702 --> 02:11:31,424
That is still how it appears in exam material and in every question bank.

1390
02:11:31,982 --> 02:11:41,344
Meanwhile, AWS plows snowball to new customers in November 2025 and will end support entirely at the end of 2026.

1391
02:11:41,614 --> 02:11:43,236
So the guidance is explicit.

1392
02:11:43,584 --> 02:11:50,628
If a question describes an enormous offline transfer with inadequate bandwidth, Snowball is still the intended answer.

1393
02:11:52,718 --> 02:11:53,444
Choose it.

1394
02:11:54,414 --> 02:12:04,004
Then know that a real project would use datasync over a beta link, the data transfer terminal for physical delivery or outposts for edge compute.

1395
02:12:05,518 --> 02:12:07,396
Storage gateway is the option.

1396
02:12:07,758 --> 02:12:11,460
Candidates forget and the distinction from datasync is clean.

1397
02:12:12,686 --> 02:12:15,876
Data sync moves data from A to B and the job ends.

1398
02:12:16,864 --> 02:12:18,564
File gateway is permanent.

1399
02:12:19,278 --> 02:12:28,224
It presents a file share that on premises applications mount and use exactly as before while the actual bytes live in Amazon S3.

1400
02:12:28,590 --> 02:12:32,464
Nothing is migrated because the file interface never goes away.

1401
02:12:32,974 --> 02:12:38,724
So a question describing a legacy application that cannot be modified, which must keep reading and writing files.

1402
02:12:39,024 --> 02:12:44,068
Meanwhile, the organization wants the data in S3 for analytics is a file gateway question.

1403
02:12:44,782 --> 02:12:48,068
A question about decommissioning a file server is a data sync question.

1404
02:12:49,774 --> 02:13:00,624
A manufacturer receives daily production files from 30 external suppliers. Each supplier system uploads to a company-managed SFTP server running on an Amazon EC2 instance.

1405
02:13:00,784 --> 02:13:06,116
A cron job then copies the files into Amazon S3 where a blue job processes them.

1406
02:13:06,862 --> 02:13:12,900
The operations team patches and monitors the EC2 instance and has had two outages from full disks.

1407
02:13:13,264 --> 02:13:15,652
Suppliers cannot change how they send files.

1408
02:13:16,078 --> 02:13:21,476
The company wants to remove the server while keeping processing starting as soon as a file lands.

1409
02:13:21,904 --> 02:13:23,844
What should the company implement?

1410
02:13:27,344 --> 02:13:35,504
Replace the EC2 instance with AWS. Transfer family for SFTP writing directly to Amazon S3.

1411
02:13:35,904 --> 02:13:36,144
b.

1412
02:13:36,494 --> 02:13:42,852
Deploy AWS datasync agents at each supplier site to pull the files into Amazon S3.

1413
02:13:43,344 --> 02:13:43,664
C.

1414
02:13:44,142 --> 02:13:50,564
Move the SFTP server to a larger EC2 instance in an auto-scaling group with a bigger volume.

1415
02:13:51,264 --> 02:13:51,504
D.

1416
02:13:52,462 --> 02:13:58,116
Ask suppliers to upload directly to Amazon S3 using presigned URLs.

1417
02:13:58,574 --> 02:13:59,588
Pause and decide.

1418
02:14:06,299 --> 02:14:10,179
The answer is A, and one sentence eliminated half the options.

1419
02:14:10,766 --> 02:14:16,516
Suppliers cannot change how they send files means the SFTP protocol is fixed.

1420
02:14:17,070 --> 02:14:19,812
That kills D outright and makes B absurd.

1421
02:14:20,302 --> 02:14:24,784
Since installing agents inside 30 other companies is a bigger change still.

1422
02:14:25,326 --> 02:14:29,380
Notice the second benefit of A that the question does not ask about directly.

1423
02:14:29,664 --> 02:14:35,812
Because transfer family writes into S3, the cron job that copied files across disappears as well.

1424
02:14:36,366 --> 02:14:39,504
An S3 event notification can start the blue job.

1425
02:14:39,694 --> 02:14:46,756
The moment a file arrives, one change removes a server, a scheduled script, and a delay.

1426
02:14:48,046 --> 02:14:50,468
The callout is the whole argument for AppFlow.

1427
02:14:50,784 --> 02:15:00,356
Pulling data from a SaaS application by hand means handling authentication tokens and refreshing them, paginating two results, respecting the vendor's rate limits,

1428
02:15:00,624 --> 02:15:03,972
rewriting all of it when the vendor changes their API.

1429
02:15:05,006 --> 02:15:06,820
That is real ongoing work.

1430
02:15:07,630 --> 02:15:09,664
App Flow does it as configuration.

1431
02:15:09,824 --> 02:15:17,700
So when a question describes needing Salesforce or ServiceNow data in a data lake, the answer is AppFlow.

1432
02:15:18,478 --> 02:15:23,024
The distractors will offer a lambda function calling the API on a schedule.

1433
02:15:24,064 --> 02:15:29,476
That distractor works and it is more development effort and more operational overhead,

1434
02:15:30,126 --> 02:15:33,124
which is exactly what the qualifier in the question is measuring.

1435
02:15:34,830 --> 02:15:41,828
Data exchange is one of the services added to the exam in the December 2025 revision, so older material does not cover it.

1436
02:15:42,350 --> 02:15:43,620
The concept is simple.

1437
02:15:44,782 --> 02:15:51,108
Negotiating with a data vendor means arranging file transfers and building a process to collect their monthly updates.

1438
02:15:51,904 --> 02:15:56,900
Instead, you subscribe through the marketplace and the data arrives in your account.

1439
02:15:57,710 --> 02:16:00,100
New revisions can flow through automatically.

1440
02:16:00,590 --> 02:16:07,908
So a question describing needing third-party market data, weather data or demographic data alongside your own.

1441
02:16:08,846 --> 02:16:12,452
Asking for the least operational overhead now has an obvious answer.

1442
02:16:13,230 --> 02:16:19,396
Before this revision, the expected answer would have been a transfer family endpoint or a manual process.

1443
02:16:20,974 --> 02:16:26,500
Sometimes no managed connector exists and you genuinely do have to call the API yourself.

1444
02:16:26,990 --> 02:16:32,772
The second bullet is the one worth internalizing because it is where the exam separates two working answers.

1445
02:16:33,184 --> 02:16:37,988
When a pull involves paging through many results, coping with a vendor that thorts you.

1446
02:16:38,926 --> 02:16:44,356
Retrying failures without losing your place, a single lambda function becomes fragile.

1447
02:16:45,024 --> 02:16:50,788
It also becomes very hard to debug because all that logic hides inside one invocation.

1448
02:16:52,014 --> 02:16:59,940
Step functions handles the looping, the waiting, and the retry policy as an explicit state machine you can watch running.

1449
02:17:00,878 --> 02:17:09,124
That is why it appears as the correct answer in questions where a naive lambda would time out partway through or silently lose records.

1450
02:17:09,966 --> 02:17:12,964
Module 6 covers step functions properly.

1451
02:17:14,544 --> 02:17:18,624
A media company stores customer records in Salesforce.

1452
02:17:18,784 --> 02:17:28,740
The analytics team wants those records in Amazon S3 daily with email addresses masked before they land and with records for one region excluded entirely.

1453
02:17:31,022 --> 02:17:36,484
A data engineer has proposed an AWS Lambda function on an event bridge schedule.

1454
02:17:37,166 --> 02:17:45,188
It authenticates to the Salesforce API, pages through results, applies the filtering and masking, then writes to S3.

1455
02:17:47,918 --> 02:17:51,344
The team has limited capacity for ongoing maintenance.

1456
02:17:52,654 --> 02:17:54,980
What should the data engineer do instead?

1457
02:17:57,390 --> 02:18:03,844
Build the Lambda function as proposed, storing the Salesforce credentials in AWS Secrets Manager.

1458
02:18:05,486 --> 02:18:05,769
B.

1459
02:18:06,704 --> 02:18:13,060
Use Amazon App Flow with a scheduled flow, applying the filter and mask in the flow configuration.

1460
02:18:14,304 --> 02:18:22,020
C Use AWS DMS with Salesforce as a source endpoint and Amazon S3 as the target.

1461
02:18:25,344 --> 02:18:29,700
subscribe to the Salesforce dataset through AWS Data Exchange.

1462
02:18:29,998 --> 02:18:31,012
Pause and decide.

1463
02:18:37,454 --> 02:18:40,544
The answer is B. Notice how the question was built.

1464
02:18:40,864 --> 02:18:46,404
The two extra requirements, masking a field and excluding a region, are not incidental color.

1465
02:18:46,798 --> 02:18:55,140
They are both native app flow capabilities listed deliberately so that the service which does them without code becomes the strongest answer.

1466
02:18:55,630 --> 02:19:03,184
Option A is worth respecting because it is not wrong and the detail about secrets manager is genuinely good practice.

1467
02:19:03,424 --> 02:19:07,300
It loses on the stated constraint of limited maintenance capacity.

1468
02:19:08,142 --> 02:19:16,900
Options C and B are category errors, and they are the kind you can eliminate instantly once you hold the one sentence definition of each service.

1469
02:19:18,544 --> 02:19:21,604
Most of this is solutions architect territory.

1470
02:19:21,838 --> 02:19:26,596
The exam guide names creating allow lists for IP addresses as a skill.

1471
02:19:26,784 --> 02:19:28,708
So a little of it matters here.

1472
02:19:31,566 --> 02:19:34,532
The direct connect row is the one to read carefully.

1473
02:19:35,374 --> 02:19:43,652
Candidates reach for direct connect whenever a question mentions large volumes, and the real differentiator is consistency rather than raw speed.

1474
02:19:44,430 --> 02:19:51,908
A VPN over the public internet can be fast and its latency varies with whatever else is happening on the internet that day.

1475
02:19:52,622 --> 02:19:56,708
Direct Connect is a dedicated circuit with predictable performance.

1476
02:19:56,912 --> 02:20:05,988
So a question emphasizing consistent or predictable throughput points at Direct Connect and one merely mentioning security points at a VPN.

1477
02:20:07,726 --> 02:20:10,404
This is a small topic with a memorable failure mode.

1478
02:20:10,766 --> 02:20:17,604
Consider a Lambda function or a view job calling an external API from inside a VPC.

1479
02:20:18,254 --> 02:20:22,276
Traffic leaves by whatever path the subnet's root table defines.

1480
02:20:23,086 --> 02:20:34,372
Without a NAT gateway carrying a fixed elastic IP, the source address the partner sees can change. A partner enforcing an IP allow list will reject some calls and accept others.

1481
02:20:34,592 --> 02:20:36,164
The symptom is maddening.

1482
02:20:36,352 --> 02:20:41,476
It works most of the time and fails unpredictably with no pattern in the code.

1483
02:20:41,952 --> 02:20:49,188
The fix is to route outbound traffic through a NAT gateway so that every call presents the same address.

1484
02:20:50,638 --> 02:21:00,644
A logistics company must migrate 400 terabytes of historical archive files from an on-premises SMB file share into Amazon S3.

1485
02:21:01,390 --> 02:21:09,732
The connection to Amazon S3 there is a 1 GB per second link that is already carrying production traffic during business hours.

1486
02:21:10,606 --> 02:21:14,352
The files must retain their original permissions and timestamps.

1487
02:21:14,512 --> 02:21:20,932
After the migration, a nightly transfer of roughly 200 GB of new files must continue indefinitely.

1488
02:21:22,062 --> 02:21:24,516
Which approach best meets the requirements?

1489
02:21:25,614 --> 02:21:33,892
Use AWS datasync for both the bulk migration and the ongoing nightly transfer, scheduling it outside business hours.

1490
02:21:34,672 --> 02:21:41,668
b Copy the files with the AWS CLI using multi-part upload and a parallel sync script.

1491
02:21:42,574 --> 02:21:42,832
C.

1492
02:21:43,630 --> 02:21:49,412
Deploy AWS storage gateway file gateway and let the cache populate over time.

1493
02:21:50,222 --> 02:21:50,512
D

1494
02:21:51,726 --> 02:21:58,436
Use AWS transfer family with an SFTP endpoint and script the uploads.

1495
02:22:00,462 --> 02:22:01,712
Pause and decide.

1496
02:22:08,110 --> 02:22:10,752
The answer is A and two requirements for strict.

1497
02:22:11,246 --> 02:22:19,396
Retaining permissions and timestamps eliminates any plain copy, which is why option B fails despite being the intuitive engineer's answer.

1498
02:22:20,302 --> 02:22:30,084
And the ongoing nightly transfer means the solution must keep working after the migration, which datasync does through incremental transfers and scheduling.

1499
02:22:30,592 --> 02:22:32,900
Note what the question did not ask.

1500
02:22:33,582 --> 02:22:40,260
400TB over a shared 1GB link is slow and there is no snowball option offered.

1501
02:22:41,134 --> 02:22:50,660
Given the retirement timeline covered earlier, expect that option to appear less often over time and read carefully for whether a question actually needs it.

1502
02:22:51,918 --> 02:22:56,388
Worth pausing on this slide and covering the right-hand column with your hand.

1503
02:22:56,910 --> 02:23:05,476
If you can produce each sentence from the service name and more importantly, produce the service name from each sentence, this module has done its job.

1504
02:23:06,254 --> 02:23:10,020
The two rows that get confused are data sync and transfer family.

1505
02:23:11,022 --> 02:23:14,468
The direction of initiation separates them every single time.

1506
02:23:14,672 --> 02:23:18,500
So make that your first question rather than comparing features.

1507
02:23:19,182 --> 02:23:26,052
The two rows that get overlooked are storage gateway because candidates forget that ongoing hybrid access is an option at all.

1508
02:23:27,022 --> 02:23:31,204
Data exchange because it is new to the exam guide.

1509
02:23:32,142 --> 02:23:40,836
And the last row is where the exam and commercial reality now part company, which the availability warning covered in detail.

1510
02:23:42,542 --> 02:23:47,552
Five questions again, and the fifth earns its place in this module more than most.

1511
02:23:48,192 --> 02:23:51,072
Migration scenarios are full of stated refusals.

1512
02:23:51,312 --> 02:23:53,220
Suppliers cannot change their systems.

1513
02:23:53,632 --> 02:23:56,324
The application team will not modify the database.

1514
02:23:57,326 --> 02:24:00,100
The team has no capacity to maintain custom code.

1515
02:24:00,910 --> 02:24:08,356
Each of those is placed to eliminate a specific option, and spotting them turns a 4-way comparison into a 2-way one.

1516
02:24:09,678 --> 02:24:20,132
Next module moves from getting data in to transforming it, comparing Blue, EMR, Lambda, and Redshift as processing engines.

1517
02:24:21,070 --> 02:24:28,036
Covering how large language models now appear in the exam guide as a data processing tool.

1518
02:24:29,582 --> 02:24:35,780
This module covers 11 separate skills from the exam guide, more than any other in the course.

1519
02:24:36,974 --> 02:24:40,644
That is because transformation is where the most services overlap.

1520
02:24:41,582 --> 02:24:51,744
Six engines can process your data, several of them can process any given dataset, and the exam builds its distractors almost entirely out of that overlap.

1521
02:24:52,064 --> 02:24:55,204
The skill being tested is selection rather than knowledge.

1522
02:24:56,142 --> 02:25:04,228
So this module carries eight worked scenarios instead of the usual four, and most of them are about choosing rather than about how something works.

1523
02:25:04,544 --> 02:25:07,588
It also covers large language models as a processing tool.

1524
02:25:08,366 --> 02:25:13,668
That arrived in the exam guide in December 2025 and appears in no older material.

1525
02:25:15,374 --> 02:25:18,244
There is a habit worth breaking before anything else.

1526
02:25:18,894 --> 02:25:23,748
Candidates learn services and then try to match a scenario to a service they know.

1527
02:25:24,384 --> 02:25:26,628
The reliable direction is the opposite.

1528
02:25:27,118 --> 02:25:29,284
Describe the work in plain terms first.

1529
02:25:29,904 --> 02:25:34,144
Is it one small file or a distributed join across terabytes?

1530
02:25:34,702 --> 02:25:37,572
Does it need Spark or would SQL do?

1531
02:25:37,966 --> 02:25:40,292
Does it run for 30 seconds or 3 hours?

1532
02:25:41,582 --> 02:25:48,740
Once the work is described, usually only one or two engines can even do it, and the qualifier in the question separates those.

1533
02:25:49,184 --> 02:25:53,668
Doing it the other way around is how four options all end up looking plausible.

1534
02:25:55,406 --> 02:26:00,068
6 engines and the 2 at the bottom are the ones people overlook under pressure.

1535
02:26:00,558 --> 02:26:04,932
Athena and Redshift are transformation engines, not only query engines.

1536
02:26:05,264 --> 02:26:10,020
Choosing one of them often means the pipeline you are about to build does not need to exist.

1537
02:26:10,862 --> 02:26:17,860
Bedrock is newer to this exam and belongs on this list because a real class of transformation is not arithmetic at all.

1538
02:26:18,894 --> 02:26:24,036
Classifying a support ticket, pulling fees out of a scanned invoice, summarizing a review.

1539
02:26:24,590 --> 02:26:26,340
No amount of spark does that.

1540
02:26:27,278 --> 02:26:35,108
Keep all six in mind when you read a question because a strong answer is frequently the engine that was not the obvious one.

1541
02:26:36,622 --> 02:26:43,824
These three words appear in the exam guide as a named skill, so they are worth more than the marketing they usually attract.

1542
02:26:44,078 --> 02:26:45,732
Treat them as a checklist.

1543
02:26:46,254 --> 02:26:49,104
Volume tells you whether one machine can do the job.

1544
02:26:49,344 --> 02:26:54,468
Velocity tells you whether this is batch or streaming, which module 3 covered.

1545
02:26:55,406 --> 02:26:58,180
Variety is the one that has genuinely moved.

1546
02:26:58,862 --> 02:27:04,548
Structured data goes to SQL engines, semi-structured like JSON goes to Spark or a dynamic frame.

1547
02:27:05,166 --> 02:27:08,036
Unstructured content used to mean building something custom.

1548
02:27:08,814 --> 02:27:14,980
Since the exam guide revision, it has a managed answer, which the last section of this module covers.

1549
02:27:16,590 --> 02:27:22,628
Module 2 flagged this as a shape. The exam marks wrong and it deserves the full treatment here.

1550
02:27:26,414 --> 02:27:34,144
Moving data out of a warehouse to transform it and then moving it back in adds a pipeline, latency, cost, and a thing to operate.

1551
02:27:34,464 --> 02:27:37,604
All of that to do work the warehouse could have done in place.

1552
02:27:38,542 --> 02:27:47,396
When a question disguises data already sitting in Redshift needing lee shaping into another Redshift table, the answer is SQL inside Redshift.

1553
02:27:47,664 --> 02:27:51,396
The distractor will be a glue job and it will look like proper engineering.

1554
02:27:52,270 --> 02:27:59,108
ETL is right when data must be cleaned before it lands or when the target cannot do the transformation.

1555
02:28:00,622 --> 02:28:07,140
Glue and EMR overlap so heavily that questions offering both are really asking whether you know the boundary.

1556
02:28:07,662 --> 02:28:12,356
Both run Apache Spark, both read from S3, both are managed.

1557
02:28:12,942 --> 02:28:19,492
If a question could be answered by either, the qualifier decides and least operational overhead points at Blue almost every time.

1558
02:28:20,142 --> 02:28:25,124
So the useful knowledge is the set of situations where Blue genuinely cannot do the job.

1559
02:28:25,806 --> 02:28:29,380
There are three, they are specific, and the next slide lists them.

1560
02:28:30,350 --> 02:28:37,284
When none of the three is present and the question mentions overhead, stop comparing and choose blue.

1561
02:28:38,926 --> 02:28:42,948
Learn these as an elimination test rather than as a comparison.

1562
02:28:43,918 --> 02:28:45,248
The first is the keynest.

1563
02:28:46,350 --> 02:28:49,380
Glue runs Spark and Python and nothing else.

1564
02:28:49,968 --> 02:28:56,932
So the moment a question names Hive HBase, Presto, or Trino, Glue is out and you have your answer in seconds.

1565
02:28:57,648 --> 02:29:01,156
The second is about migration economics rather than technology.

1566
02:29:01,582 --> 02:29:09,540
An organization with years of hive scripts can lift them onto EMR almost unchanged, where moving to Glue means rewriting everything.

1567
02:29:10,368 --> 02:29:12,996
The fourth reason is the one people forget.

1568
02:29:13,870 --> 02:29:20,164
Spot instances can cut compute costs dramatically, and Blue offers no equivalent.

1569
02:29:20,448 --> 02:29:27,300
So a sustained heavy workload under cost pressure can point at EMR even when Spark alone would do.

1570
02:29:28,942 --> 02:29:36,036
The callout matters because a lot of exam preparation still treats EMR as automatically the high overhead option.

1571
02:29:36,462 --> 02:29:38,820
EMR serverless changed that.

1572
02:29:39,438 --> 02:29:42,468
There is no cluster to size, patch, or scale.

1573
02:29:43,054 --> 02:29:46,276
Billing is per second with a one minute minimum per worker.

1574
02:29:47,182 --> 02:29:50,436
You still get the full framework choice that GRU lacks.

1575
02:29:50,608 --> 02:29:58,276
So a question needing hive with the least operational overhead has a clean answer that older material would have called contradictory.

1576
02:29:58,638 --> 02:30:01,220
EMR on EKS is narrower.

1577
02:30:02,158 --> 02:30:11,204
It is for organizations already running Kubernetes who want their analytics jobs sharing the same cluster and the same tooling as everything else.

1578
02:30:12,654 --> 02:30:18,660
The three node types matter because they decide where spot instances are safe, which the next slide covers.

1579
02:30:19,054 --> 02:30:21,444
The storage point underneath is bigger though.

1580
02:30:22,638 --> 02:30:29,252
Traditional Hadoop kept data on the cluster in HDFS, which meant the cluster had to live as long as the data.

1581
02:30:36,768 --> 02:30:38,724
Now the cluster is disposable.

1582
02:30:39,168 --> 02:30:40,644
Spin it up for a job.

1583
02:30:41,259 --> 02:30:42,959
process data that lives in S3,

1584
02:30:44,039 --> 02:30:46,884
write results back to S3, Terminate.

1585
02:30:47,982 --> 02:30:50,148
That pattern is called a transient cluster.

1586
02:30:51,088 --> 02:30:58,884
It is usually the correct answer when a question describes an EMR cluster sitting idle between nightly runs.

1587
02:31:00,494 --> 02:31:05,924
This slide is the answer to most EMR cost questions and it has a clear structure.

1588
02:31:09,390 --> 02:31:15,268
Spot belongs on task nodes because Spark simply retries a task whose executor disappeared.

1589
02:31:16,718 --> 02:31:18,852
Spot does not belong on the primary node.

1590
02:31:19,726 --> 02:31:27,876
That is because losing it kills the whole cluster or on core nodes because they hold HDFS blocks.

1591
02:31:30,222 --> 02:31:34,208
The fleets and groups distinction in the call-out is worth memorizing.

1592
02:31:34,638 --> 02:31:41,636
Instance groups are a fixed pool of one instance type. So if that type has no spot capacity, you get nothing.

1593
02:31:42,606 --> 02:31:47,888
Instance fleets let EMR substitute among several types to fill the request.

1594
02:31:48,048 --> 02:31:54,084
That is why they are the answer whenever a question mentions spot interruptions or capacity.

1595
02:31:55,854 --> 02:32:07,748
A media company runs an Amazon EMR cluster on EC2 24 hours a day. The cluster runs a nightly Apache hive job that takes about 3 hours and nothing else.

1596
02:32:08,128 --> 02:32:14,340
It uses 15 on-demand core nodes and stores intermediate data in HDFS on those nodes.

1597
02:32:14,768 --> 02:32:16,580
Finance has flagged the cost.

1598
02:32:17,488 --> 02:32:24,644
The job must still complete overnight and the team has a large body of existing hive scripts they do not want to rewrite.

1599
02:32:25,168 --> 02:32:30,052
Which combination will reduce cost the most while meeting the requirements?

1600
02:32:30,608 --> 02:32:36,740
a migrate the hive scripts to AWS Glue and run them as scheduled Spark jobs.

1601
02:32:37,518 --> 02:32:37,808
b

1602
02:32:38,766 --> 02:32:42,208
store data in Amazon S3 with EMRFS.

1603
02:32:43,054 --> 02:32:48,132
Run a transient cluster nightly and use spot instances for task nodes.

1604
02:32:49,648 --> 02:32:49,888
C.

1605
02:32:50,368 --> 02:32:54,628
Switch all 15 core nodes to spot instances and keep the cluster running.

1606
02:32:55,982 --> 02:33:00,804
D reduce the cluster from 15 nodes to 5 and accept a longer runtime.

1607
02:33:01,358 --> 02:33:02,500
Pause and decide.

1608
02:33:09,200 --> 02:33:14,788
The answer is B, and the deciding arithmetic is in the first two sentences.

1609
02:33:15,200 --> 02:33:20,804
A cluster running all day for a three hour job is ideal for seven eighths of its life.

1610
02:33:21,840 --> 02:33:25,892
No amount of resizing fixes that, which is why D is a trap.

1611
02:33:26,320 --> 02:33:30,276
It looks responsible and it is optimizing the wrong dimension.

1612
02:33:31,118 --> 02:33:33,636
Option A fails on a single word.

1613
02:33:35,246 --> 02:33:40,324
5. Glue does not run it, so migrating means rewriting, which the question refused.

1614
02:33:41,134 --> 02:33:43,684
And option C shows why the node types matter.

1615
02:33:44,654 --> 02:33:50,884
Spot on code nodes puts HDFS data on machines AWS can reclaim with 2 minutes notice.

1616
02:33:52,526 --> 02:33:59,012
Containers are a named skill in the exam guide and they appear less often than their prominence there suggests.

1617
02:34:00,174 --> 02:34:06,560
The pattern to recognize is that containers win on organizational grounds rather than technical ones.

1618
02:34:07,086 --> 02:34:14,180
Consider a team already running everything on Kubernetes with monitoring, deployment and access control built around it.

1619
02:34:14,862 --> 02:34:18,148
Analytics jobs living in the same place is real value.

1620
02:34:18,606 --> 02:34:21,060
That is what EMR on EKS is for.

1621
02:34:21,646 --> 02:34:30,148
What containers do not usually win is a straight comparison for Scheduled ETL, because Blue does that with no cluster at all.

1622
02:34:31,310 --> 02:34:36,800
So read for whether the question describes an existing platform standard or a fresh choice.

1623
02:34:38,126 --> 02:34:43,684
Every engine here has an interactive front end and the exam tests two things about them.

1624
02:34:44,302 --> 02:34:48,612
The first is that exploration and production are different modes.

1625
02:34:49,358 --> 02:34:55,840
You develop against a notebook where feedback is immediate, then schedule the finished code as a job.

1626
02:34:56,334 --> 02:35:00,320
An option proposing that the notebook itself becomes the production pipeline is

1627
02:35:00,320 --> 02:35:03,680
wrong. The second is the cost point in the call out.

1628
02:35:04,782 --> 02:35:09,988
Interactive sessions build for as long as they stay open, not for the queries you actually run.

1629
02:35:10,926 --> 02:35:14,852
A forgotten notebook accrues charges through the night and through the weekend.

1630
02:35:15,120 --> 02:35:21,572
Blue interactive sessions claim 5 dP as the moment they start, which is where the surprise usually comes from.

1631
02:35:22,990 --> 02:35:29,924
A company processes a single compressed CSV file that lands in Amazon S3 once an hour.

1632
02:35:30,542 --> 02:35:32,800
The file is between 4 and 9 MB.

1633
02:35:33,902 --> 02:35:39,620
Processing means renaming 6 columns, casting 2 date fields, and writing the result as parquet.

1634
02:35:40,494 --> 02:35:45,604
The current implementation is an AWS Glue Spark job triggered by an S3 event.

1635
02:35:46,000 --> 02:35:51,300
The finance team has asked why a job that handles 9 MB costs what it does.

1636
02:35:51,680 --> 02:35:53,732
What should the data engineer change?

1637
02:35:54,160 --> 02:35:54,480
a

1638
02:35:55,470 --> 02:35:58,692
reduce the glue job from 10 DPUs to the minimum of 2.

1639
02:36:00,654 --> 02:36:05,348
Replace the Glue Spark job with an AWS Lambda function triggered by the same event.

1640
02:36:05,840 --> 02:36:06,160
C.

1641
02:36:06,574 --> 02:36:09,572
Replace the Glue Spark job with a Blue Python shell job.

1642
02:36:10,160 --> 02:36:10,480
D.

1643
02:36:10,894 --> 02:36:15,012
Batch the files and run one glue job daily instead of hourly.

1644
02:36:15,822 --> 02:36:16,900
Pause and decide.

1645
02:36:23,662 --> 02:36:29,316
The answer is B. And option C is the distractor worth studying because it is nearly right.

1646
02:36:29,760 --> 02:36:35,876
A blue Python share job is the correct instinct. Moving away from Spark for work that is not distributed.

1647
02:36:36,160 --> 02:36:41,188
It still carries blue startup time and its one minute billing minimum, 24 times a day.

1648
02:36:42,094 --> 02:36:44,996
Lambda processes that file in under a second.

1649
02:36:45,294 --> 02:36:47,524
Notice the pairing with the previous scenario.

1650
02:36:49,742 --> 02:36:56,516
There, the engine was right and the configuration was wasteful. Here, the configuration is irrelevant because the engine is the wrong category.

1651
02:36:57,422 --> 02:37:02,372
Distinguishing those two situations is most of what this module teaches.

1652
02:37:03,758 --> 02:37:07,344
This section is where marks are quietly available.

1653
02:37:07,534 --> 02:37:15,184
That is because candidates think of Athena and Redshift as places to query data rather than as engines that transform it.

1654
02:37:15,424 --> 02:37:23,076
Both can create new tables from the results of a query in one statement with no job, no cluster, and no orchestration.

1655
02:37:23,534 --> 02:37:27,172
When data already lives where one of them can reach it,

1656
02:37:29,358 --> 02:37:32,516
that is frequently the lowest overhead answer available.

1657
02:37:33,358 --> 02:37:40,612
And an option offering a view job to do the same work will look more like real data engineering, which is exactly why it is offered.

1658
02:37:42,286 --> 02:37:47,716
The spectrum point deserves emphasis because it removes an assumption people carry into questions.

1659
02:37:48,462 --> 02:37:51,492
Data sitting in the lake does not mean redshift is out.

1660
02:37:52,462 --> 02:37:58,948
Spectrum lets a redshift query read S3 directly and join it to warehouse tables in the same statement.

1661
02:37:59,854 --> 02:38:06,532
So, a single SQL statement can combine curated warehouse data with raw lake data.

1662
02:38:06,926 --> 02:38:11,364
That is often simpler than any pipeline that would move one to the other.

1663
02:38:11,950 --> 02:38:14,084
And note the availability warning.

1664
02:38:15,758 --> 02:38:21,956
Redshift Python user defined functions reach end of support at the end of June 2026.

1665
02:38:22,894 --> 02:38:30,692
So a question offering a Python UDF as the way to implement custom logic is describing something on its way out.

1666
02:38:32,622 --> 02:38:37,988
Athena C T A S is the single most underused answer on this exam.

1667
02:38:40,944 --> 02:38:48,068
One SQL statement takes a pile of JSON files and produces a partitioned compressed parquet table.

1668
02:38:49,070 --> 02:38:53,956
That is the optimization module one recommended achieved with no job and no cluster.

1669
02:38:54,958 --> 02:38:57,828
The limits in the call out are what decide the questions though.

1670
02:38:58,224 --> 02:39:05,476
A single statement can write at most 100 partitions, and exceeding it produces an error about too many open writers.

1671
02:39:07,950 --> 02:39:10,884
Five years of daily partitions is around 1800.

1672
02:39:11,854 --> 02:39:20,708
So a one-shot conversion of a large historical dataset needs either a chain of insert statements or a blue job instead.

1673
02:39:21,934 --> 02:39:24,356
Frequency and complexity separate these two.

1674
02:39:24,718 --> 02:39:31,876
A one-off conversion of a dataset to parquet is a CTAS statement, and building a blue job for it is disproportionate.

1675
02:39:32,302 --> 02:39:36,804
A pipeline running every night, processing only what is new, needs bookmarks.

1676
02:39:37,294 --> 02:39:40,356
It probably needs logic that SQL expresses awkwardly.

1677
02:39:41,326 --> 02:39:42,468
That is a blue job.

1678
02:39:42,926 --> 02:39:45,124
The middle ground appears in questions too.

1679
02:39:45,518 --> 02:39:56,068
A recurring but simple conversion can be a CTAS statement invoked on a schedule by step functions that keeps the simplicity while adding the repetition.

1680
02:39:56,942 --> 02:40:03,140
When you see that option, take it seriously rather than assuming recurring means glue.

1681
02:40:04,784 --> 02:40:11,024
An analytics team has three years of application logs in Amazon S3 as gzip.json.

1682
02:40:11,598 --> 02:40:15,824
They sit in a single prefix with no partitioning, totaling about 2TB.

1683
02:40:16,624 --> 02:40:21,604
Analysts query them with Amazon Athena and every query scans the whole dataset.

1684
02:40:22,254 --> 02:40:25,956
The team wants a one-off conversion to parquet partitioned by day.

1685
02:40:26,286 --> 02:40:28,548
There are roughly 1100 days of data.

1686
02:40:29,838 --> 02:40:35,300
After the conversion, a new daily file will continue to arrive and must be appended in the same format.

1687
02:40:36,174 --> 02:40:38,116
What is the most appropriate approach?

1688
02:40:40,782 --> 02:40:46,148
Run a single Athena CT AS statement partition by day to create the whole table.

1689
02:40:46,766 --> 02:40:53,572
b. Run an AWS blue job for the historical conversion, then a scheduled blue job with bookmarks for the daily file.

1690
02:40:54,478 --> 02:41:02,624
Run an Athena CTAS statement for the first 100 days, then insert into for the rest and a daily insert into

1691
02:41:02,784 --> 02:41:03,588
afterwards.

1692
02:41:04,334 --> 02:41:06,064
D. Enable S3.

1693
02:41:06,544 --> 02:41:10,436
Intelligent tiering on the prefix to reduce the cost of scanning.

1694
02:41:11,246 --> 02:41:12,704
Pause and decide.

1695
02:41:19,118 --> 02:41:25,092
The answer is B, and this scenario deliberately punishes the advice from two slides ago.

1696
02:41:25,808 --> 02:41:32,516
Athena, C TAS is genuinely the right tool for a one-off conversion, and the previous slide said so.

1697
02:41:32,974 --> 02:41:38,308
Here the question plans 1100 days against a 100 partition ceiling, which rules it out.

1698
02:41:39,182 --> 02:41:45,408
Option C is a documented workaround and it is not wrong. It is simply more work than the alternative.

1699
02:41:45,648 --> 02:41:48,208
Once you also need the recurring daily append.

1700
02:41:50,638 --> 02:41:52,708
Option D is the pure trap.

1701
02:41:53,550 --> 02:42:03,108
Storage class and bytes scanned are unrelated, and a candidate who half remembers that intelligent tiering saves money will reach for it.

1702
02:42:04,814 --> 02:42:10,276
Format conversion is a named skill and it has four answers depending on when you catch the data.

1703
02:42:11,630 --> 02:42:14,276
The first row is the most commonly missed.

1704
02:42:15,150 --> 02:42:23,972
If records are still flowing through a fire hose stream, format conversion is a configuration setting and the data arrives in parquet already partitioned.

1705
02:42:24,846 --> 02:42:27,428
No conversion job ever needs to exist.

1706
02:42:27,950 --> 02:42:31,204
Module 3 covered this and it is worth repeating.

1707
02:42:32,078 --> 02:42:39,428
That is because a question describing raw JSON piling up in a lake often has a fix upstream rather than downstream.

1708
02:42:40,238 --> 02:42:48,740
The general principle is to fix the format as early in the pipeline as you can, because every stage after it inherits the problem.

1709
02:42:50,478 --> 02:42:57,124
Integrating multiple sources is another named skill and the exam test judgment rather than mechanics.

1710
02:42:57,678 --> 02:43:05,284
Athena federated query is the option that looks most elegant because it queries an operational database in place with no pipeline.

1711
02:43:05,966 --> 02:43:08,164
The call out is why it is often wrong.

1712
02:43:08,974 --> 02:43:15,172
Every federated query runs load against a production system that exists to serve an application.

1713
02:43:15,728 --> 02:43:21,248
Analysts running ad hoc queries against it is how you slow down a checkout page.

1714
02:43:21,568 --> 02:43:25,728
Landing data in the lake first isolates the analytics from the source.

1715
02:43:26,126 --> 02:43:32,964
So federated query suits occasional lookups of reference data, not a regular analytical workload.

1716
02:43:34,382 --> 02:43:38,052
A retailer needs a daily report joining out a data in Amazon Redshift.

1717
02:43:38,448 --> 02:43:44,740
Product reference data in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Clickstream files in Amazon S3.

1718
02:43:45,998 --> 02:43:49,796
The RDS database also serves the live checkout application.

1719
02:43:50,768 --> 02:43:57,540
A data engineer proposes using Amazon Athena Federated Query to join all three sources at report time.

1720
02:43:57,934 --> 02:44:01,348
The reporting query is heavy and runs each morning at 8.

1721
02:44:01,808 --> 02:44:04,900
What is the main problem and what should be done instead?

1722
02:44:05,518 --> 02:44:07,748
a Athena cannot query Redshift.

1723
02:44:08,590 --> 02:44:11,492
Move all data into Redshift and query it there.

1724
02:44:11,982 --> 02:44:15,844
b The Federated Query loads the live checkout database.

1725
02:44:16,048 --> 02:44:20,644
Replicate the RDS reference data into the lake or warehouse first.

1726
02:44:21,248 --> 02:44:21,488
C.

1727
02:44:22,318 --> 02:44:23,908
Federated query is too slow.

1728
02:44:24,398 --> 02:44:27,108
Increase the Athena workgroup concurrency limit.

1729
02:44:27,728 --> 02:44:28,048
D.

1730
02:44:28,686 --> 02:44:29,728
There is no problem.

1731
02:44:30,446 --> 02:44:34,208
Federated query is designed for exactly this pattern.

1732
02:44:34,734 --> 02:44:36,100
Pause and decide.

1733
02:44:42,542 --> 02:44:45,252
The answer is B, and one clause carries it.

1734
02:44:45,968 --> 02:44:50,148
The RDS database also serves the live checkout application.

1735
02:44:50,688 --> 02:44:56,004
Without that sentence, federated query would be a reasonable design and D would be defensible.

1736
02:44:57,486 --> 02:45:02,820
With it, you are running a heavy analytical joint against a system whose availability is revenue.

1737
02:45:03,728 --> 02:45:08,068
Notice that the fix connects back to module 4.

1738
02:45:08,622 --> 02:45:17,124
Replicating that reference data continuously is a DMS task or a zero ETL integration, which are the tools for exactly this.

1739
02:45:18,094 --> 02:45:19,140
And note option.

1740
02:45:20,078 --> 02:45:20,288
A

1741
02:45:21,518 --> 02:45:25,124
When an option asserts that something is impossible and it is not,

1742
02:45:25,966 --> 02:45:29,252
that is usually the fastest elimination available.

1743
02:45:30,990 --> 02:45:38,052
This distinction sits underneath every spark troubleshooting question, and it is worth understanding rather than memorizing.

1744
02:45:38,926 --> 02:45:42,916
A narrow transformation works on each partition independently.

1745
02:45:44,430 --> 02:45:49,092
Ten executors do a tenth of the work. Each and nothing moves between them.

1746
02:45:50,126 --> 02:45:55,012
A wide transformation needs rows, sharing a key to end up on the same executor.

1747
02:45:56,782 --> 02:46:02,020
That means writing data out, sending it across the network, and reading it back.

1748
02:46:02,638 --> 02:46:03,652
That is a shuffle.

1749
02:46:04,334 --> 02:46:08,164
Once you see this, the troubleshooting table stops being a list to memorize.

1750
02:46:09,102 --> 02:46:13,124
SKU is a shuffle where one destination receives too much.

1751
02:46:13,582 --> 02:46:17,444
Memory pressure is a shuffled partition too large to hold.

1752
02:46:18,350 --> 02:46:22,724
Slow stages are shuffles you could have avoided by restructuring the job.

1753
02:46:24,302 --> 02:46:29,092
Laziness surprises people and it produces a specific exam scenario.

1754
02:46:29,614 --> 02:46:36,644
Spark builds a plan rather than executing as it goes and only runs when something asks for output.

1755
02:46:37,518 --> 02:46:44,516
Consider a job that filters and joins into an intermediate result, then writes that result to two destinations.

1756
02:46:45,454 --> 02:46:50,372
Spark performs the filter and the join twice, once for each output.

1757
02:46:50,798 --> 02:46:54,564
Catching that intermediate step makes the second use nearly free.

1758
02:46:55,726 --> 02:46:59,172
The last bullet is why this is judgment rather than a rule.

1759
02:47:00,398 --> 02:47:04,772
Catch data occupies executor memory that shuffles also need.

1760
02:47:05,710 --> 02:47:12,484
Catching indiscriminately causes spilling to disk, which is the slowdown you were trying to prevent in the first place.

1761
02:47:13,966 --> 02:47:19,204
Troubleshooting transformation failures is a named skill, and the table is the lookup.

1762
02:47:19,950 --> 02:47:25,060
The last row deserves the attention because it is a different category from the rest.

1763
02:47:26,094 --> 02:47:29,604
Every other row describes a job that is badly configured.

1764
02:47:30,510 --> 02:47:36,004
That row describes a job that is fine and has met data it did not expect.

1765
02:47:36,368 --> 02:47:41,988
A field that has always been an integer arises as a string or a new nested field appears.

1766
02:47:44,046 --> 02:47:46,368
Spark data frames fail hard on that.

1767
02:47:47,086 --> 02:47:53,956
Glue dynamic frames survive it and let you resolve the conflict deliberately, which is precisely why dynamic frames exist.

1768
02:47:54,830 --> 02:48:01,316
So a question about intermittent failures correlated with source changes has a schema answer, not a capacity answer.

1769
02:48:03,109 --> 02:48:10,180
SKU earns its own slide because it is the one performance problem where adding resources is provably useless.

1770
02:48:10,608 --> 02:48:14,788
The exam knows that adding resources is everyone's instinct.

1771
02:48:15,342 --> 02:48:17,124
The mechanism is simple.

1772
02:48:18,926 --> 02:48:21,092
Spark divides work by key.

1773
02:48:22,030 --> 02:48:30,128
If one key holds 40% of the rows, one task does 40% of the work, no matter how many executors exist.

1774
02:48:30,990 --> 02:48:37,092
Sorting spreads that key artificially by appending a random number during the aggregation in two stages.

1775
02:48:37,888 --> 02:48:44,932
Broadcasting works when one side of a join is small enough to copy to every executor, which removes the shuffle that SKU depends on.

1776
02:48:46,638 --> 02:48:52,324
An AWS Glue job joins a transactions table to a customer table and aggregates by customer.

1777
02:48:52,750 --> 02:48:55,140
It runs on 20 G2X workers.

1778
02:48:56,494 --> 02:48:59,556
95% of tasks finish within 4 minutes.

1779
02:49:00,208 --> 02:49:05,924
Two tasks run for over an hour, and the job's total runtime is dominated by them.

1780
02:49:06,830 --> 02:49:14,564
Investigation shows that two customer identifiers account for a large share of all transactions because they are internal test accounts.

1781
02:49:14,928 --> 02:49:18,532
The customer table itself is small, around 40 megabytes.

1782
02:49:19,086 --> 02:49:21,156
Which change will most improve the runtime?

1783
02:49:23,310 --> 02:49:25,828
Increase the worker count from 20 to 60.

1784
02:49:27,918 --> 02:49:33,380
filter out the two internal test accounts and broadcast the small customer table in the join.

1785
02:49:33,902 --> 02:49:34,208
C.

1786
02:49:34,638 --> 02:49:37,828
Change the worker type from G2X to G8X.

1787
02:49:39,534 --> 02:49:39,808
D.

1788
02:49:40,622 --> 02:49:44,288
Enable blue auto scaling with a maximum of 60 workers.

1789
02:49:45,358 --> 02:49:46,468
Pause and decide.

1790
02:49:52,974 --> 02:49:54,276
The answer is B.

1791
02:49:54,638 --> 02:50:02,852
The distribution described in the question is the definition of skew, and three of the four options offer more capacity in slightly different clothing.

1792
02:50:04,974 --> 02:50:06,980
That repetition is deliberate.

1793
02:50:08,206 --> 02:50:12,064
The exam is checking whether you reach for capacity reflexively.

1794
02:50:12,590 --> 02:50:22,308
Option B is strong for two independent reasons. The test accounts are not real data, so filtering them removes the skew at source rather than working around it.

1795
02:50:22,702 --> 02:50:30,340
And a 40 MB table is small enough to broadcast, which eliminates the shuffle that made the join expensive in the first place.

1796
02:50:32,078 --> 02:50:33,796
Creating and consuming data.

1797
02:50:34,158 --> 02:50:39,620
APs is a named skill in two separate task statements, so it is worth the slide.

1798
02:50:41,806 --> 02:50:42,948
The Redshift Data.

1799
02:50:44,334 --> 02:50:47,300
API is the piece most worth understanding.

1800
02:50:48,046 --> 02:50:52,772
A traditional database connection is long-lived and a lambda function is not.

1801
02:50:53,678 --> 02:50:59,748
So functions connecting to Redshift the normal way exhaust connection limits and waste time reconnecting.

1802
02:51:00,430 --> 02:51:01,124
The data.

1803
02:51:03,406 --> 02:51:06,084
API removes connections entirely.

1804
02:51:06,384 --> 02:51:12,900
You submit a statement over HTTPS, get an identifier, and collect the result when it is ready.

1805
02:51:13,742 --> 02:51:16,036
So a question about a serverless application.

1806
02:51:16,304 --> 02:51:18,916
Querying Redshift has a specific right answer.

1807
02:51:19,598 --> 02:51:23,344
A JDBC connection from Lambda is the plausible wrong one.

1808
02:51:24,624 --> 02:51:34,244
Creating a data API is one skill and maintaining it is named separately. So expect both. Throttling shows up most often and the reason is the failure it prevents.

1809
02:51:35,086 --> 02:51:36,100
And analytics.

1810
02:51:36,942 --> 02:51:40,544
API sits in front of a warehouse with finite capacity.

1811
02:51:41,230 --> 02:51:47,044
One team writing a loop that caused it a thousand times a second can degrade that warehouse for everybody else.

1812
02:51:47,504 --> 02:51:53,316
Usage plans cap each consumer independently, so a badly behaved caller damages only itself.

1813
02:51:55,566 --> 02:51:57,604
Catching is the other high value item.

1814
02:51:58,574 --> 02:52:08,484
Repeated identical queries against slowly changing data are extremely common, and answering them from a cache removes the load entirely rather than merely limiting it.

1815
02:52:10,062 --> 02:52:16,516
A company exposes an internal API that lets other teams request aggregated sales figures.

1816
02:52:16,942 --> 02:52:29,732
The API is backed by AWS Lambda functions that query Amazon Redshift. Each invocation opens a JDBC connection at the start. Under load, teams report intermittent failures and slow responses.

1817
02:52:30,350 --> 02:52:38,596
CloudWatch shows Lambda invocations timing out while waiting to connect and Redshift reporting that the connection limit has been reached.

1818
02:52:39,118 --> 02:52:41,252
What should the data engineer change?

1819
02:52:41,584 --> 02:52:46,788
a increase the lambda function timeout and add retrees with exponential backoff.

1820
02:52:49,294 --> 02:52:55,184
Replace the JDBC connections with calls to the Amazon Redshift data API.

1821
02:52:55,504 --> 02:52:55,824
C.

1822
02:52:56,384 --> 02:53:00,224
Increase the Redshift cluster node count to raise the connection limit.

1823
02:53:00,544 --> 02:53:00,864
D.

1824
02:53:01,264 --> 02:53:05,104
Move the API from Lambda to containers on Amazon.

1825
02:53:05,454 --> 02:53:08,144
ECS so connections can be pulled.

1826
02:53:08,814 --> 02:53:10,052
Pause and decide.

1827
02:53:16,718 --> 02:53:17,796
The answer is B.

1828
02:53:18,158 --> 02:53:29,316
Option B is the interesting one because it is a real engineering solution and it appears in production systems. Connection pooling is exactly what containers give you that Lambda does not.

1829
02:53:30,094 --> 02:53:37,984
It loses here because it changes the architecture to suit a driver when a managed API designed for this situation exists.

1830
02:53:38,224 --> 02:53:46,948
Notice the pattern across this scenario and the last one. In both, three options offer more of something, time, capacity, or infrastructure.

1831
02:53:47,824 --> 02:53:52,388
One option addresses a mismatch between the tool and the shape of the problem.

1832
02:53:52,784 --> 02:53:56,196
That is a recurring structure worth recognizing.

1833
02:53:57,358 --> 02:54:05,504
Skill 1.2.10 is integrating large language models for data processing, and it did not exist before December 2025.

1834
02:54:05,742 --> 02:54:09,444
No older question bank covers it, and most courses do not either.

1835
02:54:09,870 --> 02:54:12,452
The concept is straightforward once framed correctly.

1836
02:54:13,264 --> 02:54:17,732
Every engine so far in this module does arithmetic and set operations.

1837
02:54:17,998 --> 02:54:23,204
None of them can lead a customer complaint and decide whether it is about billing or delivery.

1838
02:54:23,504 --> 02:54:28,068
None can pull a total from an invoice laid out differently by every supplier.

1839
02:54:28,846 --> 02:54:34,564
Those are judgments about meaning, and until recently, a data pipeline had to send them to a human.

1840
02:54:36,110 --> 02:54:40,164
The callout is the framing that makes this examinable rather than vague.

1841
02:54:40,368 --> 02:54:44,292
A language model in a data pipeline is almost always a converter.

1842
02:54:45,070 --> 02:54:54,244
It sits at the front, takes something no SQL engine can handle, produces structured output that the rest of the pipeline treats as ordinary data.

1843
02:54:55,182 --> 02:55:01,572
Once a review has become a sentiment score and three topic tags, Redshift can aggregate it like any other column.

1844
02:55:03,118 --> 02:55:06,404
So, the exam question is rarely about the model.

1845
02:55:07,086 --> 02:55:12,964
It is about recognizing that a stage is needed to structure the input and knowing which service provides it.

1846
02:55:14,638 --> 02:55:17,604
Six services and the boundaries are clearer than they look.

1847
02:55:18,094 --> 02:55:23,140
Texttrand does optical character recognition and structure detection, so it reads the page.

1848
02:55:25,710 --> 02:55:32,580
Comprehend does fixed categories of language analysis, entities, sentiment, and personally identifiable information.

1849
02:55:33,582 --> 02:55:39,408
Bedrock is general purpose where you write a prompt describing what you want extracted.

1850
02:55:39,822 --> 02:55:45,188
Bedrock data automation is the newest and most exam relevant.

1851
02:55:45,678 --> 02:55:52,900
That is because it does the whole intelligent document processing job through one API across four modalities.

1852
02:55:53,678 --> 02:56:01,252
The pattern of chaining Textract into a bedrock prompt still works, and data automation is the managed answer that replaces it.

1853
02:56:02,894 --> 02:56:07,300
This distinction decides which of two plausible options is right.

1854
02:56:07,726 --> 02:56:15,044
Comprehend detects entities from predefined categories people, places, organizations, dates.

1855
02:56:15,950 --> 02:56:20,740
If that is what the question needs, comprehend is cheaper, faster, and repeatable.

1856
02:56:21,678 --> 02:56:24,740
Then the requirement names field specific to the business.

1857
02:56:25,358 --> 02:56:28,868
Warranty period, policy number, delivery promise.

1858
02:56:30,286 --> 02:56:34,916
No predefined category covers those, so a generative model is the answer.

1859
02:56:35,694 --> 02:56:37,860
The determinism point matters too.

1860
02:56:38,574 --> 02:56:42,340
Comprehend given the same document twice returns the same answer.

1861
02:56:43,246 --> 02:56:48,388
A generative model may not, which is a genuine consideration for a pipeline that must reconcile.

1862
02:56:49,774 --> 02:56:56,004
This is where the streaming lesson from module 1 returns in a new form. Ask who is waiting.

1863
02:56:56,302 --> 02:57:01,604
A model call inside a live application must be interactive so it pays the on-demand rate.

1864
02:57:02,446 --> 02:57:11,748
A nightly job classifying yesterday's support tickets has nobody waiting, which makes it exactly what batch inference is for, at roughly half the cost.

1865
02:57:12,622 --> 02:57:17,648
The last point is a straightforward efficiency argument that questions do test.

1866
02:57:18,208 --> 02:57:20,900
Model invocation is priced per document.

1867
02:57:21,774 --> 02:57:30,788
So filtering the dataset down before invoking the model is far cheaper than invoking it on everything and discarding results afterwards.

1868
02:57:32,462 --> 02:57:39,748
This slide exists because the exam will offer a language model where one is not needed and the option will sound modern and impressive.

1869
02:57:40,622 --> 02:57:44,516
Passing a consistently formatted log line does not need a model.

1870
02:57:45,070 --> 02:57:48,516
Extracting a date from a fixed field does not need a model.

1871
02:57:49,486 --> 02:57:57,124
Those are pattern matching problems and a model is slower, more expensive, and less reliable at them than a regular expression.

1872
02:57:57,488 --> 02:58:01,540
The determinism point is the one with real engineering consequences.

1873
02:58:03,822 --> 02:58:13,328
If a pipeline must produce the same answer on a rerun, that any auditable pipeline must non-deterministic output is a design problem

1874
02:58:13,488 --> 02:58:15,332
you have to solve deliberately.

1875
02:58:16,942 --> 02:58:22,820
An insurance company receives around 12,000 scanned claim documents each night as PDFs.

1876
02:58:23,008 --> 02:58:24,848
Layouts differ by broker.

1877
02:58:25,088 --> 02:58:34,244
The company needs six fields from each document, including the policy number, incident date, and claim amount loaded into Amazon Redshift by 8 in the morning.

1878
02:58:35,054 --> 02:58:42,020
A previous attempt using a rules-based parser reached about 60% accuracy because the layouts vary too much.

1879
02:58:42,606 --> 02:58:44,356
Results are needed by morning.

1880
02:58:45,198 --> 02:58:47,620
Nobody is waiting on an individual document.

1881
02:58:48,302 --> 02:58:50,660
Which approach best meets the requirements?

1882
02:58:53,806 --> 02:58:58,404
An AWS Blue Job with regular expressions tuned for each broker's layout.

1883
02:58:59,182 --> 02:59:06,692
b Amazon Bedrock data automation in a nightly pipeline using batch processing, loading results into Redshift.

1884
02:59:07,534 --> 02:59:11,556
C. Amazon comprehend to detect entities in the extracted text.

1885
02:59:12,368 --> 02:59:12,688
D

1886
02:59:13,870 --> 02:59:18,884
Real time Amazon bedrock invocation per document as each one arrives.

1887
02:59:19,630 --> 02:59:20,932
Pause and decide.

1888
02:59:27,630 --> 02:59:32,352
The answer is B, and each wrong option fails on a different one of the three planted details.

1889
02:59:32,512 --> 02:59:37,412
Option A fails on varying layouts, which is stated as the reason the previous attempt failed.

1890
02:59:37,712 --> 02:59:43,850
Option C fails on business specific fields because policy number is not a category. Comprehend knows.

1891
02:59:45,038 --> 02:59:51,460
Option D fails on economics because nobody is waiting and batch inference costs roughly half.

1892
02:59:52,334 --> 02:59:58,212
That structure, where each distractor is killed by a different sentence, is common on well-written questions.

1893
02:59:59,406 --> 03:00:03,012
It rewards reading the situation carefully before looking at the options.

1894
03:00:04,718 --> 03:00:16,580
The call out is the principle underneath all five rows. Every genuine cost optimization on this exam reduces the amount of work performed rather than negotiating a lower price for the same work.

1895
03:00:16,942 --> 03:00:18,788
Bookmarks stop reprocessing.

1896
03:00:19,072 --> 03:00:21,392
Push down predicates stop reading.

1897
03:00:21,678 --> 03:00:23,428
Partitioning, stop scanning.

1898
03:00:24,334 --> 03:00:26,884
Transient clusters stop paying for idle time.

1899
03:00:27,886 --> 03:00:31,716
Filtering before a model call stops paying for documents you discard.

1900
03:00:32,718 --> 03:00:37,572
When you see a cost question, ask what work is being done that does not need to be.

1901
03:00:38,350 --> 03:00:41,636
Do that before you look at instance types or pricing modes.

1902
03:00:43,342 --> 03:00:46,404
A data platform team runs four workloads.

1903
03:00:47,118 --> 03:00:50,244
A nightly Spark ETL job with no deadline.

1904
03:00:51,118 --> 03:00:53,732
An hourly conversion of one small file.

1905
03:00:54,112 --> 03:00:57,444
A daily report joining three curated LEDshift tables.

1906
03:00:58,510 --> 03:01:03,396
A monthly classification of 20,000 free tech survey responses into six themes.

1907
03:01:04,270 --> 03:01:11,076
The team has been asked to reduce cost across all four without reducing scope or delaying anything beyond its current window.

1908
03:01:11,472 --> 03:01:13,892
Which set of changes is most appropriate.

1909
03:01:15,918 --> 03:01:24,964
A. Move all four workloads to Amazon, EMR on EC2 with spot instances to get one consistent platform.

1910
03:01:27,438 --> 03:01:32,644
Glue with flex, a lambda function, SQL inside Redshift,

1911
03:01:33,326 --> 03:01:35,428
and Bedrock Batch Inference.

1912
03:01:35,872 --> 03:01:36,192
C.

1913
03:01:36,750 --> 03:01:41,540
Glue for all four, right sized since a single service is simplest to operate.

1914
03:01:41,872 --> 03:01:42,192
D.

1915
03:01:42,638 --> 03:01:43,072
Athena.

1916
03:01:43,632 --> 03:01:49,700
C T AS for all four since serverless SQL is the cheapest option available.

1917
03:01:50,510 --> 03:01:51,556
Pause and decide.

1918
03:01:58,286 --> 03:02:03,076
The answer is B, and this scenario closes the module deliberately.

1919
03:02:05,262 --> 03:02:09,860
Three of the four options are versions of the same instinct, which is to standardize.

1920
03:02:10,382 --> 03:02:15,300
Standardization is a genuine engineering virtue, and the exam is not against it.

1921
03:02:16,206 --> 03:02:20,196
It is against applying it where the workloads have materially different shapes.

1922
03:02:20,494 --> 03:02:24,772
Each of these four has a stated characteristic pointing at a different engine.

1923
03:02:25,262 --> 03:02:26,052
No deadline.

1924
03:02:29,390 --> 03:02:30,276
Small file.

1925
03:02:31,630 --> 03:02:33,232
Data already in place.

1926
03:02:33,552 --> 03:02:35,204
Text with no waiting reader.

1927
03:02:36,078 --> 03:02:41,732
Run the four questions from module 1 on each workload separately, and this question answers itself.

1928
03:02:43,312 --> 03:02:48,772
5 questions, and the second is the one that gains the most marks for the least effort.

1929
03:02:49,152 --> 03:02:58,052
That is because it eliminates entire pipelines rather than choosing between them. Data already in the warehouse should usually be transformed there.

1930
03:02:58,926 --> 03:03:03,364
The fourth question is new to this exam and worth adding to your habits.

1931
03:03:03,950 --> 03:03:10,660
That is because unstructured input now has a managed answer rather than being a research project.

1932
03:03:11,552 --> 03:03:19,428
Next module is orchestration, which is how all of these engines get chained together. What happens when one of them fails at 3 in the morning?

1933
03:03:20,078 --> 03:03:23,108
Why the exam tests that topic in two separate domains?

1934
03:03:24,832 --> 03:03:30,660
Orchestration is the topic. Most study plans underrate, and the reason is a quirk of the exam guide.

1935
03:03:31,022 --> 03:03:32,228
It appears twice.

1936
03:03:32,672 --> 03:03:38,660
Once in domain 1 as building workflows, and again in domain 3 as automating and troubleshooting them.

1937
03:03:39,150 --> 03:03:43,716
That makes it one of very few subjects earning marks in two separate domains.

1938
03:03:44,430 --> 03:03:51,172
In the bank analyze for this course, event bridge and step functions together appeared more often than Amazon Athena.

1939
03:03:51,822 --> 03:03:56,004
Most candidates give Athena considerably more study time than both combined.

1940
03:03:56,910 --> 03:04:01,508
This module covers the four orchestration tools and how to choose between them.

1941
03:04:02,030 --> 03:04:03,524
How work gets triggered.

1942
03:04:04,078 --> 03:04:07,332
What a resilient pipeline actually does when a step fails.

1943
03:04:08,782 --> 03:04:16,096
5 work scenarios and one service that changed in late 2025 in a way that alters a standard exam answer.

1944
03:04:17,422 --> 03:04:19,268
Anything can run steps in order.

1945
03:04:19,886 --> 03:04:21,380
A shell script can do that.

1946
03:04:21,696 --> 03:04:25,924
What an orchestrator gives you is everything around the unhappy path.

1947
03:04:26,446 --> 03:04:30,468
What happens when step 3 fails or when step 4 times out?

1948
03:04:31,374 --> 03:04:39,204
What happens when a step waits 4 hours for a file that never arrives or when the same workflow is triggered twice by accident?

1949
03:04:40,238 --> 03:04:46,896
Those are the questions the exam asks, because they are the questions that matter at 3 in the morning when nobody is watching.

1950
03:04:47,438 --> 03:04:56,356
So when you read an orchestration scenario, look first for the failure described in it. The failure usually decides the answer, not the sequence of steps.

1951
03:04:56,896 --> 03:05:03,972
Notice too that most of the scenarios in this module describe a pipeline that is running, not one that has stopped.

1952
03:05:04,718 --> 03:05:07,044
Silent wrongness is the failure mode.

1953
03:05:07,278 --> 03:05:09,028
The exam cares about most.

1954
03:05:10,574 --> 03:05:14,788
The callout separates two jobs that questions deliberately conflict.

1955
03:05:15,790 --> 03:05:17,380
Event bridge is a trigger.

1956
03:05:17,776 --> 03:05:22,340
It notices that a file landed or that it is 2 in the morning and it starts something.

1957
03:05:23,278 --> 03:05:31,332
It does not know whether that something succeeded, cannot retrieve step 3, and has no concept of a workflow with stages.

1958
03:05:32,590 --> 03:05:36,996
Step functions MWAA and Glue Workflows Coordinate.

1959
03:05:37,390 --> 03:05:43,136
They know the shape of the whole pipeline, track where it is, and decide what to do when a stage fails.

1960
03:05:43,694 --> 03:05:54,336
A very common architecture uses EventBridge to start a step function state machine, and options offering EventBridge alone for a multi-step pipeline are usually wrong.

1961
03:05:55,214 --> 03:05:58,052
The third point is the one that wins questions.

1962
03:05:58,336 --> 03:06:06,980
Without an orchestrator, retry logic lives inside each job, written slightly differently every time and invisible when it misbehaves.

1963
03:06:07,790 --> 03:06:17,060
Step functions declares it as configuration on each state with back-off and a maximum attempt count and shows you every attempt in the execution history.

1964
03:06:17,696 --> 03:06:22,532
Distributed map deserves a mention too, because it solves the specific problem well.

1965
03:06:23,278 --> 03:06:30,244
Processing a million individual S3 objects with a lambda function each would previously have needed custom fan out.

1966
03:06:30,656 --> 03:06:38,340
Distributed map does it natively with high concurrency, which makes it the answer to large-scale per object processing questions.

1967
03:06:39,918 --> 03:06:46,628
Distributed map is worth knowing by name because it answers a question that used to require a custom fan out design.

1968
03:06:47,150 --> 03:06:56,420
Processing a million individual S3 objects, each independently, was previously a matter of chaining queues and functions together and managing the concurrency yourself.

1969
03:06:57,262 --> 03:07:05,668
Distributed map reads the list of objects straight from S3 and launches child executions across them at high concurrency.

1970
03:07:07,406 --> 03:07:10,948
The two configuration points are what the exam tests.

1971
03:07:11,566 --> 03:07:19,684
Maximum concurrency exists because the children all write somewhere and a hundred thousand concurrent writers will overwhelm most targets.

1972
03:07:20,398 --> 03:07:27,556
Batching exists because launching one child execution to process a 2KB object is mostly overhead.

1973
03:07:28,782 --> 03:07:34,976
You will not be asked to write a state machine, but recognizing what each state type does lets you read an auction quickly.

1974
03:07:35,216 --> 03:07:40,292
Two of them explain why the exam favors step functions over a script that calls things in order.

1975
03:07:41,166 --> 03:07:49,444
A choice state branches on the actual data flowing through. So a pipeline can behave differently when a file is empty than when it is full.

1976
03:07:49,998 --> 03:07:53,188
A wait state pauses without consuming compute.

1977
03:07:53,934 --> 03:07:57,380
Waiting four hours for an EMR cluster costs nothing.

1978
03:07:58,254 --> 03:08:03,780
A lambda function waiting 4 hours is impossible, and an EC2 instance waiting is pure expense.

1979
03:08:05,422 --> 03:08:09,540
Map is the one to associate with per item processing over a collection.

1980
03:08:11,232 --> 03:08:16,164
This is the most examinable detail in step functions and it produces a classic bug.

1981
03:08:16,622 --> 03:08:25,220
Calling a blue job with the default request response pattern starts the job and reports success straight away because the API call itself succeeded.

1982
03:08:26,094 --> 03:08:30,192
The state machine then moves to the next step while the blue job is still running.

1983
03:08:30,352 --> 03:08:32,868
So the next step reads incomplete data.

1984
03:08:33,454 --> 03:08:37,444
The dotsync pattern is what you almost always want in a data pipeline.

1985
03:08:37,712 --> 03:08:41,732
It waits for the job to finish and fails the state if the job failed.

1986
03:08:42,606 --> 03:08:49,764
The callback pattern covers cases where something outside AWS must respond, including a human approving a step.

1987
03:08:51,438 --> 03:08:55,140
The fourth point is a design rule that appears as an exam answer.

1988
03:08:55,598 --> 03:09:00,132
State machine payloads carry a size limit measured in kilobytes.

1989
03:09:00,672 --> 03:09:08,324
A pipeline attempting to pass a data frame or a file's contents between states will fail once the data grows beyond it.

1990
03:09:09,262 --> 03:09:16,836
The correct pattern is to pass a reference instead, an S3 key or a job identifier, and let the next step read the data itself.

1991
03:09:18,350 --> 03:09:21,892
That sounds obvious and it catches people building their first pipeline.

1992
03:09:22,752 --> 03:09:31,812
The same principle applies in glue Workflows, where run properties are meant for small values like a partition date rather than for data.

1993
03:09:33,358 --> 03:09:37,072
This table connects orchestration back to module 5's engines.

1994
03:09:37,454 --> 03:09:39,332
Two rows are worth attention.

1995
03:09:40,238 --> 03:09:44,516
The EMR row explains how the transient cluster pattern actually operates.

1996
03:09:45,614 --> 03:09:54,820
That is because the state machine creates the cluster, adds the steps, waits for them, and terminates it. So nothing is running between nightly executions.

1997
03:09:55,982 --> 03:10:00,452
The redshift row reconnects to the data API from module 5.

1998
03:10:01,358 --> 03:10:05,732
A state machine cannot hold a database connection any more than a Lambda function can.

1999
03:10:06,192 --> 03:10:10,020
So the data API is the mechanism rather than an optimization.

2000
03:10:10,766 --> 03:10:17,952
And the last row is how approval steps work, which appears in questions about pipelines that need sign-off before publishing.

2001
03:10:19,182 --> 03:10:26,244
For a data engineering exam, the answer is usually standard and knowing why matters more than memorizing the table.

2002
03:10:26,830 --> 03:10:34,980
A pipeline that runs a glue job takes minutes at least, which already exceeds the 5-minute express ceiling once you add anything after it.

2003
03:10:35,470 --> 03:10:41,860
Standard also keeps a full execution history, you can inspect days later, which is what makes troubleshooting possible.

2004
03:10:42,382 --> 03:10:46,020
Express exists for very high volume, very short event processing.

2005
03:10:46,574 --> 03:10:52,100
You are handling 100,000 executions a second and the individual history does not matter.

2006
03:10:52,832 --> 03:10:58,340
If a question describes ingesting clickstream events one at a time, Express is plausible.

2007
03:10:59,726 --> 03:11:02,788
If it describes an ETL pipeline, it is not.

2008
03:11:04,032 --> 03:11:07,140
This is a genuine change to a standard exam answer.

2009
03:11:07,566 --> 03:11:13,380
For years the comparison ran that step functions is serverless while MWAA is not.

2010
03:11:13,774 --> 03:11:20,868
MWAA meant paying for a scheduler and metadata database around the clock, whether any workflow ran or not.

2011
03:11:21,294 --> 03:11:23,940
That was the deciding argument in a lot of questions.

2012
03:11:24,846 --> 03:11:30,500
MWAA serverless announced in November 2025 removes it.

2013
03:11:30,990 --> 03:11:38,404
Each task runs in an isolated container, you pay for the seconds it consumes, and there is no environment sitting idle.

2014
03:11:39,342 --> 03:11:44,196
Workflows are defined in YAML or converted from Python DAGs.

2015
03:11:45,134 --> 03:11:50,884
Older material does not mention it, so treat cost alone as a weaker argument than it used to be.

2016
03:11:52,174 --> 03:12:00,672
You are not being examined on Airflow itself, but this vocabulary appears in question wording and the last two rows produce real incidents.

2017
03:12:01,006 --> 03:12:03,152
Backfill is genuinely useful.

2018
03:12:03,392 --> 03:12:10,340
It is how you reprocess history after fixing a bug, running the workflow for each past interval as though it had run at the time.

2019
03:12:11,310 --> 03:12:14,564
Catchup is the automatic version and it is the dangerous one.

2020
03:12:14,958 --> 03:12:22,820
Enable a DAG whose start date is two years ago with catch up switched on and Airflow immediately schedules 700 runs.

2021
03:12:23,552 --> 03:12:30,436
Sensors are worth knowing because they express the wait for a file pattern that scenario 3 in this module is about.

2022
03:12:31,918 --> 03:12:36,676
Pipelines spend a lot of their life waiting, and how you wait is a real cost decision.

2023
03:12:37,006 --> 03:12:38,212
The exam tests.

2024
03:12:38,766 --> 03:12:42,884
The worst version is a lambda function pulling in a loop with a sleep.

2025
03:12:43,182 --> 03:12:47,952
You pay for every second of doing nothing and you are capped at 15 minutes anyway.

2026
03:12:48,494 --> 03:12:56,032
A classic airflow sensor is better and still holds a worker slot, so 100D AGs all waiting can exhaust the environment.

2027
03:12:56,580 --> 03:13:00,804
Deferrable operators solve that by releasing the worker while waiting.

2028
03:13:02,542 --> 03:13:03,872
Step functions.

2029
03:13:04,302 --> 03:13:08,708
Wait states are free and the last point matters everywhere.

2030
03:13:09,582 --> 03:13:13,572
A wait with no timeout becomes a pipeline that hangs silently forever.

2031
03:13:15,086 --> 03:13:24,112
Modules 2 and 4 both promise this comparison, so here it is in full. The signal is the same shape as kinesis against MSK in module 3.

2032
03:13:24,302 --> 03:13:30,752
Watch for existing Airflow DAGs, a team with Airflow expertise or a pipeline reaching outside AWS.

2033
03:13:31,022 --> 03:13:32,912
Any of those means MWAA?

2034
03:13:33,966 --> 03:13:40,388
That is because rewriting working DAGs is the unnecessary migration caused the exam marks wrong.

2035
03:13:41,070 --> 03:13:49,412
If the scenario is a new pipeline coordinating AWS services only, step functions is simpler and integrates natively.

2036
03:13:50,192 --> 03:14:00,100
The second reason for MWAA is portability, which matters to organizations that will not commit their orchestration layer to one cloud.

2037
03:14:01,774 --> 03:14:09,312
Glue workflows are the lowest overhead option available when they apply because they add no service to the architecture at all. The

2038
03:14:09,312 --> 03:14:11,588
ceiling is sharp and easy to test for.

2039
03:14:12,494 --> 03:14:18,372
The moment a pipeline includes anything that is not a blue job, crawler or trigger, they run out of road.

2040
03:14:19,310 --> 03:14:28,164
A Lambda function that sends a notification, a red shift stored procedure, a wait for a human to approve something, an EMR step.

2041
03:14:28,718 --> 03:14:32,912
Any one of those forces you up to step functions or MWAA.

2042
03:14:34,926 --> 03:14:39,524
So the reading test is simply to count the non-glue steps in the described pipeline.

2043
03:14:40,430 --> 03:14:44,420
Zero means blue workflows are viable and probably correct.

2044
03:14:46,094 --> 03:14:54,244
This is one of the newer items and it is easy to overlook because it sits inside a service most people think of as machine learning.

2045
03:14:56,078 --> 03:14:57,412
Amazon SageMaker

2046
03:14:58,862 --> 03:15:03,204
Unified Studio includes workflow capability built on Apache Airflow.

2047
03:15:04,078 --> 03:15:12,272
So a project team can define its pipelines in the same place as its data catalog, its notebooks, and its queries.

2048
03:15:15,406 --> 03:15:18,084
The framing that matters for the exam is scope.

2049
03:15:18,512 --> 03:15:25,232
It suits work owned within a single project, where the people writing the queries also own the schedule.

2050
03:15:25,838 --> 03:15:33,700
It is not positioned as the platform-wide orchestrator that step functions or a standalone MWAA environment provides.

2051
03:15:34,510 --> 03:15:37,636
Module 20 covers Unified Studio properly.

2052
03:15:38,862 --> 03:15:42,980
Module 1 introduced this work and it is worth the detail here.

2053
03:15:43,392 --> 03:15:50,468
That is because the exam guide names both setting up schedulers and setting up event triggers as separate skills.

2054
03:15:50,912 --> 03:15:53,700
The wording gives it away nearly every time.

2055
03:15:54,638 --> 03:16:00,036
Every night, hourly, on the 1st of the month at 2 in the morning are all schedules.

2056
03:16:00,846 --> 03:16:06,404
As soon as the file arrives, immediately after upload, when a new record appears, are all events.

2057
03:16:07,022 --> 03:16:11,204
The interesting questions are the ones where a schedule exists and is causing a problem.

2058
03:16:12,142 --> 03:16:19,780
That is because the fix is usually to become event-driven and the question will describe the symptom rather than naming the cure.

2059
03:16:21,392 --> 03:16:25,380
The first two rows get confused and the distinction is worth holding.

2060
03:16:25,712 --> 03:16:33,380
Event bridge rules match a pattern against events flowing through an event bus and can also carry a schedule for historical reasons.

2061
03:16:34,190 --> 03:16:42,032
Event bridge scheduler is a separate, newer capability built specifically for scheduling, supporting one-time schedules,

2062
03:16:42,192 --> 03:16:45,412
time zones, and very large numbers of individual schedules.

2063
03:16:46,382 --> 03:16:54,372
So a question about running something at a fixed time points at scheduler and a question about reacting to something that happened points at rules.

2064
03:16:55,662 --> 03:17:01,988
The S3 row matters because there are two paths from an S3 upload, direct notifications and event bridge,

2065
03:17:02,830 --> 03:17:04,452
and the next slide separates them.

2066
03:17:06,272 --> 03:17:11,812
Both start work when an object lands, and questions offer both as options.

2067
03:17:12,366 --> 03:17:20,580
S3 event notifications are configured on the bucket itself. Go to a small set of targets and filter only on prefix and suffix.

2068
03:17:21,072 --> 03:17:25,792
That covers a great many real cases and is a simpler answer whenever it fits.

2069
03:17:26,606 --> 03:17:33,636
EventBridge accepts the same events onto an event bus where any number of rules can match them independently of each other.

2070
03:17:34,112 --> 03:17:41,988
Targets include step functions directly, which matters because S3 notifications cannot start a state machine on their own.

2071
03:17:42,734 --> 03:17:46,884
The filtering can also examine any field in the event rather than just the key.

2072
03:17:48,494 --> 03:17:57,540
Event Bridge additionally supports archiving events and replaying them later, which is genuinely useful when a downstream consumer was broken and you need to reprocess a period.

2073
03:17:59,182 --> 03:18:08,260
A company runs a nightly pipeline consisting of four AWS, view jobs that must run in order, a blue crawler after the second job,

2074
03:18:08,846 --> 03:18:13,252
a final AWS Lambda function that posts a summary to a Slack channel.

2075
03:18:13,966 --> 03:18:17,380
The pipeline is currently started by a crone schedule on each job.

2076
03:18:18,318 --> 03:18:22,788
Each schedule is set far enough apart that the previous job should have finished.

2077
03:18:23,072 --> 03:18:30,564
Occasionally an early job runs long and a later job starts against incomplete data without any error being raised.

2078
03:18:31,406 --> 03:18:36,112
The team wants the pipeline to be dependency driven and to alert on failure.

2079
03:18:36,654 --> 03:18:38,532
What should the data engineer implement?

2080
03:18:39,054 --> 03:18:44,708
a move all five steps into an AWS Glue workflow with conditional triggers.

2081
03:18:45,166 --> 03:18:52,516
b Build an AWS step function state machine invoking each step in sequence started by event bridge scheduler.

2082
03:18:53,552 --> 03:18:53,712
C.

2083
03:18:54,702 --> 03:18:58,404
Increase the gaps between the cron schedules to allow more headroom.

2084
03:18:59,470 --> 03:18:59,712
D.

2085
03:19:00,622 --> 03:19:05,232
Set the maximum concurrent runs on each glue job to one.

2086
03:19:06,094 --> 03:19:07,620
Pause and decide.

2087
03:19:14,350 --> 03:19:18,052
The answer is B, and option A is the distractor.

2088
03:19:18,958 --> 03:19:20,996
This module was built to make you check.

2089
03:19:23,182 --> 03:19:30,276
Blue workflows really are the lower overhead choice for a glue-only pipeline, and four jobs plus a crawler qualifies.

2090
03:19:31,214 --> 03:19:36,036
The lambda function at the end does not, and one non-blue step is enough.

2091
03:19:36,942 --> 03:19:46,180
Option C deserves attention to because widening the gaps is what teams actually do, and it is timing-based coordination, pretending to be dependency-based.

2092
03:19:47,630 --> 03:19:53,124
It fails the moment something runs longer than you guessed, which is exactly what the question describes happening.

2093
03:19:54,862 --> 03:20:03,332
The exam guide names building pipelines for performance, availability, scalability, resiliency, and fault tolerance as a single skill.

2094
03:20:04,432 --> 03:20:06,596
That is a lot of words for one idea.

2095
03:20:06,958 --> 03:20:08,036
Failures will happen.

2096
03:20:08,558 --> 03:20:13,412
Networks drop, sources go down, data arrives malformed, quotas are hit.

2097
03:20:14,350 --> 03:20:17,700
What separates a good pipeline from a bad one is what the failure does.

2098
03:20:17,952 --> 03:20:27,460
A good one stops rather than corrupting downstream data, tells somebody keeps the fair work so it can be examined, can be rerun without duplicating anything.

2099
03:20:27,950 --> 03:20:36,676
A bad one carries on quietly with partial data, which is the worst outcome available because nobody finds out until a report is wrong.

2100
03:20:38,286 --> 03:20:40,672
The callout is the distinction questions test.

2101
03:20:44,014 --> 03:20:48,100
A network timeout might succeed on the second attempt, so it should be retried.

2102
03:20:48,782 --> 03:20:57,668
A record with a malformed date will fail identically every time, so retrying it forever is pure waste and worse, it blocks everything behind it.

2103
03:20:58,478 --> 03:21:04,804
That record belongs in a dead letter queue where it is preserved for investigation while the pipeline continues.

2104
03:21:05,646 --> 03:21:10,916
Recognizing which kind of failure a scenario describes tells you which mechanism it needs.

2105
03:21:11,534 --> 03:21:13,988
And the last point connects back to module 2.

2106
03:21:14,894 --> 03:21:21,700
Retreeze on a job that appends without overwriting turn one visible failure into silent duplicate data.

2107
03:21:23,278 --> 03:21:28,708
This appears in throttling questions across several domains, and the wrong answer is always tempting.

2108
03:21:29,166 --> 03:21:35,844
When a service returns a throttling error, the instinct is to retry more aggressively so the work still gets done.

2109
03:21:36,782 --> 03:21:40,740
That adds load to a service already telling you it has too much.

2110
03:21:41,070 --> 03:21:44,836
And in a system with many clients, it produces a synchronized retry storm.

2111
03:21:48,014 --> 03:21:50,660
Backoff spaces attempts further apart each time.

2112
03:21:51,406 --> 03:21:57,668
Jitter adds randomness so that a thousand clients failing simultaneously do not all retry at the same instant.

2113
03:21:58,176 --> 03:22:03,780
If a question offers increasing the retry count against adding back-off, back off is the answer.

2114
03:22:05,390 --> 03:22:10,788
The exam guide names using notification services to send alerts, so both of these appear regularly.

2115
03:22:11,406 --> 03:22:13,604
The distinction is what each one is carrying.

2116
03:22:14,222 --> 03:22:18,468
Amazon SNS carries news that several parties might want to know about.

2117
03:22:18,656 --> 03:22:23,012
It pushes to all of them at once with no memory of what happened afterwards.

2118
03:22:23,630 --> 03:22:26,916
If a subscriber was unavailable, it missed that message.

2119
03:22:28,974 --> 03:22:36,292
Amazon SQS carries work that exactly one consumer should perform held durably until somebody actually does it.

2120
03:22:36,736 --> 03:22:41,796
The pattern in the call out combines them and appears constantly in real architectures.

2121
03:22:42,766 --> 03:22:48,676
One SNS topic publishes an event, several SQS queues subscribe to that topic.

2122
03:22:49,056 --> 03:22:56,068
Each downstream system consumes from its own queue at its own pace without affecting any of the others.

2123
03:22:57,518 --> 03:23:02,148
The visibility timeout is the mechanic most likely to appear as a diagnosis question.

2124
03:23:02,766 --> 03:23:10,916
When a consumer takes a message, SQS hides it rather than deleting it so that if the consumer dies, the message returns for somebody else.

2125
03:23:11,534 --> 03:23:14,948
Suppose the timeout is 30 seconds and processing takes 45.

2126
03:23:15,936 --> 03:23:20,900
The message reappears while the first consumer is still working on it.

2127
03:23:22,382 --> 03:23:26,020
A second consumer picks it up and the work happens twice.

2128
03:23:27,790 --> 03:23:29,028
Nothing errors.

2129
03:23:29,870 --> 03:23:37,508
The fix is to raise the visibility timeout above the realistic processing time or to extend it while processing.

2130
03:23:38,510 --> 03:23:43,716
Questions describing intermittent duplicate processing are usually describing this.

2131
03:23:45,486 --> 03:23:51,776
A pipeline reads messages from an Amazon SQS queue with an AWS Lambda function that calls a partner.

2132
03:23:52,430 --> 03:23:54,372
API for each message.

2133
03:23:54,816 --> 03:24:00,068
The partner API is rate limited and returns throttling errors during busy periods.

2134
03:24:00,416 --> 03:24:05,380
The team has configured the Lambda function with 10 retrees and no delay between them.

2135
03:24:05,902 --> 03:24:10,308
During busy periods, the queue backs up, throttling errors increase sharply.

2136
03:24:10,816 --> 03:24:17,700
Some messages eventually exceed the maximum receive count and are lost because no dead letter queue is configured.

2137
03:24:18,286 --> 03:24:21,092
Which combination of changes best addresses the problem?

2138
03:24:23,758 --> 03:24:26,628
Increase the lambda retry count from 10 to 30.

2139
03:24:26,976 --> 03:24:34,276
b. Add exponential backoff with jitter to the AP i calls and configure a dead letter queue on the SQS queue.

2140
03:24:34,896 --> 03:24:35,216
C.

2141
03:24:35,616 --> 03:24:40,868
Increase the lambda function's reserved concurrency so more messages are processed in parallel.

2142
03:24:41,230 --> 03:24:41,536
D.

2143
03:24:42,542 --> 03:24:48,804
Switch the SQSQ from standard to FIFO to control the processing order.

2144
03:24:49,550 --> 03:24:50,564
Pause and decide.

2145
03:24:57,262 --> 03:24:59,716
The answer is B and notice the structure.

2146
03:25:00,176 --> 03:25:07,776
Options A and C both respond to a throttled service by sending it more traffic, differing only in how.

2147
03:25:08,718 --> 03:25:14,468
That is the exam testing whether throttling reads to you as a capacity problem, which it is not.

2148
03:25:14,926 --> 03:25:16,420
It is a pacing problem.

2149
03:25:17,358 --> 03:25:20,004
The second half of B matters as much as the first.

2150
03:25:20,846 --> 03:25:30,532
Backoff stops the messages failing so often, and the dead letter queue means that a message which still fails is kept rather than silently lost.

2151
03:25:31,502 --> 03:25:39,428
The question told you explicitly that messages are being lost, which is a data loss problem hiding inside a performance question.

2152
03:25:41,102 --> 03:25:48,164
The fourth point is worth dwelling on because it catches experienced teams as well as inexperienced ones.

2153
03:25:48,750 --> 03:25:53,700
Every monitoring setup alerts on failures, far fewer alert on absence.

2154
03:25:54,510 --> 03:26:02,576
If a scheduled job simply never fires, that is because a trigger was disabled during a deployment or an upstream dependency

2155
03:26:02,766 --> 03:26:09,444
silently disappeared. There is no error to catch, there is no failed execution to see and no notification to receive.

2156
03:26:09,776 --> 03:26:15,268
The data just stops being fresh and somebody notices days later when a number looks wrong.

2157
03:26:16,078 --> 03:26:24,260
Alerting when a job has not succeeded within an expected window catches that, and module 14 covers how to implement it in CloudWatch.

2158
03:26:27,534 --> 03:26:29,700
The first point is the philosophical one.

2159
03:26:30,190 --> 03:26:33,924
Stopping is almost always better than continuing on partial data.

2160
03:26:35,566 --> 03:26:43,908
Backfilling is one of those things nobody designs for and everybody eventually needs, usually urgently after a bug is found.

2161
03:26:45,294 --> 03:26:47,300
The first two points are the whole design.

2162
03:26:48,238 --> 03:26:56,004
A pipeline that computes yesterday's date internally can only ever process yesterday, so recovering a week of bad data means editing code under pressure.

2163
03:26:56,526 --> 03:27:00,420
A pipeline taking the date as a parameter can be pointed at any day.

2164
03:27:01,166 --> 03:27:07,236
Combine that with overwriting the target partition from module 2 and reruns become safe and repeatable.

2165
03:27:08,014 --> 03:27:09,508
The last point is practical.

2166
03:27:10,126 --> 03:27:15,088
Backfilling 90 days by launching 90 concurrent runs will throttle whatever they write to.

2167
03:27:16,462 --> 03:27:19,844
A daily pipeline loads sales data into Amazon Redshift.

2168
03:27:20,110 --> 03:27:26,928
If the source file has not arrived by 6 in the morning, the pipeline currently proceeds anyway with whatever partial data is present.

2169
03:27:27,278 --> 03:27:30,180
The daily report is produced from incomplete figures.

2170
03:27:30,990 --> 03:27:36,356
Finance has twice made decisions on incorrect numbers without knowing they were incomplete.

2171
03:27:36,688 --> 03:27:41,124
The team wants the pipeline to handle a late or missing file correctly.

2172
03:27:42,208 --> 03:27:44,196
What is the most appropriate design?

2173
03:27:44,814 --> 03:27:49,732
a run the pipeline later in the morning so the file is more likely to have arrived.

2174
03:27:53,294 --> 03:28:00,388
Make the pipeline event driven on file arrival with a step functions wait and an alert if the file has not arrived by a deadline.

2175
03:28:00,928 --> 03:28:06,148
C. Load the partial data but add a note to the report saying it may be incomplete.

2176
03:28:06,688 --> 03:28:06,928
D.

2177
03:28:07,566 --> 03:28:11,844
Retry the load three times with exponential backoff before continuing.

2178
03:28:13,248 --> 03:28:14,372
Pause and decide.

2179
03:28:20,782 --> 03:28:22,208
The answer is B.

2180
03:28:22,830 --> 03:28:25,700
And this scenario pairs with the previous one deliberately.

2181
03:28:26,478 --> 03:28:34,980
There, exponential backoff was the correct answer because the partner AP, I was temporarily refusing requests and would recover.

2182
03:28:35,534 --> 03:28:37,348
Here, nothing has failed at all.

2183
03:28:38,318 --> 03:28:45,444
The file simply does not exist yet, and no amount of retrying a load causes an upstream system to produce it sooner.

2184
03:28:46,190 --> 03:28:49,252
Recognizing that distinction is the transferable skill.

2185
03:28:50,062 --> 03:28:59,204
Option A is the pattern from scenario 1 appearing again, guessing at timing rather than reacting to reality, and it is wrong for the same reason.

2186
03:29:00,910 --> 03:29:05,892
The fourth point is the one that produces both production incidents and exam questions.

2187
03:29:06,606 --> 03:29:09,540
Parallelism is not free at the system level.

2188
03:29:09,838 --> 03:29:11,716
It just relocates the bottleneck.

2189
03:29:13,454 --> 03:29:20,448
Fan out a step functions map to a thousand concurrent lambda functions, each opening a database connection.

2190
03:29:20,928 --> 03:29:25,956
You will exhaust the database's connection limit long before lambda runs out of capacity.

2191
03:29:26,368 --> 03:29:28,608
Nothing about lambda was the constraint.

2192
03:29:28,910 --> 03:29:31,684
Reserve concurrency is the deliberate answer.

2193
03:29:32,558 --> 03:29:41,508
Capping a function at say 50 concurrent executions protects the database behind it at the cost of the work taking longer overall.

2194
03:29:41,968 --> 03:29:46,128
That trade is usually correct when the dependency is shared with anything else.

2195
03:29:46,368 --> 03:29:50,436
It is exactly the shape of the redshift connection scenario from module 5.

2196
03:29:52,014 --> 03:29:58,756
Disaster recovery for data pipelines differs from disaster recovery for applications, and the callout explains why.

2197
03:29:59,246 --> 03:30:04,804
An application's database is the truth, so it must be replicated or you lose it.

2198
03:30:06,766 --> 03:30:09,892
A curated analytics table is derived.

2199
03:30:10,510 --> 03:30:16,740
So it does not have to be replicated at all, provided you retain the raw inputs and the code that produced them.

2200
03:30:17,390 --> 03:30:19,012
That changes what you protect.

2201
03:30:19,248 --> 03:30:27,428
The raw landing zone in Amazon S3 becomes the thing that genuinely needs cross-region replication, and everything downstream is something you rebuild.

2202
03:30:28,302 --> 03:30:35,172
Defining pipelines as code, which module 7 covers, is what makes rebuilding realistic rather than theoretical.

2203
03:30:36,014 --> 03:30:38,528
The first two bullets set the boundary of what you own.

2204
03:30:39,160 --> 03:30:42,148
Managed services already span availability zones.

2205
03:30:43,054 --> 03:30:44,868
An EMR cluster

2206
03:30:45,614 --> 03:30:47,716
or an MSK cluster

2207
03:30:49,168 --> 03:30:53,188
sits in your subnets, so its resilience is your design decision.

2208
03:30:54,894 --> 03:31:01,220
Larger organizations separate accounts by team or by environment and the exam reflects that.

2209
03:31:01,966 --> 03:31:06,180
The workable pattern is central coordination with distributed execution.

2210
03:31:06,574 --> 03:31:11,108
One account holds the state machines that describe what happens in what order.

2211
03:31:12,014 --> 03:31:16,996
Each step assumes a role in whichever account actually owns the data and the compute.

2212
03:31:17,328 --> 03:31:22,020
That keeps the pipeline definition in one place, which is what a platform team wants.

2213
03:31:22,702 --> 03:31:27,588
Meanwhile, the processing stays next to the data, which is what performance and cost want.

2214
03:31:28,398 --> 03:31:37,284
Event bridge is the equivalent mechanism for events because a bus in one account can forward matching events to a bus in another.

2215
03:31:38,542 --> 03:31:42,980
Apply the billing unit rule from module 1 and the pattern here is clear.

2216
03:31:44,846 --> 03:31:45,956
Step functions.

2217
03:31:47,598 --> 03:31:54,884
Standard charges per state transition, so a pipeline with a dozen steps running once nightly costs a genuinely trivial amount.

2218
03:31:55,470 --> 03:32:01,168
That is why cost is almost never the argument against step functions in an exam question.

2219
03:32:01,820 --> 03:32:09,924
Provisioned MWAA is the outlier on this table, billing continuously for an environment whether any DAG runs or not.

2220
03:32:10,734 --> 03:32:16,548
That was the standard exam argument against it until serverless arrived in late 2025.

2221
03:32:16,928 --> 03:32:20,388
The DU workflows row is worth remembering separately.

2222
03:32:22,190 --> 03:32:29,728
Adding an orchestration layer that costs nothing extra at all is a real advantage when the pipeline happens to be entirely GU components.

2223
03:32:30,414 --> 03:32:32,996
It is easy to forget as an option.

2224
03:32:34,702 --> 03:32:43,524
An orchestration team runs about 40 Apache Airflow DAGs on a self-managed Airflow deployment on Amazon EC2.

2225
03:32:45,006 --> 03:32:50,688
The DAGs use a mix of AWS operators and operators for two on-premises systems.

2226
03:32:51,150 --> 03:32:53,668
Roughly half the DAGs run once a week.

2227
03:32:54,414 --> 03:32:57,028
The rest run daily and complete in minutes.

2228
03:32:57,966 --> 03:33:04,516
The team spends significant time patching airflow, upgrading the metadata database, and recovering the scheduler.

2229
03:33:04,910 --> 03:33:09,636
They want to reduce that work and stop paying for capacity that is idle most of the time.

2230
03:33:10,254 --> 03:33:12,036
The DAGs must keep running.

2231
03:33:13,326 --> 03:33:14,564
What should the team do?

2232
03:33:16,654 --> 03:33:20,900
rewrite the DAGs as AWS Step Functions state machines.

2233
03:33:21,488 --> 03:33:28,100
b migrate to Amazon MWAA serverless, keeping the existing airflow workflows.

2234
03:33:28,768 --> 03:33:34,660
C. Migrate to a provisioned Amazon MWAA environment sized for peak load.

2235
03:33:35,278 --> 03:33:42,208
D. Move the Airflow deployment to containers on Amazon EKSS with cluster autoscaling.

2236
03:33:43,648 --> 03:33:44,900
Pause and decide.

2237
03:33:51,248 --> 03:33:57,156
The answer is B, and this question could not have been asked before November 2025.

2238
03:33:57,568 --> 03:33:59,168
Provision MWAA

2239
03:34:00,128 --> 03:34:02,180
would have been the best available answer.

2240
03:34:02,688 --> 03:34:10,244
It only satisfies one of the two stated requirements because the environment builds continuously whether DAGs run or not.

2241
03:34:11,054 --> 03:34:14,692
MW AA serverless satisfies both.

2242
03:34:16,174 --> 03:34:28,196
Note the detail about on-premises operators, which is doing quiet work. It reinforces that this must stay airflow. Since the airflow operator ecosystem reaches systems, step functions cannot.

2243
03:34:29,070 --> 03:34:34,308
That is the same signal as existing Kafka pointing at MSK in module 3.

2244
03:34:35,886 --> 03:34:39,460
The callout is a design point that questions test directly.

2245
03:34:40,302 --> 03:34:47,684
Putting the alerting step inside the workflow as a final state that sends a notification works perfectly when the workflow completes.

2246
03:34:48,238 --> 03:34:55,568
It does nothing at all when the workflow fails halfway or never starts because the notification step is simply never reached.

2247
03:34:55,808 --> 03:34:58,500
Reacting from outside catches those cases.

2248
03:34:59,008 --> 03:35:05,956
An event bridge rule, watching for a failed execution state change, fires regardless of where in the pipeline things went wrong.

2249
03:35:06,448 --> 03:35:12,004
Combine that with the absence alarm on the last bullet, and you cover both categories of failure.

2250
03:35:12,368 --> 03:35:16,528
The pipeline that broke and the pipeline that quietly stopped existing.

2251
03:35:17,966 --> 03:35:19,044
Step functions.

2252
03:35:19,648 --> 03:35:22,500
Execution history is the other thing worth knowing.

2253
03:35:23,342 --> 03:35:29,924
That is because it retains the input and output of every state, which is what makes a failure diagnosable days later.

2254
03:35:31,662 --> 03:35:39,812
Once a pipeline passes a few dozen steps, a single definition becomes unreadable and every team editing it confits with every other team.

2255
03:35:40,430 --> 03:35:44,292
Nesting solves the same problem that functions solve in ordinary code.

2256
03:35:45,198 --> 03:35:53,956
A parent state machine coordinates the overall sequence, and each stage is a child state machine owned by whichever team is responsible for that stage.

2257
03:35:54,288 --> 03:36:02,436
The parent waits for each child using the run-a job pattern from earlier in this module, so failure in a child propagates properly.

2258
03:36:03,328 --> 03:36:13,156
When a question describes several teams contributing stages to one pipeline, composition is usually the intended answer rather than one enormous shared definition.

2259
03:36:13,838 --> 03:36:16,196
The warning on the last bullet is real.

2260
03:36:16,974 --> 03:36:23,876
Two workflows that each wait for the other will both wait forever, and the symptom looks like a hang rather than an error.

2261
03:36:25,134 --> 03:36:33,028
This table collects the failure modes from across the module into one lookup, and the last row is worth reconnecting to module 2.

2262
03:36:33,582 --> 03:36:37,028
A glue jobs maximum concurrent runs defaults to 1.

2263
03:36:37,838 --> 03:36:46,788
Trigger it once per file, have two files land within the same minute, and the second trigger is rejected outright rather than queued behind the first.

2264
03:36:47,566 --> 03:36:54,052
No error appears anywhere in the pipeline because from the trigger's point of view, it did exactly what it was asked to

2265
03:36:54,798 --> 03:36:58,020
do. The file is simply never processed and nothing says so.

2266
03:36:58,574 --> 03:37:06,564
That combination of event-driven triggering and an untouched concurrency default is a genuinely common production bug and a fair exam question.

2267
03:37:07,086 --> 03:37:11,428
The first row is the one this module opened with, and it is the most common of all.

2268
03:37:12,910 --> 03:37:18,660
A step function's state machine processes files by invoking a lambda function per file.

2269
03:37:19,022 --> 03:37:20,964
It has run reliably for a year.

2270
03:37:21,582 --> 03:37:27,300
Last week, the upstream system began sending a small number of files with a malformed header.

2271
03:37:27,854 --> 03:37:31,408
The state machine now stops on the first malformed file.

2272
03:37:31,918 --> 03:37:38,688
Every valid file behind it in that execution is left unprocessed, and the team discovers the problem the following morning.

2273
03:37:38,928 --> 03:37:42,208
Retrees are configured with three attempts and back off.

2274
03:37:42,830 --> 03:37:44,580
What is the most appropriate change?

2275
03:37:44,942 --> 03:37:49,732
a Increase the retree attempts from 3 to 10 so the file eventually succeeds.

2276
03:37:50,608 --> 03:37:57,316
b Add a catch on the state that routes failed files to a dead letter location and continues processing the rest.

2277
03:37:58,208 --> 03:37:58,528
c.

2278
03:37:59,118 --> 03:38:02,532
Remove the retry configuration so the state machine fails faster.

2279
03:38:04,494 --> 03:38:08,292
D switch the state machine from standard to express workflows.

2280
03:38:09,102 --> 03:38:10,180
Pause and decide.

2281
03:38:16,814 --> 03:38:21,188
The answer is B and the phrase that decides it is malformed header.

2282
03:38:21,518 --> 03:38:27,908
That is a permanent property of the file, not a transient condition, so no number of retrees changes the outcome.

2283
03:38:28,160 --> 03:38:33,508
The mechanism slide earlier made exactly this distinction and this scenario tests it.

2284
03:38:34,318 --> 03:38:36,100
Notice the second harm in the question,

2285
03:38:37,006 --> 03:38:40,484
which is that valid files behind the bad one go unprocessed.

2286
03:38:41,262 --> 03:38:46,980
Catch does not just handle the bad file, it stops one bad record from holding up everything behind it.

2287
03:38:47,690 --> 03:38:52,996
That blocking behavior is the same shape as the Lambda Poison Record problem from module 3.

2288
03:38:54,720 --> 03:39:02,404
5 questions, and the fourth is the one that separates two otherwise identical options more often than any other in this module.

2289
03:39:02,800 --> 03:39:05,600
Transient failures want retry with back-off.

2290
03:39:06,160 --> 03:39:09,796
Permanent failures want routing to somewhere they can be inspected.

2291
03:39:10,670 --> 03:39:14,596
Getting that backwards produces either infinite waste or lost data.

2292
03:39:15,040 --> 03:39:18,212
The fifth is the judgment the exam rewards most.

2293
03:39:18,510 --> 03:39:25,188
A pipeline that fails visibly is a nuisance, and a pipeline that succeeds on partial data is a business problem.

2294
03:39:26,126 --> 03:39:31,204
When an option preserves correctness at the cost of a delay, it is usually the intended answer.

2295
03:39:32,526 --> 03:39:39,076
Next module closes. Domain 1 with programming concepts, lambda tuning, and infrastructure as code.

2296
03:39:40,654 --> 03:39:46,596
This module closes domain 1 and it covers 13 separate skills more than any other in the course.

2297
03:39:48,718 --> 03:39:56,164
That sounds alarming and it is not because several of them are described in the exam guide with the word describe rather than implement.

2298
03:39:57,134 --> 03:40:02,116
You are asked to recognize what distributed computing means, not to write a scheduler.

2299
03:40:02,830 --> 03:40:11,044
What the exam does test properly is Lambda configuration, infrastructure as code, and how pipeline code reaches production.

2300
03:40:11,406 --> 03:40:13,668
Those three carry most of the marks here.

2301
03:40:14,574 --> 03:40:20,356
Four worked scenarios and one important correction about which developer tools are actually in scope.

2302
03:40:21,902 --> 03:40:26,404
The distinction worth holding is between writing code and engineering it.

2303
03:40:26,800 --> 03:40:30,084
Nobody will ask you to produce Python on this exam.

2304
03:40:30,560 --> 03:40:34,500
There is no code editor and no syntax question anywhere in it.

2305
03:40:35,470 --> 03:40:43,076
What you are asked is whether pipeline code is version controlled, tested, deployed repeatedly, and monitored.

2306
03:40:44,046 --> 03:40:50,756
A pipeline is production software even when it is 300 lines of PySpark that one person wrote in an afternoon.

2307
03:40:51,886 --> 03:40:59,620
That framing explains why infrastructure as code and continuous delivery appear in a data exam at all, which surprises people.

2308
03:41:00,526 --> 03:41:05,572
A transformation that exists only because somebody kicked through a console once is a liability.

2309
03:41:05,840 --> 03:41:11,840
It cannot be reviewed, cannot be rebuilt in another region, and cannot be explained after that person leaves.

2310
03:41:12,160 --> 03:41:16,516
The exam treats it as a liability and marks the options that remove it.

2311
03:41:17,966 --> 03:41:21,764
The call out is the practical advice for how to spend your time here.

2312
03:41:22,254 --> 03:41:28,004
Language specific syntax is explicitly listed as out of scope for the target candidate.

2313
03:41:28,430 --> 03:41:32,868
So nobody is testing whether you remember a method name or an import path.

2314
03:41:33,326 --> 03:41:36,964
SQL is the exception and it is treated differently.

2315
03:41:38,030 --> 03:41:43,120
The exam describes queries in words and expects you to reason about what they cost and why.

2316
03:41:43,502 --> 03:41:51,332
Joints, grouping, window functions for rolling averages, common table expressions, and pivoting are all named in the guide by name.

2317
03:41:52,174 --> 03:41:58,212
Scala is worth recognizing because it appears as an option when creating view and EMR jobs.

2318
03:41:58,560 --> 03:42:04,612
Choosing it over Python is occasionally the right answer when a team already has Scala Spark code.

2319
03:42:05,920 --> 03:42:11,076
The exam guide asks you to define distributed computing rather than implement one.

2320
03:42:11,310 --> 03:42:13,764
So this is vocabulary rather than engineering.

2321
03:42:14,862 --> 03:42:19,300
The second and third points carry the weight, and module 5 already showed why.

2322
03:42:20,206 --> 03:42:28,868
Machines that do not share memory must send data to each other whenever work needs to be combined, and that transfer is what a shuffle is.

2323
03:42:29,774 --> 03:42:35,560
Partitioning decides how evenly the work spreads, which is why a badly chosen key produces skew.

2324
03:42:36,622 --> 03:42:40,320
The last point matters for a specific class of question.

2325
03:42:40,878 --> 03:42:48,868
A running total that depends on the previous row cannot be parallelized, so an option promising to speed it up with more workers is wrong.

2326
03:42:50,446 --> 03:42:58,020
The exam guide names graph and pre-data structures explicitly which surprises people in a data engineering exam.

2327
03:42:59,118 --> 03:43:02,468
The reason becomes clear when you connect them to services.

2328
03:43:03,118 --> 03:43:10,724
A hash-based lookup is what makes DynamoDB fast at fetching one item by key and useless at ranges.

2329
03:43:11,584 --> 03:43:18,212
A sorted structure is what makes a range query cheap, which is why Redshift sort keys matter.

2330
03:43:18,544 --> 03:43:23,812
Graphs model relationships between things, which is Amazon Neptune's entire purpose.

2331
03:43:24,462 --> 03:43:34,436
And the directed acyclic graph in the fourth bullet is the shape every orchestrator uses. So you have already been working with one since module 6.

2332
03:43:35,982 --> 03:43:42,224
This is the single most useful thing to understand about Lambda tuning, and it is genuinely counterintuitive.

2333
03:43:42,464 --> 03:43:45,184
You do not configure CPU anywhere.

2334
03:43:45,454 --> 03:43:50,596
You configure memory, and lambda allocates processor power proportionally to it.

2335
03:43:51,406 --> 03:43:57,444
So consider a function that is slow because it is computing something with plenty of memory left spare.

2336
03:43:58,414 --> 03:44:03,588
Raising the memory setting still makes it faster because more processor comes with it.

2337
03:44:04,014 --> 03:44:07,364
That leads to the result people find hardest to accept.

2338
03:44:08,046 --> 03:44:18,404
Increasing memory frequency reduces total cost because the function finishes so much sooner that the shorter duration more than offsets the higher per millisecond rate.

2339
03:44:19,118 --> 03:44:25,092
The first work scenario in this module is built on exactly that, and the intuitive answer to it is wrong.

2340
03:44:26,544 --> 03:44:30,372
Separate the hard limits from the soft ones because the exam does.

2341
03:44:30,704 --> 03:44:32,516
Timeout and memory are hard.

2342
03:44:32,864 --> 03:44:41,220
No support request raises the 15-minute ceiling, so a workload that might exceed it needs a different engine or needs breaking into pieces.

2343
03:44:43,950 --> 03:44:46,980
Concurrency is soft and can be raised on request.

2344
03:44:47,824 --> 03:44:57,796
The ephemeral storage row is worth noting because it moved. It used to be a fixed 512 MB, which forced people to route everything through S3.

2345
03:44:58,224 --> 03:45:07,108
It now reaches 10GB. So a function processing a large file locally is viable in a way older material says it is not.

2346
03:45:08,846 --> 03:45:14,784
The exam guide names mounting storage volumes from within Lambda as a skill, so both options appear.

2347
03:45:15,310 --> 03:45:17,572
The distinction is sharing and persistence.

2348
03:45:19,830 --> 03:45:28,470
Slash TMP is scratch space belonging to one execution environment, so a second concurrent invocation gets a different one and nothing survives reliably.

2349
03:45:29,750 --> 03:45:36,350
Amazon EFS is a real shared file system, so many functions can read and write the same files and the data persists.

2350
03:45:37,184 --> 03:45:43,012
That makes it the answer for a large shared reference dataset or a model file too big to package.

2351
03:45:43,504 --> 03:45:46,916
The cost is the VPC requirement on the fourth bullet.

2352
03:45:48,590 --> 03:45:51,940
The size ceiling decides this and it decides it sharply.

2353
03:45:52,384 --> 03:45:56,388
A function doing light transformation fits comfortably in a zip archive.

2354
03:45:57,678 --> 03:46:03,908
Layers let several functions share one copy of a common dependency rather than each carrying its own.

2355
03:46:04,814 --> 03:46:13,904
The moment a function needs a substantial numerical or machine learning library, the 250 MB unpack limit becomes the constraint.

2356
03:46:14,158 --> 03:46:16,864
A container image raises it to 10 GB.

2357
03:46:17,584 --> 03:46:20,388
The trade in the last bullet applies to both.

2358
03:46:21,198 --> 03:46:25,988
Every megabyte of package's time spent downloading and unpacking during a cold start.

2359
03:46:27,504 --> 03:46:31,620
These two sound similar and solve opposite problems.

2360
03:46:32,224 --> 03:46:39,984
Reserved concurrency is a cap you impose on yourself, which module 6 used to protect a database from a thousand concurrent writers.

2361
03:46:40,144 --> 03:46:46,372
It costs nothing and it also reserves that slice of the account limit so other functions cannot starve this one.

2362
03:46:47,480 --> 03:46:50,052
Provisioned concurrency is about cold starts.

2363
03:46:50,304 --> 03:46:56,676
It keeps a number of environments initialized and waiting, so an invocation does not pay initialization time.

2364
03:46:57,166 --> 03:46:58,660
It is built continuously.

2365
03:47:01,422 --> 03:47:05,572
A question about latency spikes on the first request points at provisioned.

2366
03:47:06,414 --> 03:47:10,596
A question about overwhelming something downstream points at reserved.

2367
03:47:11,968 --> 03:47:16,676
The third point is the highest value habit and it appears in questions about function performance.

2368
03:47:16,928 --> 03:47:22,756
Code outside the handler runs once when the environment is created, and code inside runs on every invocation.

2369
03:47:23,374 --> 03:47:30,724
So creating an S3 client or a database connection inside the handler pays that setup cost every single time.

2370
03:47:31,008 --> 03:47:42,180
Meanwhile, creating it outside means subsequent invocations reuse it. That is why a function can be slow on invocation 1 and fast on invocation 2. And the fourth point is a real trade.

2371
03:47:42,528 --> 03:47:47,492
Putting a function in a VPC is necessary to reach private resources and it is not free.

2372
03:47:47,808 --> 03:47:50,404
So do it deliberately rather than by default.

2373
03:47:51,694 --> 03:47:59,204
Automating data processing with Lambda is a named skill and the event source decides the failure behavior, which is what questions test.

2374
03:48:00,046 --> 03:48:09,572
Asynchronous invocation from S3 or event bridge means Lambda itself retries a failed invocation twice before giving up and nothing is waiting for the answer.

2375
03:48:10,382 --> 03:48:14,276
Bold-based sources like SQS and Kinesis behave differently again.

2376
03:48:15,246 --> 03:48:23,396
That is because Lambda takes a batch and only deletes it on success, which is why module 3's poison record problem blocks a shard.

2377
03:48:24,014 --> 03:48:30,052
Synchronous invocation returns the error straight to whatever called it. So the retree decision belongs to the caller.

2378
03:48:31,726 --> 03:48:37,092
The last bullet is the one that matters, and it is the same theme as the rest of this module.

2379
03:48:37,648 --> 03:48:42,404
An S3 event that triggers a function which fails three times is simply gone.

2380
03:48:43,406 --> 03:48:51,428
No error surfaces anywhere, the object is never processed, and the only symptom is data that quietly does not exist downstream.

2381
03:48:54,318 --> 03:49:00,932
Configuring an on-failure destination costs nothing and turns that into a visible inspectable event.

2382
03:49:01,550 --> 03:49:07,300
Destinations superseded dead letter queues here because they capture the invocation record, including the error.

2383
03:49:07,648 --> 03:49:11,716
A dead letter queue captures only the original payload and leaves you guessing.

2384
03:49:13,358 --> 03:49:19,588
An AWS Lambda function transforms JSON files from Amazon S3 and writes parquet.

2385
03:49:20,048 --> 03:49:25,764
It is configured with 512 MB of memory and takes about 40 seconds per file.

2386
03:49:26,368 --> 03:49:31,556
That time is mostly spent in the parquet conversion, which is CPU bound.

2387
03:49:32,494 --> 03:49:37,860
The team processes about 80,000 files a month and wants to reduce the lambda bin.

2388
03:49:39,054 --> 03:49:45,988
Memory user reported in CloudWatch peaks at around 180 MB, well under the configured amount.

2389
03:49:46,574 --> 03:49:48,484
What should the data engineer do?

2390
03:49:50,734 --> 03:49:57,124
reduce the memory from 512 MB to 256, since only 180 are used.

2391
03:49:57,648 --> 03:50:03,140
b Increase the memory, measure the change in duration, and settle on the cheapest total.

2392
03:50:03,568 --> 03:50:08,292
C. Enable provisioned concurrency so the functions do not pay cold start time.

2393
03:50:09,008 --> 03:50:09,328
D.

2394
03:50:09,838 --> 03:50:14,212
Increase the ephemeral storage allocation so the conversion has more room.

2395
03:50:15,278 --> 03:50:16,612
Pause and decide.

2396
03:50:23,118 --> 03:50:28,036
The answer is B, and option A is the trap the whole slide sequence was building towards.

2397
03:50:28,448 --> 03:50:36,288
Reducing memory that is visibly unused looks like textbook right sizing, and it would be correct if the function were waiting on something.

2398
03:50:36,528 --> 03:50:42,116
It is not, it is CPU bound, and memory is the only way to buy CPU.

2399
03:50:42,990 --> 03:50:49,636
Halving memory halves the processor, so the function takes about twice as long, and you pay roughly the same for a slower pipeline.

2400
03:50:50,126 --> 03:50:58,660
The word measure in option B matters too because the relationship is not perfectly linear and the cheapest setting has to be found empirically.

2401
03:51:00,366 --> 03:51:08,868
AWS batch is on the official in scope service list and almost nobody studies it, which makes it worth these few lines.

2402
03:51:09,742 --> 03:51:11,236
It fills the genuine gap.

2403
03:51:11,758 --> 03:51:13,636
Lambda stops at 15 minutes.

2404
03:51:14,446 --> 03:51:19,780
Spark is the answer when work must be distributed across a cluster which shuffles and joins.

2405
03:51:20,718 --> 03:51:27,568
Batch covers the space between, where a job runs for hours, does not need Spark, and can be packaged as a container.

2406
03:51:27,982 --> 03:51:33,668
Converting 10,000 images, running a simulation, processing a large file with a single threaded tool.

2407
03:51:34,254 --> 03:51:40,196
It provisions the compute, queues the jobs, and shuts down when the queue empties.

2408
03:51:41,774 --> 03:51:50,180
Infrastructure as code appears twice in the exam guide has two separate skills, which tells you how much weight AWS puts on it for this role.

2409
03:51:50,720 --> 03:51:56,068
The argument is the one module, six made about disaster recovery, stated more generally.

2410
03:51:56,942 --> 03:52:00,964
A pipeline you can recreate from a definition can be rebuilt in another region.

2411
03:52:01,360 --> 03:52:07,268
It can be promoted from test to production without drift, reviewed before it changes, and rolled back when it breaks.

2412
03:52:07,600 --> 03:52:11,204
A pipeline assembled by hand in the console can do none of those things.

2413
03:52:11,822 --> 03:52:14,116
It also cannot be explained six months later.

2414
03:52:14,640 --> 03:52:20,388
The person who built it has moved to another team, and nobody is sure which of the 40 jobs is still needed.

2415
03:52:20,910 --> 03:52:24,420
That is a real organizational cost, not a theoretical one.

2416
03:52:26,222 --> 03:52:29,252
The call out is the relationship most people get wrong.

2417
03:52:29,966 --> 03:52:33,124
The CDK is not an alternative deployment engine.

2418
03:52:33,520 --> 03:52:42,532
It is a way of writing cloud formation using a programming language, and it synthesizes a cloud formation template that cloud formation then deploys.

2419
03:52:43,246 --> 03:52:47,876
So stacks, change sets, drift detection, and rollback all behave identically.

2420
03:52:48,240 --> 03:52:50,916
The reason to choose the CDK is repetition.

2421
03:52:51,280 --> 03:53:00,260
Defining 40 near-identical blue jobs in YAM, L means 40 blocks of near-identical YAML. In the CDK, it is a loop.

2422
03:53:00,960 --> 03:53:08,548
When a question describes many similar resources or a reusable pattern across teams, that is the CDK signal.

2423
03:53:10,190 --> 03:53:18,020
Change sets are the feature most worth knowing for exam purposes because they answer a specific concern that appears in questions.

2424
03:53:19,520 --> 03:53:24,356
Applying a template change directly means finding out what it does by watching it happen.

2425
03:53:25,262 --> 03:53:31,076
Some resource changes are replacements rather than updates, which for a database means a new empty one.

2426
03:53:31,854 --> 03:53:38,020
A change set computes the plan first and shows which resources will be created, modified, or replaced.

2427
03:53:38,160 --> 03:53:41,444
So the destructive change is visible before anything moves.

2428
03:53:42,286 --> 03:53:51,652
Stack sets matter for the multi-account pattern module 6 described, deploying identical infrastructure into every account from one place.

2429
03:53:56,750 --> 03:53:58,084
The value is compression.

2430
03:54:04,334 --> 03:54:12,240
Defining a lambda function in raw cloud formation means declaring six separate things the function a role, a policy,

2431
03:54:12,462 --> 03:54:16,644
a log group, an event source mapping and invoke permissions.

2432
03:54:17,838 --> 03:54:20,132
That runs to well over a hundred lines.

2433
03:54:20,974 --> 03:54:23,844
The equivalent SAM resource is a handful.

2434
03:54:24,398 --> 03:54:27,460
It expands into the same cloud formation underneath.

2435
03:54:27,982 --> 03:54:30,660
The local testing point is the other reason it appears.

2436
03:54:31,200 --> 03:54:39,760
That is because being able to invoke a function locally against a sample event is what makes testing serverless code practical.

2437
03:54:41,070 --> 03:54:44,836
The exam distinguishes these two more sharply than teams often do.

2438
03:54:45,326 --> 03:54:50,180
Both hold values your code reads at runtime and both can encrypt with KMS.

2439
03:54:51,854 --> 03:54:54,468
The difference that decides questions is rotation.

2440
03:54:55,470 --> 03:55:00,900
Secrets manager can rotate a credential on a schedule, updating the stored value and the database together.

2441
03:55:01,326 --> 03:55:05,380
That is exactly the problem scenario 4 in this module describes.

2442
03:55:06,126 --> 03:55:13,988
Parameter store has no rotation and it is free for standard parameters, which makes it the sensible home for a bucket name or a threshold.

2443
03:55:14,606 --> 03:55:20,836
So a question mentioning rotating credentials has one answer and a question about configuration values has the other.

2444
03:55:22,510 --> 03:55:28,068
A data platform team manages 60 AWS Blue jobs across three environments.

2445
03:55:29,486 --> 03:55:35,364
Each environment differs only in bucket names, database names, and worker counts.

2446
03:55:37,486 --> 03:55:40,320
Jobs are currently created by hand in the console.

2447
03:55:40,878 --> 03:55:47,108
Configuration drift between environments has twice caused a production failure that did not appear in test.

2448
03:55:47,520 --> 03:55:54,340
The team wants environments to be probably identical apart from their parameters and wants changes reviewed before they are applied.

2449
03:55:54,734 --> 03:55:56,196
What should the team implement?

2450
03:55:59,342 --> 03:56:03,876
document the console steps thoroughly and have a second engineer check each change.

2451
03:56:04,366 --> 03:56:11,300
b. Define the jobs with the AWS C DK parameterized per environment and deploy through a pipeline.

2452
03:56:11,920 --> 03:56:20,164
C. Copy the production view jobs into the other environments using the AWS C LI after each change.

2453
03:56:20,878 --> 03:56:21,120
D.

2454
03:56:22,414 --> 03:56:26,628
Use AWS config rules to detect when environments diverge.

2455
03:56:27,246 --> 03:56:28,484
Pause and decide.

2456
03:56:35,200 --> 03:56:36,452
The answer is B.

2457
03:56:36,880 --> 03:56:44,900
Option D is the thoughtful distractor because AWS config genuinely does detect configuration drift and is a real answer to other questions.

2458
03:56:46,478 --> 03:56:52,068
It is detection rather than prevention. The environments still diverge, you just find out sooner.

2459
03:56:53,070 --> 03:56:58,880
The requirement asks for provably identical, which only a shared definition delivers.

2460
03:57:00,142 --> 03:57:07,108
60 near-identical resources is also the specific condition where the CDK beats plain cloud formation.

2461
03:57:07,840 --> 03:57:13,040
That is because it becomes a loop over a list rather than 60 blocks of template.

2462
03:57:14,286 --> 03:57:16,640
This is the correction promised at the start.

2463
03:57:16,960 --> 03:57:19,652
AWS Code Commit was the Git hosting service.

2464
03:57:20,080 --> 03:57:27,332
It closed to new customers during 2024 and it was removed from the in scope service list in the December 2025 revision.

2465
03:57:28,110 --> 03:57:33,092
Older material treats it as the default answer for where pipeline code lives.

2466
03:57:33,390 --> 03:57:34,212
It is not.

2467
03:57:57,040 --> 03:57:59,460
Build once, deploy many times.

2468
03:58:02,222 --> 03:58:10,820
If each environment builds its own artifact from source, they can differ because a dependency version moved between builds.

2469
03:58:11,726 --> 03:58:16,420
Promoting the identical artifact means what you tested is what you shipped.

2470
03:58:17,134 --> 03:58:20,320
The validate step is the one that is specific to data work.

2471
03:58:20,800 --> 03:58:23,812
For an application, you check that it starts and responds.

2472
03:58:24,654 --> 03:58:30,564
For a pipeline, you run it against a known input and confirm the output matches what you expect.

2473
03:58:31,600 --> 03:58:37,124
That is because a pipeline can deploy perfectly and still produce wrong numbers.

2474
03:58:38,638 --> 03:58:42,820
The last bullet is the honest limitation and it is worth saying plainly.

2475
03:58:43,310 --> 03:58:47,108
Canary deployment works beautifully for a function serving requests.

2476
03:58:47,360 --> 03:58:54,852
That is because you send 1% of traffic to the new version, watch the error rate, and shift more or rollback.

2477
03:58:55,726 --> 03:58:59,972
A Nike batch job has one invocation, so there is no traffic to split.

2478
03:59:00,526 --> 03:59:08,932
The equivalent discipline for data pipelines is running the new version against known data in a test environment and comparing the output before promoting.

2479
03:59:10,126 --> 03:59:13,124
That is what the validate step in the delivery pipeline is for.

2480
03:59:14,062 --> 03:59:19,364
Recognizing that difference is more useful than memorizing the deployment strategies.

2481
03:59:21,102 --> 03:59:25,732
The last two points are the distinction that matters and it is easy to blur.

2482
03:59:26,773 --> 03:59:32,004
Tests check that the code does what you intended against inputs you thought of when you wrote them.

2483
03:59:33,070 --> 03:59:40,452
Data quality checks run in production against real data and catch what you did not think of, which is where the interesting failures live.

2484
03:59:41,294 --> 03:59:48,356
A source system that starts sending nulls in a column will pass every unit test you wrote because your test data does not contain them.

2485
03:59:49,358 --> 03:59:52,196
Module 15 covers data quality properly.

2486
03:59:52,400 --> 03:59:56,484
For now, hold that a green build and correct output are different claims.

2487
03:59:57,998 --> 04:00:05,284
A team deploys AWS Blue job scripts by uploading them to Amazon S3 and updating the job definition manually.

2488
04:00:05,774 --> 04:00:14,212
A recent change was applied to production before it had been run in test and it silently produced incorrect aggregates for two days before anyone noticed.

2489
04:00:14,960 --> 04:00:22,436
The team wants changes to be tested before reaching production and wants to detect incorrect output rather than only failed jobs.

2490
04:00:23,440 --> 04:00:26,372
Which combination best addresses both requirements?

2491
04:00:29,390 --> 04:00:38,692
A code pipeline that builds and tests in a test environment promotes the same artifact to production plus data quality checks in the job.

2492
04:00:39,854 --> 04:00:40,153
B.

2493
04:00:40,654 --> 04:00:46,596
Add retrees and a longer timeout to the glue job so transient failures do not reach production.

2494
04:00:47,758 --> 04:00:48,000
C

2495
04:00:49,294 --> 04:00:53,252
Require a second engineer to approve each manual upload to Amazon S3.

2496
04:00:54,800 --> 04:00:55,040
D.

2497
04:00:55,726 --> 04:00:59,652
Enable blue job bookmarks so incorrect data is not reprocessed.

2498
04:01:00,206 --> 04:01:01,412
Pause and decide.

2499
04:01:08,096 --> 04:01:11,620
The answer is A, and the structure is worth noticing.

2500
04:01:12,016 --> 04:01:16,260
The question states two requirements, and only one option addresses both.

2501
04:01:16,656 --> 04:01:18,916
That is a common shape once you look for it.

2502
04:01:19,296 --> 04:01:21,476
The other detail is the word silently.

2503
04:01:21,902 --> 04:01:22,852
Nothing failed.

2504
04:01:23,696 --> 04:01:30,724
The job ran, succeeded, and wrote wrong numbers, which is why every option about failure handling is irrelevant.

2505
04:01:31,214 --> 04:01:34,980
This is the same failure mode module 6 called the worst available outcome.

2506
04:01:35,726 --> 04:01:39,588
It is why data quality checks exist as a separate discipline from testing.

2507
04:01:41,262 --> 04:01:45,764
The fourth point describes a pattern that appears in questions as a symptom.

2508
04:01:46,254 --> 04:01:55,812
A job iterates over a data frame row by row, calling an API or doing a lookup for each record, and it is inexplicably slow.

2509
04:01:56,366 --> 04:01:59,056
The fix is almost never a bigger machine.

2510
04:01:59,630 --> 04:02:06,116
It is either to batch those calls or to restructure the work as a join against a dataset rather than a loop over records.

2511
04:02:06,896 --> 04:02:11,460
That is the same lesson as Module 5 shuffle discussion approach from the code side.

2512
04:02:11,856 --> 04:02:15,876
It is why the exam expects a data engineer to think like a software engineer.

2513
04:02:17,550 --> 04:02:25,124
Calling SDKs to access AWS features from code is a named skill, and two points here become exam answers.

2514
04:02:25,456 --> 04:02:27,588
The credentials point is absolute.

2515
04:02:28,176 --> 04:02:35,716
Any option that puts an access key and secret in a script, an environment variable, or a configuration file is wrong.

2516
04:02:36,256 --> 04:02:41,604
The correct answer is an IAM role attached to whatever is running the code.

2517
04:02:42,176 --> 04:02:45,060
Module 17 covers why in detail.

2518
04:02:47,566 --> 04:02:50,736
The pagination point catches people in practice.

2519
04:02:51,310 --> 04:02:59,536
A list operation returns the first page only, so code that lists a bucket without paginating quietly, processes the first thousand objects,

2520
04:02:59,696 --> 04:03:00,868
and ignores the rest.

2521
04:03:02,446 --> 04:03:08,132
The callout is the practical version of everything this module has said about silent failure.

2522
04:03:10,126 --> 04:03:13,860
Suppose a job reports how many rows it read and how many it wrote.

2523
04:03:14,862 --> 04:03:22,020
A run where those numbers diverge unexpectedly becomes visible immediately, even though no exception was ever raised.

2524
04:03:22,990 --> 04:03:27,216
That is a custom CloudWatch metric and an alarm, and it costs almost nothing.

2525
04:03:27,456 --> 04:03:30,884
The last point is the balance. Logging is not free.

2526
04:03:31,278 --> 04:03:35,428
Consider a job logging one line per record across 100 million records.

2527
04:03:35,616 --> 04:03:39,748
The CloudWatch logs bill can exceed the compute cost of the job itself.

2528
04:03:40,430 --> 04:03:43,620
Module 14 covers the monitoring site properly.

2529
04:03:45,294 --> 04:03:48,256
A nightly AWS Blue job reads from a partner.

2530
04:03:48,736 --> 04:03:53,764
AP are using an access key and secrets stored in the job script parameters.

2531
04:03:56,142 --> 04:04:01,764
The job lists objects in an Amazon S3 bucket, processes them, and writes results.

2532
04:04:02,510 --> 04:04:04,100
Two problems have emerged.

2533
04:04:05,038 --> 04:04:10,500
The partner rotated the credentials and the job failed until someone edited the parameters.

2534
04:04:10,976 --> 04:04:19,364
Separately, the job has been quietly processing only the first 1000 objects in a bucket that now holds 40,000.

2535
04:04:21,006 --> 04:04:23,620
Which combination fixes both problems?

2536
04:04:25,838 --> 04:04:32,964
store the partner credentials in AWS Secrets Manager and paginate the S3 list operation.

2537
04:04:33,582 --> 04:04:37,188
b Increase the glue job timeout and add more.

2538
04:04:48,496 --> 04:04:48,816
D

2539
04:04:49,678 --> 04:04:55,076
Attach an IAM rule with S3 access and enable job bookmarks.

2540
04:04:56,846 --> 04:04:58,212
Pause and decide.

2541
04:05:04,878 --> 04:05:07,684
The answer is A and the number in the question is the 2.

2542
04:05:08,430 --> 04:05:11,748
A thousand is the default page size for an S3 list operation.

2543
04:05:12,176 --> 04:05:19,204
So a job seeing exactly a thousand objects out of 40,000 is not failing. It is reading one page and stopping.

2544
04:05:19,950 --> 04:05:23,780
Option D is worth attention because it is half right in an interesting way.

2545
04:05:24,016 --> 04:05:28,496
An IAAM role really is the correct answer for AWS credentials.

2546
04:05:28,816 --> 04:05:30,016
This is a partner.

2547
04:05:31,342 --> 04:05:36,324
API credential, which no IAM role can represent.

2548
04:05:36,910 --> 04:05:43,972
That is exactly what Secrets Manager exists for, and it can rotate the value without anyone editing a job.

2549
04:05:45,646 --> 04:05:52,676
Amazon Q joined the InScope service list in the December 2025 revision alongside Bedrock and Kendra.

2550
04:05:53,518 --> 04:05:57,376
So it is fair game and no older material covers it. The

2551
04:05:57,390 --> 04:06:02,084
framing that keeps it straight is that Q assists the engineer rather than processing the data.

2552
04:06:02,894 --> 04:06:08,740
It suggests code, explains an unfamiliar job someone else wrote, and helps author a query.

2553
04:06:08,976 --> 04:06:14,372
Contrast that with Bedrock from module 5, which sits inside a pipeline and transforms records.

2554
04:06:17,838 --> 04:06:22,820
So an option offering Amazon Q as the thing that classifies documents is confusing the two.

2555
04:06:23,470 --> 04:06:29,252
An option offering it to help a team write blue jobs faster is using it correctly.

2556
04:06:30,862 --> 04:06:38,340
5 questions and the last one has now appeared in three consecutive modules, which should tell you how much the exam cares about it.

2557
04:06:41,198 --> 04:06:46,693
A job that fails is an operations problem answered with retreats, alerts and dead letter queues.

2558
04:06:47,854 --> 04:06:56,068
A job that succeeds while producing wrong output is a completely different problem, answered with validation, testing, and data quality checks.

2559
04:06:56,974 --> 04:07:02,340
Options that handle failure are never the answer to a question about silent wrongness.

2560
04:07:02,816 --> 04:07:07,332
And the exam offers them every single time because they look responsible.

2561
04:07:08,814 --> 04:07:12,516
The third question is the fastest elimination in the module.

2562
04:07:13,486 --> 04:07:15,492
Credentials in code is always wrong.

2563
04:07:15,854 --> 04:07:21,284
An IAM role is right for AWS Access and Secrets Manager is right for anything else.

2564
04:07:22,190 --> 04:07:26,016
That completes domain 1, 34% of the exam.

2565
04:07:27,374 --> 04:07:31,652
Domain 2 begins here and it is worth 26% of the exam.

2566
04:07:32,142 --> 04:07:39,940
Amazon S3 appeared 343 times in the bank analyzed for this course, second only to AWS Blue.

2567
04:07:40,718 --> 04:07:45,604
Almost every pipeline in every other module lands data here.

2568
04:07:46,016 --> 04:07:50,596
So, this module is where a lot of scattered references get consolidated.

2569
04:07:51,246 --> 04:08:00,176
Storage classes and lifecycle rules, how to lay a layout, so queries stay cheap, retention and deletion, including the legal kind,

2570
04:08:00,416 --> 04:08:01,348
and resilience.

2571
04:08:02,286 --> 04:08:10,976
4 worked scenarios and 3 availability warnings because several S3 features that older question banks treat as correct answers

2572
04:08:11,182 --> 04:08:13,540
have quietly closed to new customers.

2573
04:08:15,136 --> 04:08:19,076
Putting an object into S3 is not something an exam can meaningfully test.

2574
04:08:19,534 --> 04:08:21,476
What it tests is everything that follows.

2575
04:08:22,030 --> 04:08:30,820
The question is which class the object sits in, when it moves to another, when it is deleted, and whether it can be deleted at all.

2576
04:08:31,790 --> 04:08:35,588
What all of that costs across years rather than days.

2577
04:08:35,776 --> 04:08:45,540
That is why life cycle configuration appeared 29 times in the bank analyzed for this course, while more glamorous features barely registered at all.

2578
04:08:49,550 --> 04:08:52,976
So, read an S3 question looking for the time dimension in it.

2579
04:08:53,136 --> 04:08:54,436
How old is the data?

2580
04:08:55,310 --> 04:08:58,596
How often is it read now compared to when it landed?

2581
04:08:59,374 --> 04:09:01,348
How long must it legally be kept?

2582
04:09:01,696 --> 04:09:05,060
Those three questions resolve a surprising proportion of this domain.

2583
04:09:06,638 --> 04:09:11,876
The pattern down this table is the thing to internalize rather than the individual prices.

2584
04:09:12,238 --> 04:09:19,332
Every step towards cheaper storage buys a longer minimum commitment and usually a retrieval charge.

2585
04:09:20,110 --> 04:09:26,340
Deep archive is roughly a 25th the price of standard and asks you to keep the object 180 days.

2586
04:09:26,656 --> 04:09:33,828
So a lifecycle rule that moves data to deep archive after 7 days and deletes it after 30 is not saving money at all.

2587
04:09:34,574 --> 04:09:39,012
You pay the full 180 days regardless as an early deletion charge.

2588
04:09:39,598 --> 04:09:45,988
That interaction between lifecycle rules and minimum durations is the single most common S3 cost trap.

2589
04:09:47,406 --> 04:09:51,780
The callout is a trap the exam does set and it is satisfying once you see it.

2590
04:09:52,176 --> 04:09:59,172
In frequent access and glacier classes bill a minimum of 128 kilobytes per object, whatever the object actually contains.

2591
04:09:59,950 --> 04:10:07,296
So a lake holding 50 million four kilobyte files pays as though every one of them were 128 kilobytes.

2592
04:10:07,470 --> 04:10:09,860
That is 32 times the real data volume.

2593
04:10:11,310 --> 04:10:16,708
In that situation, moving to a cheaper storage class makes the bill go up rather than down.

2594
04:10:17,774 --> 04:10:24,868
It is the small file problem from module 2 appearing again in a completely different guise, and the answer is the same one.

2595
04:10:25,136 --> 04:10:32,132
Compact the files before you optimize anything else because every other optimization is fighting the same underlying problem.

2596
04:10:33,614 --> 04:10:40,100
S3 Express One zone inverts the usual pricing shape, which is exactly what makes it examinable.

2597
04:10:40,416 --> 04:10:45,348
Every other class trays retrieval speed for cheaper storage in one direction or the other.

2598
04:10:45,998 --> 04:10:55,204
This one charges more for storage and far less per request because it is designed for workloads that make enormous numbers of small requests.

2599
04:10:55,790 --> 04:11:01,668
A Spark job reading thousands of files repeatedly during a shuffle heavy stage is the archetype.

2600
04:11:02,158 --> 04:11:07,172
So is an interactive query engine hitting the same working set over and over.

2601
04:11:07,616 --> 04:11:14,436
The single availability zone point matters for the same reason it matters for one zone infrequent access.

2602
04:11:15,376 --> 04:11:23,492
It suits intermediate or reproducible working data, and it is the wrong home for anything you could not rebuild from somewhere else.

2603
04:11:25,070 --> 04:11:33,476
This limit surprises people because S3 is usually described as infinitely scalable and for storage volume it effectively is.

2604
04:11:34,862 --> 04:11:36,196
Request rate is different

2605
04:11:36,846 --> 04:11:39,492
and it applies per prefix rather than per bucket.

2606
04:11:40,462 --> 04:11:49,220
So a job writing millions of objects into one flat prefix can hit a ceiling that the identical job writing into date partition prefixes never approaches.

2607
04:11:49,870 --> 04:11:53,028
That is a nice side benefit of a layout you wanted anyway.

2608
04:11:53,456 --> 04:11:56,516
The 3 digit error code is worth recognizing on site.

2609
04:11:57,358 --> 04:12:04,388
A 503 slowdown response is S3 asking the client to back off, not a sign that anything is broken.

2610
04:12:05,038 --> 04:12:12,164
The correct response is the exponential back-off module, 6 covered, rather than a support ticket or a retrieval loop.

2611
04:12:13,774 --> 04:12:20,292
Intelligent tiering appeared 16 times in the bank, and the trigger phrase is remarkably consistent.

2612
04:12:21,230 --> 04:12:26,180
Watch for access patterns described as unknown, unpredictable, or changing.

2613
04:12:26,830 --> 04:12:30,724
Watch for a team saying they do not know how often data will be read.

2614
04:12:31,406 --> 04:12:32,516
That is the answer.

2615
04:12:33,422 --> 04:12:40,068
It moves objects between tiers based on what actually happens, charges no retrieval fees, and imposes no minimum duration.

2616
04:12:40,416 --> 04:12:49,220
A question stating a known pattern is different. Data read heavily for 30 days and rarely afterwards suits a lifecycle rule which avoids the monitoring fee.

2617
04:12:50,094 --> 04:12:55,620
The monitoring fee is also why it is poor value for very small objects.

2618
04:12:57,166 --> 04:13:01,668
The last two bullets are where real money hides and both appear in questions.

2619
04:13:03,246 --> 04:13:06,724
When versioning is on, deleting an object does not remove it.

2620
04:13:07,214 --> 04:13:15,536
It adds a delete marker and keeps every previous version, all of which you pay for indefinitely unless a lifecycle rule expires non current

2621
04:13:15,696 --> 04:13:16,132
versions.

2622
04:13:17,313 --> 04:13:26,020
And An interrupted multipart upload leaves the parts already uploaded sitting in the bucket built as storage but not shown when you list objects.

2623
04:13:27,662 --> 04:13:33,124
A life cycle rule to abort incomplete multi-part uploads after a few days is standard practice.

2624
04:13:33,902 --> 04:13:39,300
A question describing a bill that does not match the visible data is often pointing at it.

2625
04:13:40,782 --> 04:13:44,512
A company stores IoT telemetry in Amazon S3.

2626
04:13:44,878 --> 04:13:49,732
Data is queried heavily for the first 14 days, occasionally for the next 3 months.

2627
04:13:50,670 --> 04:13:54,788
Then only if a regulator asks, which happens perhaps once a year.

2628
04:13:55,182 --> 04:13:58,276
Regulation requires the data to be kept for 7 years.

2629
04:13:58,832 --> 04:14:08,900
A data engineer proposes a lifecycle rule that transitions objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 14 days and expires them after 7 years.

2630
04:14:09,232 --> 04:14:11,232
Average object size is 4 MB.

2631
04:14:11,712 --> 04:14:13,013
What is wrong with the proposal?

2632
04:14:13,998 --> 04:14:15,300
A. Nothing.

2633
04:14:16,206 --> 04:14:20,228
Deep archive after 14 days is the cheapest option available.

2634
04:14:20,750 --> 04:14:28,228
b Retrieval from Deep archive takes up to 48 hours, which does not meet the occasional access needed in months 1 to 3.

2635
04:14:28,942 --> 04:14:29,152
C.

2636
04:14:30,286 --> 04:14:35,232
Objects smaller than 128 kilobytes cannot be transitioned to deep archive.

2637
04:14:35,662 --> 04:14:35,872
D.

2638
04:14:36,272 --> 04:14:40,004
Lifecycle rules cannot expire objects after more than 5 years.

2639
04:14:41,710 --> 04:14:42,980
Pause and decide.

2640
04:14:49,678 --> 04:14:50,724
The answer is B.

2641
04:14:51,566 --> 04:14:56,644
The question describes three distinct phases, and the proposal only accounts for two of them.

2642
04:14:58,190 --> 04:15:03,268
Heavy access for 14 days, occasional access for 3 months, then almost never.

2643
04:15:04,174 --> 04:15:14,212
Deep archive serves the third phase beautifully and cannot serve the second at all because occasional access with a 48 hour wait is not access.

2644
04:15:15,982 --> 04:15:18,436
The correct shape is a chain of transitions.

2645
04:15:19,552 --> 04:15:23,876
Standard, then standard, infrequent access or glacier.

2646
04:15:24,718 --> 04:15:28,708
Instant retrieval for the middle period, then deep archive for the long tail.

2647
04:15:29,518 --> 04:15:36,932
Notice option C, which takes a real number, the 128 kilobyte billing minimum, and turns it into a rule that does not exist.

2648
04:15:38,592 --> 04:15:44,912
Module 1 established that partitioning and columnar formats attack query cost from two different directions.

2649
04:15:45,232 --> 04:15:53,604
This section is where that becomes concrete because the layout of a lake is decided once, early, usually by whoever wrote the first ingestion job.

2650
04:15:54,192 --> 04:15:56,900
It then constrains everything built afterwards.

2651
04:15:57,152 --> 04:16:05,348
Changing it later is not a configuration change. It is rewriting every object, which for a large lake is a project rather than a task.

2652
04:16:05,632 --> 04:16:08,260
That is why the exam keeps returning to layout.

2653
04:16:08,912 --> 04:16:17,540
Why a question describing a lake that has become slow or expensive to query almost always has a layout answer rather than a compute answer.

2654
04:16:17,872 --> 04:16:23,172
Adding workers to a badly laid out lake makes it faster and more expensive at the same time.

2655
04:16:24,912 --> 04:16:29,392
The fourth point is a mistake people make when they first learn partitioning.

2656
04:16:29,582 --> 04:16:34,084
It feels natural to partition by customer because queries filter by customer.

2657
04:16:34,894 --> 04:16:45,476
With 50,000 customers, that creates 50,000 folders, most holding a few small files, and the engine now spends longer discovering partitions than reading data.

2658
04:16:46,190 --> 04:16:53,252
Date is usually right because it has low cardinality and almost every analytical query has a time range.

2659
04:16:54,158 --> 04:16:59,780
Partition projection on the last bullet is worth knowing as an Athena specific answer.

2660
04:17:00,462 --> 04:17:07,716
It calculates partition locations from a pattern rather than listing them, which removes the metadata lookup entirely.

2661
04:17:09,358 --> 04:17:18,084
The exam guide names indexing alongside partitioning and compression, and in a lake, it means something different from a database index.

2662
04:17:18,368 --> 04:17:23,236
Parquet records the minimum and maximum value of each column within each block.

2663
04:17:23,982 --> 04:17:30,048
So a query filtering on a date range can skip entire blocks whose range does not overlap.

2664
04:17:30,528 --> 04:17:34,500
That only works if the data is sorted, which is the second bullet.

2665
04:17:34,958 --> 04:17:40,036
Random order means every block spans the whole range and nothing can be skipped.

2666
04:17:40,846 --> 04:17:51,684
Partition indexes in the blue data catalog solve a different problem. That is the time spent looking up which partitions exist when a table has tens of thousands of them.

2667
04:17:53,230 --> 04:17:57,728
The Avruro is the one worth understanding because it is the format people cannot place.

2668
04:17:57,888 --> 04:18:02,404
It is row based like CSV, so it does not help a query that reads three columns.

2669
04:18:02,958 --> 04:18:08,932
What it does have is a proper schema stored with the data and strong support for that schema changing over time.

2670
04:18:09,728 --> 04:18:15,172
That makes it good for the ingestion layer where records arrive whole and structures evolve.

2671
04:18:15,854 --> 04:18:17,248
Parquet is the opposite.

2672
04:18:17,518 --> 04:18:23,172
Columna, excellent for reading a few columns from many rows, and the standard answer for the curated layer.

2673
04:18:23,918 --> 04:18:28,996
A pipeline landing, Avro and Curate into Parkate is a perfectly sensible design.

2674
04:18:30,448 --> 04:18:35,652
Splittability is the concept that separates a considered answer from a guess here.

2675
04:18:36,048 --> 04:18:41,572
A distributed engine divides a file among its workers by having each read a different byte range.

2676
04:18:43,470 --> 04:18:49,284
GZip cannot be decompressed starting from the middle, so the entire file must go to a single worker.

2677
04:18:50,222 --> 04:18:51,812
A 10GB getzip

2678
04:18:53,422 --> 04:18:57,028
therefore takes exactly as long on 100 workers as it does on one.

2679
04:18:57,390 --> 04:18:59,524
Adding capacity changes nothing at all.

2680
04:19:00,078 --> 04:19:08,612
That is why a question describing a job that stubbornly will not parallelize with everything else looking healthy often has a compression answer hiding in it.

2681
04:19:09,408 --> 04:19:18,148
Inside Parcate, the problem disappears because Part Kate compresses each block independently and the file format itself is designed to be split.

2682
04:19:19,694 --> 04:19:26,692
These three layers appear under several names, bronze, silver, gold being a common one, and the exam does not test the naming.

2683
04:19:27,118 --> 04:19:29,956
What it tests is the consequences of the separation.

2684
04:19:30,830 --> 04:19:39,168
Raw data is immutable and never rewritten, which is what makes everything downstream reproducible, and that is the disaster recovery argument

2685
04:19:39,328 --> 04:19:40,612
module 6 made.

2686
04:19:41,358 --> 04:19:49,508
It is also read rarely after the first few days, so it is the natural candidate for aggressive life cycle transitions into archive classes.

2687
04:19:50,446 --> 04:19:52,688
Curated data is the opposite.

2688
04:19:52,928 --> 04:19:59,172
It is read constantly by dashboards and analysts, so it should stay in standard where there is no retrieval charge.

2689
04:19:59,726 --> 04:20:08,676
Applying one life cycle rule to a whole bucket misses that distinction entirely, which is precisely why life cycle rules can filter by prefix.

2690
04:20:10,190 --> 04:20:15,728
Tags are more useful than they first appear because three separate systems read them and act on them.

2691
04:20:16,110 --> 04:20:22,660
A lie cycle rule can expire everything tagged as temporary without caring which prefix it sits in.

2692
04:20:23,374 --> 04:20:31,808
An IAM policy can deny access to anything tagged as restricted, which module 18 covers as attribute-based access control.

2693
04:20:32,494 --> 04:20:41,540
And cost allocation reports can break spent down by whatever tag scheme the organization uses, which is how a shared bucket gets charged back to teams.

2694
04:20:42,350 --> 04:20:45,092
The last bullet is the boundary worth knowing.

2695
04:20:45,568 --> 04:20:53,348
Tags do not help a query engine prune data because engines read object keys and partition structure rather than object metadata.

2696
04:20:53,888 --> 04:20:56,036
Tagging is for policy and accounting.

2697
04:20:56,430 --> 04:20:58,116
Partitioning is for queries.

2698
04:20:59,630 --> 04:21:05,408
An analytics team queries a 40TB dataset in Amazon S3 with Amazon Athena.

2699
04:21:05,568 --> 04:21:10,532
The data is stored as Gazep compressed CSV files partitioned by year and month.

2700
04:21:11,086 --> 04:21:15,652
There are about 600 files per partition, averaging 60 MB each.

2701
04:21:16,526 --> 04:21:21,412
Queries typically filter on a single month and read 4 columns out of 90.

2702
04:21:21,678 --> 04:21:25,284
They currently scan far more data than expected and are slow.

2703
04:21:25,808 --> 04:21:28,804
The team wants both cost and runtime improved.

2704
04:21:29,648 --> 04:21:32,004
Which change will have the largest effect?

2705
04:21:32,494 --> 04:21:36,196
a Repartition the data by day instead of by month.

2706
04:21:36,814 --> 04:21:37,143
B.

2707
04:21:38,190 --> 04:21:42,020
Convert the files to Apache Parquet with snappy compression.

2708
04:21:42,608 --> 04:21:45,968
C Increase the Athena workgroup's query result.

2709
04:21:46,414 --> 04:21:47,556
Reuse window.

2710
04:21:48,048 --> 04:21:48,368
D

2711
04:21:49,294 --> 04:21:51,408
Move the data to S3 standard.

2712
04:21:52,206 --> 04:21:54,500
IA to reduce storage cost.

2713
04:21:54,958 --> 04:21:56,260
Pause and decide.

2714
04:22:02,958 --> 04:22:07,012
The answer is B, and the ratio in the question is doing the work.

2715
04:22:08,078 --> 04:22:13,956
Reading four columns from a row based format means reading all 90 and discarding 86.

2716
04:22:14,414 --> 04:22:17,476
So Athena charges for data the query never uses.

2717
04:22:18,958 --> 04:22:20,996
Columna storage reads only the four.

2718
04:22:21,870 --> 04:22:26,212
The compression change matters as well because Gizip is not splitable.

2719
04:22:26,702 --> 04:22:33,348
That is why 660 MB files are not being processed in parallel as effectively as they could be.

2720
04:22:34,542 --> 04:22:39,648
Option D is the trap for anyone pattern matching on the words cost and S3.

2721
04:22:40,654 --> 04:22:46,724
That is because it optimizes storage cost while Athena charges for bytes scanned.

2722
04:22:48,366 --> 04:22:54,916
The exam guide names deleting data to meet business and legal requirements as a skill in its own right.

2723
04:22:55,168 --> 04:22:57,732
It is more subtle than it first sounds.

2724
04:22:58,048 --> 04:23:02,372
There are two opposite obligations and a mature organization has both.

2725
04:23:03,310 --> 04:23:12,196
Retention rules say certain data must survive for a defined period and must not be removable even by an administrator, even by mistake.

2726
04:23:13,838 --> 04:23:21,156
Deletion rules of the kind privacy legislation creates say specific records must be erased on request within a stated window.

2727
04:23:22,030 --> 04:23:27,328
A well-designed lake satisfies both and the mechanisms have nothing in common.

2728
04:23:27,694 --> 04:23:32,740
One is about making deletion impossible, the other about making it precise.

2729
04:23:33,646 --> 04:23:36,324
The next few slides cover each in turn.

2730
04:23:37,134 --> 04:23:45,124
The third worked scenario in this module is about what happens when both obligations land on the same bucket.

2731
04:23:46,574 --> 04:23:55,012
Object lock appeared 10 times in the bank analyzed for this course, and the governance against compliance distinction is what those questions turn on.

2732
04:23:55,950 --> 04:24:06,208
Governance mode protects against accident and routine mistakes while still allowing someone who holds an explicit bypass permission to remove an object.

2733
04:24:06,448 --> 04:24:10,852
That is the right choice when you want a safety net rather than a legal guarantee.

2734
04:24:11,566 --> 04:24:20,932
Compliance mode allows nobody at all, including the account root user and AWS itself, to delete the object before its retention period expires.

2735
04:24:21,728 --> 04:24:29,508
That absoluteness is what makes it acceptable to financial regulators, and it also means a mistake is permanent and unfixable.

2736
04:24:30,382 --> 04:24:36,004
So, a question describing wanting protection with an escape hatch is describing governance mode.

2737
04:24:36,288 --> 04:24:40,740
A question naming a regulator or a legal hold is describing compliance.

2738
04:24:42,510 --> 04:24:46,468
This causes real confusion, so it is worth separating clearly.

2739
04:24:47,278 --> 04:24:49,173
There are two things called Glacier.

2740
04:24:50,048 --> 04:24:57,540
The storage classes, which are properties of ordinary S3 objects and which everything in this module has described.

2741
04:24:58,350 --> 04:25:05,572
And the original standalone Glacier service with its own vaults and its own API, which predates them.

2742
04:25:06,030 --> 04:25:08,420
Vault Lock belongs to that older service.

2743
04:25:08,910 --> 04:25:16,004
It does the same job as object lock in compliance mode, making a retention policy permanently unchangeable.

2744
04:25:16,750 --> 04:25:21,220
It appears in older material as the answer to regulatory retention questions.

2745
04:25:21,728 --> 04:25:26,852
For anything new, object lock on a Glacier storage class is the current approach.

2746
04:25:28,768 --> 04:25:33,828
The second point is the hard one, and it is why open table formats matter.

2747
04:25:34,208 --> 04:25:43,364
A privacy request to erase one customer means finding every record for that customer, which sits inside Parquet files alongside thousands of other customers.

2748
04:25:43,808 --> 04:25:45,860
Deleting the file removes everyone.

2749
04:25:46,638 --> 04:25:53,088
Rewriting the file without that customer is what has to happen, and doing that by hand across a lake is impractical.

2750
04:25:53,518 --> 04:26:00,608
Apache ICBUb makes it a delete statement, which is a large part of why the exam guide added open table formats.

2751
04:26:01,550 --> 04:26:04,388
The last bullet is a genuine design conflict.

2752
04:26:04,848 --> 04:26:11,524
Data under a compliance lock cannot be erased on request, so keep personal data out of locked archives.

2753
04:26:13,198 --> 04:26:16,164
The exam guide pairs S3 versioning and DynamoDB.

2754
04:26:16,558 --> 04:26:20,452
Time to live in a single skill, which tells you it wants the parallel draw.

2755
04:26:20,928 --> 04:26:24,132
Both delete data by age without anyone running a job.

2756
04:26:24,590 --> 04:26:27,008
The two caveats are what questions test.

2757
04:26:27,886 --> 04:26:32,708
Deletion is a background process, so an item is not guaranteed gone the moment it expires.

2758
04:26:33,008 --> 04:26:35,748
It can still appear in query results in the meantime.

2759
04:26:36,718 --> 04:26:42,468
If a requirement is that expired data must never be returned, you filter on the timestamp in the query as well.

2760
04:26:43,022 --> 04:26:45,540
The streams point enables a common pattern.

2761
04:26:46,048 --> 04:26:53,648
A TTL deletion triggers a lambda function which writes the item to S3 before it disappears.

2762
04:26:55,008 --> 04:27:02,628
These are not competing options. They are the same idea in two services and questions test whether you know which belongs where.

2763
04:27:03,168 --> 04:27:07,204
A lifecycle rule is a bucket level policy expressed in days of age.

2764
04:27:08,368 --> 04:27:11,556
Time to live is an attribute on each individual item.

2765
04:27:12,430 --> 04:27:19,888
That means two items written at the same moment can expire months apart, according to whatever business rules set them.

2766
04:27:20,368 --> 04:27:26,848
That per item flexibility is the real difference. A session record expiring 30 minutes after last use.

2767
04:27:27,598 --> 04:27:33,988
A subscription record expiring at the end of a paid term cannot be expressed as a single age-based rule.

2768
04:27:35,246 --> 04:27:39,588
The call out separates three ideas that questions deliberately blur together.

2769
04:27:40,078 --> 04:27:49,892
Durability means S3 will not lose your object through hardware failure, and 11.9s makes that effectively certain across at least three availability zones.

2770
04:27:50,688 --> 04:27:56,292
Availability means you can reach it right now, which is a thing an availability zone outage affects.

2771
04:27:56,846 --> 04:28:03,236
Neither of them protects you from a job that overrides good data with bad or from an engineer deleting the wrong prefix.

2772
04:28:03,726 --> 04:28:05,568
Only versioning does that.

2773
04:28:05,966 --> 04:28:10,372
So a question about recovering from an accidental overwrite has a versioning answer.

2774
04:28:10,798 --> 04:28:17,732
Replication is specifically not it because replication faithfully and promptly copies the mistake to the second region.

2775
04:28:18,688 --> 04:28:27,620
The one zone exception on the second bullet is worth holding because it rates that three availability zone durability for a lower price.

2776
04:28:29,088 --> 04:28:32,740
The second bullet is the detail worth carrying into the exam.

2777
04:28:33,198 --> 04:28:40,228
Enabling replication on a bucket that already holds 10 years of data replicates nothing historical at all.

2778
04:28:40,910 --> 04:28:42,756
It applies from that moment forward.

2779
04:28:43,438 --> 04:28:48,868
And the existing objects need S3 batch replication run against them explicitly.

2780
04:28:49,806 --> 04:28:58,500
So a question describing a newly enabled replication rule and a destination bucket that looks suspiciously empty is describing exactly that.

2781
04:28:58,848 --> 04:29:01,508
The fourth bullet matters for a different reason.

2782
04:29:02,542 --> 04:29:13,796
Permanent deletion of a specific version never replicates by design, because otherwise a malicious or mistaken deletion would propagate to the copy meant to protect you.

2783
04:29:15,502 --> 04:29:17,648
Encryption gets its own module later.

2784
04:29:17,728 --> 04:29:20,772
So this is the short version needed to read a lake question.

2785
04:29:21,422 --> 04:29:27,620
Since 2023, every new S3 object is encrypted at REST by default with S3 managed keys.

2786
04:29:28,302 --> 04:29:33,188
So an option proposing to enable encryption on a bucket is describing something already true.

2787
04:29:33,454 --> 04:29:35,968
What questions actually turn on is key control.

2788
04:29:36,654 --> 04:29:44,900
KMS keys give you an audit trail of every decryption, the ability to revoke access by changing a key policy and cross account control.

2789
04:29:45,710 --> 04:29:51,888
That is why regulated workloads specify SSEKMS rather than SSES3.

2790
04:29:52,782 --> 04:29:55,236
Module 19 covers the detail.

2791
04:29:56,974 --> 04:30:06,180
AWS backup is on the official inScope service list and appears in almost no study material. So it is worth knowing what distinguishes it.

2792
04:30:06,670 --> 04:30:08,452
Versioning protects one bucket.

2793
04:30:09,006 --> 04:30:11,076
Replication copies one bucket.

2794
04:30:11,662 --> 04:30:20,484
AWS backup applies one retention and backup policy across many different services at once and reports on compliance with it.

2795
04:30:20,942 --> 04:30:22,692
So the signal is scope.

2796
04:30:23,408 --> 04:30:28,288
A question about protecting an S3 bucket has a versioning or replication answer.

2797
04:30:28,878 --> 04:30:38,020
A question about a single auditable retention policy covering S3, RD, S and DynamoDB together is describing AWS backup.

2798
04:30:38,574 --> 04:30:42,052
The alternative is configuring three different mechanisms separately.

2799
04:30:43,726 --> 04:30:46,912
This is the availability warning for this module.

2800
04:30:47,246 --> 04:30:47,908
S3.

2801
04:30:48,432 --> 04:30:56,452
Select Let You Run a Small SQL expression against a single object and get back only the matching rows.

2802
04:30:56,974 --> 04:31:02,980
That was genuinely useful and is the marked correct answer to several questions in circulation.

2803
04:31:03,822 --> 04:31:07,172
It closed to new customers in July 2024.

2804
04:31:08,526 --> 04:31:16,452
S3 object Lambda, which transformed objects as they were retrieved, became existing customers only in November 2025.

2805
04:31:17,390 --> 04:31:19,876
Both still appear in exam scope material.

2806
04:31:20,526 --> 04:31:22,596
So the guidance is the same as always.

2807
04:31:23,470 --> 04:31:29,444
If a question describes retrieving a subset of one object, S3 select may still be the intended answer.

2808
04:31:29,952 --> 04:31:32,356
Hethina is what you would actually build with.

2809
04:31:33,792 --> 04:31:38,564
The fourth point is a pattern worth recognizing because it answers a class of question.

2810
04:31:39,072 --> 04:31:42,532
Something may need to happen to millions of existing objects.

2811
04:31:43,022 --> 04:31:47,556
Copying, re-tagging, restoring from Glacier or deleting a subset.

2812
04:31:48,270 --> 04:31:51,172
Listing the bucket to work out which ones is impractical.

2813
04:31:52,110 --> 04:31:56,032
S3 Inventory delivers a manifest of every object on a schedule.

2814
04:31:56,334 --> 04:32:04,100
You query that manifest with Athena to select the ones you want. You hand the result to S3 batch operations to do the work.

2815
04:32:04,942 --> 04:32:12,228
Storage class analysis is the other one worth naming because it answers how you decide what a lifecycle rule should say.

2816
04:32:13,902 --> 04:32:21,572
The chain in the callout is worth memorizing as a unit because it answers questions that otherwise look like they need custom code.

2817
04:32:22,112 --> 04:32:29,092
Suppose a regulator asks for every object tagged with a particular classification to be moved to a locked archive.

2818
04:32:29,966 --> 04:32:34,340
Listing a bucket of 80 million objects to find them is impractical.

2819
04:32:35,310 --> 04:32:39,204
An S3 inventory report lists every object with its metadata.

2820
04:32:40,462 --> 04:32:44,484
You query that file with Athena to produce exactly the list you want.

2821
04:32:45,294 --> 04:32:48,580
You hand that list to batch operations as a manifest.

2822
04:32:49,422 --> 04:32:56,004
It then performs the action on each one, retries failures, and produces a completion report.

2823
04:32:58,094 --> 04:32:59,492
You can show the regulator.

2824
04:33:00,942 --> 04:33:10,468
A financial services company must retain trade records in Amazon S3 for 7 years. No employee, including administrators, may alter or delete them.

2825
04:33:10,912 --> 04:33:17,956
Separately, the same bucket holds customer support transcripts that must be erased within 30 days of a customer's deletion request.

2826
04:33:18,702 --> 04:33:28,112
A data engineer proposes enabling S3 object lock in compliance mode on the bucket with a 7-year retention period. What is the problem and what should be

2827
04:33:28,112 --> 04:33:29,956
done? A. Nothing.

2828
04:33:30,592 --> 04:33:33,156
Compliance mode satisfies both requirements.

2829
04:33:33,838 --> 04:33:34,112
B

2830
04:33:36,558 --> 04:33:39,812
Compliance mode would make the support transcripts undeletable.

2831
04:33:40,672 --> 04:33:45,188
Separate the two data types into different buckets with different policies.

2832
04:33:45,792 --> 04:33:46,032
C.

2833
04:33:46,702 --> 04:33:52,260
Use governance mode instead so administrators can delete transcripts when asked.

2834
04:33:52,832 --> 04:33:53,072
D.

2835
04:33:53,678 --> 04:33:58,724
Enable versioning instead of object lock and delete old versions as required.

2836
04:34:00,046 --> 04:34:01,392
Pause and decide.

2837
04:34:08,032 --> 04:34:16,192
The answer is B, and option C is the instructive wrong answer because it is a compromise that feigns both requirements

2838
04:34:16,432 --> 04:34:18,244
slightly rather than one badly.

2839
04:34:19,950 --> 04:34:23,876
Governance mode makes the transcripts deletable, which was the goal.

2840
04:34:24,814 --> 04:34:32,644
Simultaneously makes the trade records deletable by anyone holding the bypass permission, which breaks the requirement that no employee can remove them.

2841
04:34:33,966 --> 04:34:40,740
When two requirements genuinely conflict on the same objects, the exam expects separation rather than a middle setting.

2842
04:34:41,934 --> 04:34:49,888
That generalizes well beyond object lock and it applies to encryption keys, permissions, and lifecycle rules

2843
04:34:50,048 --> 04:34:50,688
too.

2844
04:34:51,822 --> 04:34:58,244
These come up as distractors more often than as answers. So knowing when each does nothing is the useful part.

2845
04:34:58,608 --> 04:35:07,684
Transfer acceleration works by having the upload enter the AWS network at a nearby edge location and travel the rest of the way over AWS's backbone.

2846
04:35:08,558 --> 04:35:12,516
That helps when a user in Sydney is uploading to a bucket in Ireland.

2847
04:35:13,006 --> 04:35:17,828
It does nothing at all for an EC2 instance uploading to a bucket in the same region.

2848
04:35:18,542 --> 04:35:22,052
That is because there is no long public internet path to shorten.

2849
04:35:22,958 --> 04:35:30,948
And multi-part upload is the reason the incomplete multi-part lifecycle rule from earlier exists, because those parts persist when an upload is interrupted.

2850
04:35:32,448 --> 04:35:38,628
Storage per gigabyte is the number everyone thinks about, and it is often not the one that decides a question.

2851
04:35:39,182 --> 04:35:43,716
The request row is why the small file problem keeps recurring across this course.

2852
04:35:44,526 --> 04:35:51,108
That is because a query touching 50 million objects makes 50 million requests before it reads anything useful.

2853
04:35:51,488 --> 04:35:58,852
The retrieval row is why moving frequently read data to infrequent access can increase the total bin rather than reduce it.

2854
04:35:59,630 --> 04:36:06,564
That is because you now pay per gigabyte every time it is read. And the transfer row has a rule worth memorizing.

2855
04:36:07,246 --> 04:36:08,996
Data into S3 is free.

2856
04:36:09,454 --> 04:36:14,276
Data within a region is free, data out to the internet or to another region is not.

2857
04:36:15,888 --> 04:36:21,732
This table collects the module and the fifth row deserves the emphasis.

2858
04:36:22,768 --> 04:36:32,292
Moving data to a cheaper class is the obvious cost lever and it has two ways of backfiring, both of which the exam tests.

2859
04:36:33,166 --> 04:36:40,484
Objects below 128 KB are billed at 128 KB in the infrequent access and glacier classes.

2860
04:36:40,848 --> 04:36:46,116
And objects deleted or transitioned before the minimum duration are charged for the remainder anyway.

2861
04:36:46,990 --> 04:36:55,268
So, a life cycle rule written without checking the object size distribution and the retention period can genuinely increase spend.

2862
04:36:56,048 --> 04:36:58,532
That is a satisfying question to be asked.

2863
04:37:00,174 --> 04:37:03,300
A company's S3 bill has grown steadily for a year.

2864
04:37:03,648 --> 04:37:08,688
Meanwhile, the volume of data the team knows about has stayed roughly constant at 30TB.

2865
04:37:11,054 --> 04:37:14,948
Storage Lens reports about 90 TB of build storage in the bucket.

2866
04:37:16,686 --> 04:37:20,100
Versioning was enabled two years ago after an accidental deletion.

2867
04:37:20,910 --> 04:37:24,036
A nightly job overrides a set of aggregate files.

2868
04:37:24,208 --> 04:37:31,332
Some large uploads from a flaky on-premises connection have failed over the year. Which combination explains the gap and fixes it?

2869
04:37:31,728 --> 04:37:33,636
a Enable S3.

2870
04:37:34,574 --> 04:37:38,564
Intelligent tiering so infrequently accessed data moves to cheaper tiers.

2871
04:37:40,430 --> 04:37:50,340
Add lifecycle rules to expire non-current versions and to abort incomplete multipart uploads. C. Disable versioning to stop old versions accumulating.

2872
04:37:50,848 --> 04:37:55,844
D. Enable cross region replication so data can be deleted from the primary bucket.

2873
04:37:56,526 --> 04:37:57,604
Pause and decide.

2874
04:38:04,238 --> 04:38:11,396
The answer is B, and this question is constructed so that each detail in the situation maps to one half of the answer.

2875
04:38:13,664 --> 04:38:18,820
The nightly override plus versioning explains the accumulating old versions.

2876
04:38:19,598 --> 04:38:28,228
The flaky connection and failed large uploads explains the incomplete multi-part parts which are billed and do not appear when you list the bucket.

2877
04:38:30,144 --> 04:38:35,652
Option C is worth noting because it is the intuitive response and it is doubly wrong.

2878
04:38:36,142 --> 04:38:42,788
It leaves every existing version in place and it removes the protection the team deliberately added after losing data.

2879
04:38:43,184 --> 04:38:49,504
Fixing a cost problem by removing a safety mechanism is a shape the exam consistently marks down.

2880
04:38:50,990 --> 04:38:57,220
Scenario 3 in this module was an application of the rule in the callout, so it is worth stating generally.

2881
04:38:57,838 --> 04:39:00,420
Prefixes are free and infinitely flexible.

2882
04:39:00,910 --> 04:39:06,692
They carry no policy of their own beyond what a lifecycle filter or an IAM path can express.

2883
04:39:07,534 --> 04:39:09,504
Buckets carry policy.

2884
04:39:10,062 --> 04:39:18,304
Retention settings, default encryption, replication configuration, object lock, and ownership all attach at

2885
04:39:18,464 --> 04:39:19,204
bucket level.

2886
04:39:21,198 --> 04:39:25,060
So the design question is never how much data goes in a bucket.

2887
04:39:25,966 --> 04:39:29,348
It is whether everything in it can share one set of rules.

2888
04:39:30,254 --> 04:39:36,484
The last bullet points forward to module 17 where access patterns across accounts get proper treatment.

2889
04:39:37,966 --> 04:39:42,144
Access points are the modern answer to a problem every large lake develops.

2890
04:39:42,384 --> 04:39:46,916
A single bucket policy that started clean grows a clause per consuming team.

2891
04:39:48,864 --> 04:39:55,396
After two years, nobody can safely change it because nobody knows which clause protects what.

2892
04:39:56,304 --> 04:40:01,316
An access point is a named endpoint attached to the bucket with its own policy.

2893
04:40:01,744 --> 04:40:07,268
So each consumer gets a door sized for them and the bucket policy stays simple.

2894
04:40:09,230 --> 04:40:11,588
The requester pays bullet is narrower.

2895
04:40:12,526 --> 04:40:20,612
It appears in questions about publishing a large public dataset where the publisher does not want to fund everyone as his downloads.

2896
04:40:21,902 --> 04:40:27,012
An application writes session records to an Amazon DynamoDB table.

2897
04:40:27,726 --> 04:40:33,668
Each session should be retained for exactly 90 days after its last activity, then removed.

2898
04:40:34,446 --> 04:40:38,788
Sessions have widely different lifespans, so their expiry dates differ.

2899
04:40:39,630 --> 04:40:47,044
The team also needs expired sessions archived to Amazon S3 for annual reporting before they disappear.

2900
04:40:47,630 --> 04:40:54,404
A proposal is to run a nightly lambda function that scans the table and deletes expired items.

2901
04:40:55,790 --> 04:40:58,244
What should the data engineer implement instead?

2902
04:41:00,430 --> 04:41:06,276
Keep the nightly scan but increase the table's provisioned read capacity so it completes faster.

2903
04:41:07,022 --> 04:41:15,236
b Enable Dynamo DB TTL on an expired attribute and use Dynamo DB streams to archive deleted items to Amazon S3.

2904
04:41:15,824 --> 04:41:21,316
C. Apply an S3 lifecycle expiration rule of 90 days to the DynamoDB table.

2905
04:41:21,984 --> 04:41:22,304
D.

2906
04:41:22,784 --> 04:41:27,844
Export the table to Amazon S3 every 90 days and recreate it empty.

2907
04:41:30,670 --> 04:41:32,004
Pause and decide.

2908
04:41:38,542 --> 04:41:44,260
The answer is B, and the phrase that decides it is that sessions have different lifespans.

2909
04:41:44,560 --> 04:41:52,196
Any mechanism based on the age of the data as a whole fails because two records written on the same day should expire months apart.

2910
04:41:52,560 --> 04:41:56,580
Time to live is per item, so each record carries its own expiry.

2911
04:41:57,134 --> 04:42:06,980
The archiving half is what makes B complete rather than merely correct because a TTL deletion emits a stream record containing the item.

2912
04:42:07,470 --> 04:42:11,236
So a lambda function can write it to S3 on the way out.

2913
04:42:11,950 --> 04:42:18,980
Option C is the category error worth naming since lifecycle rules belong to S3 and nothing else.

2914
04:42:20,622 --> 04:42:21,680
5 questions.

2915
04:42:21,998 --> 04:42:30,800
And the fourth is the one that separates two plausible options, most often in this domain. Storage class changes affect what you pay to keep data.

2916
04:42:31,118 --> 04:42:35,140
Partitioning and file format changes affect what you pay to read it.

2917
04:42:35,790 --> 04:42:39,440
Athena bills bytes scanned regardless of storage class.

2918
04:42:39,600 --> 04:42:51,140
So moving data to standard infrequent access does nothing for a query bill and adds a retrieval charge. Getting that distinction right eliminates a distractor in almost every S3 cost question.

2919
04:42:51,822 --> 04:42:56,772
Next module covers open table formats, which is where several problems.

2920
04:42:57,280 --> 04:42:59,428
This module could only describe.

2921
04:43:01,033 --> 04:43:02,213
Finally gets solved.

2922
04:43:03,854 --> 04:43:09,200
This is the module with the largest gap between the current exam and every question bank in circulation.

2923
04:43:09,550 --> 04:43:14,020
Apache IC work appeared 29 times in the bank analyze for this course.

2924
04:43:14,446 --> 04:43:19,236
Every one of those was as a file format sitting alongside Parquet and ORC.

2925
04:43:20,174 --> 04:43:22,960
That is not what the exam guide now asks about.

2926
04:43:23,200 --> 04:43:29,668
Open table formats became a named skill in version 1.1 published in December 2025.

2927
04:43:30,000 --> 04:43:33,892
S3 tables was added to the in-scope service list at the same time.

2928
04:43:34,880 --> 04:43:40,160
Modules 1, 2, and 3 each promised that this module would cover it properly.

2929
04:43:40,526 --> 04:43:41,200
It does.

2930
04:43:41,440 --> 04:43:43,044
Four worked scenarios.

2931
04:43:44,558 --> 04:43:51,236
You will not be asked to recite this hierarchy, and understanding its shape explains everything else in the module.

2932
04:43:51,790 --> 04:43:58,852
Reading a table means following the chain downward, and at each level there is enough information to skip whole branches.

2933
04:43:59,502 --> 04:44:05,252
A manifest records the minimum and maximum value of each column across the files it lists.

2934
04:44:06,126 --> 04:44:12,740
So a query filtering on a date can discard entire manifests without opening a single data file.

2935
04:44:13,582 --> 04:44:21,860
That is why IZBERG query planning stays fast on tables with millions of files, where listing an S3 prefix would take minutes.

2936
04:44:23,200 --> 04:44:28,900
And a commit is simply writing new files down the chain, then swapping the pointer at the top.

2937
04:44:30,478 --> 04:44:36,516
Start with what is actually broken, because the solution only makes sense once the problem is clear.

2938
04:44:38,094 --> 04:44:43,840
When a query engine reads a data lake, it lists the files under a prefix and reads them.

2939
04:44:44,366 --> 04:44:52,932
Nothing anywhere records which files belong together, what the schema was last Tuesday, or whether a write finished or died halfway.

2940
04:44:54,606 --> 04:44:57,636
Everything the word table implies is absent.

2941
04:44:58,958 --> 04:45:03,840
Atomicity, a consistent view, the ability to change one row.

2942
04:45:04,462 --> 04:45:11,492
That works perfectly well for append-only data, nobody ever edits, which describes a great many real datasets.

2943
04:45:12,206 --> 04:45:17,860
It falls apart the moment data must be corrected or deleted to satisfy a privacy request

2944
04:45:18,638 --> 04:45:21,348
or written at the same time as somebody is reading it.

2945
04:45:22,318 --> 04:45:26,180
Those three requirements are what created open table formats.

2946
04:45:27,726 --> 04:45:32,676
Recognize these five because every one of them has already appeared in this course.

2947
04:45:33,294 --> 04:45:35,396
Module 2 described DMS.

2948
04:45:35,680 --> 04:45:40,516
Writing change records into S3 and the difficulty of reconciling them into current state.

2949
04:45:41,102 --> 04:45:49,380
Module 8 described a privacy deletion request for a single customer whose records sit inside files shared with thousands of others.

2950
04:45:49,902 --> 04:45:55,652
Module 3 describes streaming rights, producing partial state that a reader might catch mid-flight.

2951
04:45:56,494 --> 04:46:00,400
They are not 5 separate problems needing 5 separate solutions.

2952
04:46:00,782 --> 04:46:07,268
They are all the same underlying gap, which is that a directory of files has no transactional layer above it.

2953
04:46:07,982 --> 04:46:10,276
Iceberg adds precisely that layer.

2954
04:46:11,086 --> 04:46:17,252
Once you see them that way, a whole class of exam question collapses into a single recognizable question.

2955
04:46:18,926 --> 04:46:24,420
The mechanism is simpler than people expect and worth holding as a single idea.

2956
04:46:24,910 --> 04:46:33,604
Alongside the data files, Heisenberg maintains metadata recording precisely which files constitute the table at each point in time.

2957
04:46:34,286 --> 04:46:38,116
A reader consults that metadata rather than listing it directly.

2958
04:46:38,958 --> 04:46:45,540
A writer adds new data files and then atomically publishes new metadata pointing at them.

2959
04:46:46,080 --> 04:46:53,440
Until that final publish happens, readers continue to see the previous state, complete and consistent, with no partial results.

2960
04:46:53,680 --> 04:46:56,132
Change one pointer and the table changes.

2961
04:46:56,974 --> 04:47:01,028
That single idea produces every capability on the next slide.

2962
04:47:01,358 --> 04:47:08,228
Transactions, time travel, safe schema changes, and concurrent writers all fall out of the same mechanism.

2963
04:47:08,782 --> 04:47:12,996
That is why they arrive together rather than as separate features.

2964
04:47:14,670 --> 04:47:21,924
Partition evolution deserves the call out because module 8 said layout decisions are effectively permanent, and this is the exception.

2965
04:47:22,318 --> 04:47:29,188
In a plain lake, changing from monthly to daily partitions means rewriting every object you have.

2966
04:47:29,920 --> 04:47:34,756
Iceberg records the partitioning scheme as part of the metadata per snapshot.

2967
04:47:35,040 --> 04:47:44,836
So new data can be written daily while 5 years of monthly history stays exactly where it is. A query spanning both is planned correctly across the two schemes.

2968
04:47:45,838 --> 04:47:50,596
That is a genuine capability, no amount of careful directory naming provides.

2969
04:47:52,160 --> 04:47:59,524
This is the iceberg internal most worth understanding because it explains a performance complaint that otherwise looks mysterious.

2970
04:48:00,320 --> 04:48:07,268
Copy on write does the work at write time, rewriting whole data files without the deleted rows.

2971
04:48:08,160 --> 04:48:11,396
So readers see a clean table and pay nothing extra.

2972
04:48:12,302 --> 04:48:17,572
Merge on read does the minimum at write time, recording separately which rows no longer count.

2973
04:48:17,920 --> 04:48:22,020
Every subsequent reader then has to apply those deletes while scanning.

2974
04:48:22,894 --> 04:48:29,188
That makes streaming updates cheap to write and gradually makes queries slower until compaction folds the delete files in.

2975
04:48:30,030 --> 04:48:36,676
So a table taking frequent small updates and getting steadily slower is often merged on read without enough compaction.

2976
04:48:38,414 --> 04:48:42,160
This solves a problem that costs real money in plain data leaks.

2977
04:48:42,800 --> 04:48:49,988
Suppose a table is partitioned by a string column holding the date and an analyst filters on the event timestamp instead.

2978
04:48:50,894 --> 04:48:59,460
The filter is logically correct, the results are right, and the engine prunes nothing at all because it cannot connect the timestamp to the partition column.

2979
04:49:00,622 --> 04:49:03,236
The query scans everything and the bill reflects it.

2980
04:49:03,822 --> 04:49:10,788
Iceberg stores that relationship as a transform so it knows the partition derives from the day part of the timestamp.

2981
04:49:11,120 --> 04:49:18,948
A filter on the timestamp then prunes automatically, and analysts stop needing to know the physical layout to write an efficient query.

2982
04:49:20,686 --> 04:49:26,884
The column identifier point is the mechanism and it is worth understanding rather than memorizing.

2983
04:49:27,726 --> 04:49:30,596
In a name-based format, matching happens by name.

2984
04:49:30,990 --> 04:49:36,196
A file written before a rename and one written after look like they have different columns entirely.

2985
04:49:37,070 --> 04:49:44,004
Iceberg assigns each column a stable numeric identifier at creation and tracks the name separately.

2986
04:49:44,910 --> 04:49:51,972
Rename the column and every historical file still maps correctly because the identifier never changed.

2987
04:49:52,782 --> 04:50:01,924
That is why iceberg schema changes are described as safe, while the same operation on a plain parquet dataset is a migration project.

2988
04:50:03,502 --> 04:50:06,756
The call-out describes the scenario the exam likes.

2989
04:50:07,088 --> 04:50:13,540
A transformation job has a bug and writes wrong values over a table and nobody notices for a day.

2990
04:50:14,478 --> 04:50:23,248
Without iceberg, the recovery is restoring from backup or reprocessing from raw, both of which take time and lose anything written since.

2991
04:50:23,950 --> 04:50:30,916
With Iceberg, it is a rollback to the snapshot before the bad job ran, which is close to instantaneous because no data moves.

2992
04:50:31,854 --> 04:50:34,128
Only a metadata pointer changes.

2993
04:50:34,606 --> 04:50:37,476
The reproducibility point matters for regulated reporting.

2994
04:50:38,382 --> 04:50:44,388
Showing exactly what the data looked like when a figure was published is an audit requirement.

2995
04:50:45,678 --> 04:50:50,820
The right audit publish pattern is worth knowing because it answers something this course has raised repeatedly.

2996
04:50:51,566 --> 04:50:56,208
Module 7 made the point that a job succeeding tells you nothing about whether the numbers are right.

2997
04:50:56,494 --> 04:50:59,460
Branches make the check enforceable rather than advisory.

2998
04:51:00,334 --> 04:51:07,968
The job writes into a branch, quality checks run against that branch, and only if they pass does it merge into main.

2999
04:51:08,942 --> 04:51:12,804
Consumers query main throughout and never see the intermediate state at all.

3000
04:51:13,678 --> 04:51:21,408
Compare that with checking quality after publishing, where wrong numbers were visible to everyone for however long the check took to run.

3001
04:51:22,798 --> 04:51:29,060
Scenario 3 in this module asks you to roll a table back to the snapshot from before a faulty job.

3002
04:51:29,646 --> 04:51:31,300
This slide is how you find it.

3003
04:51:31,886 --> 04:51:37,412
The snapshots metadata table lists every snapshot with its timestamp and its identifier.

3004
04:51:38,254 --> 04:51:42,692
So a query filtering on the time the job started returns exactly the one you need.

3005
04:51:43,054 --> 04:51:49,540
That turns a recovery from an anxious guess into an ordinary lookup, which matters when 200 analysts are waiting.

3006
04:51:50,414 --> 04:51:56,964
The files table is the other operationally useful one because it reports the size of every data file.

3007
04:51:57,422 --> 04:52:03,396
That is how you demonstrate a table has accumulated a small file problem rather than merely suspecting it.

3008
04:52:05,070 --> 04:52:10,724
All three solve broadly the same problem, and the exam does not test their internals against each other.

3009
04:52:11,278 --> 04:52:16,580
What it tests is which one AWS has built around, and the answer is unambiguous.

3010
04:52:17,422 --> 04:52:22,756
Iceberg is the format named in the exam guide scale. The format S3 tables is built on.

3011
04:52:23,694 --> 04:52:28,836
The one with native support across Athena, Redshift, EMR, GUE and Firehose.

3012
04:52:29,248 --> 04:52:34,788
Houdi and Delta are genuinely supported by BU and EMR, and they appear as options.

3013
04:52:35,598 --> 04:52:36,688
The rule is simple.

3014
04:52:36,928 --> 04:52:41,252
If the question names Hoodi or Delta explicitly, work with that.

3015
04:52:41,934 --> 04:52:45,124
If it describes a requirement, answer iceberg.

3016
04:52:46,862 --> 04:52:55,968
A healthcare company stores 10 years of patient interaction records as parquet files in Amazon S3, partitioned by month, queried with Amazon Athena.

3017
04:52:56,334 --> 04:53:02,852
Under privacy legislation, the company must erase all records for a given patient within 30 days of a request.

3018
04:53:03,598 --> 04:53:06,500
Receives around 40 such requests a month.

3019
04:53:07,342 --> 04:53:14,628
Currently, an engineer identifies every affected file, rewrites each one without the patient's rows, and replaces it.

3020
04:53:15,168 --> 04:53:21,924
The process takes two days per request and has twice produced corrupted partitions when a rewrite failed midway.

3021
04:53:22,830 --> 04:53:24,388
What should the company implement?

3022
04:53:25,070 --> 04:53:31,076
a convert the dataset to Apache ICWork tables and use delete statements to remove the rows.

3023
04:53:31,662 --> 04:53:37,572
b Partition the data by patient identifier so entire files can be deleted per request.

3024
04:53:39,086 --> 04:53:39,328
C

3025
04:53:40,590 --> 04:53:45,988
Enable S3 versioning so a failed rewrite can be rolled back to the previous version.

3026
04:53:46,448 --> 04:53:46,688
D.

3027
04:53:47,168 --> 04:53:51,364
Move the data to Amazon Redshift where delete statements are supported.

3028
04:53:51,982 --> 04:53:53,124
Pause and decide.

3029
04:53:59,854 --> 04:54:01,552
The answer is A.

3030
04:54:02,606 --> 04:54:06,212
Two harms in the question are both addressed by the same change.

3031
04:54:07,790 --> 04:54:12,484
Deletion becomes a single statement rather than two days of manual file surgery.

3032
04:54:12,992 --> 04:54:20,516
Because the right is atomic, a failure midway leaves the table exactly as it was rather than corrupting a partition.

3033
04:54:21,454 --> 04:54:30,468
Option B is worth studying because it is the answer a candidate reaches for if they have learned partitioning without learning its limits.

3034
04:54:30,752 --> 04:54:38,980
Partitioning by a high cardinality key like patient identifier creates the overpartitioning failure module 8 warned about.

3035
04:54:39,886 --> 04:54:45,028
It optimizes for the rare deletion at the cost of every ordinary query.

3036
04:54:46,766 --> 04:54:52,196
This is the part left out of enthusiastic descriptions and it is where several exam questions live.

3037
04:54:53,550 --> 04:54:57,124
Iceberg achieves atomicity by never modifying files in place.

3038
04:54:57,472 --> 04:55:04,032
An update writes new data files and new metadata, leaving the old ones present but unreferenced.

3039
04:55:04,592 --> 04:55:10,180
A delete may write a delete file recording which rows to ignore rather than removing anything.

3040
04:55:10,512 --> 04:55:19,748
Do that thousands of times and you accumulate small files, stale snapshots, and often data, all of which cost storage and slow queries down.

3041
04:55:20,622 --> 04:55:24,452
Maintenance is not optional housekeeping, you get to the eventually.

3042
04:55:24,910 --> 04:55:31,364
It is a required part of running iceberg and a table nobody maintains gets measurably worse every week.

3043
04:55:32,110 --> 04:55:35,524
That is why the next few slides matter as much as the capabilities did.

3044
04:55:37,166 --> 04:55:42,628
Learn these three by name because S3 tables is defined by performing them for you.

3045
04:55:44,078 --> 04:55:53,252
Compaction addresses the small file problem this course has raised in four separate modules now appearing as a consequence of frequent writes.

3046
04:55:54,158 --> 04:55:57,924
Snapshot expiration is the one with the trade-off worth understanding.

3047
04:55:58,702 --> 04:56:06,084
Snapshots are what make time travel and rollback possible, and they hold references to data files that would otherwise be deleted.

3048
04:56:06,958 --> 04:56:13,284
Keep them for 90 days and you can travel back 90 days and you pay for 90 days of superseded data.

3049
04:56:13,742 --> 04:56:18,564
That retention setting is a genuine business decision rather than a technical default.

3050
04:56:19,982 --> 04:56:26,788
Candidates tend to see this as a binary between S3 tables and building compaction jobs yourself.

3051
04:56:26,992 --> 04:56:29,284
And there is a middle option worth knowing.

3052
04:56:30,062 --> 04:56:38,512
The Glue data catalog can run table optimization on iceberg tables living in ordinary general purpose buckets.

3053
04:56:39,118 --> 04:56:43,652
It performs the same compaction and snapshot management on a schedule you configure.

3054
04:56:44,046 --> 04:56:49,156
That matters when a team wants managed maintenance but cannot move data into table buckets.

3055
04:56:49,392 --> 04:56:54,596
Perhaps the same bucket holds other objects or specific lifecycle rules are already in place.

3056
04:56:55,470 --> 04:57:00,932
The last bullet is the honest default and it is exactly what scenario 2 describes happening.

3057
04:57:02,542 --> 04:57:08,804
Sort and Zorder compaction arrived in 2025 and no older material covers them, so they are worth knowing.

3058
04:57:09,312 --> 04:57:11,844
The connection to module 8 is direct.

3059
04:57:12,112 --> 04:57:18,052
Packet stores minimum and maximum values per block, and a query can skip a block whose range cannot match.

3060
04:57:18,830 --> 04:57:23,652
On randomly ordered data, every block spans the whole range and nothing is skippable.

3061
04:57:24,398 --> 04:57:30,500
Sort compaction physically reorders rows during compaction so those statistics become meaningful.

3062
04:57:31,278 --> 04:57:34,276
ZORDER does the same across several columns at once.

3063
04:57:35,152 --> 04:57:42,244
That is what you want when queries filter on different combinations of two or three fields rather than always the same one.

3064
04:57:43,854 --> 04:57:48,132
Module 8 introduced general purpose buckets and directory buckets.

3065
04:57:48,942 --> 04:57:50,820
Table buckets are the third type.

3066
04:57:51,566 --> 04:57:55,108
They were announced at reInvent in December 2024.

3067
04:57:55,726 --> 04:58:01,792
Added to the exams in Scope Service list a year later in the version 1.1 revision.

3068
04:58:02,352 --> 04:58:11,972
The distinguishing feature is not that they store iceberg tables because a general purpose bucket does that perfectly well and many organizations use it that way.

3069
04:58:12,432 --> 04:58:21,316
It is that the maintenance described on the previous slides happens automatically on a schedule without anyone writing or operating a compaction job at all.

3070
04:58:21,632 --> 04:58:23,588
That is the entire proposition.

3071
04:58:24,366 --> 04:58:33,700
It makes S3 tables an operational overhead answer rather than a capability answer, which is exactly how the exam will frame it.

3072
04:58:35,312 --> 04:58:40,723
The permissions point is more significant than it looks and it answers a class of question.

3073
04:58:43,438 --> 04:58:53,348
In an ordinary bucket, granting a team access to one table means writing a policy against an S3 prefix and hoping the layout never changes.

3074
04:58:54,030 --> 04:59:01,316
A table bucket has tables as first class resources with their own ARNs and their own resource policies.

3075
04:59:01,934 --> 04:59:04,612
So a grant is genuinely on the table.

3076
04:59:05,998 --> 04:59:07,844
The integration bullet matters too.

3077
04:59:08,974 --> 04:59:12,900
Table buckets are not automatically visible to Athena and Redshift.

3078
04:59:13,838 --> 04:59:21,732
You integrate the bucket with SageMaker Lakehouse, and that is what makes the tables discoverable to the analytics services.

3079
04:59:23,470 --> 04:59:27,872
The honest framing is that managed maintenance is not free maintenance.

3080
04:59:28,112 --> 04:59:31,172
It is maintenance you buy rather than build.

3081
04:59:31,632 --> 04:59:35,972
Compaction is build by the objects and the gigabytes it processes.

3082
04:59:36,272 --> 04:59:43,204
So a table receiving constant small writes generates a great deal of compaction and a corresponding charge.

3083
04:59:44,142 --> 04:59:52,752
For a team of three supporting 15 pipelines, that is almost certainly the right trade because the alternative consumes engineering time

3084
04:59:52,912 --> 04:59:53,796
they do not have.

3085
04:59:54,542 --> 05:00:02,532
For a large platform team with existing Spark infrastructure and predictable batch rights, running compaction themselves may well be cheaper.

3086
05:00:04,206 --> 05:00:08,068
The exam signals which situation you are in by describing the team.

3087
05:00:09,710 --> 05:00:16,356
Both store iceberg tables and both work with the same query engines. So the question is never about capability.

3088
05:00:17,312 --> 05:00:19,460
It is about who runs the maintenance.

3089
05:00:20,430 --> 05:00:28,912
If the scenario mentions a team without capacity to operate compaction jobs or describes tables degrading because nobody maintains

3090
05:00:29,358 --> 05:00:31,588
them, that is, S3 tables.

3091
05:00:32,334 --> 05:00:35,204
A general purpose bucket keeps more flexibility.

3092
05:00:36,110 --> 05:00:44,260
Use it when the scenario needs specific lifecycle rules, particular storage classes, or table data sitting beside other objects.

3093
05:00:45,454 --> 05:00:54,020
The performance figures are worth remembering because AWS codes up to 3 times faster queries and 10 times the transactions per second.

3094
05:00:55,214 --> 05:01:05,072
A retailer streams point of sale transactions into Apache ICB tables in a general purpose, Amazon S3 bucket using Amazon Data Firehose.

3095
05:01:05,326 --> 05:01:10,272
Lights arrive continuously and the tables receive frequent small updates.

3096
05:01:10,894 --> 05:01:17,220
Over six months, query times have roughly tripled and storage costs have grown faster than the data volume.

3097
05:01:17,632 --> 05:01:25,732
The data platform team is three people supporting 15 other pipelines and has no capacity to build and operate table maintenance jobs.

3098
05:01:26,638 --> 05:01:27,908
What should the team do?

3099
05:01:30,432 --> 05:01:38,180
Migrate the tables to Amazon S3 tables which performs compaction and snapshot management automatically.

3100
05:01:39,246 --> 05:01:45,956
b Write a scheduled AWS glue job to run compaction and expire snapshots nightly.

3101
05:01:46,318 --> 05:01:52,324
C. Increase the firehose buffer interval so fewer larger files are written.

3102
05:01:53,312 --> 05:01:53,632
D

3103
05:01:54,926 --> 05:02:00,516
Move the tables to Amazon Redshift so that storage is managed by the warehouse.

3104
05:02:01,134 --> 05:02:02,372
Pause and decide.

3105
05:02:09,072 --> 05:02:10,832
The answer is A.

3106
05:02:11,232 --> 05:02:13,924
The two symptoms together are diagnostic.

3107
05:02:14,254 --> 05:02:22,980
Queries slowing points at accumulating small files and storage growing faster than the data points at snapshots and orphan files that nothing has expired.

3108
05:02:23,312 --> 05:02:25,860
Both are the maintenance debt described earlier.

3109
05:02:26,638 --> 05:02:30,432
Option B is the distractor that tests whether you read the constraint.

3110
05:02:30,702 --> 05:02:37,792
That is because it is genuinely the right technical fix, and the question explicitly says the team has no capacity to operate it.

3111
05:02:38,190 --> 05:02:41,636
That is the same shape as scenario 2 in module 3.

3112
05:02:42,478 --> 05:02:48,292
A blue job was the correct optimization offered to a team that had asked for less to run.

3113
05:02:49,870 --> 05:02:56,228
Module 1 described the lake house idea as one copy of the data described once, readable by every engine.

3114
05:02:56,846 --> 05:02:58,948
This slide is that claim made specific.

3115
05:02:59,406 --> 05:03:02,752
The Athena row is the one that changes what is possible day to

3116
05:03:02,752 --> 05:03:09,764
day. That is because merge, update, and delete on lake data are ordinary SQL rather than a pipeline.

3117
05:03:10,510 --> 05:03:12,996
The firehose row connects to module 3.

3118
05:03:13,838 --> 05:03:20,900
Iceberg tables were listed among the destinations there and flagged as expressing updates and deletes rather than only appends.

3119
05:03:21,742 --> 05:03:27,460
That is unusual for a streaming delivery service, and it is the kind of detail that separates two options.

3120
05:03:29,072 --> 05:03:37,060
This combination appears in questions because it is genuinely useful and it genuinely creates the problem this module has been describing.

3121
05:03:37,472 --> 05:03:44,708
Streaming gives you data within seconds. Iceberg gives you the ability to update and delete rows within that data.

3122
05:03:45,646 --> 05:03:51,940
Together they let a lake table stay current with an operational database, which is a real and common requirement.

3123
05:03:52,432 --> 05:04:00,644
The cost is that a stream writing every few seconds produces thousands of small files a day, plus delete files if updates are involved.

3124
05:04:01,294 --> 05:04:09,604
That is precisely the workload where unmaintained tables degrade fastest, and it is why scenario 2 in this module is a streaming scenario.

3125
05:04:11,214 --> 05:04:19,012
SageMaker Lake House is a name for something the course has been building towards rather than a new concept to learn from scratch.

3126
05:04:22,126 --> 05:04:25,668
Data sits in S3 tables and in Redshift.

3127
05:04:25,872 --> 05:04:32,932
It is described once, permissioned once in lake formation, and exposed through a catalog that speaks the Iceberg standard.

3128
05:04:33,870 --> 05:04:38,212
So any engine that understands Iceberg can query it without a bespoke connector.

3129
05:04:38,862 --> 05:04:44,512
The exam guide added it in the December 2025 revision, so no older material covers it.

3130
05:04:45,102 --> 05:04:54,852
Module 12 covers cataloging in detail and module 20 covers the governance layer. So treat this slide as the shape rather than the whole picture.

3131
05:04:56,494 --> 05:05:03,812
A data engineer runs a nightly blue job that rebuilds a curated iceberg table used by 200 analysts.

3132
05:05:04,526 --> 05:05:11,632
Last night, the job ran with a faulty join condition and wrote incorrect values across the entire table.

3133
05:05:11,872 --> 05:05:14,372
The error was discovered at 10 this morning.

3134
05:05:15,214 --> 05:05:19,108
The raw source data is intact in Amazon S3.

3135
05:05:20,302 --> 05:05:23,460
Rebuilding the table from RAW takes about 4 hours.

3136
05:05:24,174 --> 05:05:27,844
Analysts have been querying the incorrect data since the job completed.

3137
05:05:28,686 --> 05:05:31,076
What is the fastest way to restore correct data?

3138
05:05:32,112 --> 05:05:32,432
a

3139
05:05:33,646 --> 05:05:37,732
Restore the S3 objects from the previous versions using S3 versioning.

3140
05:05:38,192 --> 05:05:43,012
b Roll the ICBug table back to the snapshot taken before the faulty job ran.

3141
05:05:43,552 --> 05:05:43,872
C.

3142
05:05:44,462 --> 05:05:48,260
Fix the join condition and rerun the job to rebuild from raw data.

3143
05:05:48,686 --> 05:05:48,992
D.

3144
05:05:49,358 --> 05:05:52,912
Restore the table from the most recent AWS backup recovery point.

3145
05:05:54,352 --> 05:05:55,172
And decide.

3146
05:06:01,550 --> 05:06:10,192
The answer is B, and the reason it beats every other option is that nothing moves. The data files from before the faulty job are still present.

3147
05:06:10,352 --> 05:06:16,356
That is because iceberg never deleted them and the previous snapshot still points at exactly the right set.

3148
05:06:16,752 --> 05:06:19,460
Rolling back changes which snapshot is current.

3149
05:06:20,174 --> 05:06:25,252
That is a metadata operation measured in seconds against 4 hours of reprocessing.

3150
05:06:26,158 --> 05:06:29,220
Option C is not wrong as an eventual step.

3151
05:06:30,512 --> 05:06:33,156
You will fix the join and rerun tonight.

3152
05:06:34,030 --> 05:06:41,508
The question asked for the fastest way to restore correct data now, and 200 analysts are querying wrong numbers while you decide.

3153
05:06:43,232 --> 05:06:49,316
The fourth bullet is where migrations go wrong in practice and it makes a fair exam question.

3154
05:06:49,902 --> 05:06:56,260
Once a table is iceberg, the metadata is the definitive statement of which files belong to it.

3155
05:06:57,166 --> 05:07:06,276
A job that was overlooked, still writing parquet files into the prefix produces files that physically exist and are invisible to every query.

3156
05:07:06,990 --> 05:07:08,832
No metadata references them.

3157
05:07:08,912 --> 05:07:10,308
Nothing errors anywhere.

3158
05:07:10,766 --> 05:07:15,792
Data simply goes missing and it can be weeks before anyone notices a total is low.

3159
05:07:16,272 --> 05:07:21,312
So the migration checklist starts with finding every writer, not with converting the

3160
05:07:21,312 --> 05:07:31,204
data. The in place option is attractive precisely because it does not require moving petabytes to get started, which makes the conversion itself the easy part.

3161
05:07:32,846 --> 05:07:35,620
Iceberg blurs a line that used to be clear,

3162
05:07:36,366 --> 05:07:39,716
so it is worth restating where that line now sits.

3163
05:07:41,678 --> 05:07:49,536
Before open table formats, anything needing updates had to live in a warehouse because a lake simply could not express them.

3164
05:07:50,254 --> 05:07:55,140
That is no longer true, and the deciding factor has moved back to the one module one gave you.

3165
05:07:55,534 --> 05:07:59,108
How often is it queried and by how many people at once?

3166
05:07:59,662 --> 05:08:06,180
Redshift keeps data in a format it controls with sort keys and distribution keys tuned for the queries it expects.

3167
05:08:06,816 --> 05:08:10,788
It serves hundreds of concurrent users at low latency.

3168
05:08:11,296 --> 05:08:18,180
An iceberg table in S3 is open, cheap, and readable by any engine at seconds rather than milliseconds.

3169
05:08:19,886 --> 05:08:24,676
Knowing when a technology is unnecessary is worth as much as knowing when it helps.

3170
05:08:25,294 --> 05:08:28,740
The exam does offer iceberg where it adds nothing at all.

3171
05:08:29,358 --> 05:08:37,188
Log data that arrives is queried and is never modified, has no update problem, no delete problem, and no concurrent write problem.

3172
05:08:38,030 --> 05:08:45,924
Adding iceberg to it buys a metadata layer and a permanent maintenance obligation in exchange for capabilities nobody will ever use.

3173
05:08:46,542 --> 05:08:53,696
The fourth point is a boundary worth stating plainly because iceberg does provide transactions and people overread that word.

3174
05:08:54,350 --> 05:09:01,296
It is designed for analytical tables taking periodic writes, not for thousands of concurrent small transactions per second.

3175
05:09:01,536 --> 05:09:06,116
That remains a job for an operational database as module 1 set out.

3176
05:09:07,470 --> 05:09:14,276
A logistics company appends GPS ping records to Amazon S3 as parquet, partitioned by date.

3177
05:09:15,246 --> 05:09:21,540
Records are never updated or deleted, and the retention policy expires whole partitions after two years.

3178
05:09:22,190 --> 05:09:25,856
Queries always filter on a date range and read four columns.

3179
05:09:26,414 --> 05:09:35,556
A new architect has proposed converting the dataset to Apache ICBerg tables in Amazon S3 tables citing ACID transactions and time travel.

3180
05:09:36,110 --> 05:09:37,956
How should the data engineer respond?

3181
05:09:39,982 --> 05:09:44,196
Agree. ICBerg is the current best practice for data lakes on AWS.

3182
05:09:44,816 --> 05:09:46,244
b pushback.

3183
05:09:47,086 --> 05:09:54,020
The workload is append only with whole partition expiry, so iceberg adds maintenance without solving a problem the company has.

3184
05:09:54,416 --> 05:09:54,656
C.

3185
05:09:55,086 --> 05:10:00,256
Agree, but use Apache Hoodie instead, as it is better suited to append only data.

3186
05:10:00,576 --> 05:10:00,816
D.

3187
05:10:01,536 --> 05:10:06,788
Agree and additionally partition by vehicle identifier to improve query performance.

3188
05:10:08,096 --> 05:10:09,188
Pause and decide.

3189
05:10:15,598 --> 05:10:19,940
The answer is B, and this scenario pairs with the first one deliberately.

3190
05:10:20,686 --> 05:10:26,660
There, ICBerg was clearly right because rows had to be deleted from inside shared files.

3191
05:10:27,534 --> 05:10:30,692
Here, every capability it offers goes unused.

3192
05:10:31,630 --> 05:10:39,940
Nothing updates, nothing deletes, individual rows, retention works at partition granularity, and queries already prune efficiently.

3193
05:10:40,654 --> 05:10:43,684
What iceberg would add is a maintenance obligation.

3194
05:10:44,334 --> 05:10:50,948
Recognizing that a good technology is wrong for a specific situation is a judgment the exam test deliberately.

3195
05:10:51,376 --> 05:10:55,620
It is the same reasoning as the streaming scenario in module 1.

3196
05:10:57,198 --> 05:11:01,060
The third row is the one worth carrying because the symptom is so unhelpful.

3197
05:11:01,486 --> 05:11:07,620
Files exist in the bucket, the ingestion job reports success, and queries return nothing for that data.

3198
05:11:07,856 --> 05:11:11,108
Every instinct says to look at the query or the permissions.

3199
05:11:11,950 --> 05:11:17,412
The actual cause is that iceberg reads its metadata rather than listing the directory.

3200
05:11:17,678 --> 05:11:21,028
So a file nothing points at May as well not exist.

3201
05:11:22,670 --> 05:11:30,276
That happens after a partial migration or when someone adds a new job and copies an old script that lights plain parquet.

3202
05:11:31,184 --> 05:11:36,132
It is worth recognizing because nothing in the system reports an error.

3203
05:11:37,710 --> 05:11:43,716
5 questions. And the first is the one that stops you over applying everything this module taught.

3204
05:11:44,304 --> 05:11:48,932
The exam offers iceberg where it is unnecessary, exactly as scenario 4 did.

3205
05:11:49,904 --> 05:11:54,372
Agreeing with a best practice that solves no stated problem is a wrong answer.

3206
05:11:55,264 --> 05:11:58,304
The third is the cleanest split in the module.

3207
05:11:58,670 --> 05:12:07,524
That is because S3 tables and self-managed Iceberg have identical capabilities and differ only in who does the work.

3208
05:12:08,304 --> 05:12:13,860
Next module is Amazon Redshift, the largest single module in this course.

3209
05:12:14,784 --> 05:12:19,076
It covers 325 appearances in the question bank.

3210
05:12:20,464 --> 05:12:23,044
This is the largest module in the course.

3211
05:12:23,344 --> 05:12:32,836
Amazon Redshift appeared 325 times in the bank, analyzed for this course behind only AWS Glue and Amazon S3.

3212
05:12:33,504 --> 05:12:37,984
It also has more internal detail than any other service on the exam.

3213
05:12:38,574 --> 05:12:45,828
That is because performance in Redshift depends on choices you make when you design a table rather than on capacity you add afterwards.

3214
05:12:46,144 --> 05:12:53,636
We cover the architecture, the two key choices that decide everything loading and unloading, keeping a warehouse fast.

3215
05:12:54,446 --> 05:12:57,892
The four different ways Redshift reads data.

3216
05:12:59,150 --> 05:13:06,564
It does not store four worked scenarios and one very recent change that alters a standard exam answer.

3217
05:13:08,238 --> 05:13:11,172
Two properties explain most of what follows.

3218
05:13:11,694 --> 05:13:14,500
Columnar means each column is stored separately.

3219
05:13:15,184 --> 05:13:23,184
So a query reading 3 columns of 90 touches a fraction of the data, exactly as module 8 described for

3220
05:13:23,313 --> 05:13:23,733
Parquet.

3221
05:13:24,846 --> 05:13:33,316
Massively parallel means the work is divided across nodes and across slices within each node. So a query is only as fast as its slowest slice.

3222
05:13:34,158 --> 05:13:37,060
Put those together and you get the central redshift principle.

3223
05:13:37,584 --> 05:13:41,284
Performance comes from reading less and from dividing work evenly.

3224
05:13:42,094 --> 05:13:47,460
Every distribution key, sort key, and compression decision serves one of those two goals.

3225
05:13:47,918 --> 05:13:52,100
Questions that look like tuning trivia are really asking which of the two is broken.

3226
05:13:53,614 --> 05:13:58,468
The slice is the concept to hold on to because it is the unit that matters for everything later.

3227
05:13:59,182 --> 05:14:07,140
A cluster's total parallelism is the number of nodes multiplied by the slices per node, and every table's data is spread across those slices.

3228
05:14:08,078 --> 05:14:13,796
When one slice holds far more rows than the others, that slice takes longer and the whole query waits.

3229
05:14:14,382 --> 05:14:17,540
That is the skew problem from module 5 appearing in a warehouse.

3230
05:14:19,214 --> 05:14:21,380
The exchange step is the other one to remember.

3231
05:14:22,286 --> 05:14:28,772
Moving data between nodes to satisfy a join is the single most expensive thing a redshift query does.

3232
05:14:29,230 --> 05:14:32,260
The entire point of distribution keys is to avoid it.

3233
05:14:33,838 --> 05:14:36,292
Both ends of this table carry warnings.

3234
05:14:36,864 --> 05:14:44,452
DC2 was deprecated in April 2025 with AWS directing customers to RA3 or serverless.

3235
05:14:46,062 --> 05:14:50,244
It still appears in older material as a reasonable choice for small clusters.

3236
05:14:50,894 --> 05:14:58,916
At the other end, RG instances launched in May 2026, which is 5 months after the current exam guide was published.

3237
05:14:59,470 --> 05:15:03,876
So the exam will almost certainly describe RA3 as the current generation.

3238
05:15:04,784 --> 05:15:12,036
The next slide covers what RG changes because one of those changes affects a standard exam answer about spectrum.

3239
05:15:13,998 --> 05:15:15,748
And you should know both versions.

3240
05:15:17,390 --> 05:15:22,244
The fourth bullet is the one that matters for exam answers rather than for architecture.

3241
05:15:23,086 --> 05:15:33,124
Redshift Spectrum has always worked by sending queries to a separate fleet of AWS managed nodes that scan S3, charging you per terabyte scanned on top of your cluster cost.

3242
05:15:34,030 --> 05:15:42,948
Several standard exam answers depend on that charge existing because it is what makes partitioning and columnar formats matter for spectrum queries.

3243
05:15:43,184 --> 05:15:49,344
On RGE, that fleet is gone and the scanning happens on your own cluster nodes with no separate charge.

3244
05:15:49,584 --> 05:15:51,268
The exam predates this.

3245
05:15:51,790 --> 05:15:57,508
Answer as though spectrum bins per terabyte and know that on current hardware it does not.

3246
05:15:59,024 --> 05:16:05,860
The shared catalog is the point worth carrying because it connects this module to modules 8 and 12.

3247
05:16:06,510 --> 05:16:10,916
An external table is not a redshift object with a copy of the metadata.

3248
05:16:11,662 --> 05:16:14,052
It is the same Glue Data Catalog table.

3249
05:16:14,446 --> 05:16:23,524
Athena reads So defining it once makes it available to both engines with one schema and one set of lake formation permissions.

3250
05:16:24,462 --> 05:16:26,500
The fourth bullet is the failure mode.

3251
05:16:27,086 --> 05:16:33,984
Files written into a new partition prefix are invisible to queries until that partition is registered in the catalog.

3252
05:16:34,766 --> 05:16:42,084
That is why module 2 spent time on enable update catalog and on crawlers.

3253
05:16:43,822 --> 05:16:50,564
This is the same argument as everywhere else in the course, and Redshift has one wrinkle worth knowing about.

3254
05:16:50,864 --> 05:16:58,500
A provision cluster built by the hour can be paused, which stops compute charges entirely while retaining all the data.

3255
05:16:59,406 --> 05:17:05,412
That makes it a genuinely good answer for a development warehouse used a few hours a week.

3256
05:17:06,944 --> 05:17:11,108
Serverless does the same thing automatically without anyone remembering to pause it.

3257
05:17:12,046 --> 05:17:14,692
The deciding factor is usually predictability.

3258
05:17:15,694 --> 05:17:22,724
A warehouse serving a business that queries it steadily all day, every working day, is cheaper on reserved provision nodes.

3259
05:17:23,278 --> 05:17:29,540
A warehouse used in unpredictable bursts or one nobody has measured yet is cheaper on serverless.

3260
05:17:31,214 --> 05:17:34,532
The base capacity floor moved and it changes an answer.

3261
05:17:35,086 --> 05:17:48,388
Until June 2025, the minimum was 8 RPS, which made serverless comparatively expensive for a small or intermittent workload. Older guidance says to use a small DC2 cluster for development instead.

3262
05:17:49,134 --> 05:17:54,852
With a 4 RPU floor and DC2 now deprecated, that guidance is inverted.

3263
05:17:55,854 --> 05:18:00,324
The price performance target on the last bullet is worth recognizing by name.

3264
05:18:01,614 --> 05:18:10,052
That is because it is a single setting that tells Redshift whether to spend more capacity for faster results or hold back to save money.

3265
05:18:11,790 --> 05:18:16,420
Managed storage is the architectural change that made everything after it possible.

3266
05:18:16,624 --> 05:18:19,460
And the fourth bullet explains why it mattered.

3267
05:18:22,414 --> 05:18:31,108
On the older dense compute nodes, storage lies on the node itself. So a growing dataset forces you to add nodes purely to hold it.

3268
05:18:31,854 --> 05:18:38,820
You end up paying for compute you do not need and cannot use, which is exactly the shape of a cost question.

3269
05:18:39,438 --> 05:18:47,584
With managed storage, the two scale independently and hot data sits on local SSD while colder data moves to S3

3270
05:18:47,744 --> 05:18:48,516
automatically.

3271
05:18:49,264 --> 05:18:55,300
That separation is also what enables data sharing and 0EDL later in this module.

3272
05:18:55,664 --> 05:19:00,580
That is because the storage layer is no longer trapped inside one cluster's local disks.

3273
05:19:02,062 --> 05:19:05,892
This is the section that separates Redshift from every other service in the course.

3274
05:19:06,384 --> 05:19:09,892
Elsewhere, performance problems are usually solved by configuration.

3275
05:19:10,064 --> 05:19:11,492
You can change afterwards.

3276
05:19:12,398 --> 05:19:20,784
In Redshift, the two decisions that matter most are properties of the table, chosen at creation, and changing them means rebuilding the table.

3277
05:19:21,024 --> 05:19:28,356
Distribution decides which slice each row lives on, which decides whether a join needs to move data between nodes.

3278
05:19:29,134 --> 05:19:35,844
Sort decides the physical order of rows within each slice, which decides how much of the table a filtered query has to read.

3279
05:19:36,270 --> 05:19:38,276
Get both right and a cluster.

3280
05:19:38,606 --> 05:19:40,324
Half the size is fast enough.

3281
05:19:41,870 --> 05:19:45,348
AUTO is the default and it is genuinely sensible.

3282
05:19:45,632 --> 05:19:56,164
It starts small tables as ALL, then moves them to EVEN or KEY as they grow. So when a question has you choose explicitly, look for the reason it gives.

3283
05:19:57,038 --> 05:20:02,752
A large fact table repeatedly joined to another large table on the same column is the KEY case.

3284
05:20:02,912 --> 05:20:07,172
That is because co-locating those rows removes the network exchange entirely.

3285
05:20:07,472 --> 05:20:11,460
A small dimension table joined to everything is the ALL case.

3286
05:20:11,982 --> 05:20:19,076
That is because a full copy on every node means no movement at all and small enough makes the duplication cheap.

3287
05:20:19,598 --> 05:20:25,092
Ev E N is for tables that are not joined or where no column distributes well.

3288
05:20:26,670 --> 05:20:28,996
Modin 5 described data skew in Spark.

3289
05:20:29,552 --> 05:20:34,436
One key holding most of the rows leaves a single task doing most of the work

3290
05:20:35,472 --> 05:20:37,252
while the others sit idle.

3291
05:20:37,966 --> 05:20:42,148
Distribution skew in Redshift is the same phenomenon in a different service.

3292
05:20:43,086 --> 05:20:50,532
Distributing a 100 million row table on a status column with four values sends a quarter of the table to each of four slices.

3293
05:20:51,086 --> 05:20:53,732
Every other slice in the cluster sits empty.

3294
05:20:54,318 --> 05:20:59,012
Adding nodes changes nothing because the key still hashes to four places.

3295
05:21:00,032 --> 05:21:08,512
The exam tests this as a symptom, describing a cluster where adding capacity did not help, which is always the signature of

3296
05:21:08,672 --> 05:21:10,992
skew rather than shortage.

3297
05:21:12,398 --> 05:21:20,548
The compound and interleaved distinction is a fair exam question, and the practical answer is nearly always compound.

3298
05:21:21,230 --> 05:21:30,020
A compound sort key sorts by the first column, then within that by the second, exactly like sorting a spreadsheet by several columns.

3299
05:21:30,832 --> 05:21:37,220
That means a filter on the leading column prunes enormously and a filter on only the third column prunes very little.

3300
05:21:37,472 --> 05:21:45,444
Interleaved gives every column equal weight, so any of them prunes reasonably well at the cost of much heavier maintenance when data is added.

3301
05:21:46,254 --> 05:21:52,036
If a question describes queries that filter on different columns each time, interleaved is being pointed at.

3302
05:21:54,862 --> 05:22:04,003
Zone maps are the reason a sort key matters and they work identically to the parquet minimum and maximum statistics module it

3303
05:22:04,272 --> 05:22:04,932
described.

3304
05:22:05,742 --> 05:22:11,844
Every 1 MB block of every column carries the smallest and largest value it contains.

3305
05:22:12,750 --> 05:22:20,932
A query filtering on last week's dates can discard every block whose range ends before last week without reading a single byte of it.

3306
05:22:21,152 --> 05:22:25,668
On a table sorted by date, that means reading a handful of blocks out of thousands.

3307
05:22:25,872 --> 05:22:29,092
On an unsorted table, dates are scattered evenly.

3308
05:22:30,030 --> 05:22:37,472
So every block contains some recent values. No block can be skipped and the query reads the entire column.

3309
05:22:37,934 --> 05:22:41,572
Same data, same query, a hundredfold difference in work.

3310
05:22:43,182 --> 05:22:48,580
A retailer has a fact table of 2 billion sales rows and a dimension table of 800 stores.

3311
05:22:49,326 --> 05:22:55,972
Almost every query joins the two on store ID, then filters on a transaction date range covering the last 30 days.

3312
05:22:56,432 --> 05:23:01,188
The fact table currently uses EVEN distribution with no sort key.

3313
05:23:02,094 --> 05:23:04,752
The dimension table also uses EVEN.

3314
05:23:05,966 --> 05:23:12,068
Queries take several minutes and monitoring shows heavy data redistribution between nodes during the join.

3315
05:23:12,814 --> 05:23:15,716
Which combination will most improve query performance?

3316
05:23:16,750 --> 05:23:20,868
a Add nodes to the cluster so more slices share the work.

3317
05:23:22,992 --> 05:23:31,392
Distribute the fact table on store ID, set the store table to ALL and add a compound sort key on transaction date.

3318
05:23:32,334 --> 05:23:40,036
C. Set both tables to KEY distribution on store ID and add an interleaved sort key on every filtered column.

3319
05:23:40,942 --> 05:23:45,316
d Enable concurrency scaling so additional clusters absorb the load.

3320
05:23:46,192 --> 05:23:48,388
Query Pause and decide.

3321
05:23:54,830 --> 05:23:56,592
The answer is B.

3322
05:23:57,312 --> 05:24:00,996
Two details in the situation, each point at one half of it.

3323
05:24:02,958 --> 05:24:06,468
Heavy redistribution during the join is a distribution problem.

3324
05:24:07,118 --> 05:24:15,716
A dimension table of 800 rows is small enough that a full copy on every node costs almost nothing, which removes the movement completely.

3325
05:24:16,622 --> 05:24:20,772
Filtering on the last 30 days of a 2 billion row table is a sort key problem.

3326
05:24:20,992 --> 05:24:24,868
A compound sort on transaction date makes zone maps do the work.

3327
05:24:25,152 --> 05:24:31,940
Options A and D are both capacity, offered in two different forms, and neither addresses why an individual query is slow.

3328
05:24:33,552 --> 05:24:40,836
Completion in Redshift is per column and mostly automatic now, which makes it a smaller exam topic than it once was.

3329
05:24:41,312 --> 05:24:44,292
The callout is the part still worth knowing well.

3330
05:24:44,752 --> 05:24:51,472
When you copy data into an empty table, Redshift samples it and picks an appropriate encoding for each column.

3331
05:24:52,014 --> 05:24:57,252
That is why the recommended way to build a table is to create it empty and copy into it.

3332
05:24:58,510 --> 05:25:04,228
Loading the same data row by row with insert statements bypasses that sampling entirely.

3333
05:25:05,038 --> 05:25:12,484
The columns stay uncompressed and you end up with a table that is permanently larger and slower to scan for the rest of its life.

3334
05:25:13,038 --> 05:25:14,852
That nothing will ever tell you about.

3335
05:25:16,430 --> 05:25:22,724
The exam guide names loading and unloading between Amazon S3 and Redshift as a skill in its own right.

3336
05:25:23,342 --> 05:25:25,060
This section carries real weight.

3337
05:25:26,192 --> 05:25:30,052
The essential thing to understand is that a copy command is parallel.

3338
05:25:30,752 --> 05:25:35,140
Every slice in the cluster pulls part of the data simultaneously.

3339
05:25:36,078 --> 05:25:41,540
That is why it loads orders of magnitude faster than inserting rows one at a time.

3340
05:25:42,062 --> 05:25:46,032
Everything on the next slide is about giving that parallelism something to work with.

3341
05:25:46,318 --> 05:25:53,156
A single large file cannot be split across slices, so it turns a massively parallel load into a single threaded one.

3342
05:25:54,126 --> 05:25:57,952
The second word scenario in this module is built entirely on that.

3343
05:25:58,318 --> 05:26:03,204
Copy also does work beyond loading because it analyzes the data and applies compression.

3344
05:26:04,782 --> 05:26:10,500
The first two bullets are the same idea from two directions and they are examinable numbers.

3345
05:26:10,926 --> 05:26:15,908
Suppose a cluster has 16 slices and you give copy a single file.

3346
05:26:17,294 --> 05:26:19,972
15 slices watch one slice work.

3347
05:26:20,512 --> 05:26:23,844
Give it 16 files and every slice loads one.

3348
05:26:24,782 --> 05:26:28,352
Give it 32 and every slice loads two evenly.

3349
05:26:28,974 --> 05:26:32,036
The manifest point is subtler and answers a specific problem.

3350
05:26:32,942 --> 05:26:35,908
Copying from a prefix loads whatever happens to be sitting there.

3351
05:26:36,430 --> 05:26:43,472
So a file still being written or a leftover from a failed run gets loaded silently and nothing reports it.

3352
05:26:44,014 --> 05:26:53,508
A manifest names the exact files to load, which makes the operation deterministic and repeatable. And that matters for the reruns module 6 covered.

3353
05:26:55,182 --> 05:27:02,192
Module 3 covered the streaming path and module 2 covered 0 ETL. So this slide is where they all meet.

3354
05:27:02,352 --> 05:27:05,860
The distinctions that decide questions are latency and overhead.

3355
05:27:06,112 --> 05:27:11,780
Autocopy removes the scheduling from a batch load, so files load on arrival with nothing to orchestrate.

3356
05:27:12,590 --> 05:27:19,792
Streaming ingestion connects the warehouse directly to a stream and materializes rows in seconds without staging through S3.

3357
05:27:20,206 --> 05:27:27,552
Fire hose always stages through S3 and issues a copy, so it is slower than streaming ingestion but needs no consumer.

3358
05:27:28,503 --> 05:27:34,020
0ETL keeps an operational database in sync with no pipeline at all.

3359
05:27:35,438 --> 05:27:41,312
Unload is more useful than it first appears, and the second and third bullets are Y.

3360
05:27:41,806 --> 05:27:48,836
It can write parquet partitioned by a column directly into a lake layout that Athena and Drew can read immediately.

3361
05:27:49,742 --> 05:27:57,632
That makes it the natural mechanism for publishing curated data out of the warehouse for wider consumption without a

3362
05:27:57,903 --> 05:27:59,012
blue job in between.

3363
05:27:59,854 --> 05:28:05,348
The fourth bullet describes the anti-pattern the exam offers as a distractor.

3364
05:28:06,112 --> 05:28:11,556
Running a select that returns 50 million rows to a client sends all of them through the single leader node.

3365
05:28:11,872 --> 05:28:14,564
That is slow and can destabilize the cluster.

3366
05:28:16,110 --> 05:28:22,628
This is how change data actually gets applied in a warehouse and it appears in questions as a design.

3367
05:28:23,246 --> 05:28:24,900
Module 4 described.

3368
05:28:25,614 --> 05:28:32,740
DMS writing change records with an operation column marking each as insert, update or delete.

3369
05:28:33,646 --> 05:28:39,652
In Redshift, you copy that batch into a staging table, which is fast because copy is parallel.

3370
05:28:40,238 --> 05:28:45,860
Then run one merge statement that applies every change against the target in a single transaction.

3371
05:28:47,726 --> 05:28:52,388
Readers see the old state or the new state and never anything in between.

3372
05:28:52,846 --> 05:29:03,172
The alternative issuing individual update statements per row works and is unusably slow because a columnar store rewrites blocks rather than rows.

3373
05:29:04,654 --> 05:29:12,160
This surprises people coming from a transactional database and it makes a good exam question because the failure is silent.

3374
05:29:12,480 --> 05:29:20,612
Declaring a primary key in Redshift tells the query planner that values are unique, which genuinely helps it choose better plans.

3375
05:29:21,358 --> 05:29:24,100
It does not stop you inserting the same row twice.

3376
05:29:24,878 --> 05:29:33,412
So a pipeline that reruns after a failure and relies on the primary key to reject the rows it already loaded will simply load them again.

3377
05:29:34,286 --> 05:29:37,760
The only symptom is that a count is too high.

3378
05:29:38,318 --> 05:29:43,012
That is the same duplicate problem module 2 described and the fix is the same.

3379
05:29:43,662 --> 05:29:47,396
Make the load idempotent, which in Redshift means merge.

3380
05:29:49,102 --> 05:29:53,920
A nightly process loads 12GB of order data into Amazon Redshift.

3381
05:29:54,320 --> 05:30:01,188
The data is produced as a single uncompressed CSV file in Amazon S3 and loaded with a copy command.

3382
05:30:02,254 --> 05:30:05,796
The cluster has four R3.4x large nodes.

3383
05:30:06,702 --> 05:30:13,796
The load takes about 90 minutes and monitoring shows one slice at full utilization while the rest are idle.

3384
05:30:14,414 --> 05:30:19,972
Data volume grows about 5% a month and the load must finish inside a 4 hour window.

3385
05:30:20,622 --> 05:30:22,564
What should the data engineer change?

3386
05:30:24,622 --> 05:30:30,436
Split the file into multiple compressed files, ideally a multiple of the cluster's slice count.

3387
05:30:30,958 --> 05:30:36,164
b Increase the cluster from 4 nodes to 8 so more slices are available.

3388
05:30:36,640 --> 05:30:36,880
C.

3389
05:30:37,440 --> 05:30:41,892
Replace the copy command with batched insert statements running in parallel.

3390
05:30:42,414 --> 05:30:42,720
D.

3391
05:30:43,726 --> 05:30:48,868
Enable concurrency scaling so the load runs on an additional cluster.

3392
05:30:49,838 --> 05:30:51,012
Pause and decide.

3393
05:30:57,710 --> 05:30:58,960
The answer is A.

3394
05:30:59,374 --> 05:31:03,588
The symptom stated is diagnostic and it should be recognizable instantly.

3395
05:31:03,918 --> 05:31:09,604
One slice at full utilization while the rest are idle means the work cannot be divided.

3396
05:31:11,214 --> 05:31:14,212
During a copy, that means the input cannot be divided.

3397
05:31:15,118 --> 05:31:22,880
Option B is the same trap as scenario, one in a different costume, offering capacity for a problem that is not capacity.

3398
05:31:24,398 --> 05:31:28,964
It is worth noticing that this module has now offered that twice.

3399
05:31:29,934 --> 05:31:37,284
Adding slices to a job that can only use one slice is the clearest example in the course of why diagnosing before optimizing matters.

3400
05:31:39,022 --> 05:31:43,588
This will sound familiar after module 9 because the underlying reason is much the same.

3401
05:31:44,014 --> 05:31:52,036
Redshift deletes rows by marking them rather than removing them and inserts new rows at the end of a table rather than in sorted position.

3402
05:31:52,910 --> 05:32:02,980
So a table written to for months holds marked deleted rows, taking up space. It also holds unsorted regions which defeat the zone maps that make queries fast.

3403
05:32:03,280 --> 05:32:10,660
Redshift now handles most of this automatically in the background, which is why this topic is smaller than it once was on this exam.

3404
05:32:11,438 --> 05:32:18,180
Knowing what the automatic processes do and the specific situations where they fall behind is what is still examined.

3405
05:32:18,638 --> 05:32:21,060
The next three slides cover exactly that.

3406
05:32:22,702 --> 05:32:27,908
Automatic vacuum handles the ordinary case, running when the cluster is not busy.

3407
05:32:28,302 --> 05:32:32,452
So the exam is less interested in scheduling one than it used to be.

3408
05:32:33,294 --> 05:32:37,348
A manual vacuum still appears after a single very large change.

3409
05:32:38,254 --> 05:32:43,844
Deleting 100 million rows leaves 100 million marked rows occupying blocks.

3410
05:32:44,400 --> 05:32:49,444
Waiting for background processing to catch up may take considerably longer than you want.

3411
05:32:50,222 --> 05:32:55,748
The same applies after a very large load, which leaves a big unsorted region at the end of the table.

3412
05:32:56,654 --> 05:33:03,680
The reindex form is worth recognizing because it exists only for interleaved sort keys that are themselves unusual.

3413
05:33:03,760 --> 05:33:09,440
So a question mentioning both is being internally consistent rather than testing you on trivia.

3414
05:33:11,118 --> 05:33:16,964
The callout describes a symptom worth recognizing because the cause is completely invisible from the query.

3415
05:33:17,424 --> 05:33:19,104
The query has not changed.

3416
05:33:19,342 --> 05:33:24,676
The data has not grown dramatically, and yet the plan the optimizer chooses is now wrong.

3417
05:33:25,184 --> 05:33:28,612
It believes a table is far smaller than it has become.

3418
05:33:29,166 --> 05:33:34,820
So it picks the wrong join strategy and a query that took 10 seconds now takes 10 minutes.

3419
05:33:36,430 --> 05:33:41,188
Running analyze restores a correct picture and the plan reverts on the next execution.

3420
05:33:42,544 --> 05:33:46,308
Automatic analyze makes this rarer than it once was.

3421
05:33:47,214 --> 05:33:51,652
It still appears in questions describing a sudden unexplained slowdown

3422
05:33:52,494 --> 05:33:58,052
after a large load because the load finished before the background process caught up.

3423
05:33:59,662 --> 05:34:06,916
Automatic table optimization is why scenario 1 is slightly artificial and it is worth acknowledging that.

3424
05:34:07,264 --> 05:34:13,572
In practice, a table left on AUTO would eventually acquire reasonable keys by itself.

3425
05:34:14,464 --> 05:34:20,580
The exam still asks you to choose explicitly because it is testing whether you understand what the keys do.

3426
05:34:21,262 --> 05:34:23,908
The fourth bullet is the genuine limitation.

3427
05:34:24,384 --> 05:34:32,260
Optimization needs query history to learn from, so a new table or one whose user is about to change completely has nothing to go on.

3428
05:34:33,038 --> 05:34:38,468
That is exactly when a question hands you the access pattern and expects you to act on it.

3429
05:34:39,982 --> 05:34:46,436
Workload management exists to stop one workload starving another, and the exam frames it that way.

3430
05:34:47,374 --> 05:34:52,548
The classic situation is an overnight EDL jog competing with morning dashboards.

3431
05:34:53,358 --> 05:35:03,396
Automatic WLM with query priorities is the modern answer, letting you mark the dashboard workload as high priority without hand allocating memory.

3432
05:35:03,824 --> 05:35:08,944
Manual WLM still appears when a question requires a guaranteed share.

3433
05:35:09,358 --> 05:35:14,692
That is because it reserves a fixed percentage of memory per queue regardless of what else is running.

3434
05:35:16,334 --> 05:35:25,092
Query monitoring rules are the enforcement layer and they answer questions about a runaway query consuming the cluster because a rule can abort it automatically.

3435
05:35:26,766 --> 05:35:33,188
The call out is the distinction the exam tests and it has already appeared twice in this module as a distractor.

3436
05:35:34,062 --> 05:35:38,276
Concurrency scaling helps when the problem is too many queries arriving at once.

3437
05:35:38,958 --> 05:35:42,724
So they queue behind one another and users wait for their turn.

3438
05:35:43,694 --> 05:35:48,784
It does nothing whatsoever for a single query that takes 4 minutes because of a missing sort

3439
05:35:49,742 --> 05:35:53,828
key. That is because that query takes 4 minutes on the extra cluster 2.

3440
05:35:54,414 --> 05:35:56,004
So read the symptom carefully.

3441
05:35:56,654 --> 05:35:56,893
Q.

3442
05:35:58,062 --> 05:36:01,028
Wait time rising means concurrency scaling.

3443
05:36:01,998 --> 05:36:05,476
Execution time rising means table design or statistics.

3444
05:36:07,214 --> 05:36:14,624
The free hour per day is worth remembering as well because it means many bursty workloads never pay for concurrency scaling at all.

3445
05:36:15,822 --> 05:36:23,236
Managing locks in Redshift and RDS is a name skill, and the symptom is distinctive enough to be worth recognizing.

3446
05:36:23,982 --> 05:36:31,588
A query is running, it is not consuming meaningful resources, it is not failing, and it never completes.

3447
05:36:32,558 --> 05:36:38,980
That is a lock weight rather than a performance problem, and no amount of capacity or tuning affects it.

3448
05:36:40,334 --> 05:36:47,652
The usual cause is a session that opened a transaction, did something, and then went away without committing or rolling back.

3449
05:36:48,366 --> 05:36:50,436
Often because an application crashed.

3450
05:36:51,342 --> 05:36:57,348
The lock survives until that session is terminated, which is what the fourth bullet does.

3451
05:36:58,704 --> 05:37:04,580
The callout is the practical answer to the first of the five reading questions at the end of this module.

3452
05:37:05,134 --> 05:37:07,812
Query history records both numbers separately.

3453
05:37:08,384 --> 05:37:15,076
So the distinction between a query that waited and a query that ran slowly is not a judgment call, it is two columns.

3454
05:37:15,918 --> 05:37:22,692
Redshift Advisor is worth knowing by name because it is the service telling you what this module has been teaching.

3455
05:37:22,944 --> 05:37:32,068
It inspects your actual workload and reports that a particular table would benefit from a sort key or that a distribution style is causing redistribution.

3456
05:37:32,942 --> 05:37:34,564
That is the same diagnosis.

3457
05:37:35,024 --> 05:37:37,764
A question asks you to make from symptoms.

3458
05:37:39,438 --> 05:37:46,180
An Amazon Redshift cluster serves a business intelligence dashboard used by around 200 analysts each weekday morning.

3459
05:37:48,270 --> 05:37:54,148
It also runs an ETL job that loads and transforms data overnight and sometimes overruns into the morning.

3460
05:37:55,118 --> 05:38:02,468
When the ETL job overruns, dashboard queries take several minutes to start. Once they start, they complete in under two seconds.

3461
05:38:02,832 --> 05:38:05,924
Individual query execution times are unchanged.

3462
05:38:07,950 --> 05:38:10,148
The company will not delay the ETL job.

3463
05:38:10,432 --> 05:38:12,772
Which combination best addresses the problem?

3464
05:38:16,750 --> 05:38:21,060
Add a compound sort key to the dashboard tables to speed up execution.

3465
05:38:21,486 --> 05:38:30,592
b Enable concurrency scaling on the dashboard queue and use WLM query priority to rank dashboards above ETL.

3466
05:38:31,086 --> 05:38:31,312
C

3467
05:38:32,366 --> 05:38:36,580
Run vacuum and analyze after the ETL job completes each morning.

3468
05:38:37,326 --> 05:38:37,552
D.

3469
05:38:38,766 --> 05:38:42,020
Resize the cluster to double the number of nodes.

3470
05:38:42,286 --> 05:38:43,684
Pause and decide.

3471
05:38:50,350 --> 05:38:51,712
The answer is B.

3472
05:38:52,512 --> 05:38:57,284
And this scenario is the concurrency scaling callout tested directly.

3473
05:38:58,192 --> 05:39:01,252
The question separates the two numbers deliberately.

3474
05:39:02,112 --> 05:39:07,300
Time to start is several minutes, and time to execute is under two seconds.

3475
05:39:07,950 --> 05:39:09,860
Everything about the execution is healthy.

3476
05:39:10,158 --> 05:39:14,052
So every option that improves execution is answering a question nobody asked.

3477
05:39:15,246 --> 05:39:17,508
Options A and C are both genuinely good.

3478
05:39:18,158 --> 05:39:23,300
Redshift practice offered in the wrong place, which is a pattern this course has flagged repeatedly.

3479
05:39:24,174 --> 05:39:30,468
And option D is capacity again, this time expensive and permanent for an intermittent problem.

3480
05:39:31,982 --> 05:39:38,756
This is where a lot of domain 2 marks sit because four capabilities sound similar and are routinely offered as options together.

3481
05:39:39,694 --> 05:39:42,020
Spectrum reads files sitting in a data lake.

3482
05:39:42,734 --> 05:39:45,508
Federated query reads an operational database.

3483
05:39:45,952 --> 05:39:46,468
Live.

3484
05:39:46,958 --> 05:39:48,548
At the moment you ask.

3485
05:39:49,262 --> 05:39:53,380
Data sharing reads another redshift warehouse without copying anything.

3486
05:39:53,792 --> 05:39:59,108
Zero ETL copies an operational database in continuously and keeps it current.

3487
05:39:59,472 --> 05:40:05,284
They differ on three attributes, and holding those three straight is what makes the questions quick.

3488
05:40:05,792 --> 05:40:07,908
Ask where the data physically lives.

3489
05:40:08,782 --> 05:40:11,300
Ask whether it moves or is read in place.

3490
05:40:11,792 --> 05:40:14,820
And ask how fresh what you see actually is.

3491
05:40:16,014 --> 05:40:23,620
Work through each of the next five slides, asking those three questions, and the comparison at the end will already be obvious.

3492
05:40:24,878 --> 05:40:28,900
These two are easy to blur and the exam separates them deliberately.

3493
05:40:29,934 --> 05:40:35,012
A cross-database query reaches into a different database inside the same Redshift warehouse.

3494
05:40:35,598 --> 05:40:39,716
That is a way of organizing schemas rather than a way of sharing between teams.

3495
05:40:40,622 --> 05:40:44,612
Everything is still one cluster, one bill, and one set of compute.

3496
05:40:46,062 --> 05:40:54,948
Data sharing crosses warehouse boundaries entirely, including into other accounts and other regions, and each consumer brings its own compute.

3497
05:40:55,822 --> 05:41:00,512
The lake formation bullet matters for domain 4 and is worth flagging now.

3498
05:41:01,070 --> 05:41:05,060
A data share on its own grants access to whole objects.

3499
05:41:05,870 --> 05:41:12,964
Registering it with lake formation adds the column and row level controls that module 18 covers properly.

3500
05:41:14,734 --> 05:41:17,700
Spectrum answers a question that comes up constantly.

3501
05:41:18,254 --> 05:41:24,708
That is what to do with years of history that must remain queryable but does not justify warehouse storage.

3502
05:41:26,414 --> 05:41:33,988
Load the recent months into Redshift where queries are fast, leave the rest as parquet in S3, define external tables over it.

3503
05:41:34,894 --> 05:41:36,868
Write one query that spans both.

3504
05:41:37,710 --> 05:41:39,940
The user cannot tell where the boundary is.

3505
05:41:40,462 --> 05:41:47,684
The per terabyte charge on RA3 is why partitioning and columnar formats matter here exactly as they do for Athena.

3506
05:41:48,462 --> 05:41:54,500
It is worth noting the RG change again because it removes that charge and changes the economics.

3507
05:41:56,206 --> 05:42:02,052
Module 5 made this point about Athena Federated Query and it applies identically here.

3508
05:42:02,510 --> 05:42:07,492
Reading directly from a production database is elegant, requires no pipeline at all,

3509
05:42:08,430 --> 05:42:12,964
puts analytical load on a system whose actual job is serving an application.

3510
05:42:13,966 --> 05:42:20,900
For joining a handful of reference rows to a warehouse query, it is ideal and the freshness is perfect because there is no replication lag.

3511
05:42:21,422 --> 05:42:26,116
For a heavy analytical scan, it is a genuine risk to whatever that database supports.

3512
05:42:26,894 --> 05:42:32,292
So a question describing occasional lookups of reference data points at federated query.

3513
05:42:33,680 --> 05:42:42,628
A question describing a regular reporting workload against an operational database points at zero ETL or a replication pipeline instead.

3514
05:42:44,334 --> 05:42:52,580
Data sharing solves an organizational problem more than a technical one, which is why the question wording tends to describe teams rather than throughput.

3515
05:42:53,230 --> 05:43:00,080
A finance team and a marketing team both need the same sales data without sharing their content for one cluster.

3516
05:43:00,622 --> 05:43:08,356
Or somebody builds a pipeline to copy data into a second cluster and maintains it forever, with the copies drifting apart.

3517
05:43:09,006 --> 05:43:14,240
Data sharing gives each team its own compute reading, one authoritative copy.

3518
05:43:14,862 --> 05:43:21,828
The last bullet is a real constraint and it connects back to the node type slide because managed storage is what makes it possible.

3519
05:43:23,342 --> 05:43:28,580
Module 2 introduced 0ETL and this is where it lands properly.

3520
05:43:29,134 --> 05:43:38,084
The reason it wins least overhead questions so decisively is that there is genuinely nothing to operate, no DMS task to size, no blue job to schedule,

3521
05:43:39,022 --> 05:43:44,132
no replication instance to monitor, and no failure to handle at 3 in the morning.

3522
05:43:44,718 --> 05:43:47,940
The two constraints are what make it answerable in an exam.

3523
05:43:49,966 --> 05:43:57,124
The source and target pair must be supported, currently Aurora, Amazon RDS and Dynamo DB into Redshift.

3524
05:43:57,902 --> 05:44:03,620
And the data arrives exactly as it is, so any reshaping happens in Redshift afterwards with SQL.

3525
05:44:04,160 --> 05:44:12,708
A question describing operational data needed for analytics with no transformation mentioned and overhead emphasized is describing this.

3526
05:44:14,478 --> 05:44:18,404
This is Module 1's access pattern rule applied to a fifth situation.

3527
05:44:18,880 --> 05:44:22,400
Data queried a few times a month should stay where it is.

3528
05:44:22,560 --> 05:44:29,028
That is because copying it means paying to store a second copy and building something to keep it current.

3529
05:44:29,806 --> 05:44:38,820
Data queried by hundreds of people every morning should be in the warehouse in Redshift's own optimized storage, sorted and distributed for those queries.

3530
05:44:39,040 --> 05:44:43,588
The middle ground is the interesting one and it is where data sharing sits.

3531
05:44:44,078 --> 05:44:49,764
That is because it gives the consumer warehouse performance without the consumer needing a copy.

3532
05:44:51,406 --> 05:44:56,356
A company keeps three years of transactions in Amazon Redshift totaling 40 Tabytes.

3533
05:44:56,640 --> 05:44:59,588
Analysts query the most recent 90 days constantly.

3534
05:44:59,918 --> 05:45:04,836
Data older than a year is queried perhaps twice a quarter for regulatory reporting,

3535
05:45:05,710 --> 05:45:10,980
but must remain queryable alongside recent data in a single SQL statement.

3536
05:45:11,502 --> 05:45:14,852
Storage costs have become the largest line on the Redshift bill.

3537
05:45:15,374 --> 05:45:19,588
The company wants to reduce them without changing how analysts write queries.

3538
05:45:20,782 --> 05:45:22,852
What should the data engineer implement?

3539
05:45:24,846 --> 05:45:32,720
Unload data older than one year to Amazon S3 as partition parquet, define external tables and query them with

3540
05:45:32,880 --> 05:45:33,988
Redshift Spectrum.

3541
05:45:34,702 --> 05:45:42,340
b Unload data older than one year to Amazon S3 and tell analysts to use Amazon Athena for historical queries.

3542
05:45:43,680 --> 05:45:48,868
C Move the older data to a second Redshift cluster and use data sharing.

3543
05:45:49,806 --> 05:45:53,600
d Enable concurrency scaling and reduce the cluster size.

3544
05:45:54,160 --> 05:45:55,268
Pause and decide.

3545
05:46:01,998 --> 05:46:06,500
The answer is A, and one clause eliminates the closest competitor.

3546
05:46:07,008 --> 05:46:16,036
Data must remain queryable alongside recent data in a single SQL statement. Option B saves exactly the same money and fails that.

3547
05:46:16,846 --> 05:46:23,728
That is because Athena and Redshift are separate query engines and a join across them is not one statement.

3548
05:46:24,048 --> 05:46:30,628
Spectrum exists precisely so that an external table can be joined to a warehouse table in the same query.

3549
05:46:31,566 --> 05:46:34,276
Option D is worth noting for a specific reason.

3550
05:46:35,054 --> 05:46:43,588
On managed storage, shrinking the cluster reduces compute cost and does nothing to storage because the two are built independently.

3551
05:46:45,230 --> 05:46:51,728
The plan keywords are worth recognizing because they turn a vague suspicion into evidence.

3552
05:46:52,174 --> 05:46:58,148
When the exam describes heavy redistribution during a join, this is what somebody looked at to see it.

3553
05:46:58,830 --> 05:47:05,380
DS This none means both sides of the join are already on the right slices and nothing moves, which is the goal.

3554
05:47:05,728 --> 05:47:14,436
DSBcast inner means one table is being broadcast to every node, which is fine for a small table and expensive for a large one.

3555
05:47:15,086 --> 05:47:25,284
DS DIST both means both sides are being redistributed, which is the most expensive outcome and the clearest signal that the distribution keys are wrong.

3556
05:47:26,958 --> 05:47:34,724
Materialized views are the standard answer to a dashboard running the same expensive aggregation hundreds of times a day.

3557
05:47:35,374 --> 05:47:40,804
Compute it once, store the result, and refresh it as the underlying data changes.

3558
05:47:41,774 --> 05:47:47,556
The automatic rewriting capability in the fourth bullet is genuinely useful and very easy to overlook.

3559
05:47:48,110 --> 05:47:51,716
Analysts keep writing queries against the base tables exactly as before.

3560
05:47:52,206 --> 05:47:58,180
Redshift recognizes that a materialized view already contains the answer and quietly uses it instead.

3561
05:47:59,086 --> 05:48:03,684
Nobody has to be told the view exists or change any SQL.

3562
05:48:04,048 --> 05:48:06,980
And the last bullet connects back to module 3.

3563
05:48:07,342 --> 05:48:17,572
That is because Redshift streaming ingestion materializes stream records into precisely this structure, which is why the two topics share a mechanism.

3564
05:48:19,246 --> 05:48:21,956
The availability warning is the item to carry.

3565
05:48:22,318 --> 05:48:29,156
Python user-defined functions in RedshiftReach end of support at the end of June 2026.

3566
05:48:29,408 --> 05:48:34,148
Older material offers them as the way to run custom logic inside a query.

3567
05:48:35,054 --> 05:48:40,260
The replacements are SQL user-defined functions for anything expressible in SQL.

3568
05:48:53,808 --> 05:48:54,128
It

3569
05:48:54,128 --> 05:49:01,924
lets you train a model and then call it in ordinary SQL with SageMaker doing the work underneath.

3570
05:49:03,566 --> 05:49:09,156
The table level restore is worth knowing because it changes the answer to a recovery question.

3571
05:49:09,870 --> 05:49:18,724
A job that corrupted one table does not require restoring an entire 40TB cluster to a new endpoint and repointing every application at it.

3572
05:49:19,566 --> 05:49:25,220
You restore that one table from a snapshot into the running cluster and carry on.

3573
05:49:26,542 --> 05:49:30,180
Compare that with the iceberg rollback from module 9,

3574
05:49:31,150 --> 05:49:36,516
which is faster still because no data moves at all, only a metadata pointer.

3575
05:49:38,126 --> 05:49:42,820
The resize distinction on the last bullet appears in migration questions.

3576
05:49:43,630 --> 05:49:47,072
Elastic resize is a quick path for changing node count.

3577
05:49:47,232 --> 05:49:52,868
Classic resize handles a change of node type, such as moving from VC2 to RA3.

3578
05:49:54,350 --> 05:49:56,996
This is a smaller topic than the equivalent for S3.

3579
05:49:57,422 --> 05:49:59,652
That is because a Redshift cluster is compute.

3580
05:50:00,078 --> 05:50:03,780
You provision rather than a regional service that spans zones by default.

3581
05:50:04,654 --> 05:50:08,388
A cluster lives in one availability zone unless you say otherwise.

3582
05:50:09,358 --> 05:50:16,548
So losing that zone means restoring from a snapshot into another one, which takes time roughly proportional to the volume of data.

3583
05:50:16,912 --> 05:50:25,988
Multi-AZ runs the warehouse across two zones with automatic failover behind an unchanged endpoint. So applications do not reconnect anywhere new.

3584
05:50:26,752 --> 05:50:30,820
That is the answer when a question states that an outage is unacceptable.

3585
05:50:31,152 --> 05:50:36,100
It costs roughly double the compute, which is the trade the question is really asking you to weigh up.

3586
05:50:37,614 --> 05:50:45,412
Domain 4 covers all of this properly, and three items are worth knowing now because they appear in domain 2 questions.

3587
05:50:46,062 --> 05:50:49,316
The IAM role point is the practical one.

3588
05:50:50,318 --> 05:50:53,732
A copy command reading from S3 does not carry credentials.

3589
05:50:54,318 --> 05:50:56,644
It names a role attached to the cluster.

3590
05:50:57,486 --> 05:51:04,164
A copy that fails with an access error is almost always a role or bucket policy problem rather than a syntax problem.

3591
05:51:04,494 --> 05:51:09,188
Role level security and dynamic data masking are worth recognizing by name here.

3592
05:51:10,032 --> 05:51:17,956
That is because they let one table serve different audiences with different visibility, which module 19 returns to in detail.

3593
05:51:19,632 --> 05:51:22,724
This table is the module compressed into a lookup.

3594
05:51:23,406 --> 05:51:29,540
It is worth learning as a unit because redshift questions are so often diagnostic rather than architectural.

3595
05:51:30,350 --> 05:51:39,108
The second row deserves particular attention because the same symptom has two entirely different causes, distinguished only by when it happens.

3596
05:51:39,790 --> 05:51:44,512
One busy slice during a load means a single input file that cannot be split.

3597
05:51:44,672 --> 05:51:48,516
One busy slice during queries means distribution skew.

3598
05:51:49,262 --> 05:51:55,428
Both look identical in monitoring, both produce the same graph, and the fixes have nothing in common.

3599
05:51:56,078 --> 05:52:01,572
That is a fair exam question and exactly the kind of distinction that separates two plausible options.

3600
05:52:02,446 --> 05:52:09,060
The last row is the one scenario 4 turns on, and it is the only row here about money rather than time.

3601
05:52:10,606 --> 05:52:17,392
The callout is the point scenario 4 turned on, and it is worth stating separately because it is counterintuitive.

3602
05:52:17,632 --> 05:52:26,500
On managed storage, the number of nodes and the amount of data stored are priced independently. That is the whole benefit of separating compute and storage.

3603
05:52:27,342 --> 05:52:32,644
It means a cost question needs you to identify which of the two is actually large.

3604
05:52:33,294 --> 05:52:40,900
A cluster that is overprovisioned for its query load is a compute problem, answered by resizing or by reserved capacity.

3605
05:52:41,838 --> 05:52:48,036
A cluster holding years of cold history is a storage problem, answered by moving that history to S3.

3606
05:52:49,518 --> 05:52:57,796
Five questions, and the first is the highest value in the whole module because it eliminates half the options immediately on a large family of questions.

3607
05:52:58,382 --> 05:53:04,420
Q8 and execution time have entirely different answers and both are described as slow queries by users.

3608
05:53:05,326 --> 05:53:11,876
The third is the one to internalize for table design because it maps the two key choices onto the two things a query does.

3609
05:53:12,910 --> 05:53:15,812
If the pane is in the join, look at distribution.

3610
05:53:16,206 --> 05:53:19,268
If the pane is in the filter, look at the sort key.

3611
05:53:20,526 --> 05:53:24,612
Next module moves outside the warehouse to the rest of the data stores.

3612
05:53:25,454 --> 05:53:31,812
That includes the vector index types added to the exam guide in the latest revision.

3613
05:53:33,358 --> 05:53:36,836
Module 10 covered one store in enormous depth.

3614
05:53:37,806 --> 05:53:44,260
This module covers 8 of them in the depth the exam actually requires, which is far less than you might fear.

3615
05:53:44,750 --> 05:53:48,544
You are not being examined on operating Dynamo DB or tuning Aurora.

3616
05:53:49,422 --> 05:54:02,052
You are being examined on picking the right one from a list of four, which is a different and much more learnable skill. That is why this module carries 8 worked scenarios rather than 4.

3617
05:54:02,798 --> 05:54:10,756
It also covers vector storage and vector index types that entered the exam guide in December 2025

3618
05:54:11,024 --> 05:54:13,764
and appear in no older material at all.

3619
05:54:15,342 --> 05:54:24,036
The rule from module 1 was that volume almost never decides a data store because every service here handles large volumes.

3620
05:54:24,224 --> 05:54:26,628
What decides it is how the data is read.

3621
05:54:27,374 --> 05:54:31,876
One item at a time by a key or millions of rows aggregated.

3622
05:54:33,070 --> 05:54:35,876
Fixed known queries or free text search.

3623
05:54:39,438 --> 05:54:41,156
That rule got you through module 1.

3624
05:54:41,806 --> 05:54:49,508
It is too coarse for a question offering DynamoDB, Aurora, Document DB, and Key Spaces, all of which store items by key.

3625
05:54:50,350 --> 05:54:56,004
This module sharpens it and the sharpening is mostly a matter of knowing what each service was originally built for.

3626
05:54:57,664 --> 05:55:03,108
The callout is the shortcut that resolves a surprising number of questions in a single step.

3627
05:55:03,664 --> 05:55:10,724
DocumentDB exists because organizations run MongoDB. Key Spaces exist because they run Cassandra.

3628
05:55:11,504 --> 05:55:14,464
MemoryDB exists because they run Redis.

3629
05:55:15,470 --> 05:55:21,504
Aurora exists partly because they run PostgreSQL and MySQL and want them faster.

3630
05:55:21,744 --> 05:55:28,900
So when a question mentions an existing application using one of those technologies, the answer is the compatible service.

3631
05:55:29,902 --> 05:55:34,624
It is the same reason existing Kafka meant Amazon MSK in module 3.

3632
05:55:35,086 --> 05:55:38,532
Rewriting a working application onto a different

3633
05:55:39,598 --> 05:55:42,660
API is the unnecessary migration cost.

3634
05:55:42,958 --> 05:55:45,060
The exam consistently marks wrong.

3635
05:55:45,424 --> 05:55:49,316
It is worth checking for before you compare anything technical.

3636
05:55:50,958 --> 05:55:56,228
The time-based index pattern is the one worth knowing because it solves retention.

3637
05:55:56,750 --> 05:55:59,300
The way partitioning solves it in a data lake.

3638
05:56:00,494 --> 05:56:03,780
Logs go into an index name for the day or the month.

3639
05:56:04,430 --> 05:56:10,372
So removing 90-day old data means deleting one whole index, which is close to instantaneous.

3640
05:56:11,278 --> 05:56:21,348
Deleting individual documents, matching a date range instead is slow, expensive, and leaves the space to be reclaimed later by a background merge.

3641
05:56:21,998 --> 05:56:30,384
Index state management automates the entire lifecycle, moving older indices to cheaper storage tiers and eventually

3642
05:56:30,704 --> 05:56:33,028
deleting them on a schedule you define.

3643
05:56:33,504 --> 05:56:40,484
That is the open search equivalent of the S3 lifecycle rule from module 8, and questions treat them the same way.

3644
05:56:42,094 --> 05:56:46,596
The fourth question is the most important and it is worth stating starkly.

3645
05:56:47,104 --> 05:56:52,452
Every store in this module is built to retrieve specific records quickly for an application.

3646
05:56:52,784 --> 05:56:58,596
Redshift and Athena are built to scan and aggregate enormous numbers of records for analysis.

3647
05:56:59,214 --> 05:57:02,980
They are not computing options, they sit at different points in a pipeline.

3648
05:57:03,598 --> 05:57:10,660
A question describing a dashboard aggregating three years of sales is not a DynamoDB question, no matter how much data is involved.

3649
05:57:11,566 --> 05:57:18,980
Module 12nd Works Scenario made exactly this mistake deliberately, serving per user lookups from Athena.

3650
05:57:19,566 --> 05:57:22,084
This is the same error in the opposite direction.

3651
05:57:23,790 --> 05:57:31,140
A relational database lets you write any query you like against a well-normalized schema and works out how to answer it.

3652
05:57:31,854 --> 05:57:33,284
DynamoDB does not.

3653
05:57:33,902 --> 05:57:41,380
You design the table around the queries you intend to run, and queries you did not design for are either impossible or expensive.

3654
05:57:42,254 --> 05:57:48,996
In exchange, you get single-digit millisecond responses at essentially unlimited scale with no capacity planning.

3655
05:57:49,264 --> 05:57:53,504
That trade is the entire character of the service and it is what questions test.

3656
05:57:54,094 --> 05:58:00,516
When a scenario describes known, repeated access patterns and demanding latency, DynamoDB fits.

3657
05:58:01,454 --> 05:58:07,364
When it describes analysts exploring data in ways nobody anticipated, it does not.

3658
05:58:08,814 --> 05:58:14,244
The distinction between query and scan is the most reliably examined thing about Dynamo DB.

3659
05:58:14,624 --> 05:58:21,220
A query goes straight to one partition and reads the items it needs, which is why it is fast and cheap.

3660
05:58:21,774 --> 05:58:26,084
A scan reads every item in the table and then discards the ones that do not match.

3661
05:58:26,926 --> 05:58:30,660
So you pay for the whole table to retrieve a handful of rows.

3662
05:58:31,790 --> 05:58:33,956
The callout is the exam consequence.

3663
05:58:34,446 --> 05:58:38,084
When an option involves scanning, it is usually there to be rejected.

3664
05:58:38,510 --> 05:58:43,108
The better answer is an index that makes the access pattern a query instead.

3665
05:58:44,784 --> 05:58:50,468
The third bullet is a genuine constraint rather than a tuning choice and it decides questions.

3666
05:58:50,784 --> 05:58:55,460
A global secondary index is maintained asynchronously after the base table is written.

3667
05:58:56,206 --> 05:58:59,748
So there is a brief window where it does not reflect the newest item.

3668
05:59:00,398 --> 05:59:06,852
That is fine for a leaderboard and unacceptable for say checking whether a username is already taken.

3669
05:59:07,406 --> 05:59:16,132
So when a scenario states that a read must reflect the most recent write, any option routing it through a global secondary index is wrong.

3670
05:59:17,134 --> 05:59:25,540
Point in time, recovery is worth knowing by name because it is the Dynamo DB equivalent of the snapshot restore module 10 covered.

3671
05:59:27,054 --> 05:59:33,344
Both let you query on something other than the primary key and the constraint in the call out is what usually decides.

3672
05:59:33,664 --> 05:59:39,588
A local secondary index must be defined when the table is created and can never be added afterwards.

3673
05:59:40,430 --> 05:59:47,172
So any scenario describing an existing table with a new access pattern is a global secondary index question by elimination.

3674
05:59:47,566 --> 05:59:55,824
Global indexes are also more flexible, allowing a completely different partition key, which is what you need when the new query groups items differently.

3675
05:59:56,366 --> 06:00:02,404
The cost is eventual consistency and separate capacity, which is occasionally the point of a question.

3676
06:00:04,110 --> 06:00:10,276
The third bullet should feel familiar because module 3 described exactly this in Kinesis data streams.

3677
06:00:10,926 --> 06:00:17,700
Throttling occurs while overall utilization looks low because traffic concentrates on one key rather than spreading across all of them.

3678
06:00:18,542 --> 06:00:27,744
Adaptive capacity mitigates it by shifting throughput towards the busy partition, which genuinely helps and does not solve a fundamentally skewed

3679
06:00:27,904 --> 06:00:28,260
design.

3680
06:00:28,782 --> 06:00:31,780
The real answer is the same one as for kinesis.

3681
06:00:32,544 --> 06:00:35,236
Change the key so traffic distributes.

3682
06:00:36,110 --> 06:00:41,668
On the capacity mode choice above it, the signal words are the same as everywhere in this course.

3683
06:00:42,478 --> 06:00:46,436
Unpredictable, spiky, and variable point at on demand.

3684
06:00:47,982 --> 06:00:51,236
Study with a stated throughput figure, points at provisioned.

3685
06:00:52,910 --> 06:00:59,780
A gaming company stores player profiles in Amazon DynamoDB with player ID as the partition key.

3686
06:01:00,206 --> 06:01:08,996
A new feature must show a leaderboard of the top 100 players per region refreshed constantly and read by every player who opens the app.

3687
06:01:09,614 --> 06:01:14,660
The current implementation scans the whole table and sorts the results in the application.

3688
06:01:15,184 --> 06:01:20,868
The table holds 40 million players and read costs have become the largest line on the bill.

3689
06:01:21,966 --> 06:01:24,324
What should the data engineer implement?

3690
06:01:26,318 --> 06:01:31,104
Increase the table's provision read capacity so the scan completes faster.

3691
06:01:31,424 --> 06:01:31,664
b.

3692
06:01:32,224 --> 06:01:40,544
Add a global secondary index partitioned by region and sorted by score. C. Add a local secondary index sorted by score.

3693
06:01:40,942 --> 06:01:43,664
D. Export the table to Amazon S3.

3694
06:01:44,206 --> 06:01:47,140
Nightly and query the leaderboard with Amazon Athena.

3695
06:01:47,822 --> 06:01:48,964
Pause and decide.

3696
06:01:55,342 --> 06:02:00,644
The answer is B, and option C fails twice over, which is worth noticing.

3697
06:02:01,966 --> 06:02:05,028
A local secondary index keeps the table's partition key.

3698
06:02:06,798 --> 06:02:12,272
So items would still be grouped by player rather than by region, and the query could not be answered.

3699
06:02:12,814 --> 06:02:17,072
Separately, a local index cannot be added to a table that already exists.

3700
06:02:17,390 --> 06:02:19,428
Either of those alone eliminates it.

3701
06:02:21,006 --> 06:02:24,228
Option D is the analytical answer to an operational question.

3702
06:02:24,512 --> 06:02:30,752
That is the error the four question slide warned about and the phase refreshed constantly is what rules it out.

3703
06:02:32,110 --> 06:02:36,996
Aurora was added to the InScope service list in the December 2025 revision.

3704
06:02:38,094 --> 06:02:41,828
So expect it to appear more often than older material suggests.

3705
06:02:42,798 --> 06:02:45,028
The distinction itself is straightforward.

3706
06:02:45,806 --> 06:02:49,668
Aurora is compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL only.

3707
06:02:50,574 --> 06:02:58,596
Within those two, it offers the storage layer spanning 3, availability zones, considerably faster replication, and up to 15 read replicas.

3708
06:02:59,152 --> 06:03:05,924
So a question involving Oracle, SQL Server, MariaDB or DB2 rules Aurora out entirely.

3709
06:03:07,726 --> 06:03:10,724
Amazon RDS is then the answer by elimination.

3710
06:03:11,598 --> 06:03:19,472
If it involves MySQL or PostgreSQL and mentions high availability, read scaling or growth, the team is worried about Aurora

3711
06:03:19,632 --> 06:03:20,612
is being pointed at.

3712
06:03:22,190 --> 06:03:28,484
Most Aurora capability sits outside a data engineer scope and three items here are squarely inside it.

3713
06:03:28,814 --> 06:03:36,676
0ETL2 Redshift is the one module 10 spent time on. It is the main reason Aurora appears in Analytics questions at on.

3714
06:03:37,518 --> 06:03:44,420
Global database matters for questions stating a multi-region requirement, where sub-second replication lag is the figure to remember.

3715
06:03:44,832 --> 06:03:50,916
And PG vector is what makes Aurora a vector store, which the last section of this module covers in detail.

3716
06:03:51,950 --> 06:03:56,100
The renaming on the final bullet is recent enough that both names circulate freely.

3717
06:03:56,590 --> 06:04:03,684
So recognize Aurora Serverless version 2 and Aurora Serverless as the same product with a tidied up name.

3718
06:04:04,974 --> 06:04:10,788
The memory DB point deserves attention because candidates reach for Elastic Cache by habit.

3719
06:04:11,246 --> 06:04:15,632
A cache holds a copy of data that also lives somewhere authoritative.

3720
06:04:15,712 --> 06:04:18,980
So losing it costs performance rather than data.

3721
06:04:19,886 --> 06:04:26,052
MemoryDB is in memory and durable with a transaction log across availability zones.

3722
06:04:26,432 --> 06:04:31,172
So it can be the primary store for data that needs microsecond access.

3723
06:04:32,302 --> 06:04:38,372
Note also that memory DB appears on this exam's InScope service list and ElastiCache does not.

3724
06:04:39,278 --> 06:04:43,204
That tells you which one AWS expects you to reach for here.

3725
06:04:43,872 --> 06:04:51,716
Neptune is worth recognizing for one signal, which is a question about relationships between things rather than attributes of things.

3726
06:04:53,006 --> 06:04:58,660
Open search answers two quite different question shapes and it is worth separating them deliberately.

3727
06:04:59,072 --> 06:05:09,252
The first is genuine search, where a user types words and expects relevant results langed by how well they match, including partial matches and synonyms.

3728
06:05:09,712 --> 06:05:12,164
No other service in this module does that.

3729
06:05:13,070 --> 06:05:21,316
The second is log analytics, where operational logs are indexed and explored interactively and module 14 returns to it properly.

3730
06:05:22,062 --> 06:05:28,292
The last bullet is newer and increasingly the reason OpenSearch shows up in data engineering questions at all.

3731
06:05:29,166 --> 06:05:37,796
That is because Xboutem Vector Search makes it the usual production choice for retrieval augmented generation, which the vector section of this module covers.

3732
06:05:39,406 --> 06:05:45,604
A retailer runs a product catalog on a self-managed MongoDB cluster on Amazon EC2.

3733
06:05:46,112 --> 06:05:50,404
Around 60 microservices query it using MongoDB drivers.

3734
06:05:51,086 --> 06:05:57,316
The operations team spends significant time patching and managing replica sets and wants to reduce that work.

3735
06:05:58,272 --> 06:06:06,372
A proposal on the table is to migrate the catalog to Amazon DynamoDB on the grounds that it is fully managed and scans better.

3736
06:06:07,662 --> 06:06:09,636
How should the data engineer respond?

3737
06:06:10,478 --> 06:06:11,392
A. Agree.

3738
06:06:11,982 --> 06:06:15,588
DynamoDB is fully managed and eliminated the operational work.

3739
06:06:18,158 --> 06:06:24,644
Recommend Amazon DocumentDB, which is MongoDB compatible and leaves the 60 services unchanged.

3740
06:06:25,262 --> 06:06:25,552
C.

3741
06:06:26,112 --> 06:06:32,036
Recommend Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL using JSONB columns for the documents.

3742
06:06:32,432 --> 06:06:32,752
D.

3743
06:06:33,152 --> 06:06:37,380
Recommend Amazon Key Spaces which handles document workloads at scale.

3744
06:06:37,792 --> 06:06:39,108
Pause and decide.

3745
06:06:45,838 --> 06:06:51,812
The answer is B, and this is the compatibility rule from the one-line table applied directly.

3746
06:06:54,670 --> 06:07:00,452
60 applications, speaking a particular KPI is a constraint, not a detail.

3747
06:07:02,414 --> 06:07:09,472
Options A and C both satisfy the operational requirement by creating an enormous migration project.

3748
06:07:10,030 --> 06:07:16,036
This is the same shape as module 3, where existing Kafka meant Amazon MSK rather than Kinesis.

3749
06:07:17,646 --> 06:07:22,116
Module 4, where suppliers using SFTP meant transfer family.

3750
06:07:22,912 --> 06:07:29,060
Whenever a question counts the applications depending on an interface, it is telling you the interface cannot change.

3751
06:07:30,702 --> 06:07:38,948
The exam guide names designing schemas for Redshift, Dynamo DB, and Lake Formation as a single skill.

3752
06:07:40,014 --> 06:07:44,036
That is a slightly odd grouping until you see the point behind it.

3753
06:07:44,782 --> 06:07:53,060
Each is a different answer to the same question of how to arrange data, and the arrangement follows entirely from what will read it.

3754
06:07:53,966 --> 06:08:02,436
An operational store is optimized for writing and updating individual records without inconsistency, so each fact lives in one place.

3755
06:08:03,054 --> 06:08:08,036
An analytical store is optimized for reading enormous numbers of records and aggregating them.

3756
06:08:08,352 --> 06:08:11,588
So repeating a value to avoid a join is a good trade.

3757
06:08:12,494 --> 06:08:20,512
Those goals genuinely conflict, which is why the same data ends up modeled twice in most organizations and why zero

3758
06:08:20,672 --> 06:08:22,052
ETL exists.

3759
06:08:23,758 --> 06:08:29,060
The star schema is worth knowing by name because redshift questions assume it.

3760
06:08:29,632 --> 06:08:34,180
A center fact table holds the measurements, one row per transaction.

3761
06:08:34,832 --> 06:08:41,188
Dimension tables around it hold the descriptive attributes, one row per customer or product or store.

3762
06:08:42,126 --> 06:08:45,796
That shape is why module 10's first scenario worked as it did.

3763
06:08:46,192 --> 06:08:50,596
The fact table is large and gets a distribution key on the join column.

3764
06:08:51,022 --> 06:08:54,628
The dimension tables are small and get ALL distribution.

3765
06:08:55,374 --> 06:09:03,712
A Snowflake schema normalizes those dimensions further, which saves a little space and adds joins, and the exam generally prefers the star.

3766
06:09:05,198 --> 06:09:11,204
Single table design is the idea that surprises people most, and the reasoning is straightforward once stated.

3767
06:09:11,566 --> 06:09:18,672
DynamoDB has no joins, so retrieving an order and its line items from two tables means two requests.

3768
06:09:19,182 --> 06:09:26,832
If instead the order and its line items share a partition key, one query returns all of them together in a single round trip.

3769
06:09:27,072 --> 06:09:32,708
That is why an experienced Dynamo DB table often looks strange, holding several entity types at once.

3770
06:09:32,912 --> 06:09:36,228
The exam does not ask you to design one from scratch.

3771
06:09:36,750 --> 06:09:42,980
It does expect you to recognize that splitting entities into separate tables the relational way is a mistake here.

3772
06:09:44,494 --> 06:09:53,828
A logistics company needs to answer questions like which drivers have delivered to which depots and how two shipments are connected to shared handling facilities.

3773
06:09:54,478 --> 06:09:58,756
The relationships are several layers deep and the depth varies by question.

3774
06:09:59,758 --> 06:10:07,684
The data currently sits in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL and these queries require recursive joins that take minutes to return.

3775
06:10:08,192 --> 06:10:10,564
Which store best fits this access pattern?

3776
06:10:11,232 --> 06:10:15,172
a Amazon Neptune querying the relationships as a graph.

3777
06:10:15,726 --> 06:10:20,612
B Amazon Dynamo DB with a global secondary index per relationship type.

3778
06:10:21,102 --> 06:10:25,444
C Amazon Redshift loading the tables and using window functions.

3779
06:10:26,094 --> 06:10:30,532
D Amazon Open Search Service indexing the relationships as documents.

3780
06:10:31,310 --> 06:10:32,388
Pause and decide

3781
06:10:39,086 --> 06:10:44,784
The answer is A, and the phrase that decides it is that the depth varies by question.

3782
06:10:45,166 --> 06:10:49,184
A join in SQL is written for a fixed number of hops.

3783
06:10:49,806 --> 06:10:59,812
So a query connecting two shipments through an unknown number of intermediate facilities cannot be expressed cleanly and becomes a recursive query that performs badly.

3784
06:11:00,464 --> 06:11:07,620
Graph databases traverse edges as their native operation, so depth is a parameter rather than a structural problem.

3785
06:11:08,558 --> 06:11:15,460
Neptune appears rarely on this exam, and when it does, the signal is almost always this one.

3786
06:11:16,142 --> 06:11:20,004
Relationships are the thing being queried rather than attributes.

3787
06:11:21,518 --> 06:11:27,466
The exam guide groups Redshift, DynamoDB, and Lake Formation schema design into one skill.

3788
06:11:28,142 --> 06:11:33,124
Lake Formation is the one people find odd because it is a permission service rather than a store.

3789
06:11:33,904 --> 06:11:38,020
The connection is that the shape of a table decides what can be governed.

3790
06:11:38,894 --> 06:11:45,796
Module 2's 6th scenario turned on exactly this, where column level permissions required reading through the catalog.

3791
06:11:46,224 --> 06:11:52,132
If sensitive columns are scattered through a wide table, every consumer needs a column level policy.

3792
06:11:52,814 --> 06:12:00,772
If they are grouped or held in a separate table, joined when needed, the access rules become simple enough to reason about.

3793
06:12:02,286 --> 06:12:06,340
Two skills entered the exam guide in the latest revision.

3794
06:12:06,784 --> 06:12:07,984
One names

3795
06:12:08,718 --> 06:12:13,284
vector index types including HNSW and IVF.

3796
06:12:13,966 --> 06:12:19,108
The other names vectorization concepts and Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases.

3797
06:12:19,854 --> 06:12:25,060
No older question bank covers either, so this section is entirely new material.

3798
06:12:25,966 --> 06:12:29,124
The concept is less intimidating than the vocabulary.

3799
06:12:29,870 --> 06:12:37,732
An embedding turns a piece of text or an image into a list of numbers positioned so that similar things sit near each other.

3800
06:12:38,702 --> 06:12:45,892
Finding similar content then becomes finding nearby points, which is a geometry problem rather than a text matching problem.

3801
06:12:47,584 --> 06:12:55,684
The callout is the clearest way to see what vector search actually buys you. Those two phrases share no significant words at all.

3802
06:12:55,904 --> 06:12:57,860
So keyword search finds nothing.

3803
06:12:58,144 --> 06:13:01,796
And yet they obviously mean the same thing to a human reader.

3804
06:13:02,638 --> 06:13:08,868
Embeddings place them close together because the model that produced them has learned that relationship from language.

3805
06:13:09,184 --> 06:13:12,964
The last two bullets are the honest balance and they matter for the exam.

3806
06:13:13,902 --> 06:13:21,904
Vector search is weak precisely where keyword search is strong on exact identifiers like a part number,

3807
06:13:22,064 --> 06:13:24,068
an error code, or a policy number.

3808
06:13:24,464 --> 06:13:31,716
That is why serious production systems run both and combine the results, and that combination is what hybrid search means.

3809
06:13:33,584 --> 06:13:42,788
Hierarchical navigable small world abbreviated HNSW builds a layered graph where each vector links to its near neighbors.

3810
06:13:43,278 --> 06:13:48,036
So a search hops through the graph towards the answer rather than checking everything.

3811
06:13:48,846 --> 06:13:53,636
It is fast and accurate and it holds the whole structure in memory, which is the cost.

3812
06:13:54,286 --> 06:13:55,344
Converted file

3813
06:13:56,814 --> 06:14:01,476
instead groups vectors into clusters and searches only the nearest few clusters.

3814
06:14:02,222 --> 06:14:07,652
That uses far less memory and can miss a result sitting just outside the clusters it examined.

3815
06:14:08,590 --> 06:14:10,148
Both are approximate.

3816
06:14:10,766 --> 06:14:17,668
Flat is exact and compares against every vector, which is fine for thousands and impossible for millions.

3817
06:14:19,406 --> 06:14:23,812
6 places and the exam mostly tests the reasoning rather than the list.

3818
06:14:25,390 --> 06:14:27,460
The first two carry most of the questions.

3819
06:14:27,758 --> 06:14:34,756
Consider a scenario already running Aurora Postgree SQL that wants embeddings beside its relational data.

3820
06:14:35,374 --> 06:14:39,556
Pig vector keeps everything in one database and avoids a second system.

3821
06:14:40,494 --> 06:14:46,688
If a scenario describes production semantic search at scale with filtering and relevance, open search is the answer.

3822
06:14:46,928 --> 06:14:55,044
S3 vectors is the newest entry and it follows the storage pattern this course keeps returning to, trading latency for a much lower price.

3823
06:14:55,728 --> 06:15:00,324
That suits large archives of embeddings that are queried occasionally.

3824
06:15:01,902 --> 06:15:06,468
The framing that makes this examinable is the one this course applies to everything.

3825
06:15:06,928 --> 06:15:13,124
Building retrieval yourself means splitting documents into chunks and calling an embedding model for each.

3826
06:15:14,126 --> 06:15:15,844
Then storing the vectors,

3827
06:15:16,526 --> 06:15:21,284
keeping them current as document change, and writing the retrieval logic.

3828
06:15:22,254 --> 06:15:25,156
That is a genuine pipeline with genuine maintenance.

3829
06:15:25,488 --> 06:15:33,508
A knowledge base does all of it as configuration at the cost of less control over the details, particularly how documents are chunked.

3830
06:15:34,350 --> 06:15:46,528
So a question emphasizing least operational overhead points at knowledge bases and one describing a specific chunking or filtering requirement points at building it.

3831
06:15:47,822 --> 06:15:53,668
An insurance company runs its policy administration system on Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL.

3832
06:15:54,734 --> 06:16:05,316
It wants to add semantic search over policy documents so that an adjuster searching for cover for water damage from a burst pipe finds relevant clauses even when the wording differs.

3833
06:16:06,158 --> 06:16:08,516
There are around 2 million document chunks.

3834
06:16:09,568 --> 06:16:16,356
Searches must also filter by policy type and effective date, which are columns already in the Aurora database.

3835
06:16:17,230 --> 06:16:20,388
The team is small and wants to avoid running another system.

3836
06:16:21,326 --> 06:16:23,396
What should the data engineer recommend?

3837
06:16:25,454 --> 06:16:31,652
store the embeddings in Aurora PostgreSQL using PG vector with an HNSW index.

3838
06:16:32,048 --> 06:16:37,284
b Deploy an Amazon OpenSource Service Domain and index the embeddings there.

3839
06:16:37,678 --> 06:16:42,768
C. Store the embeddings in Amazon MemoryDB for the lowest possible latency.

3840
06:16:43,150 --> 06:16:43,408
D

3841
06:16:44,526 --> 06:16:49,728
Use Amazon Athena to search the document text stored in Amazon S3.

3842
06:16:50,350 --> 06:16:51,780
Pause and decide.

3843
06:16:58,510 --> 06:17:01,988
The answer is A, and option B is deliberately strong.

3844
06:17:02,448 --> 06:17:10,532
Open search genuinely is the better production choice at large scale with demanding search requirements. And this course said so two slides ago.

3845
06:17:11,374 --> 06:17:20,836
What tips it here is the combination of a small team wanting one system and more decisively the filter columns already living in Aurora.

3846
06:17:22,222 --> 06:17:28,964
With PG vector, filtering by policy type and searching by similarity is one SQL statement.

3847
06:17:29,870 --> 06:17:35,044
With OpenSearch, the vectors are in one system and the filter attributes in another.

3848
06:17:35,374 --> 06:17:39,108
So either you duplicate them or you query twice and combine.

3849
06:17:40,846 --> 06:17:45,808
A media company is building a support assistant over 800,000 help articles.

3850
06:17:46,126 --> 06:17:53,572
It needs semantic retrieval, keyword matching for exact product codes, filtering by product line, and relevance tuning.

3851
06:17:55,182 --> 06:17:59,844
Query volume is high and latency must stay under 200 milliseconds.

3852
06:18:00,558 --> 06:18:04,260
The company has a platform team with capacity to operate infrastructure.

3853
06:18:04,654 --> 06:18:06,564
Which vector store fits best?

3854
06:18:07,054 --> 06:18:12,164
a Amazon S3 vectors for the lowest storage cost at this corpus size.

3855
06:18:12,718 --> 06:18:18,084
B Amazon Open Search Service with HNSW indexing and hybrid search.

3856
06:18:18,448 --> 06:18:18,688
C

3857
06:18:19,726 --> 06:18:24,196
Aurora PostgreSQL with PG vector and an IVF flat index.

3858
06:18:24,608 --> 06:18:24,848
D.

3859
06:18:25,326 --> 06:18:29,252
Amazon Neptune Analytics treating articles as connected nodes.

3860
06:18:30,126 --> 06:18:31,332
Pause and decide.

3861
06:18:38,062 --> 06:18:41,764
The answer is B, and this pairs with the previous scenario on purpose.

3862
06:18:43,214 --> 06:18:48,324
There, Aurora won because the filter columns already lived there and the team wanted one system.

3863
06:18:49,486 --> 06:18:56,004
Here there is no relational data in the picture. The requirement explicitly names both semantic and exact keyword matching.

3864
06:18:56,878 --> 06:18:59,268
There is a team able to run infrastructure.

3865
06:19:00,366 --> 06:19:08,964
Exact product codes alongside semantic meaning is the definition of hybrid search, which is open search's particular strength.

3866
06:19:09,806 --> 06:19:19,012
Option A fails on a single stated number, and noticing that a latency budget rules out a cost-optimized store is a transferable skill here.

3867
06:19:20,718 --> 06:19:27,044
Module 1's billing unit rule applies here, unchanged, and two rows are worth calling out specifically.

3868
06:19:27,502 --> 06:19:36,452
Dynamo DB is unusual in offering both models cleanly and letting you switch between them without downtime, which makes the switch itself a genuine cost lever.

3869
06:19:37,358 --> 06:19:44,464
A question describing a table with widely variable traffic sitting on provision capacity is pointing at on demand.

3870
06:19:45,038 --> 06:19:48,196
The sixth worked scenario in this module is exactly that.

3871
06:19:48,624 --> 06:19:51,626
Open search serverless carries a minimum capacity floor.

3872
06:19:52,224 --> 06:19:55,332
That makes it surprisingly expensive for a small workload.

3873
06:19:56,110 --> 06:20:02,180
A question about a modest vector store with tight cost constraints may point somewhere else entirely.

3874
06:20:02,704 --> 06:20:08,196
Memory DB is the costliest per gigabyte here by a wide margin because memory is

3875
06:20:09,806 --> 06:20:16,064
an application writes session state to Amazon Dynamo DB with provision capacity of 4000.

3876
06:20:16,544 --> 06:20:18,064
Ride capacity units.

3877
06:20:18,304 --> 06:20:24,304
Traffic is heavily concentrated in a 2 hour window each evening and near zero for the rest of the day.

3878
06:20:24,464 --> 06:20:27,652
During the peak, writes are occasionally throttled.

3879
06:20:28,366 --> 06:20:35,492
Outside it, utilization sits below 5%. The team wants to reduce cost and stop the throttling.

3880
06:20:36,398 --> 06:20:38,020
What should the data engineer do?

3881
06:20:38,798 --> 06:20:47,748
a increase provision capacity to 8000, write capacity units, b switch the table to on-demand capacity mode.

3882
06:20:49,104 --> 06:20:49,424
C

3883
06:20:50,670 --> 06:20:55,364
Enable Dynamo DB auto scaling with the same minimum and a higher maximum.

3884
06:20:55,824 --> 06:20:56,144
D.

3885
06:20:56,654 --> 06:20:59,460
Add a global secondary index to spread the right load.

3886
06:21:00,046 --> 06:21:01,380
Pause and decide.

3887
06:21:08,078 --> 06:21:09,444
The answer is B.

3888
06:21:09,744 --> 06:21:16,516
Option C deserves respect because autoscaling is a real answer to this shape of problem and it is what many teams do.

3889
06:21:16,704 --> 06:21:22,276
It loses on the detail that autoscaling responds to observed usage adjusting after load rises.

3890
06:21:23,022 --> 06:21:29,028
So a sharp 2 hour spike can throttle during the ramp before capacity catches up.

3891
06:21:31,662 --> 06:21:33,924
Option D is the one to be sure about.

3892
06:21:34,304 --> 06:21:38,384
A global secondary index is not a load spreading mechanism.

3893
06:21:38,798 --> 06:21:42,864
Every write to the base table generates a write to the index as well.

3894
06:21:43,086 --> 06:21:47,972
So adding one increases consume write capacity rather than reducing it.

3895
06:21:49,422 --> 06:21:53,636
The callout is a genuinely useful detail and it appears in questions.

3896
06:21:53,984 --> 06:22:01,476
Copying a large DynamoDB table into a data lake by scanning it reads every item and charges read capacity for all of them.

3897
06:22:02,030 --> 06:22:06,144
That can be expensive and can affect the application sharing that capacity.

3898
06:22:06,798 --> 06:22:10,212
The export to S3 feature reads from a continuous backup instead.

3899
06:22:10,624 --> 06:22:15,108
So it consumes no table capacity at all and does not compete with production traffic.

3900
06:22:15,584 --> 06:22:23,584
When an option offers a scan to extract data for analytics and another offers export to S3, the export is almost always intended.

3901
06:22:24,782 --> 06:22:28,324
A company stores order records in Amazon DynamoDB.

3902
06:22:28,654 --> 06:22:34,276
The analytics team needs the full order history in the data lake daily for reporting around 200 million items.

3903
06:22:34,544 --> 06:22:37,252
The current process runs a parallel scan overnight.

3904
06:22:37,902 --> 06:22:47,396
The scan consumes most of the table's provisioned read capacity, and the customer-facing application has started seeing throttled reads during the overnight window.

3905
06:22:47,886 --> 06:22:49,956
What should the data engineer implement?

3906
06:22:50,734 --> 06:22:57,028
a Use Dynamo DB export to Amazon S3, which consumes no table read capacity.

3907
06:22:59,118 --> 06:23:03,460
Increase the table's provisioned read capacity for the overnight window.

3908
06:23:04,046 --> 06:23:09,744
C. Run the scan with a lower rate limit so it takes longer but uses less capacity.

3909
06:23:10,064 --> 06:23:10,384
D.

3910
06:23:10,734 --> 06:23:15,684
Enable Dynamo DB streams and rebuild the full history from the change records.

3911
06:23:17,006 --> 06:23:18,148
Pause and decide.

3912
06:23:24,878 --> 06:23:26,436
The answer is A.

3913
06:23:27,200 --> 06:23:39,492
Options B and C both accept that the export competes with the application and try to manage that competition. One buys more capacity, the other slows the export down.

3914
06:23:40,334 --> 06:23:46,692
The export feature removes the competition rather than managing it because it never touches the table's provision throughput.

3915
06:23:47,470 --> 06:23:49,892
Option D is worth knowing as a category error.

3916
06:23:50,240 --> 06:23:56,516
Dynamo DB streams carry changes as they happen with 24 hours of retention as module 8 covered.

3917
06:23:57,070 --> 06:24:01,348
So they can keep a copy current and cannot produce a history that predates them.

3918
06:24:02,958 --> 06:24:10,756
This table is the module as a lookup, and the first row is worth carrying between domains rather than learning as a dynamo DB fact.

3919
06:24:12,398 --> 06:24:19,280
Throttling that occurs while overall utilization looks comfortable means the load is concentrated rather than excessive.

3920
06:24:19,920 --> 06:24:27,748
That is equally true of kinesis shards in module 3, dynamo DB partitions here, and redshift slices in module 10.

3921
06:24:28,080 --> 06:24:33,860
In every case, adding capacity fails because the concentration survives it and changing the key succeeds.

3922
06:24:34,478 --> 06:24:40,740
Recognizing a familiar failure mode wearing a different services name is one of the highest value skills for this exam.

3923
06:24:41,902 --> 06:24:45,572
Module 21 on confusion pairs returns to it deliberately.

3924
06:24:46,254 --> 06:24:50,020
The fourth row is the newest and worth remembering as its own answer.

3925
06:24:51,726 --> 06:24:54,660
A company is choosing a store for four workloads.

3926
06:24:54,960 --> 06:24:59,172
A shopping cart read by customer ID with millisecond latency.

3927
06:25:00,110 --> 06:25:05,348
A three-year sales history queried by analysts with varied ad hoc SQL.

3928
06:25:06,830 --> 06:25:09,540
A product search where shoppers type free text.

3929
06:25:10,542 --> 06:25:15,716
A fraud investigation tool tracing connections between accounts to arbitrary depth.

3930
06:25:17,454 --> 06:25:25,380
An architect proposes putting all four in Amazon Aurora Post-Gree SQL for consistency and to reduce the number of systems.

3931
06:25:26,734 --> 06:25:28,868
How should the data engineer respond?

3932
06:25:29,550 --> 06:25:30,880
a agree.

3933
06:25:31,886 --> 06:25:36,484
One system is simpler to operate and PostgreSQL can serve all four.

3934
06:25:37,006 --> 06:25:44,484
b Recommend Dynamo DB, Redshift, OpenSearch and Neptune respectively, matching each workload to its access pattern.

3935
06:25:45,038 --> 06:25:51,108
C. Recommend Amazon Redshift for all four since it handles both operational and analytical queries.

3936
06:25:52,800 --> 06:25:52,960
D.

3937
06:25:54,190 --> 06:25:57,860
Recommend Aurora for the first two and OpenSearch for the other two.

3938
06:25:58,510 --> 06:25:59,620
Pause and decide.

3939
06:26:06,318 --> 06:26:13,988
The answer is B and this closes the module deliberately because it is module 5's final scenario in a different domain.

3940
06:26:15,054 --> 06:26:21,700
There, four workloads pointed at four processing engines, and the tempting wrong answer was to standardize on one.

3941
06:26:22,606 --> 06:26:29,124
Here, four access patterns point at four stores, and the same instinct produces the same failure.

3942
06:26:29,840 --> 06:26:34,724
Standardization is a real engineering virtue, and the exam is not against it.

3943
06:26:35,374 --> 06:26:42,660
It is against applying it where the workloads have genuinely different shapes, and this question describes four shapes as clearly as it can.

3944
06:26:44,366 --> 06:26:49,120
Five questions, and the second is the fastest elimination available in this module.

3945
06:26:49,280 --> 06:26:55,044
That is because a name technology usually reduces four options to one in a single step.

3946
06:26:55,760 --> 06:27:01,220
The fourth is worth practicing because latency numbers in questions are never decorative.

3947
06:27:02,190 --> 06:27:10,788
Microseconds means in memory, milliseconds means a key value or relational store, and seconds means an analytical engine.

3948
06:27:13,454 --> 06:27:21,732
A stated number that no option can meet is how a question eliminates its most attractive distractor, which is exactly what happened in scenario 5.

3949
06:27:22,606 --> 06:27:29,540
Next module covers cataloging, which is how everything in this module and the last becomes discoverable.

3950
06:27:31,200 --> 06:27:40,836
This module closes domain 2 and it delivers a promise made back in module 2, which said the catalog and crawlers would be covered here.

3951
06:27:41,520 --> 06:27:47,716
It carries 9 separate skills from the exam guide, the second highest of any module in the course.

3952
06:27:48,622 --> 06:27:57,892
So much lands here because a catalog sits between everything else storage on one side, query engines on the other, and permissions layered on top of it.

3953
06:27:58,702 --> 06:28:02,852
Without a catalog, a data lake is a collection of files nobody can find.

3954
06:28:03,758 --> 06:28:11,108
We cover the technical catalog, how tables and partitions get into it, what happens when schemas change.

3955
06:28:12,558 --> 06:28:17,732
Then the business catalog and lineage layer that arrived with the latest exam guide revision.

3956
06:28:19,278 --> 06:28:24,420
Every query engine in this course reads through a catalog rather than looking at files directly.

3957
06:28:25,056 --> 06:28:32,740
Athena, Redshift Spectrum, Amazon, EMR, and AWS Glue all ask the same question first.

3958
06:28:34,542 --> 06:28:35,600
What tables exist?

3959
06:28:35,886 --> 06:28:37,188
What columns do they have?

3960
06:28:37,454 --> 06:28:39,300
And where does the data actually live?

3961
06:28:40,302 --> 06:28:49,040
The Glue Data Catalog answers that question once, for all of them at the same time, which is what makes a lake house possible

3962
06:28:49,200 --> 06:28:49,540
at all.

3963
06:28:50,414 --> 06:28:59,140
That is why it appeared 52 times in the bank analyze for discourse, despite being a metadata store rather than something that processes data.

3964
06:29:00,078 --> 06:29:05,540
Get the catalog wrong, and every engine downstream is wrong in exactly the same way.

3965
06:29:06,254 --> 06:29:10,724
That is what makes it so examinable and why it earns a module of its own.

3966
06:29:12,480 --> 06:29:16,676
This distinction runs through the whole module, so fix it now.

3967
06:29:17,038 --> 06:29:21,252
A technical catalog exists so that a query engine can plan a query.

3968
06:29:21,934 --> 06:29:29,604
It knows a table has 14 columns, that the third one is a timestamp and that the data sits under a particular S3 prefix.

3969
06:29:30,446 --> 06:29:33,860
It knows nothing whatsoever about what the table means.

3970
06:29:34,160 --> 06:29:39,172
A business catalog exists so that a person can find data they did not know existed.

3971
06:29:39,520 --> 06:29:45,092
They can then judge whether it is the right data set, see who owns it, and request access.

3972
06:29:46,094 --> 06:29:53,764
Version 1.1 of the exam guide added business catalogs as a separate skill, so questions can now be about either one.

3973
06:29:55,278 --> 06:29:59,200
The last bullet catches people out and it makes a fair exam question.

3974
06:29:59,502 --> 06:30:03,844
Dropping a table from the data catalog removes the description of where the data is,

3975
06:30:05,582 --> 06:30:10,500
leaves every file exactly where it was, costing exactly what it did before.

3976
06:30:11,214 --> 06:30:14,820
That is occasionally what you want, and it is more often a surprise.

3977
06:30:15,534 --> 06:30:25,280
The high Metastore compatibility in the call out is the reason the catalog is so central to everything. Apache Hive defined a Metastore interface years ago.

3978
06:30:25,454 --> 06:30:30,596
The whole open source ecosystem speaks it and the Glue Data Catalog implements it.

3979
06:30:31,278 --> 06:30:39,492
So Spark on EMR uses it as its metastore with no translation layer, and a migrating Hadoop estate simply keeps working.

3980
06:30:41,134 --> 06:30:48,036
The exam guide names creating source and target connections for cataloging as its own skill, which is why this slide exists.

3981
06:30:48,974 --> 06:30:51,556
The capability worth understanding is the fourth bullet.

3982
06:30:52,494 --> 06:31:01,988
Pointing a crawler at a JDBC connection reads the database's own metadata and creates catalog tables describing it without copying a single row.

3983
06:31:02,446 --> 06:31:10,692
That gives you a searchable picture of what exists in an operational database, which is often the first step in planning a migration or an integration.

3984
06:31:11,694 --> 06:31:17,508
The last bullet is the practical warning, and it is module 2's connectivity section applying again.

3985
06:31:18,126 --> 06:31:22,820
A connection that cannot reach the database phase before any cataloging happens.

3986
06:31:24,366 --> 06:31:29,796
Crawlers are the best known option and frequently not the best one, which is why the callout matters.

3987
06:31:31,150 --> 06:31:38,212
Module 2 made this point about enable update catalog where a blue job registers its own partitions as it writes them.

3988
06:31:39,086 --> 06:31:47,108
That removes an entire scheduled component from the design, along with the delay between data landing and becoming queryable.

3989
06:31:47,568 --> 06:31:52,228
Explicit DDL is the right answer when a schema is known and stable.

3990
06:31:53,134 --> 06:32:00,036
That is because inferring a schema you already know is unnecessary work with a real risk of getting it wrong.

3991
06:32:00,622 --> 06:32:06,084
So a crawler is a discovery tool for when you genuinely do not know what is in the data yet.

3992
06:32:07,598 --> 06:32:14,084
The 10-minute billing minimum from module 2 is worth repeating here because it produces a specific cost question.

3993
06:32:14,734 --> 06:32:22,288
A crawler scheduled every 5 minutes to pick up new files is billed for 10 minutes each time it runs for 30 seconds.

3994
06:32:22,862 --> 06:32:27,876
That is a poor trade for freshness that enable update catalog would have given you for nothing.

3995
06:32:28,368 --> 06:32:30,948
The classifier point is the practical one.

3996
06:32:31,408 --> 06:32:39,408
A crawler reading an unusual CSV dialect or a log format it does not recognize produces a table full of single string

3997
06:32:39,568 --> 06:32:40,036
columns.

3998
06:32:41,008 --> 06:32:47,908
A custom classifier with a drop pattern or a defined delimiter tells it how to read the file properly.

3999
06:32:49,646 --> 06:32:56,644
The first two settings are where crawler questions usually live because they decide what happens when reality disagrees with the catalog.

4000
06:32:59,790 --> 06:33:02,692
Suppose an upstream system starts sending an extra column.

4001
06:33:03,214 --> 06:33:08,228
With the update policy, the crawler adds it to the table and everything downstream sees it appear.

4002
06:33:09,070 --> 06:33:16,036
With the ignore policy, the table stays as it was and the new column is invisible until somebody acts deliberately.

4003
06:33:16,654 --> 06:33:18,148
Neither is right in general.

4004
06:33:18,956 --> 06:33:23,940
A curated table feeding regulated reports probably wants stability and an alert.

4005
06:33:24,558 --> 06:33:27,844
A discovery area probably wants the catalog to track reality.

4006
06:33:28,782 --> 06:33:33,124
The exam always states which situation you are in.

4007
06:33:34,382 --> 06:33:39,408
Most questions assume the Blue Data catalog and it is worth knowing the alternatives exist.

4008
06:33:39,808 --> 06:33:51,140
An organization migrating a Hadoop estate frequently arrives with a hive metastore holding years of table definitions. Rewriting all of it before anything can run is a poor place to start.

4009
06:33:51,726 --> 06:34:01,328
Catalog Federation lets Blue present that external metastore alongside native tables, so Athena and EMR query, both without a migration project

4010
06:34:01,488 --> 06:34:01,764
first.

4011
06:34:02,606 --> 06:34:07,396
The iceberg REST catalog is the newest entry and connects to module 9.

4012
06:34:08,238 --> 06:34:17,124
That is because it is the open interface SageMaker Lakehouse uses to expose tables to any iceberg capable engine.

4013
06:34:18,798 --> 06:34:24,676
A blue job writes daily parquet output to Amazon S3 under a hive style date partition.

4014
06:34:24,942 --> 06:34:33,028
A crawler is scheduled every 15 minutes to register new partitions so that analysts can query the current day with Amazon Athena.

4015
06:34:34,318 --> 06:34:36,196
Finance has queried the blue bill.

4016
06:34:37,008 --> 06:34:45,028
The crawler accounts for a large share of it and analysts still complain that new data takes up to 15 minutes to become visible.

4017
06:34:45,328 --> 06:34:47,428
What should the data engineer change?

4018
06:34:48,302 --> 06:34:53,604
a reduce the crawler schedule to every 5 minutes so data becomes visible sooner.

4019
06:34:56,814 --> 06:35:04,196
Enable Enable Update Catalog on the glue job so it registers its own partitions and remove the crawler.

4020
06:35:04,768 --> 06:35:10,276
C. Configure the crawler to crawl new folders only and keep the 15 minute schedule.

4021
06:35:11,248 --> 06:35:16,868
D. Switch the table to partition projection in Athena and delete the registered partitions.

4022
06:35:17,550 --> 06:35:18,820
Pause and decide.

4023
06:35:25,262 --> 06:35:30,500
The answer is B, and it is worth noticing that the two complaints share one cause.

4024
06:35:32,462 --> 06:35:39,268
The crawler exists to discover something the job already knew because the job chose that partition path when it wrote the file.

4025
06:35:40,814 --> 06:35:45,124
Registering it at right time removes both the cost and the delay at once.

4026
06:35:46,094 --> 06:35:54,496
Option D is the interesting distractor because partition projection is genuinely a good answer to a different question.

4027
06:35:54,894 --> 06:36:02,148
It works only in Athena, so any other engine reading the same table loses partition awareness entirely.

4028
06:36:02,976 --> 06:36:09,508
This course has spent two modules establishing that one catalog serves many engines.

4029
06:36:10,990 --> 06:36:14,496
Module 9 made this point about iceberg, where files.

4030
06:36:14,896 --> 06:36:17,348
Nothing references may as well not exist.

4031
06:36:18,222 --> 06:36:22,180
The same is true of a hive style catalog for a slightly different reason.

4032
06:36:23,182 --> 06:36:27,696
A query engine does not list the S3 prefix to discover what partitions exist.

4033
06:36:28,656 --> 06:36:33,668
That is because listing millions of objects would be unusably slow on every query.

4034
06:36:34,096 --> 06:36:38,948
It asks the catalog instead and then reads only the paths the catalog names.

4035
06:36:39,886 --> 06:36:49,188
So a job that writes a new day's data into a correctly name folder without registering it produces data that is physically present and correctly formatted,

4036
06:36:49,678 --> 06:36:51,620
returns nothing at all when queried.

4037
06:36:52,334 --> 06:36:53,732
Nothing errors anywhere.

4038
06:36:54,670 --> 06:37:01,092
That silence is exactly what makes it a good exam question, and it is the first row of this module symptom table.

4039
06:37:02,830 --> 06:37:08,452
The last row is the one worth understanding properly because it solves a different problem from the others.

4040
06:37:09,422 --> 06:37:14,020
Every other approach registers partitions so that the catalog can list them.

4041
06:37:14,958 --> 06:37:17,636
Partition projection instead tells Athena the pattern.

4042
06:37:17,856 --> 06:37:21,572
For example, that partitions are dates between 2020 and today.

4043
06:37:21,936 --> 06:37:26,052
Athena calculates which paths to read without consulting the catalog at all.

4044
06:37:26,638 --> 06:37:34,020
On a table with tens of thousands of partitions, that removes a metadata lookup which had become the slowest part of the query.

4045
06:37:34,670 --> 06:37:37,252
The limitation is that it is an Athena feature.

4046
06:37:37,870 --> 06:37:42,980
So Redshift Spectrum and EMR reading the same table gain nothing from it.

4047
06:37:44,718 --> 06:37:47,780
This symptom is distinctive and worth recognizing on site.

4048
06:37:48,016 --> 06:37:55,588
The query has not started scanning anything, the data volume is unremarkable, and there is a noticeable delay before any work begins at all.

4049
06:37:56,110 --> 06:38:01,776
That is the engine asking the catalog which partitions exist and getting back a very long list.

4050
06:38:02,638 --> 06:38:09,348
Partition indexes and projection. Both attack that directly, and the honest answer is often the last bullet instead.

4051
06:38:10,030 --> 06:38:16,772
A table partitioned by hour that is always queried by month has 24 times more partitions than it needs.

4052
06:38:19,950 --> 06:38:24,176
Coarsening it fixes the cost rather than optimizing around it.

4053
06:38:25,518 --> 06:38:31,620
An hourly ingestion process writes files to Amazon S3 under year, month, day and hour partitions.

4054
06:38:31,856 --> 06:38:35,684
The table now holds around 90,000 partitions covering 10 years.

4055
06:38:35,936 --> 06:38:39,908
Analysts query it exclusively by date range, never by hour.

4056
06:38:40,494 --> 06:38:47,716
Athena queries take 12 to 20 seconds before scanning begins, regardless of how narrow the date filter is.

4057
06:38:48,302 --> 06:38:51,524
Once scanning starts, queries complete quickly.

4058
06:38:52,110 --> 06:38:53,988
What is the most effective change?

4059
06:38:54,990 --> 06:38:58,564
a add a group partition index on the partition keys.

4060
06:39:00,430 --> 06:39:00,736
B

4061
06:39:02,030 --> 06:39:06,692
Repartition the data by year, month and day only, removing the hour partition.

4062
06:39:07,136 --> 06:39:07,456
C.

4063
06:39:08,046 --> 06:39:10,948
Increase the Athena workgroups query timeout.

4064
06:39:11,536 --> 06:39:11,856
D.

4065
06:39:12,302 --> 06:39:16,644
Run MSCK repair table to refresh the partition metadata.

4066
06:39:17,358 --> 06:39:18,496
Pause and decide.

4067
06:39:25,230 --> 06:39:28,900
The answer is B, and option A is deliberately reasonable.

4068
06:39:30,990 --> 06:39:37,988
A partition index genuinely does speed up lookup, and if the hourly partitioning were needed, it would be the right answer here.

4069
06:39:38,478 --> 06:39:42,628
The question rules that out by stating that analysts never query by hour.

4070
06:39:43,566 --> 06:39:48,672
That means 23 out of every 24 partitions exist for nobody at all.

4071
06:39:48,910 --> 06:39:55,748
This is the overpartitioning failure module 8 warned about, arriving as a catalog symptom rather than a storage one.

4072
06:39:56,718 --> 06:40:04,004
The delay before scanning is the diagnostic detail because it separates a metadata problem from a data volume problem.

4073
06:40:05,486 --> 06:40:13,764
The exam guide names addressing changes to the characteristics of data as a skill, and schema change is the sharpest version of it.

4074
06:40:14,446 --> 06:40:18,032
An upstream team adds a field without telling anyone.

4075
06:40:18,606 --> 06:40:25,988
A column that was always an integer starts arriving as a string because somebody allowed a code meaning not applicable.

4076
06:40:27,214 --> 06:40:31,300
A field is renamed in a source system on a release nobody read.

4077
06:40:32,110 --> 06:40:37,552
None of these are unusual, and all of them reach a pipeline written before they happened.

4078
06:40:38,094 --> 06:40:44,004
What separates a robust design from a fragile one is not whether the change happens because it will.

4079
06:40:44,558 --> 06:40:49,636
It is whether the change causes a loud failure, a silent wrong answer, or nothing at all.

4080
06:40:50,350 --> 06:40:53,392
The next few slides work through which is which.

4081
06:40:55,150 --> 06:40:57,764
The type change row deserves the emphasis.

4082
06:40:58,414 --> 06:41:01,572
When a numeric column starts receiving occasional strings,

4083
06:41:02,286 --> 06:41:06,372
a strict leader fails loudly, which is inconvenient and honest.

4084
06:41:07,406 --> 06:41:15,472
A permissive reader may coerce the unparsable values to null instead, and a sum over that column then quietly returns a number that is too low.

4085
06:41:15,662 --> 06:41:18,948
Nobody is alerted, the job succeeds, and a report is wrong.

4086
06:41:19,950 --> 06:41:32,100
Module 2's answer to this was the blue dynamic frame with resolve choice, which keeps both possibilities alive and makes you decide deliberately rather than guessing.

4087
06:41:32,814 --> 06:41:35,712
That is the whole reason dynamic frames exist.

4088
06:41:36,270 --> 06:41:39,108
And this is precisely the scenario they exist for.

4089
06:41:40,814 --> 06:41:45,412
The CSV row explains a specific production disaster that questions describe.

4090
06:41:45,870 --> 06:41:48,484
CSV has no column names inside the file.

4091
06:41:49,070 --> 06:41:55,588
So a column inserted in the middle by an upstream system shifts every subsequent column one place to the left.

4092
06:41:56,078 --> 06:41:59,492
Every value after that point is then read into the wrong field.

4093
06:42:00,366 --> 06:42:06,244
Dates land in an amount column, the job may still succeed and the data is nonsense.

4094
06:42:06,574 --> 06:42:09,540
That cannot happen with a self-describing format.

4095
06:42:09,792 --> 06:42:12,516
Avro is the row worth remembering separately.

4096
06:42:13,454 --> 06:42:21,220
That is because it was designed for evolution and is therefore the sensible choice for an ingestion layer where schemas change often.

4097
06:42:22,894 --> 06:42:28,512
The registry is the preventive answer and it changes where a schema problem gets discovered.

4098
06:42:28,686 --> 06:42:35,172
Without it, a producer starts sending a change record. The stream accepts it because a stream accepts bytes.

4099
06:42:36,078 --> 06:42:38,724
The failure surfaces in a consumer somewhere downstream.

4100
06:42:39,694 --> 06:42:44,164
That might be hours later, and it might be wrong data rather than an error.

4101
06:42:44,432 --> 06:42:52,100
With a registry and a compatibility rule, the producer's own right is rejected at the moment the incompatible change is attempted.

4102
06:42:52,782 --> 06:43:00,132
Module 3 mentioned this in passing under Amazon MSK, and it is worth knowing that it works with Kinesis and Flink too.

4103
06:43:01,632 --> 06:43:05,124
This is the half of the skill that gets overlooked.

4104
06:43:06,030 --> 06:43:12,868
Nothing about the schema changed and the pipeline degrades anyway because the data inside it is different.

4105
06:43:13,550 --> 06:43:21,604
A partition key that distributed traffic evenly across a hundred customers becomes skewed when one customer grows to 60% of the volume.

4106
06:43:22,542 --> 06:43:27,396
Suddenly the redshift slice or Dynamo DB partition holding that customer is throttling.

4107
06:43:28,270 --> 06:43:33,060
A view job correctly sized for last year's volume is not sized for this year's.

4108
06:43:33,998 --> 06:43:41,508
These are the same failures, modules 10 and 11 described, arriving through the passage of time rather than through bad design.

4109
06:43:43,216 --> 06:43:48,740
This slide exists to connect domain 2 to domain 4 before you get there.

4110
06:43:49,216 --> 06:43:56,548
Lake Formation does not grant permissions on S3 prefixes, it grants them on tables and columns registered in the data catalog.

4111
06:43:57,326 --> 06:44:04,420
That is why module 2's healthcare scenario required changing the read path before any grant could take effect.

4112
06:44:05,294 --> 06:44:12,324
The tag-based approach on the second bullet is worth recognizing by name now because it scales in a way per table grants do not.

4113
06:44:13,102 --> 06:44:20,100
You tag a table as containing personal data once, and a policy about personal data applies to it automatically.

4114
06:44:20,494 --> 06:44:23,236
Module 18 covers the mechanics properly.

4115
06:44:24,526 --> 06:44:29,796
A Glue job reads JSON events from a partner and writes parquet to a curated table.

4116
06:44:30,286 --> 06:44:33,732
Last week, the partner changed a field that had always been an integer.

4117
06:44:34,382 --> 06:44:39,300
About 2% of records now carry it as a coded string, representing an unknown value.

4118
06:44:39,918 --> 06:44:41,828
The job still reports success.

4119
06:44:42,416 --> 06:44:49,668
Analysts have noticed that a daily total is roughly 2% lower than the source system reports. No error appears in any log.

4120
06:44:50,222 --> 06:44:53,124
What is happening and what is the most appropriate fix?

4121
06:44:53,616 --> 06:44:56,516
a The blue job is dropping records.

4122
06:44:57,038 --> 06:45:00,164
Increase the DPU allocation so it processes all of them.

4123
06:45:02,510 --> 06:45:05,252
unpassable values are being coerced to null.

4124
06:45:05,646 --> 06:45:11,300
Read as a dynamic frame and use resolve choice to handle both types explicitly.

4125
06:45:12,238 --> 06:45:12,496
C.

4126
06:45:13,056 --> 06:45:15,204
The crawler has updated the schema.

4127
06:45:15,758 --> 06:45:18,724
Set the schema change policy to ignore changes.

4128
06:45:19,470 --> 06:45:19,696
D.

4129
06:45:20,430 --> 06:45:24,996
Enable job bookmarks so the job does not reprocess the affected records.

4130
06:45:26,734 --> 06:45:28,256
Pause and decide.

4131
06:45:34,574 --> 06:45:38,276
The answer is B, and the numbers do the work for you.

4132
06:45:40,526 --> 06:45:45,348
2% of records carry an unparsable value, and the total is 2% low.

4133
06:45:46,446 --> 06:45:48,132
That is not a coincidence.

4134
06:45:48,816 --> 06:45:51,044
The exam expects you to miss.

4135
06:45:51,662 --> 06:45:58,116
The job succeeded because a permissive read turned those values into nulls and a sum over nulls simply omits them.

4136
06:45:58,446 --> 06:46:03,876
This is the silent wrongness failure that has now appeared in modules 6, 7, and here.

4137
06:46:04,334 --> 06:46:08,816
It always needs a correctness answer rather than a capacity or scheduling one.

4138
06:46:09,390 --> 06:46:18,372
Resolve choice is Buse specific mechanism letting you cast, keep both types as separate columns or route the problem records aside.

4139
06:46:20,078 --> 06:46:27,716
Module 4 introduced this and it belongs here too, because schema conversion is a cataloging skill as much as a migration.

4140
06:46:30,478 --> 06:46:30,976
1.

4141
06:46:31,630 --> 06:46:34,372
The point worth adding is the assessment report.

4142
06:46:35,278 --> 06:46:41,764
Before converting anything, the tool inspects the source and produces a list of objects it can convert automatically.

4143
06:46:42,096 --> 06:46:43,844
Objects needing manual attention.

4144
06:46:44,110 --> 06:46:45,988
Objects it cannot convert at all.

4145
06:46:46,446 --> 06:46:51,716
That list is what turns a heterogeneous migration from a guess into a plan with a size.

4146
06:46:52,558 --> 06:47:05,476
The contradiction on the last two bullets is real and worth knowing about. The InScope Service List dropped AWSSCD in the very same division where a task statement still names it.

4147
06:47:07,150 --> 06:47:14,724
Version 1.1 of the exam guide added business data catalogs and Amazon SageMaker catalog as a named skill.

4148
06:47:15,214 --> 06:47:19,428
So this section is new material that older banks do not cover at all.

4149
06:47:20,046 --> 06:47:23,780
The problem it addresses is organizational rather than technical.

4150
06:47:24,736 --> 06:47:28,900
Picture a company with 400 tables across three accounts.

4151
06:47:29,296 --> 06:47:36,004
An analyst who needs revenue by region cannot tell which of nine plausible tables is authoritative.

4152
06:47:36,782 --> 06:47:44,996
They cannot tell whether the numbers are current, who owns it, whether anyone else trusts it, or how to get access without asking around.

4153
06:47:45,582 --> 06:47:50,596
The blue data catalog cannot help with any of that because none of it is technical metadata.

4154
06:47:51,086 --> 06:47:53,604
This is a different problem needing a different layer.

4155
06:47:55,278 --> 06:47:59,876
The naming is worth addressing directly because both versions appear in current documentation.

4156
06:48:00,430 --> 06:48:08,036
Amazon DataZone is the service, and the same capability appears inside the next generation of SageMaker as SageMaker Catalog.

4157
06:48:10,126 --> 06:48:13,348
Treat them as the same thing described from two directions.

4158
06:48:13,870 --> 06:48:20,100
The subscription workflow on the last bullet is the piece that changes how an organization actually works.

4159
06:48:20,590 --> 06:48:26,948
Without it, getting access to a dataset means finding the right engineer and asking them to write a policy.

4160
06:48:27,918 --> 06:48:36,580
With it, a consumer requests a subscription, the data owner approves it, and the underlying lake formation permissions are granted automatically.

4161
06:48:38,160 --> 06:48:45,200
This structure appears in two domains, four skills as well as in this one, which tells you AWS considers it important.

4162
06:48:45,440 --> 06:48:50,116
The idea is that access follows from what you are working on rather than from who you are.

4163
06:48:50,894 --> 06:48:59,812
A person joins a project, the project holds subscriptions to certain data sets, and membership grants the underlying permissions automatically.

4164
06:49:00,558 --> 06:49:03,716
When they leave the project, that access goes with them.

4165
06:49:04,590 --> 06:49:13,444
It is a meaningfully different model from granting individuals permissions on tables, which is how access sprawl accumulates over years.

4166
06:49:13,998 --> 06:49:17,476
Module 20 returns to it as a governance question.

4167
06:49:19,118 --> 06:49:27,360
Data lineage became generally available in December 2024 and entered the exam guide a year later, so it is new material for this exam.

4168
06:49:27,600 --> 06:49:32,420
The two questions in the heading are the two directions you traverse it and both matter.

4169
06:49:33,262 --> 06:49:40,132
Upstream, an analyst who distrusts a figure can trace it back through every transformation to the source system.

4170
06:49:42,542 --> 06:49:48,068
That is what makes a number defensible to an auditor rather than merely asserted.

4171
06:49:48,974 --> 06:49:57,636
Downstream, an engineer about to change a table can see everything that reads it, which turns a change from a guess into an assessment.

4172
06:49:58,318 --> 06:50:04,900
Both are impossible to answer reliably from memory once an organization has more than a handful of pipelines.

4173
06:50:05,806 --> 06:50:09,604
The fourth work scenario in this module is exactly that situation.

4174
06:50:11,310 --> 06:50:16,000
The last bullet is the harness limitation and it appears in questions about completeness.

4175
06:50:16,240 --> 06:50:19,876
Lineage is assembled from events that processing engines emit.

4176
06:50:20,160 --> 06:50:25,040
So a transformation performed by something that does not emit them appears as a break in the

4177
06:50:25,040 --> 06:50:29,956
chain. A handwritten script on an EC2 instance, for example.

4178
06:50:32,000 --> 06:50:36,324
The open lineage compatibility on the second bullet is the answer to that.

4179
06:50:37,518 --> 06:50:42,820
That is because it is an open standard and anything can be made to publish events into the same graph.

4180
06:50:43,630 --> 06:50:51,876
The versioning point is worth remembering for audit questions, where the requirement is to show what the lineage was at the time a figure was published.

4181
06:50:53,678 --> 06:50:59,360
A data engineering team is about to change the data type of a column in a widely used curated table.

4182
06:50:59,630 --> 06:51:08,132
They do not know which downstream jobs, dashboards, and reports read that column, and a previous similar change broke three reports nobody had been told about.

4183
06:51:08,750 --> 06:51:16,960
The estate uses AWS Blue for transformation, Amazon Redshift for the warehouse, and Amazon Athena for ad hoc queries.

4184
06:51:17,120 --> 06:51:20,516
The team wants to assess the impact before making the change.

4185
06:51:21,006 --> 06:51:22,372
What should the team use?

4186
06:51:22,894 --> 06:51:29,124
a query the AWS Blue Data Catalog for every table containing a column of that name.

4187
06:51:29,680 --> 06:51:30,000
b

4188
06:51:31,086 --> 06:51:36,585
Use data lineage in Amazon SageMaker catalog to trace downstream consumers at column level.

4189
06:51:37,200 --> 06:51:37,520
C.

4190
06:51:37,760 --> 06:51:42,212
Search AWS CloudRail for AP. I call that reference the table.

4191
06:51:42,670 --> 06:51:47,716
D. Enable Blue job bookmarks so affected jobs can be reprocessed after the change.

4192
06:51:48,526 --> 06:51:49,764
Pause and decide.

4193
06:51:56,430 --> 06:51:57,508
The answer is

4194
06:52:00,174 --> 06:52:10,308
B Option C is the thoughtful wrong answer because Cloud Trail genuinely does record who called what, and an engineer without lineage would probably start there.

4195
06:52:10,766 --> 06:52:14,852
The gap is that Cloud Trail records API calls against resources.

4196
06:52:15,566 --> 06:52:22,756
It can tell you a table was queried, and it cannot tell you that a glue job read one column from it, derived another column.

4197
06:52:23,534 --> 06:52:26,372
Wrote that into a table, three dashboards depend on.

4198
06:52:27,246 --> 06:52:30,916
Lineage tracks derivation rather than access,

4199
06:52:32,014 --> 06:52:37,092
which is exactly the difference between knowing something was touched and knowing what depends on it.

4200
06:52:38,766 --> 06:52:44,288
Two rows here describe failures where nothing reports an error and they are the ones worth carrying forward.

4201
06:52:44,654 --> 06:52:50,788
Unregistered partitions produce missing data because the engine never looks at files, the catalog does not name.

4202
06:52:51,726 --> 06:52:58,436
Tight coercion produces wrong data because values that could not be parsed became null and vanished from a sum.

4203
06:52:59,406 --> 06:53:06,848
Both look like a perfectly healthy pipeline from the outside, and neither is fixed by anything to do with capacity or scheduling.

4204
06:53:07,342 --> 06:53:14,756
Recognizing that a question describes silence rather than failure is what points you at this half of the table.

4205
06:53:16,046 --> 06:53:21,728
A catalog with 400 tables where 60 are live is worse than one with 60.

4206
06:53:22,030 --> 06:53:28,516
That is because every search returns plausible wrong answers and analysts lose confidence in all of it.

4207
06:53:29,198 --> 06:53:35,492
The last bullet is the mechanism for fixing that, and it is a use of lineage people do not immediately think of.

4208
06:53:37,294 --> 06:53:44,164
A table with no downstream lineage at all is one that nothing reads, which makes it a candidate for deprecation.

4209
06:53:45,166 --> 06:53:49,572
The deprecate rather than delete point patterns because deletion is irreversible.

4210
06:53:49,966 --> 06:53:54,820
A table might be seasonal, read once a year at audit time, and entirely legitimate.

4211
06:53:56,270 --> 06:54:00,516
5 questions, and the second is the one that recurs most across this domain.

4212
06:54:01,358 --> 06:54:08,932
A crawler is a discovery tool, and a great many pipelines run one to discover something. The writing job already knew perfectly well.

4213
06:54:09,550 --> 06:54:14,756
Whenever an option removes a crawler by registering metadata at write time, take it seriously.

4214
06:54:15,088 --> 06:54:19,780
The third is the diagnostic split that this module and module 6 both turn on.

4215
06:54:20,558 --> 06:54:29,060
Data that is absent and data that is wrong have different causes and different fixes, and options addressing one never solve the other.

4216
06:54:29,806 --> 06:54:33,444
That completes domain 2, 26% of the exam.

4217
06:54:34,382 --> 06:54:37,220
Domain 3 begins next with querying and analyzing.

4218
06:54:38,958 --> 06:54:44,452
The callout is module 10's most useful distinction appearing in a different service.

4219
06:54:45,008 --> 06:54:53,828
When Athena users complain about slowness, the first question is whether queries are waiting to start or running slowly once they have started.

4220
06:54:54,528 --> 06:54:58,212
Waiting means the account level concurrency quota is being reached.

4221
06:54:59,118 --> 06:55:07,300
The answers are capacity reservations or separating workloads into different work groups. So one team's burst does not starve another.

4222
06:55:07,886 --> 06:55:12,452
Running slowly means bytes scanned, which is everything else in this module.

4223
06:55:13,198 --> 06:55:20,324
Both present identically to a user who simply says the dashboard is slow and they have no answers in common at all.

4224
06:55:20,814 --> 06:55:24,548
That is precisely the kind of split the exam builds questions around.

4225
06:55:26,190 --> 06:55:29,796
Domain 3 begins here and it is worth 22% of the exam.

4226
06:55:30,224 --> 06:55:34,724
This module covers the consumption end of every pipeline the course has built so far.

4227
06:55:35,310 --> 06:55:42,692
Amazon Athena in depth, the SQL constructs the exam guide names explicitly, Spark notebooks, and business intelligence.

4228
06:55:43,504 --> 06:55:47,588
It also carries the most confusing naming situation on the whole exam.

4229
06:55:48,064 --> 06:55:53,668
That is because the service you know as Amazon QuickSight has been renamed twice in under a year.

4230
06:55:54,144 --> 06:55:56,964
Both old and new names appear in current material.

4231
06:55:57,678 --> 06:56:03,460
Four worked scenarios and one availability warning that affects what an option is even called.

4232
06:56:04,878 --> 06:56:11,620
Athena charges $5 per terabyte scanned, and that single fact drives most of what the exam asks about it.

4233
06:56:11,904 --> 06:56:13,828
Query duration barely matters.

4234
06:56:14,350 --> 06:56:16,224
Result size does not matter at all.

4235
06:56:16,494 --> 06:56:22,756
A select star over a 100 GB table costs the same whether it returns 10 rows or 10 million.

4236
06:56:23,694 --> 06:56:27,076
That is because you are built for what was read rather than what came back.

4237
06:56:27,534 --> 06:56:33,540
So when a question describes an Athena cost problem, the answer is always something that reduces bytes read.

4238
06:56:34,222 --> 06:56:36,964
The levers are ones this course has already established.

4239
06:56:37,742 --> 06:56:41,412
Columna format, partitioning, compression, and compaction.

4240
06:56:41,966 --> 06:56:50,084
What this module adds is the Athena specific machinery around those and the cases where the pricing model itself is the thing worth changing.

4241
06:56:51,822 --> 06:56:56,740
The callout is the connection back to module 12 and it is worth holding on to.

4242
06:56:57,614 --> 06:57:04,452
An external table defined once in the blue data catalog is queryable by Athena and by Redshift Spectrum.

4243
06:57:04,864 --> 06:57:09,732
Both see the same columns, the same partitions, and the same lake formation permissions.

4244
06:57:10,510 --> 06:57:18,704
They are two engines over one definition rather than two separate systems, which is what the word lake house has been describing since module

4245
06:57:18,864 --> 06:57:24,612
1. That is also why the partition projection warning from module 12 mattered so much.

4246
06:57:25,518 --> 06:57:30,564
That is because it is the one optimization Athena can apply that Spectrum cannot see.

4247
06:57:31,246 --> 06:57:38,212
The Trino heritage on the second bullet explains the SQL dialect you write and why federated connectors exist at all.

4248
06:57:39,982 --> 06:57:45,572
Module 0's demonstration scenario turned on the call out, so it is worth stating plainly here.

4249
06:57:47,022 --> 06:57:52,004
A workgroup data scan limit cancels any query that would read more than a stated volume.

4250
06:57:53,262 --> 06:57:58,372
That protects you from an analyst writing a select star against a 50TB table by accident.

4251
06:57:59,182 --> 06:58:01,860
It is a spending control rather than a performance feature.

4252
06:58:02,414 --> 06:58:08,612
An option proposing to raise it in response to a cost problem is doing the precise opposite of what was asked.

4253
06:58:08,944 --> 06:58:13,024
The result location point matters operationally and gets forgotten.

4254
06:58:13,486 --> 06:58:22,244
Every Athena query writes its results into S3, one object per query, and those accumulate and cost money indefinitely

4255
06:58:22,702 --> 06:58:24,868
unless a lifecycle rule expires them.

4256
06:58:26,606 --> 06:58:33,988
The iceberg support on the fourth bullet is what connects this module back to module 9 and it changes what Athena is for.

4257
06:58:34,446 --> 06:58:42,180
Historically, Athena read data and produced results, and any modification to that data meant a glue job rewriting files.

4258
06:58:42,926 --> 06:58:51,204
With Iceberg tables on engine version 3, an analyst can issue a merge, an update, or a delete against lake data as ordinary SQL.

4259
06:58:51,584 --> 06:58:55,044
Can time travel to a past snapshot inside a where clause?

4260
06:58:55,950 --> 06:59:03,664
That is exactly why module 9's first scenario answered a privacy deletion request with a delete statement rather than a rewriting job.

4261
06:59:03,824 --> 06:59:11,492
The engine version point matters because these capabilities depend on it, and a work group left on an older version does not have them.

4262
06:59:12,878 --> 06:59:21,124
The fourth bullet is worth calling out because it is the mistake analysts make constantly and it undoes everything else on this slide.

4263
06:59:22,542 --> 06:59:30,404
A table stored as parquet, carefully partitioned and then queried with select star reads every column of every matching partition.

4264
06:59:31,328 --> 06:59:39,076
The entire columnar advantage disappears the moment you ask for all the columns and the partitioning is still working while the format is not.

4265
06:59:39,488 --> 06:59:46,180
Query result reuse on the fifth bullet is Athena specific and useful in a narrow but common case.

4266
06:59:46,990 --> 06:59:53,828
Consider the same query running repeatedly against data that has not changed for a dashboard refreshing every minute.

4267
06:59:55,214 --> 06:59:59,556
The cached result comes back at no charge within a window you configure.

4268
07:00:01,038 --> 07:00:03,012
Two rows here surprise people.

4269
07:00:03,408 --> 07:00:12,708
Query results accumulate in S3 indefinitely, one object per query, and on a busy account that becomes a real storage line nobody looks at.

4270
07:00:13,582 --> 07:00:17,444
That is why a lifecycle rule on the results location is standard practice.

4271
07:00:18,606 --> 07:00:21,248
And the call out matters for a specific reason.

4272
07:00:21,870 --> 07:00:28,420
A query cancelled by a workgroup scan limit has already read whatever it read before being stopped, and you pay for that.

4273
07:00:28,608 --> 07:00:32,388
The limit prevents a catastrophic bill rather than a small one.

4274
07:00:32,782 --> 07:00:38,608
The rounding on the first row is minor and worth knowing because every query is billed as at least 10 MB.

4275
07:00:40,590 --> 07:00:48,768
Capacity reservations are the part of Athena that older material treats as impractical and that changed in February 2026.

4276
07:00:49,166 --> 07:00:53,668
The minimum fell to 4 DPUs and billing moved to 1 minute granularity.

4277
07:00:54,446 --> 07:00:59,716
That makes them viable for short bursts rather than only for permanent heavy workloads.

4278
07:01:00,430 --> 07:01:03,812
The important structural point is on the right hand side.

4279
07:01:04,288 --> 07:01:09,444
With a reservation, you pay for compute and there are no data scan charges at all.

4280
07:01:09,934 --> 07:01:17,124
So the entire byte scanned optimization story stops being a cost argument and becomes purely a performance.

4281
07:01:17,488 --> 07:01:17,892
1.

4282
07:01:19,246 --> 07:01:25,060
Reservations also give guaranteed concurrency, which is the other reason questions reach for them.

4283
07:01:26,862 --> 07:01:33,568
A retailer's analysts query a 200TB clickstream dataset with Amazon Athena.

4284
07:01:33,934 --> 07:01:39,780
The data is park it, partitioned by date, and queries filter on a date range.

4285
07:01:40,302 --> 07:01:43,972
Monthly Athena spend has reached a level finance has flagged.

4286
07:01:44,462 --> 07:01:47,972
Investigation shows that most queries are written as select start,

4287
07:01:48,750 --> 07:01:55,812
that four dashboards each rerun the same three queries every 15 minutes against data that only changes once a day.

4288
07:01:56,462 --> 07:01:58,820
Which combination will reduce the bill most?

4289
07:02:00,494 --> 07:02:00,768
A.

4290
07:02:01,422 --> 07:02:05,892
Increase the workgroups per query data scan limit so queries stop being cancelled.

4291
07:02:17,806 --> 07:02:23,568
Select only the required columns and enable query result reuse for the dashboard queries.

4292
07:02:23,888 --> 07:02:24,128
C.

4293
07:02:24,608 --> 07:02:25,408
Repartition

4294
07:02:26,062 --> 07:02:28,260
The data by hour to improve pruning.

4295
07:02:28,688 --> 07:02:29,008
D.

4296
07:02:29,454 --> 07:02:31,728
Move the dataset to Amazon S.

4297
07:02:32,654 --> 07:02:33,008
3.

4298
07:02:33,968 --> 07:02:35,648
Standard infrequent access.

4299
07:02:36,334 --> 07:02:37,380
Pause and decide.

4300
07:02:44,128 --> 07:02:49,220
The answer is B and the question names both causes explicitly.

4301
07:02:50,126 --> 07:02:57,412
Select star against a columnar format reads every column, throwing away the main reason to use parquet at all.

4302
07:02:58,158 --> 07:03:09,008
And four dashboards running 12 identical queries, an hour against data that changes daily means the overwhelming majority of those runs could return a cached result.

4303
07:03:09,646 --> 07:03:15,492
Option D is the trap that has now appeared twice in this course, in module 8 as well.

4304
07:03:16,334 --> 07:03:22,660
That is because storage class and byte scanned are unrelated and the words cost and s3 attract each other.

4305
07:03:34,126 --> 07:03:40,900
That does not mean you write SQL in the exam because there is no editor and no syntax question anywhere in it.

4306
07:03:41,678 --> 07:03:47,620
It means a question can describe a requirement in business terms and expect you to recognize which construct answers it.

4307
07:03:48,048 --> 07:03:51,488
Or it can show you a query and ask why it is low or expensive.

4308
07:03:51,822 --> 07:03:53,092
Both shapes appear.

4309
07:03:53,646 --> 07:04:04,068
The next few slides cover the constructs named in the guide, the reasoning that turns them into marks, the specific mistakes that make a correct query expensive.

4310
07:04:05,774 --> 07:04:09,540
The window function family is the one worth genuinely understanding.

4311
07:04:09,984 --> 07:04:15,940
That is because three of these six rows use it, and it is the construct people find least intuitive.

4312
07:04:16,558 --> 07:04:20,964
A group by collapses many rows into one per group, losing the detail.

4313
07:04:21,966 --> 07:04:27,172
A window function computes across a set of related rows while keeping every row.

4314
07:04:27,744 --> 07:04:32,452
That is what lets you show each day's sales alongside a 7-day rolling average of them.

4315
07:04:33,422 --> 07:04:39,940
Once you see that distinction, the phrase rolling average in a question maps immediately to a window function.

4316
07:04:40,784 --> 07:04:46,276
An option proposing group by for it can be eliminated without further thought.

4317
07:04:47,662 --> 07:04:54,948
This is the most useful SQL distinction for the exam because it maps requirement wording straight onto a construct.

4318
07:04:55,918 --> 07:05:04,864
Group by answers, how much did each region sell, producing one row per region and discarding the individual transactions.

4319
07:05:05,424 --> 07:05:13,604
A window function answers show each transaction alongside its regions running total, keeping every transaction and adding a column.

4320
07:05:14,702 --> 07:05:18,020
The vocabulary in the call out is what gives it away.

4321
07:05:18,894 --> 07:05:27,104
Alongside running, rolling, and rank within a group all require the detail rows to survive, which group by

4322
07:05:27,504 --> 07:05:28,036
cannot do.

4323
07:05:28,398 --> 07:05:33,412
Recognizing that in a sentence eliminates the wrong options before you think about anything else.

4324
07:05:35,054 --> 07:05:39,844
This is worth knowing because nested data is normal in anything that arrived as JSON.

4325
07:05:40,430 --> 07:05:44,068
Candidates assume it must be flattened before it can be queried.

4326
07:05:44,544 --> 07:05:45,540
It does not.

4327
07:05:46,318 --> 07:05:53,892
Athena reads structs and arrays natively, and UNNEST expands an array so that each element becomes a row you can group and count.

4328
07:05:54,702 --> 07:05:59,364
That means an exploratory question about nested events needs no pipeline at all.

4329
07:05:59,744 --> 07:06:07,556
The alternative from module 2 is relationalize, which flattens nested structures into related tables during a blue job.

4330
07:06:08,046 --> 07:06:15,428
That is the better answer when the same nested data is queried constantly and the flattening should be paid for once.

4331
07:06:17,006 --> 07:06:21,184
The approximate functions point is the one most likely to appear.

4332
07:06:21,422 --> 07:06:26,224
Because it involves a genuine trade, the exam can ask you to make deliberately.

4333
07:06:26,544 --> 07:06:35,044
Counting distinct values exactly requires tracking every value the engine has seen, which for billions of rows is memory intensive and slow.

4334
07:06:35,918 --> 07:06:42,468
Approximate distinct uses a sketch that answers within a small error margin at a fraction of the cost and memory.

4335
07:06:44,398 --> 07:06:52,644
For a dashboard reporting, roughly how many unique visitors there were yesterday, that is entirely adequate and nobody would notice the difference.

4336
07:06:53,582 --> 07:07:02,052
For a financial reconciliation that must balance to the penny it is not, and the question will always tell you which situation you are in.

4337
07:07:03,424 --> 07:07:06,980
The distinction between the first two bullets matters and gets missed.

4338
07:07:07,264 --> 07:07:17,380
Every Athena query already writes a result file, and that file is CSV, unpartitioned, named after the query execution rather than after anything meaningful.

4339
07:07:18,030 --> 07:07:25,540
Unload gives you control over that. Writing parquet or another columnar format in parallel across workers into a location you choose.

4340
07:07:25,966 --> 07:07:33,252
That turns a query result into a proper dataset, the rest of your platform can read which is the difference between an answer and an artifact.

4341
07:07:33,774 --> 07:07:41,316
The asynchronous API on the fourth bullet is worth knowing because it is how any application integrates with Athena.

4342
07:07:41,824 --> 07:07:45,284
Module 5 covered the equivalent LEDshift data.

4343
07:07:45,902 --> 07:07:46,916
API B.

4344
07:07:48,718 --> 07:07:52,612
The distinction in the call out decides questions and it is very easy to blur.

4345
07:07:53,104 --> 07:07:55,620
A plain view is a saved query definition.

4346
07:07:55,824 --> 07:08:01,220
So wrapping an expensive aggregation in one makes it easier to write and exactly as expensive to run.

4347
07:08:02,064 --> 07:08:06,436
Every consumer pays the full cost of that underlying query every single time.

4348
07:08:07,406 --> 07:08:13,732
A materialized view computes the result once and stores it. So consumers read a small pre-computed table instead.

4349
07:08:14,144 --> 07:08:18,308
That is the compute once, read many principle appearing yet again in this module.

4350
07:08:18,544 --> 07:08:23,044
It means an option offering a view in response to a cost complaint is usually wrong.

4351
07:08:23,790 --> 07:08:29,220
Meanwhile, one offering a materialized view for the same complaint is usually right.

4352
07:08:30,894 --> 07:08:39,024
The fourth bullet is module lines hidden partitioning argument seen from the query side, and it is the most expensive

4353
07:08:39,264 --> 07:08:41,284
silent mistake in this module.

4354
07:08:42,158 --> 07:08:51,524
A table partition on a date string filtered with a function applied to a timestamp column returns entirely correct results and prunes absolutely nothing.

4355
07:08:52,064 --> 07:08:57,348
The engine cannot connect the function's output to the partition key, so it reads every partition.

4356
07:08:58,350 --> 07:09:02,116
The query works, the numbers are right, and the bill is enormous.

4357
07:09:02,862 --> 07:09:06,372
That is why IZBL's hidden partitioning earned a slide of its own.

4358
07:09:07,054 --> 07:09:12,292
In a plain hive table, the answer is simply to write the filter against the partition column directly.

4359
07:09:13,582 --> 07:09:20,100
This slide exists because domain 3 questions frequently offer Athena and Redshift as alternatives for the same query.

4360
07:09:20,384 --> 07:09:23,332
The separator has not changed since module 1.

4361
07:09:26,894 --> 07:09:33,344
Athena charges per query with nothing standing between queries, which suits work that happens sometimes.

4362
07:09:33,902 --> 07:09:40,144
Redshift charges for capacity you keep whether you use it or not, which suits work that happens constantly.

4363
07:09:40,654 --> 07:09:47,396
A dashboard refreshed every minute by 400 people is a redshift workload even though Athena could technically answer it.

4364
07:09:48,238 --> 07:09:52,516
A monthly regulatory extract is an Athena workload, even though Redshift could.

4365
07:09:52,944 --> 07:09:59,108
If a question tells you how many users there are and how often they query, that sentence exists specifically to decide this.

4366
07:10:00,526 --> 07:10:06,500
Federated query is genuinely useful, and the two warnings in the call out decide most questions about it.

4367
07:10:06,784 --> 07:10:13,412
The connector runs as a lambda function, so a federated query incurs lambda charges on top of Athena zone.

4368
07:10:14,062 --> 07:10:17,636
A poorly performing connector becomes the bottleneck for the whole query.

4369
07:10:18,544 --> 07:10:25,764
More importantly, the query leaches into a live system, which modules 5 and 10 both flagged in their own contexts.

4370
07:10:27,886 --> 07:10:34,852
Joining a small reference table from Amazon RDS to a lake query occasionally is a perfectly good use of it.

4371
07:10:35,726 --> 07:10:44,644
Running a heavy analytical scan against a production database that also serves a customer-facing application is how you slow down whatever that application does.

4372
07:10:46,094 --> 07:10:52,772
An analyst needs a report showing for each product daily sales alongside a 7-day rolling average of those sales.

4373
07:10:53,550 --> 07:10:58,148
It must also show the rank of each product within its category by monthly revenue.

4374
07:10:59,086 --> 07:11:02,436
The data is three years of transactions in Amazon Redshift.

4375
07:11:03,310 --> 07:11:12,100
A colleague has proposed exporting the data to Amazon S3, computing the rolling average and rankings in an AWS Blue job with Python.

4376
07:11:13,102 --> 07:11:15,428
Loading the results back into Redshift.

4377
07:11:16,782 --> 07:11:19,108
What should the data engineer recommend?

4378
07:11:21,550 --> 07:11:26,180
Build a blue job as proposed since window calculations are easier in Python.

4379
07:11:26,704 --> 07:11:31,204
B Compute both with SQL window functions directly in Redshift.

4380
07:11:31,824 --> 07:11:38,724
C Export to Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena, which handles window functions better than Redshift.

4381
07:11:40,270 --> 07:11:40,544
D

4382
07:11:41,806 --> 07:11:46,436
Load the data into Amazon quick and use calculated fields.

4383
07:11:47,374 --> 07:11:49,024
Pause and decide.

4384
07:11:55,885 --> 07:12:02,725
The answer is B and the reasoning is Module 5's ELT argument applied to a specific SQL requirement.

4385
07:12:03,824 --> 07:12:13,424
Data already sitting in Redshift should be transformed in Redshift, and both requirements here map directly onto window functions. The engine supports natively.

4386
07:12:14,030 --> 07:12:16,132
Option A is the one to recognize.

4387
07:12:23,824 --> 07:12:28,644
The fact that a colleague proposed it is the question telling you it is plausible and wrong.

4388
07:12:29,966 --> 07:12:35,236
Skill 3.2.4 names using Apache Spark within Amazon Athena.

4389
07:12:35,744 --> 07:12:40,484
That surprises people who think of Athena purely as an SQL service.

4390
07:12:41,262 --> 07:12:49,700
Athena for Apache Spark provides notebooks running Spark against the same data and the same Drew Data catalog without provisioning a cluster at all.

4391
07:12:50,574 --> 07:12:52,452
That fills a real gap in the story.

4392
07:12:52,784 --> 07:13:01,828
Some work is genuinely awkward in SQL, particularly iterative exploration, complex reshaping of nested structures, or anything that needs a library.

4393
07:13:02,224 --> 07:13:05,764
The exam point is simply that this exists as a serverless option.

4394
07:13:06,606 --> 07:13:13,412
So, question about ad hoc Spark analysis does not automatically mean spinning up an Amazon EMR cluster.

4395
07:13:15,054 --> 07:13:19,972
The callout repeats a warning from module 5 because it applies identically here.

4396
07:13:20,400 --> 07:13:29,508
A notebook session builds for the time it is open rather than for the queries you run. So a forgotten notebook accrues charges overnight and through the weekend.

4397
07:13:30,190 --> 07:13:35,780
That is the practical difference from Athena SQL where nothing is built between queries.

4398
07:13:36,206 --> 07:13:39,972
The choice between them follows the language rather than the data.

4399
07:13:40,590 --> 07:13:46,080
If the work is expressible as a query, SQL is simpler and cheaper.

4400
07:13:46,606 --> 07:13:53,252
If it needs a loop, a library or a shape, SQL handles awkwardly, the notebook is there and needs no cluster.

4401
07:13:54,862 --> 07:14:01,124
This is the most confusing naming situation on the whole exam, so it is worth getting straight before anything else.

4402
07:14:03,534 --> 07:14:09,092
On the 9th of October 2025, Amazon Quick site was renamed Amazon Quick Suite.

4403
07:14:09,774 --> 07:14:16,004
That was part of expanding it into a broader agentic workspace rather than a pure business intelligence tool.

4404
07:14:16,782 --> 07:14:20,772
Within months, the name was shortened again to simply Amazon Quick.

4405
07:14:21,614 --> 07:14:29,675
The original business intelligence capability still exists inside it as a component AWS calls QuickSight

4406
07:14:29,920 --> 07:14:31,332
with a space in the middle.

4407
07:14:32,206 --> 07:14:39,236
Every existing QuickSight API, SDK, and embedded dashboard continues to work unchanged.

4408
07:14:40,110 --> 07:14:45,220
The next slide sets out exactly what that means when you are answering a question under time pressure.

4409
07:14:46,702 --> 07:14:54,596
The practical guidance is the last bullet and it is worth internalizing because a name you do not recognize is unsettling under time pressure.

4410
07:14:55,200 --> 07:15:01,092
Suppose an option says Amazon QuickSight, and the requirement is a business intelligence dashboard.

4411
07:15:01,934 --> 07:15:06,372
That is the intended answer, regardless of what the service is currently called.

4412
07:15:07,918 --> 07:15:12,548
The exam guides in scope service list has been updated to say Amazon Quick.

4413
07:15:12,960 --> 07:15:19,812
Meanwhile, questions written before October 2025 will still say QuickSight, and both will circulate for years.

4414
07:15:20,622 --> 07:15:25,956
Do not talk yourself out of a correct answer because the name looks dated.

4415
07:15:26,560 --> 07:15:29,604
And note one related change while we are here.

4416
07:15:30,830 --> 07:15:36,036
Amazon Q Business closed to new customers on the 31st of July 2026.

4417
07:15:37,710 --> 07:15:44,804
SPICE is the piece that appears in questions because it decides where the load falls.

4418
07:15:46,320 --> 07:15:52,644
With SPICE, data is imported into an in-memory cache and refreshed on a schedule.

4419
07:15:53,294 --> 07:16:01,316
So a thousand people opening a dashboard produce no queries against Redshift at all and the numbers are as fresh as the last refresh.

4420
07:16:01,600 --> 07:16:09,540
Querying directly instead means every dashboard view runs a query against the underlying source, which is always current and puts real load on it.

4421
07:16:10,254 --> 07:16:15,396
So a question about a dashboard overwhelming a warehouse points at SPICE.

4422
07:16:16,014 --> 07:16:19,492
A question requiring up to the second figures points away from it.

4423
07:16:21,166 --> 07:16:27,844
It is worth knowing what changed so you are not surprised by an option describing capabilities QuickSight never had.

4424
07:16:28,942 --> 07:16:36,420
The rebrand bundled the dashboarding tool into a broader workspace with agents that answer questions, run flows, and index documents.

4425
07:16:37,134 --> 07:16:39,428
For this exam, that is mostly context.

4426
07:16:40,398 --> 07:16:44,932
The skills named in the guide are about visualizing data and preparing it.

4427
07:16:45,454 --> 07:16:52,196
That is the QuickSight component and that components engine and its SPICE cache are unchanged.

4428
07:16:53,070 --> 07:16:59,396
So a question about dashboards, SPICE or embedding is answerable exactly as it always was.

4429
07:17:00,270 --> 07:17:04,164
That holds whichever of the three names the option happens to use.

4430
07:17:05,614 --> 07:17:09,520
A company publishes an executive dashboard built on Amazon Redshift.

4431
07:17:09,760 --> 07:17:15,076
It is opened by about 600 people each weekday morning, mostly within the same half hour.

4432
07:17:16,430 --> 07:17:20,800
The underlying data is refreshed once overnight and does not change during the

4433
07:17:20,800 --> 07:17:28,676
day. During that half hour, the Redshift cluster shows heavy query load and other analytical workloads slow noticeably.

4434
07:17:29,070 --> 07:17:32,836
The dashboard queries themselves are identical for every viewer.

4435
07:17:34,480 --> 07:17:36,676
What is the most appropriate change?

4436
07:17:38,862 --> 07:17:43,812
Import the dashboard dataset into SPI CE and refresh it after the overnight load.

4437
07:17:44,160 --> 07:17:50,080
b Enable redshift concurrency scaling so the extra queries run on additional clusters.

4438
07:17:50,400 --> 07:17:53,860
C. Resize the Redshift cluster to handle the morning peak.

4439
07:17:54,320 --> 07:17:54,640
D

4440
07:17:55,470 --> 07:18:00,320
Move the dashboard to query Amazon Athena over the same data in Amazon S3.

4441
07:18:00,640 --> 07:18:01,700
Pause and decide.

4442
07:18:08,014 --> 07:18:12,064
The answer is A, and option B is the well-constructed distractor.

4443
07:18:12,384 --> 07:18:18,020
Concurrency scaling is exactly right for a burst of queries that need running, and module 10 said so.

4444
07:18:18,798 --> 07:18:25,796
Here the queries do not need running because 600 people are asking the same question of data that has not changed since the overnight load.

4445
07:18:26,224 --> 07:18:29,764
SPICE answers all of them from one cached copy.

4446
07:18:30,734 --> 07:18:36,132
This is the same principle as QD result. Reuse in scenario 1, which is worth noticing.

4447
07:18:37,102 --> 07:18:45,028
When work is redundant, removing it beats absorbing it and options that add capacity are answering a different question.

4448
07:18:46,446 --> 07:18:50,944
Module 2 introduced Databrew, and this is where its analytical side belongs.

4449
07:18:51,184 --> 07:18:57,284
The profiling capability on the third bullet is the one worth knowing because it answers a specific question shape.

4450
07:18:57,664 --> 07:19:03,172
Before transforming an unfamiliar dataset, a profile job reports what is actually in each column.

4451
07:19:04,224 --> 07:19:09,184
How many values are missing, what the distribution looks like, and where the outliers sit.

4452
07:19:09,744 --> 07:19:16,260
That is discovery work rather than transformation, and module 15 returns to it as a data quality topic.

4453
07:19:16,624 --> 07:19:19,716
The framing on the last bullet is what decides questions.

4454
07:19:20,558 --> 07:19:27,748
Watch for a scenario describing a business analyst who cannot write code, needing to clean or explore data.

4455
07:19:28,064 --> 07:19:31,972
These tools are the intended answer over a glue job every time.

4456
07:19:33,454 --> 07:19:40,704
The fourth bullet is why option D in the second scenario was wrong, and the principle generalizes well beyond dashboards.

4457
07:19:40,944 --> 07:19:44,868
Work done in the visualization layer happens once per viewer per view.

4458
07:19:45,262 --> 07:19:50,724
So a calculation taking 2 seconds for one person takes 2 seconds 600 times over.

4459
07:19:51,662 --> 07:20:00,420
Work done once in the warehouse in a materialized view or a scheduled aggregation is paid for once and read cheaply by everyone thereafter.

4460
07:20:00,846 --> 07:20:09,984
That single principle connects materialized views from module 10, SPICE from this module and query result reuse from earlier in it.

4461
07:20:10,544 --> 07:20:17,412
Three implementations of the same idea at three different layers of the stack and questions offer all three.

4462
07:20:19,022 --> 07:20:26,464
The third bullet is a real failure that module 6's philosophy predicts. Picture a dashboard set to refresh at 7.

4463
07:20:26,704 --> 07:20:30,224
Importing from a table, the pipeline usually finishes by 6.30.

4464
07:20:30,606 --> 07:20:36,064
One day it imports halfway through a slow load and shows figures that are quietly wrong.

4465
07:20:36,366 --> 07:20:37,316
Nothing fails.

4466
07:20:38,222 --> 07:20:44,996
The fix is the first bullet which is to trigger the refresh from the pipeline's completion rather than from a clock.

4467
07:20:45,424 --> 07:20:49,252
That is the same dependency vs timing argument module.

4468
07:20:49,838 --> 07:20:59,108
6 is first scenario turned on. The alerting bullets matter because they move the dashboard from something people look at to something that tells them.

4469
07:21:00,526 --> 07:21:07,524
The licensing split matters more than it first appears because it turns a distribution requirement into a cost calculation.

4470
07:21:08,046 --> 07:21:10,180
The exam can ask about directly.

4471
07:21:11,630 --> 07:21:14,212
Readers cost a fraction of what authors cost.

4472
07:21:14,798 --> 07:21:18,944
And in most organizations, the overwhelming majority of people only ever led.

4473
07:21:20,110 --> 07:21:28,036
Anonymous embedding is the one worth recognizing by name because it answers a specific requirement that nothing else on this slide does.

4474
07:21:28,526 --> 07:21:34,692
Serving a dashboard to customers or to the public needs a capacity based model rather than per user licensing.

4475
07:21:35,342 --> 07:21:37,668
Those viewers have no accounts and never will.

4476
07:21:38,864 --> 07:21:45,828
So, a question describing external, unauthenticated, or customer facing viewers is pointing straight at that option.

4477
07:21:47,438 --> 07:21:52,100
Reading this table from top to bottom shows the trade-off in a single moment.

4478
07:21:52,430 --> 07:22:00,548
At the top, every request computes against live data, which is maximally fresh and maximally expensive because nothing is reused.

4479
07:22:01,198 --> 07:22:08,448
At the bottom, the work is repeated for every individual viewer, which is the worst of both worlds, being neither fresh nor cheap.

4480
07:22:08,688 --> 07:22:14,468
The two middle rows are where most correct answers sit and they differ in where the cache actually lives.

4481
07:22:15,374 --> 07:22:23,076
A materialized view caches inside the warehouse, so any consumer benefits from it, including other queries and other tools.

4482
07:22:24,494 --> 07:22:28,996
SPI CE caches inside the business intelligence service.

4483
07:22:29,648 --> 07:22:34,564
So the warehouse is not touched at all, which is exactly what scenario 3 turned on.

4484
07:22:36,238 --> 07:22:44,452
The fourth bullet is the consequence of scenario 3's answer, and it is stated here so the recommendation is honest rather than convenient.

4485
07:22:45,422 --> 07:22:48,048
Importing a dataset into SPICE

4486
07:22:49,134 --> 07:22:52,356
creates a copy inside the business intelligence service.

4487
07:22:53,294 --> 07:22:59,236
Permissions enforced by LakeFormation on the source table do not automatically govern that copy.

4488
07:22:59,566 --> 07:23:04,388
Row and column level security has to be defined on the imported dataset itself.

4489
07:23:05,038 --> 07:23:08,676
That is a genuine design consideration rather than an exam trick.

4490
07:23:09,518 --> 07:23:14,692
It is the kind of detail that turns a question about dashboard performance into a domain.

4491
07:23:15,335 --> 07:23:17,060
4 questions about who can see what.

4492
07:23:17,390 --> 07:23:22,276
Module 19 covers the underlying masking and encryption mechanisms properly.

4493
07:23:23,488 --> 07:23:29,988
A data science team needs to explore a new 100GB dataset in Amazon S3.

4494
07:23:30,542 --> 07:23:41,092
The work is iterative and involves reshaping nested structures, testing several statistical approaches, and using a Python library that has no SQL equivalent.

4495
07:23:41,998 --> 07:23:46,768
The team runs this kind of exploration perhaps twice a month for a day at a time.

4496
07:23:47,054 --> 07:23:51,844
They have no existing Amazon EMR clusters and no capacity to operate one.

4497
07:23:52,142 --> 07:23:54,208
What should the data engineer recommend?

4498
07:23:54,542 --> 07:24:00,836
a provision A long-running Amazon EMR cluster with EMR Studio Notebooks.

4499
07:24:01,328 --> 07:24:07,492
B Use Athena for Apache Spark Notebooks, shutting the session when work finishes.

4500
07:24:07,918 --> 07:24:08,128
C.

4501
07:24:08,878 --> 07:24:12,356
Write the analysis as a series of Athena SQL queries.

4502
07:24:12,688 --> 07:24:13,008
D.

4503
07:24:13,454 --> 07:24:17,540
Load the dataset into Amazon Redshift and analyze it with SQL.

4504
07:24:17,968 --> 07:24:19,140
Pause and decide.

4505
07:24:25,774 --> 07:24:26,884
The answer is B.

4506
07:24:27,310 --> 07:24:37,892
One clause eliminates half the options, which is the reading skill this course keeps returning to. A Python library with no SQL equivalent means the work cannot be expressed as a query.

4507
07:24:38,574 --> 07:24:42,564
So options C and D are gone regardless of their other merits.

4508
07:24:43,470 --> 07:24:53,380
Between A and B, the deciding phrase is that the team has no capacity to operate a cluster that sits alongside a usage pattern of two days a month.

4509
07:24:54,126 --> 07:24:59,332
Option A would work and would run continuously for the 28 days nobody is using it.

4510
07:25:00,288 --> 07:25:06,500
That is the transient cluster argument from module 5 stated as a serverless one instead.

4511
07:25:08,014 --> 07:25:13,488
The third row is the one that catches people and module 14 returns to it in detail.

4512
07:25:13,728 --> 07:25:19,556
Operational logs, error rates, and latency percentiles belong in open search dashboards.

4513
07:25:20,238 --> 07:25:27,876
That is because that is where the logs are indexed, and because the query patterns are fundamentally different from business intelligence.

4514
07:25:28,768 --> 07:25:35,328
Business metrics, revenue by region and the like belong in the business intelligence tool.

4515
07:25:35,950 --> 07:25:46,660
So, question describing engineers investigating an incident is pointing at open search, and one describing executives reviewing quarterly performance is pointing at QIC.

4516
07:25:47,566 --> 07:25:57,316
Manage Grafana is worth recognizing by name because it is on the in-scope service list and it appears specifically for infrastructure and operational metrics.

4517
07:25:58,830 --> 07:26:05,860
The fourth row deserves the attention because it produces no error and no wrong answer, only a bill, nobody expected.

4518
07:26:06,350 --> 07:26:12,324
Consider a query filtering on a function of a column where the table is partitioned on that column's raw value.

4519
07:26:12,768 --> 07:26:15,652
It is logically correct and prunes nothing at all.

4520
07:26:16,448 --> 07:26:24,132
Every partition is read, the results are entirely right, and nobody notices until Finance asks a question three months later.

4521
07:26:25,230 --> 07:26:31,236
Module 9's hidden partitioning solves this structurally in Iceberg by recording the transform.

4522
07:26:32,110 --> 07:26:37,636
In a plain hive table, the fix is simply to write the filter against the partition column directly.

4523
07:26:37,888 --> 07:26:42,500
This is now the second module in which that distinction has decided an answer.

4524
07:26:43,790 --> 07:26:49,412
5 questions and the second is the one that recurs across this module in three separate forms.

4525
07:26:49,888 --> 07:26:56,804
Query result, reuse, SPICE, and materialized views are all the same underlying insight.

4526
07:26:57,728 --> 07:27:06,436
That is that repeating identical work against unchanged data is waste and removing that waste beats scaling to accommodate it.

4527
07:27:06,926 --> 07:27:15,684
Options offering concurrency scaling or a bigger cluster in those situations are answering a demand question when the real question is about redundancy.

4528
07:27:16,526 --> 07:27:19,396
The first is the fastest elimination in the module.

4529
07:27:19,728 --> 07:27:28,836
That is because once you know Athena builds bytes scanned, every option about storage class, timeouts, or scan limits falls away immediately.

4530
07:27:29,678 --> 07:27:35,648
Next module is monitoring and troubleshooting, which carries 12 skills and is the largest in domain.

4531
07:27:37,870 --> 07:27:45,668
This module carries 12 skills from the exam guide more than any other in the course. Four of those come from domain 4 rather than domain 3.

4532
07:27:46,030 --> 07:27:51,684
That is because logging and log analysis appear in both, which tells you how much weight AWS puts on them.

4533
07:27:52,398 --> 07:27:57,892
The subject matter is the operational half of everything this course has built so far.

4534
07:27:58,862 --> 07:28:00,900
How you know a pipeline is healthy?

4535
07:28:01,486 --> 07:28:06,916
How you find out why it is not, and how you prove to an auditor what actually happened.

4536
07:28:07,886 --> 07:28:09,732
Four worked scenarios along the way.

4537
07:28:10,702 --> 07:28:19,844
One service that has changed substantially since almost all study material was written, to the point where several older exam answers are now unnecessary.

4538
07:28:21,550 --> 07:28:25,328
The distinction in the call out answers a class of question quickly.

4539
07:28:25,568 --> 07:28:34,980
A view job, a lambda function, or a step function's execution writes to CloudWatch logs because AWS runs it and configures that.

4540
07:28:35,408 --> 07:28:39,524
An application on an EC2 instance writes to a file on that instance.

4541
07:28:40,398 --> 07:28:44,900
Nothing reaches CloudWatch until an agent is installed and configured to ship it.

4542
07:28:45,328 --> 07:28:51,108
So a scenario describing logs missing from a self-managed component usually has an agent answer.

4543
07:28:52,208 --> 07:28:57,572
Vended logs are the third category where a service produces logs about itself.

4544
07:28:58,382 --> 07:29:05,124
The choice of destination between CloudWatch, S3, and Firehose is often what a question turns on.

4545
07:29:06,766 --> 07:29:15,888
Vended logs are produced by AWS about its own services, and the recurring exam decision is where to send them rather than whether to collect them.

4546
07:29:16,368 --> 07:29:19,968
The pattern matches the second worked scenario in this module.

4547
07:29:20,494 --> 07:29:28,964
CloudWatch logs suits things you investigate interactively soon after they happen because logs insights is there and the data is queryable immediately.

4548
07:29:29,806 --> 07:29:33,488
Amazon S3 suits things you keep for years and read rarely.

4549
07:29:34,094 --> 07:29:39,556
That is because storage is priced for that and Athena can query it when an auditor asks.

4550
07:29:40,110 --> 07:29:46,852
Firehose appears as a third option when logs need transforming or routing onward, which module 3 covered.

4551
07:29:48,528 --> 07:29:53,028
Sorting the tools by the question they answer makes this whole area tractable.

4552
07:29:53,408 --> 07:29:58,276
CloudWatch metrics and alarms tell you what is happening right now and whether it is normal.

4553
07:29:58,735 --> 07:30:05,604
CloudTrail records who called which API, when and from where, which is an audit and forensics question.

4554
07:30:06,382 --> 07:30:13,540
CloudWatch logs holds what your applications and services actually wrote, and the tooling around it is how you search that.

4555
07:30:13,968 --> 07:30:20,228
Most exam questions in this area are really asking which of those three questions is being posed.

4556
07:30:21,102 --> 07:30:29,540
A surprising number of wrong answers come from reaching for Cloud Trail when the question is about application behavior or the reverse.

4557
07:30:31,214 --> 07:30:38,116
The call out is the single most useful distinction here, and it decided the last scenario of module 12.

4558
07:30:40,014 --> 07:30:46,400
CloudRail records that somebody called start job run at a particular time with particular parameters.

4559
07:30:46,640 --> 07:30:52,004
It does not record that the job read a column, derived a value and wrote it somewhere.

4560
07:30:52,878 --> 07:30:55,920
That is because that is not an API call.

4561
07:30:56,366 --> 07:30:58,468
It is the job's internal behavior.

4562
07:30:59,120 --> 07:31:02,404
So questions about who did something are cloud trained.

4563
07:31:02,720 --> 07:31:06,756
And questions about what a pipeline actually did are logs and lineage.

4564
07:31:07,694 --> 07:31:15,760
AWS config on the fifth row is worth recognizing because it answers a third question again, which is whether a resources

4565
07:31:15,920 --> 07:31:17,572
configuration matches the rule.

4566
07:31:19,246 --> 07:31:25,600
The call-out describes a genuine operational problem that anomaly detection exists to solve.

4567
07:31:25,920 --> 07:31:34,000
A pipeline processing 10 million records on weekdays and 2 million at weekends has no single sensible threshold available.

4568
07:31:34,320 --> 07:31:37,636
Set it low and every weekend produces an alert.

4569
07:31:37,870 --> 07:31:38,948
Nobody acts on.

4570
07:31:39,920 --> 07:31:44,452
Set it high and a real weekday failure passes completely unnoticed.

4571
07:31:44,880 --> 07:31:49,540
Anomaly detection learns the pattern from history, including that weekly cycle.

4572
07:31:50,190 --> 07:31:55,556
Alerts when a value falls outside the expected band for that particular time of that particular day.

4573
07:31:56,462 --> 07:32:03,332
Composite alarms solve the adjacent problem, which is that one underlying failure often trips six alarms at once.

4574
07:32:04,110 --> 07:32:08,356
Six simultaneous notifications convey less information than one would.

4575
07:32:10,080 --> 07:32:13,120
This is the mechanism people look for and cannot find.

4576
07:32:13,614 --> 07:32:19,556
CloudWatch alarms evaluate metrics, so there is no way to alarm directly on a log line containing a particular word.

4577
07:32:20,160 --> 07:32:22,308
A metric filter closes that gap,

4578
07:32:23,214 --> 07:32:31,844
it matches a pattern in incoming log events and publishes a metric counting them, and the alarm then watches that metric like any other.

4579
07:32:32,430 --> 07:32:38,180
So a requirement to be notified when an application logs a specific error is a two-step answer.

4580
07:32:38,542 --> 07:32:39,908
Filter then alarm.

4581
07:32:40,814 --> 07:32:46,960
An option proposing to alarm on the log group itself is describing something that does not exist.

4582
07:32:47,566 --> 07:32:52,260
Note the constraint on the last bullet because it interacts with the log class decision.

4583
07:32:53,742 --> 07:33:01,348
Each row here maps to a diagnosis already covered somewhere in this course, which is the whole point of collecting them onto one slide.

4584
07:33:01,920 --> 07:33:03,040
Redshift Q.

4585
07:33:03,758 --> 07:33:07,556
Wait against execution time is module 10's central distinction.

4586
07:33:08,270 --> 07:33:14,468
CloudWatch exposes the two separately, so the split is a matter of fact rather than judgment.

4587
07:33:14,720 --> 07:33:23,920
Throttle requests in DynamoDB alongside consumed capacity sitting well below the provision limit is module 11's hot partition signature

4588
07:33:24,080 --> 07:33:24,580
exactly.

4589
07:33:25,454 --> 07:33:33,600
Iterator age rising in kinesis or on a lambda function with a stream source is module 3's consumer falling behind.

4590
07:33:33,760 --> 07:33:41,956
If you can read this table fluently, you can read the monitoring half of most troubleshooting questions on this exam without any further thought.

4591
07:33:43,566 --> 07:33:47,940
Module 6 raised this and promised the implementation would come here.

4592
07:33:48,622 --> 07:33:55,600
The missing data treatment on the second bullet is the specific mechanism and it is a drop-down most people never open.

4593
07:33:56,206 --> 07:34:03,396
An alarm can be told what to do when a metric reports nothing at all, and the default is to ignore the absence entirely.

4594
07:34:03,918 --> 07:34:06,276
That is exactly wrong for a scheduled job.

4595
07:34:08,206 --> 07:34:12,548
Setting missing data to breaching means an absent metric is treated as a failure.

4596
07:34:13,454 --> 07:34:17,988
So a job that never started raises the alarm rather than passing silently.

4597
07:34:18,574 --> 07:34:22,788
The last bullet is worth repeating because it looks like good practice and is not.

4598
07:34:23,040 --> 07:34:29,828
A workflow that fails at step 2 never reaches the notification step, somebody carefully added at the end of it.

4599
07:34:31,470 --> 07:34:37,028
The exam guide names implementing notifications during monitoring as its own skill.

4600
07:34:37,518 --> 07:34:40,932
So both mechanisms on this slide appear in questions.

4601
07:34:42,286 --> 07:34:45,348
The distinction between them is worth holding clearly.

4602
07:34:46,446 --> 07:34:49,700
An alarm watch is a numeric metric cross a threshold.

4603
07:34:50,638 --> 07:34:57,348
So it answers questions about volume, latency, error rates, and anything else that is a number over time.

4604
07:34:59,406 --> 07:35:02,756
An event bridge rule matches an event that a service emitted.

4605
07:35:03,758 --> 07:35:10,788
So it answers questions about a state change, such as a glue job entering the failed state or an object landing in a bucket.

4606
07:35:11,662 --> 07:35:17,668
Some situations produce an event and no useful metric, and some produce a metric and no event.

4607
07:35:18,190 --> 07:35:21,092
That is usually what decides which one a question wants.

4608
07:35:22,702 --> 07:35:27,236
A nightly AWS glue job loads data into Amazon Redshift.

4609
07:35:27,566 --> 07:35:35,652
An operations team has a CloudWatch alarm on the job's failed run count, notifying an SNS topic. It has worked for a year.

4610
07:35:35,984 --> 07:35:41,380
Last week, the event bridge rule that starts the job was disabled during an unrelated change.

4611
07:35:42,126 --> 07:35:44,356
The job did not run for four nights.

4612
07:35:44,718 --> 07:35:49,060
No alarm fired, and the problem was found when an analyst noticed stale figures.

4613
07:35:49,678 --> 07:35:51,172
What change would have caught this?

4614
07:35:51,694 --> 07:35:56,100
a lower the failure alarms threshold so it fires on a single failure.

4615
07:35:58,990 --> 07:36:05,252
Alarm on the job succeeded run count falling below 1 in 24 hours, treating missing data as breaching.

4616
07:36:05,904 --> 07:36:09,476
C. Add a notification step at the end of the blue workflow.

4617
07:36:10,062 --> 07:36:14,916
d Enable AWS Cloud Train so the disabling of the rule is recorded.

4618
07:36:15,470 --> 07:36:16,708
Pause and decide.

4619
07:36:23,424 --> 07:36:27,140
The answer is B and the structure is worth noticing.

4620
07:36:27,854 --> 07:36:33,828
Three of the four options monitor for failure in different ways, and the scenario opens by telling you that nothing failed.

4621
07:36:34,144 --> 07:36:39,812
That is the exam checking whether you read the situation or pattern matched on the word alarm.

4622
07:36:40,718 --> 07:36:45,828
Option D is genuinely useful and it is a post-mortem tool rather than a detection.

4623
07:36:46,254 --> 07:36:51,140
1. Cloud Trail would tell you afterwards exactly who disabled the rune and when.

4624
07:36:51,534 --> 07:36:56,324
That matters for preventing a recurrence and it would not have shortened those four nights by a minute.

4625
07:36:57,902 --> 07:37:01,924
Many people picture CloudWatch logs as a place logs go.

4626
07:37:02,894 --> 07:37:07,812
Where searching is painful and exporting them somewhere better is the real answer.

4627
07:37:08,942 --> 07:37:11,428
That model is several years out of date.

4628
07:37:12,064 --> 07:37:22,180
Since late 2024, it supports three query languages, including standard SQL which joins across log groups and OpenSearch's piped processing language.

4629
07:37:23,184 --> 07:37:33,188
It has a cost-optimized ingestion class, feed indexing to make queries cheaper as well as faster, live streaming of events as they arrive.

4630
07:37:35,349 --> 07:37:39,044
Scheduled queries that deliver results to S3 or EventBridge.

4631
07:37:40,526 --> 07:37:45,444
Several exam answers that used to require shipping logs to another system now do not.

4632
07:37:46,286 --> 07:37:51,844
The next few slides cover which ones and where exporting is still genuinely the right call.

4633
07:37:53,582 --> 07:37:59,108
The retention default is worth stating plainly because it costs real money and nobody notices.

4634
07:37:59,630 --> 07:38:04,196
A new log group keeps everything forever unless somebody sets a retention period.

4635
07:38:04,464 --> 07:38:10,020
So an account with hundreds of lambda functions accumulates years of logs nobody will ever read.

4636
07:38:10,830 --> 07:38:17,104
Setting retention per log group is the single most effective cloud watch logs cost action.

4637
07:38:17,344 --> 07:38:23,664
The subscription filter on the third bullet is the mechanism behind a lot of architectures in this module.

4638
07:38:24,558 --> 07:38:32,864
That is because it turns a log group into a stream that anything can consume, which is how logs reach OpenSearch or a data lake.

4639
07:38:34,224 --> 07:38:40,996
The infrequent access class is newer than most study material and it gained a lot of capability in March 2026.

4640
07:38:41,344 --> 07:38:45,156
Data protection and the open search query languages were both added to it.

4641
07:38:45,806 --> 07:38:48,004
That matters because the older objection

4642
07:38:48,686 --> 07:38:53,476
that the cheap tier could not be analyzed properly simply no longer holds.

4643
07:38:54,190 --> 07:39:00,836
Storage charges and logs inside charges are identical between the two classes, only the ingestion price differs.

4644
07:39:01,742 --> 07:39:10,916
So the decision reduces to whether you actively monitor these logs with metric filters and live tail or keep them for occasional forensic work.

4645
07:39:12,430 --> 07:39:14,468
The callout is the hard constraint.

4646
07:39:15,278 --> 07:39:20,324
You choose the class when the log group is created and you cannot change your mind afterwards.

4647
07:39:21,614 --> 07:39:25,444
The callout is the change that most affects exam answers in this area.

4648
07:39:25,870 --> 07:39:31,812
Consider correlating an API gateway log with a lambda log to follow one request through the system.

4649
07:39:32,208 --> 07:39:35,140
That used to mean shipping both somewhere that could join them.

4650
07:39:35,470 --> 07:39:37,828
That meant a pipeline and a second system.

4651
07:39:38,670 --> 07:39:46,916
Open search SQL inside logs insights now does it in place with familiar select and join syntax across log groups.

4652
07:39:48,078 --> 07:39:52,164
Field indexes on the third bullet are the performance and cost answer together.

4653
07:39:53,102 --> 07:40:01,168
That is because indexing a request identifier means a query filtering on it scans far less data and logs insights

4654
07:40:01,328 --> 07:40:02,788
bills by data scanned.

4655
07:40:03,566 --> 07:40:10,884
Scheduled queries, which arrived in November 2025, turn a manual investigation into a recurring report delivered somewhere useful.

4656
07:40:12,558 --> 07:40:18,532
This connects domain 3 to domain 4, and it is a real problem rather than a theoretical one.

4657
07:40:21,774 --> 07:40:30,208
Applications log request payloads to help with debugging, and those payloads contain whatever customers typed, including card numbers,

4658
07:40:30,368 --> 07:40:32,548
addresses, and personal details.

4659
07:40:33,070 --> 07:40:36,848
Nothing about the logging pipeline notices or objects.

4660
07:40:37,230 --> 07:40:45,408
That data then sits in a log group with a retention of never expiring, readable by anyone who has CloudWatch access.

4661
07:40:45,648 --> 07:40:49,060
That is usually a much wider group than anyone intended.

4662
07:40:49,774 --> 07:40:56,996
Data protection policies scan incoming events for recognized patterns and mask them before storage.

4663
07:40:58,510 --> 07:41:06,448
It is the same discovery and protection idea as Amazon Macy from module 2 applied to logs instead of to S3

4664
07:41:06,688 --> 07:41:07,204
objects.

4665
07:41:08,878 --> 07:41:14,564
Two skins in the exam guide name this exact list, one in domain 3 and one in domain 4.

4666
07:41:14,958 --> 07:41:17,956
So it is worth learning as a decision rather than a list.

4667
07:41:22,030 --> 07:41:22,404
Logs.

4668
07:41:23,054 --> 07:41:27,972
Insights is for a person investigating something now over logs that are still in CloudWatch.

4669
07:41:28,686 --> 07:41:36,260
Open search is for an ongoing operational practice with dashboards teams keep open and alerting on log content.

4670
07:41:36,688 --> 07:41:44,452
Athena over exported logs in S3 is for the long tail where retention is measured in years and queries are rare.

4671
07:41:44,878 --> 07:41:47,204
That makes storage price the dominant cost.

4672
07:41:48,078 --> 07:41:54,308
And EMR appears specifically for volumes where the work is heavy processing rather than search.

4673
07:41:55,886 --> 07:42:05,316
Cross account observability is worth knowing by name because the multi-account pattern from module 6 creates precisely the problem it solves.

4674
07:42:08,014 --> 07:42:14,048
Pipelines running across several accounts produce metrics and logs in several accounts.

4675
07:42:14,542 --> 07:42:20,164
An operations team should not have to switch rows repeatedly during an incident just to see them.

4676
07:42:21,070 --> 07:42:28,196
A designated monitoring account with cross-account observability enable queries, all of them from one place.

4677
07:42:29,198 --> 07:42:37,488
Manage Grafana appears in questions where dashboards must combine AWS metrics with something else, an on-premises system

4678
07:42:37,648 --> 07:42:39,140
or another cloud provider.

4679
07:42:39,566 --> 07:42:44,228
That is because CloudWatch dashboards display CloudWatch data and nothing else.

4680
07:42:45,166 --> 07:42:51,844
Manage service for Prometheus is narrower again and appears for container workloads emitting Prometheus metrics.

4681
07:42:53,518 --> 07:43:00,548
The embedded metric format on the fourth bullet is worth understanding because it solves a real problem with custom metrics.

4682
07:43:01,070 --> 07:43:04,452
Publishing a metric normally means an API call.

4683
07:43:05,134 --> 07:43:11,908
A lambda function invoked 10,000 times a second, making an extra call each time adds latency and cost.

4684
07:43:12,846 --> 07:43:22,340
Embedded metric format instead writes the metric values inside a structured log event that the function was writing anyway, and CloudWatch extracts them.

4685
07:43:22,928 --> 07:43:25,700
No extra call, no added latency.

4686
07:43:26,478 --> 07:43:28,292
The last bullet is the honest note.

4687
07:43:28,848 --> 07:43:36,196
Because each of these features is built and instrumenting everything produces a monitoring bin, nobody scrutinized.

4688
07:43:37,742 --> 07:43:46,084
A company must retain application logs for 7 years to satisfy a regulator and expects to query them perhaps twice a year during audits.

4689
07:43:46,608 --> 07:43:53,808
Logs currently go to CloudWatch, logs with no retention policy set, and the CloudWatch bill has grown steadily.

4690
07:43:54,350 --> 07:43:59,876
The security team also needs to run occasional forensic searches over the last 30 days interactively.

4691
07:44:00,462 --> 07:44:02,692
Which approach best meets both needs?

4692
07:44:04,720 --> 07:44:04,880
A

4693
07:44:06,606 --> 07:44:11,684
Keep everything in CloudWatch logs indefinitely and query with logs insights.

4694
07:44:13,806 --> 07:44:14,080
B

4695
07:44:15,438 --> 07:44:23,680
Set a 30-day retention on the log groups and export older logs to Amazon S3 with a lifecycle policy to Glacier

4696
07:44:23,920 --> 07:44:25,156
queried with Athena.

4697
07:44:26,320 --> 07:44:26,640
C

4698
07:44:27,854 --> 07:44:32,612
Stream all logs to Amazon Open Search Service and retain 7 years of indices.

4699
07:44:33,518 --> 07:44:33,760
D.

4700
07:44:34,574 --> 07:44:38,596
Reduce retention to 30 days and accept the loss of older logs.

4701
07:44:39,559 --> 07:44:40,644
Pause and decide.

4702
07:44:47,022 --> 07:44:53,520
The answer is B, and it is module 8's lifecycle reasoning applied to a different kind of data.

4703
07:44:54,830 --> 07:45:02,532
Two distinct access patterns are described, one interactive over 30 days and one occasional over 7 years, and they need different storage.

4704
07:45:03,310 --> 07:45:12,036
Option C is worth pausing on because OpenSearch genuinely is the right answer to a lot of log questions, and here it fails badly.

4705
07:45:15,086 --> 07:45:22,948
Keeping 7 years of indices on a search cluster to serve 2 queries a year is the most expensive way to satisfy the requirement.

4706
07:45:23,520 --> 07:45:27,620
It is exactly the kind of technically capable answer.

4707
07:45:28,078 --> 07:45:31,236
The exam builds to catch pattern matching.

4708
07:45:32,910 --> 07:45:36,388
CloudLear records AP i calls made in your account.

4709
07:45:36,880 --> 07:45:37,828
Who made them?

4710
07:45:38,160 --> 07:45:44,132
When, from which network address, with which parameters, and whether they succeeded or were denied.

4711
07:45:44,640 --> 07:45:51,588
That makes it the answer to audit questions, to security investigations, and to anything phrased as who did this.

4712
07:45:52,526 --> 07:45:58,148
It is emphatically not the answer to why a job is slow or what a transformation produced.

4713
07:45:58,560 --> 07:46:01,860
That is because neither of those is an API call.

4714
07:46:02,400 --> 07:46:09,892
That distinction has now come up twice in this course in Module 12's lineage scenario and again in the third scenario here.

4715
07:46:10,734 --> 07:46:16,260
It separates two plausible options often enough to be worth internalizing as a reflex.

4716
07:46:16,814 --> 07:46:18,720
Auditing is Cloud Trail.

4717
07:46:19,758 --> 07:46:22,756
Behavior is logs, metrics, and lineage.

4718
07:46:24,494 --> 07:46:29,348
The second bullet is the detail that catches people and makes a genuinely fair exam question.

4719
07:46:30,190 --> 07:46:37,028
By default, Cloud Trail records that somebody created or deleted a bucket and not that somebody read an object out of it.

4720
07:46:37,934 --> 07:46:45,200
Object level access is a data event. It is switched off by default because the volume would be enormous on a busy account.

4721
07:46:45,486 --> 07:46:48,612
Enabling it costs money per event recorded.

4722
07:46:49,102 --> 07:46:50,052
So a question.

4723
07:46:50,766 --> 07:46:56,740
Asking how to determine who downloaded a particular file needs data events enabled for that bucket.

4724
07:46:57,678 --> 07:47:01,860
An answer relying on default Cloud Trail configuration would find nothing at all.

4725
07:47:02,414 --> 07:47:07,812
Cloud Trail insights on the last bullet is worth knowing by name because it detects unusual.

4726
07:47:08,558 --> 07:47:11,684
API call rates without anyone defining a threshold.

4727
07:47:13,038 --> 07:47:17,028
Both keep a durable record and the difference is who does the work.

4728
07:47:17,280 --> 07:47:21,508
A trail delivering to S3 gives you compressed J, Sun files.

4729
07:47:21,840 --> 07:47:29,924
Turning those into something queryable means defining a table, handling the partitioning, and maintaining it, which module 12 covered.

4730
07:47:30,670 --> 07:47:37,092
Cloud Trail Lake skips all of that, offering SQL over a managed event store with retention up to 10 years.

4731
07:47:37,838 --> 07:47:43,396
So a question emphasizing least operational overhead for an audit requirement points at lake.

4732
07:47:44,302 --> 07:47:52,292
One describing a team already running Athena over a log lake points at the trail because the marginal cost of one more table is small.

4733
07:47:53,582 --> 07:48:03,280
X Way is worth knowing and worth placing correctly because it is offered in questions where it does not fit. It shines where a request passes through several components quickly.

4734
07:48:03,630 --> 07:48:08,484
An API gateway calling a lambda function calling DynamoDB for instance.

4735
07:48:09,294 --> 07:48:15,748
That is because the service map shows the whole path and exactly where the latency accumulated.

4736
07:48:16,462 --> 07:48:19,364
It is much less useful for a long running batch job.

4737
07:48:22,958 --> 07:48:28,772
The equivalent tools there are the Spark UI and the job level metrics from module 2.

4738
07:48:29,520 --> 07:48:35,972
The correlation identifier point on the third bullet is the low technology version and it is genuinely effective.

4739
07:48:36,560 --> 07:48:45,252
Module 7 made this point, and logging one identifier consistently across every component turns 5 separate log searches into a single one.

4740
07:48:46,798 --> 07:48:53,124
After 13 modules of failure modes, the patterns repeat enough to be worth consolidating into one frame.

4741
07:48:53,582 --> 07:48:59,044
There is not enough capacity, so everything is slow evenly and the slowness scales with volume.

4742
07:48:59,360 --> 07:49:04,676
The work is unevenly distributed, so one worker is at full load while the other is idle.

4743
07:49:05,358 --> 07:49:14,240
Something is waiting on a lock, a queue, a rate limit, or a file that has not arrived yet, or nothing failed at all,

4744
07:49:14,510 --> 07:49:16,068
and the output is simply wrong.

4745
07:49:16,622 --> 07:49:19,972
Those four families have almost nothing in common in their fixes.

4746
07:49:20,910 --> 07:49:29,440
The entire diagnostic skill this exam tests is placing a described symptom into the right family before you look at any of the options.

4747
07:49:30,862 --> 07:49:33,700
The next slide gives each family its signature.

4748
07:49:35,374 --> 07:49:40,164
The callout is the most valuable sentence in this module and possibly in the course.

4749
07:49:41,006 --> 07:49:45,540
Across 13 modules, adding capacity has been offered as a distractor repeatedly.

4750
07:49:46,414 --> 07:49:53,156
For data skew, for lock weights, for single file loads, for API throttling, and for outright correctness problems.

4751
07:49:54,080 --> 07:49:57,092
It has been the correct answer for almost none of them.

4752
07:49:57,520 --> 07:50:01,924
Getting the family right takes a few seconds once you know to ask the question.

4753
07:50:02,286 --> 07:50:08,036
It eliminates two or three options immediately before you have weighed any technical detail at all.

4754
07:50:08,942 --> 07:50:13,060
The fourth family is the one this course has flagged most often.

4755
07:50:13,902 --> 07:50:22,372
That is because every option about retries, alerting, and scaling is irrelevant when the job succeeded and produced a wrong number.

4756
07:50:23,662 --> 07:50:31,440
Skills 3.3.6 names Troubleshooting and Maintaining AWS, Glue and Amazon EMR, Pipeline specifically.

4757
07:50:31,600 --> 07:50:33,636
That is why these get their own slides.

4758
07:50:33,998 --> 07:50:40,100
Module 2 introduced continuous logging as a setting to enable, and the last bullet is why it matters operationally.

4759
07:50:40,974 --> 07:50:48,964
Without it, logs are written when the job completes, so a job that hangs and is eventually killed leaves you with nothing to look at.

4760
07:50:49,934 --> 07:50:56,132
The Spark UI is the tool for the different question of why a job that succeeded took 4 times longer than usual.

4761
07:50:56,800 --> 07:51:02,916
That is because it shows which stage consumed the time and whether tasks were balanced.

4762
07:51:04,558 --> 07:51:10,880
The second bullet is the practical trap with transient clusters, which module 5 recommended for cost reasons.

4763
07:51:11,120 --> 07:51:16,868
A cluster that spins up, runs a job, fails, and terminates, has taken its logs with it.

4764
07:51:17,582 --> 07:51:21,540
That happens unless log delivery to S3 was configured in advance.

4765
07:51:22,446 --> 07:51:26,820
That is a decision made at cluster creation, not after the failure.

4766
07:51:27,840 --> 07:51:36,964
MWAAA is named in its own skill, 3.1.2, and the useful detail is that each component logs separately.

4767
07:51:39,150 --> 07:51:44,676
A scheduling problem shows in the scheduler logs while a failing task shows in the task logs.

4768
07:51:45,710 --> 07:51:48,868
Knowing which to look at is most of the answer.

4769
07:51:50,094 --> 07:52:02,628
The fourth bullet reframes a metric that people treat as a simple performance indicator. If consumers are two hours behind and stream retains 24 hours, the situation is uncomfortable and recoverable.

4770
07:52:03,246 --> 07:52:09,380
If they are 22 hours behind on that same retention, records begin disappearing within 2 hours and never come back.

4771
07:52:09,870 --> 07:52:17,508
Same metric, entirely different urgency, and the difference is the gap to the retention window rather than the absolute number.

4772
07:52:17,998 --> 07:52:26,404
That is exactly the sort of reasoning the exam rewards, and it connects module 3's discussion of replayability to this module's discussion of alerting.

4773
07:52:26,894 --> 07:52:32,996
An alarm on iterator age should be set relative to retention, not to an arbitrary number of hours.

4774
07:52:34,702 --> 07:52:40,900
An Amazon EMR transient cluster runs a nightly spark job and terminates on completion.

4775
07:52:41,312 --> 07:52:49,572
Last night the job failed after two hours. The cluster terminated automatically, and the operations team can see only that the step failed.

4776
07:52:50,158 --> 07:52:52,516
There are no application logs available.

4777
07:52:53,454 --> 07:52:57,444
The team needs to diagnose this failure and prevent the same blind spot recurring.

4778
07:52:58,254 --> 07:52:59,748
What should the data engineer do?

4779
07:53:00,654 --> 07:53:05,604
a rerun the job on a long-running cluster and reproduce the failure to read the logs.

4780
07:53:07,886 --> 07:53:14,992
Configure the cluster to archive logs to Amazon S3 and enable the persistent Spark History server.

4781
07:53:15,312 --> 07:53:15,632
C.

4782
07:53:16,206 --> 07:53:19,204
Increase the cluster size so the job does not fail.

4783
07:53:19,712 --> 07:53:20,032
D.

4784
07:53:20,590 --> 07:53:24,228
Enable AWS CloudRail to capture what the job did.

4785
07:53:24,654 --> 07:53:25,700
Pause and decide.

4786
07:53:32,352 --> 07:53:33,872
The answer is B.

4787
07:53:34,352 --> 07:53:41,764
Option C is the reflex this module has been building towards, offering capacity for a failure whose cause is unknown.

4788
07:53:42,272 --> 07:53:48,292
The honest response is that you cannot size a cluster for a problem you have not diagnosed.

4789
07:53:48,592 --> 07:53:52,164
Option D is the Cloud Trail distinction tested head on.

4790
07:53:52,992 --> 07:54:02,659
Cloud Trail would show that a cluster was created and a step submitted with timestamps and the caller's identity and absolutely nothing about the exceptions parked

4791
07:54:02,912 --> 07:54:03,472
through.

4792
07:54:03,886 --> 07:54:08,032
That is the difference between the audit question and the behavior question.

4793
07:54:08,192 --> 07:54:12,388
And it decides the fourth scenario in module 12 2.

4794
07:54:13,742 --> 07:54:20,580
Module 7 recommended emitting row counts as custom metrics and this slide is the payoff for having done it.

4795
07:54:21,006 --> 07:54:28,708
When a report is wrong and a pipeline has six stages, the only real question is which stage lost or corrupted the data.

4796
07:54:29,550 --> 07:54:36,832
Without per stage counts, that means opening six sets of logs and reasoning carefully about each

4797
07:54:36,832 --> 07:54:39,428
one. That is the day-long investigation.

4798
07:54:41,326 --> 07:54:44,548
The fourth scenario in this module describes

4799
07:54:45,390 --> 07:54:51,588
With them, it is one dashboard and the stage where the count changes unexpectedly is the stage to open.

4800
07:54:52,526 --> 07:55:00,388
That single practice converts a whole class of difficult investigation into an obvious one and it costs almost nothing to implement.

4801
07:55:01,870 --> 07:55:04,580
The first bullet is the discipline underneath it.

4802
07:55:05,390 --> 07:55:12,388
Work backwards from the symptom because starting at the source means checking five healthy stages before reaching the broken one.

4803
07:55:14,030 --> 07:55:19,108
This is the bridge into the next module, and it is the fourth failure family made measurable.

4804
07:55:19,470 --> 07:55:27,748
Everything else in this module watches the machinery. Whether executors are busy, whether a queue is growing, whether an AP I returned an error.

4805
07:55:28,526 --> 07:55:38,212
None of it notices that a job ran perfectly and produced numbers that are 2% low, which is the failure module 12's third scenario described.

4806
07:55:39,534 --> 07:55:45,796
Data quality metrics are different in kind because they are computed from the contents rather than from the infrastructure.

4807
07:55:46,734 --> 07:55:50,532
They are the only thing that catches silent wrongness.

4808
07:55:51,438 --> 07:55:55,780
Module 15 covers how to define and enforce them properly.

4809
07:55:57,486 --> 07:56:06,628
Skill 3.3.1 Names extracting logs for audits and the call out is the part engineers consistently underweight.

4810
07:56:09,582 --> 07:56:12,740
An auditor is not only asking whether you have the records,

4811
07:56:13,390 --> 07:56:19,588
they are asking whether those records could have been altered by the very people they describe, which is a different and harder question.

4812
07:56:20,206 --> 07:56:23,968
Write log archives into a bucket with object lock in compliance

4813
07:56:23,968 --> 07:56:31,940
mode. Nobody can then modify or delete them within the retention period, not even an administrator with full permissions.

4814
07:56:32,750 --> 07:56:35,972
That is exactly the guarantee module 8 described.

4815
07:56:36,814 --> 07:56:46,020
Combining that with consistent retention across cloud trail and application logs is what makes an audit trail defensible rather than merely present and hopeful.

4816
07:56:47,648 --> 07:56:53,540
This is the operational discipline that prevents a specific and entirely avoidable class of outage.

4817
07:56:53,888 --> 07:57:03,268
Quotas exist on almost everything. Concurrent Lambda executions, kinesis shards per account, Athena concurrent queries, Glue concurrent job runs.

4818
07:57:03,886 --> 07:57:08,548
A pipeline growing 5% a month will meet one of them on a particular Tuesday.

4819
07:57:09,710 --> 07:57:13,028
The first sign will be throttling during the busiest period of the week.

4820
07:57:14,446 --> 07:57:21,888
Alarming when usage passes, 80% of a quota converts that into a routine increase request raised weeks earlier.

4821
07:57:22,382 --> 07:57:25,252
That is the difference between a ticket and an incident.

4822
07:57:25,806 --> 07:57:31,172
The distinction on the third bullet matters too because some limits are hard and cannot be raised at all.

4823
07:57:32,078 --> 07:57:35,428
For those, the answer is to change the design instead.

4824
07:57:36,718 --> 07:57:39,140
This is the operational blind spot word naming.

4825
07:57:39,470 --> 07:57:43,428
When a pipeline starts failing, the instinct is to examine the pipeline.

4826
07:57:43,808 --> 07:57:49,540
Occasionally, the cause is an AWS service event in that region rather than anything you changed.

4827
07:57:50,318 --> 07:57:51,408
AWS.

4828
07:57:54,574 --> 07:58:01,764
Health reports those events specific to your account and your resources rather than a general status page you have to interpret.

4829
07:58:03,022 --> 07:58:11,684
Routing health events through event bridge into the same notification path as your own alarms means that context arrives at the same time as the symptom.

4830
07:58:12,448 --> 07:58:20,228
The schedule changes point matters separately because it is how you learn about a required upgrade before it becomes urgent.

4831
07:58:21,728 --> 07:58:31,204
The fourth bullet is the judgment, the exam rewards, and it echoes module 6's distinction between transient and permanent failures.

4832
07:58:31,968 --> 07:58:38,500
Consider a job that fails for a known transient reason, such as a source that is occasionally unavailable.

4833
07:58:39,374 --> 07:58:45,028
Automatically restarting it is a sensible remediation that saves somebody a call at 3 in the morning.

4834
07:58:45,888 --> 07:58:53,648
Automatically restarting a job that faints for reasons nobody has diagnosed converts a visible problem into an invisible one.

4835
07:58:54,190 --> 07:58:57,668
The pipeline quietly succeeds on the third attempt every night for a year.

4836
07:58:58,158 --> 07:59:06,052
The last bullet matters because automation that leaves no trace is indistinguishable from a system that never had a problem.

4837
07:59:07,790 --> 07:59:13,348
This does not look like an exam topic and it sits underneath several questions that are.

4838
07:59:14,128 --> 07:59:19,620
Watch for a scenario describing an operations team taking hours to resolve incidents.

4839
07:59:20,718 --> 07:59:25,488
Or the same problem recurring because whoever fixed it last time has left.

4840
07:59:26,030 --> 07:59:30,372
The underlying answer is recorded procedure, not a new service.

4841
07:59:31,278 --> 07:59:34,500
Systems manager documents are the AWS mechanism.

4842
07:59:35,086 --> 07:59:42,244
They hold a procedure a human can follow or that automation can execute directly, which connects to the previous slide.

4843
07:59:43,086 --> 07:59:45,956
The last bullet is worth stating plainly.

4844
07:59:46,862 --> 07:59:53,636
A pipeline only one person can diagnose is fragile in exactly the way an unmonitored pipeline is fragile.

4845
07:59:55,246 --> 08:00:01,732
The callout is the honest summary of this module's first scenario, and it is worth stating as a general principle.

4846
08:00:02,382 --> 08:00:09,924
The failure alarm there was correctly configured, notified a real topic, and had presumably fired usefully in the past.

4847
08:00:10,862 --> 08:00:16,164
It simply could not detect the situation that occurred because that situation produced no failure.

4848
08:00:16,782 --> 08:00:22,084
An alarm being held E tells you nothing about whether it covers the failures you actually face.

4849
08:00:22,830 --> 08:00:26,148
The last two bullets are the routine practice that catches this.

4850
08:00:27,022 --> 08:00:35,332
An alarm which has never fired in two years is either protecting you from something that never happens or watching a metric that does not move.

4851
08:00:36,910 --> 08:00:41,444
The callout describes a mistake that is easy to make and genuinely expensive.

4852
08:00:41,776 --> 08:00:46,724
Custom metrics are built per unique combination of metric name and dimensions.

4853
08:00:47,056 --> 08:00:54,628
So publishing a processing time metric with a customer identifier as a dimension creates one metric per customer.

4854
08:00:55,376 --> 08:01:02,980
With 50,000 customers, that is 50,000 build metrics every month to answer a question nobody actually asked.

4855
08:01:03,950 --> 08:01:07,460
The general principle is that dimensions should be low cardinality.

4856
08:01:08,302 --> 08:01:15,908
Anything high cardinality belongs in a log where you can query it on demand rather than in a metric where you pay to keep it indefinitely.

4857
08:01:16,718 --> 08:01:22,816
The retention default on the second bullet is the other large lever and it is a single setting per log group.

4858
08:01:24,078 --> 08:01:28,804
A data platform team runs 40 glue jobs and 12-step functions workflows.

4859
08:01:29,582 --> 08:01:34,496
When a business report is wrong, diagnosing which stage caused it takes most of a

4860
08:01:34,496 --> 08:01:42,116
day. That is because each investigation means opening logs for several services and correlating timestamps by hand.

4861
08:01:43,136 --> 08:01:49,764
The team wants to reduce that investigation time. Jobs generally succeed, so failure alerting is not the gap.

4862
08:01:50,496 --> 08:01:52,096
What should the team implement?

4863
08:01:52,398 --> 08:01:57,536
a increase the CloudWatch logs retention period so more history is available.

4864
08:01:58,016 --> 08:01:58,336
b

4865
08:01:59,438 --> 08:02:06,788
Emit per stage row counts as custom metrics and log a correlation identifier through every component.

4866
08:02:07,296 --> 08:02:07,536
C.

4867
08:02:08,270 --> 08:02:11,300
Enable AWS X-ray tracing on the blue jobs.

4868
08:02:11,776 --> 08:02:12,096
D.

4869
08:02:12,622 --> 08:02:16,580
Add failure alarms to every blue job and step function state machine.

4870
08:02:17,262 --> 08:02:18,340
Pause and decide.

4871
08:02:25,070 --> 08:02:29,668
The answer is B, and one sentence in the question does most of the eliminating.

4872
08:02:30,254 --> 08:02:37,700
Jobs generally succeed, so failure alerting is not the gap which removes option D outright and undermines A.

4873
08:02:38,446 --> 08:02:43,236
This is the fourth family from earlier in the module where nothing failed and the output is wrong.

4874
08:02:44,238 --> 08:02:48,132
The tools for it are entirely different from the tools for failure.

4875
08:02:49,006 --> 08:02:53,412
The two halves of option B map to the two halves of the difficulty.

4876
08:02:54,446 --> 08:03:04,260
Row counts tell you which stage, and a correlation identifier lets you pull one runs records out of five services without matching timestamps by hand.

4877
08:03:05,998 --> 08:03:13,284
The callout is the pattern running through this table and it is worth carrying into the exam room. Retention defaults to never expiring.

4878
08:03:13,616 --> 08:03:15,936
CloudRail data events default to off.

4879
08:03:16,462 --> 08:03:22,948
Missing data handling on an alarm defaults to ignoring the absence. Continuous logging on a blue job defaults to off.

4880
08:03:23,918 --> 08:03:32,228
None of those defaults is wrong exactly, and each one produces a specific and predictable gap that a question can describe.

4881
08:03:33,774 --> 08:03:42,628
So when a monitoring scenario describes something surprising, checking whether an unhelpful default was simply left in place is a fast route to the answer.

4882
08:03:43,296 --> 08:03:50,756
It is correct more often than any option that introduces a new service, which is what the tempting distractors usually do.

4883
08:03:51,982 --> 08:03:58,756
5 questions, and the third is the one worth practicing hardest because it transfers across every domain in this exam.

4884
08:03:59,438 --> 08:04:04,260
Once a symptom is placed into the correct failure family, most options eliminate themselves.

4885
08:04:04,750 --> 08:04:09,188
Capacity in particular stops looking attractive for three of the four families.

4886
08:04:10,094 --> 08:04:15,012
The first question is the fastest single elimination available in this module.

4887
08:04:15,456 --> 08:04:24,612
That is because Cloud Trail and CloudWatch logs are offered against each other constantly and they answer genuinely different questions about genuinely different things.

4888
08:04:25,582 --> 08:04:34,596
The fifth is the one that feels least like knowledge and pays off most often because unhelpful defaults are behind a surprising share of monitoring gaps.

4889
08:04:37,262 --> 08:04:45,732
Next module is data quality, SKU and Sampling, which takes the fourth failure family and finally gives it the treatment it has been waiting for.

4890
08:04:47,150 --> 08:04:53,316
Module 2 promised that data quality would be covered here, and this is that module.

4891
08:04:54,224 --> 08:05:03,684
It takes the fourth failure family from module 14, the one where nothing fails and the output is wrong, and gives it a proper treatment.

4892
08:05:05,646 --> 08:05:08,356
7 skins from the exam guide land here.

4893
08:05:09,326 --> 08:05:16,772
Defining and running quality rules, investigating consistency, sampling techniques, and the mechanics of SKU.

4894
08:05:17,518 --> 08:05:22,276
That this course has referenced in four separate services without ever explaining properly.

4895
08:05:22,862 --> 08:05:24,704
Four worked scenarios.

4896
08:05:25,358 --> 08:05:29,572
This is the shortest module in domain 3 and one of the most transferable.

4897
08:05:29,904 --> 08:05:35,172
That is because everything in it applies regardless of which service the data happens to be in.

4898
08:05:37,006 --> 08:05:42,756
This distinction has appeared in modules 6, 7, 12, and 14 and it finally gets a module of its own.

4899
08:05:43,342 --> 08:05:47,204
Every monitoring signal from module 14 describes the pipeline itself.

4900
08:05:47,950 --> 08:05:50,372
Did the job start? Did it finish?

4901
08:05:50,830 --> 08:05:52,196
How long did it take?

4902
08:05:52,686 --> 08:05:54,468
How much memory did it consume?

4903
08:05:55,470 --> 08:06:03,204
None of those notices that a source began sending nulls, that a currency code changed, or that 2% of records now fail to pass.

4904
08:06:04,014 --> 08:06:09,700
Data quality is a genuinely separate discipline, measuring the contents rather than the process.

4905
08:06:10,544 --> 08:06:16,004
The exam treats it that way with its own task statement and its own dedicated service.

4906
08:06:16,464 --> 08:06:21,668
If a question describes a successful pipeline and a wrong number, this is the module it belongs to.

4907
08:06:23,214 --> 08:06:25,700
This slide exists to connect two facts.

4908
08:06:26,318 --> 08:06:28,228
The course has taught separately.

4909
08:06:29,422 --> 08:06:38,180
Module 10 established that primary key, foreign key, and uniqueness constraints in Redshift are informational only, so declaring them prevents nothing.

4910
08:06:39,118 --> 08:06:45,636
This module establishes that data quality is a separate discipline you implement deliberately.

4911
08:06:46,224 --> 08:06:49,380
Put those together and the conclusion is unavoidable.

4912
08:06:51,534 --> 08:06:57,124
If a warehouse table must not contain duplicates, something in the pipeline has to guarantee it.

4913
08:06:57,998 --> 08:07:03,364
Either a merge statement making the load idem potent or a quality rule that checks afterwards.

4914
08:07:03,950 --> 08:07:07,268
Assuming the database will refuse the duplicate is the mistake.

4915
08:07:09,099 --> 08:07:17,028
These six give you vocabulary for reading a question because a scenario usually describes a symptom belonging to exactly one of them.

4916
08:07:17,582 --> 08:07:19,620
Missing values are completeness.

4917
08:07:20,782 --> 08:07:23,140
Duplicated orders are uniqueness.

4918
08:07:24,462 --> 08:07:27,396
A postcode that is not a postcode is validity.

4919
08:07:28,366 --> 08:07:32,100
A total that disagrees with the source system is consistency.

4920
08:07:32,526 --> 08:07:35,268
And the call out names the one people forget.

4921
08:07:36,366 --> 08:07:46,276
A dashboard showing entirely accurate, complete, valid figures from Tuesday on Friday is a data quality failure even though every value in it is correct.

4922
08:07:47,214 --> 08:07:53,732
Freshness checks are the answer, and Module 14's absence alarming is one way to implement them.

4923
08:07:55,406 --> 08:08:04,324
AWS Blue Data Quality is the named service for this, and the two entry points in the heading matter because they answer genuinely different questions.

4924
08:08:05,966 --> 08:08:10,340
Running rules against a table in the Blue Data Calog is a monitoring activity.

4925
08:08:11,246 --> 08:08:20,068
You are checking data that already exists and reporting on it, which suits a scheduled assessment of a curated dataset that several teams depend on.

4926
08:08:22,126 --> 08:08:26,244
Running rules inside an ETL job is a control rather than a report.

4927
08:08:26,480 --> 08:08:29,092
The rules evaluate as data flows through.

4928
08:08:29,966 --> 08:08:37,444
The job can act on the result, stopping the pipeline outright or routing bad records aside before anything downstream ever sees them.

4929
08:08:38,030 --> 08:08:43,588
The exam distinguishes those two situations clearly and the first work scenario turns on it.

4930
08:08:44,846 --> 08:08:47,908
The callout is the exam framing and it is worth holding.

4931
08:08:48,622 --> 08:08:50,884
Nobody will ask you to compose a rule set.

4932
08:08:52,400 --> 08:08:57,412
What questions test is whether you know that a declarative rule language exists,

4933
08:08:58,254 --> 08:09:06,692
so that an option proposing to write custom validation code in a glue job is doing by hand what a managed feature does by configuration.

4934
08:09:07,182 --> 08:09:13,188
That is the same shape as module 4's app flow argument and module 2's enable update catalog argument.

4935
08:09:14,030 --> 08:09:19,108
The custom SQL rule type on the last bullet matters as the escape hatch.

4936
08:09:20,080 --> 08:09:26,372
That is because it means a genuinely unusual business rule is still expressible without abandoning the framework.

4937
08:09:28,014 --> 08:09:28,708
Two rows.

4938
08:09:29,200 --> 08:09:32,644
Here answer question shapes this module raises elsewhere.

4939
08:09:33,326 --> 08:09:42,660
Referential integrity is what catches the second verb scenario where an inner join silently discards orders whose currency code is missing from a reference table.

4940
08:09:43,680 --> 08:09:51,556
Freshness is the rule type for the dimension people forget, asserting that the newest record is no more than a stated age.

4941
08:09:51,790 --> 08:09:56,400
That turns a stale pipeline into a rule failure rather than a business complaint.

4942
08:09:56,814 --> 08:10:00,772
Row count match is the consistency check between two datasets.

4943
08:10:01,550 --> 08:10:09,508
If you can associate those three rule names with those three problems, most view data quality questions become straightforward.

4944
08:10:10,720 --> 08:10:19,012
The callout is the distinction the exam tests and it mirrors module 14's threshold against anomaly detection argument exactly.

4945
08:10:19,534 --> 08:10:27,684
A rule saying row count must exceed 10,000 catches, a catastrophic drop, and misses a gradual 20% decline.

4946
08:10:28,686 --> 08:10:31,780
That is because somebody had to choose that number in advance.

4947
08:10:32,160 --> 08:10:39,076
Anomaly detection watches statistics over time and reports when a pattern breaks, including patterns nobody anticipated.

4948
08:10:39,360 --> 08:10:43,748
A sudden rise in nulls in a column that has always been complete, for instance.

4949
08:10:44,526 --> 08:10:51,300
Dynamic rules set between the two, expressing a rule relative to recent history rather than against a fixed constant.

4950
08:10:52,654 --> 08:10:56,004
This mirrors module 14's point about alarms.

4951
08:10:56,240 --> 08:10:59,172
Nobody acts on and the failure mode is identical.

4952
08:10:59,600 --> 08:11:07,972
A completeness rule demanding that an optional field is always populated fails every single night. Somebody mutes the notification within a fortnight.

4953
08:11:08,846 --> 08:11:12,080
The rule set now provides false comfort rather than protection.

4954
08:11:12,366 --> 08:11:17,476
Profiling is what prevents this because it tells you the actual null rate before you choose a number.

4955
08:11:17,742 --> 08:11:25,156
The last bullet is the healthy pattern. Start at a threshold slightly below current reality so that only genuine degradation fails.

4956
08:11:26,062 --> 08:11:32,420
Then raise it as the upstream data improves and the rule keeps its meaning.

4957
08:11:33,838 --> 08:11:37,760
A retailer's nitri pipeline loads order data into Amazon Redshift.

4958
08:11:38,222 --> 08:11:45,956
Last month, a source system began populating a currency column with an empty string instead of the currency code for a small share of records.

4959
08:11:48,046 --> 08:11:52,516
Revenue reports were understated for 11 days before anyone noticed.

4960
08:11:53,326 --> 08:12:01,348
The team wants to catch this class of problem before bad data reaches the warehouse and does not want to write and maintain custom validation code.

4961
08:12:02,478 --> 08:12:04,516
What should the data engineer implement?

4962
08:12:05,454 --> 08:12:08,996
a A CloudWatch alarm on the view jobs failed run count.

4963
08:12:09,440 --> 08:12:09,760
B

4964
08:12:10,798 --> 08:12:18,820
AWS View Data Quality Rules Inside the ETL job failing the job or quarantining rows when they do not pass.

4965
08:12:19,440 --> 08:12:25,700
C A Custom Python validation step in the Blue job that checks each column.

4966
08:12:26,160 --> 08:12:31,652
D Increase the redshift cluster size so the load completes before business hours.

4967
08:12:32,494 --> 08:12:33,892
Pause and decide.

4968
08:12:40,622 --> 08:12:41,668
The answer is B.

4969
08:12:41,824 --> 08:12:52,740
And this is module 14's fourth failure family in its purest form. The job succeeded 11 times. So nothing about failure detection, retrees, or capacity has any bearing.

4970
08:12:56,110 --> 08:13:00,516
Option C is the one worth studying because it is not wrong technically.

4971
08:13:01,424 --> 08:13:03,940
Custom validation would catch the problem.

4972
08:13:04,144 --> 08:13:11,876
It loses on the stated constraint about maintaining custom code, and that constraint is the question telling you a managed alternative exists.

4973
08:13:13,390 --> 08:13:16,900
This is the same reasoning as module 4's Salesforce scenario.

4974
08:13:17,984 --> 08:13:24,388
Handwritten API code was defeated there by a managed connector doing the same job.

4975
08:13:25,774 --> 08:13:33,156
This mirrors module 6 retry against dead letter Q decision applied to records rather than to messages.

4976
08:13:33,806 --> 08:13:39,748
Failing the job is right when partial data is worse than no data, which is the case for anything financial or regulated.

4977
08:13:40,558 --> 08:13:45,828
That is because a report built on 98% of transactions is wrong rather than incomplete.

4978
08:13:46,064 --> 08:13:52,944
Quarantining is right when the good records have value on their own and the bad ones are a small investigable set.

4979
08:13:53,646 --> 08:13:55,588
The warning in the last bullet is real.

4980
08:13:56,302 --> 08:14:05,024
A quarantine location nobody reviews is a deletion with extra steps, and the exam does test whether the design includes somebody looking at it.

4981
08:14:06,478 --> 08:14:12,036
The callout connects this module to module 9, and the two together are worth more than either alone.

4982
08:14:12,304 --> 08:14:14,980
Quality checks that run after data is published.

4983
08:14:15,504 --> 08:14:18,276
Tell you that consumers have already seen something wrong.

4984
08:14:18,864 --> 08:14:22,704
That is better than not knowing and considerably worse than prevention.

4985
08:14:23,438 --> 08:14:27,396
Writing to an iceberg branch, running the rule set against that branch.

4986
08:14:28,110 --> 08:14:36,740
Merging into main only when it passes means consumers never see the intermediate state at all. That is the write, audit, publish pattern.

4987
08:14:37,358 --> 08:14:40,804
And it is the difference between detecting a problem and preventing one.

4988
08:14:41,806 --> 08:14:44,644
The last bullet is the general principle underneath.

4989
08:14:45,102 --> 08:14:53,584
A bad record caught at ingestion affects one file, while the same record caught after publication has already flowed into every downstream

4990
08:14:53,824 --> 08:14:55,172
table that read it.

4991
08:14:56,846 --> 08:15:01,188
Skill 3.4.3 names investigating data consistency.

4992
08:15:02,126 --> 08:15:08,324
It is distinct from everything else in this module because it involves comparing two things rather than inspecting one.

4993
08:15:08,782 --> 08:15:12,036
A warehouse total that disagrees with the source system.

4994
08:15:13,006 --> 08:15:21,572
A dimension table containing customers, the fact table has never referenced. A daily aggregate that does not sum to the monthly one.

4995
08:15:21,984 --> 08:15:28,804
Each of those is internally valid on both sides and wrong only when compared, which is what makes it hard.

4996
08:15:29,326 --> 08:15:36,196
That also makes consistency the dimension most likely to be discovered by a business user rather than by a pipeline.

4997
08:15:36,784 --> 08:15:40,196
That is because the pipeline has no reason to look at the other side.

4998
08:15:40,784 --> 08:15:43,460
That is exactly why it needs deliberate checking.

4999
08:15:45,102 --> 08:15:48,484
The callout matters because row counts are the check.

5000
08:15:48,910 --> 08:15:52,228
Everybody implements and they are weaker than they look.

5001
08:15:53,070 --> 08:16:00,260
A pipeline that reads a million rows and writes a million rows with every value shifted by one column passes a count check perfectly.

5002
08:16:01,166 --> 08:16:09,348
Aggregate comparison is meaningfully stronger because summing a revenue column on both sides catches wrong values as well as missing ones.

5003
08:16:10,382 --> 08:16:16,900
Data set match is stronger again and correspondingly more expensive, comparing records by key.

5004
08:16:17,424 --> 08:16:24,580
The right level depends on what the data is for, and financial reconciliation genuinely does need the expensive version.

5005
08:16:25,934 --> 08:16:31,812
Module 2 listed fine matches among the dynamic frame transforms and this is where it belongs conceptually.

5006
08:16:32,384 --> 08:16:36,068
Exact duplication is a uniqueness problem with a simple answer.

5007
08:16:36,654 --> 08:16:39,844
Fuzzy duplication is a different problem entirely.

5008
08:16:40,654 --> 08:16:48,932
The same customer appears as two records because one has a middle initial and the other does not, or a street is abbreviated differently.

5009
08:16:49,294 --> 08:16:54,532
No comparison you can write in SQL reliably decides whether those are the same person.

5010
08:16:55,054 --> 08:16:58,212
That is because the rule depends on your data and your tolerance.

5011
08:16:59,150 --> 08:17:04,144
Find matches is trained on examples you label so it learns your definition of sameness.

5012
08:17:04,622 --> 08:17:09,028
A question describing records that are similar but not identical is pointing at it.

5013
08:17:10,384 --> 08:17:18,980
A finance team reports that monthly revenue in the warehouse is consistently around 1% below the figure in the source order system.

5014
08:17:19,424 --> 08:17:25,316
The Blue Pipeline reports success every night and row counts between source and target match exactly.

5015
08:17:26,158 --> 08:17:30,692
The pipeline joins orders to a currency conversion reference table before loading.

5016
08:17:31,264 --> 08:17:35,556
Some orders use currency codes that were added to the source system recently.

5017
08:17:36,304 --> 08:17:40,036
What is the most likely cause and which check would have caught it?

5018
08:17:40,624 --> 08:17:43,108
a rows are being dropped by the join.

5019
08:17:44,046 --> 08:17:47,620
A referential integrity rule on the currency code would have caught it.

5020
08:17:47,984 --> 08:17:48,224
b.

5021
08:17:48,750 --> 08:17:51,908
The blue job is under provisioned and timing out partway through.

5022
08:17:52,366 --> 08:17:56,548
C. Redshift is applying compression that loses precision on the revenue column.

5023
08:17:57,024 --> 08:17:57,344
D.

5024
08:17:58,030 --> 08:18:01,892
The row count check is sufficient and the source system figure must be wrong.

5025
08:18:02,766 --> 08:18:03,908
Pause and decide.

5026
08:18:10,350 --> 08:18:15,940
The answer is A, and the detail that makes it fair is the mention of recently added currency codes.

5027
08:18:16,224 --> 08:18:22,308
An inner join to a reference table that does not yet contain those codes silently drops every order using them.

5028
08:18:22,864 --> 08:18:27,012
That is a small and consistent share, matching the 1% gap.

5029
08:18:27,758 --> 08:18:30,660
The callout explains why the row count check passed.

5030
08:18:31,662 --> 08:18:38,436
It compared the rows written to the rows the job processed after the join. So the discarded rows were never in either number.

5031
08:18:39,342 --> 08:18:46,884
Referential integrity is the rule type that names this exact failure, and it is why the DQDL rule list is worth recognizing.

5032
08:18:48,462 --> 08:18:52,164
Skill 3.4.4 names data sampling techniques.

5033
08:18:52,464 --> 08:19:01,892
That is unusual for an AWS exam because it is a statistical topic rather than a service one. The reason it appears is entirely practical.

5034
08:19:04,718 --> 08:19:09,988
Profiling a 100 billion row table completely is expensive and frequently unnecessary.

5035
08:19:10,926 --> 08:19:15,940
A well-chosen sample answers the same question for a fraction of the cost and time.

5036
08:19:16,686 --> 08:19:22,532
The exam does not test statistical theory and will not ask you to compute a confidence interval.

5037
08:19:23,406 --> 08:19:32,516
It tests whether you know which sampling approach suits a situation and more importantly, where sampling gives a misleading answer.

5038
08:19:33,326 --> 08:19:38,756
That second half is the more useful one, and the fourth worked scenario is built on it.

5039
08:19:40,384 --> 08:19:45,904
The last row is included because it is what people actually do and it is where the exam sets a trap.

5040
08:19:46,144 --> 08:19:52,292
Reading the first thousand rows of a file sorted by date tells you about January and nothing about the rest of the year.

5041
08:19:52,942 --> 08:19:59,300
A crawler sampling the beginning of a file infers a schema from records that may not represent the whole.

5042
08:19:59,664 --> 08:20:05,476
Stratified sampling on the second row is the one worth understanding because it solves a real problem.

5043
08:20:06,286 --> 08:20:12,224
Suppose 99% of records are ordinary and 1% are the fraudulent ones you care about.

5044
08:20:12,464 --> 08:20:15,524
A random sample of a thousand contains about 10 of them.

5045
08:20:16,206 --> 08:20:19,780
Stratified sampling deliberately takes enough from each group.

5046
08:20:21,326 --> 08:20:24,976
The distinction in the call out is the genuinely useful exam point.

5047
08:20:25,390 --> 08:20:32,612
A null rate, an average, a distribution all estimate well from a reasonable sample because they are properties of the population.

5048
08:20:33,390 --> 08:20:42,596
Uniqueness does not, because proving that no duplicate exists requires looking at every row, and a sample showing no duplicates proves nothing.

5049
08:20:43,598 --> 08:20:49,924
Referential integrity is the same, since the one orphaned record may be the one you did not sample.

5050
08:20:50,766 --> 08:20:58,532
So a question describing a completeness check on an enormous table can reasonably sample, and a question about detecting duplicates cannot.

5051
08:20:59,950 --> 08:21:03,908
The first bullet connects sampling to module 13's economics.

5052
08:21:04,494 --> 08:21:11,780
Because Athena charges for data scanned, sampling a table is not merely faster, it is proportionally cheaper.

5053
08:21:12,174 --> 08:21:18,372
That makes exploratory profiling on a large dataset affordable in a way it would not be otherwise.

5054
08:21:19,216 --> 08:21:21,796
The last bullet is the warning worth carrying.

5055
08:21:22,176 --> 08:21:29,508
If you are sampling to detect skew, a random sample of a skewed dataset contains the hotkey roughly in proportion.

5056
08:21:30,016 --> 08:21:32,976
So it looks less dramatic than reality.

5057
08:21:33,582 --> 08:21:42,084
Detecting skew reliably means counting rows per key across everything, which is cheap to do as an aggregate even on large data.

5058
08:21:43,822 --> 08:21:54,180
Module 5 described it in Spark, module 3 in Kinesis shards, Module 10 in Redshift Distribution, and Module 11 in DynamoDB partitions.

5059
08:21:55,086 --> 08:22:03,492
The exam guide names understanding data skew mechanisms as a skill in domain 3, so it deserves consolidating into one place.

5060
08:22:04,590 --> 08:22:08,196
The underlying mechanism is identical in all four cases.

5061
08:22:09,102 --> 08:22:10,916
Work is divided by a key.

5062
08:22:11,374 --> 08:22:14,660
One key value holds far more data than the others.

5063
08:22:15,726 --> 08:22:22,500
Whichever unit of parallelism receives that key does far more work than its peers while they finish and wait.

5064
08:22:23,406 --> 08:22:32,676
Everything else about the symptom and the fix follows from that single sentence, which is why learning it once is better than learning it four times.

5065
08:22:34,350 --> 08:22:38,816
Reading down this table, the symptom is recognizably the same each time.

5066
08:22:39,086 --> 08:22:44,516
Uneven load, with total capacity apparently sufficient and one unit saturated.

5067
08:22:44,816 --> 08:22:50,788
And in every case, the exam offers more capacity as a plausible option because that is the instinct.

5068
08:22:51,534 --> 08:23:00,900
It fails identically in all four, since doubling the shards, slices, partitions, or executors does not change which one a given key hashes to.

5069
08:23:01,294 --> 08:23:05,412
That is why this is worth learning as one concept rather than four facts.

5070
08:23:06,222 --> 08:23:14,084
Recognizing the shape means you eliminate the capacity option in every service without re deriving the reasoning.

5071
08:23:15,246 --> 08:23:23,364
Salting is the technique worth understanding rather than memorizing because it appears in questions and sounds like magic otherwise.

5072
08:23:23,856 --> 08:23:28,068
Suppose one customer identifier holds 40% of the rows.

5073
08:23:28,576 --> 08:23:34,820
Appending a random number from 1 to 10 creates 10 distinct keys, spreading across 10 units.

5074
08:23:35,790 --> 08:23:43,236
The aggregation then happens per sorted key, and a second pass combines those 10 partial results back into a single answer.

5075
08:23:44,014 --> 08:23:50,148
That is why it is described as a two-stage operation and why it costs something in complexity and compute.

5076
08:23:51,054 --> 08:23:59,844
Module 5 scenario preferred filtering the test accounts precisely because removing the cause outright is cheaper and simpler than engineering a way around it.

5077
08:24:00,336 --> 08:24:03,588
This module's third scenario makes the same choice.

5078
08:24:13,422 --> 08:24:21,028
Monitoring shows two tasks running for the full duration, while the remaining 118 finish within 4 minutes.

5079
08:24:21,582 --> 08:24:25,776
Data volume has grown about 10% since the job was written.

5080
08:24:26,318 --> 08:24:35,172
Investigation shows that a monitoring bot and a load testing harness account for a large share of all events, both using a fixed session identifier.

5081
08:24:35,822 --> 08:24:37,476
What should the data engineer do?

5082
08:24:38,062 --> 08:24:41,476
a Increase the number of workers from 30 to 90.

5083
08:24:41,936 --> 08:24:48,516
b Exclude the bot and load test session identifiers and sort any remaining hotkeys.

5084
08:24:48,896 --> 08:24:49,136
C.

5085
08:24:49,536 --> 08:24:53,988
Switch to memory optimized R workers so the long task have more memory.

5086
08:24:54,416 --> 08:24:59,140
d Enable Glue auto scaling so capacity adjusts to the workload.

5087
08:24:59,502 --> 08:25:00,772
Pause and decide.

5088
08:25:07,310 --> 08:25:08,516
The answer is B.

5089
08:25:08,974 --> 08:25:17,380
The 10% volume growth is planted to make capacity look reasonable, and a 10% increase does not turn 20 minutes into 2 hours.

5090
08:25:17,998 --> 08:25:20,452
The distribution changed, not the volume.

5091
08:25:21,262 --> 08:25:27,428
Three of the four options offer capacity in different forms, which by now should be a recognizable pattern.

5092
08:25:27,856 --> 08:25:31,460
What makes B strong is the first half rather than the second.

5093
08:25:31,856 --> 08:25:36,068
The boat and the load testing harness are not real user sessions.

5094
08:25:36,910 --> 08:25:45,540
So excluding them removes the skew rather than working around it, and sorting is only needed for whatever genuine concentration remains.

5095
08:25:47,214 --> 08:25:51,716
Skill 3.1.6 Names preparing data with Databrew and SageMaker.

5096
08:25:52,176 --> 08:25:57,056
Unified Studio, Skill 3.2.2 names, verifying and cleaning it.

5097
08:25:57,216 --> 08:26:00,196
So this topic sits inside two separate task statements.

5098
08:26:01,134 --> 08:26:05,988
The callout is the practical sequence and it connects to the rest of this module directly.

5099
08:26:06,416 --> 08:26:13,796
A profile job reports the actual distribution of each column, the null rate, the cardinality, and the outliers.

5100
08:26:14,256 --> 08:26:17,860
That is precisely what tells you which rules are worth writing.

5101
08:26:18,638 --> 08:26:30,436
Writing a completeness rule on a column that is legitimately optional produces a nightly failure nobody acts on. Profiling is how you find out which columns those are before you commit to a threshold.

5102
08:26:32,014 --> 08:26:36,976
The labels feature on the fourth bullet is recent and it addresses a genuine scaling problem.

5103
08:26:37,216 --> 08:26:43,876
An organization with 2000 rules across 300 tables produces a great many failures, most of them minor.

5104
08:26:44,496 --> 08:26:47,396
Finding the ones that actually matter becomes its own job.

5105
08:26:48,366 --> 08:26:56,996
Labels attach metadata to each rule, such as business criticality, the owning team or a compliance regime like GDPR.

5106
08:26:57,390 --> 08:27:03,076
So a dashboard can show only high priority customer data failures owned by marketing.

5107
08:27:03,822 --> 08:27:06,896
The last bullet connects back to module 12.

5108
08:27:07,406 --> 08:27:12,420
Quality results surfacing in the catalog are what let a consumer judge a dataset.

5109
08:27:12,656 --> 08:27:16,932
They can see whether it is trustworthy before building anything on top of it.

5110
08:27:18,574 --> 08:27:25,092
A data platform team must profile a new 200 billion row dataset before designing quality rules for it.

5111
08:27:25,456 --> 08:27:31,556
They need to understand null rates per column, value distributions, and approximate cardinality.

5112
08:27:32,270 --> 08:27:37,732
They also need to confirm that a customer identifier column contains no duplicates.

5113
08:27:38,638 --> 08:27:44,576
A full scan of the dataset is expensive and the team wants to limit cost where they reasonably can.

5114
08:27:44,896 --> 08:27:47,332
What approach should the data engineer take?

5115
08:27:47,776 --> 08:27:55,044
a Profile a random sample for distributions and null rates and run the uniqueness check across the full dataset.

5116
08:27:57,006 --> 08:27:57,296
b

5117
08:27:58,446 --> 08:28:02,052
Profile a random sample and check uniqueness on the same sample.

5118
08:28:02,576 --> 08:28:05,936
C. Read the first million rows and profile those.

5119
08:28:06,256 --> 08:28:10,336
D. Run a full profile of every column across the entire dataset.

5120
08:28:10,496 --> 08:28:11,524
Pause and decide.

5121
08:28:18,336 --> 08:28:24,548
The answer is A, and it is the only option that distinguishes between the two kinds of check.

5122
08:28:25,326 --> 08:28:28,736
That distinction is the whole point of the sampling section.

5123
08:28:29,326 --> 08:28:37,296
Distributions, null rates, and approximate cardinality are population properties that a sample estimates well,

5124
08:28:37,616 --> 08:28:41,444
and the word approximate in the requirement is a deliberate hint.

5125
08:28:41,966 --> 08:28:47,172
Uniqueness is an absolute claim about every row, and no sample can support it.

5126
08:28:48,046 --> 08:28:51,428
Option C is worth recognizing as the mistake it is.

5127
08:28:51,696 --> 08:28:58,372
That is because reading the first million rows feels like sampling and describes only the beginning of the data.

5128
08:29:00,046 --> 08:29:06,756
This is the organizational framing the exam guide implies without stating, and it connects several modules.

5129
08:29:08,334 --> 08:29:13,892
Module 12 described a business catalog where consumers discover datasets and request access.

5130
08:29:14,400 --> 08:29:17,520
This module describes rules producing a score.

5131
08:29:18,062 --> 08:29:25,252
Put them together, and a consumer browsing the catalog can see that a dataset passed 98% of its quality rules last night.

5132
08:29:25,966 --> 08:29:29,700
That is information they cannot otherwise get without asking somebody.

5133
08:29:30,222 --> 08:29:33,636
The last bullet is the practical argument for writing rules at all.

5134
08:29:34,542 --> 08:29:41,860
Every pipeline carries assumptions about its input, and rules are simply those assumptions written down where they can be checked.

5135
08:29:43,342 --> 08:29:51,428
The first row collects three separate failures this course has described in modules 2, 12, and here and they share a symptom.

5136
08:29:51,840 --> 08:29:54,692
A successful job producing understated totals.

5137
08:29:55,758 --> 08:29:56,836
The causes differ.

5138
08:29:57,806 --> 08:30:05,860
An inner join discarding unmatched rows, a permissive read nulling unpassable values, or a filter stricter than intended.

5139
08:30:06,240 --> 08:30:08,560
The investigation is the same in each case.

5140
08:30:08,910 --> 08:30:12,388
Compare counts and sums at each stage until the number changes.

5141
08:30:13,262 --> 08:30:22,372
The last row is the honest limitation of rule-based quality, and it is why the anomaly detection slide earlier matters rather than being a footnote.

5142
08:30:24,000 --> 08:30:33,476
Five questions and the first has now appeared in three consecutive modules, which should tell you how reliably the exam builds questions around it.

5143
08:30:34,286 --> 08:30:43,120
A pipeline that succeeded while producing wrong output needs a correctness answer, and every option about alarms, retrees, and capacity is there to be eliminated.

5144
08:30:43,440 --> 08:30:49,604
The fourth is the one that transfers furthest because Q appears in four services and the reasoning is identical in each.

5145
08:30:50,414 --> 08:30:52,068
Next module closes.

5146
08:30:53,070 --> 08:31:01,280
Domain 3 with cost and performance optimization, which consolidates the levers this course has introduced, surveyed by service.

5147
08:31:02,990 --> 08:31:06,480
This module is different in character from the 15 before it.

5148
08:31:06,720 --> 08:31:12,228
Cost is not a separate topic on this exam, it is a qualifier attached to questions about everything else.

5149
08:31:13,006 --> 08:31:17,092
That is why it maps to only one named skill while appearing constantly.

5150
08:31:18,094 --> 08:31:19,748
So this is a synthesis module.

5151
08:31:20,334 --> 08:31:30,820
It collects the levers, introduce service by service across the course, sets them side by side so the pattern is visible, covers the provisioned against serverless decision properly.

5152
08:31:31,760 --> 08:31:37,604
It also covers the cost management tooling itself, cost explorer and AWS budgets

5153
08:31:38,606 --> 08:31:43,044
that are on the in-scope service list and have not appeared anywhere else.

5154
08:31:44,110 --> 08:31:49,316
4 work scenarios, each requiring more than one module's knowledge.

5155
08:31:51,054 --> 08:31:56,420
Module 0 gave you these numbers and they are worth repeating here with their consequence attached.

5156
08:31:56,878 --> 08:32:02,404
Least operational overhead appeared 71 times across 341 questions.

5157
08:32:02,859 --> 08:32:05,459
Most cost effective appeared 42 times.

5158
08:32:06,158 --> 08:32:12,484
Least development effort and least latency appeared less often but no less decisively when they did.

5159
08:32:13,038 --> 08:32:18,116
So the difference between two candidates who know the services equally well is often one habit.

5160
08:32:18,670 --> 08:32:23,600
Whether they read that phrase and let it eliminate options before comparing anything technical.

5161
08:32:24,110 --> 08:32:29,092
Everything in this module exists to make that elimination automatic rather than effortful.

5162
08:32:29,600 --> 08:32:35,748
So that it costs you five seconds rather than a minute of weighing merits that were never in question.

5163
08:32:37,454 --> 08:32:43,172
The callout is the misreading that costs marks and it is worth being deliberate about.

5164
08:32:43,854 --> 08:32:47,140
An option can be genuinely cheaper and still be wrong.

5165
08:32:47,950 --> 08:32:55,520
That is because it misses a latency requirement or a compliance requirement or a refusal stated elsewhere in the question.

5166
08:32:56,080 --> 08:32:58,884
So the reading order matters more than the reading speed.

5167
08:32:59,278 --> 08:33:06,916
Find every constraint first, eliminate the options that violate any one of them, and only then compare cost among whatever survives.

5168
08:33:07,854 --> 08:33:16,324
Candidates who compare cost first frequently choose something the question already ruled out two sentences earlier.

5169
08:33:17,710 --> 08:33:24,516
That is a mistake of process rather than of knowledge, and it is entirely avoidable with a habit.

5170
08:33:26,350 --> 08:33:31,504
Module 1 introduced this and every module since has applied it in one service or another.

5171
08:33:31,744 --> 08:33:40,944
You do not need to memorize a single price to answer cost questions on this exam, and memorizing prices would be actively unhelpful because they change.

5172
08:33:41,184 --> 08:33:44,304
What you need is to know what each service meters.

5173
08:33:45,024 --> 08:33:53,584
Some charge for capacity you hold, so they cost exactly the same whether you use them or not, and they punish idle time relentlessly.

5174
08:33:53,904 --> 08:34:01,668
Some charge for work performed, so they cost nothing at all when idle and become expensive under sustained heavy load.

5175
08:34:02,606 --> 08:34:09,092
Almost every cost question turns on identifying which of those two situations the scenario describes.

5176
08:34:09,742 --> 08:34:14,304
The next slide puts every service in this course into one place.

5177
08:34:16,046 --> 08:34:25,796
This table and the next consolidated billing units introduce service by service across the course and reading them shows a pattern rather than a price list.

5178
08:34:26,382 --> 08:34:28,708
The division to make is the one in the callout.

5179
08:34:29,344 --> 08:34:38,340
Some services charge continuously for capacity you hold, so idle time is expensive, and the answer to a cost problem is usually to stop holding it.

5180
08:34:38,784 --> 08:34:45,988
Others charge for work performed, so idle time costs nothing at all, and heavy sustained use is what becomes expensive.

5181
08:34:46,830 --> 08:34:52,068
Amazon EMR on EC2 is the clearest example of the first kind.

5182
08:34:53,038 --> 08:34:59,904
It bills for every instance hour whether a job is running or not, which is exactly why transient clusters exist.

5183
08:35:01,614 --> 08:35:03,844
Three rows here are worth pausing on.

5184
08:35:04,526 --> 08:35:12,624
Athena is the only service in the course whose billing model you can change entirely, from bytes scanned to reserved capacity, which module

5185
08:35:12,784 --> 08:35:13,664
13 covered.

5186
08:35:14,030 --> 08:35:18,244
Redshift builds compute and manage storage as genuinely separate lines.

5187
08:35:19,118 --> 08:35:26,468
That is why module 10's fourth scenario turned on the fact that resizing a cluster does nothing to a storage problem.

5188
08:35:26,944 --> 08:35:35,876
And DynamoDB offers both models cleanly with a switch between them, which makes that switch a real cost lever rather than a migration.

5189
08:35:36,782 --> 08:35:46,692
That single division, build while idle against build when working, answers a large share of cost questions without knowing a single actual price.

5190
08:35:48,334 --> 08:35:56,036
Look back across this course and almost every correct cost answer removed work rather than negotiating a discount on it.

5191
08:35:56,558 --> 08:35:59,652
Job bookmarks stop reprocessing yesterday's data.

5192
08:36:00,526 --> 08:36:02,784
Pushdown predicates stop reading partitions.

5193
08:36:02,958 --> 08:36:04,292
The query does not need.

5194
08:36:05,024 --> 08:36:08,388
Partitioning stops scanning irrelevant prefixes entirely.

5195
08:36:09,230 --> 08:36:11,940
Columna formats stop reading unwanted columns.

5196
08:36:12,224 --> 08:36:16,868
Materialized views stop recomputing the same aggregation for every viewer.

5197
08:36:19,022 --> 08:36:19,744
Query result.

5198
08:36:20,654 --> 08:36:23,300
Reuse stops running an identical query twice.

5199
08:36:24,398 --> 08:36:27,044
Kansian clusters stop paying for idle hours.

5200
08:36:28,078 --> 08:36:31,076
Every one of those reduces the work actually performed.

5201
08:36:31,918 --> 08:36:41,104
That is why they produce order of magnitude savings where a cheaper instance type or a commitment discount produces 20 or 30%.

5202
08:36:42,862 --> 08:36:47,748
The ordering on this slide is deliberate and it is roughly the order of impact.

5203
08:36:47,984 --> 08:36:52,544
Reading less data is the largest lever in almost every scenario.

5204
08:36:52,784 --> 08:36:58,980
That is because it compounds across every query and every job for the entire life of the dataset.

5205
08:36:59,694 --> 08:37:05,028
Committing to usage is genuinely useful and it is a discount on whatever you are already doing.

5206
08:37:05,504 --> 08:37:09,316
So it cannot fix a workload that is doing the wrong work in the first place.

5207
08:37:09,584 --> 08:37:12,004
The exam reflects this ordering faithfully.

5208
08:37:12,782 --> 08:37:20,260
Options offering reserved capacity for a pipeline that reprocesses everything nightly are answering the second question before the first.

5209
08:37:20,784 --> 08:37:24,164
The first question is why it reprocesses everything at all.

5210
08:37:25,774 --> 08:37:32,804
Almost everything else in this module either removes work or commits to a term, and this is the exception worth knowing.

5211
08:37:33,264 --> 08:37:41,584
Moving the same workload onto Graviton based hardware reduces the price of each unit of work with no change to the pipeline and no commitment.

5212
08:37:42,126 --> 08:37:44,324
Module 10 covered the Redshift case.

5213
08:37:44,814 --> 08:37:51,744
RG instance is launched in May 2026 at 30% lower price per virtual CPU than RA3.

5214
08:37:52,302 --> 08:37:55,300
The caveat on the last bullet is the only real one.

5215
08:37:55,584 --> 08:37:57,540
Manage services handle it for you.

5216
08:37:58,446 --> 08:38:06,084
A Lambda function or container carrying a compiled dependency built for x86 needs rebuilding for ARM.

5217
08:38:07,694 --> 08:38:14,500
Data transfer is the cost line that surprises Teams because nothing about it appears when you design a pipeline.

5218
08:38:15,086 --> 08:38:19,268
The last row connects directly to module 2's connectivity section.

5219
08:38:20,878 --> 08:38:26,144
Picture a glue job in a private subnet reaching S3 through a NAT gateway.

5220
08:38:26,734 --> 08:38:29,984
It pays a per gigabyte processing charge on every byte.

5221
08:38:30,224 --> 08:38:33,284
A gateway endpoint carries the same traffic for nothing.

5222
08:38:33,504 --> 08:38:42,340
On a pipeline moving terabytes, that is a substantial and entirely avoidable line. The cross-availability zone row matters for anything self-managed.

5223
08:38:42,944 --> 08:38:51,012
That is because a Kafka cluster or an EMR cluster spanning zones, pays for the chatter between its own nodes.

5224
08:38:52,718 --> 08:38:56,420
A company's analytic spend is dominated by three things.

5225
08:38:56,944 --> 08:39:03,172
A nightly GU job that reprocesses all historical data because bookmarks were never enabled.

5226
08:39:03,822 --> 08:39:08,132
Athena queries over uncompressed CSV in a single unpartitioned prefix

5227
08:39:08,974 --> 08:39:14,660
and an EMR cluster running continuously for a job that takes 90 minutes each night.

5228
08:39:15,264 --> 08:39:17,764
Finance has asked for a substantial reduction.

5229
08:39:18,318 --> 08:39:23,940
The team has been offered a reserved instance discount on the EMR cluster and is considering taking it.

5230
08:39:24,718 --> 08:39:26,884
What should the data engineer prioritize?

5231
08:39:29,421 --> 08:39:29,601
A.

5232
08:39:30,606 --> 08:39:35,024
Purchase the reserved instances which discount the largest single line item.

5233
08:39:35,374 --> 08:39:42,148
B. Enable bookmarks, convert to partition parquet, and switch EMR to a transient cluster.

5234
08:39:42,990 --> 08:39:43,264
C.

5235
08:39:43,854 --> 08:39:45,668
Move the S3 data to standard.

5236
08:39:47,758 --> 08:39:50,116
Infrequent access to reduce storage cost.

5237
08:39:51,104 --> 08:39:51,264
D

5238
08:39:52,558 --> 08:39:58,064
Set an AWS budget so the team is alerted when spend exceeds the target.

5239
08:39:58,704 --> 08:40:00,068
Pause and decide.

5240
08:40:06,574 --> 08:40:11,204
The answer is B. And option A is the clap this module was built around.

5241
08:40:11,744 --> 08:40:15,428
Reserved instances are a genuine and substantial discount.

5242
08:40:15,824 --> 08:40:22,084
Here they would commit the company to a cluster that should exist for 90 minutes a night rather than 24 hours.

5243
08:40:22,830 --> 08:40:31,780
Worse, the commitment makes the right fix awkward because a transient cluster does not benefit from a reservation on a cluster you no longer run.

5244
08:40:32,224 --> 08:40:33,584
The sequence matters.

5245
08:40:33,824 --> 08:40:38,244
Fix what the workload does, measure what remains, and commit to that.

5246
08:40:38,944 --> 08:40:44,996
Option D is worth noticing as a category error because visibility is not reduction.

5247
08:40:46,350 --> 08:40:57,732
Skill 3.2.5 is understanding the trade-offs between provisioned services and serverless services. It is the only cost-related skill named anywhere in the guide.

5248
08:40:58,158 --> 08:41:00,676
That is unusual enough to be worth noticing.

5249
08:41:01,614 --> 08:41:07,364
It tells you the exam expects a genuine understanding rather than a stated preference for one or the other.

5250
08:41:08,430 --> 08:41:17,540
Almost every service covered in this course now has both forms, which was not true three years ago. So the decision arises constantly rather than occasionally.

5251
08:41:18,414 --> 08:41:21,412
The rest of this section sets out what actually decides it.

5252
08:41:21,824 --> 08:41:29,796
The honest answer is that it is neither always serverless nor a close call, and the deciding factors are usually stated plainly in the question.

5253
08:41:31,182 --> 08:41:33,504
Two rows on this table are worth pausing on.

5254
08:41:33,870 --> 08:41:38,916
Athena inverts the usual arrangement because its default is already pay per query.

5255
08:41:39,662 --> 08:41:47,104
The reservation adds guaranteed capacity and removes the data scan charge, which module 13 covered in detail.

5256
08:41:47,726 --> 08:41:56,644
And MW AA A Serverless is the newest entry here, arriving in November 2025.

5257
08:41:58,030 --> 08:42:04,548
That module 6 flagged as changing a standard exam answer about paying for an idle airflow environment.

5258
08:42:05,294 --> 08:42:14,596
The general observation from this table is that the question is almost never whether a serverless option exists, because for these services it usually does.

5259
08:42:14,944 --> 08:42:17,636
It is whether the shape of the workload favors it.

5260
08:42:19,310 --> 08:42:25,156
The crossover is the concept that makes this decision answerable rather than a matter of preference.

5261
08:42:25,870 --> 08:42:31,140
Every serverless option costs more per unit of work than a well-utilized provisioned one.

5262
08:42:31,568 --> 08:42:34,500
Because you are paying for elasticity, you may not need.

5263
08:42:35,406 --> 08:42:41,380
Below a certain steady utilization, that premium is far cheaper than paying for capacity that sits idle.

5264
08:42:41,902 --> 08:42:44,804
Above it, the premium dominates and provision wins.

5265
08:42:45,168 --> 08:42:48,932
The exam never asks you to calculate where the crossover falls.

5266
08:42:49,838 --> 08:42:56,484
It describes which side of it you are on using words about how steady, predictable, and heavy the workload is.

5267
08:42:57,518 --> 08:43:03,108
Your job is to hear those words as a statement about cost rather than a scene setting.

5268
08:43:04,430 --> 08:43:11,428
The crossover in the callout is the honest version of this decision, and it is why the exam can ask about it meaningfully at all.

5269
08:43:11,728 --> 08:43:17,956
Every serverless service charges a premium per unit of work in exchange for charging nothing whatsoever when idle.

5270
08:43:18,702 --> 08:43:25,732
Below some level of utilization, that trade is clearly good, and above it, the premium exceeds everything you saved on the idle hours.

5271
08:43:26,510 --> 08:43:34,340
The exam does not expect you to calculate where the crossover sits. It does expect you to know which side of it a described workload is on.

5272
08:43:34,862 --> 08:43:40,516
A warehouse queried steadily by 400 analysts all day is well above it.

5273
08:43:40,942 --> 08:43:45,348
A development environment used two afternoons a week is well below.

5274
08:43:46,606 --> 08:43:50,180
A company runs two Amazon Redshift Environments.

5275
08:43:50,608 --> 08:43:57,028
Production serves around 300 analysts continuously through the working day with a measured and stable workload.

5276
08:43:58,350 --> 08:44:05,328
Development is used by four engineers for a few hours, two or three days a week, and its usage pattern is irregular.

5277
08:44:05,806 --> 08:44:11,940
Both currently run identically sized provision clusters on demand 24 hours a day.

5278
08:44:13,454 --> 08:44:17,604
The company wants to reduce cost without affecting production performance.

5279
08:44:18,158 --> 08:44:20,484
What should the data engineer recommend?

5280
08:44:21,678 --> 08:44:24,932
a move both environments to Redshift serverless.

5281
08:44:25,968 --> 08:44:26,288
b

5282
08:44:27,502 --> 08:44:33,124
Keep production provisioned with reserved instances and move development to Redshift serverless.

5283
08:44:33,568 --> 08:44:33,888
C.

5284
08:44:34,350 --> 08:44:38,116
Keep both provisioned and purchase reserved instances for both.

5285
08:44:39,248 --> 08:44:39,568
D.

5286
08:44:40,048 --> 08:44:44,164
Move production to Redshift serverless and keep development provisioned.

5287
08:44:46,288 --> 08:44:47,568
Pause and decide.

5288
08:44:54,126 --> 08:45:04,928
The answer is B, and this closes a loop. The course has been running since module 5. There, four workloads pointed at four engines and standardizing was wrong.

5289
08:45:05,088 --> 08:45:11,300
In module 11, 4 access patterns pointed at 4 data stores and standardizing was wrong.

5290
08:45:12,128 --> 08:45:15,780
Here, 2 environments point at 2 capacity models.

5291
08:45:16,110 --> 08:45:22,884
The exam is not against consistency, it is against applying one model to workloads with genuinely different shapes.

5292
08:45:23,488 --> 08:45:31,556
The word measured in the production description and the word irregular in the development description are the two signals placed deliberately.

5293
08:45:33,102 --> 08:45:40,388
Commitments are the pricing half of cost optimization as distinct from the work reduction half. This module argues for first.

5294
08:45:41,038 --> 08:45:46,980
Reserved instances apply to specific services including Redshift, Amazon RDS,

5295
08:45:47,662 --> 08:45:51,568
OpenSearch, and ElasticEch for one or three year terms.

5296
08:45:52,014 --> 08:46:05,248
Savings plans commit to an hourly spend across compute services, specifically EC2, Fargate and Lambda. That is worth remembering because it means they do not cover Glue, Athena, or S3 at all.

5297
08:46:05,614 --> 08:46:08,644
That coverage gap appears in the fourth worked scenario.

5298
08:46:09,486 --> 08:46:13,764
The judgment the exam occasionally tests is whether the workload will survive the term.

5299
08:46:14,350 --> 08:46:20,740
That is because a three year commitment on a pipeline being redesigned in six months is a loss rather than a saving.

5300
08:46:22,446 --> 08:46:28,868
AWS Cost Explorer and AWS Budgets are both on the exams in Scope Service List.

5301
08:46:29,870 --> 08:46:32,544
Neither has appeared anywhere in this course until now.

5302
08:46:32,704 --> 08:46:37,188
So this section is entirely new material rather than consolidation.

5303
08:46:38,862 --> 08:46:42,212
The framing that makes them examinable is the one in the heading.

5304
08:46:43,214 --> 08:46:47,844
An organization looking at a single large analytics bill cannot tell which team.

5305
08:46:48,494 --> 08:46:55,076
That pipeline or which dataset produced it, so every optimization discussion becomes speculation and argument.

5306
08:46:55,744 --> 08:46:57,668
Attribution has to come first.

5307
08:46:58,190 --> 08:47:01,220
And on AWS attribution means tags.

5308
08:47:02,190 --> 08:47:08,708
The next few slides cover the tooling and more usefully what each part of it is actually for.

5309
08:47:10,190 --> 08:47:13,092
That is because they are easy to confuse with each other.

5310
08:47:14,766 --> 08:47:21,124
Tagging is the mechanism underneath every attribution question, and it has one detail that catches people.

5311
08:47:21,742 --> 08:47:26,212
A tag applied to a resource does not appear in Cost Explorer straight away.

5312
08:47:26,798 --> 08:47:31,812
The tag key must first be activated as a cost allocation tag in the billing console.

5313
08:47:32,750 --> 08:47:35,364
And activation is not retrospective.

5314
08:47:36,782 --> 08:47:45,892
Spend from before activation stays unattributable permanently, which is what makes the third work scenario in this module partly unanswerable.

5315
08:47:46,894 --> 08:47:53,732
So the practical advice is to activate the tag keys you intend to use on day one before there is anything to attribute.

5316
08:47:54,190 --> 08:48:03,652
Cost categories sit above tags and group spend by rules, which is useful when the organization's structure does not map neatly onto how resources were tagged.

5317
08:48:05,294 --> 08:48:10,980
Cost Explorer is the analytical half of the pair and it appears on the InScope service list.

5318
08:48:11,264 --> 08:48:14,468
So it is fair game despite feeling like a finance tool.

5319
08:48:14,784 --> 08:48:19,140
It aggregates billing data into dimensions you can filter and group by.

5320
08:48:20,078 --> 08:48:26,276
Service, linked account, region, instance type, and crucially cost allocation tags.

5321
08:48:29,230 --> 08:48:35,492
That last one is what turns a total into an attribution, which is why the tagging slide sits alongside it.

5322
08:48:36,558 --> 08:48:41,956
The forecasting capability is worth knowing because it appears in questions about anticipating spend.

5323
08:48:42,862 --> 08:48:48,324
It projects from recent history with a confidence interval that widens when a workload is variable.

5324
08:48:48,704 --> 08:48:56,612
It also surfaces reservation and savings plans coverage, which is how you discover you are paying on demand rates for something predictable.

5325
08:48:58,126 --> 08:49:00,356
Budgets is the proactive half of the pair.

5326
08:49:00,704 --> 08:49:03,140
You set a threshold and receive an alert.

5327
08:49:03,566 --> 08:49:08,420
The detail worth knowing is that a budget can alert on forecast spend as well as on actual.

5328
08:49:09,230 --> 08:49:11,108
That difference matters practically.

5329
08:49:12,046 --> 08:49:18,660
An alert when actual spend crosses a line arrives on the 28th of the month when there is nothing left to do about it.

5330
08:49:19,118 --> 08:49:25,284
An alert on forecast arrives on the 9th, saying you are on course to exceed, which leaves time to act.

5331
08:49:25,774 --> 08:49:26,304
Cost.

5332
08:49:28,046 --> 08:49:33,220
Anomaly detection is the alternative when spend varies too much for a fixed threshold.

5333
08:49:33,584 --> 08:49:40,292
It is exactly module 14's anomaly detection argument applied to money instead of to operational metrics.

5334
08:49:41,678 --> 08:49:44,704
There is a pleasing symmetry here that is worth naming.

5335
08:49:45,038 --> 08:49:50,276
The cost and usage report is delivered as partition files in an S3 bucket.

5336
08:49:50,464 --> 08:49:58,084
That means analyzing your own AWS spend is exactly the lake querying problem this course has spent 16 modules on.

5337
08:49:58,830 --> 08:50:05,028
You define a table over it, partition by month, query it with Athena, and build a dashboard on top.

5338
08:50:05,550 --> 08:50:12,804
Cost Explorer is the convenient managed view and the report is where the full detail lives when a question cannot be answered from the console.

5339
08:50:13,584 --> 08:50:22,704
Cost categories are worth recognizing because they map spend onto how the business is organized rather than how AWS

5340
08:50:22,944 --> 08:50:23,264
is.

5341
08:50:24,704 --> 08:50:30,144
A platform team supports 12 product teams, sharing one AWS account for analytics.

5342
08:50:30,464 --> 08:50:33,508
The monthly bill has grown 40% in 6 months.

5343
08:50:34,830 --> 08:50:43,012
Leadership wants each team held accountable for its own spend and wants to know within a day if any team's costs jump unexpectedly.

5344
08:50:43,854 --> 08:50:45,984
Resources are currently untagged.

5345
08:50:46,318 --> 08:50:51,584
The team has proposed setting a single account wide budget alert at the current spend level.

5346
08:50:51,790 --> 08:50:54,020
What should the data engineer implement?

5347
08:50:54,318 --> 08:50:57,744
a The account wide budget alert as proposed.

5348
08:50:58,064 --> 08:50:58,384
B.

5349
08:50:59,342 --> 08:51:08,324
Apply and activate cost allocation tags per team, create per type budgets, and enable cost anomaly detection monitors scope to those tags.

5350
08:51:09,102 --> 08:51:12,804
C. Move each team into its own AWS account.

5351
08:51:13,422 --> 08:51:17,604
D Use Cost Explorer Weekly to review spend by service.

5352
08:51:20,590 --> 08:51:21,904
Pause and decide.

5353
08:51:28,462 --> 08:51:33,124
The answer is B, and the structure is the one this course has flagged repeatedly.

5354
08:51:34,670 --> 08:51:38,436
Three requirements are stated, and only one option satisfies all three.

5355
08:51:39,342 --> 08:51:47,620
Option C deserves respect because account separation genuinely is how large organizations achieve clean cost attribution

5356
08:51:47,824 --> 08:51:49,924
and it fails here on proportion.

5357
08:51:51,630 --> 08:51:59,716
Replatforming 12 teams into separate accounts to answer a reporting question is enormous and tags give the same attribution in days.

5358
08:52:00,654 --> 08:52:07,236
Note also that the anomaly detection half of B is what meets the within a day requirement.

5359
08:52:08,014 --> 08:52:12,900
That is because a budget alerts on a threshold rather than on a change.

5360
08:52:14,544 --> 08:52:19,184
The last bullet has now appeared in five separate modules and deserves collecting.

5361
08:52:19,344 --> 08:52:22,756
The glue default of 10 dPUs on a job that needs 3.

5362
08:52:23,264 --> 08:52:24,384
CloudWatch.

5363
08:52:33,102 --> 08:52:38,384
An S3 bucket accumulating non-current versions because no lifecycle rule expires them.

5364
08:52:38,734 --> 08:52:41,028
None of those is a design flaw exactly.

5365
08:52:41,710 --> 08:52:49,412
Each is a sensible default left in place for a year and collectively they account for a large share of the waste in a typical account.

5366
08:52:50,446 --> 08:52:57,220
So when a cost question describes something running unchanged for a long time, check the defaults before anything else.

5367
08:52:58,798 --> 08:53:04,644
This table collects the traps rather than the levers, and the callout explains why it is worth studying carefully.

5368
08:53:05,102 --> 08:53:11,524
Each of these is a reasonable sounding action that increases cost or leaves it unchanged while feeling like progress.

5369
08:53:13,134 --> 08:53:15,460
The exam offers all six of them as options.

5370
08:53:16,174 --> 08:53:20,932
The common shape running through them is applying a correct principle in the wrong situation.

5371
08:53:21,424 --> 08:53:27,268
Archive storage genuinely is cheaper for data that is genuinely cold and genuinely large.

5372
08:53:27,904 --> 08:53:34,660
Reserved capacity genuinely is cheaper for workloads that are genuinely steady and correctly designed already.

5373
08:53:35,664 --> 08:53:42,564
What makes each row wrong is a specific condition in the scenario that the optimization quietly ignores.

5374
08:53:44,078 --> 08:53:50,276
The multi-account pattern from module 6 creates a billing question as well as an operational one.

5375
08:53:52,046 --> 08:54:01,092
Consolidated billing under AWS organizations produces a single bill across every account, which is what finance wants.

5376
08:54:01,998 --> 08:54:07,236
Meanwhile, each account remains a clean boundary for attribution, which is what engineering wants.

5377
08:54:10,318 --> 08:54:16,868
That combination is why separate accounts per team or per environment is a stronger answer than tagging alone.

5378
08:54:17,678 --> 08:54:22,820
Tags can be applied inconsistently or forgotten and an account boundary cannot.

5379
08:54:23,630 --> 08:54:29,444
Reserved capacity and savings plans purchased in one account can be shared across the organization.

5380
08:54:30,304 --> 08:54:35,940
That matters because it means the commitment does not have to sit where the workload happens to run.

5381
08:54:37,664 --> 08:54:43,940
It is worth stating explicitly that most of this module's levers improve both cost and performance together.

5382
08:54:44,384 --> 08:54:46,596
Reading less data is faster and cheaper.

5383
08:54:46,864 --> 08:54:49,284
Removing redundant work is faster and cheaper.

5384
08:54:49,710 --> 08:54:51,940
Compacting small files is faster and cheaper.

5385
08:54:52,750 --> 08:54:58,064
That alignment is precisely why partitioning and columnar formats answer so many questions.

5386
08:54:58,304 --> 08:55:03,424
It holds regardless of whether the qualifier asked about cost or about speed.

5387
08:55:03,664 --> 08:55:07,332
Where the two diverge, the question tells you which one matters.

5388
08:55:07,886 --> 08:55:10,084
Adding workers is faster and dearer.

5389
08:55:10,864 --> 08:55:13,828
Larger buffers are cheaper and slower.

5390
08:55:14,350 --> 08:55:18,308
Approximate aggregations are faster, cheaper, and less exact.

5391
08:55:19,214 --> 08:55:24,516
Recognizing which of those situations you are in is the whole skill this slide is teaching.

5392
08:55:26,254 --> 08:55:34,112
This consolidates the performance diagnosis from modules 5, 8, 10, 12, and 13 into one place.

5393
08:55:35,232 --> 08:55:38,276
The callout is the pattern running across all of them.

5394
08:55:39,278 --> 08:55:47,556
The fourth row is the only one where more capacity genuinely helps because query skewing means there is not enough concurrency available to serve them.

5395
08:55:48,590 --> 08:55:55,268
Every other row describes work being done unnecessarily, done unevenly, or done in a way that cannot be divided.

5396
08:55:55,952 --> 08:55:58,272
Capacity changes none of those.

5397
08:55:58,862 --> 08:56:02,340
Module 14 made the same point as the four failure families.

5398
08:56:03,214 --> 08:56:08,512
This is the identical insight expressed as a lookup table you can scan quickly during a question.

5399
08:56:09,806 --> 08:56:13,632
An Athena workload costs around $40,000 a month.

5400
08:56:13,952 --> 08:56:18,404
Investigation shows the data is already parquet and partitioned by date.

5401
08:56:18,958 --> 08:56:22,948
Queries filter on date and most queries name specific columns.

5402
08:56:23,758 --> 08:56:33,732
Roughly 240,000 queries run per month, largely from scheduled dashboards refreshing every 5 minutes against data that updates hourly.

5403
08:56:34,192 --> 08:56:41,444
The team has already applied the standard optimizations and wants a further reduction. What should the data engineer do next?

5404
08:56:44,432 --> 08:56:47,611
Convert the data to ORC instead of Parquet.

5405
08:56:48,192 --> 08:56:48,432
b.

5406
08:56:48,846 --> 08:56:54,212
Enable query, result reuse, and align dashboard refresh to the hourly data update.

5407
08:56:54,766 --> 08:56:55,072
C.

5408
08:56:55,374 --> 08:56:59,172
Repartition the data by hour to improve pruning further.

5409
08:56:59,952 --> 08:57:00,192
D.

5410
08:57:00,686 --> 08:57:03,844
Move the workload to an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster.

5411
08:57:04,558 --> 08:57:05,572
Pause and decide.

5412
08:57:12,110 --> 08:57:16,420
The answer is B, and the arithmetic in the question is the whole point.

5413
08:57:16,752 --> 08:57:23,524
Refreshing every 5 minutes against data that changes hourly means 12 queries per hour where one would do.

5414
08:57:24,366 --> 08:57:32,804
That is not a small inefficiency. It is roughly 92% of the query volume, producing an answer that has not changed.

5415
08:57:33,678 --> 08:57:41,284
Option D is the interesting one. Because at 240,000 queries a month, Redshift genuinely might be cheaper.

5416
08:57:42,222 --> 08:57:45,412
Module 13 said frequency decides between them.

5417
08:57:45,806 --> 08:57:51,204
It loses because moving the workload carries the waste along with it, and the waste is the larger factor.

5418
08:57:52,878 --> 08:57:58,032
Right sizing is a habit rather than a one-off exercise, and the reason is that workloads drift.

5419
08:57:58,382 --> 08:58:05,796
A blue job size correctly for last year's volume is oversized or undersized for this year's, and nothing announces the change.

5420
08:58:06,542 --> 08:58:15,076
The practical version is to review the largest line items on a schedule, compare provision capacity against observed utilization, and adjust.

5421
08:58:16,750 --> 08:58:22,340
Module 10's first worked scenario turned on a job that had never been reconfigured in 18 months.

5422
08:58:23,342 --> 08:58:25,924
That is the common case rather than an unusual one.

5423
08:58:26,894 --> 08:58:35,748
The exam signals this by describing something created long ago and left alone, which is an invitation to check the configuration rather than to add capacity.

5424
08:58:37,326 --> 08:58:44,548
The well architected tool is on the exams in scope service list and gets very little attention, so it is worth a slide.

5425
08:58:46,222 --> 08:58:51,748
The value it offers over somebody experienced looking at an architecture is structure.

5426
08:58:52,750 --> 08:59:00,791
It asks the same questions every time, including the ones nobody thought to ask, and produces a documented list of findings with severities.

5427
08:59:01,518 --> 08:59:05,188
That turns an opinion into a plan that can be prioritized and tracked.

5428
08:59:05,774 --> 08:59:08,900
The lens on the fourth bullet is the detail worth knowing.

5429
08:59:09,472 --> 08:59:19,716
That is because RWS publishes a data analytics specific version of the framework rather than expecting you to apply the general one.

5430
08:59:21,280 --> 08:59:25,252
The callout is practical exam advice rather than a technical point.

5431
08:59:25,440 --> 08:59:30,436
Questions on this exam are not designed to be answered with a calculator and there is no time for one.

5432
08:59:30,800 --> 08:59:35,172
When two options look genuinely close on cost, that is usually a signal.

5433
08:59:35,600 --> 08:59:44,292
Cost is not the deciding factor, and something else in the question is the last bullet is the pattern that has recurred across this whole course.

5434
08:59:44,910 --> 08:59:54,596
An option that deletes a crawler, removes a cluster, or eliminates a scheduled job tends to win on cost and on operational overhead simultaneously.

5435
08:59:55,534 --> 09:00:00,228
That is why it survives whichever qualifier the question actually used.

5436
09:00:01,486 --> 09:00:07,428
The order genuinely matters and it is worth practicing deliberately between now and the exam.

5437
09:00:08,046 --> 09:00:16,996
Reading four plausible options before identifying the constraint means you form a view about the technology and then hunt for reasons to support it.

5438
09:00:17,774 --> 09:00:24,400
Reading the qualifier and the refusals first means you arrive at the options already knowing what cannot be true.

5439
09:00:24,686 --> 09:00:27,556
Two of them frequently disqualify themselves immediately.

5440
09:00:28,174 --> 09:00:33,604
The signal call out on every solution slide in this course has been pointing at the deciding sentence.

5441
09:00:34,542 --> 09:00:39,236
In almost every case, that sentence sits in the scenario rather than in the options.

5442
09:00:40,974 --> 09:00:45,540
5 questions, and the third is the one that separates a good answer from the best one.

5443
09:00:47,886 --> 09:00:51,652
Several options in a typical cost question genuinely reduce spend.

5444
09:00:52,590 --> 09:00:58,148
The strongest is almost always the one that stops work happening rather than the one that buys it more cheaply.

5445
09:00:59,086 --> 09:01:04,260
The fifth is the newest and appears specifically in questions about cost management tooling.

5446
09:01:05,550 --> 09:01:12,132
That is because a budget or a tag is offered as an answer to a reduction requirement and it is not one.

5447
09:01:13,998 --> 09:01:17,284
That completes domain 3, 22% of the exam.

5448
09:01:18,320 --> 09:01:26,596
Domain 4 begins next with authentication and network access, and it is the domain the question bank under represents most.

5449
09:01:28,078 --> 09:01:31,876
Domain 4 begins here and it is worth 18% of the exam.

5450
09:01:32,462 --> 09:01:36,580
Module 0 warned that this is the domain most candidates under prepare.

5451
09:01:37,422 --> 09:01:45,280
That is because security terms accounted for under 5% of mentions in the bank analyzed for this course while being 18%

5452
09:01:45,600 --> 09:01:46,276
of the exam.

5453
09:01:47,214 --> 09:01:51,680
That gap is worth roughly 9 scored questions and it is entirely avoidable.

5454
09:01:51,950 --> 09:01:56,772
This module covers who a request is and how it reaches your data across a network.

5455
09:01:57,518 --> 09:02:05,284
Module 18 covers what that identity is then allowed to do, 4 worked scenarios, and 2 promises from earlier modules to deliver.

5456
09:02:07,022 --> 09:02:12,480
The split between this module and the next follows the exam guide's own task statements.

5457
09:02:12,960 --> 09:02:17,924
It is worth holding because questions tend to sit clearly on one side or the other.

5458
09:02:18,958 --> 09:02:27,876
Task statement 4.1 is about applying authentication mechanisms, which means identities, roles, credentials, and the network path.

5459
09:02:28,334 --> 09:02:29,764
A request has to travel.

5460
09:02:30,574 --> 09:02:39,108
Task statement 4.2 is about authorization, which means the policy is deciding what an already authenticated identity may touch.

5461
09:02:39,950 --> 09:02:46,160
So a question about a job that cannot reach a database is usually authentication or networking.

5462
09:02:46,702 --> 09:02:50,948
A question about a team seeing columns they should not see is authorization.

5463
09:02:52,320 --> 09:03:00,132
Getting that division right in the first few seconds tells you which half of your knowledge to reach for and it eliminates options accordingly.

5464
09:03:01,742 --> 09:03:08,644
The call out is the fastest elimination available in this whole domain and it holds with very few exceptions.

5465
09:03:09,408 --> 09:03:15,076
Roles issue temporary credentials that rotate automatically and expire on their own.

5466
09:03:15,568 --> 09:03:21,316
That means there is nothing to leak, nothing to rotate, and nothing to leave in a configuration file.

5467
09:03:22,254 --> 09:03:28,368
An IAM user carries an access key and a secret key that live until somebody removes them.

5468
09:03:28,608 --> 09:03:33,220
That is exactly the material that ends up in a repository or a job parameter.

5469
09:03:34,222 --> 09:03:38,916
So when a question offers creating a user with programmatic access for a pipeline.

5470
09:03:39,854 --> 09:03:42,116
Another offers attaching a role.

5471
09:03:42,510 --> 09:03:45,412
The role is the answer nearly every time.

5472
09:03:47,086 --> 09:03:50,208
Root deserves a slide because it is the one identity.

5473
09:03:50,446 --> 09:03:56,868
None of this module controls apply to. Service control policies do not restrict the management accounts root user.

5474
09:03:57,248 --> 09:03:59,108
Permissions boundaries do not apply.

5475
09:03:59,918 --> 09:04:05,764
There is no policy you can write that constrains it, which is why the advice is always the same.

5476
09:04:06,208 --> 09:04:13,828
Set up multi-factor authentication, which AWS now enforces, record the credentials somewhere safe and do not use it.

5477
09:04:14,368 --> 09:04:17,728
The alarming point on the fourth bullet is the practical control.

5478
09:04:18,702 --> 09:04:22,756
Route activity should be rare enough that any occurrence is worth a notification.

5479
09:04:23,470 --> 09:04:27,716
That makes it one of the easiest high-value alarms to configure in an account.

5480
09:04:29,390 --> 09:04:33,220
Module 7 promised this module would cover why in detail.

5481
09:04:33,518 --> 09:04:35,044
So here it is properly.

5482
09:04:36,302 --> 09:04:41,412
An access key in a script is a permanent credential setting where it can be copied,

5483
09:04:42,190 --> 09:04:48,356
committed to a repository, printed in a log or simply read by anybody who can view the job definition.

5484
09:04:49,328 --> 09:04:56,164
It does not expire, so a leak from three years ago is still valid today unless somebody happened to notice and revoke it.

5485
09:04:56,688 --> 09:05:03,812
It carries no context, so nothing in CloudRail records which application was using it, only that the key was.

5486
09:05:04,558 --> 09:05:08,452
And rotating it means finding every copy anybody ever made.

5487
09:05:09,262 --> 09:05:20,452
A role solves all four problems simultaneously. That is because the credentials are issued on demand, expire within hours, are tied to a named role in the audit trail, never exist in a file at all.

5488
09:05:22,190 --> 09:05:31,748
Scale 4.1.4 names, creating IAM rules for Lambda, API Gateway, the CLI, and CloudFormation.

5489
09:05:33,006 --> 09:05:35,524
The pattern is examinable in its own right.

5490
09:05:36,622 --> 09:05:44,708
Every AWS service that acts on your behalf assumes a role you define, and the credentials arrive without ever being written down.

5491
09:05:45,326 --> 09:05:51,908
The last bullet is the boundary that Module 7's fourth scenario turned on, and it is worth stating clearly.

5492
09:05:52,528 --> 09:05:55,236
A role authenticates you to AWS.

5493
09:05:55,886 --> 09:06:02,468
It cannot authenticate you to a partner's API, a third-party database, or a SAAS platform.

5494
09:06:02,928 --> 09:06:06,084
That is because those systems have never heard of IAM.

5495
09:06:06,862 --> 09:06:10,884
Those credentials have to be stored and the next section covers where.

5496
09:06:12,654 --> 09:06:15,651
The second bullet contains a consequence people miss.

5497
09:06:16,571 --> 09:06:20,971
Granting a wildcard on a service does not merely grant the actions that exist today.

5498
09:06:21,582 --> 09:06:28,004
It grants every action AWS adds to that service in future without anyone reviewing

5499
09:06:28,048 --> 09:06:34,372
them. That is why a policy written three years ago can be broader now than when it was approved.

5500
09:06:35,630 --> 09:06:39,968
The condition bullet is where domain 4 gets interesting.

5501
09:06:40,462 --> 09:06:49,732
A condition can require that a request arise through a particular VPC endpoint or carries a particular tag or that an upload specifies encryption.

5502
09:06:50,288 --> 09:06:57,476
Those turn a permission into a permission with a context, which is how the data perimeter from the evaluation slide is actually built.

5503
09:06:59,054 --> 09:07:05,956
The call out describes the mechanic that catches people and it appears in questions as a failure that looks inexplicable.

5504
09:07:06,638 --> 09:07:14,052
Within one account, an identity policy alone is enough because the account implicitly trusts its own principles.

5505
09:07:14,734 --> 09:07:16,996
Across accounts, both sides must agree.

5506
09:07:17,934 --> 09:07:24,452
The bucket policy in the owning account must name the other account's role, and that role's own policy must permit the action.

5507
09:07:25,006 --> 09:07:30,404
Grant only one and access is denied with an error that does not say which half is missing.

5508
09:07:31,470 --> 09:07:39,652
So a question describing cross account access that has been configured and still fails is usually describing exactly one of the two being present.

5509
09:07:41,102 --> 09:07:45,764
The two policy distinction is the one to internalize because error messages depend on it.

5510
09:07:46,096 --> 09:07:50,692
If the trust policy does not name the caller, the assumed role call itself fails.

5511
09:07:51,406 --> 09:07:55,136
The error is about assuming rather than about the action you intended.

5512
09:07:55,662 --> 09:08:05,216
If the trust policy is right and the permissions policy is missing something, the assumption succeeds and the subsequent action is denied.

5513
09:08:05,422 --> 09:08:13,156
Those are different symptoms pointing at different files and a question describing which error appeared is telling you where to look.

5514
09:08:14,094 --> 09:08:20,676
The external identifier on the fourth bullet matters when a third party assumes a role in your account.

5515
09:08:21,198 --> 09:08:25,124
That is how many SAAS integrations work.

5516
09:08:26,734 --> 09:08:34,692
An AWS Glue job reads from Amazon S3, writes to Amazon Redshift, and calls a partner API for exchange rates.

5517
09:08:36,366 --> 09:08:44,004
The job currently holds an AWS access key and secret key as job parameters plus the partner's API key in the same place.

5518
09:08:44,782 --> 09:08:54,564
A security review has required that no long-lived AWS credentials exist anywhere and that the partner credential can be rotated without editing the job.

5519
09:08:55,118 --> 09:08:57,316
What should the data engineer implement?

5520
09:08:58,190 --> 09:09:04,036
a move all three credentials into AWS Secrets Manager and read them at job start.

5521
09:09:04,718 --> 09:09:04,976
B

5522
09:09:06,158 --> 09:09:10,276
Attach an IAM role to the blue job for AWS access.

5523
09:09:10,702 --> 09:09:16,612
Store the partner API key in AWS Secrets Manager with rotation enabled.

5524
09:09:17,136 --> 09:09:17,376
C.

5525
09:09:17,966 --> 09:09:21,936
Move all three credentials into Systems Manager parameter.

5526
09:09:22,176 --> 09:09:24,868
Store as secure string parameters.

5527
09:09:25,486 --> 09:09:32,164
D Rotate the August access keys every 30 days using a scheduled lambda function.

5528
09:09:32,846 --> 09:09:34,468
Pause and decide.

5529
09:09:41,134 --> 09:09:45,092
The answer is B and it delivers the promise module 7 made.

5530
09:09:45,870 --> 09:09:50,052
The question is built so that treating both credentials the same way fails.

5531
09:09:51,374 --> 09:10:01,412
Options A and C do exactly that, moving everything into a store which is better than job parameters and still leaves permanent AWS keys in existence.

5532
09:10:02,382 --> 09:10:09,828
The requirement said none should exist, and a role is the only thing that satisfies that because there is no credential to store.

5533
09:10:11,342 --> 09:10:18,820
The partner key is genuinely different because no IAM role can represent an identity in somebody else's system.

5534
09:10:19,758 --> 09:10:21,508
So it has to live in a store.

5535
09:10:22,190 --> 09:10:25,092
And secrets manager is the one that rotates.

5536
09:10:26,894 --> 09:10:32,256
This slide exists because there is a middle option. People forget between a role and a stored secret.

5537
09:10:32,416 --> 09:10:42,276
Several databases can accept an IAM identity directly, issuing a short-lived token in place of a password. That means there is no credential to store or rotate at all.

5538
09:10:42,798 --> 09:10:45,732
Amazon RDS and Aurora support this.

5539
09:10:59,056 --> 09:10:59,296
So

5540
09:10:59,456 --> 09:11:08,452
when a question describes wanting to remove a database password entirely rather than store it more safely, that is the capability being pointed at.

5541
09:11:09,710 --> 09:11:15,076
The callout is the argument that matters organizationally and it appears in questions about scale.

5542
09:11:15,630 --> 09:11:22,976
When each engineer has an IAM user in each of 12 accounts, removing somebody who leaves means finding 12 users.

5543
09:11:23,566 --> 09:11:29,412
The one somebody misses is a permanent credential belonging to a person who no longer works there.

5544
09:11:30,798 --> 09:11:32,420
Federation inverts that.

5545
09:11:52,366 --> 09:11:57,604
The service control policy point is the one people get wrong. So state it precisely.

5546
09:11:58,064 --> 09:12:00,996
An SCP does not grant permissions to anybody.

5547
09:12:01,184 --> 09:12:07,332
It defines the maximum set of permissions that identity policies in that account are allowed to grant.

5548
09:12:08,078 --> 09:12:13,732
So a permission must be allowed by both the SCP and an identity policy to take effect.

5549
09:12:14,382 --> 09:12:21,860
Resource control policies arrived in late 2024 and do the equivalent job for resource-based policies, which closes a gap.

5550
09:12:22,670 --> 09:12:28,164
Together they are how organizations build what AWS calls a data perimeter.

5551
09:12:29,070 --> 09:12:36,644
Only identities from within the organization can reach sensitive resources, whatever an individual account's policies say.

5552
09:12:38,318 --> 09:12:41,252
A data lake bucket lives in a central account.

5553
09:12:41,744 --> 09:12:46,212
An analytics team in a second account needs read access from a GRU job.

5554
09:12:46,766 --> 09:12:51,021
The bucket policy has been updated to allow the second account's Blue role.

5555
09:12:51,662 --> 09:12:55,364
and the job still fails with an access denied error on get object.

5556
09:12:56,270 --> 09:13:01,892
The Blue role in the second account has a policy granting Blue Star and LogStar only.

5557
09:13:02,414 --> 09:13:06,372
There are no service control policies restricting Amazon S3.

5558
09:13:08,174 --> 09:13:09,732
What is the most likely cause?

5559
09:13:10,446 --> 09:13:16,068
a The bucket policy must name the second account's root user rather than the role.

5560
09:13:16,846 --> 09:13:17,104
B

5561
09:13:18,414 --> 09:13:21,988
The view role's own policy does not allow S3 get object.

5562
09:13:22,224 --> 09:13:24,580
Cross account access needs both sides.

5563
09:13:26,670 --> 09:13:31,588
C. Cross account access to Amazon S3 requires an S3 access point.

5564
09:13:32,270 --> 09:13:36,144
D. The bucket must be made public for another account to read it.

5565
09:13:36,910 --> 09:13:38,148
Pause and decide.

5566
09:13:44,846 --> 09:13:49,796
The answer is B and the question hands it to you by quoting the roles permissions.

5567
09:13:50,286 --> 09:13:52,864
View and logs with no mention of S3.

5568
09:13:53,344 --> 09:13:56,388
That is the half of the cross account requirement that is missing.

5569
09:13:56,864 --> 09:14:01,636
The reason this makes a good exam question is that the bucket policy was updated.

5570
09:14:02,222 --> 09:14:06,084
That feels like the harder half and creates the sense that the work is done.

5571
09:14:07,566 --> 09:14:11,268
Option D is worth naming as the answer to reject on principle.

5572
09:14:12,014 --> 09:14:18,756
Making a bucket public to solve a cross account access problem is the shape the exam marks wrong every time.

5573
09:14:19,598 --> 09:14:21,732
It appears more often than you might expect.

5574
09:14:23,531 --> 09:14:29,184
Scale 4.1.3 names creating and rotating credentials with AWS Secrets Manager.

5575
09:14:29,710 --> 09:14:32,384
Scale 4.2.2 names, storing them.

5576
09:14:32,622 --> 09:14:35,812
So the topic appears in two separate task statements.

5577
09:14:36,462 --> 09:14:40,900
The framing that keeps it straight is the one from the earlier slide about identities.

5578
09:14:41,774 --> 09:14:47,620
Rules cover anything AWS itself can authenticate, which is most of what a data pipeline touches.

5579
09:14:48,078 --> 09:14:49,764
Everything else has to be stored.

5580
09:14:52,014 --> 09:15:00,384
A partner, API key, a database password for an engine without IA, AM authentication, ASAAS

5581
09:15:00,624 --> 09:15:01,184
token.

5582
09:15:01,966 --> 09:15:04,836
The question then becomes which store to use?

5583
09:15:05,104 --> 09:15:11,824
And the answer turns on a single capability more than any other, which the next slide covers.

5584
09:15:13,166 --> 09:15:16,580
Both hold values, your code reads at runtime,

5585
09:15:17,326 --> 09:15:23,268
both encrypt with AWSKMS, and both are perfectly respectable.

5586
09:15:23,918 --> 09:15:27,108
Rotation is what separates them for exam purposes.

5587
09:15:28,014 --> 09:15:37,828
Secrets manager can change a credential on a schedule, updating the stored value and for supported databases, the credential in the database at the same time.

5588
09:15:38,064 --> 09:15:40,228
So nothing has to be edited anywhere.

5589
09:15:40,942 --> 09:15:42,980
Parameter store has no equivalent.

5590
09:15:43,310 --> 09:15:45,220
That makes the decision mechanical.

5591
09:15:46,094 --> 09:15:48,612
A rotating credential is secrets manager.

5592
09:15:49,262 --> 09:15:55,524
A bucket name, a threshold, or an environment flag is a parameter store parameter and it is free.

5593
09:15:57,230 --> 09:16:03,876
The last bullet is the practical failure and it makes a fail question because it looks like an optimization.

5594
09:16:04,750 --> 09:16:09,348
Reading a secret once at startup avoids an API call per operation.

5595
09:16:09,774 --> 09:16:17,380
That seems sensible until the secret rotates and every long-lived process is holding a credential that no longer works.

5596
09:16:18,190 --> 09:16:26,144
The pattern that works is to cache with a short lifetime and refresh, or to fetch on a failure and retry.

5597
09:16:26,862 --> 09:16:32,996
The built in rotation on the second bullet is worth remembering because it covers the database engines.

5598
09:16:33,550 --> 09:16:35,812
A data engineer actually meets.

5599
09:16:36,782 --> 09:16:41,476
So, a question about rotating an Amazon RDS password has a fully managed answer.

5600
09:16:42,990 --> 09:16:51,780
Module 2 established that a great many blue failures are network problems rather than code problems, and the same is true across this whole domain.

5601
09:16:52,336 --> 09:16:59,076
A perfectly correct rule with perfectly correct policies still fails if the request cannot physically reach the service.

5602
09:17:00,014 --> 09:17:03,296
The errors in that situation are famously unhelpful.

5603
09:17:03,854 --> 09:17:08,644
The exam guide names updating VPC security groups as its own skill.

5604
09:17:09,486 --> 09:17:16,836
Creating endpoints as part of another, so this is examinable in its own right rather than as background context.

5605
09:17:17,326 --> 09:17:24,964
The next few slides cover the pieces, and most of them have already appeared somewhere in this course in a service-specific form.

5606
09:17:25,296 --> 09:17:28,868
That means this is consolidation rather than new material.

5607
09:17:30,510 --> 09:17:36,676
The stateful point is what makes security groups easy to reason about, and it is the detailed questions test.

5608
09:17:37,056 --> 09:17:43,588
Allow an inbound connection on a port and the response is permitted automatically because the group remembers the connection.

5609
09:17:44,366 --> 09:17:53,296
A network, ACL does not remember anything, so allowing inbound traffic without also allowing the outbound reply produces a connection that half

5610
09:17:53,536 --> 09:17:53,828
works.

5611
09:17:54,638 --> 09:18:00,196
The self-referencing capability on the fourth bullet is the one module too needed.

5612
09:18:01,056 --> 09:18:09,124
That is because Blue's own workers talk to each other and the security group must therefore allow traffic from itself.

5613
09:18:10,670 --> 09:18:16,708
This division answers a large share of network questions quickly and it is not obvious from the service names.

5614
09:18:16,974 --> 09:18:24,708
Some services are deployed into your subnets, so they have private addresses and security groups, and network isolation is the default state.

5615
09:18:25,486 --> 09:18:30,980
Amazon MSK, Amazon RDS, and EMR clusters all work this way.

5616
09:18:31,216 --> 09:18:42,052
Others are regional services you reach across the network. So by default, that traffic goes to a public endpoint even from a private subnet, and an interface endpoint is what changes that.

5617
09:18:42,702 --> 09:18:45,636
The blue and lambda row is the one that catches people.

5618
09:18:45,966 --> 09:18:52,836
That is because both run outside your VPC until you deliberately configure otherwise, which module 2 covered.

5619
09:18:54,416 --> 09:19:02,724
This table consolidates pieces that modules 2 and 4 introduce separately, and the call out is the detail that decided module 2's second scenario.

5620
09:19:02,896 --> 09:19:10,820
A gateway endpoint appears in the console as a thing you created. It has no effect until the subnet's root table sends S3 traffic to it.

5621
09:19:11,616 --> 09:19:18,736
That is why a question about a correctly created endpoint that does not work is almost always a root table question.

5622
09:19:19,246 --> 09:19:27,856
Interface endpoints behave differently, being genuine network interfaces with private addresses, which is also why they carry an hourly charge where

5623
09:19:28,016 --> 09:19:29,572
gateway endpoints do not.

5624
09:19:31,214 --> 09:19:37,668
The endpoint policy on the third bullet is worth knowing because it is a permission check people forget exists.

5625
09:19:39,086 --> 09:19:44,932
An interface endpoint can carry a policy limiting which resources may be accessed through it.

5626
09:19:45,870 --> 09:19:53,668
So a request can satisfy the identity policy and the bucket policy and still be refused because the endpoint does not permit that bucket.

5627
09:19:54,254 --> 09:19:58,436
That is deliberate and it is a core part of building a data perimeter.

5628
09:19:58,926 --> 09:20:03,684
That is because it means traffic leaving your VPC can only reach approved destinations.

5629
09:20:04,590 --> 09:20:11,012
It also means troubleshooting an access denied error involves one more file than most people check.

5630
09:20:12,622 --> 09:20:20,836
The three directions in the call out are the structure worth remembering because a question describing a regulated environment usually names one of them.

5631
09:20:21,998 --> 09:20:29,156
Resource control policies stop an identity from outside the organization using your resources, even if some account granted them.

5632
09:20:30,158 --> 09:20:40,132
Endpoint policies stop workloads inside your network reaching resources you did not approve, which is what the third worked scenario in this module turns on.

5633
09:20:40,494 --> 09:20:46,596
And conditions on the source VPC stop your own credentials being used from somewhere unexpected.

5634
09:20:47,438 --> 09:20:50,564
That is the control that limits the damage of a leaked credential.

5635
09:20:51,534 --> 09:20:55,332
Block public access is the blank version applied once at the account level.

5636
09:20:56,974 --> 09:21:01,764
Module 8 raised access points and promised the detail here.

5637
09:21:02,478 --> 09:21:04,656
The problem they solve is 1.

5638
09:21:04,942 --> 09:21:07,012
Every large data lake develops.

5639
09:21:07,822 --> 09:21:12,420
A bucket policy that started clean acquires a clause per consuming team.

5640
09:21:13,390 --> 09:21:19,108
After two years, nobody can safely change it because nobody knows which clause protects what.

5641
09:21:19,982 --> 09:21:24,976
An access point gives each consumer its own named endpoint with its own policy.

5642
09:21:25,136 --> 09:21:30,212
So the bucket policy stays simple and each team's access can be reasoned about independently.

5643
09:21:32,046 --> 09:21:35,492
The VPC restriction on the third bullet is the strong version.

5644
09:21:36,096 --> 09:21:43,396
That is because an access point limited to a VPC makes that path unreachable from anywhere else.

5645
09:21:45,006 --> 09:21:49,856
A financial services company runs blue jobs in private subnets.

5646
09:21:50,446 --> 09:21:54,736
A compliance requirement states that no data may traverse the public internet.

5647
09:21:55,534 --> 09:22:01,572
That jobs must be able to reach only two, named S3 buckets, not any bucket in AWS.

5648
09:22:02,318 --> 09:22:10,468
The subnet's currently route outbound traffic through an NAT gateway and the jobs reach Amazon S3 over the public endpoint.

5649
09:22:10,926 --> 09:22:13,060
What should the data engineer implement?

5650
09:22:13,486 --> 09:22:20,964
a add an S3 gateway endpoint with an endpoint policy naming the two buckets and remove the NAT route for S3.

5651
09:22:22,830 --> 09:22:27,108
Keep the NAT gateway and add a bucket policy on each of the two buckets.

5652
09:22:27,536 --> 09:22:27,856
C.

5653
09:22:28,366 --> 09:22:33,412
Add an interface endpoint for Amazon S3 and rely on the Blue Rolls IAM policy.

5654
09:22:34,158 --> 09:22:38,468
D. Move the blue jobs to public subnets so the routing is simpler.

5655
09:22:40,494 --> 09:22:41,572
Pause and decide.

5656
09:22:48,078 --> 09:22:51,776
The answer is A and option C is the instructive near miss.

5657
09:22:54,478 --> 09:23:03,780
An interface endpoint does keep traffic off the public internet, so it satisfies the first requirement. For S3, a gateway endpoint is the conventional and free choice.

5658
09:23:05,006 --> 09:23:07,364
Where C fails is the second requirement.

5659
09:23:08,270 --> 09:23:15,812
An IAM policy on the view role controls what that role may do, and somebody with permission to edit the role can widen it.

5660
09:23:17,614 --> 09:23:24,068
An endpoint policy controls what can be reached through that network path at all, regardless of identity.

5661
09:23:25,006 --> 09:23:29,412
That is what a data perimeter means and what the compliance requirement is describing.

5662
09:23:30,990 --> 09:23:40,932
Skill 4.1.6 names this explicitly, and the useful framing is that choosing a managed service shrinks your responsibility rather than removing it.

5663
09:23:41,296 --> 09:23:47,524
With Amazon MSK, AWS patches the brokers and you configure the authentication and the network.

5664
09:23:47,776 --> 09:23:50,788
With Kafka on EC2, both are yours.

5665
09:23:51,726 --> 09:23:58,244
With Blue, there is no instance to patch at all, and you still decide which role the job assumes and which buckets it may read.

5666
09:23:58,736 --> 09:24:00,612
The last bullet is the constant.

5667
09:24:01,006 --> 09:24:07,780
No service anywhere in this course decides who should see your data, and that decision is always yours to express in policy.

5668
09:24:09,518 --> 09:24:16,016
The call out describes the capability worth knowing because least privilege is easy to agree with and hard to implement.

5669
09:24:16,302 --> 09:24:22,372
Nobody knows exactly which API cause a job makes, so the safe thing is to grant broadly and move on.

5670
09:24:23,310 --> 09:24:31,844
Access analyzer can read Cloud Trail history for a role and generate a policy containing only the actions it actually used, which turns a guess into evidence.

5671
09:24:32,016 --> 09:24:36,036
The unused access findings on the second bullet address the other half.

5672
09:24:36,736 --> 09:24:41,700
That is, that permissions granted for a project three years ago are still there.

5673
09:24:42,096 --> 09:24:45,028
And the last bullet explains why that matters.

5674
09:24:45,582 --> 09:24:47,812
Nothing removes them automatically.

5675
09:24:49,518 --> 09:24:54,084
A company has 12 AWS accounts and around 40 engineers.

5676
09:24:54,958 --> 09:24:58,308
Each engineer has an IAM user in every account.

5677
09:24:58,894 --> 09:25:02,468
They work in with access keys for CLI use.

5678
09:25:03,310 --> 09:25:08,176
Three people have left in the past year and the security team is not confident.

5679
09:25:08,496 --> 09:25:10,436
All their credentials were removed.

5680
09:25:11,310 --> 09:25:17,092
The company wants access tied to employment and wants to stop issuing long-lived keys.

5681
09:25:17,376 --> 09:25:19,428
What should the company implement?

5682
09:25:19,918 --> 09:25:26,256
a A scheduled Lambda function that deletes IAM users not seen in 90 days.

5683
09:25:26,576 --> 09:25:26,816
b

5684
09:25:27,854 --> 09:25:35,456
AWS IAM Identity Center federated to the corporate directory with permission sets granting rules per account.

5685
09:25:35,696 --> 09:25:44,964
C. Enforce a 90 day access key rotation policy across all accounts. D. Consolidate the 12 accounts into one. So there is a single set of users.

5686
09:25:45,326 --> 09:25:46,468
Pause and decide.

5687
09:25:53,168 --> 09:25:59,236
The answer is B, and the clause that decides it is access tied to employment.

5688
09:26:00,782 --> 09:26:06,148
That is a statement about where identity should live, and only federation satisfies it.

5689
09:26:07,054 --> 09:26:13,284
That is because access follows from the directory account rather than from 12 independent copies.

5690
09:26:14,222 --> 09:26:20,996
Option C is worth respecting as a genuine security improvement that answers neither stated requirement.

5691
09:26:21,614 --> 09:26:28,164
Rotating a key every 90 days reduces the window of a leak and leaves a long-lived credential in existence.

5692
09:26:28,718 --> 09:26:33,008
A departed engineer's key remains valid until somebody removes it.

5693
09:26:33,568 --> 09:26:40,452
This is the same shape as module 14's alum, where a sensible control simply cannot detect the situation described.

5694
09:26:41,902 --> 09:26:46,276
The fourth row deserves the emphasis because it produces the longest investigations.

5695
09:26:46,702 --> 09:26:54,884
An engineer reads the roles policy, reads the bucket policy, confirms both, allow the action, and the request is still denied.

5696
09:26:55,502 --> 09:26:58,340
The explanation is usually somewhere they did not look.

5697
09:26:59,246 --> 09:27:06,564
A service control policy, a resource control policy, or a VPC endpoint policy can each deny a request.

5698
09:27:06,928 --> 09:27:10,884
They do so even when identity and resource policies both permit it.

5699
09:27:11,534 --> 09:27:18,308
Knowing that those three exist and that an explicit deny anywhere wins turns a mystery into a checklist.

5700
09:27:19,310 --> 09:27:24,068
The last row is module 4's allow list problem appearing as a symptom.

5701
09:27:25,678 --> 09:27:29,732
5 questions and the first is the fastest elimination in domain 4.

5702
09:27:30,958 --> 09:27:37,636
That is because an option storing AWS access keys is wrong regardless of where it stores them.

5703
09:27:38,190 --> 09:27:40,580
The fourth is the one that costs people time.

5704
09:27:41,614 --> 09:27:47,396
An access denied error and a connection timeout are both described as the job cannot reach the data.

5705
09:27:47,808 --> 09:27:49,796
They have entirely different answers.

5706
09:27:50,574 --> 09:27:55,140
Reading for whether the request was refused or never arrived is what separates them.

5707
09:27:56,046 --> 09:28:07,248
Next module is authorization, which covers what an authenticated identity is allowed to touch, including the column and row level controls that lake

5708
09:28:07,408 --> 09:28:08,484
formation provides.

5709
09:28:10,254 --> 09:28:19,991
Module 17 covered who a request is. This module covers what that identity is then permitted to touch, and it is where several earlier modules deferred their hardest question.

5710
09:28:20,558 --> 09:28:24,452
Module 2 showed a scenario needing column level permissions and pointed here.

5711
09:28:25,230 --> 09:28:28,128
Module 8 promised tag-based access control.

5712
09:28:28,558 --> 09:28:35,268
Modules 10, 12, and 13 each deferred a fine-grained access question to this module. All of them get answered.

5713
09:28:35,950 --> 09:28:38,308
6 skills from the exam guide land here.

5714
09:28:39,118 --> 09:28:45,604
The central one is that IAM alone cannot express column and row level control over a data lake.

5715
09:28:45,902 --> 09:28:49,220
That is precisely why AWS lake formation exists.

5716
09:28:50,928 --> 09:28:57,488
This single sentence explains why lake formation exists at all and it is the most useful thing in this module.

5717
09:28:57,728 --> 09:29:02,244
An IARM policy grants actions on resources named by ARN.

5718
09:29:02,368 --> 09:29:07,236
So it can allow reading a bucket, a prefix, a dynamo DB table, or a blue job.

5719
09:29:07,982 --> 09:29:15,460
It has no vocabulary whatsoever for allowing 11 of a table's 14 columns or for allowing only the rows where the region happens to be Europe.

5720
09:29:15,808 --> 09:29:17,248
Those are not resources.

5721
09:29:17,408 --> 09:29:23,108
They are parts of the contents of a resource, and IAM does not reach inside an object to inspect what is in it.

5722
09:29:23,822 --> 09:29:32,164
Every fine-grained requirement this course has raised has run into that same wall, and lake formation is the layer built specifically to get past it.

5723
09:29:33,710 --> 09:29:37,188
Reading down this table, the unit of control gets finer.

5724
09:29:38,128 --> 09:29:41,444
IAM sees objects and prefixes.

5725
09:29:42,030 --> 09:29:49,408
Lake Formation sees the structure inside them because it works through the Blue Data catalog where columns and rows are described.

5726
09:29:49,902 --> 09:29:57,988
Redshift has its own grant system for data inside the warehouse, which is separate from Lake Formation and does a similar job in a different place.

5727
09:29:59,758 --> 09:30:06,980
And the business intelligence layer has its own controls again. Which module 13 flag does a copy that needs its own rules?

5728
09:30:07,822 --> 09:30:15,108
A question asking to hide a column from one team is asking which of these layers can express that. The answer is not the first two.

5729
09:30:16,526 --> 09:30:22,624
Skill 4.2.5 names creating custom, i.e., AM policies for user roles.

5730
09:30:22,944 --> 09:30:26,884
So the structure is worth knowing even though you will not write one in the exam.

5731
09:30:27,534 --> 09:30:30,224
The four common elements are straightforward.

5732
09:30:30,702 --> 09:30:37,124
Effect, action, resource, and in a resource-based policy, also a principle saying who it applies to.

5733
09:30:37,550 --> 09:30:44,228
The condition element is the interesting one because it is what turns a policy from a static grant into a contextual one.

5734
09:30:44,878 --> 09:30:49,476
A condition can require that the request arrived through a specific VPC endpoint.

5735
09:30:50,064 --> 09:30:55,172
Or that the object carries a particular tag, or that an upload specifies encryption.

5736
09:30:55,584 --> 09:30:59,460
That is how the data perimeter from module 17 is actually expressed.

5737
09:31:01,070 --> 09:31:06,820
These are the condition keys a data engineer actually meets and two are worth dwelling on properly.

5738
09:31:07,264 --> 09:31:14,948
The organization identifier condition is the simplest way to ensure a resource can only be used by principles from inside your own organization.

5739
09:31:15,758 --> 09:31:19,716
That means an accidental grant to an outside account has no effect.

5740
09:31:20,302 --> 09:31:23,524
That is a genuine safety net rather than a theoretical one.

5741
09:31:24,622 --> 09:31:30,596
The encryption condition is preventive rather than detective, and module 19 returns to it.

5742
09:31:31,534 --> 09:31:35,428
You could audit a bucket afterwards to find objects encrypted the wrong way.

5743
09:31:35,950 --> 09:31:36,516
Better.

5744
09:31:36,942 --> 09:31:40,932
The bucket policy refuses any upload that does not name the required key.

5745
09:31:41,344 --> 09:31:44,228
So the wrong thing can never be written in the first place.

5746
09:31:45,966 --> 09:31:51,428
The practical guidance is on the fourth bullet, and the reason is auditability rather than tidiness.

5747
09:31:51,744 --> 09:31:58,404
A customer managed policy exists once, is attached to whatever needs it and can be reviewed in a single place.

5748
09:31:58,864 --> 09:32:10,468
The same rules copied in line into nine different roles have to be reviewed nine times. They will drift apart because somebody will amend one of them under pressure and not the others.

5749
09:32:11,438 --> 09:32:15,940
The last bullet is what appears in exam questions as the tempting shortcut.

5750
09:32:16,782 --> 09:32:26,148
Attaching an AWS managed full access policy makes a pipeline work immediately and grants every action on every resource in that service.

5751
09:32:26,510 --> 09:32:33,444
It is the finding that turns up in a security review two years later, long after the person who applied it has moved on.

5752
09:32:34,990 --> 09:32:40,644
The third bullet is the security problem this solves and it is not obvious until stated.

5753
09:32:41,326 --> 09:32:48,868
Granting a team the ability to create IAM roles for their own pipelines seems reasonable and is a privilege escalation.

5754
09:32:49,838 --> 09:32:55,744
That is because nothing stops them creating a role with full administrator permissions and assuming it.

5755
09:32:56,302 --> 09:33:02,276
A permissions boundary caps the maximum any role they create can hold, so the delegation becomes safe.

5756
09:33:03,054 --> 09:33:10,532
The distinction from a service control policy on the last bullet is worth holding because both are ceilings rather than grants.

5757
09:33:11,150 --> 09:33:18,660
An SCP caps an entire account and a boundary caps an individual role and questions about safe delegation want the second.

5758
09:33:20,302 --> 09:33:28,708
The mechanism is worth understanding properly because it explains both the power and the limitation that the second work scenario turns on.

5759
09:33:30,350 --> 09:33:34,628
Lake Formation does not sit in front of Amazon S3 inspecting every request.

5760
09:33:35,566 --> 09:33:44,132
It holds permissions against Vue Data Catalog databases, tables and columns, and the query engines consulted before returning anything.

5761
09:33:44,622 --> 09:33:53,156
Athena, Redshift Spectrum, Amazon EMR, and Blue all ask Lake Formation what this particular principle is allowed to see.

5762
09:33:54,062 --> 09:34:01,156
That is why permissions are enforced consistently across every engine from a single grant, which is the real benefit.

5763
09:34:01,424 --> 09:34:11,460
It is also exactly why module 2's healthcare scenario mattered. That is because a job reading files from S3 directly is not asking Lake Formation anything at all.

5764
09:34:13,006 --> 09:34:19,748
The four levels are worth learning in order because questions describe requirements in exactly these terms.

5765
09:34:20,398 --> 09:34:22,704
Table level is ordinary access control.

5766
09:34:23,726 --> 09:34:32,740
Column level answers the most common real requirement, which is that a table is useful to many teams, but three of its columns are personal data.

5767
09:34:33,614 --> 09:34:40,932
Row level answers the regional and tenant cases where everyone queries one table and each sees only their own rows.

5768
09:34:41,454 --> 09:34:49,636
Cell level combines them, and the mechanism for both row and cell is a data filter, which names the columns and carries a filter expression.

5769
09:34:50,510 --> 09:34:54,596
The iceberg support on the last bullet connects this to module 9.

5770
09:34:56,302 --> 09:34:58,532
Two of these are worth separating carefully.

5771
09:34:59,374 --> 09:35:04,996
Describe let's a principle see that a table exists and view its schema without reading a single row.

5772
09:35:05,774 --> 09:35:10,532
That is what makes a catalog browsable to people who cannot see most of the data in it.

5773
09:35:11,342 --> 09:35:19,136
That matters for the Discovery Workflow module well described, because an analyst needs to find a dataset before requesting access to it.

5774
09:35:19,376 --> 09:35:23,428
Data location access is the one that catches people building pipelines.

5775
09:35:24,302 --> 09:35:29,892
That is because a job writing to a registered location needs it in addition to the table permissions.

5776
09:35:30,542 --> 09:35:34,436
Its absence produces a failure that looks like an S3 problem.

5777
09:35:36,174 --> 09:35:43,972
The call out is the organizational problem this addresses and it connects directly to module 12 subscription workflow.

5778
09:35:44,976 --> 09:35:48,004
A data lake administrator is a powerful role.

5779
09:35:48,622 --> 09:35:54,404
Suppose every access request in a 900-table estate must pass through the two people who hold it.

5780
09:35:55,310 --> 09:36:06,180
The platform team becomes a bottleneck and analysts wait days for access to data that was always meant for them. Grantable permissions let a data owner pass access on for the datasets they own.

5781
09:36:06,830 --> 09:36:11,076
That distributes the decision to whoever actually knows whether it should be allowed.

5782
09:36:11,598 --> 09:36:14,816
The last bullet is the separation of duties.

5783
09:36:15,310 --> 09:36:21,860
Argument keeping the ability to define what a tag means, apart from the ability to grant against it.

5784
09:36:23,502 --> 09:36:28,516
Module 8, promised tag-based access control, and this slide is it.

5785
09:36:29,358 --> 09:36:35,172
The arithmetic in the call out comes from AWS's own documentation and it makes the case efficiently.

5786
09:36:36,142 --> 09:36:46,116
Named grants multiply as principles times resources. So a growing lake produces a permissions estate that nobody can audit and everybody is afraid to change.

5787
09:36:46,798 --> 09:36:54,820
With tags, you label a table as containing personal data or as belonging to the finance domain, grant principles against those labels once.

5788
09:36:55,630 --> 09:37:02,416
A table created next month carrying the same labels is automatically covered with no action at all.

5789
09:37:02,990 --> 09:37:04,996
The cost sits on the last bullet.

5790
09:37:05,806 --> 09:37:12,772
That is because a tag scheme only works if the organization agrees what the tags mean and applies them consistently to everything.

5791
09:37:14,478 --> 09:37:19,268
The second bullet is worth stating clearly because three different tagging systems appear in this course.

5792
09:37:19,758 --> 09:37:24,036
Object tags in module 8, which drive lifecycle rules and cost allocation.

5793
09:37:25,646 --> 09:37:29,092
IAM tags which appear in policy conditions.

5794
09:37:29,966 --> 09:37:34,308
And LF tags which are lake formation's own and are managed inside it.

5795
09:37:34,798 --> 09:37:39,268
They are not interchangeable and a question naming one is not talking about the others.

5796
09:37:39,616 --> 09:37:43,844
The fourth and fifth bullets are the constraints that shape a tag ontology.

5797
09:37:44,302 --> 09:37:49,540
One value per key means a table belongs to one domain rather than several.

5798
09:37:50,094 --> 09:37:54,896
Multiple tags in one grant behave as an AND rather than an OR.

5799
09:37:56,590 --> 09:38:04,164
A hospital stores patient records in Amazon S3, catalogued in AWS GUI and queried with Amazon Athena.

5800
09:38:04,736 --> 09:38:12,132
A research team must analyze the clinical columns but must never see patient name, address, or national identifier.

5801
09:38:12,910 --> 09:38:20,132
A data engineer proposes writing an IAM policy for the research team's role that denies access to those three columns.

5802
09:38:20,416 --> 09:38:23,396
Why will this not work and what should be done instead?

5803
09:38:24,416 --> 09:38:24,576
A.

5804
09:38:25,216 --> 09:38:28,900
IAAM cannot express column level permissions.

5805
09:38:29,216 --> 09:38:32,900
Use lake formation column level grants or a data filter.

5806
09:38:34,336 --> 09:38:34,656
B.

5807
09:38:35,982 --> 09:38:41,028
The IAM policy will work, but it must be attached to the bucket rather than the role.

5808
09:38:41,616 --> 09:38:41,936
C.

5809
09:38:42,336 --> 09:38:47,300
Create a second copy of the table without the sensitive columns and grant access to that.

5810
09:38:47,790 --> 09:38:48,096
D.

5811
09:38:48,558 --> 09:38:51,044
Encrypt the three columns with a KMS.

5812
09:38:51,441 --> 09:38:51,701
Key.

5813
09:38:52,142 --> 09:38:53,776
The research team cannot use.

5814
09:38:54,734 --> 09:38:56,068
Pause and decide.

5815
09:39:02,670 --> 09:39:07,172
The answer is A, and this delivers the promise module 2 made.

5816
09:39:09,294 --> 09:39:14,276
The reason the proposal fails is structural rather than a matter of getting the policy right.

5817
09:39:15,566 --> 09:39:19,780
There is no ARN for a column, so no IAM policy can name one.

5818
09:39:20,686 --> 09:39:27,748
Option C is worth taking seriously because organizations really do this, maintaining a redacted copy for one audience.

5819
09:39:28,128 --> 09:39:30,788
It is why fine-grained access control was built.

5820
09:39:31,088 --> 09:39:37,380
Two copies means two pipelines, two sets of storage, and the certainty that one day they will disagree.

5821
09:39:37,934 --> 09:39:42,820
That is the same argument module 9 made about data duplication generally.

5822
09:39:44,398 --> 09:39:51,588
The call-out describes the single most confusing behavior in lake formation and it makes a genuinely fair exam question.

5823
09:39:52,048 --> 09:40:01,220
When a database or table carries the IAAM Allowed Principle setting, lake formation differs to IAM entirely for that resource.

5824
09:40:02,126 --> 09:40:05,284
So you can create beautifully specific column level grants.

5825
09:40:05,648 --> 09:40:10,688
See them listed in the console and observe that everybody still sees every column.

5826
09:40:10,848 --> 09:40:12,356
Nothing errors anywhere.

5827
09:40:13,328 --> 09:40:18,208
Removing that setting is what activates enforcement and it is the step people skip.

5828
09:40:18,862 --> 09:40:23,408
Hybrid access mode is the modern and considerably safer path for an existing lake.

5829
09:40:23,568 --> 09:40:31,556
That is because it lets you move principles across one at a time rather than switching an entire estate and discovering afterwards which jobs broke.

5830
09:40:33,168 --> 09:40:44,804
This is the operational half of the first work scenario, and it is where real implementations go wrong. Lake formation enforces permissions when an engine asks it what a principal may see.

5831
09:40:45,248 --> 09:40:50,340
It does that by vending temporary credentials, scoped to the allowed columns and rows.

5832
09:40:51,182 --> 09:40:56,288
Suppose the research team's role still carries a direct S3 read policy on the bucket.

5833
09:40:56,448 --> 09:41:02,768
They can point any tool at the files and read every column, and lake formation is never consulted at any point.

5834
09:41:02,928 --> 09:41:07,236
So implementing column level security is genuinely two pieces of work.

5835
09:41:08,014 --> 09:41:13,188
Granting through lake formation and removing the direct access that made the previous approach function.

5836
09:41:15,118 --> 09:41:19,364
Skipping the second is how the second work scenario in this module happens.

5837
09:41:20,942 --> 09:41:27,844
A company has implemented lake formation column level grants, excluding three sensitive columns from an analytics team.

5838
09:41:28,368 --> 09:41:33,328
Testing shows the team's Athena queries correctly return only the permitted columns.

5839
09:41:33,902 --> 09:41:41,808
A week later, an auditor discovers that the same team's AWS Blue job reads the underlying parquet files directly from Amazon S3,

5840
09:41:42,190 --> 09:41:45,252
processes every column, including the three that were excluded.

5841
09:41:45,646 --> 09:41:47,588
What is the cause and what should be done?

5842
09:41:49,038 --> 09:41:51,972
a Lake formation does not apply to blue jobs.

5843
09:41:52,974 --> 09:41:54,128
Nothing can be done.

5844
09:41:54,382 --> 09:41:59,568
b The Blue role retains a direct S3 red policy bypassing the catalog.

5845
09:41:59,968 --> 09:42:02,404
Remove it and grant through lake formation.

5846
09:42:03,408 --> 09:42:03,728
C.

5847
09:42:04,128 --> 09:42:06,724
Column level grants only work in Athena.

5848
09:42:07,088 --> 09:42:09,156
Use a redacted copy for blue.

5849
09:42:11,328 --> 09:42:11,648
D.

5850
09:42:12,302 --> 09:42:16,900
The three columns need to be encrypted at rest with a separate KMS key.

5851
09:42:17,728 --> 09:42:19,332
Pause and decide.

5852
09:42:25,742 --> 09:42:32,368
The answer is B and the detail that gives it away is that Athena behaved correctly while Blue did not.

5853
09:42:32,750 --> 09:42:36,768
The glands are therefore light, and something about the second path is different.

5854
09:42:37,088 --> 09:42:43,332
Athena has no way to read S3 except through the catalog, so it always consults lake formation.

5855
09:42:43,982 --> 09:42:50,884
A blue job can be written to read a path directly, and if its role permits that, nothing stops it.

5856
09:42:51,694 --> 09:42:55,428
This is module 2's healthcare scenario appearing from the other side.

5857
09:42:56,110 --> 09:43:01,668
The general lesson is that fine grained control is only as strong as the weakest path to the data.

5858
09:43:03,406 --> 09:43:08,804
This is a genuine documented restriction and it is the kind of detail that separates a considered answer.

5859
09:43:09,422 --> 09:43:14,020
Suppose an LF tag expression grants access to only some of a table's columns.

5860
09:43:14,670 --> 09:43:21,476
Permissions needing full column access, such as alter, drop, insert or delete, are then not granted.

5861
09:43:22,414 --> 09:43:24,656
The principle is left with describe.

5862
09:43:24,976 --> 09:43:28,164
A blue job needing to write the table then fails.

5863
09:43:29,454 --> 09:43:32,356
The practical design is on the last two bullets.

5864
09:43:33,776 --> 09:43:37,188
Pipelines that build a table need full access to it.

5865
09:43:38,126 --> 09:43:46,176
The fine-grained restriction belongs on the analysts and applications reading the result, which is where the business requirement actually sits.

5866
09:43:47,470 --> 09:43:56,356
Views are the traditional answer and they appear as options constantly, so it is worth being precise about why the exam usually prefers permissions.

5867
09:43:57,166 --> 09:44:05,636
A view restricting columns only protects the data if consumers cannot query the underlying table, which means you need a second set of permissions anyway.

5868
09:44:06,350 --> 09:44:12,964
Miss that step, and the view is purely advisory, protecting nobody from anybody who knows the base table's name.

5869
09:44:13,934 --> 09:44:21,188
A permission applies to the table itself, so it holds no matter which object somebody queries or which engine they reach it with.

5870
09:44:21,998 --> 09:44:23,908
The maintenance argument matters too.

5871
09:44:25,070 --> 09:44:32,004
The third verb scenario in this module is built on it, where 14 views existed to serve one table's worth of data.

5872
09:44:33,582 --> 09:44:39,172
The callout is a genuinely useful piece of design advice and it makes a fair exam question.

5873
09:44:39,758 --> 09:44:45,700
A filter listing the columns a principal may see has to be updated every time the table gains a column.

5874
09:44:46,478 --> 09:44:51,876
Until somebody does that, the new column is invisible, which is inconvenient but safe.

5875
09:44:52,814 --> 09:44:57,220
A filter listing the columns they may not see behaves the opposite way.

5876
09:44:58,670 --> 09:45:01,220
A new column is visible immediately.

5877
09:45:02,318 --> 09:45:08,708
If it happens to be a sensitive one that nobody remembered to add to the exclusion, it has been exposed.

5878
09:45:09,550 --> 09:45:12,580
So including is safe by default and

5879
09:45:13,262 --> 09:45:18,916
excluding is convenient by default, and for sensitive data, the safe default is the one you want.

5880
09:45:20,622 --> 09:45:26,244
The callout is what makes cross-account sharing genuinely useful rather than an all-or-nothing decision.

5881
09:45:26,606 --> 09:45:31,652
Sharing a table with a partner account does not mean sharing everything in that table.

5882
09:45:32,430 --> 09:45:39,204
That is because the column and row filters are enforced on the consumer's queries exactly as they are on your own.

5883
09:45:39,694 --> 09:45:46,116
That is how a company shares transaction data with an analytics partner while excluding customer identifiers entirely.

5884
09:45:47,342 --> 09:45:50,564
The version numbers matter for a practical reason worth knowing.

5885
09:45:51,534 --> 09:45:57,764
An estate with thousands of tables used to hit resource association limits in Resource Access Manager.

5886
09:45:58,382 --> 09:46:06,756
Version 5, released in February 2026, removes that by sharing through wildcard patterns instead of per resource associations.

5887
09:46:08,430 --> 09:46:16,292
Skill 4.2.4 names granting and revoking database access, and Redshift is where that language properly belongs.

5888
09:46:17,614 --> 09:46:28,496
Data physically inside a Redshift table is governed by Redshift's own grant and revoke statements that look like any SQL database and have nothing to do with lake formation.

5889
09:46:28,816 --> 09:46:36,976
Lake formation governs catalog objects which includes the external tables, Redshift Spectrum reads, and increasingly

5890
09:46:37,216 --> 09:46:40,356
data shares registered with it for fine-grained control.

5891
09:46:40,942 --> 09:46:44,228
So a question about restricting a warehouse table has a redshift answer.

5892
09:46:46,318 --> 09:46:51,856
A question about restricting a lake table queried to spectrum has a lake formation answer.

5893
09:46:52,302 --> 09:46:59,268
Knowing which side of that line a scenario sits on is most of the work and the third work scenario tests it directly.

5894
09:47:00,526 --> 09:47:06,816
Dynamic data masking is the feature worth knowing because it answers a requirement people usually solve badly.

5895
09:47:06,976 --> 09:47:12,336
A support agent should see the last four digits of a card number and an auditor should see all of it.

5896
09:47:12,654 --> 09:47:15,652
The traditional answer is two views or two tables.

5897
09:47:16,430 --> 09:47:20,548
Masking attaches a rule to the column that applies differently by rule.

5898
09:47:21,102 --> 09:47:26,404
So the same query against the same table returns different values depending on who runs it.

5899
09:47:26,990 --> 09:47:31,108
That eliminates the duplication problem this module keeps returning to.

5900
09:47:31,776 --> 09:47:40,708
The last bullet connects to module 10, where data shares were described as granting access to whole objects until lake formation is involved.

5901
09:47:42,734 --> 09:47:48,324
This is worth a slide precisely because it contradicts the framing this module opened with.

5902
09:47:48,974 --> 09:47:57,892
IAM cannot express row level control in general, and Dynamo DB is the case where it can for a specific structural reason.

5903
09:47:58,734 --> 09:48:03,684
In Dynamo DB, the partition key is part of the request rather than buried inside the data.

5904
09:48:03,920 --> 09:48:08,800
So a policy condition can inspect it and refuse a request for somebody else's items.

5905
09:48:09,040 --> 09:48:16,640
That is how a multi-tenant application serves every customer from one table while guaranteeing each can only read their own.

5906
09:48:17,166 --> 09:48:23,620
It does not generalize to S3 or to a relational table where the filtering values are inside the contents.

5907
09:48:25,230 --> 09:48:30,564
A retailer's Redshift warehouse holds a transactions table used by 12 country teams.

5908
09:48:31,118 --> 09:48:33,828
Each team must see only their own country's rows.

5909
09:48:34,382 --> 09:48:40,720
A support team must see all rows but with the customer email masked, while the fraud team must see it in full.

5910
09:48:41,262 --> 09:48:49,924
There are currently 12 views, one per country plus two more for the support and fraud cases. Keeping them in step with schema changes has become a burden.

5911
09:48:50,480 --> 09:48:52,772
What should the data engineer implement?

5912
09:48:53,294 --> 09:49:01,060
a row level security policies for the country filtering and dynamic data masking on the email column by role.

5913
09:49:01,680 --> 09:49:07,364
b Keep the views but generate them automatically from a script when the schema changes.

5914
09:49:08,240 --> 09:49:08,560
C.

5915
09:49:09,486 --> 09:49:13,892
Move the table to Amazon S3 and use lake formation data filters.

5916
09:49:15,374 --> 09:49:15,600
D.

5917
09:49:16,846 --> 09:49:23,760
Create 12 separate tables, one per country, with the email column omitted where required.

5918
09:49:24,430 --> 09:49:25,732
Pause and decide.

5919
09:49:32,398 --> 09:49:38,692
The answer is A, and the question describes two different requirements that happen to have two different native features.

5920
09:49:39,438 --> 09:49:46,500
Country filtering is row level security, where a policy attached to the table filters rows according to who is asking.

5921
09:49:47,310 --> 09:49:53,316
The email requirement is dynamic data masking, where the same column returns different values by role.

5922
09:49:53,870 --> 09:49:57,860
Option C is worth noting because it is the wrong direction.

5923
09:49:58,480 --> 09:50:06,052
Data already in the warehouse should be governed in the warehouse, which is module 5's argument about transformation applied to permissions.

5924
09:50:06,240 --> 09:50:12,420
Moving it to the lake to gain a feature Redshift already has is a large change for nothing.

5925
09:50:13,934 --> 09:50:21,760
Skill 4.2.1 names updating permissions across Lambda, step functions, Amazon M, WAA,

5926
09:50:22,080 --> 09:50:25,348
Amazon EMR, Redshift, Glue, and Athena.

5927
09:50:26,702 --> 09:50:29,668
The distinction on this slide is what it is really testing.

5928
09:50:30,190 --> 09:50:33,220
There are two entirely separate questions for every pipeline.

5929
09:50:34,158 --> 09:50:38,111
What may this pipeline touch, which is answered by its execution role?

5930
09:50:38,638 --> 09:50:43,108
Who may operate this pipeline, which is answered by a permission on the service resource?

5931
09:50:44,531 --> 09:50:53,124
Conflicting them is how an engineer who simply needed to rerun a failed job ends up holding read access to every data set that job touches.

5932
09:50:53,966 --> 09:51:00,356
The correct shape is a narrow permission to start the job while the job itself carries the data access it needs.

5933
09:51:01,838 --> 09:51:05,840
Skill 4.2.1 names exactly these services.

5934
09:51:06,126 --> 09:51:10,240
Reading down the table, the pattern is consistent enough to be worth stating as a rule.

5935
09:51:10,480 --> 09:51:15,396
There are two permission questions for every service and they are answered in different places.

5936
09:51:15,918 --> 09:51:17,732
What may this thing do to data,

5937
09:51:18,414 --> 09:51:20,388
which is an execution or runtime rule?

5938
09:51:20,686 --> 09:51:26,340
Who may operate this thing, which is an IAAM permission on the service resource?

5939
09:51:26,990 --> 09:51:31,040
The EMR runtime roles row is worth knowing specifically.

5940
09:51:31,598 --> 09:51:41,412
That is because it lets different jobs on one shared cluster carry different data permissions rather than everything on the cluster inheriting a single powerful role.

5941
09:51:43,022 --> 09:51:53,732
The EMR runtime role capability solves a problem that used to force awkward architectures. Without it, everything running on a cluster inherits the cluster's role.

5942
09:51:54,000 --> 09:52:01,760
So a cluster used by two teams must hold the union of both teams' permissions, and either team's job can read the other's data.

5943
09:52:02,286 --> 09:52:06,372
Runtime rows attach an identity to each job submission instead.

5944
09:52:06,574 --> 09:52:11,556
So lake formations, column, and row grants apply per job on shared infrastructure.

5945
09:52:13,454 --> 09:52:18,660
The MWAA row is the honest contrast and it matters for design questions.

5946
09:52:20,270 --> 09:52:23,940
An MWAA environment has one execution role.

5947
09:52:24,686 --> 09:52:32,324
So every DAG in it shares permissions, and separating sensitive workflows means running a separate environment.

5948
09:52:34,030 --> 09:52:41,252
The fourth bullet is a genuine gap that people miss when designing fine-grained access, and it follows from module 13.

5949
09:52:41,806 --> 09:52:49,252
Every Athena query writes its results into S3 as a file, and that file contains whatever the query returned.

5950
09:52:50,320 --> 09:52:54,308
So a user with permission to see a sensitive column runs a query.

5951
09:52:54,640 --> 09:53:02,960
The result sits in a bucket where anybody with access to the results location can read it, entirely outside Lake Formation's control.

5952
09:53:03,758 --> 09:53:09,732
Separate work groups with separate result locations are the answer, along with encryption on those results.

5953
09:53:10,926 --> 09:53:16,356
It is the kind of second-order detail that distinguishes a design system from a configured one.

5954
09:53:17,966 --> 09:53:24,516
The distinction on the third bullet is the one that decides architecture questions and it is easy to state.

5955
09:53:25,440 --> 09:53:35,460
Role-based access control handles people changing very well because roles outlive individuals and a new joiner simply receives the right one on their first day.

5956
09:53:36,430 --> 09:53:43,812
It handles resources changing badly because every table created has to be added to every role that should be able to see it.

5957
09:53:44,334 --> 09:53:49,124
That work is manual, unglamorous, and routinely forgotten.

5958
09:53:49,742 --> 09:53:53,060
Attribute-based control inverts the problem entirely.

5959
09:53:53,998 --> 09:54:02,080
A table tagged as finance and restricted is automatically covered by whatever grants already exist for those attributes, with no per

5960
09:54:02,240 --> 09:54:03,236
table action at all.

5961
09:54:03,918 --> 09:54:06,560
That is exactly the LF tag argument from earlier.

5962
09:54:06,720 --> 09:54:07,492
Generalized.

5963
09:54:09,198 --> 09:54:14,724
This connects module 17's Federation argument to this module's grants and completes the picture.

5964
09:54:16,014 --> 09:54:19,492
Suppose a lake formation grant names an individual principal.

5965
09:54:19,918 --> 09:54:23,600
Removing that person's access is then a data permission change.

5966
09:54:23,790 --> 09:54:27,748
Somebody must remember to make in every place a grant exists.

5967
09:54:28,622 --> 09:54:36,548
If grants name groups from the corporate directory, removing the person from that group removes their data access everywhere at once.

5968
09:54:36,960 --> 09:54:46,660
The data platform team is not involved at all. That is the same argument the exam applies to IEM users against federated identities.

5969
09:54:47,566 --> 09:54:50,692
It is why the two modules belong next to each other.

5970
09:54:52,430 --> 09:54:59,876
A data platform team manages a lake with about 900 tables across four domains, growing by roughly 30 tables a month.

5971
09:55:00,334 --> 09:55:10,372
Access is granted with named resource, lake formation grants, and the team spends significant time processing access requests and adding new tables to existing grants.

5972
09:55:11,214 --> 09:55:19,108
New tables are frequently missed, so analysts discover they cannot see data that should have been available to them. What should the team implement?

5973
09:55:20,430 --> 09:55:20,720
A

5974
09:55:22,862 --> 09:55:28,260
Grant each team access to all tables in their domains database at database level.

5975
09:55:29,742 --> 09:55:36,480
Adopt LFPags, tagging tables by domain and sensitivity and granting principles against tag values.

5976
09:55:36,800 --> 09:55:41,920
C. Write a lambda function that runs nightly and adds new tables to the existing grants.

5977
09:55:42,560 --> 09:55:47,588
D. Move to IAM policies with wildcards on the Glue Data Catalog.

5978
09:55:49,198 --> 09:55:50,372
Pause and decide.

5979
09:55:57,070 --> 09:55:58,384
The answer is B.

5980
09:55:59,024 --> 09:56:03,588
Option A deserves attention because it genuinely does solve the stated complaint.

5981
09:56:04,398 --> 09:56:12,484
That is because a database level grant covers new tables automatically and it fails on something the question implies rather than states.

5982
09:56:13,422 --> 09:56:21,892
A lake with four domains almost certainly has tables of differing sensitivity within each domain, and a database level grant cannot distinguish them.

5983
09:56:22,510 --> 09:56:27,972
Tags handle both dimensions at once because a table can carry a domain tag and a sensitivity tag.

5984
09:56:28,544 --> 09:56:31,332
Grants can be expressed against either or both.

5985
09:56:32,064 --> 09:56:36,580
That is the capability that makes tags worth the ontology work.

5986
09:56:38,064 --> 09:56:43,300
This table is the module as a single decision and it is worth learning as a unit.

5987
09:56:44,590 --> 09:56:52,420
That is because the exam presents the same business requirement in different locations and expects a different answer each time.

5988
09:56:54,254 --> 09:56:58,692
Hiding a column is a lake formation grant when the data is in the lake.

5989
09:56:59,694 --> 09:57:04,484
A redshift grant or a dynamic masking policy when it is in the warehouse.

5990
09:57:05,198 --> 09:57:10,884
Filtering rows is a data filter in one place and a row level security policy in the other.

5991
09:57:11,790 --> 09:57:17,060
Choosing the layer that matches where the data actually lives is most of the answer.

5992
09:57:17,902 --> 09:57:27,012
Reaching for lake formation on a warehouse table, exactly as option C in the third scenario did, is the characteristic mistake this table exists to prevent.

5993
09:57:28,526 --> 09:57:33,504
This is the question every audit begins with and it is harder to answer than it looks.

5994
09:57:33,904 --> 09:57:37,220
Listing the grants on a table tells you the direct ones.

5995
09:57:39,104 --> 09:57:47,620
It does not tell you who reaches it through an LF tag expression, nor through a database level grant the table inherits,

5996
09:57:47,984 --> 09:57:51,524
nor a cross-account share to an entire organizational unit.

5997
09:57:52,334 --> 09:57:55,844
Those combine and the effective answer is the union.

5998
09:57:57,134 --> 09:58:02,384
The last bullet is the practical advice and it applies to the exam's reasoning too.

5999
09:58:02,926 --> 09:58:10,084
Assuming a principal and running the query is the definitive test, where reading the policies is an inference that can miss a path.

6000
09:58:11,694 --> 09:58:17,584
Module 17 made this point about IAM and it applies with equal force to lake formation.

6001
09:58:18,286 --> 09:58:28,644
Permissions are granted in response to a genuine need. The need ends within weeks. Nobody ever evokes the grant. So an access estate accumulates indefinitely and only ever grows.

6002
09:58:29,486 --> 09:58:36,388
The unused access findings from Access Analyzer are the mechanical answer for IAM roles and permissions.

6003
09:58:38,094 --> 09:58:45,444
Lake Formation's own records answer the equivalent question for catalog objects, which is who can currently see this particular table?

6004
09:58:46,382 --> 09:58:51,044
The time-bound suggestion on the fourth bullet is the preventive version and the better one.

6005
09:58:51,534 --> 09:58:56,932
That is because a grant that expires removes itself and nobody has to remember to do it.

6006
09:58:58,606 --> 09:59:04,516
The first two rows are the ones worth memorizing because both produce the same bewildering experience.

6007
09:59:04,910 --> 09:59:08,068
A correctly configured permission that visibly does nothing at all.

6008
09:59:08,750 --> 09:59:09,024
I

6009
09:59:10,222 --> 09:59:10,784
AM.

6010
09:59:11,694 --> 09:59:19,664
Allowed principles means lake formation is differing to I AM entirely for that resource.

6011
09:59:20,206 --> 09:59:24,388
So grants are recorded, appear in the console, and are never consulted.

6012
09:59:24,974 --> 09:59:30,116
Direct S3 access means one particular engine never asked the catalog in the first place.

6013
09:59:30,624 --> 09:59:37,348
In both cases, the fix is to close the path that bypasses enforcement rather than to adjust the grant itself.

6014
09:59:37,870 --> 09:59:45,028
An engineer who does not know these two behaviors exist will spend a very long time defining a policy that was correct from the beginning.

6015
09:59:46,606 --> 09:59:51,204
5 questions and the first is the single fastest elimination in this module.

6016
09:59:51,694 --> 10:00:03,652
That is because any option proposing an IAM policy for a column or row requirement can be discarded without reading the rest of it. The fourth is the one that catches genuinely experienced engineers.

6017
10:00:04,558 --> 10:00:11,300
That is because a fine-grained permission model is only ever as strong as the narrowest path to the data.

6018
10:00:11,664 --> 10:00:17,220
A role that retains direct S3 access has an open door standing beside the locked

6019
10:00:17,966 --> 10:00:25,904
one. The third is worth practicing because the answer changes with scale and a design that is perfectly sensible for 40 tables

6020
10:00:26,224 --> 10:00:28,580
becomes unmanageable at 900.

6021
10:00:29,294 --> 10:00:36,964
Next module covers encryption and masking, which is the layer beneath this one, protecting data whether or not the permission model holds.

6022
10:00:38,446 --> 10:00:41,316
This module is the layer beneath module 18.

6023
10:00:42,286 --> 10:00:45,028
Authorization decides who may read a dataset.

6024
10:00:45,701 --> 10:00:51,044
And encryption decides whether the bytes mean anything to somebody who obtains them another way.

6025
10:00:51,854 --> 10:00:58,884
Modules 8, 10, 13, and 14 each deferred an encryption question here and they all get answered.

6026
10:00:59,374 --> 10:01:09,984
The exam guide gives this its own task statement covering encryption at rest, in transit, across account boundaries, and the masking and anonymization of personal data.

6027
10:01:10,414 --> 10:01:17,092
Four worked scenarios and one change to an AWS default that happened in April 2026.

6028
10:01:18,638 --> 10:01:28,228
Since January 2023, every object written to Amazon S3 is encrypted at rest by default. Most other AWS data services encrypt by default too.

6029
10:01:29,070 --> 10:01:35,264
So an option proposing to enable encryption on a bucket is usually describing something that is already true.

6030
10:01:35,758 --> 10:01:39,044
That makes it a weak answer rather than an obviously wrong one.

6031
10:01:39,566 --> 10:01:42,384
What questions actually turn on is key control.

6032
10:01:43,790 --> 10:01:44,996
Who can use the key?

6033
10:01:45,390 --> 10:01:46,532
Who can revoke it?

6034
10:01:46,894 --> 10:01:51,744
Whether its use is audited, and whether another account can decrypt with it.

6035
10:01:52,238 --> 10:01:58,916
Read an encryption question looking for those four things because that is where the requirements and the distractors both live.

6036
10:02:00,814 --> 10:02:05,028
The first worked scenario in this module is built entirely on the fourth.

6037
10:02:06,286 --> 10:02:09,700
Module 8 promised this table, and here it is in full.

6038
10:02:11,024 --> 10:02:18,532
The row worth flagging is customer provided keys, where you send the key with every request and AWS uses it without storing it.

6039
10:02:19,790 --> 10:02:24,804
From the 6th of April 2026, that option is disabled by default on new buckets.

6040
10:02:25,582 --> 10:02:30,212
So it must be deliberately enabled, which reflects how rarely it is the right answer.

6041
10:02:30,798 --> 10:02:34,384
The practical division for the exam is between the first two rows.

6042
10:02:34,864 --> 10:02:37,892
S3 managed keys are free and invisible.

6043
10:02:38,766 --> 10:02:46,372
KMS keys give you a key policy, an audit trail, and the ability to revoke, which is what regulated workloads need.

6044
10:02:47,984 --> 10:02:54,852
The call out is the mechanism and it is what makes KMS valuable rather than merely different.

6045
10:02:55,264 --> 10:03:00,004
With S3 managed keys, permission on the object is the whole story.

6046
10:03:00,464 --> 10:03:07,236
With a KMS key, a principal needs permission to read the object and permission to use the key.

6047
10:03:08,174 --> 10:03:11,780
Those live in different places controlled by potentially different people.

6048
10:03:12,462 --> 10:03:19,172
That separation is why a compliance team can hold key policy control while a data team holds bucket policy control.

6049
10:03:20,014 --> 10:03:23,268
It is why revoking key access is such a strong control.

6050
10:03:24,144 --> 10:03:30,884
Remove the principle from the key policy, and every object encrypted with it becomes unreadable to them immediately.

6051
10:03:32,526 --> 10:03:38,308
The exam mostly cares about the material, and the outer rows are worth recognizing so you can eliminate them.

6052
10:03:38,958 --> 10:03:45,060
An AWS-owned key is used by a service on behalf of many customers and you never see it or control it.

6053
10:03:45,518 --> 10:03:49,188
At the other extreme, sit imported key material and external key stores.

6054
10:03:49,966 --> 10:03:56,932
Some regulators require the organization to generate and hold the key material itself outside AWS.

6055
10:03:57,422 --> 10:04:03,716
Those are real capabilities with real costs because if you lose imported material, the data is gone permanently.

6056
10:04:04,654 --> 10:04:09,220
An external key store means AWS cannot decrypt without reaching your hardware.

6057
10:04:10,126 --> 10:04:14,404
A question mentioning a regulator requiring sole custody is pointing at them.

6058
10:04:15,886 --> 10:04:24,900
This is a genuine cost problem with a specific answer and it appears in questions phrased as encryption being expensive.

6059
10:04:26,670 --> 10:04:32,576
Without bucket keys, every single object read triggers a call to KMS to decrypt the data

6060
10:04:32,576 --> 10:04:39,216
key. So a spark job opening a million packet files makes a million KMS requests, each one build.

6061
10:04:39,950 --> 10:04:46,692
S3 bucket keys change the mechanism using a short-lived bucket level key to derive per object keys locally.

6062
10:04:47,694 --> 10:04:50,336
That cuts KMS calls by up to 99%.

6063
10:04:51,502 --> 10:04:56,388
The encryption is unchanged and the audit trail is coarser, which is the trade,

6064
10:04:57,102 --> 10:05:00,260
it is almost always worth enabling on a lake.

6065
10:05:01,902 --> 10:05:05,060
The callout gives you two mechanical eliminations.

6066
10:05:06,030 --> 10:05:14,096
An AWS managed key is the kind that appears automatically with a name like a WS S3.

6067
10:05:15,054 --> 10:05:18,852
It cannot be used by another account and you cannot disable it.

6068
10:05:19,694 --> 10:05:29,316
So any requirement mentioning cross-account access to encrypted data or the ability to revoke access by turning off a key needs a customer managed key.

6069
10:05:29,838 --> 10:05:39,332
That is a common exam shape because sharing encrypted data across accounts requires both the bucket policy and the key policy to permit the other account.

6070
10:05:40,494 --> 10:05:44,656
A question describing sharing that phase is often missing the second.

6071
10:05:45,966 --> 10:05:48,336
Scale 4.3.3 names.

6072
10:05:48,622 --> 10:05:52,004
This explicitly and the first bullet is the whole answer.

6073
10:05:52,782 --> 10:05:59,844
Cross account access to encrypted data requires three separate permissions to line up and module. 18 covered two of them.

6074
10:06:00,814 --> 10:06:03,300
The bucket policy allowing the other account.

6075
10:06:10,976 --> 10:06:15,300
other account's role allowing the action, and the key policy allowing that role to decrypt. The third is the one that produces the first work scenario in this module.

6076
10:06:16,238 --> 10:06:22,884
That is because encryption is normally invisible and nobody thinks about the key until a decrypt call is denied.

6077
10:06:25,166 --> 10:06:32,004
The replication rows matter for module 8 because a replicated object must be decryptable wherever it lands.

6078
10:06:33,678 --> 10:06:37,296
A company shares a data lake bucket with a partner account.

6079
10:06:37,536 --> 10:06:45,508
The bucket policy grants the partner's role read access and the partner's role has the matching Amazon S3 permissions.

6080
10:06:46,414 --> 10:06:51,108
Objects are encrypted with SSEKMS using a customer managed key.

6081
10:06:52,014 --> 10:06:55,844
The partner receives an access denied error when reading objects.

6082
10:06:56,334 --> 10:06:59,428
Cloud Trail shows the S3 get object call succeeding

6083
10:07:00,110 --> 10:07:03,204
and a subsequent KMS decrypt call being denied.

6084
10:07:04,110 --> 10:07:04,964
What is missing?

6085
10:07:05,921 --> 10:07:10,692
A. The KMS key policy does not grant the partner's role permission to decrypt.

6086
10:07:12,462 --> 10:07:18,020
The bucket must be re encrypted with SSES3 for cross account access to work.

6087
10:07:18,670 --> 10:07:18,976
C.

6088
10:07:19,598 --> 10:07:22,468
The partner needs a copy of the objects in their own account.

6089
10:07:22,990 --> 10:07:28,576
D S Three bucket keys must be enabled before cross account decryption is possible.

6090
10:07:28,976 --> 10:07:30,180
Pause and decide.

6091
10:07:36,878 --> 10:07:41,156
The answer is A, and this is the two permissions point tested directly.

6092
10:07:42,862 --> 10:07:48,208
The question is generous because CloudTeil separates the two calls and shows exactly which succeeded.

6093
10:07:48,462 --> 10:07:51,236
That is also how you would diagnose it in practice.

6094
10:07:51,662 --> 10:07:59,268
It is worth remembering that an access denied on an encrypted object has two candidate causes rather than one.

6095
10:07:59,808 --> 10:08:02,948
Option B is the shape to reject on principle.

6096
10:08:03,408 --> 10:08:11,492
Removing a security control to make an access problem go away answers the question asked and defeats the reason the control existed.

6097
10:08:12,302 --> 10:08:16,004
That is a pattern the exam consistently marks down.

6098
10:08:17,486 --> 10:08:19,908
The call out names the object questions refer to.

6099
10:08:20,208 --> 10:08:27,268
In both Glue and Amazon EMR, encryption is not a checkbox on a job or a cluster, but a security configuration.

6100
10:08:27,886 --> 10:08:32,836
That is a named reusable object holding the encryption settings and then attached.

6101
10:08:33,248 --> 10:08:35,076
That indirection is worth knowing.

6102
10:08:36,046 --> 10:08:44,644
A question about encrypting Blue job output or EMR local disks has a specific answer with a specific name.

6103
10:08:46,190 --> 10:08:50,208
The redshift row carries a constraint worth remembering.

6104
10:08:50,702 --> 10:08:58,788
On a provisioned cluster, encryption is decided at creation, and changing it afterwards means migrating the cluster rather than flipping a setting.

6105
10:09:00,558 --> 10:09:05,380
Module 3 Build the streaming pipelines and this is the security layer over them.

6106
10:09:06,222 --> 10:09:10,148
The second bullet is worth carrying because it produces exactly the failure.

6107
10:09:10,542 --> 10:09:13,764
The first work scenario described in a different service.

6108
10:09:14,638 --> 10:09:24,196
A consumer granted permission on a kinesis stream but not on the KMS key encrypting it will fail to read. The error points at the stream rather than at the key.

6109
10:09:25,328 --> 10:09:28,260
The last bullet is the principle worth generalizing.

6110
10:09:28,750 --> 10:09:34,308
A stream carrying customer records is as sensitive as the table those records land in.

6111
10:09:35,246 --> 10:09:42,308
A governance design that secures the warehouse while leaving the stream open has protected the destination and not the data.

6112
10:09:44,046 --> 10:09:48,228
The callout names a component people overlook when securing a pipeline.

6113
10:09:49,742 --> 10:09:55,140
A Glue security configuration covers three distinct things and job bookmarks are one of

6114
10:09:55,982 --> 10:10:02,468
them. That is because a bookmark records which data has been processed and can therefore reveal something about the data itself.

6115
10:10:03,374 --> 10:10:07,044
Logs are another for the reasons module 14 described.

6116
10:10:07,630 --> 10:10:12,292
The redshift constraint on the first bullet is the one most likely to appear as a scenario.

6117
10:10:12,974 --> 10:10:20,048
A question describing an unencrypted production cluster that must become encrypted has a particular answer.

6118
10:10:20,590 --> 10:10:24,804
A snapshot and a restore rather than a configuration change.

6119
10:10:26,254 --> 10:10:29,668
The callout is the detail that separates a considered answer.

6120
10:10:30,208 --> 10:10:34,928
Traffic between your application and an AWS API endpoint is over.

6121
10:10:37,102 --> 10:10:39,908
TLS by default and there is little to configure.

6122
10:10:40,814 --> 10:10:44,004
Traffic inside a cluster you own is different.

6123
10:10:44,526 --> 10:10:55,812
Spark executors on an Amazon EMR cluster, shuffling data between nodes, are not encrypting the traffic unless the security configuration enables in transit encryption.

6124
10:10:56,558 --> 10:10:59,364
For a regulated workload, that is a genuine gap.

6125
10:11:00,270 --> 10:11:08,644
So a question about encrypting data in transit for a Spark job on EMR is not about TLS2S3, it is about the cluster's own internal traffic.

6126
10:11:10,222 --> 10:11:15,780
Module 18 Introduce the encryption condition and this is where it turns its place.

6127
10:11:16,238 --> 10:11:20,484
There are two ways to ensure everything in a bucket is encrypted with the right key.

6128
10:11:21,326 --> 10:11:28,112
Audit it periodically and remediate what is wrong, which means the wrong thing existed for a while and somebody has to fix it.

6129
10:11:28,192 --> 10:11:35,524
Or write a bucket policy that refuses any put request not specifying that key, in which case the wrong thing was never written at all.

6130
10:11:36,174 --> 10:11:45,188
The exam prefers the second consistently and AWS config is the answer when the requirement is to report on compliance rather than to enforce it.

6131
10:11:46,638 --> 10:11:55,108
A regulated company requires that every object in a data lake bucket is encrypted with a specific customer managed KMS key.

6132
10:11:55,758 --> 10:11:59,780
An audit has found 7,000 objects encrypted with SSE.

6133
10:12:00,430 --> 10:12:00,992
S3

6134
10:12:01,838 --> 10:12:05,472
written by a job whose author was unaware of the requirement.

6135
10:12:05,934 --> 10:12:13,220
The company wants to prevent recurrence and to be alerted if any resource in the organization drifts from the encryption standard.

6136
10:12:13,712 --> 10:12:16,548
Which combination best meets both needs?

6137
10:12:19,950 --> 10:12:28,676
A bucket policy condition refusing uploads that do not specify the required key plus AWS config rules reporting non-compliant resources.

6138
10:12:29,742 --> 10:12:30,032
B.

6139
10:12:30,352 --> 10:12:33,380
Enable default encryption on the bucket with the required key.

6140
10:12:34,112 --> 10:12:34,352
C

6141
10:12:35,758 --> 10:12:40,036
A nightly lambda function that re-encrypts objects found with the wrong encryption.

6142
10:12:40,686 --> 10:12:40,912
B.

6143
10:12:42,126 --> 10:12:46,180
Enable Amazon Massey to detect the unencrypted objects.

6144
10:12:47,790 --> 10:12:49,124
Pause and decide.

6145
10:12:55,758 --> 10:12:59,972
The answer is A. And option B is the distinction worth learning properly.

6146
10:13:00,878 --> 10:13:09,220
Default encryption on a bucket determines what happens when a request does not specify any encryption, which covers accidental omission and nothing else.

6147
10:13:10,158 --> 10:13:15,312
A job explicitly requesting S3 managed keys is honored because it asked.

6148
10:13:15,726 --> 10:13:24,912
A bucket policy condition is different in kind because it evaluates the request and refuses it. So the non-compliant rate fails rather than succeeding differently.

6149
10:13:25,486 --> 10:13:33,028
Two requirements were stated here prevention and organization, wide alerting, and only one option addresses both.

6150
10:13:34,638 --> 10:13:39,620
Every control in the last two modules assumes somebody knows which columns are sensitive.

6151
10:13:40,014 --> 10:13:47,780
In a lake of 900 tables built over 5 years by PEMs that have long since moved on, that assumption is usually false.

6152
10:13:48,654 --> 10:13:52,484
Personal data arrives in unexpected places and nobody records it.

6153
10:13:53,358 --> 10:13:57,668
A free text notes field containing a phone number somebody typed.

6154
10:13:58,190 --> 10:14:00,260
A log file carrying a request payload.

6155
10:14:00,974 --> 10:14:05,316
A test dataset copied from production three years ago and never cleaned up.

6156
10:14:06,158 --> 10:14:11,812
Discovery is therefore a prerequisite for protection rather than a separate exercise you get to later.

6157
10:14:12,686 --> 10:14:16,132
Amazon Macy is the service the exam names for it.

6158
10:14:17,710 --> 10:14:23,492
The third worked scenario in this module is about discovery that appears to be working and is not.

6159
10:14:24,910 --> 10:14:29,700
Macy answers two questions rather than one and the call out names the second.

6160
10:14:30,318 --> 10:14:38,752
The first is where sensitive data actually is, that it establishes by continuously sampling objects and matching them against built-in

6161
10:14:38,912 --> 10:14:42,372
identifiers for personal, financial, and credential data.

6162
10:14:43,152 --> 10:14:51,012
That produces a sensitivity score per bucket and an interactive map across every account where Macy is enabled.

6163
10:14:52,366 --> 10:14:57,188
The second question is whether the buckets holding that data are configured safely.

6164
10:14:57,742 --> 10:15:03,396
It reports those that are unencrypted, publicly accessible, or shared outside the organization.

6165
10:15:04,206 --> 10:15:07,396
The credentials detection is worth noting for a practical reason.

6166
10:15:07,712 --> 10:15:16,324
Access keys committed into a file and uploaded to a lake is exactly the failure module 17 argued against, and Macy is what finds them afterwards.

6167
10:15:17,902 --> 10:15:22,688
This interaction is worth knowing because it produces a confident and wrong conclusion.

6168
10:15:22,928 --> 10:15:31,716
Massey runs across a lake, encrypted with customer managed keys, reports no sensitive data findings, and somebody concludes the lake is clean.

6169
10:15:32,288 --> 10:15:36,964
In fact, Macy could not decrypt anything and reported metadata only.

6170
10:15:37,838 --> 10:15:43,044
Granting Macy use of the key in the key policy is what makes Discovery actually work.

6171
10:15:43,534 --> 10:15:47,108
AWS provides a script to enumerate which keys it needs.

6172
10:15:48,302 --> 10:15:51,908
The retention point on the last bullet matters for audit questions.

6173
10:15:52,814 --> 10:15:59,236
That is because detailed results are kept for 90 days unless you configure a bucket to hold them long term.

6174
10:16:00,814 --> 10:16:05,092
A company enabled Amazon Macy across its data lake six months ago.

6175
10:16:06,126 --> 10:16:14,980
The lake holds around 40TB across 200 buckets, all encrypted with SSEKMS using several customer managed keys.

6176
10:16:15,488 --> 10:16:18,660
Macy reports almost no sensitive data findings.

6177
10:16:19,630 --> 10:16:27,460
A manual review of one dataset has found unredacted customer email addresses and national identifiers in a free text notes column.

6178
10:16:27,808 --> 10:16:29,892
What is the most likely explanation?

6179
10:16:30,510 --> 10:16:35,108
A. Messi does not inspect free text columns, only structured fields.

6180
10:16:36,494 --> 10:16:36,768
B

6181
10:16:37,808 --> 10:16:43,492
Messi lacks permission to use the KMS keys, so it can only report object metadata.

6182
10:16:44,208 --> 10:16:44,368
C.

6183
10:16:45,134 --> 10:16:48,128
Messi only scans buckets smaller than 1TB.

6184
10:16:49,102 --> 10:16:49,408
D.

6185
10:16:49,888 --> 10:16:53,796
The findings expired because Messi retains results for 90 days.

6186
10:16:54,798 --> 10:16:55,844
Pause and decide.

6187
10:17:02,528 --> 10:17:07,111
The answer is B, and the reasoning worth taking from it is broader than mucky.

6188
10:17:07,728 --> 10:17:14,596
A security tool reporting nothing has two possible explanations, and the comfortable one is that there is nothing to report.

6189
10:17:14,928 --> 10:17:16,804
The other is that it cannot see.

6190
10:17:17,742 --> 10:17:23,780
Six months of near silence across 200 buckets known to contain customer data is the second.

6191
10:17:25,070 --> 10:17:27,620
The encryption detail in the question is a clue.

6192
10:17:29,134 --> 10:17:33,488
This connects to module 14's point about alarms that never fire.

6193
10:17:34,030 --> 10:17:41,028
An alarm that has not triggered in two years is either protecting you from something rare or watching the wrong thing.

6194
10:17:42,542 --> 10:17:49,956
This distinction is the one that makes the exam guide's grouping of encryption and masking into a single task statement make sense.

6195
10:17:50,574 --> 10:17:56,688
Encryption defends against somebody who obtains the storage, intercepts the traffic, or walks out with a disk.

6196
10:17:57,518 --> 10:18:05,888
It does absolutely nothing about a support agent who legitimately queries a table and sees a full national identifier

6197
10:18:06,062 --> 10:18:07,460
they had no business seeing.

6198
10:18:08,430 --> 10:18:17,124
That is what masking addresses, transforming a value for a particular audience while leaving it intact for those who are entitled to it.

6199
10:18:17,998 --> 10:18:22,244
Anonymization goes further and removes the link to an individual permanently.

6200
10:18:22,862 --> 10:18:28,644
The difference between the two matters legally as well as technically, which the technique table covers next.

6201
10:18:30,286 --> 10:18:35,140
Reading a question for which of these two it wants is the fastest split in this module.

6202
10:18:35,758 --> 10:18:44,368
If the concern is somebody obtaining data they were never granted, whether through a stolen disk, an intercepted connection, or a misconfigured bucket,

6203
10:18:44,608 --> 10:18:45,764
that is encryption.

6204
10:18:46,638 --> 10:18:53,220
If the concern is somebody who has been granted access and sees more of it than their job requires, that is masking.

6205
10:18:53,742 --> 10:18:58,148
No amount of encryption helps because they decrypt legitimately every time they query.

6206
10:19:00,750 --> 10:19:05,412
The fourth worked scenario in this module needs both, which is common in practice.

6207
10:19:05,902 --> 10:19:11,396
The question tells you so by describing three different audiences with three different needs.

6208
10:19:12,782 --> 10:19:17,668
The hashing row is the one worth understanding for data engineering specifically.

6209
10:19:18,254 --> 10:19:25,540
Analytics frequently needs to know that two records refer to the same person without needing to know who that person is.

6210
10:19:26,350 --> 10:19:33,316
Hashing an identifier consistently preserves exactly that property because equal inputs produce equal outputs.

6211
10:19:33,408 --> 10:19:38,532
So joins, counts, and distinct operations all still work while the readable value is gone.

6212
10:19:39,054 --> 10:19:42,596
It is not anonymization though, and that distinction carries legal weight.

6213
10:19:43,438 --> 10:19:50,628
Suppose the set of possible inputs is small, an email address or a national identifier with a known format.

6214
10:19:50,848 --> 10:19:54,448
A hash can then be reversed by simply trying candidates.

6215
10:19:54,688 --> 10:20:01,540
That is why sorting matters and why hash data is often still treated as personal data by regulators.

6216
10:20:03,310 --> 10:20:08,324
The last bullet describes the strongest option and it is not always available.

6217
10:20:08,878 --> 10:20:16,484
If a pipeline hashes or tokenizes an identifier as data arrives, the readable value never lands in storage at all.

6218
10:20:16,910 --> 10:20:20,196
So no permission mistake downstream can expose it.

6219
10:20:20,718 --> 10:20:27,716
That is genuinely different from every other row, which protect data you are holding and depend on the permission model holding.

6220
10:20:29,070 --> 10:20:36,288
The trade is that you cannot recover what you never stored. So if a legitimate need for the raw value emerges later, it is gone.

6221
10:20:36,878 --> 10:20:38,768
Tokenization is the middle path.

6222
10:20:38,928 --> 10:20:43,588
Keeping a reversible mapping in a separate, tightly controlled store.

6223
10:20:45,328 --> 10:20:48,388
The callout is the framing that makes these questions quick.

6224
10:20:48,768 --> 10:20:56,448
Sensitivity tells you that something must be controlled and not which technique to use because every technique on this slide protects the value.

6225
10:20:57,088 --> 10:21:00,356
What decides is what the audience still needs to accomplish.

6226
10:21:00,974 --> 10:21:06,404
A support agent confirming they have the right customer needs to recognize a value, which is masking.

6227
10:21:07,008 --> 10:21:13,188
An analyst counting distinct customers needs equality without readability, which is hashing.

6228
10:21:15,694 --> 10:21:21,028
A billing process that must eventually retrieve the real value needs tokenization.

6229
10:21:21,998 --> 10:21:29,508
Reading a question for the audience's task rather than the field's sensitivity gets you to the answer directly.

6230
10:21:31,214 --> 10:21:37,604
Crypto shedding is the technique worth knowing because it answers a genuinely hard problem that comes up in erasure requests.

6231
10:21:37,888 --> 10:21:40,132
Some data cannot be rewritten in place.

6232
10:21:40,846 --> 10:21:48,868
It may sit in immutable archives under object lock, in backups taken months ago, or in formats where row level deletion is impractical.

6233
10:21:49,248 --> 10:21:57,412
Destroying the encryption key achieves the practical effect of deletion because the ciphertext remains and is meaningless to everybody, including you.

6234
10:21:58,318 --> 10:22:02,468
The last bullet is what makes it usable rather than theoretical.

6235
10:22:02,958 --> 10:22:10,372
Shedding only works at the granularity of the key, so a single key covering a whole lake gives you an all-or-nothing option.

6236
10:22:10,926 --> 10:22:16,196
A key per tenant or per subject makes targeted erasure genuinely possible.

6237
10:22:17,902 --> 10:22:25,444
A company must let its support team see customer records for troubleshooting, but the team must never see full national identifiers.

6238
10:22:25,902 --> 10:22:28,772
The fraud investigation team must see them in full.

6239
10:22:29,088 --> 10:22:33,828
The data sits in Amazon Redshift and both teams query the same tables.

6240
10:22:34,608 --> 10:22:42,768
Analytics also needs to count distinct customers across datasets without any team seeing the identifier at all, which

6241
10:22:42,928 --> 10:22:45,636
combination meets all three requirements.

6242
10:22:47,406 --> 10:22:55,524
Dynamic data masking on the identifier column by role for support and fraud and a sorted hash of the identifier for the analytics join key.

6243
10:22:56,128 --> 10:23:01,476
b Encrypt the identifier column with the KMS key and give the fraud team the key.

6244
10:23:01,968 --> 10:23:05,860
C Create three copies of the table, one per audience.

6245
10:23:06,208 --> 10:23:06,528
D

6246
10:23:07,662 --> 10:23:12,291
Remove the identifier column entirely and rely on customer name for joins.

6247
10:23:13,166 --> 10:23:14,340
Pause and decide.

6248
10:23:21,006 --> 10:23:26,852
The answer is A, and the question is built so that no single technique covers everything.

6249
10:23:27,344 --> 10:23:35,492
Masking handles the support and fraud requirement because the same column returns a partial value or a full one depending on who is asking.

6250
10:23:36,302 --> 10:23:40,868
It does not help analytics who need to join across datasets without seeing anything.

6251
10:23:41,198 --> 10:23:46,628
That is what a consistent sorted hash provides because equal identifiers produce equal hashes.

6252
10:23:47,118 --> 10:23:51,620
Option D is worth naming because joining on a name is wrong twice over.

6253
10:23:52,462 --> 10:23:57,892
Names are not unique or stable, and a name is itself personal data, so nothing was protected.

6254
10:23:59,278 --> 10:24:03,584
The callout removes a common misconception that produces wrong answers.

6255
10:24:03,824 --> 10:24:10,244
Rotating a KMS key does not re-encrypt anything and does not invalidate existing objects.

6256
10:24:10,862 --> 10:24:20,704
The key keeps its identifier and its policy, new material is generated for future encryption, and the previous material is retained so older data still decrypts.

6257
10:24:21,006 --> 10:24:24,164
Nothing has to be rewritten and no application changes.

6258
10:24:25,024 --> 10:24:30,500
The multi-region point on the last bullet matters for module 8's cross-region replication.

6259
10:24:30,944 --> 10:24:39,108
That is because an object replicated to another region must be decryptable there, and a single region key cannot do that.

6260
10:24:40,622 --> 10:24:42,884
The call out states the trade honestly.

6261
10:24:43,184 --> 10:24:49,924
A KMS key is a strong control because disabling it renders everything encrypted with it unreadable at once.

6262
10:24:50,304 --> 10:24:52,036
No object has to be touched.

6263
10:24:52,814 --> 10:25:02,596
That same property means an accidental disable or a key policy change that removes a service's access, takes out every pipeline depending on it simultaneously.

6264
10:25:04,686 --> 10:25:08,740
The quota point on the fourth bullet is the operational version of the same concern.

6265
10:25:09,390 --> 10:25:12,004
KMS has request limits for account.

6266
10:25:12,782 --> 10:25:21,732
A large spar job decrypting millions of objects can reach them, producing throttling that looks like a storage problem rather than a key one.

6267
10:25:23,470 --> 10:25:28,548
Module 14 raise the logs half and module 18 raise the query results.

6268
10:25:28,864 --> 10:25:31,524
Half and the call out generalizes both.

6269
10:25:31,744 --> 10:25:39,332
Any artifact derived from sensitive data is itself sensitive and the controls protecting the original do not automatically follow the copy.

6270
10:25:40,014 --> 10:25:47,812
A query result written to S3, a log line containing a payload, a CSV exported for a spreadsheet,

6271
10:25:48,174 --> 10:25:49,124
or an SP.

6272
10:25:50,222 --> 10:25:53,028
ICE dataset imported into a dashboard.

6273
10:25:54,446 --> 10:25:58,404
Each is a new object with its own permissions and its own encryption.

6274
10:25:59,342 --> 10:26:04,624
A governance design that stops at the source table has left several rows open behind it.

6275
10:26:05,902 --> 10:26:14,304
The last bullet is the argument for continuous evaluation over periodic review, and it is the same argument module 14

6276
10:26:14,478 --> 10:26:15,716
made about monitoring.

6277
10:26:16,334 --> 10:26:24,944
An audit conducted in March tells you about March, and an estate where teams create resources weekly has drifted by April.

6278
10:26:25,550 --> 10:26:34,276
Config rules evaluate continuously and report the moment something non-compliant appears, which turns compliance from a quarterly exercise into a signal.

6279
10:26:36,302 --> 10:26:40,516
Security Hub is worth recognizing by name as the aggregation point.

6280
10:26:41,518 --> 10:26:51,044
That is because a large organization running Macy, config, and several other services, otherwise, has findings scattered across as many consoles.

6281
10:26:52,622 --> 10:26:58,596
The second row is worth carrying because it presents as an encryption problem and is really a request volume problem.

6282
10:26:59,246 --> 10:27:07,524
A KMS bill that scales with query volume rather than with how much data exists is the signature of per object decryption calls.

6283
10:27:08,590 --> 10:27:10,660
Bucket keys are the specific answer.

6284
10:27:11,630 --> 10:27:19,300
The third row is the one that produces false confidence rather than an error, which makes it the most dangerous on this table.

6285
10:27:20,238 --> 10:27:22,340
Everything else here fails visibly.

6286
10:27:22,608 --> 10:27:29,568
A discovery tool that cannot decrypt simply reports nothing, and nothing looks exactly like good news.

6287
10:27:30,926 --> 10:27:36,804
5 questions, and the fourth is the one that separates this module from the last two most cleanly.

6288
10:27:38,126 --> 10:27:42,372
Encryption is a control against people who should not have access at all.

6289
10:27:42,798 --> 10:27:48,100
It does nothing about someone who has legitimate access and sees more than their role requires.

6290
10:27:49,006 --> 10:27:50,052
That is masking.

6291
10:27:50,542 --> 10:27:56,100
And a question describing an internal audience seeing too much has a masking answer rather than an encryption one.

6292
10:27:56,846 --> 10:28:04,608
The second question is the one that decides scenarios because prevention and detection are both reasonable and the exam usually states which it wants.

6293
10:28:05,134 --> 10:28:10,500
Next module covers governance and sharing, closing the teaching content of domain 4.

6294
10:28:11,982 --> 10:28:14,468
This is the last teaching module in the course.

6295
10:28:15,022 --> 10:28:19,204
Everything after it is exam technique, confusion pairs, and practice.

6296
10:28:19,488 --> 10:28:22,020
It covers 8 skills from the exam guide.

6297
10:28:22,926 --> 10:28:29,028
Three of those were added in the December 2025 revision, so they appear in no older study material at all.

6298
10:28:29,934 --> 10:28:38,628
Data classification schemes, protecting personal and health information, sharing data inside and outside an organization, maintaining data sovereignty.

6299
10:28:39,214 --> 10:28:42,052
The project-based governance model in Amazon SageMaker.

6300
10:28:43,438 --> 10:28:45,380
Four work scenarios along the way.

6301
10:28:46,190 --> 10:28:48,128
Modules 6 and 12.

6302
10:28:48,368 --> 10:28:53,648
Both promise that SageMaker Unified Studio and its governance model would be covered here.

6303
10:28:53,808 --> 10:28:56,324
Both promises are delivered in the second half.

6304
10:28:57,902 --> 10:29:01,488
Modules 17, 18, and 19 build the machinery.

6305
10:29:01,728 --> 10:29:05,348
Identities, permissions, encryption and masking.

6306
10:29:07,758 --> 10:29:12,644
All of it answers one question, which is whether a particular request will succeed.

6307
10:29:14,286 --> 10:29:18,788
Governance is the layer above that and it asks entirely different questions.

6308
10:29:19,630 --> 10:29:23,168
Who decided this team should have this data on what basis?

6309
10:29:23,328 --> 10:29:26,660
And can we show an auditor that decision two years later?

6310
10:29:27,088 --> 10:29:32,548
Is this dataset classified and do the controls actually applied to it match its classification?

6311
10:29:33,358 --> 10:29:38,128
If we share it with a partner, what did we agree and how would we know if it were breached?

6312
10:29:38,288 --> 10:29:40,612
None of that is enforced by a policy engine.

6313
10:29:41,710 --> 10:29:47,108
All of it is the difference between a secure system and a governed one, and all of it is examinable.

6314
10:29:48,750 --> 10:29:55,524
The last bullet is a useful observation because it means this module is largely consolidation rather than new material.

6315
10:29:56,398 --> 10:30:00,996
Classification maps onto tags and LF tags from module 18.

6316
10:30:01,934 --> 10:30:06,768
Ownership maps onto the catalog and the subscription workflow from module 12.

6317
10:30:07,246 --> 10:30:18,244
Access decisions map onto lake formation grants and cloud trail. Lineage maps onto SageMaker catalog and quality onto Blue Data. Quality from module 15.

6318
10:30:19,008 --> 10:30:21,604
Retention maps onto lifecycle rules.

6319
10:30:22,062 --> 10:30:29,124
So governance question is almost always asking which technical mechanism implements a stated policy concern.

6320
10:30:30,510 --> 10:30:37,316
If you can make that mapping reliably, then this whole area becomes straightforward rather than vague.

6321
10:30:38,606 --> 10:30:48,996
Skill 4.5.1 names data classification schemes and the exam is not testing any particular taxonomy because organizations legitimately use different ones.

6322
10:30:49,550 --> 10:30:55,716
What it tests is the consequence of classification, which is that a level implies a defined set of controls.

6323
10:30:56,686 --> 10:31:03,364
Data classified as restricted is encrypted with a customer managed key, masked for most audiences,

6324
10:31:04,208 --> 10:31:07,972
has its access logged and carries a retention rule.

6325
10:31:08,878 --> 10:31:18,048
Once that mapping exists and is agreed, classifying a new dataset determines its controls automatically rather than requiring a fresh design decision every time.

6326
10:31:18,574 --> 10:31:27,364
The callout is the practical warning worth taking seriously because an elaborate scheme nobody applies consistently is genuinely worse than a simple one.

6327
10:31:27,982 --> 10:31:29,060
Everybody does.

6328
10:31:30,574 --> 10:31:37,604
The call out is what separates governance that works from governance that is merely described in a policy document nobody reads.

6329
10:31:37,968 --> 10:31:43,876
A classification is only useful if some system acts on it, and this course has covered three that do.

6330
10:31:44,462 --> 10:31:47,620
An LF tag drives lake formation grants.

6331
10:31:48,558 --> 10:31:57,188
So tagging a new table as restricted immediately applies. Whatever access rules already exist for restricted data with no further action.

6332
10:31:57,678 --> 10:32:04,228
An S3 object tagged drives lifecycle transitions and can be required by a bucket policy condition.

6333
10:32:05,038 --> 10:32:17,316
A glossary term in the business catalog tells a human browsing for data exactly what they are looking at. The last bullet describes what most organizations actually have, which is a spreadsheet.

6334
10:32:18,702 --> 10:32:25,444
This is the organizational question every data governance program eventually faces and the exam frames it through the tooling.

6335
10:32:26,414 --> 10:32:35,908
Fully centralized governance produces consistency and a queue, Because a handful of people approve everything and cannot possibly understand every data set they are approving.

6336
10:32:37,006 --> 10:32:43,588
Fully federated governance scales and drifts because each domain invents its own classification and nothing is comparable.

6337
10:32:44,448 --> 10:32:48,608
The answer in the call-out is the one AWS builds toward.

6338
10:32:49,422 --> 10:32:59,648
Central definition of what the classifications mean and what controls each implies with the decision about individual datasets delegated to the people who own them.

6339
10:33:00,206 --> 10:33:04,484
That is exactly the domain and project structure this module covers later.

6340
10:33:06,222 --> 10:33:11,524
The third bullet is the one worth understanding because it quietly undermines the obvious approach.

6341
10:33:12,942 --> 10:33:21,188
Removing direct identifiers feels like anonymization and frequently is not, because the fields that remain still single out an individual.

6342
10:33:21,678 --> 10:33:30,788
A postcode, a date of birth, and a gender together identify a large proportion of people in most populations, which is a well-established result.

6343
10:33:31,502 --> 10:33:39,940
That is precisely why module 19 distinguished anonymization, meaning no root bad by any means from masking or redaction

6344
10:33:40,302 --> 10:33:42,020
which remove a particular field.

6345
10:33:42,926 --> 10:33:50,768
Generalization is the technique that addresses it, reducing precision until a record could plausibly describe many people

6346
10:33:50,928 --> 10:33:52,612
rather than exactly one.

6347
10:33:54,254 --> 10:33:58,916
The callout is the framing that keeps this in scope for a data engineering exam.

6348
10:33:59,566 --> 10:34:05,668
You are not being asked to interpret a regulation and you are being asked what a stated requirement implies technically.

6349
10:34:06,190 --> 10:34:10,404
Write to erasure means you need to know where data went, which is lineage.

6350
10:34:11,310 --> 10:34:15,044
To be able to delete a row, which is an open table format.

6351
10:34:15,950 --> 10:34:17,732
Breach notification means

6352
10:34:18,670 --> 10:34:25,668
you need logs good enough to establish what was accessed and by whom, which is cloud trail with data events.

6353
10:34:26,717 --> 10:34:33,197
Reading the left column and producing the right column is the whole skill and every entry has appeared somewhere in this course.

6354
10:34:34,894 --> 10:34:39,328
A company is building a governance model for a lake with several hundred tables.

6355
10:34:39,488 --> 10:34:47,568
It wants a dataset classified as restricted to automatically be encrypted with a customer managed key hidden from general analyst roles

6356
10:34:47,808 --> 10:34:49,476
and retained for 7 years.

6357
10:34:50,254 --> 10:34:57,796
The team currently records classifications in a shared spreadsheet and applies the controls by hand when a table is created.

6358
10:34:58,670 --> 10:35:00,772
What should the data engineer implement?

6359
10:35:01,230 --> 10:35:04,996
a move the spreadsheet into a wiki so it is easier to find.

6360
10:35:05,488 --> 10:35:14,980
b Apply the classification as an LF tag and an S3 object tag with lake formation grants and lifecycle rules written against the tag values.

6361
10:35:15,648 --> 10:35:15,888
C.

6362
10:35:16,528 --> 10:35:20,324
Write a runbook describing the manual steps for each classification level.

6363
10:35:20,848 --> 10:35:21,168
D.

6364
10:35:21,486 --> 10:35:24,772
Create a separate AWS account per classification level.

6365
10:35:25,328 --> 10:35:26,564
Pause and decide.

6366
10:35:32,878 --> 10:35:38,756
The answer is B, and the word automatically in the requirement rules out three options immediately.

6367
10:35:39,182 --> 10:35:46,596
A spreadsheet, a wiki, and a runbook all describe what a person is supposed to do, and the failure mode for all three is identical.

6368
10:35:47,568 --> 10:35:52,196
Somebody does not do it, usually under time pressure on the day it matters most.

6369
10:35:52,768 --> 10:35:55,488
Tags are different in kind because systems wreck them.

6370
10:35:55,728 --> 10:36:00,608
Lake formation grants written against tag values apply the moment a table is tagged.

6371
10:36:00,848 --> 10:36:05,156
Lifecycle rules filtering on an object tag apply to objects as they are written.

6372
10:36:06,048 --> 10:36:12,292
That is what makes the classification enforce itself, which is the entire point of classifying anything.

6373
10:36:13,966 --> 10:36:21,700
The callout is the organizational point underneath the subscription workflow and it explains why AWS Build 1.

6374
10:36:22,478 --> 10:36:28,768
A data platform engineer asked to approve access to a finance data set has no basis for deciding.

6375
10:36:29,262 --> 10:36:35,268
That is because they do not know who should see it. So they either refuse and escalate or approve and hope.

6376
10:36:36,750 --> 10:36:37,924
Neither is governance.

6377
10:36:38,478 --> 10:36:44,580
Putting the decision with the owner who understands both the data and the requester's purpose makes the approval meaningful.

6378
10:36:45,422 --> 10:36:55,044
The engineering contribution is the mechanism that carries the decision to an actual permission, which is what the flow later in this module describes.

6379
10:37:02,830 --> 10:37:09,380
Data exchange together, and the distinction the exam draws is between two quite different situations.

6380
10:37:13,358 --> 10:37:20,417
Inside an organization, you control both sides of the arrangement. Identities are federated from a single directory.

6381
10:37:21,518 --> 10:37:25,540
The mechanisms are the ones modules 10 and 18 covered.

6382
10:37:26,446 --> 10:37:36,100
Outside, the other party has their own accounts, their own identities, their own security posture and a commercial relationship with you. There may be money changing hands.

6383
10:37:37,038 --> 10:37:44,036
The mechanisms differ accordingly, and choosing an internal mechanism for an external requirement is the characteristic error here.

6384
10:37:45,358 --> 10:37:49,604
The second work scenario in this module is built on exactly that mistake.

6385
10:37:51,310 --> 10:38:00,608
AWS data exchange is usually discussed from the publisher's side and the consumer side raises governance questions worth knowing about.

6386
10:38:03,758 --> 10:38:12,804
Data subscribed from a third party lands in your account and immediately becomes subject to your own classification, retention, and access rules.

6387
10:38:13,646 --> 10:38:18,404
That is because your regulator does not care where it came from originally.

6388
10:38:18,688 --> 10:38:26,820
It also carries license terms the supplier set, which may restrict onward sharing or require deletion when the subscription ends.

6389
10:38:27,248 --> 10:38:31,492
Those terms are a constraint your access model has to respect deliberately.

6390
10:38:32,238 --> 10:38:40,292
The revisions point connects back to module 12 because a new monthly revision is a schema change waiting to happen in somebody's pipeline.

6391
10:38:41,968 --> 10:38:50,948
The last row is deliberately included because copying data to a consumer is what organizations do when they do not know the alternatives.

6392
10:38:51,950 --> 10:38:55,140
It appears as a distractor constantly across this exam.

6393
10:38:56,142 --> 10:39:02,884
A copy means two datasets that drift apart within weeks, two sets of permissions to maintain, two storage bills.

6394
10:39:03,566 --> 10:39:07,332
A governance question about the second copy that nobody has taken ownership of.

6395
10:39:07,808 --> 10:39:12,100
Every other row on this table shares access to one authoritative dataset instead.

6396
10:39:12,942 --> 10:39:19,204
So when a question describes a consumer needing data, look first for the mechanism that avoids duplication.

6397
10:39:21,422 --> 10:39:27,748
Treat an export or a copy as the answer only when the question genuinely rules the alternatives out.

6398
10:39:29,678 --> 10:39:36,420
AWS Data Exchange was added to the InScope service list in the December 2025 revision.

6399
10:39:36,720 --> 10:39:39,556
So it is genuinely new material for this exam.

6400
10:39:40,622 --> 10:39:47,972
The framing that makes it examinable is that it handles everything around the data rather than the data itself.

6401
10:39:48,878 --> 10:39:52,800
Who is entitled to this data set? For how long? At what price?

6402
10:39:53,070 --> 10:39:55,716
How do they receive new revisions when you publish them?

6403
10:39:57,582 --> 10:40:01,092
How does entitlement cleanly end when a subscription lapses?

6404
10:40:02,030 --> 10:40:11,556
Building that yourself means a delivery pipeline plus a billing relationship plus an access management problem, all of which have to keep working.

6405
10:40:12,462 --> 10:40:21,092
So a question describing a company wanting to sell or systematically distribute a dataset to external parties is pointing directly at it.

6406
10:40:22,766 --> 10:40:28,100
The mechanisms overlap, which is precisely why the distinction is worth stating carefully.

6407
10:40:28,480 --> 10:40:37,732
LakeFormation clause account sharing works perfectly well to a partner's account, and module 18 covered how column and row filter survive it intact.

6408
10:40:38,414 --> 10:40:44,772
What changes outside the organization is everything surrounding the grant rather than the grant itself.

6409
10:40:45,326 --> 10:40:51,428
There is a contract rather than an internal policy. Entitlement typically has a start and an end date.

6410
10:40:52,462 --> 10:40:55,844
Revocation becomes a commercial event as well as a technical one.

6411
10:40:56,782 --> 10:41:01,700
AWS data exchange exists because that surrounding machinery is substantial.

6412
10:41:02,350 --> 10:41:09,508
A question mentioning subscribers, entitlement, revisions, or payment is describing it rather than a plain cross-account grant.

6413
10:41:11,120 --> 10:41:17,892
A market data company wants to sell a curated dataset to around 200 external customers.

6414
10:41:18,400 --> 10:41:28,900
Subscribers should receive monthly revisions automatically. Access should end when a subscription lapses and the company does not want to build billing or entitlement management.

6415
10:41:29,440 --> 10:41:37,028
A proposal is to create an S3 bucket per customer and copy the dataset into each one after every monthly build.

6416
10:41:38,286 --> 10:41:40,644
What should the data engineer recommend instead?

6417
10:41:42,414 --> 10:41:48,484
a AWS Data Exchange Publishing the dataset with managed entitlements and revisions.

6418
10:41:50,960 --> 10:41:51,280
b

6419
10:41:52,494 --> 10:41:56,996
Lake Formation cross account grants to each of the 200 customer accounts.

6420
10:41:57,440 --> 10:41:57,680
C.

6421
10:41:58,254 --> 10:42:02,468
One shared bucket with a bucket policy listing all 200 accounts.

6422
10:42:02,880 --> 10:42:03,120
D.

6423
10:42:03,758 --> 10:42:07,620
Keep the per customer buckets but automate the copying with a glue job.

6424
10:42:08,000 --> 10:42:09,284
Pause and decide.

6425
10:42:15,982 --> 10:42:23,812
The answer is A, and the requirement names the three things that distinguish external commercial sharing from ordinary cross account access.

6426
10:42:24,430 --> 10:42:29,700
Subscribers, automatic monthly revisions and access ending when a subscription lapses.

6427
10:42:30,702 --> 10:42:40,644
Option B is worth respecting because it would genuinely work for the access part, and Lake Formation cross account grants do reach partner accounts perfectly well.

6428
10:42:41,422 --> 10:42:47,040
What it leaves is the company building exactly the entitlement, revision, and billing machinery.

6429
10:42:47,374 --> 10:42:50,084
They explicitly said they did not want to build.

6430
10:42:51,790 --> 10:42:56,516
Option D is the automation trap that has appeared several times in this course.

6431
10:42:57,520 --> 10:43:02,564
Automating a bad design makes it faster to execute without making it any better.

6432
10:43:04,206 --> 10:43:12,772
Skill 4.5.5 names maintaining data sovereignty and it is a genuinely different kind of requirement from everything else in this domain.

6433
10:43:13,326 --> 10:43:16,676
Encryption and permissions control who can read data.

6434
10:43:17,454 --> 10:43:25,060
Sovereignty controls where it physically resides and increasingly who is permitted to operate the infrastructure holding it.

6435
10:43:26,222 --> 10:43:29,924
A regulator may require that citizens' health records never leave the country.

6436
10:43:30,798 --> 10:43:39,076
Or that personal data of European residents stays inside the European Union and is not accessible to personnel outside it.

6437
10:43:39,662 --> 10:43:46,020
Those constraints shape architecture rather than configuration, they cannot be retrofitted with a policy change.

6438
10:43:46,926 --> 10:43:52,164
AWS has built specific capabilities for them, which the next few slides cover.

6439
10:43:53,710 --> 10:43:59,908
The call out describes the failure that audits actually find rather than the one people design against.

6440
10:44:00,304 --> 10:44:05,572
A team correctly places a dataset in a compliant region and thinks the job is done.

6441
10:44:06,190 --> 10:44:09,252
Then a backup is replicated elsewhere for resilience.

6442
10:44:10,126 --> 10:44:13,828
Or CloudWatch logs from the processing job land in a different region.

6443
10:44:14,384 --> 10:44:19,780
Or an Atena result location was configured once years ago and never reviewed since.

6444
10:44:20,224 --> 10:44:27,744
Each of those is a copy of regulated data outside the boundary created by a system doing something entirely sensible.

6445
10:44:28,238 --> 10:44:32,740
So a sovereignty design has to account for every derived artifact.

6446
10:44:32,944 --> 10:44:35,588
That is the same principle module 19.

6447
10:44:35,904 --> 10:44:41,476
Stated about logs and query results inheriting the sensitivity of the source.

6448
10:44:43,086 --> 10:44:51,812
This is why the European Sovereign Cloud exists as a separate partition rather than as simply another region, and the progression is worth understanding.

6449
10:44:53,486 --> 10:44:59,268
The original sovereignty question was about geography and choosing a region answered it completely.

6450
10:45:00,046 --> 10:45:05,828
Regulators then began asking who can access the infrastructure, which geography does not address at all.

6451
10:45:06,318 --> 10:45:11,396
That is because a support engineer on another continent may hold operational access to it.

6452
10:45:12,238 --> 10:45:18,276
Then they asked who holds the encryption keys, which is what the external key store from module 19 exists for.

6453
10:45:19,790 --> 10:45:22,532
And finally, where operational metadata and logs go.

6454
10:45:23,502 --> 10:45:28,612
That is the hardest of the four because that data is generated by the platform rather than by you.

6455
10:45:30,382 --> 10:45:36,064
This went generally available in January 2026, which is after the exam guide was published.

6456
10:45:36,558 --> 10:45:40,704
So treat it as context rather than as expected exam content.

6457
10:45:41,024 --> 10:45:47,108
It is worth knowing because it changes what the strongest answer to a sovereignty requirement looks like.

6458
10:45:48,014 --> 10:45:57,412
Previously, the answer was choosing an EU region and restricting the rest, which controls where data sits and not who operates the infrastructure.

6459
10:45:58,254 --> 10:46:05,860
The European Sovereign Cloud is a separate partition with its own identity and billing systems operated by EU residents.

6460
10:46:06,766 --> 10:46:10,276
That means operational metadata stays inside as well.

6461
10:46:11,406 --> 10:46:16,708
If a question predates it, answer with region selection and service control policies.

6462
10:46:18,446 --> 10:46:22,884
A healthcare provider must keep patient data within a single country.

6463
10:46:24,142 --> 10:46:30,820
The data lake and redshift warehouse are correctly deployed in the required region and access is restricted appropriately.

6464
10:46:31,344 --> 10:46:33,476
An audit has found three issues.

6465
10:46:34,382 --> 10:46:38,624
Redshift snapshots are copied to a second region for disaster recovery.

6466
10:46:38,784 --> 10:46:43,044
Blue job logs are delivered to a log archive account in another region.

6467
10:46:43,502 --> 10:46:49,124
An Athena work group writes query results to a bucket created years ago in a third region.

6468
10:46:49,774 --> 10:46:52,676
What is the underlying problem and what should be done?

6469
10:46:56,046 --> 10:47:05,744
Derived copies inherit the residency requirement. Bring snapshots, logs, and results into the required region and use an SCP to restrict region use.

6470
10:47:06,414 --> 10:47:11,556
b Encrypt the snapshots, logs, and results so their location no longer matters.

6471
10:47:12,544 --> 10:47:12,864
C.

6472
10:47:14,030 --> 10:47:17,924
Delete the disaster recovery copies and accept the reduced resilience.

6473
10:47:19,104 --> 10:47:19,424
D.

6474
10:47:19,918 --> 10:47:23,492
Apply lake formation column level permissions to the affected tables.

6475
10:47:24,270 --> 10:47:25,380
Pause and decide.

6476
10:47:32,064 --> 10:47:38,660
The answer is A, and recognizing that three separate findings share one cause is the skill being tested.

6477
10:47:39,024 --> 10:47:46,084
Snapshots, logs, and query results are all copies of regulated data created by systems behaving reasonably.

6478
10:47:46,464 --> 10:47:50,532
Each escaped a boundary that was correctly applied to the primary dataset.

6479
10:47:51,118 --> 10:48:00,388
Option B is the tempting one because encryption feels like it should help, and residency is a question about physical location rather than readability.

6480
10:48:02,062 --> 10:48:04,996
The second half of A matters as much as the first.

6481
10:48:05,902 --> 10:48:14,116
That is because relocating three artifacts fixes today and a service control policy, restricting regions prevents the fourth one appearing next year.

6482
10:48:15,630 --> 10:48:19,904
Modules 6 and 12 both deferred this topic to here.

6483
10:48:20,144 --> 10:48:23,504
Scales 4.1.7 and 4.5.6.

6484
10:48:24,270 --> 10:48:28,580
Name SageMaker Unified Studio and SageMaker Catalog Projects.

6485
10:48:28,864 --> 10:48:34,304
Both are new in the December 2025 revision, so no older material covers them.

6486
10:48:34,862 --> 10:48:40,064
The model is worth understanding because it organizes access differently from anything else in this course.

6487
10:48:40,334 --> 10:48:46,180
Rather than granting individuals permissions on datasets, you create a project representing a piece of work.

6488
10:48:46,478 --> 10:48:49,572
That project subscribes to the datasets it needs.

6489
10:48:50,382 --> 10:48:55,652
People who join the project inherit that access for exactly as long as they remain on it.

6490
10:48:56,110 --> 10:49:00,228
Access follows the work rather than the person, which is a meaningful shift.

6491
10:49:01,902 --> 10:49:06,212
The callout is what stops this being a separate word to learn from scratch.

6492
10:49:06,734 --> 10:49:13,060
When a project subscribes to a dataset and the owner approves, the underlying grant is a lake formation permission.

6493
10:49:13,742 --> 10:49:18,144
Exactly the kind module 18 described applied automatically.

6494
10:49:18,638 --> 10:49:24,932
Enforcement happens in the same place through the same catalog with the same column and row filters applied by the same engines.

6495
10:49:25,424 --> 10:49:29,924
What the project model changes is who decides and how it is recorded.

6496
10:49:30,254 --> 10:49:34,596
It moves from a ticket to a platform engineer to an approval by the data's owner.

6497
10:49:35,504 --> 10:49:42,180
The request and the approval are both captured against the asset, which is what makes the decision auditable later.

6498
10:49:43,758 --> 10:49:53,700
This flow is what module 12 described as the subscription workflow, and the callout is why it matters for domain 4 rather than only for discoverability.

6499
10:49:54,318 --> 10:49:58,704
In a conventional model, an analyst asks an engineer for access.

6500
10:49:59,726 --> 10:50:01,412
The engineer writes a grant.

6501
10:50:02,158 --> 10:50:06,212
Six months later, nobody can say who approved it or why.

6502
10:50:06,862 --> 10:50:13,447
Here the request states a purpose. the owner rather than the platform team decides and the approval is recorded against the asset.

6503
10:50:14,542 --> 10:50:21,092
When an auditor asks why this team can see this data, the answer is a record rather than an archaeology exercise.

6504
10:50:21,486 --> 10:50:24,964
That is exactly what skill 4.5.3 is about.

6505
10:50:26,414 --> 10:50:33,508
Module 12 introduce the business catalog as a discovery problem, and this is the governance consequence.

6506
10:50:34,510 --> 10:50:40,804
A consumer choosing between 9 tables that all look like they contain revenue needs more than names.

6507
10:50:41,710 --> 10:50:50,372
They need to know who owns each one, whether it's quality checks passed last night, where its data came from, whether it is still being updated.

6508
10:50:51,022 --> 10:50:54,180
Every one of those has a mechanism this course has covered.

6509
10:50:55,086 --> 10:51:02,224
Publishing them alongside the asset is what turns a catalog from a list into something a person can make a decision from.

6510
10:51:02,384 --> 10:51:08,772
Without it, dataset selection happens by reputation and rumor, and different teams report different numbers.

6511
10:51:10,446 --> 10:51:11,424
Module 12.

6512
10:51:11,630 --> 10:51:19,396
Introduce lineage for impact assessment, which is the engineering use, and the compliance use is the same mechanism pointed the other way.

6513
10:51:20,110 --> 10:51:29,380
When a regulator asks where personal data ends up or a customer requests erasure, the question is which datasets contain data derived from this source.

6514
10:51:29,902 --> 10:51:35,524
Without lineage, that is answered by asking people what they remember, which is neither complete nor defensible.

6515
10:51:36,302 --> 10:51:38,468
With column level lineage, it is a query.

6516
10:51:39,342 --> 10:51:43,716
The versioning point on the second bullet is what makes historical reporting defensible.

6517
10:51:44,622 --> 10:51:51,140
That is because you can show what the pipeline was at the time a figure was published rather than what it is now.

6518
10:51:52,814 --> 10:51:59,707
Module 14 made the point that an auditor asks whether records could have been altered by the very people they describe.

6519
10:52:00,462 --> 10:52:03,588
This slide generalizes it across the whole domain.

6520
10:52:03,982 --> 10:52:09,668
Each mechanism in modules 17 through 20 produces an artifact somebody can be shown.

6521
10:52:10,222 --> 10:52:12,964
Cloud Trail for who changed what and when.

6522
10:52:13,518 --> 10:52:18,980
The subscription workflow for who requested access, on what stated purpose, and who approved it.

6523
10:52:19,694 --> 10:52:22,180
Lineage for where a published number originated.

6524
10:52:22,862 --> 10:52:29,764
And object lock on the archives holding all of it. So the evidence itself is credible rather than merely present.

6525
10:52:30,222 --> 10:52:38,052
A governance program that cannot produce those artifacts on request is a set of intentions rather than a set of controls.

6526
10:52:39,790 --> 10:52:43,780
A bank must respond to customer erasure requests within 30 days.

6527
10:52:44,302 --> 10:52:54,308
Customer records enter through three ingestion pipelines and are derived into an unknown number of downstream tables, dashboards, and extracts across four accounts.

6528
10:52:55,246 --> 10:53:05,028
The team currently answers each request by asking every data owner whether they hold data for that customer, which takes weeks and is not provably complete.

6529
10:53:05,550 --> 10:53:07,204
What should the bank implement?

6530
10:53:07,790 --> 10:53:15,972
a column level data lineage in SageMaker catalog to trace forward from the ingestion points to every derived dataset.

6531
10:53:16,430 --> 10:53:21,892
b Amazon Macy to scan every bucket for the customer's identifiers on each request.

6532
10:53:22,352 --> 10:53:27,876
C. An AWS config rule that reports which datasets contain personal data.

6533
10:53:28,462 --> 10:53:28,672
D.

6534
10:53:29,614 --> 10:53:30,308
Object.

6535
10:53:32,014 --> 10:53:35,872
Lock on the ingestion buckets so the source records cannot be altered.

6536
10:53:36,112 --> 10:53:37,252
Pause and decide.

6537
10:53:43,918 --> 10:53:50,948
The answer is A. An option D is worth naming because it is actively harmful rather than merely unhelpful.

6538
10:53:51,598 --> 10:53:58,244
Applying object lock to data subject to Irasha requests creates exactly the conflict module A described.

6539
10:53:59,182 --> 10:54:05,956
A retention control and a deletion obligation cannot both be satisfied on the same objects, so that option makes the problem permanent.

6540
10:54:06,350 --> 10:54:09,956
The distinction between A and B is a genuinely useful one to carry.

6541
10:54:10,926 --> 10:54:17,284
Macy answers what kind of sensitive data is in this bucket, which is a discovery question about categories.

6542
10:54:17,632 --> 10:54:21,348
Lineage answers where did data from this source end up?

6543
10:54:22,032 --> 10:54:28,132
That is a derivation question about a specific data set and only the second scales to an Idashar obligation.

6544
10:54:29,870 --> 10:54:35,332
This slide states the through line of the module because it is what the four scenarios have in common.

6545
10:54:35,758 --> 10:54:42,432
Each one gave you a requirement expressed in business or regulatory language and asked which technical mechanism makes it

6546
10:54:42,432 --> 10:54:50,272
true. Restricted data must be encrypted and hidden, becomes tags driving grants and lifecycle rules.

6547
10:54:50,512 --> 10:54:55,712
Subscribers must lose access when they lapse, becomes data exchange entitlements.

6548
10:54:56,032 --> 10:55:02,756
Patient data must not leave the country, becomes region restriction, plus accounting for derived copies.

6549
10:55:08,238 --> 10:55:15,364
That translation is the data engineer's contribution to governance, and it is precisely what this domain examines.

6550
10:55:17,102 --> 10:55:25,284
The last bullet is the reading skill this domain rewards most, and it is worth practicing because the four layers use heavily overlapping vocabulary.

6551
10:55:26,030 --> 10:55:33,348
A question about a team seeing columns they should not is authorization, and every encryption option in it is a distractor.

6552
10:55:33,998 --> 10:55:42,020
A question about protecting data if a disk is stolen or a connection is intercepted is encryption, and permissions are the distractors.

6553
10:55:43,022 --> 10:55:50,660
A question about proving to an auditor why a team has access is governance, and both of the above become distractors.

6554
10:55:51,472 --> 10:56:00,388
Identifying which of the four layers is being asked about eliminates most options before you evaluate any of them on technical merit at all.

6555
10:56:00,992 --> 10:56:04,004
That is exactly what makes domain 4 learnable.

6556
10:56:05,614 --> 10:56:14,148
This table is the module as a diagnosis, and the callout states what makes governance examinable at all on a technical certification.

6557
10:56:14,784 --> 10:56:23,012
Each row is a policy or organizational failure, and each has a specific AWS mechanism that addresses it directly.

6558
10:56:24,238 --> 10:56:34,212
Tags for classification, service control policies for residency, subscription workflows for approval, recorded grants for auditability,

6559
10:56:34,544 --> 10:56:39,972
lineage for erasure obligations, and proper sharing mechanisms for duplication.

6560
10:56:40,910 --> 10:56:45,956
A governance question describes the failure in business terms and expects you to name the mechanism.

6561
10:56:46,446 --> 10:56:53,188
That is exactly why the framework slide near the start of this module mapped each concern onto a service you already know.

6562
10:56:54,926 --> 10:56:55,984
5 questions.

6563
10:56:56,750 --> 10:57:00,100
And the fourth is the one that catches people in sovereignty scenarios.

6564
10:57:00,686 --> 10:57:07,108
That is because the primary dataset is almost always placed correctly and the failure is in something derived from it.

6565
10:57:07,918 --> 10:57:10,864
That completes the teaching content of this course.

6566
10:57:11,342 --> 10:57:17,860
20 modules covering all four domains from the mental model in module 1 through do governance here.

6567
10:57:18,542 --> 10:57:20,964
The remaining modules are different in character.

6568
10:57:21,678 --> 10:57:24,516
How the exam writes its questions and how to read them.

6569
10:57:25,104 --> 10:57:30,052
The specific gap between the December 2025 revision and older material.

6570
10:57:30,990 --> 10:57:36,292
The confusion pairs that separate similar services and three full practice exams.

6571
10:57:38,030 --> 10:57:39,204
The teaching is finished.

6572
10:57:39,630 --> 10:57:45,028
This module and the two after it are about answering questions rather than about AWS itself.

6573
10:57:46,606 --> 10:57:53,188
The subject here is the overlap between services, because that overlap is exactly where the exam burns its distractors.

6574
10:57:53,934 --> 10:58:01,540
When four options are all technically capable of solving the problem described, product knowledge cannot separate them and something else has to.

6575
10:58:02,062 --> 10:58:08,260
This module collects every pair this course has distinguished into one place, with the deciding signal for each one.

6576
10:58:09,038 --> 10:58:18,020
Eight worked scenarios deliberately arranged in pairs so that the same situation with a single changed constraint moves the answer somewhere else entirely.

6577
10:58:18,478 --> 10:58:22,532
That structure is the point of the module rather than a presentational choice.

6578
10:58:24,014 --> 10:58:30,340
That single sentence explains most of the wrong answers across this course's 80 worked scenarios.

6579
10:58:31,904 --> 10:58:35,584
Concurrency scaling in module 10 was not a bad idea.

6580
10:58:35,824 --> 10:58:40,864
It was the correct answer to a queuing problem offered in the question about execution time.

6581
10:58:41,358 --> 10:58:49,344
A blue job in module 3 was genuinely the better engineering offered to a team who had just said they had nobody available to run it.

6582
10:58:49,614 --> 10:58:57,732
Amazon EMR in module 5 was entirely capable of the work, and so was Blue, had considerably less operational overhead.

6583
10:58:58,542 --> 10:59:05,636
In each case, the option was defensible on its own terms, and the question had already ruled it out in a sentence.

6584
10:59:07,470 --> 10:59:11,012
Learning to see that is worth more than learning another service.

6585
10:59:12,750 --> 10:59:18,116
The callout is the practical argument for this module's format, and it is worth taking seriously.

6586
10:59:18,638 --> 10:59:22,468
65 questions in 130 minutes is 2 minutes each.

6587
10:59:22,894 --> 10:59:27,876
Some of those questions run to a full paragraph of scenario before you reach the options.

6588
10:59:28,750 --> 10:59:33,664
Suppose your method is to recall what each service does and reason about which fits.

6589
10:59:33,904 --> 10:59:38,784
That is a minute spent on one question and you will run out of time before you run out of questions.

6590
10:59:39,024 --> 10:59:45,188
Scanning instead for a non-spark engine or for the phrase least operational overhead takes a few seconds.

6591
10:59:45,998 --> 10:59:46,980
Then you move on.

6592
10:59:48,238 --> 10:59:54,308
This module is organized around signals rather than comparisons for exactly that reason.

6593
10:59:55,598 --> 10:59:57,252
Two of these deserve a second look.

6594
10:59:57,424 --> 11:00:04,164
The fire hose against flink distinction is about whether anything happens between the producer and the destination.

6595
11:00:05,024 --> 11:00:07,012
The signal words are windowing,

6596
11:00:07,758 --> 11:00:13,504
aggregation over time, joining two streams, or detecting a pattern across events.

6597
11:00:14,094 --> 11:00:22,308
If none of those appear, firehose is delivering rather than processing, and flink is more machinery than the question needs.

6598
11:00:24,910 --> 11:00:33,604
The DMS against Gluro is about the source rather than the destination, because both can write into a lake perfectly well.

6599
11:00:34,510 --> 11:00:42,500
Ongoing change from a live database is DMS which change data capture and files arriving in a bucket are blue.

6600
11:00:43,278 --> 11:00:46,212
Neither is about where the data ends up.

6601
11:00:47,502 --> 11:00:54,148
This is the pair most often confused because both are called kinesis in older material and both carry records.

6602
11:00:56,142 --> 11:00:59,268
The two capabilities in the callout are the whole difference.

6603
11:00:59,840 --> 11:01:05,700
A data stream retains records for at least 24 hours, so a consumer that failed can reprocess.

6604
11:01:06,606 --> 11:01:11,652
Several consumers can read the same records independently for different purposes.

6605
11:01:12,160 --> 11:01:20,868
Firehose does neither because it exists to move data from a producer to one destination with no consumer for you to write.

6606
11:01:21,774 --> 11:01:30,800
So, a question mentioning replaying data, reprocessing after a bug, or two teams consuming the same stream has already ruled Firehose

6607
11:01:31,040 --> 11:01:31,360
out.

6608
11:01:32,622 --> 11:01:37,600
The GNU against EMRO is the highest value elimination in domain

6609
11:01:38,318 --> 11:01:40,560
1 because it resolves in a few words.

6610
11:01:41,440 --> 11:01:50,852
GRU runs Spark and Python and nothing else. So naming hive, hbase, presto, trino or pig removes it instantly.

6611
11:01:51,822 --> 11:01:57,732
Absent one of those and absent an existing Hadoop estate, somebody does not want to rewrite.

6612
11:01:58,254 --> 11:02:02,532
The phrase least operational overhead points at blue every time.

6613
11:02:03,310 --> 11:02:06,880
The last row is worth carrying as a habit rather than a pair.

6614
11:02:07,280 --> 11:02:12,868
Data already sitting in Redshift being reshaped into another Redshift table should not leave the warehouse.

6615
11:02:13,422 --> 11:02:16,324
The option that exports it is there to be rejected.

6616
11:02:17,838 --> 11:02:25,348
This is the most frequently offered pair in domain 1, and treating it as an elimination rather than a comparison is what makes it fast.

6617
11:02:27,534 --> 11:02:30,788
Glue runs Spark and Python and nothing else.

6618
11:02:31,278 --> 11:02:40,516
So the moment a question names Hive, HBase, Presto, Krino, Flink or PIG, Glue is gone and you have the answer in seconds.

6619
11:02:40,942 --> 11:02:49,380
The second reason is migration economics, where an organization with years of hive scripts can lift them onto EMR almost unchanged.

6620
11:02:50,190 --> 11:02:58,084
The third is spot pricing, which Glue has no equivalent for and which can cut a sustained workload's compute cost dramatically.

6621
11:02:58,830 --> 11:03:02,500
Absent all three, least operational overhead points at Blue.

6622
11:03:04,270 --> 11:03:10,596
The Snow family rows carry the availability warning module for covered and the guidance is unchanged.

6623
11:03:10,880 --> 11:03:20,580
Questions written before the retirement will still offer Snowball as the answer for moving petabytes where bandwidth makes a network transfer take months.

6624
11:03:21,486 --> 11:03:31,268
That reasoning is still what the exam tests. Answer from the documented catalog and know that in reality AWS now points at data sync, data transfer terminal and outposts.

6625
11:03:32,142 --> 11:03:35,652
The DMS row is the one to read carefully.

6626
11:03:36,142 --> 11:03:46,756
That is because the deciding question is whether the source keeps changing during the migration, which is exactly what change data capture exists for.

6627
11:03:48,398 --> 11:03:57,284
A retailer streams point of sale events and needs them delivered into Amazon S3 as per K, partitioned by date for later analysis.

6628
11:03:57,840 --> 11:04:04,324
No processing is required between the producer and the destination, and a delay of a minute or two is acceptable.

6629
11:04:05,102 --> 11:04:09,060
The team has nobody available to build or operate a stream consumer.

6630
11:04:09,518 --> 11:04:11,460
What should the data engineer use?

6631
11:04:12,622 --> 11:04:16,996
a Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with a Lambda consumer writing parquet.

6632
11:04:17,710 --> 11:04:22,116
B Amazon Data Firehose with format conversion and dynamic partitioning.

6633
11:04:22,894 --> 11:04:23,200
C

6634
11:04:24,462 --> 11:04:28,196
Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink writing to Amazon S3.

6635
11:04:28,782 --> 11:04:31,120
D Amazon MSK with a Kafka.

6636
11:04:31,726 --> 11:04:33,252
Connect S3 sync.

6637
11:04:33,600 --> 11:04:34,948
Pause and decide.

6638
11:04:41,648 --> 11:04:48,068
The answer is B, and the question states all three signals explicitly, which is unusually generous.

6639
11:04:48,368 --> 11:04:55,716
Nothing needs to happen to the data between the producer and the destination, so no processing engine is required and flink is eliminated.

6640
11:04:56,462 --> 11:05:01,220
Latency of a minute or two is acceptable, so buffering is entirely fine.

6641
11:05:01,728 --> 11:05:07,620
And there is nobody available to operate a consumer, which is the sentence that removes every other option.

6642
11:05:08,110 --> 11:05:12,772
That is because each of them involves something to build, deploy, and run.

6643
11:05:13,774 --> 11:05:22,020
Now hold this scenario in mind because the next one changes exactly one thing about it, and the answer moves.

6644
11:05:23,790 --> 11:05:30,884
The same retailer, the same point of sale events, the same parquet destination in Amazon S3.

6645
11:05:31,790 --> 11:05:33,968
The requirement has changed in one respect.

6646
11:05:34,286 --> 11:05:39,300
A fraud team now needs to read the same events independently in near real time.

6647
11:05:40,110 --> 11:05:49,860
And a reporting team needs to reprocess the last three days of events after fixing a bug in their logic. The delivery to Amazon S3 must continue as before.

6648
11:05:50,288 --> 11:05:52,528
What should the data engineer use now?

6649
11:05:52,782 --> 11:05:56,260
a second firehose stream from the same producer.

6650
11:05:57,248 --> 11:05:57,568
B

6651
11:05:58,862 --> 11:06:04,772
Kinesis data streams with firehose as one consumer and the fraud team as another.

6652
11:06:05,248 --> 11:06:10,884
C. Firehose with an S3 destination and have the fraud team read the S3 files.

6653
11:06:11,726 --> 11:06:16,932
D. Increase the firehose buffer interval so more data is available for reprocessing.

6654
11:06:18,958 --> 11:06:20,260
Pause and decide.

6655
11:06:27,310 --> 11:06:32,368
The answer is B, and this pairing is the module's whole method in miniature.

6656
11:06:32,608 --> 11:06:38,180
Nothing about the destination changed, and Firehose is still the right way to reach Amazon S3.

6657
11:06:39,086 --> 11:06:47,620
What changed is that two capabilities, Firehose does not have became requirements, and both were named on the compare slide a few minutes ago.

6658
11:06:48,302 --> 11:06:54,180
Replay, because the reporting team must reprocess three days of events after fixing their logic.

6659
11:06:54,990 --> 11:07:00,528
And independent consumers, because the fraud team needs the same records live.

6660
11:07:01,168 --> 11:07:10,340
So a data stream sits in front, Firehose becomes one of its consumers, and the original design survives intact inside the new one.

6661
11:07:11,758 --> 11:07:18,180
The last row is the one worth generalizing because the same decision appears in three separate modules under three different names.

6662
11:07:18,528 --> 11:07:26,884
A transient failure, a network timeout, or a throttled API should be retried with back-off because it may well succeed on the next attempt.

6663
11:07:27,630 --> 11:07:34,532
A permanent failure, a malformed record or a missing reference value will fail identically every time.

6664
11:07:35,374 --> 11:07:40,484
So retrying is pure waste and worse, it blocks everything queued behind it.

6665
11:07:41,008 --> 11:07:42,756
Route it aside instead.

6666
11:07:44,046 --> 11:07:51,556
That is a dead letter Q in module 6, a catch state in step functions, and quarantining rows in module 15.

6667
11:07:52,398 --> 11:07:55,844
One decision, three mechanisms, and the exam tests.

6668
11:07:56,288 --> 11:07:56,964
All three.

6669
11:07:58,670 --> 11:08:03,968
This pair is unusual because the answer is frequently both and the exam still makes you choose.

6670
11:08:04,208 --> 11:08:10,564
The distinction is that event bridge notices something and starts something with no memory and no notion of a workflow.

6671
11:08:11,214 --> 11:08:18,404
Step functions holds the shape of a multi-step pipeline, knows which step is running, and decides what happens when one fails.

6672
11:08:18,608 --> 11:08:24,228
So a question describing a single action triggered by a file landing is event bridge alone.

6673
11:08:25,070 --> 11:08:30,596
A question describing four steps that must run in order with alerting on failure needs coordination.

6674
11:08:31,470 --> 11:08:36,644
An option offering event bridge on its own, for that is missing everything after the first step.

6675
11:08:37,998 --> 11:08:42,564
A pipeline runs four AWS blue jobs in sequence, followed by a blue crawler.

6676
11:08:42,958 --> 11:08:48,900
Each step must start only when the previous one has succeeded, and the team wants to be alerted if any step fails.

6677
11:08:49,168 --> 11:08:50,688
There are no other components.

6678
11:08:50,990 --> 11:08:53,156
The team wants the least operational overhead.

6679
11:08:53,998 --> 11:08:55,888
What should the data engineer use?

6680
11:08:56,238 --> 11:08:59,888
a an AWS glue workflow with conditional triggers.

6681
11:09:00,208 --> 11:09:04,708
B an AWS step functions, state machine invoking each job.

6682
11:09:05,518 --> 11:09:13,444
C Amazon MW A A A with a D A G defining the dependencies.

6683
11:09:14,126 --> 11:09:17,956
D event bridge rules chaining each job to the next.

6684
11:09:18,606 --> 11:09:20,004
Pause and decide.

6685
11:09:26,702 --> 11:09:31,104
The answer is A and it turns entirely on a count rather than on a comparison.

6686
11:09:31,344 --> 11:09:36,836
Four jobs and a crawler are all blue components, so the pipeline never leaves the service.

6687
11:09:37,184 --> 11:09:41,744
Blue workflows orchestrate it with nothing new to operate, learn, or pay for.

6688
11:09:42,382 --> 11:09:52,228
Step functions would work perfectly well here and is a genuinely good answer to a slightly different question. That is exactly the question the next scenario asks.

6689
11:09:52,654 --> 11:09:58,468
This is the shape module 6's first scenario had and the counting habit is what makes it quick.

6690
11:09:59,342 --> 11:10:04,260
Look at the components in the pipeline before you look at the orchestrators offered.

6691
11:10:05,518 --> 11:10:08,004
The same pipeline with one addition.

6692
11:10:09,454 --> 11:10:15,428
After the crawler completes, an AWS Lambda function must post a summary to a chat channel.

6693
11:10:16,174 --> 11:10:20,420
A redshift stored procedure must run to refresh a set of aggregates.

6694
11:10:21,390 --> 11:10:27,556
The dependency and alerting requirements are unchanged and the team still wants the least operational overhead.

6695
11:10:28,206 --> 11:10:30,084
What should the data engineer use now?

6696
11:10:30,766 --> 11:10:35,396
a Keep the Blue workflow and trigger the Lambda function from a glue job.

6697
11:10:36,462 --> 11:10:41,412
B an AWS step function state machine coordinating all seven steps.

6698
11:10:42,030 --> 11:10:42,304
C

6699
11:10:43,566 --> 11:10:46,724
Two Glue workflows with an event bridge rule between them.

6700
11:10:47,214 --> 11:10:53,824
D Amazon M WAA because the pipeline now spans several services.

6701
11:10:55,342 --> 11:10:56,484
Pause and decide.

6702
11:11:03,182 --> 11:11:04,944
The answer is B.

6703
11:11:05,678 --> 11:11:08,356
One change from zero non-blue components

6704
11:11:09,166 --> 11:11:14,244
to two moves the answer from blue workflows to step functions.

6705
11:11:15,086 --> 11:11:18,244
Nothing else about the pipeline is different at all.

6706
11:11:18,864 --> 11:11:24,580
Option A is the instructive wrong answer because it is what teams actually do.

6707
11:11:25,518 --> 11:11:30,404
They call a lambda function from inside a blue script to avoid changing the orchestrator.

6708
11:11:30,704 --> 11:11:34,276
It works and it hides those two steps from the workflow completely.

6709
11:11:34,894 --> 11:11:39,268
So a failure in the redshift procedure is invisible to whatever was watching the pipeline.

6710
11:11:40,064 --> 11:11:47,044
That defeats the alerting requirement while appearing to satisfy the design, which is the worst combination available.

6711
11:11:48,686 --> 11:11:59,024
This pair appears more often than any single service pair. The exam asks it about Glue against EMR on EC2 and Redshift serverless against a cluster.

6712
11:11:59,662 --> 11:12:04,836
About kinesis on demand against provision shards and Dynamo DBs 2 capacity modes.

6713
11:12:05,678 --> 11:12:14,596
About MSK serverless against brokers, Athena against capacity reservations, and Aurora serverless against instances.

6714
11:12:15,598 --> 11:12:19,588
Learning it once as a single decision is far more efficient than learning

6715
11:12:20,302 --> 11:12:21,892
eight separate comparisons.

6716
11:12:22,702 --> 11:12:24,900
The signal words are consistent throughout

6717
11:12:25,742 --> 11:12:32,036
Unpredictable, spiky, variable, bursty, and intermittent all point at serverless.

6718
11:12:33,326 --> 11:12:40,772
Steady, continuous, around the clock, and a stated throughput figure all point at provisioned.

6719
11:12:42,062 --> 11:12:49,284
The fifth row is worth stating clearly because these two get offered against each other and they solve genuinely different problems.

6720
11:12:49,774 --> 11:12:55,652
Versioning protects you from yourself, keeping the previous version when a job overrides good data with bad.

6721
11:12:56,078 --> 11:12:59,108
That is by far the most common data loss in practice.

6722
11:12:59,904 --> 11:13:07,524
Replication protects you from losing a region and it faithfully copies that same bad write to the second region within seconds of it happening.

6723
11:13:08,558 --> 11:13:13,220
So a question about recovering from an accidental override or a deletion is versioning.

6724
11:13:13,710 --> 11:13:15,908
And replication is the distractor.

6725
11:13:16,784 --> 11:13:23,236
A question about surviving the loss of an entire region is replication, and versioning does nothing for it at all.

6726
11:13:24,718 --> 11:13:29,348
The first row is worth applying as a habit whenever a crawler appears in an option.

6727
11:13:30,126 --> 11:13:33,732
A crawler exists to discover a schema you do not already know.

6728
11:13:34,670 --> 11:13:41,252
That makes it right for exploring unfamiliar data and unnecessary for output your own job has just produced.

6729
11:13:42,158 --> 11:13:50,628
Enable update catalog registers partitions at right time, removing both the crawler's cost and the delay before new data becomes queryable.

6730
11:13:51,822 --> 11:13:54,944
Module 12's first scenario turned on exactly that.

6731
11:13:55,342 --> 11:13:58,148
The fourth row is the compression trap worth carrying.

6732
11:13:58,958 --> 11:14:04,740
A 10GB gigzip file is read by exactly one worker, no matter how large the cluster is.

6733
11:14:05,104 --> 11:14:09,220
That is because GIGZIP cannot be decompressed starting from the middle.

6734
11:14:10,926 --> 11:14:17,840
The callout is worth internalizing because it resolves three rows at a glance and the same rule appears throughout the exam in other forms.

6735
11:14:18,160 --> 11:14:27,280
MongoDB names, document db, Cassandra names, key spaces, Redis names, MemoryDB, Kafka names, Amazon MSK,

6736
11:14:27,440 --> 11:14:31,876
PostgreSQL, and MySQL name, Aurora or Amazon RDS.

6737
11:14:32,622 --> 11:14:41,360
In every one of those cases, the question is telling you that applications already speak a particular API. Rewriting them is the unnecessary migration cost.

6738
11:14:41,600 --> 11:14:43,760
The exam consistently marks wrong.

6739
11:14:44,366 --> 11:14:52,260
The last row has a different and equally mechanical answer because a local secondary index cannot be added to a table that already exists.

6740
11:14:52,560 --> 11:14:58,084
Any new access pattern on an existing table is therefore a global secondary index.

6741
11:14:59,662 --> 11:15:07,716
A company runs a self-managed Apache Cassandra cluster on Amazon EC2, supporting a high-ride telemetry application.

6742
11:15:08,480 --> 11:15:11,524
Around 30 services use Cassandra drivers.

6743
11:15:12,000 --> 11:15:15,520
The operations team wants to stop managing the cluster.

6744
11:15:16,078 --> 11:15:20,932
Correct throughput is high and predictable, and the access pattern is a lookup by partition key.

6745
11:15:21,262 --> 11:15:23,108
What should the data engineer recommend?

6746
11:15:23,918 --> 11:15:29,252
a Amazon Dynamo DB, which handles high write key value workloads at scale.

6747
11:15:29,934 --> 11:15:33,828
b Amazon Key Spaces, which is Cassandra compatible.

6748
11:15:34,542 --> 11:15:34,800
C

6749
11:15:35,790 --> 11:15:39,044
Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with a wide table.

6750
11:15:40,046 --> 11:15:43,812
D Amazon Document DB. Pause and decide.

6751
11:15:50,400 --> 11:15:55,492
The answer is B, and option A is carefully built to be attractive rather than merely plausible.

6752
11:15:55,920 --> 11:16:02,308
The access pattern described, a high right lookup by partition key, is precisely what DynamoDB is best at.

6753
11:16:02,800 --> 11:16:06,980
So a candidate reading for the access pattern and nothing else will choose it confidently.

6754
11:16:07,854 --> 11:16:11,396
The 30 services are the constraint that overrides it.

6755
11:16:11,822 --> 11:16:16,292
Because moving to a managed service is not worth 30 application rewrites.

6756
11:16:17,230 --> 11:16:20,132
This is the compatibility rule doing its work.

6757
11:16:21,134 --> 11:16:28,100
The detail worth noticing for future questions is that the scenario describes an access pattern which fits the wrong answer.

6758
11:16:28,720 --> 11:16:31,588
That is how a good distractor is constructed.

6759
11:16:33,262 --> 11:16:38,916
The fifth row is worth stating plainly because the words are so similar and the behavior is not.

6760
11:16:39,502 --> 11:16:41,828
A plain view stores a query definition.

6761
11:16:43,118 --> 11:16:45,476
So wrapping an expensive aggregation in one

6762
11:16:46,222 --> 11:16:52,132
makes it easier to write and exactly as expensive to run every time for every consumer.

6763
11:16:53,102 --> 11:17:01,540
A materialized view stores the result computed once and refreshed on a schedule. So consumers read a small pre-computed table.

6764
11:17:02,286 --> 11:17:08,420
That means an option offering a view in response to a cost or performance complaint is almost always wrong.

6765
11:17:09,262 --> 11:17:13,956
One offering a materialized view for the same complaint is almost always right.

6766
11:17:15,630 --> 11:17:21,860
This pair is offered constantly and it is never about whether the SQL would run because it would run in both.

6767
11:17:22,240 --> 11:17:26,084
The separator is how often and by how many people.

6768
11:17:26,926 --> 11:17:32,240
A monthly regulatory extract belongs in Athena even though Redshift could serve it.

6769
11:17:32,560 --> 11:17:37,040
Because a warehouse standing all month for one query is waste.

6770
11:17:37,486 --> 11:17:42,852
A dashboard opened by 400 people every morning belongs in Redshift even though Athena could serve it.

6771
11:17:43,854 --> 11:17:51,140
That is because 400 per query charges against a scanned dataset is far worse than capacity you already hold.

6772
11:17:51,918 --> 11:17:57,988
When a question states a user count and a frequency, that sentence exists to decide this pair.

6773
11:17:59,630 --> 11:18:05,604
These four are offered against each other constantly and the distinction is mechanical once stated.

6774
11:18:06,190 --> 11:18:08,164
Ask where the data currently is.

6775
11:18:09,838 --> 11:18:11,620
Files in S3 is Spectrum.

6776
11:18:12,640 --> 11:18:21,668
A live operational database is federated query if you are reading it occasionally, or 0 ETL if you need it in the warehouse continuously.

6777
11:18:23,694 --> 11:18:26,688
Another redshift warehouse is data sharing.

6778
11:18:27,246 --> 11:18:33,604
Then ask whether the data moves because only Zero ETL copies it and the other three read it where it sits.

6779
11:18:33,934 --> 11:18:39,780
A question describing a heavy regular reporting workload against a production database is the one to watch.

6780
11:18:40,368 --> 11:18:47,364
That is because federated query is technically able and puts analytical load on a system serving customers.

6781
11:18:48,846 --> 11:18:51,940
An analytics team queries 5 years of transaction history.

6782
11:18:52,654 --> 11:18:57,860
The most recent 90 days are queried by 400 people through dashboards throughout the working day.

6783
11:18:58,542 --> 11:19:03,780
Data older than a year is queried perhaps twice a quarter for regulatory reporting.

6784
11:19:04,814 --> 11:19:12,132
Regulatory queries must be able to join old data to recent data in a single SQL statement.

6785
11:19:13,870 --> 11:19:21,796
What is the most appropriate design? a Keep everything in Amazon Redshift sized for the full five years.

6786
11:19:22,688 --> 11:19:23,008
b

6787
11:19:24,078 --> 11:19:30,948
Recent data in Redshift older data in Amazon S3 as park queried together with Redshift Spectrum.

6788
11:19:31,648 --> 11:19:31,968
C

6789
11:19:33,070 --> 11:19:37,156
Keep everything in Amazon S3 and query it all with Amazon Athena.

6790
11:19:38,208 --> 11:19:38,528
D.

6791
11:19:39,008 --> 11:19:45,092
Recent data in Redshift older data in S3 with analysts using Athena for historical queries.

6792
11:19:45,934 --> 11:19:47,268
Pause and decide.

6793
11:19:53,934 --> 11:20:01,316
The answer is B and option D is the closest competitor saving identical money and failing on one clause.

6794
11:20:02,446 --> 11:20:10,820
Data must be joinable in a single SQL statement, and Athena and Redshift are separate query engines, so no single query can span them.

6795
11:20:11,598 --> 11:20:20,452
Redshift spectrum exists precisely so that an external table over S3 can be joined to a warehouse table inside the same query.

6796
11:20:22,448 --> 11:20:25,924
The general pattern is worth carrying beyond this question.

6797
11:20:26,208 --> 11:20:31,076
Watch for a scenario describing two different access patterns over one logical dataset.

6798
11:20:31,918 --> 11:20:37,700
The answer usually splits the storage between two tiers and keeps the query interface unified.

6799
11:20:39,342 --> 11:20:46,212
The second row deserves its call out because both symptoms are reported identically by users who simply say the dashboard is slow.

6800
11:20:46,862 --> 11:20:51,748
Redshift separates the two numbers in query history so it is a fact rather than a judgment.

6801
11:20:52,590 --> 11:21:02,084
If queries wait several minutes and then execute in two seconds, that is queuing, and the answers are workload management priority and concurrency scaling.

6802
11:21:03,328 --> 11:21:07,012
If they start immediately and take four minutes, that is execution,

6803
11:21:08,448 --> 11:21:12,708
and the answers are sort keys, distribution keys and statistics.

6804
11:21:13,648 --> 11:21:20,740
Options addressing one never help the other, so getting the split right eliminates half the options immediately.

6805
11:21:22,286 --> 11:21:28,388
This pair decided two verb scenarios in this course and it is worth being able to split instantly.

6806
11:21:28,768 --> 11:21:38,128
Cloud Trail answers who, which makes it the tool for audit questions, security investigations, and anything phrased as who deleted this or who

6807
11:21:38,288 --> 11:21:39,108
accessed that.

6808
11:21:40,046 --> 11:21:44,932
CloudWatch logs answers what happened, which makes it the tool for diagnosing a failure.

6809
11:21:45,678 --> 11:21:51,588
The trap that appears repeatedly is offering Cloud Trail as the way to understand why a job produced wrong output.

6810
11:21:52,448 --> 11:21:57,860
It cannot, because a transformation reading a column and deriving a value from it is not an AP.

6811
11:21:58,928 --> 11:22:00,644
That is logs or lineage.

6812
11:22:02,208 --> 11:22:08,228
An Amazon Redshift cluster serves dashboards used by 300 analysts each morning.

6813
11:22:08,688 --> 11:22:13,168
During the busiest half hour, users report that dashboards are slow.

6814
11:22:13,408 --> 11:22:20,772
Query history shows execution times unchanged at around 1 second and queue wait times of 2 to 4 minutes.

6815
11:22:21,454 --> 11:22:25,764
An overnight ETL job sometimes overruns into that window.

6816
11:22:26,382 --> 11:22:28,036
What should the data engineer do?

6817
11:22:28,622 --> 11:22:32,580
a add sort and distribution keys to the dashboard tables.

6818
11:22:33,168 --> 11:22:41,568
b enable concurrency scaling on the dashboard queue and set WLM query priority above the ETL

6819
11:22:41,728 --> 11:22:42,276
workload.

6820
11:22:43,918 --> 11:22:44,128
C

6821
11:22:45,358 --> 11:22:48,804
Run vacuum and analyze on the dashboard tables each morning.

6822
11:22:49,454 --> 11:22:52,768
d Move the dashboards to Amazon Athena.

6823
11:22:53,358 --> 11:22:54,820
Pause and decide.

6824
11:23:01,392 --> 11:23:06,500
The answer is B, and the question hands you the distinction by quoting both numbers separately.

6825
11:23:06,832 --> 11:23:13,252
Execution is 1 second and unchanged, so nothing about the tables, the keys, or the query plans is wrong.

6826
11:23:14,254 --> 11:23:19,620
Wait is 2 to 4 minutes, so the problem is entirely contention for a slot during a busy window.

6827
11:23:20,270 --> 11:23:23,232
Options A and C are both genuinely good.

6828
11:23:23,662 --> 11:23:26,948
Redshift practice offered exactly where they cannot help.

6829
11:23:27,502 --> 11:23:31,312
That is the definition of a distractor from the opening slide of this module.

6830
11:23:31,758 --> 11:23:39,588
This is module 10's central distinction, and being able to spot it in a few seconds is worth real marks across domain 3.

6831
11:23:41,006 --> 11:23:48,580
The first row eliminates options faster than anything else in the security domain because an IAM policy has no vocabulary for a column.

6832
11:23:48,912 --> 11:23:51,140
There is no ARN for one.

6833
11:23:51,758 --> 11:24:01,444
So any option proposing to deny access to three columns with an IAM policy can be discarded without reading the rest of it. The last row is nearly as quick.

6834
11:24:02,510 --> 11:24:10,500
If the concern is somebody who has legitimate access, seeing more than they should, encryption is irrelevant because they decrypt correctly every time they query.

6835
11:24:11,406 --> 11:24:14,020
The answer is masking or a column grant.

6836
11:24:15,694 --> 11:24:22,404
This is the fastest elimination available anywhere in domain 4 and it is worth being able to apply without thinking.

6837
11:24:22,752 --> 11:24:29,632
An IAM policy grants actions on resources named by ARN and a column is not a resource.

6838
11:24:29,966 --> 11:24:32,132
There is no identifier to put in a policy.

6839
11:24:33,102 --> 11:24:40,260
So an option describing an IAM policy that denies access to three columns is describing something that cannot be written.

6840
11:24:40,974 --> 11:24:43,172
Whatever else about it looks attractive.

6841
11:24:44,046 --> 11:24:47,844
The second half of the callout is the operational catch from module 18.

6842
11:24:48,814 --> 11:24:57,892
Lake formation only enforces when the engine consults the catalog. So a role retaining direct S3 access bypasses it entirely.

6843
11:24:59,438 --> 11:25:09,124
Both hold values your code reads at runtime, both encrypt with AWSK MS, and both are entirely respectable choices.

6844
11:25:09,678 --> 11:25:13,732
Rotation is the single capability that separates them for exam purposes.

6845
11:25:14,446 --> 11:25:23,716
Secrets manager can change a credential on a schedule, updating the stored value and for supported databases, the credential in the database at the same moment.

6846
11:25:24,558 --> 11:25:26,756
So nothing has to be edited anywhere.

6847
11:25:27,310 --> 11:25:30,180
Parameter store has no equivalent and never will.

6848
11:25:30,830 --> 11:25:32,592
That makes the decision mechanical.

6849
11:25:33,552 --> 11:25:41,380
It is also worth remembering that a bucket name or a threshold held in Secrets Manager is simply a monthly charge for nothing.

6850
11:25:43,150 --> 11:25:46,884
The callout is the fastest way to separate these under pressure.

6851
11:25:47,630 --> 11:25:56,708
Encryption protects data from somebody who was never granted access and obtains it another way, and it is entirely transparent to anybody who was granted access.

6852
11:25:57,678 --> 11:26:04,164
So consider a question describing a person who legitimately queries a table and sees more than their role requires.

6853
11:26:04,462 --> 11:26:12,836
Encryption is irrelevant there, and every encryption option is a distractor, that is, masking, a column level grant or a row filter.

6854
11:26:13,646 --> 11:26:20,580
The reverse holds true because a question about a backup tape leaving a building is encryption and permissions do nothing about it.

6855
11:26:22,094 --> 11:26:30,180
A healthcare analytics team must be prevented from seeing three columns in a patient table stored in Amazon S3 and queried through Amazon Athena.

6856
11:26:31,822 --> 11:26:38,436
A separate requirement states that if a backup tape or disk were obtained by an outside party, the data must be unreadable.

6857
11:26:39,342 --> 11:26:42,148
Both requirements applied to the same table.

6858
11:26:42,592 --> 11:26:44,740
Which combination meets both?

6859
11:26:45,166 --> 11:26:51,632
a An IAM policy denying access to the three columns plus SSES3.

6860
11:26:52,272 --> 11:27:01,188
B Lake formation column level grants excluding the three columns plus SSE KMS with a customer managed key.

6861
11:27:01,792 --> 11:27:02,112
C

6862
11:27:03,246 --> 11:27:07,748
SSEK MS alone since encryption prevents unauthorized reading.

6863
11:27:08,112 --> 11:27:12,912
D. A redacted copy of the table for the analytics team plus SSES3.

6864
11:27:13,614 --> 11:27:14,852
Pause and decide.

6865
11:27:21,454 --> 11:27:28,708
The answer is B and the question is built so that each wrong option fails on a different principle from this module.

6866
11:27:29,040 --> 11:27:36,676
Option A fails on the IAM against late formation row because a column has no ARN and no policy can name one.

6867
11:27:37,600 --> 11:27:46,660
Option C fails on the encryption against masking row because the analytics team are insiders who decrypt correctly every time they query.

6868
11:27:47,120 --> 11:27:52,772
Option D fails on the duplication argument that has appeared in five separate modules.

6869
11:27:53,440 --> 11:27:57,380
Three distinct confusion pairs tested inside one question.

6870
11:27:58,240 --> 11:28:04,164
That is precisely how the exam builds its harder items and why this module is organized the way it is.

6871
11:28:05,774 --> 11:28:10,276
The first row recurs most, and the phrasing in the call out is the test to apply.

6872
11:28:10,830 --> 11:28:17,604
Streaming infrastructure runs and builds all the time, so it earns its cost only when a faster answer is worth something to somebody.

6873
11:28:18,094 --> 11:28:21,956
A fraud decision at the point of sale is worth a great deal within one minute.

6874
11:28:22,798 --> 11:28:26,276
A daily sales report is worth nothing at all faster than daily.

6875
11:28:26,638 --> 11:28:31,492
The fourth row is the operational version of the same distinction, and the words are reliable.

6876
11:28:33,550 --> 11:28:37,156
Every night, hourly and on the first of the month are schedules.

6877
11:28:37,614 --> 11:28:41,380
As soon as it arrives and immediately after upload are events.

6878
11:28:43,118 --> 11:28:50,724
This slide exists because a question sometimes offers a combination and a candidate rejects it for looking like two answers rather than one.

6879
11:28:51,342 --> 11:28:57,092
These four combinations are standard architecture rather than indecision on the question writer's part.

6880
11:28:57,998 --> 11:29:10,980
Event Bridge notices something and starts a state machine that coordinates the actual work. An SNS topic publishes once and several SQSQs subscribe, each feeding a consumer at its own pace.

6881
11:29:11,918 --> 11:29:19,268
A kinesis data stream retains records while Firehose reads it as one consumer and delivers to Amazon S3.

6882
11:29:19,982 --> 11:29:24,420
That is exactly what the second work scenario in this module concluded.

6883
11:29:25,198 --> 11:29:31,076
When an option pairs two services correctly, that is frequently the intended answer rather than a trap.

6884
11:29:32,750 --> 11:29:36,240
This is a smaller category and it costs people marks.

6885
11:29:36,942 --> 11:29:44,772
That is because a candidate who does not know a service was renamed will spend time trying to distinguish two names that describe one thing.

6886
11:29:45,678 --> 11:29:46,692
Kinesis Data.

6887
11:29:47,214 --> 11:29:52,702
Firehose became Amazon Data, Firehose in February 2024.

6888
11:29:52,702 --> 11:29:59,300
QuickSight became Quick Suite in October 2025 and then simply Amazon quit during 2026.

6889
11:29:59,600 --> 11:30:04,640
DataZone and SageMaker catalog are the same capability described from two directions.

6890
11:30:04,880 --> 11:30:08,900
In each case, the correct move is to recognize both names as one answer.

6891
11:30:09,646 --> 11:30:15,440
The exam is more likely to use the older name because most of its questions predate the change.

6892
11:30:16,750 --> 11:30:23,236
This table is the most transferable thing in the module because it collects failure modes rather than services.

6893
11:30:24,206 --> 11:30:29,412
Each row is one underlying problem that appears in several different places under different names.

6894
11:30:30,350 --> 11:30:36,516
Recognizing the shape means you carry the diagnosis across services rather than learning it repeatedly from scratch.

6895
11:30:37,198 --> 11:30:43,812
SKU looks identical whether it is a spap task, a kinasis shard, a redshift slice or a dynamo DB partition.

6896
11:30:44,750 --> 11:30:49,508
The callout is the reason it matters for answering questions rather than for engineering.

6897
11:30:50,510 --> 11:30:53,956
Capacity is offered as a distractor for every single row here.

6898
11:30:54,800 --> 11:31:01,860
That is because adding capacity is everyone's instinct under pressure and it is the correct answer for almost none of them.

6899
11:31:03,598 --> 11:31:07,520
The last bullet is the reading habit worth building from all of these.

6900
11:31:07,854 --> 11:31:15,460
Exam questions are written tightly, so a specific number appearing in a scenario is usually load-bearing rather than scene setting.

6901
11:31:16,334 --> 11:31:23,844
1100 days of partitions in module 8 was there to exceed the 100 partition ceiling on a CTA statement.

6902
11:31:24,430 --> 11:31:27,844
A job running for 18 minutes rules out lambda entirely.

6903
11:31:28,718 --> 11:31:36,740
40 million objects of 4KB each crosses the 128KB billing minimum, so a cheaper storage class costs more.

6904
11:31:37,006 --> 11:31:42,820
A lifecycle rule moving data to deep archive after 30 days collides with a 180 day minimum.

6905
11:31:43,726 --> 11:31:47,684
Check the number against the limit before you evaluate anything else.

6906
11:31:49,166 --> 11:31:55,108
This is the module compressed into a scanning list, and it is worth rereading before the exam more than anything else here.

6907
11:31:56,430 --> 11:32:04,048
Each phase is a constraint, the question has already stated, and each removes options mechanically rather than by comparison or judgment.

6908
11:32:04,528 --> 11:32:10,448
The habit to build is reading the scenario for these first, forming a short list of what cannot be true.

6909
11:32:10,608 --> 11:32:12,708
Only then looking at the four options.

6910
11:32:13,088 --> 11:32:19,968
Every work scenario in this course was constructed so that habit works, because that is how the exam constructs them.

6911
11:32:20,526 --> 11:32:25,668
The signal call out on each solution slide has been pointing at the deciding phrase throughout.

6912
11:32:26,542 --> 11:32:28,772
Which was the point of including it?

6913
11:32:30,478 --> 11:32:36,036
The last bullet is a practical observation about how these questions actually look on screen.

6914
11:32:37,806 --> 11:32:45,284
When two options are nearly identical in wording and differ in a single service name or a single setting, that is the pair being tested.

6915
11:32:46,222 --> 11:32:49,648
Everything else in the question is context leading up to that one word.

6916
11:32:49,888 --> 11:32:56,548
Spotting it says reading the other two options carefully at all. The first bullet is worth building as a habit too.

6917
11:32:57,358 --> 11:33:05,284
That is because the constraint that decides a question frequently sits in the final sentence of the scenario after all the technical description.

6918
11:33:06,702 --> 11:33:12,516
Next module steps back from services entirely and looks at how the exam writes its questions.

6919
11:33:13,102 --> 11:33:17,828
That is what you need for items this module has not anticipated.

6920
11:33:19,502 --> 11:33:22,724
This module contains no AWS services at all.

6921
11:33:23,246 --> 11:33:25,380
It is about the exam as an object.

6922
11:33:27,790 --> 11:33:36,516
What its published format actually says, how a question is constructed, the specific ways a wrong answer is made to look right.

6923
11:33:37,518 --> 11:33:40,900
How to manage 130 minutes without running out of them.

6924
11:33:41,870 --> 11:33:48,484
Everything here is drawn from the exam guide itself, checked against the version published at the time of recording.

6925
11:33:49,968 --> 11:33:54,468
From the structure of the 80 worked scenarios earlier in this course,

6926
11:33:55,328 --> 11:34:04,132
four worked scenarios of a different kind follow, where the thing being tested is your reading and your judgment rather than your knowledge of any service.

6927
11:34:05,710 --> 11:34:07,428
Two rows deserve immediate attention.

6928
11:34:07,728 --> 11:34:17,444
The response types row matters because AWS introduced three new question types ordering, matching, and case study on some certifications from 2024.

6929
11:34:18,286 --> 11:34:27,524
The DEA C01 exam guide as published still lists two types only, multiple choice and multiple response. So expect those.

6930
11:34:28,014 --> 11:34:31,204
The guessing row also has a practical consequence.

6931
11:34:31,662 --> 11:34:38,052
There is no penalty for a wrong answer, so leaving anything blank is strictly worse than a guess, however uncertain.

6932
11:34:39,022 --> 11:34:43,492
That sounds obvious and people still run out of time with questions unanswered.

6933
11:34:45,166 --> 11:34:47,940
The third row is worth acting on if it applies to you.

6934
11:34:48,528 --> 11:34:54,564
That is because it is a 23% increase in time and a great many eligible candidates never request it.

6935
11:34:55,008 --> 11:35:01,188
You ask for it in your AWS certification account, choosing the ESL plus 30 minutes accommodation.

6936
11:35:02,062 --> 11:35:05,156
You must do that before scheduling rather than at the test center.

6937
11:35:06,350 --> 11:35:08,836
The retake row is worth knowing for a different reason.

6938
11:35:09,710 --> 11:35:13,668
14 days is short enough that a near miss is recoverable quickly.

6939
11:35:14,382 --> 11:35:19,556
That is an argument for booking the exam rather than waiting until you feel completely ready.

6940
11:35:21,262 --> 11:35:27,748
The callout is worth working through because compensatory scoring gets overinterpreted as permission to skip a domain.

6941
11:35:28,240 --> 11:35:34,884
The scaled score does not map linearly onto questions answered, so nobody can tell you exactly how many you need.

6942
11:35:35,790 --> 11:35:40,400
What is clear is that 50 scored questions means every single one carries real weight.

6943
11:35:40,654 --> 11:35:44,320
Abandoning domain 4 gives away roughly 9 of them before you start.

6944
11:35:44,654 --> 11:35:47,524
Module 0 made this point with numbers from the bank.

6945
11:35:48,142 --> 11:35:54,756
Security terms accounted for under 5% of mentions while being 18% of the exam.

6946
11:35:55,040 --> 11:35:58,052
That gap is the cheapest available improvement.

6947
11:35:59,566 --> 11:36:05,476
Nearly every scenario question on this exam follows one structure and knowing it tells you where to look.

6948
11:36:05,840 --> 11:36:11,760
First comes the situation describing an organization, a technology, and a current stake.

6949
11:36:11,920 --> 11:36:15,940
That is usually the longest part and mostly context you can move through quickly.

6950
11:36:16,846 --> 11:36:23,492
Then the requirement or the problem, which is what must change or what must now be possible.

6951
11:36:24,174 --> 11:36:34,980
Then the qualifier, a phrase like least operational overhead or most cost effective, which is frequently the final sentence before the question itself.

6952
11:36:35,790 --> 11:36:43,812
Recognizing that shape is worth real time, and the third part decides the answer far more often than the first part does.

6953
11:36:45,422 --> 11:36:52,804
The constraint row is the one worth learning to hunt for because it is stated as a refusal and it is easy to skim past.

6954
11:36:53,422 --> 11:36:58,980
The team has nobody available to operate a cluster. The company will not modify the source system.

6955
11:36:59,790 --> 11:37:01,572
The application cannot be changed.

6956
11:37:02,510 --> 11:37:06,212
Each of those sentences eliminates at least one option outright.

6957
11:37:06,480 --> 11:37:12,868
In this course's work scenarios, the constraint was the deciding factor more often than the qualifier was.

6958
11:37:13,774 --> 11:37:22,320
When you read a scenario, the sentence beginning with the word cannot or will not or does not want is the one to underline mentally.

6959
11:37:23,662 --> 11:37:30,532
The order in the call out is a genuine time saver and it is worth practicing on the three practice exams that follow.

6960
11:37:31,118 --> 11:37:37,440
A long scenario read without knowing the question is read as a story and you finish it holding a general impression.

6961
11:37:37,934 --> 11:37:44,292
Read the question first, and the same scenario is read as a search where each sentence is either relevant or not.

6962
11:37:44,640 --> 11:37:47,268
The last bullet is the failure this prevents.

6963
11:37:47,502 --> 11:37:51,748
Options are written to be attractive, so reading them early gives you a favorite.

6964
11:37:52,494 --> 11:37:57,988
Everything after that becomes an argument for the favorite rather than an evaluation.

6965
11:37:59,438 --> 11:38:08,324
The fourth bullet is worth saying because scenarios open with a sentence about a company in an industry and it almost never changes the answer.

6966
11:38:09,998 --> 11:38:16,068
A healthcare provider and a retailer with the same technical requirement get the same technical answer.

6967
11:38:16,942 --> 11:38:24,004
Unless the question separately states a regulatory constraint, and then that constraint is the sentence carrying the weight rather than the industry.

6968
11:38:27,214 --> 11:38:28,388
The fifth is similar.

6969
11:38:28,640 --> 11:38:33,348
A description of the existing pipeline is usually there so you understand what is being changed.

6970
11:38:33,742 --> 11:38:37,924
Only becomes a constraint when the question says it cannot change.

6971
11:38:39,374 --> 11:38:49,220
The exam guide says distractors are plausible responses that match the content area chosen to attract a candidate with incomplete knowledge or skill.

6972
11:38:49,742 --> 11:38:52,676
That is a design brief rather than an accident.

6973
11:38:52,880 --> 11:38:57,604
And after 80 worked scenarios in this course, the patterns are recognizable.

6974
11:38:58,510 --> 11:39:02,244
A distractor is usually the right answer to a slightly different question.

6975
11:39:02,800 --> 11:39:06,852
Or a real capability applied where it cannot possibly help.

6976
11:39:07,374 --> 11:39:11,920
Or an appearing shortcut that quietly breaks a constraint the scenario stated.

6977
11:39:12,494 --> 11:39:19,780
The next two slides name the patterns, and once you can name them, you begin spotting them while answering rather than afterwards in the review.

6978
11:39:21,454 --> 11:39:28,004
The last row appeared several times in this course and it is worth watching for because it resolves without any weighing.

6979
11:39:28,558 --> 11:39:33,124
Module 11 had an option claiming Athena cannot query Redshift, which it can.

6980
11:39:33,710 --> 11:39:35,780
Module 16 had an option.

6981
11:39:36,270 --> 11:39:41,700
Claiming cost allocation tag activation applies retrospectively, which it does not.

6982
11:39:42,958 --> 11:39:44,560
Module 18 had an option.

6983
11:39:44,878 --> 11:39:49,092
Claiming lake formation does not apply to glue jobs, which it does.

6984
11:39:49,614 --> 11:39:54,480
In each case, the option was eliminated by a fact rather than by a judgment.

6985
11:39:55,214 --> 11:40:00,708
That is far quicker and far safer than comparing two plausible designs.

6986
11:40:02,094 --> 11:40:05,124
The third and fourth points work together in practice.

6987
11:40:05,838 --> 11:40:11,204
Two options may read almost identically, apart from a service name or one setting.

6988
11:40:11,918 --> 11:40:15,748
Everything else in the question was context leading up to that difference.

6989
11:40:16,110 --> 11:40:18,880
You can ignore the other two options entirely.

6990
11:40:19,440 --> 11:40:22,372
The fourth point is worth trusting more than people do.

6991
11:40:22,720 --> 11:40:30,596
An option naming dynamic data masking or partition projection or a data filter is precise about a real capability.

6992
11:40:31,310 --> 11:40:34,852
Precision usually indicates the writer had that feature in mind.

6993
11:40:35,726 --> 11:40:41,668
Vague options describing an approach rather than a mechanism are more often the distractors.

6994
11:40:43,150 --> 11:40:45,636
This is the honest warning in the module.

6995
11:40:46,080 --> 11:40:52,740
If you have built data pipelines for years, you have built things that AWS now provides as features.

6996
11:40:53,120 --> 11:40:56,548
Your instinct is to reach for the approach you know works.

6997
11:40:57,422 --> 11:41:05,956
The exam consistently prefers the managed option because least operational overhead is its most common qualifier and a thing you do not operate has none.

6998
11:41:06,734 --> 11:41:13,412
So when you find yourself thinking that you would build it a particular way, check whether one of the options removes the building entirely.

6999
11:41:14,318 --> 11:41:20,324
That instinct cost marks in module 4, module 5, and module 15 in this course.

7000
11:41:21,998 --> 11:41:31,684
The call out is a practical revision suggestion for the final week. Each signal call out in this course names the specific phrase that decided a question.

7001
11:41:32,240 --> 11:41:39,812
Reading AP of them in sequence is a concentrated hour on the one skill that transfers to questions nobody has anticipated.

7002
11:41:40,686 --> 11:41:45,060
The last bullet is worth stating plainly for your own confidence.

7003
11:41:45,614 --> 11:41:53,508
These scenarios were constructed from the exam guide's task statements and from verified AWS documentation, not copied from anywhere.

7004
11:41:54,062 --> 11:41:58,852
That is why they can be discussed openly and why the reasoning is spelled out rather than hidden.

7005
11:42:00,558 --> 11:42:04,036
Read this question for its structure rather than its content.

7006
11:42:04,558 --> 11:42:08,560
A company ingests clickstream data into Amazon S3.

7007
11:42:09,040 --> 11:42:13,360
Analysts query it with Amazon Athena and complain about cost.

7008
11:42:14,030 --> 11:42:17,636
The data is uncompressed JSON in a single prefix.

7009
11:42:18,640 --> 11:42:26,564
The team has no capacity to build and maintain a new pipeline, and the change must not alter how analysts write their queries.

7010
11:42:27,120 --> 11:42:30,852
The question then asks for the most cost-effective approach.

7011
11:42:31,918 --> 11:42:35,332
Which sentence in that scenario eliminates the most options?

7012
11:42:36,750 --> 11:42:37,005
A.

7013
11:42:38,158 --> 11:42:41,700
The data is uncompressed JSON in a single prefix.

7014
11:42:42,286 --> 11:42:46,628
b The team has no capacity to build and maintain a new pipeline.

7015
11:42:47,280 --> 11:42:47,520
C.

7016
11:42:48,046 --> 11:42:50,320
Analysts query it with Amazon Athena.

7017
11:42:50,800 --> 11:42:54,948
D. The change must not alter how analysts write their queries.

7018
11:42:55,680 --> 11:42:56,836
Pause and decide.

7019
11:43:03,374 --> 11:43:09,760
The answer is B, and the distinction it teaches is between two kinds of sentence that both feel important.

7020
11:43:10,240 --> 11:43:13,668
The uncompressed JSON tells you what the problem is.

7021
11:43:14,080 --> 11:43:19,140
It is genuinely the most useful sentence for working out what the answer should look like.

7022
11:43:20,014 --> 11:43:24,100
It does not remove any options because several options might address it.

7023
11:43:24,400 --> 11:43:33,060
The refusal in B removes every option that adds a component, which on a typical 4 option question is one or two of them immediately.

7024
11:43:33,870 --> 11:43:40,996
In 130 minutes, sentences that remove options are worth more than sentences that suggest answers.

7025
11:43:42,446 --> 11:43:48,228
The callout is a pattern worth knowing because it speeds up the second choice once you have found the first.

7026
11:43:48,960 --> 11:43:57,028
When a question asks for two answers, they frequently address different halves of a stated problem rather than being two versions of the same idea.

7027
11:43:57,966 --> 11:44:06,276
Module 6 second scenario needed back-off and a dead letter Q, one to reduce failures and 1 to preserve the ones that still occur.

7028
11:44:07,040 --> 11:44:14,475
Module 19 second scenario needed a policy condition and a config rule. one to prevent and one to report on compliance.

7029
11:44:15,598 --> 11:44:23,360
So having found one answer, ask yourself what the other half of the requirement is rather than looking for something similar to what you already chose.

7030
11:44:24,910 --> 11:44:32,004
A multiple response question describes a blue job that succeeds nightly while producing totals that are 2% low.

7031
11:44:32,400 --> 11:44:38,596
The company wants to detect this class of problem in future and to stop bad data reaching the warehouse.

7032
11:44:39,118 --> 11:44:41,988
It asks you to select two answers from five options.

7033
11:44:42,830 --> 11:44:45,284
Which pair of answers is the shape to look for?

7034
11:44:47,918 --> 11:44:52,868
two options that both add capacity because the job may be under-resourced.

7035
11:44:53,742 --> 11:44:54,000
D

7036
11:44:55,022 --> 11:44:59,492
One that detects the problem and one that prevents bad data continuing downstream.

7037
11:45:00,320 --> 11:45:00,560
C.

7038
11:45:01,326 --> 11:45:05,280
Two options that both improve monitoring since the problem went unnoticed.

7039
11:45:05,934 --> 11:45:06,240
D

7040
11:45:07,374 --> 11:45:11,120
Any two options that are individually correct in any combination.

7041
11:45:12,160 --> 11:45:13,540
Pause and decide.

7042
11:45:20,174 --> 11:45:21,476
The answer is B.

7043
11:45:21,760 --> 11:45:25,220
And the technique generalizes to every multiple response question.

7044
11:45:26,190 --> 11:45:29,796
Count the requirements in the scenario before you look at the options.

7045
11:45:31,598 --> 11:45:36,580
That is because the number of correct answers usually matches the number of things being asked for.

7046
11:45:37,486 --> 11:45:40,640
Two requirements, select two, one answer each.

7047
11:45:40,942 --> 11:45:49,668
Option C is the trap worth naming because choosing two answers that do the same thing well feels safe and leaves half the question unanswered.

7048
11:45:50,320 --> 11:45:56,132
And since there is no partial credit, a pair that covers one requirement twice scores exactly zero.

7049
11:45:57,774 --> 11:46:03,204
The callout is worth internalizing because narrowing to two and then feeling stuck is unsettling.

7050
11:46:03,982 --> 11:46:07,076
It is exactly what a well-written question is designed to produce.

7051
11:46:07,726 --> 11:46:10,148
Getting to two means the elimination worked.

7052
11:46:10,478 --> 11:46:14,660
The qualifier is then the tool for the last step and it is there for that purpose.

7053
11:46:15,502 --> 11:46:21,732
If the qualifier is least operational overhead, the more managed of the two wins, even if the other is what you would build.

7054
11:46:25,038 --> 11:46:33,284
If it is most cost effective, the one that removes work rather than reprising it usually wins, which module 16 spent its time on.

7055
11:46:34,734 --> 11:46:38,564
The callout describes a small mistake with a real cost attached.

7056
11:46:39,150 --> 11:46:45,220
Flagging a question for review is exactly the right thing to do when you are genuinely torn between two options.

7057
11:46:45,934 --> 11:46:52,964
Flagging it without selecting anything means that if you run out of time, that question is blank and scored as wrong.

7058
11:46:53,870 --> 11:46:55,428
That is strictly worse than a guess.

7059
11:46:55,600 --> 11:47:00,800
Selecting your best guess takes two seconds and converts a certain zero into a reasonable chance.

7060
11:47:01,040 --> 11:47:06,148
The pacing target on the last bullet is worth rehearsing during the three practice exams that follow.

7061
11:47:06,894 --> 11:47:14,625
That is because knowing what question 30 should feel like at 60 minutes stops you discovering the problem at question 60.

7062
11:47:16,526 --> 11:47:22,148
The callout comes from how people describe their own failures afterwards, and it is worth planning around.

7063
11:47:22,656 --> 11:47:26,176
Two hours of dense scenario reading is genuinely tiring.

7064
11:47:26,656 --> 11:47:33,700
The failure it produces is not forgetting a service, it is skipping the sentence that said the application cannot be modified.

7065
11:47:34,638 --> 11:47:39,460
That is why the reading order from earlier in this module matters more in the final third than in the first.

7066
11:47:40,110 --> 11:47:44,580
You may notice yourself reading a scenario as narrative rather than as a search.

7067
11:47:45,070 --> 11:47:50,244
That is the signal to stop, reread the question, and go through it again deliberately.

7068
11:47:51,534 --> 11:47:56,388
The last bullet is the argument for eliminating even when you do not know the answer.

7069
11:47:56,846 --> 11:48:00,580
A blind guess among four options is a 25% chance.

7070
11:48:01,358 --> 11:48:08,900
Removing two on facts and constraints makes it 50, which across a handful of uncertain questions is worth a meaningful number of marks.

7071
11:48:09,742 --> 11:48:13,316
That is why the order in the first three bullets matters.

7072
11:48:14,030 --> 11:48:22,564
That is because factual elimination and constraint elimination require no judgment at all and are therefore fast and reliable.

7073
11:48:23,214 --> 11:48:29,828
Comparing services on merit is the expensive step, and doing it last means doing it on fewer options.

7074
11:48:31,566 --> 11:48:34,116
The callout is the useful review technique.

7075
11:48:34,496 --> 11:48:40,132
When you return to a flagged question, the instinct is to compare the two options again.

7076
11:48:41,358 --> 11:48:42,820
That is what you already did,

7077
11:48:43,726 --> 11:48:45,284
and doing it a second time

7078
11:48:45,966 --> 11:48:48,004
rarely produces new information.

7079
11:48:48,974 --> 11:48:57,892
Rereading the scenario does because under time pressure the sentence people skip is the constraint, and reading it properly frequently resolves the question instantly.

7080
11:48:58,798 --> 11:49:02,256
The last bullet matters because review time is finite.

7081
11:49:02,798 --> 11:49:09,776
Rereading questions you answered confidently is a way of spending your remaining minutes without improving anything.

7082
11:49:10,702 --> 11:49:13,796
You are at question 48 with 35 minutes remaining.

7083
11:49:14,478 --> 11:49:16,644
You have six questions flagged for review.

7084
11:49:17,838 --> 11:49:24,868
Question 48 describes a complex multi-account governance scenario and you are torn between two options after 3 minutes of reading.

7085
11:49:25,358 --> 11:49:27,536
You are not confident about either.

7086
11:49:28,046 --> 11:49:30,852
What is the best use of the next 30 seconds?

7087
11:49:31,374 --> 11:49:35,616
a Keep working on question 48 until you are confident.

7088
11:49:36,016 --> 11:49:41,092
b Select your better guess, flag it, and move to question 49.

7089
11:49:42,254 --> 11:49:46,884
C. Leave it blank, flag it, and return at the end with fresh eyes.

7090
11:49:48,736 --> 11:49:48,896
D

7091
11:49:50,350 --> 11:49:53,444
Go back and review the six flagged questions first.

7092
11:49:54,382 --> 11:49:55,396
Pause and decide.

7093
11:50:01,838 --> 11:50:06,532
The answer is B, and the reasoning in the call out is the one to internalize before exam day.

7094
11:50:11,246 --> 11:50:16,772
17 questions you have not read may contain several. You would answer correctly in 40 seconds each.

7095
11:50:17,262 --> 11:50:21,936
One question you have already thought about for three minutes is unlikely to yield to a fourth.

7096
11:50:22,478 --> 11:50:29,092
So the arithmetic always favors moving forward, and the flag exists precisely so you can move forward without losing the question.

7097
11:50:29,376 --> 11:50:37,056
Option C is nearly right and gives up a free chance because with no penalty for guessing, a blank is a choice to score zero.

7098
11:50:38,256 --> 11:50:43,936
The caution in the call out is AWS's own wording, and the reason is statistical.

7099
11:50:44,334 --> 11:50:48,100
9 scored questions in domain 4 is a small sample.

7100
11:50:48,576 --> 11:50:55,940
So a needs improvement classification might mean you got 4 wrong, or it might mean you got 5 wrong on a bad day.

7101
11:50:56,416 --> 11:50:59,136
It is a direction rather than a measurement.

7102
11:50:59,694 --> 11:51:04,804
What it is genuinely useful for is deciding where to spend preparation time before a retake.

7103
11:51:05,390 --> 11:51:08,612
That is what the fourth work scenario in this module is about.

7104
11:51:10,670 --> 11:51:16,836
What it cannot do is tell you which specific questions you got wrong because AWS does not release that.

7105
11:51:18,478 --> 11:51:22,756
The out of scope list is worth knowing because it tells you what not to revise.

7106
11:51:23,758 --> 11:51:26,340
Language specific syntax is excluded.

7107
11:51:26,894 --> 11:51:30,308
So nobody will ask you to spot an error in a Python snippet.

7108
11:51:31,150 --> 11:51:39,760
Machine learning training and inference is excluded, which is why module 5 treats bedrock as a service you call rather than a model you

7109
11:51:39,920 --> 11:51:40,228
tune.

7110
11:51:41,582 --> 11:51:44,548
And drawing business conclusions from data is excluded.

7111
11:51:45,262 --> 11:51:48,932
So a question will never ask what the numbers mean commercially.

7112
11:51:49,870 --> 11:51:58,372
The vectors line on the fourth bullet is new and confirms that the material in module 11 is expected knowledge rather than optional.

7113
11:52:00,014 --> 11:52:06,340
The last bullet draws the line worth drawing because candidates waste real time memorizing figures that never appear.

7114
11:52:06,734 --> 11:52:10,020
A limit is worth knowing. When crossing it eliminates an option.

7115
11:52:10,695 --> 11:52:13,115
Lambda's 15 minutes eliminates Lambda outright.

7116
11:52:14,158 --> 11:52:19,876
Athena's 100 partitions per statement eliminates a single CTAS for a large backfill.

7117
11:52:20,240 --> 11:52:26,532
The 128 kilobyte billing minimum turns a cheaper storage class into a more expensive one for small objects.

7118
11:52:27,374 --> 11:52:29,188
All three of those change answers.

7119
11:52:30,030 --> 11:52:33,828
The exact price per gigabyte of Glacier Deep Archive changes nothing.

7120
11:52:34,320 --> 11:52:39,652
That is because the question will describe an access pattern and expect you to reason from it instead.

7121
11:52:41,120 --> 11:52:44,100
These numbers are the reason this course is shaped the way it is.

7122
11:52:44,320 --> 11:52:50,276
Domain 1 alone is roughly 17 scored questions, which is why 6 modules cover it and why they are the longest.

7123
11:52:50,640 --> 11:52:53,476
Domain 4 is 9 questions, which sounds small.

7124
11:52:54,160 --> 11:53:00,868
It is also the domain candidates most often neglect entirely, which makes it the cheapest place to gain marks.

7125
11:53:01,518 --> 11:53:08,900
The word roughly matters because the scale scoring model means the mapping from questions to score is not exactly linear.

7126
11:53:10,080 --> 11:53:14,948
These figures are the proportions applied to 50 scored questions.

7127
11:53:16,654 --> 11:53:19,520
You have finished a practice exam and scored 68%.

7128
11:53:20,240 --> 11:53:27,844
Your section feedback shows you are strongest in domain 1 and weakest in domain 4, where you got roughly half the questions right.

7129
11:53:28,240 --> 11:53:32,388
You have 2 weeks before the exam and about an hour a day to study.

7130
11:53:33,230 --> 11:53:35,524
What is the most effective use of that time?

7131
11:53:36,174 --> 11:53:41,280
a revise domain 1 further since it is a third of the exam and you are already strong.

7132
11:53:41,600 --> 11:53:48,160
B. Focus on domain 4 and review the specific questions you got wrong across all domains. C.

7133
11:53:48,558 --> 11:53:51,680
Take another 4 practice exams to build familiarity.

7134
11:53:52,000 --> 11:53:52,320
D.

7135
11:53:53,198 --> 11:53:55,460
Rewatch the entire course from the beginning.

7136
11:53:55,840 --> 11:53:57,444
Pause and decide.

7137
11:54:04,000 --> 11:54:05,600
The answer is B.

7138
11:54:06,000 --> 11:54:10,640
And option C is the one worth arguing against because it feels productive.

7139
11:54:10,894 --> 11:54:17,348
A practice exam measures where you are and the value is entirely in the review afterwards rather than in the score.

7140
11:54:17,998 --> 11:54:23,012
Taking four more without studying the mistakes produces four more measurements of the same knowledge.

7141
11:54:23,200 --> 11:54:27,140
The domain 4 reasoning is the arithmetic from earlier in this module.

7142
11:54:27,600 --> 11:54:33,092
9 scored questions where you currently get half right is 4 or 5 marks available.

7143
11:54:33,838 --> 11:54:35,844
That is a larger gain than anything.

7144
11:54:36,270 --> 11:54:39,492
Domain 1 can offer when you are already strong there.

7145
11:54:41,166 --> 11:54:46,372
The call out describes the difference between recognizing an answer and understanding a question.

7146
11:54:46,958 --> 11:54:52,036
You may be able to say why the correct option is correct and not why the other three are wrong.

7147
11:54:52,718 --> 11:54:55,684
That is one fact learned rather than a decision.

7148
11:54:56,334 --> 11:54:59,120
The exam will phrase the same decision differently.

7149
11:54:59,630 --> 11:55:05,604
Every solution slide in this course gives a reason for all four options for exactly that purpose.

7150
11:55:06,158 --> 11:55:13,924
In the final week, working through wrong answers until each one has an explanation is worth more than any amount of new material.

7151
11:55:15,374 --> 11:55:18,852
The call out is the honest arithmetic and it is worth believing.

7152
11:55:19,200 --> 11:55:26,852
The failures people describe from the last 20 questions are misreadings rather than knowledge gaps, and misreadings come from fatigue.

7153
11:55:27,374 --> 11:55:31,076
An extra service learned the night before might appear in one question.

7154
11:55:31,982 --> 11:55:35,280
Being alert for the final third affects perhaps 15.

7155
11:55:36,174 --> 11:55:39,908
The logistics bullet is less interesting and it catches people.

7156
11:55:40,430 --> 11:55:48,004
That is especially true for the online proctored option, where the room and identification rules are stricter than most candidates expect.

7157
11:55:48,590 --> 11:55:50,500
They are checked before the exam starts.

7158
11:55:52,110 --> 11:55:56,324
5 habits, and the last is the one this course has returned to most often.

7159
11:55:58,830 --> 11:56:07,268
Your production instinct is a genuine asset and it biases you towards building things, where the exam consistently prefers the option that removes the building.

7160
11:56:08,270 --> 11:56:14,212
Elimination may leave you with an answer that feels less like real engineering than another option does.

7161
11:56:15,374 --> 11:56:20,672
The elimination is usually right and the feeling is usually your experience talking.

7162
11:56:21,166 --> 11:56:30,788
Next module collects everything that changed in the December 2025 revision, which is where older study material and the exam diverge.

7163
11:56:32,334 --> 11:56:42,912
This module exists because the exam guide was revised on the 12th of December 2025. Almost every question bank, course, and set of nodes in circulation predates it.

7164
11:56:43,150 --> 11:56:45,796
That creates two separate problems rather than one.

7165
11:56:46,702 --> 11:56:51,396
There is material the exam now tests that older sources do not cover at all.

7166
11:56:51,712 --> 11:56:58,912
There is material older sources treat as perfectly current, which has since been retired, closed, or renamed.

7167
11:56:59,472 --> 11:57:07,748
This module collects both drawn from the verified log built while writing the previous 22 modules with the date recorded for each.

7168
11:57:08,782 --> 11:57:10,596
There are no verb scenarios here.

7169
11:57:10,832 --> 11:57:15,232
It is a reference and it is the one to reread the night before the exam.

7170
11:57:19,886 --> 11:57:24,592
An exam guide is published on a date and the questions behind it were written before that date.

7171
11:57:24,752 --> 11:57:26,372
So the exam is a snapshot.

7172
11:57:26,752 --> 11:57:30,916
AWS meanwhile duplicates, renames, and launches continuously.

7173
11:57:31,566 --> 11:57:34,948
When those two disagree, the exam is testing the snapshot.

7174
11:57:35,374 --> 11:57:39,844
So answer from the documented catalog rather than from what you would actually build today.

7175
11:57:40,782 --> 11:57:45,956
This course has flagged every divergence as it arose and this module gathers them.

7176
11:57:46,798 --> 11:57:54,404
Knowing both versions is the goal because it protects you in the exam and stops you giving out-of-date advice afterwards.

7177
11:57:56,110 --> 11:58:03,952
These eight are the clearest gap between this exam and older preparation material, and they cluster into three themes

7178
11:58:04,112 --> 11:58:06,116
rather than eight separate topics.

7179
11:58:07,182 --> 11:58:13,865
Generative capability, which is the large language model integration skill, and the vectorization. 1

7180
11:58:13,902 --> 11:58:24,420
Open table formats which is Iceberg and everything module 9 covered about transactions and time travel And the SageMaker governance layer,

7181
11:58:24,752 --> 11:58:32,100
which appears three times across two different domains, telling you how much weight AWS puts on it.

7182
11:58:32,590 --> 11:58:39,012
If you have been revising from a bank collected before December 2025, these eight are simply absent from it.

7183
11:58:39,312 --> 11:58:46,112
That makes them the cheapest marks available because most candidates sitting alongside you have not seen them either.

7184
11:58:46,990 --> 11:58:53,316
Reading the eight skills as a group tells you something useful about what to expect from questions on them.

7185
11:58:53,870 --> 11:58:56,644
AWS added them because the roles changed.

7186
11:58:57,294 --> 11:59:05,284
Data engineers now routinely put an unstructured document through a model to get structured output. So that became a skin.

7187
11:59:06,222 --> 11:59:11,312
Lakes acquired transactional table formats, so open table formats became a skill.

7188
11:59:11,472 --> 11:59:16,992
And organizations started governing data through catalogs and projects rather than through tickets.

7189
11:59:17,232 --> 11:59:18,785
So that became three skills.

7190
11:59:19,662 --> 11:59:28,548
Questions on new material tend to be less convoluted than questions on long-established services because there is less accumulated overlap to build distractors from.

7191
11:59:30,245 --> 11:59:37,125
Aurora is the one to prioritize because it now appears across several domains rather than in one place.

7192
11:59:37,774 --> 11:59:45,805
0 EPE into Redshift from Module 10, PG, Vector as a Vector Store from Module 11.

7193
11:59:46,272 --> 11:59:54,340
As the relational answer, whenever MySQL or PostgreSQL is named in a scenario, S3 tables is the second priority.

7194
11:59:54,752 --> 12:00:03,268
That is because module 9 established that it is the managed answer to iceberg maintenance and it is genuinely new to everybody.

7195
12:00:04,078 --> 12:00:11,684
Amazon Q is worth a moment of care because it assists an engineer writing code or a query rather than processing data inside a pipeline.

7196
12:00:12,032 --> 12:00:15,556
Confusing it with bedrock is an easy error and an avoidable one.

7197
12:00:17,134 --> 12:00:22,404
The contradiction on the last bullet is real and worth knowing about rather than being surprised by.

7198
12:00:22,672 --> 12:00:27,236
A WS SCCT was removed from the InScope services list.

7199
12:00:29,230 --> 12:00:34,532
In the same revision, a task statement continues to name it as an example of schema conversion.

7200
12:00:35,342 --> 12:00:41,092
The safe interpretation is that the concept is examinable and the tooling name is unstable.

7201
12:00:41,456 --> 12:00:43,876
So learn what schema conversion does.

7202
12:00:44,302 --> 12:00:52,164
Know that the capability now lives inside DMS as DMS S schema conversion and recognize either name.

7203
12:00:52,846 --> 12:01:00,324
Code commits removal is the cleaner one, and module 7 covered its practical consequence for continuous delivery questions.

7204
12:01:02,062 --> 12:01:06,496
This is the reverse problem and it is larger than most candidates expect.

7205
12:01:06,958 --> 12:01:16,676
These are services that older material treats as perfectly current answers and that AWS has since withdrawn close to new customers or renamed entirely.

7206
12:01:17,646 --> 12:01:21,828
The exam may still use them because the questions behind it predate the change.

7207
12:01:22,382 --> 12:01:29,348
The next three slides list every one this course encountered while being written, with the date and the successor for each.

7208
12:01:29,696 --> 12:01:34,052
The guidance is identical for all of them and it is worth stating once here.

7209
12:01:34,958 --> 12:01:43,332
Recognize the service, know its current status, and answer from the exam's catalog rather than from what you would deploy today.

7210
12:01:45,038 --> 12:01:50,820
The Snow family occupies four rows and it is the change most likely to affect a question you actually see.

7211
12:01:52,110 --> 12:01:57,924
Every older source teaches Snowball as the answer when moving petabytes over a network would take months.

7212
12:01:58,222 --> 12:02:01,188
And that reasoning is exactly what the exam tests.

7213
12:02:02,062 --> 12:02:09,732
So if a question describes limited bandwidth and an enormous one-off transfer with a deadline, Snowball is still the intended answer.

7214
12:02:10,816 --> 12:02:20,548
In reality, AWS now points at data sync, a data transfer terminal for physically delivering media to an AWS location and at outposts.

7215
12:02:21,454 --> 12:02:30,244
The kinesis data analytics for SQL Row is worth noting separately because those applications were actually deleted rather than merely closed.

7216
12:02:31,918 --> 12:02:38,532
The callout gives you the frequency and it explains why these two matter more than their obscurity suggests.

7217
12:02:41,655 --> 12:02:42,235
S3

7218
12:02:42,235 --> 12:02:50,295
Select let you run a small SQL expression against a single object and return only matching rows.

7219
12:02:51,536 --> 12:02:57,828
That is genuinely useful and is the marked correct answer to several questions still in circulation.

7220
12:02:58,416 --> 12:03:01,924
It closed to new customers in July 2024.

7221
12:03:02,510 --> 12:03:10,656
Object Lambda, which transformed objects as they were retrieved, became existing customers only in November 2025.

7222
12:03:11,566 --> 12:03:18,724
Both remain inside the exam scope material, so recognize them, and know that Athena is what you would build with today.

7223
12:03:20,334 --> 12:03:29,856
Renames cost marks in a specific and avoidable way. A candidate who does not know a service was renamed will try to distinguish two names describing one thing.

7224
12:03:30,096 --> 12:03:34,244
That costs time and sometimes costs a correct option that looked unfamiliar.

7225
12:03:35,136 --> 12:03:40,740
The quick site row is the worst of these because it happened twice inside a single year.

7226
12:03:40,976 --> 12:03:45,796
So three names for one business intelligence tool are now in circulation simultaneously.

7227
12:03:46,096 --> 12:03:49,348
The rule is simple and it applies to every row on this table.

7228
12:03:50,096 --> 12:03:59,460
If an option uses an older name, that is not a signal, the option is wrong because the exam's questions were written before the changes happened.

7229
12:04:01,198 --> 12:04:03,536
These six are the opposite problem again.

7230
12:04:03,822 --> 12:04:11,844
They are real, current, and post-date the exam guide, so they will not be tested and they change what the correct real world answer is.

7231
12:04:13,614 --> 12:04:14,724
Two mattermost.

7232
12:04:15,566 --> 12:04:20,836
RG instances remove redshift spectrums per terabyte scanning charge entirely.

7233
12:04:21,838 --> 12:04:27,332
Several classic spectrum answers depend on that charge existing, so answer as though it does.

7234
12:04:27,982 --> 12:04:33,536
MWA A serverless removes the paying for an idle environment argument.

7235
12:04:33,774 --> 12:04:37,444
That argument decides a lot of step functions against MWA.

7236
12:04:38,286 --> 12:04:40,036
A questions in older material.

7237
12:04:40,976 --> 12:04:46,756
Module 6 fourth scenario was built on that change specifically.

7238
12:04:48,398 --> 12:04:53,540
These five states are worth separating because they imply different behavior in the exam room.

7239
12:04:53,984 --> 12:05:00,900
A rename service is entirely safe because both names describe the same working thing and you can pick either.

7240
12:05:01,774 --> 12:05:08,420
A superseded one is safe too, since the older option still functions and the newer one is usually the better answer if both appear.

7241
12:05:09,422 --> 12:05:14,660
The first three are where the exam and reality genuinely diverge and where you answer from the catalog.

7242
12:05:15,598 --> 12:05:24,064
Snowball close to new customers is the clearest case because a question describing an offline petabyte transfer still expects it

7243
12:05:24,224 --> 12:05:25,668
and you could not order one.

7244
12:05:27,344 --> 12:05:33,860
These two deserve separating from the rest of the post guide changes because they do not merely add a capability.

7245
12:05:34,286 --> 12:05:36,804
Each removes a reason that decided questions.

7246
12:05:38,094 --> 12:05:42,532
Redsheft Spectrum has always charged per terabyte scanned on top of your cluster.

7247
12:05:43,504 --> 12:05:51,332
That is exactly why partitioning and columnar formats matter for spectrum queries in the way they do for Athena.

7248
12:05:53,678 --> 12:06:01,796
On RG instances, the scanning happens on your own nodes with no separate charge so that reasoning evaporates.

7249
12:06:02,670 --> 12:06:12,100
Similarly, the standard argument against MWAA was a scheduler and metadata database billing around the clock and serverless removes it.

7250
12:06:13,518 --> 12:06:17,636
Answer both from the guide and know why the guide says what it says.

7251
12:06:18,830 --> 12:06:28,260
The call out is worth being explicit about because everywhere else, this course has argued that the exam rewards genuinely good engineering.

7252
12:06:28,784 --> 12:06:30,052
This is the exception.

7253
12:06:30,958 --> 12:06:39,396
A question describing a petabyte transfer over limited bandwidth with a deadline is expecting snowball, and snowball is closed to new customers.

7254
12:06:40,238 --> 12:06:46,628
A question about retrieving a subset of a single object may well expect S3 select, which is also closed.

7255
12:06:47,502 --> 12:06:52,804
Answering either of those with what you would actually build today loses a mark you should have had.

7256
12:06:53,230 --> 12:07:01,252
The honest position is to hold both versions, answer the exam using its own catalog, and know what you would genuinely recommend on Monday morning.

7257
12:07:02,606 --> 12:07:05,828
This slide is the practical version of the new skills table.

7258
12:07:06,222 --> 12:07:12,484
That is because a new skill means a new question shape rather than simply a new fact to memorize.

7259
12:07:13,454 --> 12:07:22,436
Before the revision, a question about extracting six named fields from scanned invoices had no clean AWS answer and probably was not asked at all.

7260
12:07:23,022 --> 12:07:23,748
Now it does.

7261
12:07:24,206 --> 12:07:28,260
And Module 57th work scenario is exactly that question.

7262
12:07:29,230 --> 12:07:37,700
Before, a privacy erasure request against a data lake was a hard architectural problem involving rewriting files.

7263
12:07:38,158 --> 12:07:41,668
Now it is a delete statement against an iceberg table.

7264
12:07:42,510 --> 12:07:48,164
If you are preparing on older material, these are the question shapes you have never once practiced.

7265
12:07:49,902 --> 12:07:57,028
These five are worth reading twice because they are statements you may have read in a course or a blog post and internalized as fact.

7266
12:07:57,264 --> 12:07:59,172
Each was correct advice when written.

7267
12:07:59,886 --> 12:08:09,284
The Redshift serverless floor dropping to 4 RPS combined with DC2's deprecation inverts the old development environment advice completely.

7268
12:08:10,926 --> 12:08:17,824
MWA A serverless removes the cost argument that decided many orchestration comparisons.

7269
12:08:18,318 --> 12:08:25,860
And govern tables were lake formations answered to transactions in a lake before iceberg existed, which is why module 9 barely mentions them.

7270
12:08:26,382 --> 12:08:30,852
If a source you are revising from says any of these, treat the rest of it with care.

7271
12:08:32,622 --> 12:08:35,620
The call out gives you the single most reliable check.

7272
12:08:35,904 --> 12:08:43,824
Before the revision, iceberg appeared in study material as one more file format in a list beside Parquet and ORC.

7273
12:08:43,984 --> 12:08:48,964
That is how it appeared 29 times in the bank analyzed for this course.

7274
12:08:49,774 --> 12:08:57,344
After the revision, it is a named skill about transactions, time travel, and row level operations, which is what module 9 covered.

7275
12:08:57,504 --> 12:09:03,908
So a source describing Isaberg as a file format is not merely incomplete, it is teaching the wrong mental model.

7276
12:09:04,654 --> 12:09:07,972
It almost certainly predates December 2025.

7277
12:09:08,494 --> 12:09:11,748
2 minutes of checking, sales revising from something stale.

7278
12:09:13,454 --> 12:09:25,348
This slide exists to keep the previous ones in proportion because a list of 38 changes can feel like the ground has moved. 8 news cases out of well over 100 is meaningful and it is not a rewrite.

7279
12:09:25,904 --> 12:09:34,212
Four services appeared most often in the bank analyzed for this course Blue, S3, Redshift, and Athena.

7280
12:09:36,110 --> 12:09:39,300
Those are exactly the services dominating the current exam guide.

7281
12:09:40,686 --> 12:09:51,012
The reasoning this course has taught throughout about access patterns, frequency, existing technology, and operational overhead decides the same proportion of questions it always did.

7282
12:09:51,918 --> 12:09:54,308
An older course is therefore not worthless.

7283
12:09:54,894 --> 12:09:59,332
It is incomplete at the edges, and this module is the patch for those edges.

7284
12:10:00,910 --> 12:10:06,852
38 items is the size of the gap between an older preparation source and this exam.

7285
12:10:07,406 --> 12:10:12,004
It is worth seeing as a single number because it is neither trivial nor overwhelming.

7286
12:10:12,590 --> 12:10:17,316
It is roughly an evening's reading and most of it is recognition rather than understanding.

7287
12:10:18,350 --> 12:10:22,660
The verification point in the callout matters for how much weight to give this module.

7288
12:10:22,926 --> 12:10:30,276
Each of these was checked against AWS documentation while the relevant teaching module was written with the date recorded at the time.

7289
12:10:30,990 --> 12:10:33,668
None of it was recalled from memory afterwards.

7290
12:10:34,432 --> 12:10:41,316
Where a date appears in this module, it came from AWS's own announcement or its documentation.

7291
12:10:43,054 --> 12:10:47,940
The call-out describes a deliberate choice worth explaining because it would have been easier to do otherwise.

7292
12:10:48,686 --> 12:10:55,012
When AWS's current position and the exam guide's position differ, the tidy approach is to teach one of them and say nothing.

7293
12:10:55,872 --> 12:11:03,108
This course states both every time, which occasionally makes a slide more complicated than it needs to be.

7294
12:11:03,342 --> 12:11:07,812
The reason is that you are preparing for an exam and then doing a job.

7295
12:11:08,558 --> 12:11:16,004
A course that teaches only the exam answer leaves you confidently recommending a service that closed to new customers 18 months ago.

7296
12:11:16,512 --> 12:11:18,340
The fourth bullet matters too.

7297
12:11:18,672 --> 12:11:21,604
No question from the source bank was ever reproduced.

7298
12:11:23,310 --> 12:11:28,900
The fourth bullet is worth saying because these tables contain a lot of dates and none of them is examinable.

7299
12:11:29,806 --> 12:11:35,396
The dates are here, so the tables are verifiable, and so you can judge how current a source is when you meet one.

7300
12:11:36,430 --> 12:11:39,044
What matters is the status and the successor.

7301
12:11:39,982 --> 12:11:43,620
Snowball is closed and datasync is where AWS points.

7302
12:11:43,935 --> 12:11:44,295
S3.

7303
12:11:45,375 --> 12:11:47,455
Select is closed and Athena does the job.

7304
12:11:48,752 --> 12:11:55,396
Python user-defined functions in Redshift are ending, and SQL or Lambda user-defined functions replace them.

7305
12:11:56,206 --> 12:12:01,412
Next come three practice exams, 20 questions each with every option explained.

7306
12:12:03,118 --> 12:12:06,660
Three practice exams follow, 20 questions each.

7307
12:12:06,832 --> 12:12:08,592
They are weighted like the real thing.

7308
12:12:08,752 --> 12:12:15,780
So this one has 7 questions from domain 1, 5 from domain 2, 4 from domain 3, and 4 from domain 4.

7309
12:12:16,462 --> 12:12:25,380
Every question was written from the exam guide's task statements and from facts verified against AWS documentation while this course was being made.

7310
12:12:25,712 --> 12:12:28,420
None reproduces a question from anywhere else.

7311
12:12:28,672 --> 12:12:36,548
Each one is followed by a worked answer explaining not only why the correct option is correct, but why each of the other three is wrong,

7312
12:12:36,912 --> 12:12:39,140
because that is where the learning is.

7313
12:12:40,398 --> 12:12:45,604
The fourth bullet is the one that turns a practice exam into study rather than measurement.

7314
12:12:45,872 --> 12:12:53,764
Choosing the right answer for the wrong reason feels identical to choosing it for the right reason, and only writing down your reasoning exposes the difference.

7315
12:12:54,478 --> 12:13:00,548
You might reject an option because it mentions a service you dislike rather than because it breaks a stated constraint.

7316
12:13:01,072 --> 12:13:05,252
You will make that mistake again on a question where the service is correct.

7317
12:13:06,832 --> 12:13:13,312
Two questions in this set are multiple response, asking for two answers, which reflects the real exam.

7318
12:13:13,872 --> 12:13:15,908
The question tells you when.

7319
12:13:17,422 --> 12:13:23,620
The last bullet is worth explaining because it was a genuine defect when these questions were first drafted.

7320
12:13:25,774 --> 12:13:28,932
21 of the 22 correct marks landed on A or B.

7321
12:13:29,966 --> 12:13:34,372
A question writer tends to put the answer they thought of first near the top.

7322
12:13:35,278 --> 12:13:42,980
A candidate practicing against the act learns to favor the early options, which is a habit that transfers badly.

7323
12:13:45,614 --> 12:13:50,912
The answers here are now distributed evenly across all four letters.

7324
12:13:51,392 --> 12:13:59,876
When you review your own performance, that means a letter bias in your answers tells you something about your reading rather than about the question set.

7325
12:14:01,326 --> 12:14:02,468
Question 1 of 20.

7326
12:14:03,054 --> 12:14:03,716
Domain 1.

7327
12:14:04,112 --> 12:14:08,836
A logistics company runs a self-managed Apache Kafka cluster on Amazon EC2.

7328
12:14:09,486 --> 12:14:15,396
Around 40 microservices produce and consume from it using Kafka client libraries and a schema registry.

7329
12:14:16,334 --> 12:14:24,272
The operations team spends significant time patching brokers and rebalancing partitions. The company wants to reduce that operational work.

7330
12:14:24,512 --> 12:14:30,340
Throughput is high, steady, and well understood, and the services must not be rewritten.

7331
12:14:31,118 --> 12:14:33,156
What should the data engineer recommend?

7332
12:14:34,254 --> 12:14:36,772
a Amazon MSK serverless.

7333
12:14:37,552 --> 12:14:37,792
B

7334
12:14:39,182 --> 12:14:42,500
Amazon Kinesis Data streams in on-demand mode.

7335
12:14:42,832 --> 12:14:43,072
C.

7336
12:14:43,534 --> 12:14:47,428
Amazon MSK provisioned migrating the existing topics.

7337
12:14:48,334 --> 12:14:52,196
D. Amazon data firehose delivering to Amazon S3.

7338
12:14:52,992 --> 12:14:54,276
Pause and decide.

7339
12:15:04,014 --> 12:15:05,952
The answer is D.

7340
12:15:06,638 --> 12:15:11,588
Existing Kafka with 40 dependent services eliminates A and D immediately.

7341
12:15:11,918 --> 12:15:17,188
That is because neither speaks the Kafka protocol and both would mean rewriting every one of those services.

7342
12:15:17,678 --> 12:15:22,116
That leaves two Amazon MSK options, which is where the question actually lives.

7343
12:15:23,022 --> 12:15:31,556
Serverless is the right answer when load is unpredictable or nobody has measured it, and this question deliberately says the opposite.

7344
12:15:34,606 --> 12:15:44,292
High, steady, and well understood describes a workload somebody has measured, and measured means provision capacity is cheaper per unit of throughput.

7345
12:15:45,102 --> 12:15:48,644
This is the two-stage elimination module 21 described.

7346
12:15:48,992 --> 12:15:55,780
Compatibility removes half the options and then the provisioned against serverless signal separates what remains.

7347
12:15:57,326 --> 12:15:58,468
Question 2 of 20.

7348
12:15:58,832 --> 12:15:59,748
Domain 1.

7349
12:16:00,110 --> 12:16:06,564
An AWS blue job reads JSON files from a partner and writes parquet to a curated table.

7350
12:16:07,182 --> 12:16:08,964
It has run nightly for a year.

7351
12:16:09,518 --> 12:16:16,228
Last week, the partner added a nested field and changed one integer column to sometimes contain a string.

7352
12:16:17,166 --> 12:16:20,752
The job now fails on some nights and succeeds on others.

7353
12:16:20,912 --> 12:16:25,604
When it succeeds, downstream totals are slightly lower than the source system reports.

7354
12:16:26,126 --> 12:16:28,388
What should the data engineer implement?

7355
12:16:29,038 --> 12:16:32,804
a increase the number of workers so the job has more memory.

7356
12:16:35,470 --> 12:16:39,172
Enable job bookmarks so previously processed files are skipped.

7357
12:16:39,552 --> 12:16:39,792
C.

7358
12:16:40,334 --> 12:16:43,684
Configure the crawler's schema change policy to ignore changes.

7359
12:16:44,366 --> 12:16:44,592
D.

7360
12:16:45,294 --> 12:16:50,660
Read the data as a dynamic frame and use resort choice to handle the ambiguous column explicitly.

7361
12:16:52,142 --> 12:16:53,284
Pause and decide.

7362
12:17:03,022 --> 12:17:09,060
The answer is B, and the question gives you two symptoms that look unrelated and share a cause.

7363
12:17:09,902 --> 12:17:14,148
Failing on some nights is the strict read encountering an unpassable value.

7364
12:17:14,702 --> 12:17:20,868
Succeeding with low totals is the permissive path coercing those values to null, so they vanish from a sum.

7365
12:17:21,838 --> 12:17:24,356
Both are the type confit the partner introduced.

7366
12:17:24,912 --> 12:17:28,868
A dynamic frame keeps both possible types alive rather than guessing.

7367
12:17:29,710 --> 12:17:37,444
Resolve choice makes you decide deliberately whether to cast, keep both as separate columns, or root the problem rows aside.

7368
12:17:38,286 --> 12:17:41,028
That is precisely what dynamic frames exist for.

7369
12:17:42,752 --> 12:17:44,644
Question 3 of 20.

7370
12:17:44,912 --> 12:17:45,956
Domain 1.

7371
12:17:46,702 --> 12:17:55,876
A step functions state machine starts an AWS blue job, then runs a view crawler, then triggers a lambda function that publishes a summary.

7372
12:17:56,432 --> 12:17:59,204
The state machine reports success every night.

7373
12:18:00,110 --> 12:18:07,716
Analysts report that the crawler sometimes registers only part of the day's data and the summary occasionally reports figures that later change.

7374
12:18:08,942 --> 12:18:10,372
What is the most likely cause?

7375
12:18:12,078 --> 12:18:15,872
a The Lambda function is timing out before the summary is written.

7376
12:18:16,206 --> 12:18:19,204
b. The crawler needs a longer schedule interval.

7377
12:18:20,462 --> 12:18:20,672
C.

7378
12:18:21,710 --> 12:18:28,772
The blue job is being invoked with the default request response pattern. So the state machine does not wait for it to finish.

7379
12:18:29,358 --> 12:18:33,668
d. The state machine should be an express workflow rather than standard.

7380
12:18:34,478 --> 12:18:35,844
Pause and decide.

7381
12:18:45,486 --> 12:18:50,400
The answer is A, and it is the single most examinable detail in step functions.

7382
12:18:50,798 --> 12:19:00,516
Calling a blue job with the default request response pattern starts the job and reports success immediately because the API call itself succeeded.

7383
12:19:01,360 --> 12:19:09,092
The state machine then runs the crawler against a table the job is still writing into, which is why the crawler registers partial data.

7384
12:19:09,710 --> 12:19:15,748
The dot sync pattern is what makes the state wait for the job to finish and fail if the job failed.

7385
12:19:16,590 --> 12:19:23,620
This question is deliberately built so that nothing errors anywhere and every other option addresses a failure that is not occurring.

7386
12:19:24,110 --> 12:19:27,524
Success everywhere with wrong output points at ordering.

7387
12:19:29,262 --> 12:19:30,560
Question 4 of 20.

7388
12:19:30,830 --> 12:19:31,840
Domain 1.

7389
12:19:32,080 --> 12:19:46,852
A nightly AWS GUE job processes about 400GB and takes 90 minutes. It runs on 20 G2X workers and must finish before 8 in the morning. It currently finishes at 2. The company wants to reduce cost.

7390
12:19:47,662 --> 12:19:54,500
The job has no deadline other than 8 in the morning and monitoring shows all workers evenly utilized throughout.

7391
12:19:55,406 --> 12:19:57,124
What should the data engineer do?

7392
12:19:57,998 --> 12:20:03,364
a migrate the job to Amazon EMR on EC2 with spot instances.

7393
12:20:03,760 --> 12:20:06,724
B. Run the job on BlueFlex execution.

7394
12:20:07,502 --> 12:20:10,596
C. Switch to memory optimized R workers.

7395
12:20:11,502 --> 12:20:15,492
D. Reduce the worker count to 5 and accept a longer runtime.

7396
12:20:16,334 --> 12:20:17,700
Pause and decide.

7397
12:20:27,374 --> 12:20:28,960
The answer is A.

7398
12:20:29,440 --> 12:20:33,668
The question tells you the job finishes at 2 and is needed by 8.

7399
12:20:34,414 --> 12:20:36,132
That is 6 hours of slack.

7400
12:20:37,102 --> 12:20:40,996
And that phrasing exists to establish that start time does not matter.

7401
12:20:41,902 --> 12:20:49,860
Flex execution runs on spare capacity at up to 34% less in exchange for no guarantee about when it starts.

7402
12:20:50,446 --> 12:20:56,100
Option B is worth understanding because it feels like light sizing and blue bills by DPU hour.

7403
12:20:56,974 --> 12:20:59,760
Halving the workers roughly doubles the runtime.

7404
12:20:59,920 --> 12:21:04,708
So the product is similar and you have simply made the job slower for nothing.

7405
12:21:06,094 --> 12:21:07,524
Question 5 of 20.

7406
12:21:08,000 --> 12:21:08,868
Domain 1.

7407
12:21:09,280 --> 12:21:11,492
This question asks for two answers.

7408
12:21:12,080 --> 12:21:18,660
A company is migrating a 40TB Oracle database to Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL.

7409
12:21:19,920 --> 12:21:26,756
The source database must remain available to its application throughout, and cut over downtime must be under 15 minutes.

7410
12:21:27,600 --> 12:21:31,280
The database contains a number of large object columns.

7411
12:21:31,520 --> 12:21:36,240
The team has assessed the schema and knows some stored procedures need manual work.

7412
12:21:36,480 --> 12:21:37,796
Select two actions.

7413
12:21:38,160 --> 12:21:46,276
The data engineer should take a run an AWS DMS task with full load plus change data capture.

7414
12:21:46,880 --> 12:21:52,708
B Run an AWS DMS full load only task during a maintenance window.

7415
12:21:53,200 --> 12:21:53,520
C.

7416
12:21:53,966 --> 12:22:00,356
Use DMS schema conversion to convert the schema and identify objects needing manual work.

7417
12:22:01,102 --> 12:22:01,360
D.

7418
12:22:02,094 --> 12:22:05,540
Use AWS DataSync to transfer the database files.

7419
12:22:06,254 --> 12:22:07,492
Pause and decide.

7420
12:22:17,136 --> 12:22:27,012
The answers are A and C, and the pairing follows module 22's advice about multiple response questions exactly. Count the requirements before you look at the options.

7421
12:22:27,456 --> 12:22:33,156
Different database engines, Oracle to PostgreSQL, means the schema has to be converted.

7422
12:22:33,934 --> 12:22:39,972
That is C, and the assessment report is what identifies the stored procedures needing manual work.

7423
12:22:40,336 --> 12:22:45,856
Near zero downtime with the source staying live means change data capture, which is A.

7424
12:22:46,990 --> 12:22:49,220
One answer for each requirement.

7425
12:22:49,870 --> 12:22:52,420
Option B is the one to reject carefully.

7426
12:22:53,296 --> 12:22:59,616
That is because full load only is a perfectly good DMS mode when a source can be read only during the move.

7427
12:22:59,918 --> 12:23:02,756
This question rules that out in its second sentence.

7428
12:23:04,238 --> 12:23:05,616
Question 6 of 20.

7429
12:23:05,776 --> 12:23:06,596
Domain 1.

7430
12:23:06,816 --> 12:23:19,652
A retailer sends clickstream events to Amazon Data Firehose, which writes raw.json to Amazon S3 in a single prefix. Analysts query the data with Amazon Athena and costs are rising steeply.

7431
12:23:20,238 --> 12:23:26,052
A fraud team has now also asked to consume the same events within seconds independently.

7432
12:23:27,502 --> 12:23:31,652
The team wants to address both the query cost and the fraud team's requirement.

7433
12:23:32,558 --> 12:23:34,500
What should the data engineer implement?

7434
12:23:35,566 --> 12:23:41,092
a Increase the Firehose buffer size so fewer larger files are written.

7435
12:23:42,350 --> 12:23:42,656
B

7436
12:23:43,854 --> 12:23:52,416
Enable Firehose format conversion to parquet with dynamic partitioning and add a second firehose stream for the fraud

7437
12:23:52,576 --> 12:23:53,056
team.

7438
12:23:53,376 --> 12:23:59,972
C Keep the current design and have the fraud team query the S3 files with Athena.

7439
12:24:00,974 --> 12:24:01,216
D.

7440
12:24:02,478 --> 12:24:10,436
Put a Kinesis data stream in front with Firehose as one consumer converting to partition parquet and the fraud team as another consumer.

7441
12:24:10,830 --> 12:24:12,036
Pause and decide.

7442
12:24:21,550 --> 12:24:25,348
The answer is B and option A is the near miss worth studying.

7443
12:24:25,856 --> 12:24:33,341
Its first half is exactly right because format conversion to parquet with dynamic partitioning is the correct fix for Athena costs on Raw JSON.

7444
12:24:34,382 --> 12:24:41,028
Its second half fails because a firehose stream delivers to a destination and does not provide a consumable feed.

7445
12:24:41,742 --> 12:24:48,548
The fraud team needs to read events independently within seconds, and that is what a Kinesis data stream provides.

7446
12:24:49,070 --> 12:24:58,020
This is module 21's firehose against data streams pair, where replay and multiple consumers are the two capabilities that decide it.

7447
12:24:59,598 --> 12:25:01,028
Question 7 of 20.

7448
12:25:01,422 --> 12:25:02,244
Domain 1.

7449
12:25:03,182 --> 12:25:10,692
An AWS Lambda function converts small JSON files to Parquet as they arrive in Amazon S3.

7450
12:25:11,056 --> 12:25:17,252
It is configured with 512 MB of memory and takes about 25 seconds per file.

7451
12:25:17,696 --> 12:25:22,096
CloudWatch shows peak memory usage of around 200 MB.

7452
12:25:22,976 --> 12:25:25,700
The conversion is CPU bound.

7453
12:25:26,096 --> 12:25:31,908
The company processes around 100,000 files a month and wants to reduce the lambda cost.

7454
12:25:32,736 --> 12:25:34,628
What should the data engineer do?

7455
12:25:37,422 --> 12:25:42,916
Increase the memory allocation, measure the change in duration, and select the cheapest total.

7456
12:25:43,536 --> 12:25:50,244
b reduce the memory allocation to 256 MB to match observed usage.

7457
12:25:50,736 --> 12:25:55,300
c enable provision concurrency to remove cold start time.

7458
12:25:55,776 --> 12:25:56,096
d

7459
12:25:57,038 --> 12:26:00,976
Increase the function timeout to allow more processing time.

7460
12:26:01,710 --> 12:26:03,268
Pause and decide.

7461
12:26:12,686 --> 12:26:14,080
The answer is B.

7462
12:26:14,734 --> 12:26:20,260
Option A is the intuitive answer, and it is wrong for a specific reason worth remembering.

7463
12:26:20,814 --> 12:26:23,012
Lambda has no CPU setting.

7464
12:26:23,406 --> 12:26:32,356
You configure memory and processor power is allocated proportionally, so reducing memory that looks unused also reduces the processor doing the work.

7465
12:26:33,134 --> 12:26:37,316
For a CPU bound function that roughly doubles the duration.

7466
12:26:37,870 --> 12:26:44,708
Since you are built for duration multiplied by memory, the total barely changes while the pipeline gets slower.

7467
12:26:45,198 --> 12:26:47,780
The word measure in option B matters.

7468
12:26:48,238 --> 12:26:54,276
2. Because the relationship is not perfectly linear and the cheapest setting has to be found empirically.

7469
12:26:55,918 --> 12:26:57,316
Question 8 of 20.

7470
12:26:57,774 --> 12:26:58,628
Domain 2.

7471
12:26:59,022 --> 12:27:01,380
A company stores sensor readings in Amazon.

7472
12:27:01,840 --> 12:27:02,320
S3.

7473
12:27:02,798 --> 12:27:04,708
Objects average 30 kilobytes.

7474
12:27:05,678 --> 12:27:12,900
Data is queried frequently for 14 days, then very rarely, but must be retained for 5 years for regulatory reasons.

7475
12:27:13,742 --> 12:27:26,244
A data engineer proposes a lifecycle rule transitioning objects to S3 standard in frequent access after 14 days and expiring them after 5 years. What is the problem with this proposal?

7476
12:27:26,862 --> 12:27:28,160
a standard.

7477
12:27:28,960 --> 12:27:33,380
IA retrieval takes several hours, which is too slow for regulatory access.

7478
12:27:34,400 --> 12:27:34,720
B

7479
12:27:35,950 --> 12:27:39,492
Standard IA cannot be used for objects retained longer than one year.

7480
12:27:40,080 --> 12:27:40,320
C.

7481
12:27:40,720 --> 12:27:44,164
Lifecycle rules cannot transition objects until they are 30 days old.

7482
12:27:44,560 --> 12:27:50,480
D. Objects of 30 kilobytes are built at a 128KB minimum in standard IA.

7483
12:27:51,120 --> 12:27:53,700
So storage cost may rise rather than fall.

7484
12:27:54,640 --> 12:27:56,068
Pause and decide.

7485
12:28:05,550 --> 12:28:10,884
The answer is A. And this is module 21's advice about numbers applied directly.

7486
12:28:11,790 --> 12:28:13,796
30 kilobytes is not seen setting.

7487
12:28:22,640 --> 12:28:22,880
So

7488
12:28:22,960 --> 12:28:30,532
each object is charged as more than 4 times what it is, and the supposedly cheaper class costs more per real gigabyte than standard.

7489
12:28:32,142 --> 12:28:37,600
Option C is worth reading carefully because it contains a real number used wrongly.

7490
12:28:38,190 --> 12:28:44,836
There is a 30-day minimum storage duration in standard IA, which is a different thing from a minimum age before transitioning.

7491
12:28:46,510 --> 12:28:48,484
Question 9 of 20.

7492
12:28:48,974 --> 12:28:49,796
Domain 2.

7493
12:28:51,918 --> 12:28:58,320
A media company streams viewing events into Apache IZBulk tables in a general purpose.

7494
12:28:58,798 --> 12:29:02,660
Amazon S3 bucket using Amazon Data Firehose.

7495
12:29:03,310 --> 12:29:05,252
Updates arrive continuously.

7496
12:29:06,126 --> 12:29:12,804
Over 8 months, query times have roughly tripled and storage costs have grown faster than the underlying data volume.

7497
12:29:13,760 --> 12:29:21,412
The platform team is two people supporting 20 pipelines and has no capacity to build and operate maintenance jobs.

7498
12:29:22,542 --> 12:29:24,516
What should the data engineer recommend?

7499
12:29:27,438 --> 12:29:31,172
Increase the Firehose buffer interval to reduce the number of files written.

7500
12:29:31,840 --> 12:29:39,812
b Migrate the tables to Amazon S3 tables, which performs compaction and snapshot management automatically.

7501
12:29:39,870 --> 12:29:41,081
c.

7502
12:29:41,520 --> 12:29:44,561
Convert the tables from iceberg back to plain Parquet.

7503
12:29:45,710 --> 12:29:45,920
D

7504
12:29:47,150 --> 12:29:53,348
Write a scheduled Spark job on Amazon EMR to compact files and expire snapshots.

7505
12:29:54,382 --> 12:29:55,492
Pause and decide.

7506
12:30:05,230 --> 12:30:06,496
The answer is A.

7507
12:30:08,174 --> 12:30:12,932
The two symptoms together are diagnostic and they are worth recognizing as a pair.

7508
12:30:14,158 --> 12:30:18,020
Query slowing over months points at small files accumulating.

7509
12:30:18,830 --> 12:30:25,476
Storage growing faster than the underlying data points at snapshots and orphaned files that nothing has expired.

7510
12:30:26,382 --> 12:30:31,296
Both are the maintenance deck that Izeberg accrues when nobody runs compaction.

7511
12:30:31,536 --> 12:30:35,856
Option B is the distractor that tests whether you read the final sentence.

7512
12:30:36,686 --> 12:30:46,308
That is because a scheduled compaction job is genuinely the correct technical fix and the question explicitly says the team cannot build or operate one.

7513
12:30:48,942 --> 12:30:56,068
Two people supporting 20 pipelines is the constraint, and constraints decide questions more often than technical merit does.

7514
12:30:57,678 --> 12:30:58,980
Question 10 of 20.

7515
12:30:59,342 --> 12:31:00,132
Domain 2.

7516
12:31:00,336 --> 12:31:06,276
An Amazon Redshift cluster holds a 3 billion row events table and a 50,000 row campaigns table.

7517
12:31:06,576 --> 12:31:13,572
Almost every query joins the two on campaign ID and filters on an event date range covering the last 7 days.

7518
12:31:14,446 --> 12:31:18,788
Both tables currently use EVEN distribution and neither has a sort key.

7519
12:31:19,310 --> 12:31:22,436
Monitoring shows heavy data redistribution during joins.

7520
12:31:23,790 --> 12:31:26,020
Which combination will most improve performance?

7521
12:31:26,478 --> 12:31:29,412
a Enable concurrency scaling on the cluster.

7522
12:31:30,670 --> 12:31:33,316
b Add 4 more nodes to the cluster.

7523
12:31:34,286 --> 12:31:34,496
C

7524
12:31:35,470 --> 12:31:41,700
Set both tables to KEY distribution on campaign ID and add interleaved sort keys.

7525
12:31:42,542 --> 12:31:46,820
D. Set the events table to KEY distribution on campaign.

7526
12:31:48,206 --> 12:31:51,296
Id the campaigns table to ALL.

7527
12:31:52,174 --> 12:31:54,628
Add a compound sort key on event date.

7528
12:31:55,118 --> 12:31:56,452
Pause and decide.

7529
12:32:06,126 --> 12:32:08,176
The answer is B

7530
12:32:09,198 --> 12:32:13,284
and two details in the scenario, each point at one half of it.

7531
12:32:14,030 --> 12:32:17,572
Heavy redistribution during joins is a distribution style problem.

7532
12:32:18,446 --> 12:32:23,844
A 50,000 row dimension table is small enough to replicate to every node at negligible cost.

7533
12:32:24,816 --> 12:32:32,336
That removes the data movement entirely rather than reducing it. Filtering 7 days out of 3 billion rows is a sort key problem.

7534
12:32:32,496 --> 12:32:38,576
A compound sort on event date lets the zone maps discard almost every block before reading it.

7535
12:32:39,214 --> 12:32:47,140
Options A and D are both capacity offered in two different forms, and neither addresses why an individual query is slow.

7536
12:32:48,430 --> 12:32:52,548
That pattern capacity twice is worth noticing on site.

7537
12:32:54,286 --> 12:32:55,776
Question 11 of 20.

7538
12:32:56,046 --> 12:32:57,028
Domain 2.

7539
12:32:57,486 --> 12:33:03,268
An application stores orders in Amazon DynamoDB with order ID as the partition key.

7540
12:33:03,726 --> 12:33:07,136
A new requirement is to list all orders for a given customer.

7541
12:33:07,310 --> 12:33:08,708
Sorted by order date.

7542
12:33:09,550 --> 12:33:12,964
The table already holds 200 million items.

7543
12:33:13,838 --> 12:33:18,692
Reads for this new pattern do not need to reflect the most recent light immediately.

7544
12:33:19,182 --> 12:33:21,188
What should the data engineer implement?

7545
12:33:22,062 --> 12:33:28,416
a A global secondary index partitioned by customer, I B and sorted by order date. B.

7546
12:33:28,656 --> 12:33:31,012
A local secondary index with customer

7547
12:33:31,790 --> 12:33:33,444
ID as the sort key.

7548
12:33:34,256 --> 12:33:34,496
C

7549
12:33:35,726 --> 12:33:38,916
A scan with a filter expression on customer windy.

7550
12:33:40,430 --> 12:33:40,656
D

7551
12:33:42,256 --> 12:33:46,660
Export the table to Amazon S3 and query it with Amazon Athena.

7552
12:33:47,278 --> 12:33:48,260
Pause and decide.

7553
12:33:57,902 --> 12:34:04,420
The answer is B, and option A fails twice over, which is worth noticing because either failure alone would settle it.

7554
12:34:04,800 --> 12:34:08,164
A local secondary index keeps the base table's partition key.

7555
12:34:09,166 --> 12:34:14,788
So items would still be grouped by order rather than by customer, and the required query could not be answered at all.

7556
12:34:15,630 --> 12:34:24,900
Separately, a local secondary index must be created with the table and can never be added afterwards. And this table already holds 200 million items.

7557
12:34:26,126 --> 12:34:32,868
The sentence about not needing the most recent write is there deliberately to tell you that eventual consistency is acceptable.

7558
12:34:33,760 --> 12:34:38,116
That is a global secondary index's one meaningful limitation.

7559
12:34:39,411 --> 12:34:41,604
Question 12 of 20, domain 2.

7560
12:34:42,990 --> 12:34:47,780
A GU job writes hourly output to Amazon S3 under Hive style partitions.

7561
12:34:48,398 --> 12:34:54,480
A crawler runs every 10 minutes to register new partitions so Athena users can query recent data.

7562
12:34:55,022 --> 12:35:01,060
Finance has flagged the crawler's cost and analysts still wait up to 10 minutes for new data to appear.

7563
12:35:01,678 --> 12:35:04,772
The table is also queried by Amazon Redshift Spectrum.

7564
12:35:05,710 --> 12:35:07,268
What should the data engineer do?

7565
12:35:07,950 --> 12:35:11,300
a reduce the crawler schedule to every 3 minutes.

7566
12:35:13,921 --> 12:35:14,041
B.

7567
12:35:15,022 --> 12:35:18,148
Configure partition projection on the table in Athena.

7568
12:35:18,720 --> 12:35:19,040
C.

7569
12:35:19,694 --> 12:35:25,732
Enable EnableUpdate Catalog on the BlueJob so it registers its own partitions and removes the crawler.

7570
12:35:26,318 --> 12:35:26,560
D.

7571
12:35:26,880 --> 12:35:31,268
Run MSCK repair table on a schedule instead of the crawler.

7572
12:35:31,918 --> 12:35:33,156
Pause and decide.

7573
12:35:42,798 --> 12:35:47,520
The answer is B, and the last sentence of the scenario is doing deliberate work.

7574
12:35:47,918 --> 12:35:52,836
Partition projection is genuinely an excellent answer for an hourly partition scheme.

7575
12:35:53,710 --> 12:36:00,420
That is because it calculates partition paths from a known pattern and removes the catalog lookup entirely.

7576
12:36:00,942 --> 12:36:02,532
It is an Athena feature.

7577
12:36:03,502 --> 12:36:11,172
Redshift Spectrum reading the same catalog table gains nothing from it and loses the registered partitions it had been relying on.

7578
12:36:12,430 --> 12:36:19,200
Enable update catalog works for every engine because the job registers real partitions that all of them read.

7579
12:36:19,726 --> 12:36:24,228
Notice also that option A fails on the crawler's 10 minute billing minimum.

7580
12:36:24,846 --> 12:36:28,484
So running it more often triples a cost while shortening a delay.

7581
12:36:29,038 --> 12:36:30,916
The job could remove complete key.

7582
12:36:32,590 --> 12:36:34,080
Question 13 of 20.

7583
12:36:34,320 --> 12:36:35,236
Domain 3.

7584
12:36:35,790 --> 12:36:37,892
This question asks for two answers.

7585
12:36:38,240 --> 12:36:44,132
An analytics team queries a 50TB dataset in Amazon S3 with Amazon Athena.

7586
12:36:45,966 --> 12:36:48,612
The data is parquet, partitioned by date.

7587
12:36:49,102 --> 12:36:50,628
Costs have risen sharply.

7588
12:36:51,280 --> 12:36:55,840
Investigation shows analysts write queries as select star across 90 columns.

7589
12:36:56,080 --> 12:36:57,380
6 dashboards.

7590
12:36:57,966 --> 12:37:03,396
Each rerun the same 4 queries every 10 minutes against data that is refreshed once daily.

7591
12:37:04,878 --> 12:37:07,812
Select two changes that will most reduce the Athena bill.

7592
12:37:08,558 --> 12:37:11,364
a name only the required columns in queries.

7593
12:37:15,360 --> 12:37:17,220
Reuse for the dashboard queries.

7594
12:37:17,840 --> 12:37:22,404
C. Move the dataset to S3 standard infrequent access.

7595
12:37:22,894 --> 12:37:23,200
D.

7596
12:37:23,470 --> 12:37:27,076
Repartition the data by hour instead of by date.

7597
12:37:27,726 --> 12:37:28,964
Pause and decide.

7598
12:37:38,638 --> 12:37:40,292
The answers are A and B.

7599
12:37:40,878 --> 12:37:48,612
The question names both causes explicitly in its second paragraph, which is generous, and each cause has exactly one matching option.

7600
12:37:49,614 --> 12:37:54,564
Select star against a columnar format reads every column of every matching partition,

7601
12:37:55,214 --> 12:37:58,308
which throws away the entire reason for using parquet.

7602
12:37:59,246 --> 12:38:08,512
Identical dashboard queries repeated every 10 minutes against data refreshed once a day means the overwhelming majority of those runs could return a cached result

7603
12:38:09,230 --> 12:38:10,276
at no charge.

7604
12:38:12,110 --> 12:38:16,228
Option C is the trap that has now appeared three times in this course.

7605
12:38:17,166 --> 12:38:25,604
That is because the words cost and S3 attract each other and storage class has no relationship whatsoever to bytes scanned.

7606
12:38:27,246 --> 12:38:28,740
Question 14 of 20.

7607
12:38:29,230 --> 12:38:30,148
Domain 3.

7608
12:38:30,912 --> 12:38:35,492
A nightly AWS Blue job loads data into Amazon Redshift.

7609
12:38:36,752 --> 12:38:40,996
An operations team has a CloudWatch alarm on the job's failed run count.

7610
12:38:42,272 --> 12:38:44,388
It has worked for 18 months.

7611
12:38:44,832 --> 12:38:50,532
During a recent change, the event bridge rule that triggers the job was disabled.

7612
12:38:51,470 --> 12:38:53,412
The job did not run for three nights.

7613
12:38:53,792 --> 12:38:59,012
No alarm fired, and the problem was found when a business user noticed stale figures.

7614
12:39:02,478 --> 12:39:04,516
What change would have detected this?

7615
12:39:05,521 --> 12:39:05,681
a.

7616
12:39:05,902 --> 12:39:09,700
Lower the failure alarm threshold to fire on a single failed run.

7617
12:39:10,272 --> 12:39:14,948
b Enable AWS Cloud Trail so the rule change is recorded.

7618
12:39:17,486 --> 12:39:24,772
Create an alarm on the job's succeeded run count falling below 1 in 24 hours with missing data treated as breaching.

7619
12:39:25,712 --> 12:39:25,952
D.

7620
12:39:26,672 --> 12:39:29,700
Add a notification step at the end of the blue job.

7621
12:39:29,934 --> 12:39:31,076
Pause and decide.

7622
12:39:40,718 --> 12:39:42,032
The answer is B.

7623
12:39:42,912 --> 12:39:46,272
Three of the four options watch for failure in different ways.

7624
12:39:46,446 --> 12:39:49,284
And the scenario opens by telling you nothing failed.

7625
12:39:49,966 --> 12:39:55,556
The missing data treatment is the specific mechanism and it is a setting most people never open.

7626
12:39:56,494 --> 12:40:04,900
An alarm can be told what to do when a metric reports nothing at all. The default is to ignore the absence, which is exactly wrong for a scheduled job.

7627
12:40:05,422 --> 12:40:08,740
Setting it to breaching means an absent metric raises the alarm.

7628
12:40:09,550 --> 12:40:15,392
Option D is genuinely useful and it is a post-mortem tool rather than a detection.

7629
12:40:16,174 --> 12:40:16,612
1.

7630
12:40:18,126 --> 12:40:19,684
Question 15 of 20.

7631
12:40:19,918 --> 12:40:20,836
Domain 3.

7632
12:40:21,152 --> 12:40:31,712
A blue pipeline joins transactions to a merchant reference table before loading a warehouse. Finance reports that monthly revenue is consistently around half a percent below the source system.

7633
12:40:32,302 --> 12:40:37,700
The job succeeds every night and row counts between the job's output and the warehouse match exactly.

7634
12:40:38,112 --> 12:40:41,156
New merchant codes are added to the source system regularly.

7635
12:40:42,254 --> 12:40:45,476
What is the most likely cause and which check would detect it?

7636
12:40:46,222 --> 12:40:46,432
A

7637
12:40:47,470 --> 12:40:52,036
The row count check is sufficient, so the source system figure must be wrong.

7638
12:40:52,750 --> 12:40:52,992
b.

7639
12:40:53,582 --> 12:40:56,484
The job is running out of memory and dropping records.

7640
12:40:57,806 --> 12:41:00,164
A CloudWatch memory alarm would detect it.

7641
12:41:01,472 --> 12:41:01,712
C.

7642
12:41:02,382 --> 12:41:04,804
Redshift compression is losing precision.

7643
12:41:05,454 --> 12:41:07,652
A checksum comparison would detect it.

7644
12:41:08,672 --> 12:41:08,992
D

7645
12:41:10,352 --> 12:41:16,132
An inner join is dropping transactions whose merchant code is missing from the reference table.

7646
12:41:16,512 --> 12:41:19,152
A referential integrity rule would detect it.

7647
12:41:19,472 --> 12:41:20,900
Pause and decide.

7648
12:41:30,768 --> 12:41:32,368
The answer is A.

7649
12:41:33,614 --> 12:41:40,516
The count check passing is the detail that makes this question hard, and the explanation is worth carrying beyond it.

7650
12:41:41,294 --> 12:41:44,644
The comparison was between the job's output and the warehouse.

7651
12:41:45,582 --> 12:41:51,652
Both of those are downstream of the join, so rows the join discarded were never counted on either side.

7652
12:41:51,888 --> 12:41:59,076
That is why a row count check is considerably weaker than it looks and why comparing an aggregate against the source system is stronger.

7653
12:41:59,982 --> 12:42:05,732
Referential integrity is the DQDL rule type, naming this precise failure.

7654
12:42:06,208 --> 12:42:11,044
It asserts that every value in a column exists in a reference data set.

7655
12:42:11,662 --> 12:42:14,948
It would have caught the first unmatched merchant code.

7656
12:42:16,208 --> 12:42:18,212
Question 16 of 20.

7657
12:42:18,798 --> 12:42:19,652
Domain 3.

7658
12:42:20,270 --> 12:42:26,084
An Amazon Redshift cluster serves dashboards for 200 analysts each weekday morning.

7659
12:42:26,848 --> 12:42:30,404
Users report slowness during the busiest hour.

7660
12:42:30,894 --> 12:42:39,908
Query history shows execution time steady at around 1.5 seconds and queue wait times of 3 to 5 minutes during that hour.

7661
12:42:41,518 --> 12:42:45,348
An overnight transformation job sometimes overruns into the morning.

7662
12:42:46,510 --> 12:42:48,772
Which combination best addresses this?

7663
12:42:52,078 --> 12:42:59,008
Enable concurrency scaling on the dashboard queue and set WLM query priority above the transformation workload.

7664
12:42:59,248 --> 12:43:02,884
b. Add sort and distribution keys to the dashboard tables.

7665
12:43:03,598 --> 12:43:07,620
c. Run vacuum and analyze every morning before business hours.

7666
12:43:08,110 --> 12:43:11,044
D. Resize the cluster to double the node count.

7667
12:43:11,662 --> 12:43:12,836
Pause and decide.

7668
12:43:22,222 --> 12:43:23,268
The answer is B.

7669
12:43:23,568 --> 12:43:30,532
The question quotes both numbers deliberately, which is the exam being fair rather than tricky, and separating them is the whole skill.

7670
12:43:31,008 --> 12:43:36,868
Execution Study at one and a half seconds means the tables, the keys, and the query plans are all healthy.

7671
12:43:37,088 --> 12:43:39,268
So nothing about table design can help.

7672
12:43:39,950 --> 12:43:47,140
Q Wait of 3 to 5 minutes means the problem is entirely contention for a slot during one busy hour.

7673
12:43:48,878 --> 12:43:52,208
Options A and C are both genuinely good.

7674
12:43:52,558 --> 12:43:55,620
Redshift practice offered where they cannot possibly help.

7675
12:43:56,494 --> 12:44:04,452
Option D buys permanent capacity for an event lasting an hour a day while leaving the transformation job competing for it.

7676
12:44:06,158 --> 12:44:07,648
Question 17 of 20.

7677
12:44:07,888 --> 12:44:08,868
Domain 4.

7678
12:44:09,168 --> 12:44:14,212
A company shares a curated dataset in Amazon S3 with a partner's AWS account.

7679
12:44:14,448 --> 12:44:21,476
The bucket policy grants the partner's role, S3 getObject, and the partner's role policy allows the same action.

7680
12:44:22,574 --> 12:44:29,028
Objects are encrypted with SSC KMS using a customer managed key. The partner receives access denied errors.

7681
12:44:29,870 --> 12:44:34,468
Love trail shows get object succeeding and a subsequent KMS decrypt call being denied.

7682
12:44:35,150 --> 12:44:39,780
What is missing? A. The KMS key policy does not permit the partner's role to use the key.

7683
12:44:40,558 --> 12:44:40,848
B.

7684
12:44:41,902 --> 12:44:46,608
The objects must be re-encrypted with SSE S3 for cross account access.

7685
12:44:46,928 --> 12:44:47,168
C.

7686
12:44:47,630 --> 12:44:51,492
S3 bucket keys must be enabled before cross account decryption works.

7687
12:44:51,888 --> 12:44:52,208
D.

7688
12:44:52,528 --> 12:44:57,028
The partner must be added as an IAM user in the owning account.

7689
12:44:58,862 --> 12:44:59,908
Pause and decide.

7690
12:45:09,614 --> 12:45:10,848
The answer is A.

7691
12:45:11,568 --> 12:45:18,788
Cross account access to encrypted objects requires three separate permissions to line up, and the question has already given you two of them.

7692
12:45:19,694 --> 12:45:25,248
The bucket policy allows the partner's role, and the role's own policy allows the action.

7693
12:45:25,806 --> 12:45:30,564
The third is the KMS key policy allowing that role to decrypt.

7694
12:45:31,182 --> 12:45:36,688
It is the one people forget because encryption is normally invisible until something is denied.

7695
12:45:37,321 --> 12:45:43,876
CloudTrail separating the two calls is the diagnostic detail and it is exactly how you would find this in practice.

7696
12:45:44,750 --> 12:45:49,412
Option B is worth rejecting on principle rather than on mechanics.

7697
12:45:49,808 --> 12:45:57,540
That is because removing a security control to make an access problem disappear abandons the requirement that put the control there.

7698
12:45:59,118 --> 12:46:02,052
Question 18 of 20. Domain 4.

7699
12:46:02,478 --> 12:46:09,508
A bank stores customer records in Amazon S3, catalogued in AWS view, and queried with Amazon Athena.

7700
12:46:10,062 --> 12:46:16,388
A modeling team must analyze its transaction behavior but must never see customer name, address, or account number.

7701
12:46:17,168 --> 12:46:25,124
A data engineer proposes writing an IAM policy for the modeling team's role that denies access to those three columns.

7702
12:46:25,648 --> 12:46:27,268
Why will this not work?

7703
12:46:27,630 --> 12:46:28,928
And what should be done?

7704
12:46:29,262 --> 12:46:32,676
a Encrypt the three columns with a KMS.

7705
12:46:33,248 --> 12:46:35,396
Key the modeling team cannot use.

7706
12:46:35,822 --> 12:46:40,356
b IAAM cannot express column level permissions.

7707
12:46:41,262 --> 12:46:45,476
Use AWS Lake Formation column level grants or a data filter.

7708
12:46:45,888 --> 12:46:49,444
C. Create a redacted copy of the table for the modeling team.

7709
12:46:50,126 --> 12:46:54,820
D. The IAAM policy must be attached to the bucket rather than the role.

7710
12:46:55,310 --> 12:46:56,324
Pause and decide.

7711
12:47:06,094 --> 12:47:11,332
The answer is A, and this is the fastest elimination available anywhere in domain.

7712
12:47:12,014 --> 12:47:17,700
4. An IAM policy grants actions on resources identified by ARN.

7713
12:47:18,670 --> 12:47:20,484
A column has no ARN,

7714
12:47:21,166 --> 12:47:23,808
so there is simply nothing for a deny statement to

7715
12:47:23,808 --> 12:47:30,948
name. That makes the proposal impossible rather than merely inferior, which is a much quicker judgment to make.

7716
12:47:32,654 --> 12:47:42,128
Option C deserves respect because organizations genuinely do maintain redacted copies, and it is precisely the duplication that fine-grained access control

7717
12:47:42,288 --> 12:47:43,972
was built to eliminate.

7718
12:47:44,782 --> 12:47:52,292
Two copies means two pipelines, two sets of permissions, and the certainty that one day they will disagree about something that matters.

7719
12:47:53,550 --> 12:47:55,248
Question 19 of 20.

7720
12:47:55,488 --> 12:47:56,388
Domain 4.

7721
12:47:56,974 --> 12:48:00,228
An AWS Blue Job connects to a third-party analytics.

7722
12:48:02,030 --> 12:48:04,292
API using an API key.

7723
12:48:04,910 --> 12:48:07,460
To Amazon Redshift using a database password.

7724
12:48:07,694 --> 12:48:10,468
Both are currently stored as blue job parameters.

7725
12:48:11,342 --> 12:48:21,444
A security review requires that both credentials are stored outside the job definition and that the Redshift password is rotated automatically every 30 days.

7726
12:48:21,998 --> 12:48:24,260
What should the data engineer implement?

7727
12:48:24,750 --> 12:48:27,620
a store both in Systems Manager,

7728
12:48:28,302 --> 12:48:30,948
parameter store as secure string parameters.

7729
12:48:32,208 --> 12:48:32,528
B

7730
12:48:33,646 --> 12:48:39,408
Store both in AWS Secrets Manager with rotation enabled on the Redshift secret.

7731
12:48:39,888 --> 12:48:47,460
C. Store the API key in parameter, store and the redshift password in secrets manager with rotation enabled.

7732
12:48:48,048 --> 12:48:48,368
D.

7733
12:48:48,974 --> 12:48:53,060
Attach an IAM role to the glue job that covers both credentials.

7734
12:48:53,742 --> 12:48:54,852
Pause and decide.

7735
12:49:04,558 --> 12:49:06,176
The answer is A.

7736
12:49:06,766 --> 12:49:07,616
Two constraints.

7737
12:49:07,776 --> 12:49:09,604
Each remove an option cleanly.

7738
12:49:09,870 --> 12:49:17,412
Automatic rotation eliminates parameter store, which has no such capability, so B fails on the second requirement.

7739
12:49:17,870 --> 12:49:23,556
A third party, AP API key, eliminates an IAM role.

7740
12:49:24,430 --> 12:49:30,820
That is because a role authenticates to AWS and the analytics provider has never heard of

7741
12:49:33,134 --> 12:49:34,340
IAAM.

7742
12:49:34,816 --> 12:49:37,732
So D fails on the first credential.

7743
12:49:39,502 --> 12:49:40,016
Option

7744
12:49:41,486 --> 12:49:48,932
C is not wrong exactly and it splits two secrets across two services for no reason, adding a store to manage.

7745
12:49:49,870 --> 12:49:55,172
Both values here are credentials, and secrets manager is where credentials belong.

7746
12:49:56,718 --> 12:49:58,468
Question 20 of 20.

7747
12:49:59,054 --> 12:50:00,004
Domain 4.

7748
12:50:01,102 --> 12:50:04,804
A healthcare provider must keep all patient data within one country.

7749
12:50:05,710 --> 12:50:10,756
The data lake and warehouse are deployed in the required region and access controls are correct.

7750
12:50:11,662 --> 12:50:16,996
An audit finds that redshift snapshots are copied to a second region for disaster recovery.

7751
12:50:17,934 --> 12:50:24,068
That an Athena work group writes query results to a bucket in a third region created several years ago.

7752
12:50:24,686 --> 12:50:26,308
What should the data engineer do?

7753
12:50:26,958 --> 12:50:32,356
a encrypt the snapshots and query results so their location is no longer material.

7754
12:50:33,038 --> 12:50:37,284
b apply lake formation row level filters to the affected tables.

7755
12:50:38,176 --> 12:50:38,416
C

7756
12:50:39,696 --> 12:50:44,324
Delete the disaster recovery snapshots and accept the reduced resilience.

7757
12:50:44,750 --> 12:50:45,056
D.

7758
12:50:45,536 --> 12:50:55,620
Relocate the snapshot copies and the Athena results location into the required region and apply a service control policy restricting which regions may be used.

7759
12:50:56,686 --> 12:50:57,764
Pause and decide.

7760
12:51:07,502 --> 12:51:08,816
The answer is B.

7761
12:51:09,614 --> 12:51:16,740
Snapshots and query results are both copies of regulated data created by systems behaving entirely reasonably.

7762
12:51:18,126 --> 12:51:22,500
Each escaped a boundary that had been correctly applied to the primary data set.

7763
12:51:23,310 --> 12:51:31,204
That is the pattern to carry from this question because sovereignty programs fail on derived artifacts rather than on the data everybody is watching.

7764
12:51:31,776 --> 12:51:36,708
Option A is tempting because encryption feels like it should help with a compliance requirement.

7765
12:51:37,518 --> 12:51:41,540
Residency is a question about physical location rather than about readability.

7766
12:51:41,776 --> 12:51:48,644
The second half of B matters as much as the first because relocating two artifacts fixes what this audit found.

7767
12:51:49,136 --> 12:51:54,308
A service control policy restricting regions prevents the third one appearing next year.

7768
12:51:55,616 --> 12:52:02,084
This mapping is here, so a wrong answer turns into a specific action rather than a vague sense of weakness.

7769
12:52:02,336 --> 12:52:04,836
The call out is the interpretation to apply.

7770
12:52:06,158 --> 12:52:12,836
Missing both questions in a single row points at a genuine gap in that material, and the module named is where to go.

7771
12:52:13,742 --> 12:52:18,692
Misses scattered singly across four different rows usually means something else entirely.

7772
12:52:19,456 --> 12:52:24,196
That is that you know the services and are missing constraints or qualifiers when you read.

7773
12:52:25,070 --> 12:52:34,212
That is a module 22 problem rather than a technical one, and the fix is practicing the reading order rather than revising any service.

7774
12:52:35,918 --> 12:52:40,420
This table is the reason a practice exam is worth reviewing rather than just scoring.

7775
12:52:40,816 --> 12:52:46,884
Every wrong option in these 20 questions was built using one of the patterns module 22 named.

7776
12:52:47,296 --> 12:52:50,308
Seeing where each appeared makes the patterns concrete.

7777
12:52:50,638 --> 12:52:53,220
The call out gives the one to watch hardest.

7778
12:52:54,094 --> 12:53:05,380
Adding capacity was offered as a wrong answer four times here. Once each for a type conflict, a CPU bound function, a distribution problem, and a queuing problem.

7779
12:53:05,776 --> 12:53:07,620
It was correct in none of them.

7780
12:53:08,462 --> 12:53:15,268
If you chose a capacity option more than once, that is the single most valuable thing this exam has told you.

7781
12:53:16,864 --> 12:53:21,184
The callout is the whole method for using a practice exam properly.

7782
12:53:21,454 --> 12:53:26,756
A missed fact is fixed by rereading one slide, and it is the cheapest kind of gap.

7783
12:53:27,502 --> 12:53:34,500
A missed constraint is a reading habit fixed by practicing the order from module 22 on the next 20 questions.

7784
12:53:35,406 --> 12:53:38,244
Choosing the option you would build in production is the hardest.

7785
12:53:39,022 --> 12:53:45,412
That is because your experience is telling you something true about engineering and something unhelpful about this exam.

7786
12:53:46,286 --> 12:53:51,844
If two or three of your misses share a category, that category is what to work on.

7787
12:53:52,782 --> 12:53:56,100
It will be worth more than revising a whole domain.

7788
12:53:57,710 --> 12:54:00,068
The last bullet is worth stating plainly.

7789
12:54:01,051 --> 12:54:06,224
20 questions written for a course cannot be calibrated against a professionally developed exam.

7790
12:54:06,464 --> 12:54:11,716
Anybody claiming a precise mapping between a practice score and a scale score is guessing.

7791
12:54:12,464 --> 12:54:15,716
What these are genuinely useful for is diagnosis.

7792
12:54:16,686 --> 12:54:21,252
If you missed both domain 4 questions on encryption, module 19 is where to go.

7793
12:54:21,518 --> 12:54:28,356
If you chose capacity in two separate questions, module 21's failure families, table is the thing to reread.

7794
12:54:28,624 --> 12:54:33,380
Two more practice exams follow, each with 20 different questions across the same weighting.

7795
12:54:35,022 --> 12:54:39,588
20 more questions weighted the same way as the first set and as the real exam.

7796
12:54:39,982 --> 12:54:45,188
7 from domain 1, 5 from domain 2, 4 each from domains 3 and 4.

7797
12:54:45,904 --> 12:54:52,356
None of these repeats a question from practice exam 1 and they deliberately cover different ground within the same domains.

7798
12:54:53,262 --> 12:54:56,676
So a service that appeared there in one form appears here in another.

7799
12:54:57,358 --> 12:54:59,504
2 questions are multiple response.

7800
12:55:00,014 --> 12:55:07,012
As before, every option carries a reason, including the correct ones, because the review is where the learning happens.

7801
12:55:08,590 --> 12:55:14,020
The call out is why taking a second practice exam is worth more than taking the first one twice.

7802
12:55:14,384 --> 12:55:19,748
One wrong answer tells you very little because everybody misreads a sentence occasionally.

7803
12:55:20,590 --> 12:55:28,548
The same category of mistake appearing in both sets is a genuine finding about how you read under pressure and it is actionable.

7804
12:55:28,944 --> 12:55:36,516
If you chose a capacity option in exam 1 and choose one again here, that is the thing to work on rather than any particular service.

7805
12:55:37,614 --> 12:55:43,620
The fourth bullet is a small discipline that occasionally reveals something surprising about where your eye goes.

7806
12:55:45,230 --> 12:55:50,884
The third bullet is the design principle behind this set and it is worth knowing as you work through it.

7807
12:55:53,006 --> 12:56:02,532
AWS Glue appears in three questions here. Once about reprocessing history, once about a hard time limit, and once about how a crawler infers a schema.

7808
12:56:03,310 --> 12:56:06,052
Knowing what Glue is answers, none of them.

7809
12:56:06,926 --> 12:56:08,864
That reflects the real exam.

7810
12:56:09,104 --> 12:56:18,116
The same handful of services carry most of the questions, and each one turns on a specific behavior rather than on recognizing the service name.

7811
12:56:18,926 --> 12:56:25,572
If you find yourself thinking that you already know this service, that is usually the moment to read the scenario more carefully.

7812
12:56:27,150 --> 12:56:30,276
Question 1 of print Domain 1.

7813
12:56:30,894 --> 12:56:36,100
A payments company streams card transactions into Amazon Kinesis data streams.

7814
12:56:38,926 --> 12:56:48,388
A new requirement is to detect within seconds when a single card is used more than 5 times in any 2-minute window to publish those cards to a downstream queue.

7815
12:56:49,230 --> 12:56:53,220
The team wants to avoid building and operating stream processing infrastructure.

7816
12:56:53,504 --> 12:56:55,460
What should the data engineer use?

7817
12:56:56,782 --> 12:57:01,572
a Amazon Managed Service for Apache, Flink with a 2-minute tumbling window.

7818
12:57:01,998 --> 12:57:09,508
B Amazon Data Firehose delivering to Amazon S3 with a scheduled glue job checking for the pattern.

7819
12:57:10,384 --> 12:57:10,544
C

7820
12:57:11,662 --> 12:57:16,784
An AWS Lambda consumer that counts transactions per card in memory.

7821
12:57:17,166 --> 12:57:21,184
B Amazon Athena querying the raw stream data every minute.

7822
12:57:21,454 --> 12:57:22,704
Pause and decide.

7823
12:57:32,272 --> 12:57:33,668
The answer is B.

7824
12:57:34,062 --> 12:57:41,572
The deciding phrase is a 2 minute window because that is a statement about aggregating across time rather than about moving data.

7825
12:57:43,072 --> 12:57:49,380
Firehose delivers, and this requirement needs something to happen between the producer and the destination.

7826
12:57:50,222 --> 12:57:57,284
Option C is the one worth understanding properly because it looks like a lightweight answer and it fains on a fundamental point.

7827
12:57:57,838 --> 12:58:03,620
A lambda function processes a batch of records and exits, holding no state between invocations.

7828
12:58:04,590 --> 12:58:10,180
So counting occurrences of a card across two minutes is not something it can do reliably.

7829
12:58:11,790 --> 12:58:13,152
Managed service for Apache.

7830
12:58:13,326 --> 12:58:17,924
Flink handles the windowing and AWS operates it, which answers the last sentence.

7831
12:58:19,566 --> 12:58:20,992
Question 2 of 20.

7832
12:58:21,232 --> 12:58:22,192
Domain 1.

7833
12:58:22,432 --> 12:58:29,252
An AWS BlueJob reads a growing Amazon S3 prefix every night and writes aggregates to a curated table.

7834
12:58:29,792 --> 12:58:38,180
Input volume has grown from 200 GB to 4TB over 18 months, and the job's runtime and cost have grown proportionally.

7835
12:58:39,086 --> 12:58:42,212
Files are only ever added to the prefix.

7836
12:58:42,592 --> 12:58:44,932
Existing files are never modified.

7837
12:58:45,232 --> 12:58:48,388
Each run reprocesses the entire prefix.

7838
12:58:49,582 --> 12:58:51,236
What should the data engineer do?

7839
12:58:52,270 --> 12:58:56,868
a Increase the number of workers so the job completes in the original time.

7840
12:58:58,990 --> 12:59:04,164
Enable job bookmarks so each run processes only files added since the last run.

7841
12:59:04,686 --> 12:59:04,992
C.

7842
12:59:05,312 --> 12:59:07,524
Switch the job to BlueFlex execution.

7843
12:59:07,952 --> 12:59:08,272
D.

7844
12:59:08,654 --> 12:59:10,512
Partition the output table by date.

7845
12:59:10,702 --> 12:59:12,032
Pause and decide.

7846
12:59:21,390 --> 12:59:30,032
The answer is B. The diagnostic detail is that cost scales with the total size of the prefix rather than with the amount of new data arriving each night.

7847
12:59:30,286 --> 12:59:34,468
That means the job is reading history. It has already processed many times over.

7848
12:59:34,926 --> 12:59:39,940
Bookmarks track what has been consumed, so each run picks up only the new files.

7849
12:59:40,112 --> 12:59:43,908
The question also gives you the two conditions that make bookmarks safe to use.

7850
12:59:44,272 --> 12:59:48,804
That are that files are only ever added and existing files are never modified.

7851
12:59:49,614 --> 12:59:54,180
Option C is worth noting because Flex is a genuine cost lever.

7852
12:59:55,598 --> 12:59:59,236
It reduces the price of work that should not be happening at all.

7853
13:00:00,622 --> 13:00:02,020
Question 3 of 20.

7854
13:00:02,222 --> 13:00:03,172
Domain 1.

7855
13:00:03,790 --> 13:00:08,644
A company runs an Amazon EMR cluster on EC2 continuously.

7856
13:00:09,390 --> 13:00:13,924
It executes a 4-hour Apache hive job each night and is otherwise idle.

7857
13:00:14,670 --> 13:00:21,444
Intermediate data is written to HDFS on the core nodes and the team has a large body of existing hive scripts.

7858
13:00:22,352 --> 13:00:28,068
Finance wants the cost reduced. The job must still finish overnight and the scripts must not be rewritten.

7859
13:00:28,590 --> 13:00:30,592
Which combination reduces cost the most?

7860
13:00:30,832 --> 13:00:35,652
a migrate the hive scripts to AWS Glue and run them as Spark jobs.

7861
13:00:36,032 --> 13:00:40,164
B. Move all core nodes to spot instances and keep the cluster running.

7862
13:00:42,272 --> 13:00:42,512
C

7863
13:00:43,726 --> 13:00:47,460
Halve the cluster size and accept a longer runtime.

7864
13:00:48,992 --> 13:00:49,312
D.

7865
13:00:49,742 --> 13:00:53,796
Store data in Amazon S3 with EMRFS.

7866
13:00:54,318 --> 13:00:58,916
Run a transient cluster nightly and use spot instances for task nodes.

7867
13:01:01,006 --> 13:01:02,340
Pause and decide.

7868
13:01:12,000 --> 13:01:13,280
The answer is B.

7869
13:01:13,550 --> 13:01:15,396
Two eliminations happen instantly.

7870
13:01:15,680 --> 13:01:22,500
Hive removes option A because Blue runs Spark and Python and nothing else, and the question separately forbids rewriting.

7871
13:01:22,960 --> 13:01:26,500
Core nodes holding HDFS removes option.

7872
13:01:27,200 --> 13:01:27,360
C.

7873
13:01:28,718 --> 13:01:35,684
That is because spot instances can be reclaimed with two minutes, notice and taking HDFS blocks with them mid-job.

7874
13:01:36,174 --> 13:01:38,756
Between B and D, the arithmetic decides.

7875
13:01:39,200 --> 13:01:46,884
A cluster running 24 hours for 4 hours of work is idle, 5 sixths of its life, and no amount of resizing addresses that.

7876
13:01:47,790 --> 13:01:53,860
Moving the data to S3 is what makes a transient cluster possible at all.

7877
13:01:55,118 --> 13:01:56,452
Question 4 of 20.

7878
13:01:56,750 --> 13:01:57,680
Domain 1.

7879
13:01:57,920 --> 13:02:02,500
A pipeline converts uploaded video metadata files into a normalized format.

7880
13:02:02,800 --> 13:02:10,372
Each file takes between 8 and 40 minutes to process depending on size, and processing is single-threaded and cannot be distributed.

7881
13:02:10,958 --> 13:02:14,564
About 60 files arrive each day at unpredictable times.

7882
13:02:14,880 --> 13:02:19,971
The current implementation is an AWS Lambda function and the longer files now fail.

7883
13:02:20,494 --> 13:02:22,308
What should the data engineer use?

7884
13:02:22,800 --> 13:02:26,308
a increase the lambda function's timeout and memory allocation.

7885
13:02:26,800 --> 13:02:30,980
b an Amazon EMR cluster with a step per file.

7886
13:02:31,840 --> 13:02:32,080
C

7887
13:02:33,422 --> 13:02:37,348
AWS batch running each file as a containerized job.

7888
13:02:37,806 --> 13:02:38,080
D.

7889
13:02:38,542 --> 13:02:41,732
An AWS blues path job triggered per file.

7890
13:02:42,318 --> 13:02:43,396
Pause and decide.

7891
13:02:52,846 --> 13:02:56,132
The answer is B, and the number in the question does the work.

7892
13:02:57,102 --> 13:03:03,204
40 minutes exceeds Lambda's 15 minute maximum, which is a hard limit that no support request can raise.

7893
13:03:04,078 --> 13:03:08,080
So option A is impossible rather than merely inadequate.

7894
13:03:08,590 --> 13:03:15,140
The second detail is that the processing is single-threaded and cannot be distributed, which removes both Spark options.

7895
13:03:15,502 --> 13:03:21,440
That is because distributing work that cannot be divided gains nothing and adds startup time to every file.

7896
13:03:21,838 --> 13:03:24,164
AWS batch fills exactly this gap.

7897
13:03:25,070 --> 13:03:34,116
Longer than Lambda allows, simpler than Spark requires, and it provisions compute when jobs are queued, then shuts down when the queue empties.

7898
13:03:35,854 --> 13:03:37,124
Question 5 of 20.

7899
13:03:37,678 --> 13:03:38,436
Domain 1.

7900
13:03:39,566 --> 13:03:49,120
A company needs to ingest opportunity and account records from Salesforce into Amazon S3 daily with field mapping and light filtering applied.

7901
13:03:49,710 --> 13:03:59,108
A developer has proposed writing a lambda function that calls the Salesforce REST API handles pagination, manages or token refresh and retrace.

7902
13:04:00,334 --> 13:04:02,276
Writes JSON to Amazon S3.

7903
13:04:03,022 --> 13:04:05,732
The team wants to minimize the code they build and maintain.

7904
13:04:07,214 --> 13:04:09,252
What should the data engineer recommend?

7905
13:04:11,246 --> 13:04:16,356
Amazon App Flow with the scheduled flow performing the mapping and filtering.

7906
13:04:16,800 --> 13:04:22,436
B Build the Lambda function as proposed with EventBridge Scheduler triggering it daily.

7907
13:04:23,040 --> 13:04:23,360
C.

7908
13:04:23,918 --> 13:04:28,356
AWSDMS with Salesforce as a source endpoint.

7909
13:04:29,006 --> 13:04:33,572
D AWS Data Sync to transfer the Salesforce data.

7910
13:04:34,670 --> 13:04:35,908
Pause and decide.

7911
13:04:45,616 --> 13:04:52,452
The answer is B, and the question is constructed so that the wrong option describes the work, the right option removes.

7912
13:04:56,558 --> 13:05:01,252
Pagination, O auth, token, refresh, retries, and handling.

7913
13:05:01,856 --> 13:05:08,036
API changes are all real engineering and all of them are APRO's job rather than yours.

7914
13:05:08,846 --> 13:05:13,536
A scenario may spend a sentence listing plumbing somebody would have to write.

7915
13:05:13,742 --> 13:05:18,660
That list usually points at a managed alternative rather than justifying the build.

7916
13:05:19,182 --> 13:05:22,372
This is the same shape as several questions in module 4.

7917
13:05:23,278 --> 13:05:28,804
The qualifier about minimizing the code they maintain confirms the reading rather than introducing it.

7918
13:05:29,781 --> 13:05:32,932
Options C and D both fail on what Salesforce is.

7919
13:05:33,326 --> 13:05:38,916
That is because it is a SAAS application rather than a database or a file system.

7920
13:05:40,622 --> 13:05:42,532
Question 6 of 20.

7921
13:05:42,862 --> 13:05:43,748
Domain 1.

7922
13:05:44,016 --> 13:05:46,096
This question asks for two answers.

7923
13:05:46,336 --> 13:05:53,572
For AWS blue jobs run nightly, each triggered by its own cron schedule spaced 90 minutes apart.

7924
13:05:55,376 --> 13:06:03,108
Job 2 occasionally overruns, and when it does, job 3 starts against incomplete data and produces incorrect aggregates.

7925
13:06:04,014 --> 13:06:05,412
No error is raised.

7926
13:06:05,616 --> 13:06:11,136
The team wants the pipeline to be dependency driven and to be alerted when any step fails.

7927
13:06:11,566 --> 13:06:14,496
Select two actions the data engineer should take.

7928
13:06:14,926 --> 13:06:21,220
a replace the cron schedules with an AWS step function state machine invoking each job in sequence.

7929
13:06:23,086 --> 13:06:23,296
B

7930
13:06:24,430 --> 13:06:27,652
Widen the gaps between the cron schedules to three hours.

7931
13:06:28,096 --> 13:06:28,336
C.

7932
13:06:28,736 --> 13:06:33,536
Add a catch on each state routing failures to an SNS notification.

7933
13:06:33,856 --> 13:06:34,176
B.

7934
13:06:34,416 --> 13:06:37,536
Set each brew job's maximum concurrent runs to one.

7935
13:06:38,126 --> 13:06:39,268
Pause and decide.

7936
13:06:48,910 --> 13:06:50,980
The answers are A and C.

7937
13:07:25,216 --> 13:07:34,372
Option B is the instructive wrong answer because widening the cron gaps is precisely what teams do when this happens in production.

7938
13:07:34,976 --> 13:07:42,852
It is timing-based coordination, pretending to be dependency-based. It fails the first time something runs longer than the new guess.

7939
13:07:43,790 --> 13:07:46,276
It delays every night's completion in the meantime.

7940
13:07:46,606 --> 13:07:54,276
Option D addresses a genuinely different problem, which is one job overlapping with itself rather than one job overtaking another that is still running.

7941
13:07:55,854 --> 13:07:57,156
Question 7 of 20.

7942
13:07:57,774 --> 13:07:58,532
Domain 1.

7943
13:07:59,406 --> 13:08:06,084
A retailer lights order events to Amazon Kinesis data streams using store ID as the partition key.

7944
13:08:06,766 --> 13:08:12,896
The chain has 400 stores, but one flagship store generates around 30% of all events.

7945
13:08:13,358 --> 13:08:19,456
Producers receive throttling errors during peak hours while total stream utilization is around 40%.

7946
13:08:20,302 --> 13:08:24,004
The team has already increased the shard count twice without improvement.

7947
13:08:24,430 --> 13:08:26,212
What should the data engineer do?

7948
13:08:27,086 --> 13:08:29,764
a Increase the shard count again.

7949
13:08:31,054 --> 13:08:31,296
B.

7950
13:08:32,558 --> 13:08:35,524
Switch the stream to on demand capacity mode.

7951
13:08:35,936 --> 13:08:36,256
C.

7952
13:08:36,846 --> 13:08:42,404
Use a composite partition key combining store ID with a random suffix.

7953
13:08:42,736 --> 13:08:42,976
D.

7954
13:08:43,470 --> 13:08:46,180
Enable enhanced fan out for the consumers.

7955
13:08:48,558 --> 13:08:49,700
Pause and decide.

7956
13:08:59,406 --> 13:09:00,388
The answer is B.

7957
13:09:01,550 --> 13:09:03,684
Two details together are diagnostic here.

7958
13:09:04,526 --> 13:09:11,492
Throttling while overall utilization sits at around 40% means the load is concentrated rather than excessive.

7959
13:09:12,398 --> 13:09:21,156
True failed capacity increases, conform it, because adding shards does not change which shard a given partition key hashes to.

7960
13:09:21,838 --> 13:09:30,560
This is module 21's QRO appearing as a kinesis hot shard, and the same shape appears in Spark, Redshift, and Dynamo

7961
13:09:30,720 --> 13:09:30,980
DB.

7962
13:09:32,078 --> 13:09:40,516
Sorting the key with a random suffix spreads one store's traffic across several shards and consumers reassemble by store afterwards.

7963
13:09:42,702 --> 13:09:52,452
Option C is worth understanding because on-demand genuinely removes shard management while distributing by partition key exactly as provision mode does.

7964
13:09:53,678 --> 13:09:55,204
Question 8 of 20.

7965
13:09:55,630 --> 13:09:56,388
Domain 2.

7966
13:09:57,998 --> 13:10:07,364
A subscription business stores customer interaction records as parked in Amazon S3, partition by month, and queries them with Amazon Athena.

7967
13:10:08,238 --> 13:10:16,932
Under privacy legislation, it must erase all records for a customer within 30 days of a request and receives around 60 such requests a month.

7968
13:10:18,126 --> 13:10:24,516
Currently, an engineer identifies affected files, rewrites each without the customer's rows, and replaces them.

7969
13:10:25,454 --> 13:10:28,452
This takes days and has price corrupted a partition.

7970
13:10:28,960 --> 13:10:30,916
What should the company implement?

7971
13:10:31,470 --> 13:10:36,196
a convert the dataset to Apache IZBERG tables and use delete statements.

7972
13:10:36,686 --> 13:10:41,028
b Enable S3 versioning so a failed rewrite can be rolled back.

7973
13:10:41,774 --> 13:10:42,000
C

7974
13:10:43,278 --> 13:10:47,652
Repartition the data by customer identifier so whole files can be deleted.

7975
13:10:48,080 --> 13:10:48,320
d.

7976
13:10:48,878 --> 13:10:50,400
Apply S3 object.

7977
13:10:50,606 --> 13:10:53,380
Lock so files cannot be corrupted during rewriting.

7978
13:10:53,966 --> 13:10:55,280
Pause and decide.

7979
13:11:04,654 --> 13:11:08,292
The answer is B and both stated harms are addressed by the same change.

7980
13:11:09,518 --> 13:11:13,732
Deletion becomes a single SQL statement rather than days of manual file surgery.

7981
13:11:15,022 --> 13:11:21,636
Because an iceberg write is atomic, a failure part way through leaves the table exactly as it was rather than corrupting a partition.

7982
13:11:22,542 --> 13:11:30,400
Option A is the overpartitioning trap from module 8, creating thousands of tiny partitions for a filter nobody uses,

7983
13:11:30,560 --> 13:11:32,868
since queries here filter by month.

7984
13:11:34,702 --> 13:11:39,652
Option D is worth naming because it is actively harmful rather than merely unhelpful.

7985
13:11:40,560 --> 13:11:46,436
It applies an immutability control to data subject to a legal deletion obligation.

7986
13:11:47,982 --> 13:11:49,604
Question 9 of 20.

7987
13:11:50,286 --> 13:11:51,268
Domain 2.

7988
13:11:51,598 --> 13:11:57,316
A company keeps 6 years of order history in an Amazon Redshift cluster totaling 90 terabytes.

7989
13:11:57,806 --> 13:12:01,412
The last six months are queried continuously by 300 users.

7990
13:12:02,158 --> 13:12:09,828
Older data is queried a handful of times each year for audits, and those audits must join old and recent data in a single query.

7991
13:12:10,542 --> 13:12:14,052
Manage storage is now the largest line on the Redshift bill.

7992
13:12:14,606 --> 13:12:16,676
What should the data engineer implement?

7993
13:12:17,710 --> 13:12:21,796
a reduce the cluster node count to lower the storage cost.

7994
13:12:24,000 --> 13:12:24,320
b

7995
13:12:25,358 --> 13:12:29,796
Move the older data to a second Redshift cluster and use data sharing.

7996
13:12:30,240 --> 13:12:30,560
C.

7997
13:12:30,862 --> 13:12:37,636
Unload data older than 6 months to Amazon S3 as partitioned, park it, and query it with Redshift Spectrum.

7998
13:12:38,080 --> 13:12:42,980
D. Unload older data to Amazon S3 and have auditors use Amazon Athena.

7999
13:12:43,438 --> 13:12:44,548
Pause and decide.

8000
13:12:53,888 --> 13:12:55,808
The answer is B.

8001
13:12:57,518 --> 13:13:06,436
Option C saves identical money and phase one clause, which is that audits must join old and recent data in a single query.

8002
13:13:07,886 --> 13:13:13,188
Athena and Redshift are separate query engines and no single statement spans them.

8003
13:13:13,966 --> 13:13:16,100
Spectrum exists precisely so.

8004
13:13:16,528 --> 13:13:21,764
An external table over S3 can be joined to a warehouse table in the same query.

8005
13:13:23,502 --> 13:13:27,908
Option A is worth understanding because it reflects a genuine misconception.

8006
13:13:28,846 --> 13:13:33,568
On managed storage, the node count and the volume of data stored are priced independently.

8007
13:13:33,742 --> 13:13:38,468
So resizing a cluster addresses compute cost and leaves storage untouched.

8008
13:13:40,078 --> 13:13:41,412
Question 10 of 20.

8009
13:13:41,774 --> 13:13:42,596
Domain 2.

8010
13:13:42,926 --> 13:13:54,532
A media company runs a self-managed MongoDB cluster on Amazon EC2 holding a content catalog. Around 80 services query it using MongoDB drivers and aggregation pipelines.

8011
13:13:56,846 --> 13:14:00,996
The operations team wants to stop managing replica sets and backups.

8012
13:14:01,902 --> 13:14:06,116
Documents are deeply nested and the schema varies between content types.

8013
13:14:06,368 --> 13:14:08,448
What should the data engineer recommend?

8014
13:14:08,768 --> 13:14:12,528
a Amazon Key Spaces, which is designed for flexible schemas.

8015
13:14:12,848 --> 13:14:17,380
B. Amazon Dynamo DB, which is fully managed and handles document workloads.

8016
13:14:17,968 --> 13:14:18,208
C

8017
13:14:19,342 --> 13:14:23,876
Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL using JSONB columns.

8018
13:14:24,846 --> 13:14:25,088
D.

8019
13:14:25,614 --> 13:14:29,028
Amazon Document DB which is MongoDB compatible.

8020
13:14:29,648 --> 13:14:31,300
Pause and decide.

8021
13:14:41,038 --> 13:14:42,148
The answer is B.

8022
13:14:42,638 --> 13:14:49,668
This is the compatibility rule from module 21, where an existing technology names its compatible service.

8023
13:14:50,446 --> 13:14:56,608
MongoDB names DocumentDB exactly as Cassandra names, keyspaces, and Kafka names.

8024
13:14:57,001 --> 13:15:07,940
Amazon MSK The mention of aggregation pipelines tightens the requirement further because those are a MongoDB specific query construct rather than simple document reads.

8025
13:15:09,038 --> 13:15:12,164
So even a partial compatibility layer would not be enough.

8026
13:15:12,974 --> 13:15:20,068
Options A and C both satisfy the stated operational requirement by creating an AT service migration project.

8027
13:15:20,288 --> 13:15:24,068
That is the unnecessary cost this exam consistently marks wrong.

8028
13:15:24,448 --> 13:15:27,684
Option D fails on which database it is compatible with.

8029
13:15:29,134 --> 13:15:31,044
Question 11 of 20.

8030
13:15:31,568 --> 13:15:32,580
Domain 2.

8031
13:15:32,974 --> 13:15:39,968
A company's Amazon S3 build has grown steadily while the data the team knows about has stayed around 20TB.

8032
13:15:41,294 --> 13:15:45,700
S3 storage lens reports approximately 70 TB of build storage.

8033
13:15:46,574 --> 13:15:48,612
Versioning was enabled three years ago.

8034
13:15:49,454 --> 13:15:52,324
A nightly job overrides a set of aggregate files.

8035
13:15:52,688 --> 13:15:57,316
Several large uploads from an unreliable laplock link have failed over that period.

8036
13:15:58,830 --> 13:16:01,508
Which combination explains the gap and addresses it.

8037
13:16:03,086 --> 13:16:04,996
a Enable S3.

8038
13:16:06,158 --> 13:16:10,244
Intelligent tearings who infrequently access data moves to cheaper tiers.

8039
13:16:10,926 --> 13:16:11,168
B

8040
13:16:12,238 --> 13:16:16,132
Enable cross-region replication and delete the primary copies.

8041
13:16:17,488 --> 13:16:21,412
C Disable versioning to stop old versions accumulating.

8042
13:16:22,608 --> 13:16:29,088
D Add lifecycle rules to expire non current versions and to avoid incomplete multi part uploads.

8043
13:16:29,806 --> 13:16:30,788
Pause and decide.

8044
13:16:40,560 --> 13:16:46,800
The answer is B, and the question maps each detail in the scenario onto one half of the answer.

8045
13:16:47,054 --> 13:16:53,280
The nightly overwrite combined with versioning explains the accumulating non-current versions.

8046
13:16:54,382 --> 13:17:01,284
That is because overwriting an object on a version bucket retains the previous version indefinitely and you pay for it.

8047
13:17:03,790 --> 13:17:09,284
The unreliable link and the failed large uploads explains the incomplete multi-part parts

8048
13:17:09,966 --> 13:17:13,252
that are built as storage and do not appear when you list a bucket.

8049
13:17:14,126 --> 13:17:17,700
Option C is the intuitive response and it is doubly wrong.

8050
13:17:18,414 --> 13:17:27,040
It leaves every existing version in place and it removes the protection the team deliberately enabled after presumably losing something.

8051
13:17:28,334 --> 13:17:29,796
Question 12 of 20.

8052
13:17:30,222 --> 13:17:31,172
Domain 2.

8053
13:17:31,662 --> 13:17:36,388
A crawler has catalogued a set of pipe delimited text files in Amazon S3.

8054
13:17:37,006 --> 13:17:41,220
Analysts querying the resulting table in Amazon Athena find a problem.

8055
13:17:41,680 --> 13:17:47,620
Every row appears in a single column named core 0 containing the entire line as one string.

8056
13:17:48,558 --> 13:17:51,268
The files have a consistent structure with a header row.

8057
13:17:51,822 --> 13:17:53,412
What should the data engineer do?

8058
13:17:54,350 --> 13:17:58,212
a increase the crawler's sample size so it reads more files.

8059
13:17:58,990 --> 13:18:04,036
b. Create a custom classifier defining the pipe delimiter and rerun the crawler.

8060
13:18:04,718 --> 13:18:08,292
C. Set the crawler schema change policy to update the table.

8061
13:18:09,040 --> 13:18:09,280
D.

8062
13:18:10,640 --> 13:18:13,444
Convert the files to parquet before crawling them.

8063
13:18:13,774 --> 13:18:14,948
Pause and decide.

8064
13:18:24,494 --> 13:18:25,600
The answer is B.

8065
13:18:25,840 --> 13:18:29,956
The symptom described is distinctive and worth recognizing instantly.

8066
13:18:30,240 --> 13:18:36,868
A table where every row is a single string column means the crawler could not work out how the file is structured.

8067
13:18:37,454 --> 13:18:44,292
It fell back to treating each line as one value, which is the safest thing it can do when it does not recognize a format.

8068
13:18:44,782 --> 13:18:53,348
Built-in classifiers cover common formats like CSV and JSON, and an unusual delimiter needs a custom classifier defining the pattern.

8069
13:18:54,254 --> 13:18:58,560
Option D is interesting because converting to parquet would indeed solve it.

8070
13:18:58,800 --> 13:19:04,420
It requires something to parse the pipe delimited files first, which is the capability that is missing.

8071
13:19:05,934 --> 13:19:07,440
Question 13 of 20.

8072
13:19:07,726 --> 13:19:15,844
Domain 3 An Athena table over 5 years of parquet data is partitioned by a string column named event underscore date.

8073
13:19:16,400 --> 13:19:23,268
Analysts filter with a function that extracts the date from an event underscore timestamp column instead. Results are correct.

8074
13:19:23,886 --> 13:19:29,380
Queries scan the entire table every time, and the Athena bill is far higher than expected.

8075
13:19:29,760 --> 13:19:30,916
What is the cause?

8076
13:19:31,438 --> 13:19:34,500
a The table needs a blue partition index.

8077
13:19:34,880 --> 13:19:38,468
b The workgroup's data scan limit has been set too high.

8078
13:19:38,990 --> 13:19:43,588
C. The filter is applied to a derived value, so Athena cannot prune partitions.

8079
13:19:44,080 --> 13:19:44,400
D.

8080
13:19:45,321 --> 13:19:48,420
Parquet files are too small and need compaction.

8081
13:19:49,486 --> 13:19:50,820
Pause and decide.

8082
13:20:00,526 --> 13:20:05,123
The answer is B, and this is the most expensive silent mistake available in Athena.

8083
13:20:05,742 --> 13:20:12,068
Filtering on a function applied to a timestamp column is logically correct and produces exactly the right rows.

8084
13:20:12,400 --> 13:20:17,316
The engine has no way to connect that function's output to the partition column it was told about.

8085
13:20:17,934 --> 13:20:23,040
So it reads every partition, discards almost all of it, and charges you for the not.

8086
13:20:23,440 --> 13:20:26,372
The fix is to filter on the partition column directly.

8087
13:20:26,800 --> 13:20:32,900
This is also why module 9's hidden partitioning matters because iceberg records the transform itself.

8088
13:20:33,742 --> 13:20:38,884
So a filter on the source column prunes correctly without the analyst knowing the layout.

8089
13:20:40,494 --> 13:20:41,956
Question 14 of 20.

8090
13:20:42,286 --> 13:20:43,200
Domain 3.

8091
13:20:43,502 --> 13:20:45,120
An Amazon EMR.

8092
13:20:45,454 --> 13:20:49,920
Transient cluster runs a nightly sparp job and terminates on completion.

8093
13:20:50,160 --> 13:20:52,960
Last night the job failed after 90 minutes.

8094
13:20:53,120 --> 13:20:58,596
The cluster terminated automatically and the operations team can see only that the step failed.

8095
13:20:59,438 --> 13:21:05,508
No application logs are available. The team needs to diagnose this and prevent the same blind spot recurring.

8096
13:21:06,958 --> 13:21:08,612
What should the data engineer do?

8097
13:21:09,838 --> 13:21:14,240
a rerun the job on a long-running cluster to reproduce the failure.

8098
13:21:14,560 --> 13:21:21,636
b Configure the cluster to archive logs to Amazon S3 and enable the persistent Spark History server.

8099
13:21:22,160 --> 13:21:22,480
C.

8100
13:21:22,798 --> 13:21:26,372
Enable AWS Cloud Trail to record what the job did.

8101
13:21:26,894 --> 13:21:27,200
D.

8102
13:21:27,520 --> 13:21:30,372
Increase the cluster size so the job does not fail.

8103
13:21:30,926 --> 13:21:31,972
Pause and decide.

8104
13:21:41,422 --> 13:21:42,532
The answer is A.

8105
13:21:43,022 --> 13:21:48,068
Transient clusters are the right cost decision and they carry this specific consequence.

8106
13:21:49,838 --> 13:21:54,980
That is, that a cluster which spins up, fails, and terminates takes its local logs with it.

8107
13:21:55,822 --> 13:22:03,652
Log delivery to S3 has to be configured before the cluster is created, so the choice is made in advance rather than after the failure.

8108
13:22:04,270 --> 13:22:10,340
Option C is that CloudRail distinction tested directly, and it appeared in module 14 for the same reason.

8109
13:22:11,278 --> 13:22:21,376
CloudRail would show that a cluster was created and a step submitted with timestamps and a caller identity, and absolutely nothing about the exception sparked

8110
13:22:21,536 --> 13:22:22,336
through.

8111
13:22:23,406 --> 13:22:25,028
Question 15 of 20.

8112
13:22:25,390 --> 13:22:26,308
Domain 3.

8113
13:22:26,496 --> 13:22:29,572
A daily pipeline loads product data into a warehouse.

8114
13:22:29,998 --> 13:22:31,364
AWS Glue Data.

8115
13:22:32,430 --> 13:22:37,508
Quality rules check that key columns are complete and that prices fall within an expected range.

8116
13:22:37,696 --> 13:22:39,268
All rules pass every day.

8117
13:22:40,174 --> 13:22:47,136
Over three weeks, the proportion of products with a null description rose from under 1% to 11%.

8118
13:22:47,856 --> 13:22:50,852
Nobody noticed until a catalog page looked wrong.

8119
13:22:51,470 --> 13:22:53,540
What should the data engineer implement?

8120
13:22:54,702 --> 13:22:59,748
a add an iscomplete rule on the description column with a 100% threshold.

8121
13:23:00,494 --> 13:23:04,004
b. Run the rule set twice daily instead of once.

8122
13:23:04,416 --> 13:23:08,836
C. Increase the Blue Jobs worker count so more records are validated.

8123
13:23:09,550 --> 13:23:11,652
D. Enable anomaly detection.

8124
13:23:13,326 --> 13:23:17,380
So statistics gathered across runs flag unexpected changes.

8125
13:23:18,190 --> 13:23:19,268
Pause and decide.

8126
13:23:29,038 --> 13:23:33,616
The answer is B, and the question is built to show the limit of rule-based quality.

8127
13:23:33,966 --> 13:23:42,596
Static rules catch failures, somebody anticipated, and nobody wrote a rule saying the null rate on an optional column should not increase 11 fold over three weeks.

8128
13:23:43,438 --> 13:23:46,852
That is because that is not a rule anybody thinks to write.

8129
13:23:47,182 --> 13:23:54,180
Anomaly detection watches statistics over time and reports when a pattern breaks, including patterns nobody predicted.

8130
13:23:55,022 --> 13:23:58,244
Option A is worth studying because it is the instinct.

8131
13:23:59,150 --> 13:24:05,764
It would produce a rule failing every day on a legitimately optional column, which is how alerting gets muted.

8132
13:24:07,438 --> 13:24:09,376
Question 16 of 20.

8133
13:24:09,678 --> 13:24:10,532
Domain 3.

8134
13:24:11,118 --> 13:24:13,124
This question asks for two answers.

8135
13:24:13,774 --> 13:24:19,716
An analytics team's Amazon Athena spend has grown to a level finance has queried.

8136
13:24:20,142 --> 13:24:21,860
The data is uncompressed.

8137
13:24:22,606 --> 13:24:25,924
JSON in Amazon S3 in a single prefix.

8138
13:24:26,766 --> 13:24:30,820
Queries filter on a date field and read 8 columns out of 70.

8139
13:24:31,694 --> 13:24:37,252
A proposal has been made to purchase Athena capacity reservations to make the cost predictable.

8140
13:24:37,806 --> 13:24:41,376
Select two changes the data engineer should make first.

8141
13:24:41,902 --> 13:24:45,380
a convert the data to parquet with snappy completion.

8142
13:24:45,936 --> 13:24:48,132
B. Partition the data by date.

8143
13:24:48,656 --> 13:24:51,876
C. Purchase Athena capacity reservations.

8144
13:24:52,430 --> 13:24:52,736
D

8145
13:24:53,710 --> 13:24:57,476
move the data to S3 Glacier instant retrieval.

8146
13:24:57,776 --> 13:24:58,948
Pause and decide.

8147
13:25:08,526 --> 13:25:10,340
The answers are A and B.

8148
13:25:10,656 --> 13:25:14,052
The word first in the question tells you the ordering matters.

8149
13:25:14,496 --> 13:25:20,804
The principle from module 16 is that you reduce the work before you buy capacity to perform it.

8150
13:25:21,870 --> 13:25:26,016
Capacity reservations are a real and useful Athena feature.

8151
13:25:26,574 --> 13:25:34,916
Buying them here means sizing a reservation around queries that read 70 columns of uncompressed JSON to answer a date filter.

8152
13:25:35,822 --> 13:25:41,216
Fix the format and the layout and the reservation may become unnecessary or much smaller.

8153
13:25:41,742 --> 13:25:48,452
Two named causes and two matching options with the reservation proposal sitting there as the distractor.

8154
13:25:49,902 --> 13:25:51,748
The whole question was built around.

8155
13:25:53,134 --> 13:25:54,816
Question 17 of 20.

8156
13:25:55,056 --> 13:25:56,096
Domain 4.

8157
13:25:56,398 --> 13:25:59,908
A bank runs blue jobs in private subnets.

8158
13:26:02,336 --> 13:26:06,016
Compliance requires that no data traverses the public internet.

8159
13:26:06,496 --> 13:26:13,284
The jobs can reach only 3, named Amazon S3 buckets rather than any bucket in AWS.

8160
13:26:14,190 --> 13:26:23,268
The subnets currently route outbound traffic through a NIT gateway and jobs reach Amazon S3 over its public endpoint.

8161
13:26:24,142 --> 13:26:26,244
What should the data engineer implement?

8162
13:26:27,214 --> 13:26:32,548
a an IAM policy on the view role permitting only those three buckets.

8163
13:26:34,414 --> 13:26:38,564
Bucket policies on each of the three buckets, keeping the NAT gateway.

8164
13:26:39,278 --> 13:26:43,204
C. Move the glue jobs into public subnets to simplify routing.

8165
13:26:43,776 --> 13:26:44,096
D.

8166
13:26:44,622 --> 13:26:51,296
An S3 gateway endpoint with an endpoint policy naming the three buckets and remove the S3 route through the NAT gateway.

8167
13:26:51,536 --> 13:26:52,612
Pause and decide.

8168
13:27:01,934 --> 13:27:03,056
The answer is A.

8169
13:27:03,182 --> 13:27:06,916
and option C is the instructive near MissWord study.

8170
13:27:10,222 --> 13:27:17,316
An IAM policy limiting the blue role to three buckets is genuine least privilege and it governs the identity.

8171
13:27:17,856 --> 13:27:24,452
Anyone who can edit that role can widen it, and the traffic still travels the public internet path regardless.

8172
13:27:25,296 --> 13:27:31,856
An endpoint policy governs what can be reached through a network path at all, whichever identity is asking.

8173
13:27:32,096 --> 13:27:34,148
That is what a data perimeter means.

8174
13:27:34,416 --> 13:27:42,404
It is what the compliance requirement is describing when it says jobs may reach only three name buckets, rather than any bucket in AWS.

8175
13:27:44,078 --> 13:27:45,536
Question 18 of 20.

8176
13:27:45,776 --> 13:27:46,660
Domain 4.

8177
13:27:48,654 --> 13:27:56,416
A company has configured AWS Lake Formation column level grants excluding three sensitive columns from an analytics

8178
13:27:56,656 --> 13:27:57,136
team.

8179
13:27:57,614 --> 13:28:00,740
The grants appear correctly in the lake formation console.

8180
13:28:01,678 --> 13:28:06,020
Testing shows the analytics team can still query all columns through Amazon Athena.

8181
13:28:06,496 --> 13:28:09,636
The database was created before lake formation was adopted.

8182
13:28:09,936 --> 13:28:11,844
What is the most likely cause?

8183
13:28:12,270 --> 13:28:17,572
a column level grants only apply to Amazon Redshift Spectrum, not to Athena.

8184
13:28:18,256 --> 13:28:18,576
b

8185
13:28:19,694 --> 13:28:25,956
The IAM Allowed Principles Setting is still present, so Lake Formation differs to IAMC.

8186
13:28:27,534 --> 13:28:30,244
The grants need up to 24 hours to propagate.

8187
13:28:30,576 --> 13:28:35,428
D. The table must be converted to Apache Iceberg for column grants to apply.

8188
13:28:35,982 --> 13:28:37,188
Pause and decide

8189
13:28:46,894 --> 13:28:53,348
The answer is B, and the sentence about the database predating lake formation is the clue that gives it away.

8190
13:28:54,478 --> 13:29:03,648
When a database or table carries the IAM Allowed Principles setting, lake formation differs to IAM entirely for that resource.

8191
13:29:04,174 --> 13:29:12,260
So you can create precise column level grants, see them listed correctly in the console, and observe that everybody still sees every column.

8192
13:29:12,846 --> 13:29:17,796
Nothing errors anywhere, which is exactly what makes it so confusing to diagnose.

8193
13:29:18,638 --> 13:29:25,508
Removing that setting is what activates enforcement and hybrid access mode is the safer path for an existing lake.

8194
13:29:26,318 --> 13:29:29,924
That is because it lets you move principles across one at a time.

8195
13:29:31,568 --> 13:29:33,444
Question 19 of 20.

8196
13:29:33,774 --> 13:29:34,820
Domain 4.

8197
13:29:35,408 --> 13:29:41,348
A company enabled Amazon Messi across its data lake 9 months ago.

8198
13:29:41,888 --> 13:29:51,428
The lake holds 60TB across 300 buckets, all encrypted with SSEKMS using several customer managed keys.

8199
13:29:51,888 --> 13:29:55,236
Macy reports almost no sensitive data findings.

8200
13:29:56,142 --> 13:30:00,836
A manual review of two datasets has found unredacted email addresses and payment card numbers.

8201
13:30:01,088 --> 13:30:02,980
What is the most likely explanation?

8202
13:30:03,534 --> 13:30:09,252
a Macy lacks permission to use the KMS keys so it can only report object metadata.

8203
13:30:09,728 --> 13:30:09,968
b.

8204
13:30:10,638 --> 13:30:14,596
Macy does not detect payment card numbers without a custom data identifier.

8205
13:30:15,374 --> 13:30:15,648
C.

8206
13:30:16,398 --> 13:30:19,460
Macy samples only the first megabyte of each object.

8207
13:30:19,982 --> 13:30:24,356
D. The findings expired because Macy retains results for 90 days.

8208
13:30:24,688 --> 13:30:25,796
Pause and decide.

8209
13:30:39,568 --> 13:30:48,676
A security tool reporting nothing has two possible explanations. And the comfortable one is that there is nothing to report. The other is that it cannot see.

8210
13:30:49,166 --> 13:30:55,248
Nine months of near silence across 300 buckets known to hold customer data is the second.

8211
13:30:55,648 --> 13:30:58,436
The encryption detail in the question is the clue.

8212
13:30:58,928 --> 13:31:06,756
Messi needs to be permitted in the key policy to decrypt objects it inspects, and without that it reports object metadata only.

8213
13:31:07,568 --> 13:31:12,804
AWS publishes a script to enumerate which keys needed.

8214
13:31:14,286 --> 13:31:15,808
Question 20 of 20.

8215
13:31:15,982 --> 13:31:16,932
Domain 4.

8216
13:31:17,710 --> 13:31:24,036
A market research firm wants to sell three curated datasets to around 150 external customers.

8217
13:31:24,448 --> 13:31:30,884
Subscribers should receive quarterly revisions automatically and access must end when a subscription lapses.

8218
13:31:31,726 --> 13:31:35,408
The firm does not want to build entitlement management or billing.

8219
13:31:35,648 --> 13:31:42,052
A proposal is to create an S3 bucket per customer and copy the datasets into each after every revision.

8220
13:31:42,368 --> 13:31:44,868
What should the data engineer recommend instead?

8221
13:31:46,798 --> 13:31:51,428
A single bucket with a bucket policy listing all 150 accounts.

8222
13:31:52,528 --> 13:31:55,940
Automate the per customer copying with a scheduled blue job.

8223
13:31:56,622 --> 13:31:56,848
C.

8224
13:31:57,262 --> 13:32:01,476
AWS Lake Formation cross account grants to each customer account.

8225
13:32:02,368 --> 13:32:08,612
D. AWS Data Exchange Publishing the Datasets with Managed entitlements and Revisions.

8226
13:32:20,142 --> 13:32:22,176
The answer is A.

8227
13:32:23,118 --> 13:32:29,216
The requirement names the three things that distinguish external commercial sharing from ordinary cross account access.

8228
13:32:29,806 --> 13:32:35,172
Subscribers, automatic revisions, and access ending when a subscription lapses.

8229
13:32:35,950 --> 13:32:42,532
Option C deserves respect because Lake Formation cross account grants genuinely would deliver the access part.

8230
13:32:42,990 --> 13:32:49,444
They leave the firm building exactly the entitlement and billing machinery it explicitly said it did not want to build.

8231
13:32:50,222 --> 13:32:58,884
Option D is the automation trap, where automating a poor design makes it faster to execute without making it any better.

8232
13:33:00,366 --> 13:33:09,536
150 copies still drift apart, still cost storage, and still have to be revoked individually when somebody stops paying.

8233
13:33:10,926 --> 13:33:15,588
The call-out is the reason to keep both sets of results rather than only the second.

8234
13:33:16,110 --> 13:33:21,060
A row you missed in exam 1 and passed here was probably a reading slip the first time.

8235
13:33:21,678 --> 13:33:27,204
A row you missed in both is a real gap in the material, and the module named is where to go.

8236
13:33:28,174 --> 13:33:35,524
Two exams of 20 questions each is a small sample. So treat a single miss as noise and a repeated one as signal.

8237
13:33:36,110 --> 13:33:39,780
That distinction is more useful than either score on its own.

8238
13:33:40,096 --> 13:33:44,612
And it is the reason these exams cover different ground within the same domains.

8239
13:33:46,190 --> 13:33:52,656
The callout names the pattern worth hunting for deliberately because it resolves without any weighing of alternatives.

8240
13:33:52,896 --> 13:33:56,004
Lambda's timeout cannot be raised past 15 minutes.

8241
13:33:56,334 --> 13:34:00,772
Lake formation does apply to Athena. Macy does detect payment card numbers.

8242
13:34:01,358 --> 13:34:04,256
A crawler with more samples still cannot pass a delimiter.

8243
13:34:04,496 --> 13:34:05,700
It does not recognize.

8244
13:34:06,256 --> 13:34:12,132
Each of those eliminates an option on a fact rather than a judgment, which takes seconds and carries no risk.

8245
13:34:12,656 --> 13:34:17,856
Spotting one impossible option improves a blind guess from 25% to 33%.

8246
13:34:18,510 --> 13:34:20,516
That is before you have done any real thinking.

8247
13:34:21,998 --> 13:34:29,444
The callout points at the most likely cause of running over, which is reading the options before the constraint and then arguing with yourself.

8248
13:34:30,016 --> 13:34:35,376
Read the question first, then the scenario, then hunt for the sentence that refuses something.

8249
13:34:35,918 --> 13:34:39,428
You arrive at the options already knowing what cannot be true.

8250
13:34:40,110 --> 13:34:43,716
That is faster and more accurate than weighing four plausible designs.

8251
13:34:44,430 --> 13:34:49,796
The last bullet is worth taking seriously as well, because finishing comfortably is information.

8252
13:34:50,736 --> 13:34:56,740
It means you can afford to slow down on the two or three questions that genuinely deserve it.

8253
13:34:58,190 --> 13:35:00,256
The fourth bullet is the one people skip.

8254
13:35:00,782 --> 13:35:09,828
Taking a third practice exam immediately after a second, with no review in between, produces a third measurement of the same knowledge, not an improvement.

8255
13:35:10,670 --> 13:35:18,244
The review is where the learning happens, which is why every option in these exams carries a reason rather than just a mark.

8256
13:35:20,430 --> 13:35:25,732
Practice exam 3 follows and it closes with a look at the trend across all 60 questions.

8257
13:35:26,638 --> 13:35:32,036
That is more informative than any single score because it shows whether the reviewing is working.

8258
13:35:33,550 --> 13:35:42,576
The final module, 20 more questions on ground, neither of the earlier sets covered, weighted the same way with two multiple response questions among them.

8259
13:35:42,736 --> 13:35:46,176
Afterwards, this module does something the other two did not.

8260
13:35:46,894 --> 13:35:52,452
That is to look at the trend across all 60 questions rather than at a single score.

8261
13:35:53,934 --> 13:36:02,096
That trend is more informative than any one result because it tells you whether reviewing between attempts is actually changing anything.

8262
13:36:02,894 --> 13:36:05,252
Then a short close to the course.

8263
13:36:05,696 --> 13:36:10,212
If you have worked through all 26 modules, this is the last of about 10 hours.

8264
13:36:11,694 --> 13:36:14,564
The callout is worth acting on rather than reading past.

8265
13:36:16,398 --> 13:36:22,564
If you have just finished exam 2 and are starting this immediately, stop and review the questions you got wrong first.

8266
13:36:23,406 --> 13:36:29,860
That is because otherwise this set will find the same gaps and you will have spent 40 minutes confirming what you already knew.

8267
13:36:30,190 --> 13:36:37,668
20 questions is a small sample, and 3 sets of 20 is still a small sample. So the useful signal is not the score.

8268
13:36:38,158 --> 13:36:46,148
It is whether a category of mistake you identified after exam one has stopped appearing by exam 3.

8269
13:36:47,918 --> 13:36:52,356
The fourth bullet is the design choice worth knowing about as you work through this set.

8270
13:36:52,974 --> 13:36:57,540
That is because it is the closest these exams come to reproducing what the real one does.

8271
13:36:58,350 --> 13:37:05,540
A partition question in exam one had analysts who never filtered by hour. So removing the hour partition was correct.

8272
13:37:06,032 --> 13:37:07,152
Question 12.

8273
13:37:07,312 --> 13:37:14,116
Here states that analysts do occasionally filter by hour and the answer moves to a partition index instead.

8274
13:37:14,592 --> 13:37:16,772
Nothing about the technology changed.

8275
13:37:17,152 --> 13:37:23,940
One clause in the scenario changed, and with it the right answer, which is exactly the skill the exam is testing.

8276
13:37:25,582 --> 13:37:26,852
Question 1 of 20.

8277
13:37:27,598 --> 13:37:28,420
Domain 1.

8278
13:37:30,766 --> 13:37:36,516
A company needs order events available in Amazon Redshift within seconds for an operational dashboard.

8279
13:37:36,752 --> 13:37:39,812
Events are produced to Amazon Kinesis data streams.

8280
13:37:40,112 --> 13:37:45,092
The team does not want to stage files in Amazon S3 or operate a consumer application.

8281
13:37:46,094 --> 13:37:50,500
Volumes are moderate and the dashboard queries aggregate the most recent events.

8282
13:37:50,926 --> 13:37:52,964
What should the data engineer implement?

8283
13:37:54,126 --> 13:38:00,324
A Amazon data firehose delivering to Amazon S3 with a scheduled copy into Redshift.

8284
13:38:00,878 --> 13:38:05,892
B Redshift streaming ingestion materializing the stream into a materialized view.

8285
13:38:07,470 --> 13:38:07,712
C

8286
13:38:08,878 --> 13:38:14,532
An AWS Lambda consumer writing rose to Redshift with the data API.

8287
13:38:14,990 --> 13:38:19,300
B. AWS Blue Streaming Job Writing to Redshift every minute.

8288
13:38:19,726 --> 13:38:20,932
Pause and decide.

8289
13:38:30,670 --> 13:38:31,716
The answer is B.

8290
13:38:32,112 --> 13:38:35,940
The two refusals in the scenario each remove options cleanly.

8291
13:38:37,198 --> 13:38:44,912
Not staging in S3 eliminates A because Firehose always writes to a destination and a redshift delivery goes via S3

8292
13:38:45,072 --> 13:38:46,628
and a copy command.

8293
13:38:47,118 --> 13:38:52,932
Not operating a consumer eliminates C and D because both are applications somebody has to write and run.

8294
13:38:53,518 --> 13:38:59,812
Redshift streaming ingestion connects the warehouse directly to a Kinesis stream or an Amazon MSK topic.

8295
13:39:00,430 --> 13:39:04,260
Records materialize into a materialized view within seconds.

8296
13:39:05,198 --> 13:39:13,156
Module 3 introduced it and module 10 returned to it and it exists precisely for this shape of requirement.

8297
13:39:14,752 --> 13:39:16,260
Question 2 of 20.

8298
13:39:16,590 --> 13:39:17,412
Domain 1.

8299
13:39:18,446 --> 13:39:25,872
An AWS Blue job created 2 years ago still runs with default settings. It processes about 8 GB nightly.

8300
13:39:26,112 --> 13:39:32,516
Monitoring shows executor utilization below 15% throughout and the job completes in 12 minutes.

8301
13:39:33,326 --> 13:39:41,252
A cost review has flagged it as one of the more expensive jobs in the account relative to the data it handles. What should the data engineer do?

8302
13:39:41,792 --> 13:39:44,992
a switch the job to BlueFlex execution.

8303
13:39:45,312 --> 13:39:47,684
B Enable job bookmarks.

8304
13:39:48,912 --> 13:39:49,232
C.

8305
13:39:49,792 --> 13:39:52,804
Move the job to Amazon EMR serverless.

8306
13:39:53,422 --> 13:39:53,712
D.

8307
13:39:54,832 --> 13:40:00,260
Reduce the number of workers from the default allocation to match observed utilization.

8308
13:40:00,832 --> 13:40:02,340
Pause and decide.

8309
13:40:12,014 --> 13:40:13,028
The answer is B.

8310
13:40:13,742 --> 13:40:16,484
Three details in the scenario all point the same way.

8311
13:40:17,230 --> 13:40:24,036
Created two years ago, still running on default settings and executor utilization below 15% throughout.

8312
13:40:25,006 --> 13:40:31,872
Drew's default allocation is 10 DPUs and 8GB processed in 12 minutes does not need anything like that.

8313
13:40:32,112 --> 13:40:38,820
Module 16 made the point that defaults nobody changed account for a large share of real waste in an account.

8314
13:40:39,630 --> 13:40:45,092
That a question describing something running unchanged for a long time is usually pointing at 1.

8315
13:40:46,990 --> 13:40:52,260
Option A is worth noting because Flex is a real cost lever applied to the wrong problem.

8316
13:40:53,998 --> 13:40:55,152
Question 3 of 20.

8317
13:40:55,392 --> 13:40:56,260
Domain 1.

8318
13:40:56,750 --> 13:41:06,852
An AWS Lambda function is triggered per file arriving in Amazon S3 and writes results to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database.

8319
13:41:07,534 --> 13:41:13,988
During bulk uploads, thousands of files arrive at once and Lambda scales to hundreds of concurrent executions.

8320
13:41:14,926 --> 13:41:20,772
The database exhausts its connection limit and the application that also uses it begins failing.

8321
13:41:21,422 --> 13:41:23,312
What should the data engineer implement?

8322
13:41:23,632 --> 13:41:27,908
a increase the RDS instance size to raise the connection limit.

8323
13:41:28,352 --> 13:41:28,672
b.

8324
13:41:29,198 --> 13:41:32,004
Enable provision concurrency on the lambda function.

8325
13:41:33,152 --> 13:41:33,472
C

8326
13:41:34,606 --> 13:41:40,272
Set reserved concurrency on the lambda function to cap simultaneous executions.

8327
13:41:40,622 --> 13:41:43,392
D. Increase the lambda function timeout.

8328
13:41:44,014 --> 13:41:45,092
Pause and decide.

8329
13:41:54,446 --> 13:41:55,712
The answer is B.

8330
13:41:56,686 --> 13:42:05,316
Module 6 made the point that parallelism is not free at the system level because it relocates the bottleneck rather than removing it.

8331
13:42:06,254 --> 13:42:15,172
Hundreds of concurrent lambda functions, each opening a database connection, will exhaust a connection limit long before lambda runs out of capacity.

8332
13:42:15,632 --> 13:42:22,468
Reserved concurrency is the deliberate answer, capping the function so it cannot overwhelm what sits behind it.

8333
13:42:23,312 --> 13:42:31,312
The trade is that the bulk load takes longer, which is almost always correct when the dependency is shared with an application

8334
13:42:31,472 --> 13:42:32,452
serving users.

8335
13:42:32,974 --> 13:42:42,020
Options B and C are also worth separating carefully because reserved and provisioned concurrency sound similar and solve opposite problems.

8336
13:42:43,438 --> 13:42:44,804
Question 4 of 20.

8337
13:42:45,038 --> 13:42:45,892
Domain 1.

8338
13:42:46,478 --> 13:42:51,524
A company must process around 4 million individual small JSON objects in Amazon.

8339
13:42:52,302 --> 13:42:56,004
S3 Applying a transformation to each and writing the result back.

8340
13:42:56,558 --> 13:43:00,644
The work is embarrassingly parallel and each object takes under a second.

8341
13:43:01,454 --> 13:43:08,196
The team wants to run this without building custom fan out logic and needs per object success and failure tracking.

8342
13:43:08,718 --> 13:43:10,724
What should the data engineer use?

8343
13:43:11,598 --> 13:43:16,996
a an AWS blue spar job listing the prefix and processing every object.

8344
13:43:17,646 --> 13:43:21,828
b a single lambda function iterating over the objects in a loop.

8345
13:43:23,022 --> 13:43:23,312
C.

8346
13:43:24,622 --> 13:43:28,932
A step function's inline map state over a list of 4 million keys.

8347
13:43:29,472 --> 13:43:29,792
D.

8348
13:43:30,112 --> 13:43:35,268
A step function's distributed map state invoking a lambda function per object.

8349
13:43:37,152 --> 13:43:38,500
Pause and decide.

8350
13:43:48,272 --> 13:43:49,604
The answer is A.

8351
13:43:49,872 --> 13:43:52,832
Distributed Map exists specifically for this shape.

8352
13:43:52,992 --> 13:44:01,060
It reads the item list directly from S3 and launches child executions at very high concurrency with per item results rather than one overall outcome.

8353
13:44:01,838 --> 13:44:09,988
Option C is the one worth understanding because inline map and distributed map look similar in a question and differ enormously in scale.

8354
13:44:11,246 --> 13:44:14,436
Inline map runs the child steps inside the parent execution

8355
13:44:15,086 --> 13:44:27,108
that limits concurrency and puts every item to the state machine's payload and 4 million items is far beyond it. The distinction between the two map modes is a fair exam detail.

8356
13:44:28,782 --> 13:44:30,148
Question 5 of 20.

8357
13:44:30,382 --> 13:44:31,236
Domain 1.

8358
13:44:31,792 --> 13:44:35,236
60 suppliers send daily inventory files to a retailer.

8359
13:44:35,552 --> 13:44:41,508
Each supplier uses SFTP with its own credentials and cannot change how it transmits files.

8360
13:44:41,966 --> 13:44:44,932
The retailer wants the files to land in Amazon S3.

8361
13:44:45,902 --> 13:44:50,756
To stop operating the current SFTP servers on Amazon EC2.

8362
13:44:51,232 --> 13:44:54,212
File sizes are modest and arrive throughout the day.

8363
13:44:54,702 --> 13:44:56,996
What should the data engineer implement?

8364
13:44:57,870 --> 13:45:03,748
A AWS transfer family with an SFTP endpoint backed by Amazon S3.

8365
13:45:04,654 --> 13:45:08,164
B AWS datasync agents at each supplier site.

8366
13:45:08,752 --> 13:45:09,072
C

8367
13:45:09,902 --> 13:45:12,836
Amazon App Flow with an SFTP connector.

8368
13:45:13,582 --> 13:45:13,872
D.

8369
13:45:14,478 --> 13:45:18,020
AWS snowball edge devices shipped to each supplier.

8370
13:45:18,606 --> 13:45:19,780
Pause and decide.

8371
13:45:29,390 --> 13:45:30,404
The answer is B.

8372
13:45:30,832 --> 13:45:40,004
The constraint is that 60 external parties cannot change their process, which rules out anything requiring software on their side or a different protocol.

8373
13:45:42,446 --> 13:45:48,804
Transfer family provides a managed SFTP endpoint that writes directly into Amazon S3.

8374
13:45:49,774 --> 13:45:54,852
So suppliers see exactly the interface they had while the retailer stops running servers.

8375
13:45:55,312 --> 13:46:00,036
This is the compatibility rule again, applied to a protocol rather than a database.

8376
13:46:02,030 --> 13:46:03,460
APEPI.

8377
13:46:04,366 --> 13:46:07,392
Option D is worth recognizing for two reasons.

8378
13:46:07,662 --> 13:46:13,220
Because the volume is wrong and because Snowball edge closed to new customers in November 2025.

8379
13:46:14,670 --> 13:46:16,484
Question 6 of 20.

8380
13:46:17,792 --> 13:46:18,980
Domain 1.

8381
13:46:19,758 --> 13:46:21,732
This question asks for two answers.

8382
13:46:22,286 --> 13:46:26,820
A blue job writes to a curated Amazon S3 table used by Athena.

8383
13:46:27,502 --> 13:46:35,652
Output consists of roughly 40,000 files per day, most under 200 kilobytes, because the job writes one file per input partition.

8384
13:46:36,622 --> 13:46:44,708
Athena queries have become slow, spending most of their time before any data is read. The data is parquet and correctly partitioned by date.

8385
13:46:45,998 --> 13:46:48,900
Select two actions that will improve query performance.

8386
13:46:49,550 --> 13:46:51,876
a coalesce or repartition.

8387
13:46:52,270 --> 13:46:55,268
The output so the job writes fewer larger files.

8388
13:46:57,134 --> 13:47:00,452
run a scheduled compaction job over existing partitions.

8389
13:47:00,942 --> 13:47:04,068
C. Convert the output from parquet to CSV.

8390
13:47:04,526 --> 13:47:06,788
D. Add more workers to the blue job.

8391
13:47:07,022 --> 13:47:08,196
Pause and decide.

8392
13:47:17,870 --> 13:47:19,492
The answers are A and B.

8393
13:47:19,840 --> 13:47:27,748
The pairing is the standard multiple response shape where one option addresses the cost and the other addresses the accumulated effect.

8394
13:47:28,334 --> 13:47:39,108
Coalescing the output stops tomorrow producing 40,000 files and compaction repairs everything already written because otherwise months of existing partitions remain slow to query.

8395
13:47:40,046 --> 13:47:44,164
Option D is worth understanding because it is actively counterproductive.

8396
13:47:45,102 --> 13:47:50,948
Spark writes roughly one file per output partition, so adding workers usually increases the file count.

8397
13:47:51,566 --> 13:47:58,564
Time spent before any data is read is the diagnostic phrase here, pointing at file listing rather than at scanning.

8398
13:47:59,854 --> 13:48:01,280
Question 7 of 20.

8399
13:48:01,440 --> 13:48:02,400
Domain 1.

8400
13:48:02,640 --> 13:48:10,483
A data pipeline reads a 2MB CSV file each hour, renames 4 columns, casts two dates, and writes parquet.

8401
13:48:11,040 --> 13:48:18,660
It currently runs as an AWS Blue Spark job with the default allocation and takes about 4 minutes, most of which is startup.

8402
13:48:19,438 --> 13:48:22,724
The team wants the lowest cost for this specific step.

8403
13:48:23,118 --> 13:48:24,932
What should the data engineer use?

8404
13:48:26,382 --> 13:48:30,308
a An AWS Lambda function triggered by the file arrival.

8405
13:48:31,040 --> 13:48:31,280
B.

8406
13:48:31,886 --> 13:48:35,760
Keep the Glue Spark job and reduce it to the minimum 2 dB use.

8407
13:48:36,320 --> 13:48:36,560
C.

8408
13:48:37,038 --> 13:48:38,564
A Glue Python shell job.

8409
13:48:39,342 --> 13:48:39,600
D.

8410
13:48:40,078 --> 13:48:43,108
An Amazon EMR serverless application.

8411
13:48:44,334 --> 13:48:45,508
Pause and decide.

8412
13:48:55,022 --> 13:49:01,060
The answer is B. And option C is the distractor worth studying because it is nearly right.

8413
13:49:01,806 --> 13:49:10,400
A blue Python shell job is the correct instinct moving away from Spark for work that is not distributed and it is meaningfully cheaper.

8414
13:49:10,926 --> 13:49:17,440
It still carries blue startup time and its one minute billing minimum incurred 24 times a

8415
13:49:17,440 --> 13:49:22,084
day. A Lambda function processes this file in well under a second.

8416
13:49:22,960 --> 13:49:30,564
The phrase, most of which is startup is the deciding detail because it tells you the engine's overhead exceeds the actual work.

8417
13:49:31,438 --> 13:49:35,748
That means the engine is the wrong category rather than badly configured.

8418
13:49:37,390 --> 13:49:39,076
Question 8 of 20.

8419
13:49:39,502 --> 13:49:40,324
Domain 2.

8420
13:49:40,814 --> 13:49:45,920
A company is launching a new product and will store its telemetry in Amazon S3.

8421
13:49:46,382 --> 13:49:53,188
Nobody can predict how often the data will be read, and access patterns are expected to change as the product matures.

8422
13:49:54,350 --> 13:49:56,160
Objects average 4 MB.

8423
13:49:57,280 --> 13:50:02,273
The team wants to minimize storage cost without risking retrieval charges from a wrong guess.

8424
13:50:02,720 --> 13:50:04,772
What should the data engineer configure?

8425
13:50:05,294 --> 13:50:08,068
a a lifecycle rule transitioning to standard.

8426
13:50:08,480 --> 13:50:10,372
I A after 30 days.

8427
13:50:10,734 --> 13:50:13,540
B S3 Intelligent Tiering.

8428
13:50:14,254 --> 13:50:14,480
C

8429
13:50:15,470 --> 13:50:18,372
A life cycle rule transitioning to glacier instant.

8430
13:50:18,574 --> 13:50:20,132
Retrieval after 7 days.

8431
13:50:21,200 --> 13:50:21,520
D

8432
13:50:22,286 --> 13:50:23,396
S31 zone.

8433
13:50:23,886 --> 13:50:25,760
Infrequent access from the outset.

8434
13:50:26,000 --> 13:50:27,236
Pause and decide.

8435
13:50:36,942 --> 13:50:38,020
The answer is B.

8436
13:50:38,574 --> 13:50:48,420
Module 8 established that the trigger phrase for intelligent tiering is remarkably consistent across questions, and this scenario uses two forms of it in one paragraph.

8437
13:50:48,880 --> 13:50:53,700
Access patterns cannot be predicted and they are expected to change as the product matures.

8438
13:50:54,606 --> 13:50:59,428
Every lifecycle rule encodes a guess about how access will behave over time.

8439
13:51:00,238 --> 13:51:07,396
When nobody can make that guess reliably, the class that observes actual access and moves objects accordingly is the right answer.

8440
13:51:08,270 --> 13:51:17,188
The 4 MB object size matters too because the monitoring charge makes intelligent tiering poor value for very small objects.

8441
13:51:18,862 --> 13:51:20,836
Question 9 of 20.

8442
13:51:21,296 --> 13:51:22,212
Domain 2

8443
13:51:23,470 --> 13:51:30,532
A nightly pipeline loads order records into an Amazon Redshift table that has a declared primary key on order.

8444
13:51:31,502 --> 13:51:39,972
ID, after a failure and rerun last week, the table now contains around 12,000 duplicated orders and reports are overstated.

8445
13:51:40,878 --> 13:51:45,188
The engineer is surprised because the primary key should have rejected them.

8446
13:51:46,030 --> 13:51:48,996
What is the cause and what should be implemented?

8447
13:51:49,616 --> 13:51:54,628
a. The rerun used a different distribution key, creating duplicate slices.

8448
13:51:55,502 --> 13:52:02,096
b The primary key was declared after the table was created. So it applies only to new rows.

8449
13:52:03,342 --> 13:52:09,776
Redshift constraints are informational and not enforced. Make the load idempotent with a staging table and a merge statement.

8450
13:52:10,016 --> 13:52:14,340
D. Enable automatic table optimization so redshift enforces the key.

8451
13:52:14,798 --> 13:52:15,844
Pause and decide.

8452
13:52:25,422 --> 13:52:30,916
The answer is A, and this one catches people arriving from transactional databases.

8453
13:52:33,550 --> 13:52:38,692
Primary key, foreign key, and uniqueness constraints in Redshift are informational only.

8454
13:52:39,726 --> 13:52:44,964
They tell the query planner that values are unique, which genuinely helps it choose better plans.

8455
13:52:45,806 --> 13:52:49,136
They do not prevent you inserting the same row twice.

8456
13:52:49,390 --> 13:52:58,692
Only not null is actually enforced. So a pipeline relying on the key to reject rows it has already loaded will simply load them again after a rerun.

8457
13:52:59,056 --> 13:53:01,028
Nothing anywhere reports a problem.

8458
13:53:01,838 --> 13:53:09,796
The fix is to make the load idem potent, which in Redshift means copying to a staging table and applying a merge.

8459
13:53:11,470 --> 13:53:13,060
Question 10 of 20.

8460
13:53:13,646 --> 13:53:14,564
Domain 2.

8461
13:53:14,736 --> 13:53:18,496
A trading platform needs a session store holding around 200GB.

8462
13:53:19,406 --> 13:53:26,724
Reads and writes must complete in microseconds and the data must survive an availability zone failure without loss.

8463
13:53:27,534 --> 13:53:31,684
That is because it is the authoritative record for in-flight sessions.

8464
13:53:31,936 --> 13:53:39,012
The team currently uses an in-memory cache backed by a relational database. What should the data engineer recommend?

8465
13:53:39,502 --> 13:53:46,393
a Amazon Dynamo DB with Dynamo DB Accelerator, B Amazon S3 Express One Zone.

8466
13:53:47,182 --> 13:53:47,456
C

8467
13:53:48,558 --> 13:53:52,676
Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with a large buffer cache.

8468
13:53:53,390 --> 13:53:55,908
D Amazon Memory DB.

8469
13:53:56,430 --> 13:53:57,764
Pause and decide.

8470
13:54:07,470 --> 13:54:13,572
The answer is B and the question is built around the distinction between a cache and a durable in-memory store.

8471
13:54:14,478 --> 13:54:18,896
A cache holds a copy of data that also lives somewhere authoritative.

8472
13:54:19,056 --> 13:54:22,020
So losing it costs performance rather than data.

8473
13:54:22,958 --> 13:54:33,092
MemoryDB is in memory and durable with a transaction log replicated across availability zones, which lets it be the primary store for data needing microsecond access.

8474
13:54:34,158 --> 13:54:40,292
Note also that MemoryDB appears on this exams in Scope Service List while ElastiCache does not.

8475
13:54:41,390 --> 13:54:47,460
That is a fairly direct signal about which one AWS expects you to reach for in a question like this.

8476
13:54:48,974 --> 13:54:50,628
Question 11 of 20.

8477
13:54:53,070 --> 13:54:53,956
Domain 2.

8478
13:54:54,478 --> 13:55:03,140
An analytics team must rename a column in a 5-year-old dataset of parquet files in Amazon S3 because the business term has changed.

8479
13:55:03,982 --> 13:55:10,436
Historical queries must continue to return the same rows and rewriting 5 years of files is not acceptable.

8480
13:55:11,278 --> 13:55:14,896
The table is queried by Amazon Athena and Amazon EMR.

8481
13:55:15,136 --> 13:55:16,868
What should the data engineer do?

8482
13:55:17,376 --> 13:55:22,596
a rename the column in the blue data catalog and leave the files unchanged.

8483
13:55:23,342 --> 13:55:27,940
b Create a view that aliases the old column name to the new one.

8484
13:55:28,576 --> 13:55:28,816
C.

8485
13:55:29,518 --> 13:55:33,924
Convert the dataset to Apache Iceberg, then rename the column.

8486
13:55:34,414 --> 13:55:39,268
d Rewrite all 5 years of parquet files with the new column name.

8487
13:55:39,918 --> 13:55:40,996
Pause and decide.

8488
13:55:50,702 --> 13:55:51,812
The answer is B.

8489
13:55:52,174 --> 13:55:56,676
Module 9 covered the mechanism and this question tests it directly.

8490
13:55:57,422 --> 13:56:01,892
In a name-based format like plain parquet, columns are matched by name.

8491
13:56:02,382 --> 13:56:10,532
So a file written before a rename and one written after appear to contain different columns entirely, and the older files would read as null.

8492
13:56:10,976 --> 13:56:18,404
Iceberg assigns each column a stable numeric identifier when it is created and tracks the display name separately.

8493
13:56:19,310 --> 13:56:25,252
So a rename changes what you call it while every historical file continues to map correctly.

8494
13:56:27,374 --> 13:56:35,172
Option C is worth respecting because a view is a legitimate workaround and it leaves the underlying inconsistency in place.

8495
13:56:36,590 --> 13:56:37,956
Question 12 of 20.

8496
13:56:38,446 --> 13:56:39,268
Domain 2.

8497
13:56:39,776 --> 13:56:47,524
A table in the AWS Blue Data Catalog has around 140,000 partitions after 4 years of hourly partitioning.

8498
13:56:48,096 --> 13:56:54,692
Ethina queries take 15 to 25 seconds before scanning begins, regardless of how narrow the filter is.

8499
13:56:55,502 --> 13:56:57,892
Once scanning starts, they complete quickly.

8500
13:56:58,446 --> 13:57:01,572
Analysts filter by date range and occasionally by hour.

8501
13:57:02,222 --> 13:57:06,263
Which action addresses the delay while preserving the hourly granularity?

8502
13:57:07,022 --> 13:57:08,708
analysts occasionally use?

8503
13:57:09,422 --> 13:57:14,596
a Repartition the data by day and discard the hour partition.

8504
13:57:16,496 --> 13:57:16,816
B

8505
13:57:18,094 --> 13:57:21,220
Add a GUE partition index on the partition keys.

8506
13:57:21,710 --> 13:57:22,016
C.

8507
13:57:22,496 --> 13:57:25,136
Increase the Athena work group's query timeout.

8508
13:57:25,806 --> 13:57:26,096
D.

8509
13:57:26,542 --> 13:57:30,692
Run MS CK repair table to refresh the partition metadata.

8510
13:57:31,406 --> 13:57:32,548
Pause and decide.

8511
13:57:42,256 --> 13:57:49,028
The answer is A, and this question is deliberately the mirror image of a similar one in practice exam.

8512
13:57:50,638 --> 13:57:59,108
1. There, analysts never queried by hour, so removing the hour partition was the correct fix because it addressed the cost directly.

8513
13:58:00,014 --> 13:58:08,964
Here the question states that analysts occasionally do filter by hour, which makes courseing the partitions a loss of capability rather than a clean win.

8514
13:58:09,838 --> 13:58:18,852
When a partition scheme is genuinely needed, a partition index is the right answer, speeding the catalog lookup without changing the layout at all.

8515
13:58:19,438 --> 13:58:22,276
One changed clause and the better answer moves.

8516
13:58:22,702 --> 13:58:25,060
That is worth practicing deliberately.

8517
13:58:26,574 --> 13:58:28,096
Question 13 of 20.

8518
13:58:28,336 --> 13:58:29,156
Domain 3.

8519
13:58:29,902 --> 13:58:36,068
An application writes a log line containing the text record underscore rejected whenever it discards a malformed record.

8520
13:58:36,686 --> 13:58:38,692
These lines go to CloudWatch logs.

8521
13:58:39,086 --> 13:58:43,140
Operations want to be notified when the rate of rejections exceeds the threshold.

8522
13:58:43,696 --> 13:58:48,612
They have tried and failed to configure a CloudWatch alarm directly against the log group.

8523
13:58:49,376 --> 13:58:51,684
What should the data engineer implement?

8524
13:58:53,646 --> 13:59:00,068
a A CloudWatch logs metric filter matching the pattern with an alarm on the resulting metric.

8525
13:59:01,070 --> 13:59:01,376
b

8526
13:59:02,542 --> 13:59:06,820
A CloudWatch alarm configured with the log group as its metric source.

8527
13:59:09,166 --> 13:59:14,820
A CloudWatch logs subscription filter delivering matching events to Amazon S3.

8528
13:59:16,206 --> 13:59:19,428
d An event bridge rule matching the log content.

8529
13:59:20,558 --> 13:59:21,828
Pause and decide.

8530
13:59:31,424 --> 13:59:32,704
The answer is A.

8531
13:59:33,184 --> 13:59:42,340
The sentence saying they tried and failed to alarm on a log group is doing deliberate work. That is because it tells you option. B describe something that does not exist.

8532
13:59:42,958 --> 13:59:49,828
CloudWatch alarms evaluate metrics rather than log content, so there is no way to alarm directly on a log line containing a particular word.

8533
13:59:50,464 --> 13:59:56,864
A metric filter closes that gap, matching a pattern in incoming events and publishing a metric counting them.

8534
13:59:57,422 --> 14:00:01,092
The alarm then watches that metric exactly like any other.

8535
14:00:01,518 --> 14:00:08,132
It is a two-step answer, and an option offering only one of those two steps is incomplete rather than wrong.

8536
14:00:09,870 --> 14:00:11,684
Question 14 of 20.

8537
14:00:14,574 --> 14:00:15,556
Domain 3.

8538
14:00:15,984 --> 14:00:23,492
An executive dashboard built on Amazon Redshift is opened by around 800 people between 8 and 9 each morning.

8539
14:00:23,744 --> 14:00:28,164
The underlying data is refreshed once overnight and does not change during the day.

8540
14:00:28,878 --> 14:00:33,348
During that hour, other analytical workloads on the cluster slow noticeably.

8541
14:00:33,902 --> 14:00:36,740
Every viewer's dashboard runs the same set of queries.

8542
14:00:37,262 --> 14:00:38,948
What is the most appropriate change?

8543
14:00:40,782 --> 14:00:47,184
Import the dashboard dataset into SPICE and refresh it after the overnight load completes.

8544
14:00:49,070 --> 14:00:54,464
Enable redshift concurrency scaling so additional clusters absorb the morning burst.

8545
14:00:54,784 --> 14:00:58,436
C. Resize the redshift cluster to handle the peak.

8546
14:00:58,784 --> 14:00:59,104
D.

8547
14:00:59,344 --> 14:01:03,744
Point the dashboard at Amazon Athena over the same data in Amazon S3.

8548
14:01:04,270 --> 14:01:05,316
Pause and decide.

8549
14:01:19,790 --> 14:01:26,276
Concurrency scaling is exactly right for a burst of queries that genuinely need running, and module 10 said so.

8550
14:01:27,214 --> 14:01:35,780
Here they do not need running because 800 people are asking an identical question of data that has not changed since the overnight load.

8551
14:01:36,494 --> 14:01:43,424
S P I C E answers all of them from one cached copy and the warehouse is never touched.

8552
14:01:43,744 --> 14:01:47,460
The principle is the one module 13 kept returning to.

8553
14:02:12,704 --> 14:02:20,228
Partial figures are worse than no figures for this use case because reports built on incomplete data have twice been filed.

8554
14:02:20,704 --> 14:02:25,252
The team wants to cache this and does not want to write custom validation code.

8555
14:02:26,542 --> 14:02:29,668
Select two actions the data engineer should take.

8556
14:02:32,014 --> 14:02:38,944
Add AWS Glue data quality rules inside the ETL job checking completeness of the currency column.

8557
14:02:39,310 --> 14:02:50,532
B. Configure the job to faint when the rule set does not pass so nothing downstream is loaded. C. Configure the job to route failing records to a quarantine location and continue.

8558
14:02:51,086 --> 14:02:51,344
D.

8559
14:02:52,366 --> 14:02:55,780
Add a cloud watch alarm on the blue job's failed run count.

8560
14:02:56,430 --> 14:02:57,604
Pause and decide.

8561
14:03:07,246 --> 14:03:09,412
The answers are A and B.

8562
14:03:10,000 --> 14:03:16,708
Options B and C are the same decision pointing in opposite directions, and one sentence in the scenario chooses between them.

8563
14:03:17,838 --> 14:03:22,052
Partial figures being worse than no figures means stopping the pipeline is correct.

8564
14:03:22,702 --> 14:03:30,404
That is because a quarantine would let the remaining 99% load and produce exactly the incomplete report they have already filed twice.

8565
14:03:30,880 --> 14:03:37,988
For a different use case where good records have value on their own, C would be right and B would be overcautious.

8566
14:03:38,798 --> 14:03:45,636
That is, module 15's fail against quarantine decision and the business context is what sections it every time.

8567
14:03:47,310 --> 14:03:49,060
Question 16 of 20.

8568
14:03:49,440 --> 14:03:50,372
Domain 3.

8569
14:03:50,862 --> 14:04:00,740
An analyst accidentally ran a select star query against a 40-terabyte unpartitioned table in Amazon Athena, producing a single query costing several hundred dollars.

8570
14:04:02,382 --> 14:04:04,740
Management wants to prevent a recurrence.

8571
14:04:05,454 --> 14:04:09,476
The underlying data is scheduled to be converted to partition paquet next quarter.

8572
14:04:09,934 --> 14:04:12,100
What should the data engineer configure now?

8573
14:04:12,560 --> 14:04:15,492
a remove the analyst's Athena permissions.

8574
14:04:15,950 --> 14:04:19,492
B. An AWS budget alert on the Athena service.

8575
14:04:19,840 --> 14:04:23,652
C. A per query data scan limit on the Athena work group.

8576
14:04:24,302 --> 14:04:24,560
D.

8577
14:04:25,550 --> 14:04:29,988
Increase the workgroup's query timeout so large queries are less likely to be retried.

8578
14:04:30,400 --> 14:04:31,460
Pause and decide.

8579
14:04:41,070 --> 14:04:47,044
The answer is A and the distinction is the prevent against detect split from module 19.

8580
14:04:47,600 --> 14:04:50,468
A per query scan limit is a genuine control.

8581
14:04:50,960 --> 14:04:58,180
That is because Athena cancels the query once it would exceed the stated volume, so the worst case is bounded rather than reported.

8582
14:04:59,086 --> 14:05:07,044
A budget alert is detection arriving after the money is spent. Both have their place and the question asks how to prevent a recurrence.

8583
14:05:07,694 --> 14:05:11,236
Note also that the scan limit is a guardrail rather than a fix.

8584
14:05:12,046 --> 14:05:18,564
That is why the last sentence mentions the conversion to partition parquet next quarter as the real remedy.

8585
14:05:20,238 --> 14:05:22,052
Question 17 of 20.

8586
14:05:22,574 --> 14:05:23,428
Domain 4.

8587
14:05:24,430 --> 14:05:28,772
A company has 15 AWS accounts and around 60 engineers.

8588
14:05:29,614 --> 14:05:34,468
Each engineer has an IAM user with access keys in every account they work in.

8589
14:05:35,726 --> 14:05:40,964
Two people left last quarter and the security team cannot confirm all their credentials were removed.

8590
14:05:41,870 --> 14:05:48,516
The company wants access to end when employment ends and to stop issuing long-lived keys.

8591
14:05:49,262 --> 14:05:51,200
What should the company implement?

8592
14:05:52,560 --> 14:05:58,532
A scheduled lambda function deleting IAM users inactive for 60 days.

8593
14:05:59,054 --> 14:06:04,708
B A 90-day access key rotation policy enforced across all accounts.

8594
14:06:07,022 --> 14:06:15,428
AWS IA M Identity Center federated to the corporate directory with permission sets granting roles per account.

8595
14:06:15,920 --> 14:06:19,076
D. Consolidate the 15 accounts into three.

8596
14:06:20,430 --> 14:06:21,668
Pause and decide.

8597
14:06:31,374 --> 14:06:32,740
The answer is A.

8598
14:06:33,742 --> 14:06:38,660
The clause that decides it is that access should end when employment ends.

8599
14:06:39,040 --> 14:06:44,100
That is a statement about where identity lives rather than about how credentials are managed.

8600
14:06:45,006 --> 14:06:53,380
Only Federation satisfies it because access follows from the directory account rather than from 15 independent copies scattered across accounts.

8601
14:06:54,062 --> 14:06:59,972
Option B deserves respect as a genuine security improvement that answers neither stated requirement.

8602
14:07:00,878 --> 14:07:05,284
Rotating a key every 90 days shortens the window, a leak is useful for.

8603
14:07:06,062 --> 14:07:10,852
It keeps a long lived credential in existence throughout, which is the thing the company said.

8604
14:07:11,246 --> 14:07:13,028
It wants to stop issuing.

8605
14:07:14,670 --> 14:07:16,240
Question 18 of 20.

8606
14:07:16,622 --> 14:07:17,508
Domain 4.

8607
14:07:17,934 --> 14:07:21,600
An insurer holds claims data in Amazon Redshift.

8608
14:07:22,126 --> 14:07:25,508
Regional teams must see only their own regions' claims.

8609
14:07:25,998 --> 14:07:28,100
A fraud team must see all regions.

8610
14:07:28,942 --> 14:07:36,388
A support team must see all regions, but with the claimant's national identifier partially masked while the fraud team sees it in full.

8611
14:07:37,422 --> 14:07:44,196
There are currently 11 views maintaining these combinations and keeping them aligned with schema changes has become a burden.

8612
14:07:45,040 --> 14:07:47,332
What should the data engineer implement?

8613
14:07:47,790 --> 14:07:52,960
a create one table per region with the identifier column omitted where required.

8614
14:07:53,280 --> 14:07:58,201
B. Continue with views, generating them automatically when the schema changes.

8615
14:07:58,350 --> 14:08:04,068
c. Move the data to Amazon S3 and apply lake formation data filters.

8616
14:08:04,942 --> 14:08:05,200
D.

8617
14:08:06,126 --> 14:08:12,996
Role level security policies for the regional filtering and dynamic data masking on the identifier column by role.

8618
14:08:13,614 --> 14:08:14,692
Pause and decide.

8619
14:08:24,174 --> 14:08:25,600
The answer is A.

8620
14:08:25,920 --> 14:08:32,640
The scenario describes two genuinely different requirements that happen to have two different native redshift features.

8621
14:08:33,198 --> 14:08:40,036
Regional filtering is row level security where a policy attached to the table filters rows according to the session's identity.

8622
14:08:40,942 --> 14:08:45,120
So every team queries the same table and sees its own region.

8623
14:08:45,518 --> 14:08:53,572
The masking requirement is dynamic data masking where the same column returns a partial or a full value depending on the role asking.

8624
14:08:54,350 --> 14:08:58,308
Option C is worth noting because it is the wrong direction entirely.

8625
14:08:59,150 --> 14:09:04,932
It moves data out of the warehouse to reach a capability the warehouse already has natively.

8626
14:09:06,222 --> 14:09:07,940
Question 19 of 20.

8627
14:09:08,400 --> 14:09:09,540
Domain 4.

8628
14:09:11,086 --> 14:09:19,588
A data lake of around 50 million parket objects is encrypted with SSEKMS using a customer managed key.

8629
14:09:20,078 --> 14:09:25,732
Compliance requires a customer managed key with an audit trail, so the encryption cannot be changed.

8630
14:09:26,254 --> 14:09:34,084
Finance has flagged that the AWS KMS bill scales with query volume rather than with the amount of data stored.

8631
14:09:34,480 --> 14:09:36,452
What should the data engineer do?

8632
14:09:37,006 --> 14:09:40,960
a move the data to S3 standard infrequent access.

8633
14:09:41,280 --> 14:09:43,588
B switch the bucket to SS

8634
14:09:44,238 --> 14:09:45,988
E S3 encryption.

8635
14:09:46,960 --> 14:09:47,280
C

8636
14:09:48,238 --> 14:09:52,068
request a KMS request quota increase.

8637
14:09:53,120 --> 14:09:53,280
D

8638
14:09:54,862 --> 14:09:57,636
enable S3 bucket keys on the bucket.

8639
14:09:58,800 --> 14:10:00,196
Pause and decide.

8640
14:10:09,943 --> 14:10:10,820
The answer is

8641
14:10:11,103 --> 14:10:12,563
A.

8642
14:10:12,880 --> 14:10:18,852
The diagnostic phrase is that the bill scales with query volume rather than stored volume.

8643
14:10:19,822 --> 14:10:24,663
That means you are paying per decryption request rather than for the data at rest.

8644
14:10:25,710 --> 14:10:30,084
Without bucket keys, every object read triggers a call to KMS.

8645
14:10:30,400 --> 14:10:34,916
So a job opening 50 million objects makes 50 million billable requests.

8646
14:10:35,760 --> 14:10:41,924
Bucket keys change the mechanism using a short-lived bucket level key to derive per object keys locally.

8647
14:10:42,702 --> 14:10:48,132
KMS calls FALL dramatically with the same customer managed key and the same encryption.

8648
14:10:49,646 --> 14:10:55,876
Option B is the shape to reject on principle, solving a cost problem by removing the control compliance required.

8649
14:10:57,422 --> 14:10:58,724
Question 20 of 20.

8650
14:10:59,022 --> 14:10:59,876
Domain 4.

8651
14:11:00,558 --> 14:11:05,316
A large organization has around 800 datasets across six business units.

8652
14:11:05,920 --> 14:11:12,836
Access requests are handled by a central platform team who receive around 40 a week and take several days each.

8653
14:11:13,680 --> 14:11:18,948
The team approving requests often does not know whether the requester should have the data.

8654
14:11:19,854 --> 14:11:26,404
The organization wants requests decided by whoever owns the data with each decision recorded for audit.

8655
14:11:27,854 --> 14:11:30,148
What should the data engineer implement?

8656
14:11:32,590 --> 14:11:38,404
Grant each business unit database level lake formation permissions on its own databases.

8657
14:11:39,022 --> 14:11:42,244
b Publish datasets as assets in Amazon.

8658
14:11:42,640 --> 14:11:48,260
SageMaker catalog and use the subscription workflow with data owners approving requests.

8659
14:11:48,974 --> 14:11:49,200
C.

8660
14:11:50,158 --> 14:11:52,804
Add more engineers to the central platform team.

8661
14:11:53,440 --> 14:11:53,680
D.

8662
14:11:54,926 --> 14:12:02,084
Give every analyst read access to all 800 datasets and audit usage with CloudRail.

8663
14:12:02,958 --> 14:12:04,228
Pause and decide.

8664
14:12:13,966 --> 14:12:19,600
The answer is A, and the question states two requirements that only one option satisfies together.

8665
14:12:19,840 --> 14:12:28,004
Decisions should be made by whoever owns the data, which addresses the central team's lack of context and each decision must be recorded for audit.

8666
14:12:28,814 --> 14:12:30,692
The subscription workflow does both.

8667
14:12:31,054 --> 14:12:38,468
Because a consumer requests access with a stated purpose, the owner approves or refuses, the decision is captured against the asset.

8668
14:12:39,086 --> 14:12:41,924
The underlying lake formation grant is applied automatically.

8669
14:12:42,798 --> 14:12:52,708
Option C is the one worth naming because adding people to a queue is the response organizations actually reach for. It scales the bottleneck rather than removing it.

8670
14:12:54,160 --> 14:13:00,080
With three sets of results, this mapping becomes considerably more useful than it was after one.

8671
14:13:00,480 --> 14:13:02,404
A row missed once is noise.

8672
14:13:02,894 --> 14:13:11,044
A row missed twice across 60 questions is a genuine gap, and the module named is where the remaining preparation time should go.

8673
14:13:11,950 --> 14:13:14,800
A row missed in exam 1 and passed in exams.

8674
14:13:14,960 --> 14:13:17,444
2 and 3 is evidence that reviewing worked.

8675
14:13:17,920 --> 14:13:20,132
That is worth knowing for its own sake.

8676
14:13:20,814 --> 14:13:24,996
It tells you the method is sound and you simply need to apply it to whatever is left.

8677
14:13:26,640 --> 14:13:31,236
The callout is the finding worth taking from 60 questions rather than 20.

8678
14:13:31,840 --> 14:13:35,364
Across all three sets, one construction dominated.

8679
14:13:35,760 --> 14:13:41,220
An option that solved a technical problem while breaking something the scenario had explicitly refused.

8680
14:13:42,126 --> 14:13:47,268
No new code, no source changes, no capacity to operate cannot change the protocol.

8681
14:13:48,142 --> 14:13:52,644
That is why module 22 put finding the constraint before reading the options.

8682
14:13:53,550 --> 14:14:02,884
Why the signal call out on almost every solution in this course points at a sentence in the scenario rather than at anything in the options themselves.

8683
14:14:04,622 --> 14:14:08,484
This is a small diagnostic and it occasionally reveals something useful.

8684
14:14:10,542 --> 14:14:17,604
The correct answers in these three exams are distributed roughly evenly across the four letters, which was a deliberate correction.

8685
14:14:18,560 --> 14:14:24,964
That is because the first draft of exam 1 put 21 of its 22 correct marks on A or B.

8686
14:14:25,280 --> 14:14:31,652
If your own choices cluster heavily on one letter across 60 questions, the questions are not causing that.

8687
14:14:32,494 --> 14:14:37,572
The most common explanation is settling on an option that reads well and skimming the rest.

8688
14:14:38,222 --> 14:14:41,604
That is also the habit that makes you vulnerable to a better option.

8689
14:14:42,254 --> 14:14:43,524
Sitting in position.

8690
14:14:44,160 --> 14:14:44,480
B.

8691
14:14:46,382 --> 14:14:50,560
The second bullet is the most common outcome and it is worth being honest about.

8692
14:14:50,990 --> 14:14:55,460
If your three scores are similar, the likeliest explanation is not that you have plateaued.

8693
14:14:55,950 --> 14:14:59,364
It is that you took three tests without studying the results of the first two.

8694
14:15:00,110 --> 14:15:03,652
The fourth bullet describes the shape most candidates have.

8695
14:15:04,014 --> 14:15:08,548
That is because domain 1 is a third of the exam and gets most of the study time.

8696
14:15:09,198 --> 14:15:12,772
Meanwhile, domain 4 is 18% and gets neglected.

8697
14:15:13,422 --> 14:15:18,084
Module 0 opened this course with that observation and it is worth closing on it.

8698
14:15:19,022 --> 14:15:24,196
That is because 9 score questions is the cheapest improvement available to most people.

8699
14:15:25,710 --> 14:15:27,840
The third bullet is the one that matters most.

8700
14:15:28,000 --> 14:15:33,380
It is why every solution slide in this course explains all four options rather than only the correct one.

8701
14:15:34,126 --> 14:15:37,092
Being able to recognize the right answer is one thing.

8702
14:15:37,582 --> 14:15:44,932
Being able to say why the other three fail is what survives a differently worded question about the same decision.

8703
14:15:45,582 --> 14:15:53,348
If you can work through 60 questions and articulate the flaw in every distractor, you understand the decisions rather than the answers.

8704
14:15:54,030 --> 14:15:56,612
That is what this exam is actually testing.

8705
14:15:58,350 --> 14:15:59,972
That is the whole course.

8706
14:16:00,560 --> 14:16:08,388
20 teaching modules covered all four domains and every task statement in the December 2025 exam guide.

8707
14:16:08,942 --> 14:16:14,960
From the mental model in module 1 through to governance in module 20.

8708
14:16:15,534 --> 14:16:24,452
Then confusion pairs, how the exam writes its questions, the revision gap between this exam and older material, and three practice exams.

8709
14:16:25,038 --> 14:16:31,588
About 100 word scenarios in total, each one naming the sentence that decided it and giving a reason for every option.

8710
14:16:32,334 --> 14:16:40,292
If a service felt thin somewhere, that was usually deliberate because the module that covers it properly said so at the time.

8711
14:16:42,000 --> 14:16:48,560
The last bullet is worth restating because the numbers behind it were the reason this course is shaped as it is.

8712
14:16:48,880 --> 14:16:54,308
Security and governance terms accounted for under 5% of mentions in the material analyzed.

8713
14:16:54,862 --> 14:16:59,460
Domain 4 is 18% of the exam, which is roughly 9 score questions.

8714
14:16:59,854 --> 14:17:04,260
That gap is the single cheapest improvement available to almost anybody preparing.

8715
14:17:05,294 --> 14:17:07,844
The fourth bullet was a deliberate choice, too.

8716
14:17:08,558 --> 14:17:18,820
That is because a course that teaches only the exam answer leaves you confidently recommending a service that closed to new customers 18 months ago.

8717
14:17:19,822 --> 14:17:21,188
Good luck with the exam.

